COLLDATA("seerah","en",[[1,"The term Jāhiliyyah (the Age of Ignorance) applies to the people before the coming of Islam, and there is no disagreement about that. There is, however, disagreement about when this period begins. The preferred view is that it began after ʿĪsā (Jesus), peace be upon him, was raised alive to the heavens, and with Paul’s innovation in Christianity. Keep in mind that there is no prophet between ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, and the Prophet ﷺ. With the sending of the Prophet ﷺ, this age of ignorance ended and the light of Islam spread. Allah the Exalted said: “There has certainly come to you from Allah a light and a clear Book.” (al-Mā’idah 15) And Allah the Exalted said: “O people! There has certainly come to you a clear proof from your Lord, and We have sent down to you a clear light.” (al-Nisā’ 174)"],[2,"Without doubt, the emergence of the Muhammadan message is the greatest event in the history of the Arabs in particular and in the history of all humanity in general. For not even ten years had passed after the Hijrah when Allah inflicted a crushing defeat on the polytheists and the Islamic state was established, with Madinah the Illuminated as its capital. In a very short time the Islamic state expanded from Andalusia (Spain) to China; it was a great state that stretched across three continents (Asia, Africa and Europe). Islamic history is very long, and mentioning it is not the purpose here."],[3,"Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I saw ʿAmr ibn Luḥayy ibn Qamaʿah ibn Khindif, the brother of these Banū Kaʿb, dragging his intestines in the Fire.”"],[4,"Abū Rajā’ al-ʿUṭāridī said: We used to worship a stone. If we found a better stone than it, we would throw it away and take the other. If we could not find a stone, we would gather a mound of earth, then bring a sheep and milk it over it, then circumambulate it. When the month of Rajab entered, we would say: “Remove the iron points,” and we would not leave any spear with an iron tip, nor any arrow with an iron head, except that we removed it and discarded it during the month of Rajab. He also said: I heard Abū Rajā’ saying: On the day the Prophet ﷺ was sent, I was a boy grazing camels for my family. When we heard of his emergence, we fled to the Fire — to Musaylimah the Liar."],[5,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: If it pleases you to know the ignorance of the Arabs, then recite what comes after one hundred and thirty in Sūrat al-Anʿām: “Lost indeed are those who killed their children foolishly without knowledge, and forbade what Allah had provided for them, inventing lies against Allah. They have gone astray and were not guided.” [al-Anʿām: 140]"],[6,"Our Prophet Muhammad al-Muṣṭafā ﷺ was born in 569 CE in Makkah the Honored, which was known as Umm al-Qurā (the Mother of Towns), as Allah the Exalted said in the Qur’an: ﴿وَ هٰذَا كِتٰبٌ اَنْزَلْنٰهُ مُبٰرَكٌ مُّصَدِّقُ الَّذِيْ بَيْنَ يَدَيْهِ وَ لِتُنْذِرَ اُمَّ الْقُرٰى وَ مَنْ حَوْلَهَا١ؕ﴾ “And this is a Book which We have sent down, blessed, confirming what came before it, so that you may warn Umm al-Qurā (Makkah) and those around it.” [al-An‘ām: 92].\nMakkah the Honored had virtue over the whole world in general and over the Arabian Peninsula in particular in several respects. Its greatest virtue was the House of Allah (the noble Ka‘bah), about which the supplication of Ibrāhīm, peace be upon him, is mentioned in the Qur’an in these words: ﴿رَبَّنَاۤ اِنِّيْۤ اَسْكَنْتُ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِيْ بِوَادٍ غَيْرِ ذِيْ زَرْعٍ عِنْدَ بَيْتِكَ الْمُحَرَّمِ١ۙ رَبَّنَا لِيُقِيْمُوا الصَّلٰوةَ فَاجْعَلْ اَفْىِٕدَةً مِّنَ النَّاسِ تَهْوِيْۤ اِلَيْهِمْ وَ ارْزُقْهُمْ مِّنَ الثَّمَرٰتِ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَشْكُرُوْنَ۰۰۳۷﴾ “Our Lord! I have settled some of my offspring in a barren valley near Your Sacred House, so that they may establish prayer. So make the hearts of some people incline toward them, and provide them with fruits, so that they may give thanks.” [Ibrāhīm: 37].\nLikewise, Makkah was famous as a trading center. Its people made trading journeys to Syria in the summer and to Yemen in the winter, as Allah the Exalted said in Sūrat Quraysh: ﴿لِاِيْلٰفِ قُرَيْشٍۙ۰۰۱اٖلٰفِهِمْ رِحْلَةَ الشِّتَآءِ وَ الصَّيْفِۚ۰۰۲﴾ “For the familiarity of Quraysh, their familiarity with the journey of winter and summer.” [Quraysh: 1-2].\nMakkah the Honored holds honor in various respects both before Islam and after Islam, and its greatest virtue is precisely that the Prophet ﷺ was born there."],[7,"Ibn ʿAbbas said: The Prophet ﷺ was born in the Year of the Elephant."],[8,"Al-Muṭṭalib ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Qays ibn Makhramah narrated from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were born in the Year of the Elephant, so we are peers; we were born in the same year."],[9,"The saying of Allah the Exalted: “Did He not find you an orphan and give you shelter?” (al-Ḍuḥā 6)"],[10,"Qays ibn Makhramah said: I and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were born in the Year of the Elephant, so we are peers."],[11,"He is Muhammad ﷺ, son of ʿAbdullāh, son of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, son of Hāshim, son of ʿAbd Manāf, son of Quṣayy, son of Kilāb, son of Murrah, son of Kaʿb, son of Luʾayy, son of Ghālib, son of Fihr, son of Mālik, son of al-Naḍr, son of Kinānah, son of Khuzaymah, son of Mudrikah, son of Ilyās, son of Muḍar, son of Nizār, son of Maʿadd, son of ʿAdnān.\nImam al-Bukhārī, may Allah have mercy on him, mentioned the lineage of the Prophet ﷺ up to this point in his Ṣaḥīḥ, in Kitāb Manāqib al-Anṣār, chapter on the sending of the Prophet ﷺ."],[12,"The scholars are unanimous on the soundness of the lineage of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ up to ʿAdnān. It is reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that when he recounted his blessed lineage he would stop at ʿAdnān and say: “The genealogists have lied.” The Lord says: ﴿وَ قُرُوْنًۢا بَيْنَ ذٰلِكَ كَثِيْرًا۰۰۳۸﴾ (al-Furqān: 38) “and many generations in between.” That is, there are many more links between them."],[13,"Zaynab bint Abī Salamah — the stepdaughter of the Prophet ﷺ — was asked: “Was the Prophet ﷺ from Muḍar?” She said: “From whom else would he be except from Muḍar? He was from Banū al-Naḍr ibn Kinānah.”"],[14,"Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Allah chose Kinānah from the children of Ismāʿīl, chose Quraysh from Kinānah, chose Banū Hāshim from Quraysh, and chose me from Banū Hāshim.”"],[15,"Al-Ashʿath ibn Qays said: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in a delegation, and they did not think that I was the best of them. I said: “O Messenger of Allah, we claim that you are from us.” He said: “We are Banū al-Naḍr, Banū Kinānah. We do not follow our mother in lineage, nor do we disown our father.”"],[16,"Abū Qatādah said: A Bedouin said: “O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about fasting on Monday?” He said: “That is the day on which I was born, and on which revelation was sent down to me.”"],[17,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Deeds are presented on Monday and Thursday, and I love for my deed to be presented while I am fasting.”"],[18,"Al-ʿIrbāḍ ibn Sāriyah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, with Allah I was the Seal of the Prophets while Adam, peace be upon him, was still cast down in his clay. I will inform you of the beginning of that: the supplication of my father Ibrahim, the glad tidings of ʿIsa concerning me, and the vision which my mother saw; and likewise the mothers of the prophets see such visions.” In another narration: “And the vision which my mother saw, that a light came out from her by which the palaces of al-Shām were illuminated.”"],[19,"Khālid ibn Maʿdān narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that they said: O Messenger of Allah, tell us about yourself. He said: “I am the supplication of my father Ibrahim, the glad tidings of ʿIsa, and my mother saw, when she carried me, that a light came out from her by which Buṣrā was illuminated. Buṣrā is in the land of al-Shām.”"],[20,"ʿA’ishah said: A Jewish man had settled in Makkah and traded there. On the night when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was born, he said in an assembly of Quraysh: “O people of Quraysh, has a child been born among you tonight?” The people said: “By Allah, we do not know.” He said: “Allah is greater. If you missed it, then there is no harm. Look and remember what I say to you: Tonight the prophet of this last nation has been born among you. Between his shoulders is a sign with consecutive hairs like the mane of a horse. He will not nurse for two nights, because an ʿifrit from the jinn placed his finger in his mouth and prevented him from nursing.” The people dispersed from their gathering, amazed at what he said and narrated. When they reached their homes, each of them informed his family, and they said: “A boy has been born to ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib; they named him Muhammad.” The people met and said: “Did you hear the speech of this Jew? Has the birth of this boy reached you?” So they went until they came to the Jew and informed him of the news. He said: “Go with me so I may look at him.” They went with him until they brought him in to Āminah. He said: “Bring out your son to us.” She brought him out, and they uncovered his back for him. He saw that mark, and the Jew fell unconscious. When he regained consciousness, they said: “Woe to you, what is wrong with you?” He said: “By Allah, prophethood has gone from the Children of Israel. Are you happy about him, O people of Quraysh? By Allah, he will surely strike you with a mighty blow, the news of which will spread from the east to the west.” Among those to whom the Jew said what he said were Hishām and al-Walīd, the two sons of al-Mughīrah, Musāfir ibn Abī ʿAmr, ʿUbaydah ibn al-Ḥārith, and ʿUqbah ibn Rabīʿah — a young man above the age of puberty — along with a group from Banū ʿAbd Manāf and others from Quraysh."],[21,"§1- Thuwaybah"],[22,"ʿUrwah said: Thuwaybah was the freedwoman of Abū Lahab. Abū Lahab had freed her, and she nursed the Prophet ﷺ."],[23,"ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAbbas said: It was said to the Prophet ﷺ: “Will you not marry the daughter of Ḥamzah?” He said: “She is the daughter of my brother through nursing.”"],[24,"Khālid ibn Maʿdān narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that they said to him: “Tell us about yourself.” He said: “Yes. I am the supplication of my father Ibrahim and the glad tidings of ʿIsa, peace be upon them both. My mother saw, when she was carrying me, that a light came out from her by which the palaces of al-Shām were illuminated. I was suckled among Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr. While I was with some small livestock belonging to us, two men came to me wearing white garments, carrying a golden basin full of snow. They laid me down, split open my abdomen, then took out my heart and split it open. They removed from it a black clot and threw it away. Then they washed my heart and abdomen with that snow until they purified them, then returned them as they had been. Then one of them said to his companion: Weigh him against ten of his Ummah. So he weighed me against ten, and I outweighed them. Then he said: Weigh him against one hundred of his Ummah. So he weighed me against one hundred, and I outweighed them. Then he said: Weigh him against one thousand of his Ummah. So he weighed me against one thousand, and I outweighed them. He said: Leave him, for if you weighed him against his entire Ummah, he would outweigh them.”"],[25,"Sayyidunā ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, may Allah be pleased with him, relates that the foster mother of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Ḥalīmah bint Abī Dhu’ayb al-Saʿdiyyah, set out from her settlement with her husband. With her was her nursing son. With her were also several other women of Banū Saʿd. Their purpose was to take a foster child. It was a year of famine; there was nothing to eat or drink. She says: I was riding my white she-donkey, and we had with us a thin she-camel that did not give even a drop of milk. My child kept crying from hunger, so we could not sleep at all that night. There was nothing in my breast for the child, and nothing in the udder of the she-camel. We kept travelling in search of sustenance and relief. Because of my weak she-donkey the people of the caravan were quite fed up with me. I fell behind the caravan because of my slowness. When we reached Makkah, Muhammad ﷺ was offered to every one of us women. But when it was said that he was an orphan, everyone refused to take him, because our aim was to receive rewards and gifts from the child’s father. We said: He is an orphan! What would we get from his mother for nursing him? For this reason we did not like to take him. All the women who came with me got children except me. When we were about to go back, I said to my husband: It is not good for me alone to go back empty-handed. So I will take that orphan child. He said: Fine, perhaps Allah will make him a source of blessing for us. I went and took him, because there was no other child left besides him. When I came back with him to my mount and sat him in my lap, my breast filled with milk. He and my son drank their fill, and both went to sleep, whereas before that we could not sleep at all because of my child’s hunger. My husband went to the she-camel, and she too was full of milk. We drank the camel’s milk to our fill. We spent the night in comfort and well-being. When morning came, my husband said: Ḥalīmah! By Allah, you have received a blessed child. I said: By Allah, I hope the same. We set out for the return, and I took him ﷺ with me in my lap. We travelled, and by Allah, my mount left all the other mounts behind. The other women asked in astonishment: Daughter of Abū Dhu’ayb! Look after us too. Is this not the same weak mount you came on? I said: By Allah, this is the very same mount. The women said: Then there is something remarkable about it now.\nWe reached our home, where there was severe famine. But with his coming our condition changed completely. Our goats too became full of milk, though before they gave no milk at all because of hunger and thirst. We would milk the goats and drink to our fill, while other people’s goats were completely empty of milk. Our neighbors would say to their shepherds: You too should graze your flocks where the daughter of Abū Dhu’ayb’s flocks graze. Yet their flocks would still come back hungry and thirsty, with no milk at all in their udders, while our goats were full of milk. Allah blessed us abundantly with goodness and blessing, until two years passed in this way. He grew faster than other children; within two years he looked healthy and quite big. We brought him to his mother. We were very eager to keep him with us for a longer period, because blessings and bounties were descending on us because of him. We spoke to his mother and said: Let your child stay with us longer, for we fear the epidemic of Makkah for him. We kept talking to his mother for a long time until she handed him over to us again."],[26,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that Jibrīl came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was playing with the boys. He took him, threw him down, and split open his chest over his heart. He took out the heart, removed a clot from it, and said: “This is Satan’s portion of you.” Then he washed it in a golden basin with Zamzam water, stitched it back together, and returned it to its place. The boys came running to his mother — meaning his wet nurse — saying: “Muḥammad has been killed.” They met him while his color had changed. Anas said: I used to see the mark of that stitching on his chest."],[27,"ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbd al-Sulamī narrated that a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “How was the beginning of your affair, O Messenger of Allah?” He said: “My nurse was from Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr. I went out with a son of hers among some small livestock belonging to us, and we had not taken any provision with us. I said: My brother, go and bring us some provision from our mother. So my brother went, and I remained with the small livestock. Then two white birds came toward me, as though they were eagles. One said to the other: Is it he? He said: Yes. They hastened toward me, took me, and laid me flat on my back. They split open my abdomen, then took out my heart and split it. They removed two black clots from it. One said to the other — Yazīd said in his narration: Bring me snow water — and they washed my inside with it. Then he said: Bring me hail water, and they washed my heart with it. Then he said: Bring me tranquility, and he sprinkled it into my heart. Then one of them said to his companion: Stitch it. So he stitched it and sealed it with the Seal of Prophethood — and Ḥaywah said in his narration: Stitch it, so he stitched it, and seal it with the Seal of Prophethood. Then one of them said to his companion: Place him in one pan of the scale, and place one thousand of his Ummah in the other pan. I then saw the thousand above me, and I feared that some of them would fall upon me. He said: If his Ummah were weighed against him, he would outweigh them. Then they departed and left me, and I became extremely frightened. I went to my mother and told her what I had experienced. She feared that something had confused me, and said: I seek refuge for you in Allah. She saddled a camel of hers and placed me — and in another narration: carried me — on the saddle, and rode behind me until we reached my mother. She said: Have I fulfilled my trust and responsibility? And she told her what I had experienced. That did not frighten her. She said: I saw a light come out from me by which the palaces of al-Shām were illuminated.”"],[28,"Anas ibn Mālik said: Abū Dharr used to narrate that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The roof of my house was opened while I was in Makkah. Jibrīl descended, opened my chest, then washed it with Zamzam water. Then he brought a golden basin full of wisdom and faith, poured it into my chest, and then closed it.”"],[29,"Allah the Exalted said: “Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and those with him are severe against the disbelievers.” (al-Fatḥ 29)"],[30,"And the saying of Allah the Exalted on the tongue of ʿĪsā, peace be upon him: “…whose name is Aḥmad, who will come after me.” (al-Ṣaff: 6)"],[31,"Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have five names: I am Muḥammad, and Aḥmad. I am al-Māḥī, through whom Allah erases disbelief. I am al-Ḥāshir, at whose feet people will be gathered. And I am al-ʿĀqib.”"],[32,"Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Do you not marvel at how Allah turns away from me the insults and curses of Quraysh? They insult Mudhammam and curse Mudhammam, while I am Muḥammad.”"],[33,"Abū Musa al-Ashʿari said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to name himself for us with names. He said: “I am Muḥammad, al-Muqaffā, al-Ḥāshir, the Prophet of Repentance, and the Prophet of Mercy.”"],[34,"Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say in one of the streets of Medina: “I am Muḥammad, I am Aḥmad, al-Ḥāshir, al-Muqaffā, and the Prophet of Mercy.”"],[35,"Anas said: A man called another man in al-Baqīʿ: “O Abū al-Qāsim!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned toward him. The man said: “O Messenger of Allah, I did not mean you; I only called so-and-so.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Name yourselves with my name, but do not use my kunyah.” In another narration: The Prophet ﷺ was in the market."],[36,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullah said: A boy was born to a man among us, and he named him al-Qāsim. The Anṣār said: “We will not give you the kunyah Abū al-Qāsim, nor will we let your eye be delighted.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “The Anṣār have done well. Name yourselves with my name, but do not use my kunyah, for I am only a distributor.” In Muslim’s narration: “For I am Abū al-Qāsim; I distribute among you.”"],[37,"Abū Hurayrah said: Abū al-Qāsim ﷺ said: “Name yourselves with my name, but do not use my kunyah.”"],[38,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Ḥazm relates that Āminah, the mother of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, took him with her to Madinah, to Banū ʿAdī ibn al-Najjār, his maternal uncles (that is, his mother’s family). When she was returning with him, she died at al-Abwā’. The blessed age of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at that time was six years.\nThis report is mursal. The majority of the scholars of biography and history hold that Āminah, the mother of the Prophet ﷺ, died at al-Abwā’, in the settlement of Waddān. It is a settlement between Madinah and Makkah."],[39,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that a man said: “O Messenger of Allah, where is my father?” He said: “In the Fire.” When he turned away, he called him and said: “Indeed, my father and your father are in the Fire.”"],[40,"Abū Hurayrah said: The Prophet ﷺ visited the grave of his mother, so he wept and made those around him weep. Then he said: “I sought permission from my Lord to visit the grave of my mother, and He permitted me. I sought permission from Him to ask forgiveness for her, but He did not permit me. So visit graves, for they remind you of death.”"],[41,"Buraydah said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Makkah, he came to the base of a grave and sat beside it. He sat as though addressing someone, while the people sat around him. Then he stood while weeping. ʿUmar met him — and he was among the boldest of people with him — and said: “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah, what made you weep?” He said: “This is the grave of my mother. I asked my Lord for permission to visit it, and He permitted me. I asked Him for permission to seek forgiveness for her, but He did not permit me. I remembered her, my heart softened, and I wept.” He said: No day was seen in which he wept more than that day."],[42,"Anas ibn Mālik said: When the Emigrants came from Makkah to Medina, they came with nothing in their hands, while the Anṣār were people of land and property. The Anṣār shared with them on the condition that they would give them half of the fruits of their property every year, and the Emigrants would spare them the work and expenses. The mother of Anas ibn Mālik, who was called Umm Sulaym, and who was also the mother of ʿAbdullah ibn Abī Ṭalḥah — he was Anas’s brother through his mother — had given the Messenger of Allah ﷺ some date-palms of hers. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave them to Umm Ayman, his freedwoman, the mother of Usāmah ibn Zayd. Ibn Shihāb said: Anas ibn Mālik informed me that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished fighting the people of Khaybar and returned to Medina, the Emigrants returned to the Anṣār the grants of fruit that they had given them. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned to my mother her date-palms, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave Umm Ayman in their place from his orchard. Ibn Shihāb said: The matter of Umm Ayman, the mother of Usāmah ibn Zayd, was that she was a young slave-girl of ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, and she was from Abyssinia. When Āminah gave birth to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ after his father had died, Umm Ayman cared for him until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ grew up. He freed her, then married her to Zayd ibn Ḥārithah. She died five months after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died."],[43,"It is reported from Sayyidunā ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, that ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib circumcised the Prophet ﷺ on the seventh day, held a feast on his account, and named him Muhammad.\nOn this subject it is also reported from Sayyidunā Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “It is among my honors that I was born circumcised, and no one ever saw my private parts.”\nḤāfiẓ Ibn al-Qayyim, may Allah have mercy on him, mentions in Tuḥfat al-Mawdūd, p. 345, that some reports state that his grandfather ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib circumcised him on the seventh day.\nDespite their weaknesses, these reports are the closest to being correct and the nearest to the truth. The Imam added in Zād al-Maʿād 1/81 that there are three views about his circumcision:\nThe first view: The Prophet ﷺ was born circumcised with the umbilical cord already cut. The report narrated on this is not sound. Ibn al-Jawzī mentioned this report among the fabricated reports (al-Mawḍūʿāt); no ḥadīth on this is established as sound. Nor is it one of his special characteristics, for many people are born circumcised.\nThe second view: When the Prophet ﷺ was with his foster mother Ḥalīmah, the angels split open his chest, and it was on that occasion that he was circumcised.\nThe third view: His grandfather ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib circumcised him on the seventh day, arranged a feast on his account, and chose the name Muhammad for him.\nAfter this the Imam writes: This question has also been debated between two learned authors. One of them wrote a book about his being born circumcised, in which he collected many baseless reports. He is Kamāl al-Dīn ibn Ṭalḥah. Kamāl al-Dīn ibn al-ʿAdīm wrote a critique of him. He proved that the Prophet ﷺ was circumcised according to the Arab custom. Because this was the general custom throughout Arabia, there is absolutely no need to mention any special event concerning it. And Allah knows best."],[44,"It is related that ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, the grandfather of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, had a special seat in the shade of the Kaʿbah. Because of his awe and majesty, not even any of his own children dared to sit in his seat. But when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat in his grandfather’s seat and one of his uncles tried to stop him, ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib would say: “Do not stop him,” and, stroking the blessed back of the Prophet ﷺ, he would say: “This son of mine will have great standing.” ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib died eight years after the Year of the Elephant. The Prophet ﷺ was then eight years old. ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib entrusted him to his uncle Abū Ṭālib.\nIbn Saʿd mentions in al-Ṭabaqāt (11/119) that ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib was buried in the Ḥajūn cemetery in Makkah. He was 82 years old. It is also said that he was 120 years old. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked whether he remembered the death of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, he would say: “Yes, I was eight years old then.” Sayyidah Umm Ayman, may Allah be pleased with her, relates: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ walking behind the body of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, weeping with grief at being parted from him."],[45,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullah said: When the Kaʿbah was being built, the Prophet ﷺ and al-ʿAbbās went to carry stones. Al-ʿAbbās said to the Prophet ﷺ: “Place your lower garment over your neck; it will protect you from the stones.” He fell to the ground, and his eyes looked upward toward the sky. Then he regained consciousness and said: “My lower garment.” So he tied his lower garment around himself."],[46,"Abū Musa al-Ashʿari said: Abū Ṭālib went out to al-Shām, and the Prophet ﷺ went out with him among the elders of Quraysh. When they came near the monk, they stopped and unloaded their mounts. The monk came out to them, though before that they used to pass by him and he would neither come out to them nor pay attention. He said: While they were unloading their mounts, the monk began moving among them until he came and took the hand of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “This is the master of the worlds. This is the messenger of the Lord of the worlds. Allah will send him as a mercy to the worlds.” The elders of Quraysh said to him: “How do you know?” He said: “When you came near from the mountain pass, there was no tree or stone except that it fell in prostration, and they only prostrate to a prophet. I recognize him by the Seal of Prophethood below the cartilage of his shoulder, like an apple.” Then he returned and prepared food for them. When he brought it to them, the Prophet ﷺ was among the camels. He said: “Send for him.” He came with a cloud shading him. When he came near the people, he found that they had preceded him to the shade of the tree. When he sat down, the shade of the tree moved over him. The monk said: “Look at the shade of the tree; it has moved over him.” He said: While he was standing over them and imploring them not to take him to the Romans — for if the Romans saw him, they would recognize him by his description and kill him — he turned and saw seven men approaching from the Romans. He met them and said: “What has brought you?” They said: “We have come because this prophet is emerging in this month. No road has been left except that men were sent to it, and we were informed of his news, so we were sent to this road of yours.” He said: “Is there anyone behind you who is better than you?” They said: “We were only informed of his news on this road of yours.” He said: “Tell me, if Allah wants to decree a matter, can any person repel it?” They said: “No.” He said: So they pledged allegiance to him and stayed with him. He said: “I adjure you by Allah, which of you is his guardian?” They said: “Abū Ṭālib.” He kept imploring him until Abū Ṭālib sent him back. Abū Bakr sent Bilāl with him, and the monk provided him with cakes and olive oil."],[47,"Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “There is no prophet except that he herded sheep.” They said: “And you, O Messenger of Allah?” He said: “Yes, I used to herd them for qirāṭs for the people of Makkah.”"],[48,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullah said: We were with the Prophet ﷺ at Marr al-Ẓahrān, gathering kabāth, and the Prophet ﷺ said: “Take the black ones.” He said: We said: “O Messenger of Allah, it is as though you herded sheep.” He said: “Yes, and was there any prophet except that he herded them?” or words similar to this."],[49,"ʿAbdah ibn Ḥazn said: The camel-owners and the sheep-owners boasted to one another. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Musa was sent while he was a shepherd of sheep. Dawud was sent while he was a shepherd of sheep. And I was sent while I was herding sheep for my family at Ajyād.”"],[50,"It is reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: “I used to scatter arrows before my uncles,” to be shot at the enemy. One meaning given is that he would pick up the arrows the enemy had thrown and hand them to his uncles. In this war the commander of Quraysh and Banū Kinānah was Ḥarb ibn Umayyah ibn ʿAbd Shams. Ibn Hishām (1/126) relates this incident without a chain of narration. Another meaning given is that “I used to hold arrows for my uncles to shoot.” I have not found this report with a chain of narration.\nAccording to Ibn Isḥāq, the blessed age of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the time of Ḥarb al-Fijār was 20 years. It is called the War of al-Fijār (Violation) because in it both tribes, Banū Kinānah and Qays ʿAylān, violated one another’s sacred things. The leader of Quraysh and Banū Kinānah was Ḥarb ibn Umayyah ibn ʿAbd Shams. At the start of the war Qays prevailed over Banū Kinānah, but at midday Banū Kinānah gained the upper hand over Qays.\nIbn Hishām disagreed with Ibn Isḥāq regarding the determination of his age. He says that he was then fourteen or fifteen years old."],[51,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I witnessed with my uncles the alliance of al-Muṭayyibīn. I would not love to have red camels in exchange for breaking it.”"],[52,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “There is no new alliance in Islam, and every alliance that existed in the Jāhiliyyah was only increased in strength by Islam. It would not please me to have red camels in exchange for breaking the alliance that took place in Dār al-Nadwah.”"],[53,"Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I did not witness any alliance of Quraysh except the alliance of al-Muṭayyibīn, and I would not love to have red camels in exchange for having broken it.” He said: The Muṭayyibūn were Hāshim, Umayyah, Zuhrah, and Makhzūm."],[54,"Regarding why this pact is called Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl, it is mentioned that before this Quraysh pact, the tribe of Banū Jurhum had made a similar pact. Among its members were these three men: the first was al-Faḍl ibn Fuḍālah, the second al-Faḍl ibn Wadāʿah, and the third al-Fuḍayl ibn al-Ḥārith. It is also said, rather, that the three men were: Fuḍayl ibn Shurāʿah, al-Faḍl ibn Wadāʿah and Fuḍayl ibn Quḍāʿah. Because the Quraysh pact resembled that earlier pact, it too was called Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl.\nIt is said that it was called Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl because the parties to the pact swore that property taken from people through wrongdoing and injustice would be returned to them, and that no wrongdoer would oppress any wronged person. At the time of this pact the blessed age of the Prophet ﷺ was twenty years. It was a very excellent and beneficial pact.\nal-Qutaybī says that this pact was concluded in the mansion of ʿAbdullāh ibn Judʿān. It was called Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl because it resembled the pact that Banū Jurhum made in Makkah regarding the distribution of offices. It was also agreed in it that the weak would be given their due against the strong, and that the stranger would also be helped. These men of Banū Jurhum were also parties to that pact: al-Faḍl ibn al-Ḥārith, al-Faḍl ibn Wadāʿah, al-Faḍl ibn Fuḍālah. Because of their names containing “Faḍl,” this pact was named Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl.\nSome have mentioned the names of the parties to the pact as: Faḍl, Fuḍāl, Fuḍayl and Fuḍālah.\nIbn Isḥāq says that the tribes of Quraysh signaled their willingness to make a pact, and because of the standing and rank of ʿAbdullāh ibn Judʿān, people gathered at his house. The following families were part of this pact: Banū Hāshim, Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, Zuhrah ibn Kilāb and Taym ibn Murrah. They pledged that whoever in Makkah was wronged, whether a local or an outsider, we would stand with him. He would be supported and defended until the wronged person received his right. Quraysh also wrote the name of this pact as Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl. Sīrat Ibn Hishām (1/133-134)"],[55,"Khadījah, may Allah be pleased with her, daughter of Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā al-Qurashiyyah"],[56,"Jābir said: Khadījah hired the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for two journeys to Jurash, each journey for a young she-camel."],[57,"Ibn Isḥāq says that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married Khadījah, may Allah be pleased with her. Before the descent of revelation, there were born to her Zaynab, Umm Kulthūm, Ruqayyah, Fāṭimah, al-Qāsim, al-Ṭāhir and al-Ṭayyib, may Allah be pleased with them all. Al-Qāsim, al-Ṭāhir and al-Ṭayyib, may Allah be pleased with them, died before the advent of Islam. The Prophet ﷺ used to be called by the kunyah (name) of al-Qāsim, may Allah be pleased with him. All the daughters of the Prophet ﷺ lived to see Islam, migrated together with him, followed him, and believed in him.\nI (the author, may Allah have mercy on him) say: There is no disagreement that he had four daughters. There is, however, disagreement about the number of sons. According to al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār, besides Ibrāhīm, may Allah be pleased with him, the children of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ included al-Qāsim and ʿAbdullāh. Al-Ṣāliḥī also states this in Subul al-Hudā, and this is the view of most of the genealogists. A statement is reported from al-Dāraquṭnī that this is the most firmly established, and Ḥāfiẓ ʿAbd al-Ghanī al-Maqdisī, may Allah have mercy on him, declared it sound."],[58,"It is reported from Ibn Isḥāq that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ grew into a young man. Allah the Exalted protected him from the bad habits and ways of the Jāhiliyyah, because he was to be honored in the future with distinction, dignity, prophethood and messengership. Before Islam he was on the religion and way of his people. He passed through the stages of life and proved to be the best man among his people. He was the most outstanding in character and good conduct, distinguished in neighborliness and fine manners, unique in truthfulness and trustworthiness. He was free from indecent acts and habits, and was protected even from things that were considered bad socially in that society. Allah the Exalted gathered in him all the fine habits and qualities, so much so that he became known among his people by the title al-Amīn (the Trustworthy). The reports also mention matters from which Allah protected him in childhood and the practices of the Jāhiliyyah from which He guarded him.\nThe proof of the title al-Amīn for the Prophet ﷺ is also found in the reports that will come in a later chapter. As for the words of the report “he was on the religion and way of his people,” these words are munkar (objectionable); this will be explained in the chapter that mentions that before the prophetic mission he was a follower of the religion of Ibrāhīm."],[59,"Mujāhid narrated from his patron that he told him he was among those who built the Kaʿbah during the Jāhiliyyah. He said: I had a stone that I carved with my own hand and worshiped besides Allah, Blessed and Most High. I would bring thick milk that I preferred for myself and pour it over it, then a dog would come and lick it, then lift its leg and urinate. We built until we reached the place of the Stone, while no one could see the Stone. Then it was in the middle of our stones, like the head of a man, and a person’s face could almost be seen in it. One clan of Quraysh said: “We will place it.” Others said: “We will place it.” They said: “Appoint a judge between you.” They said: “The first man to appear from the mountain path.” Then the Prophet ﷺ came, and they said: “The Trustworthy One has come to you.” They told him. He placed it in a cloth, then called their clans, and they took its sides along with him. Then he ﷺ placed it."],[60,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib said: When they wanted to raise the Black Stone, they disputed over it. They said: “We will appoint as judge between us the first man who comes out from this lane.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the first to come out to them. He judged between them that they should place it in a cloak, then all the tribes would lift it together. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took it and placed it."],[61,"Abū al-Ṭufayl said: In the Jāhiliyyah, the Kaʿbah was built of stacked stones, without clay. Its height was about what a young goat could leap over. It was not roofed; its coverings were only placed upon it and then let down over it. The Black Corner was placed visibly upon its wall, and it had two corners like the form of this ring. A ship came from the land of the Romans, and when they were near Jeddah, the ship broke apart. Quraysh went out to take its wood, and they found a Roman man beside it. They took the wood; he gave it to them. The ship had been heading toward Abyssinia, and the Roman who was on the ship was a carpenter. They came with the wood and brought the Roman man. Quraysh said: “We will build the House of our Lord with this wood.” When they wanted to demolish it, there was a snake upon the wall of the House, like a piece of a large beam, black-backed and white-bellied. Whenever anyone came near the House to demolish it or take any of its stones, it would rush at him with its mouth open. Quraysh gathered at the Sacred Precinct and cried out to Allah, saying: “Our Lord! We are not seeking harm. We only intend to honor Your House and set it in order. If You are pleased with that, then let it be; otherwise, do what You will.” They heard a cry in the sky, and there was a bird greater than an eagle, black-backed with white belly and legs. It dug its claws into the back of the snake’s neck, then flew away dragging it, its tail hanging down greatly, until it took it toward Ajyād. Then Quraysh demolished it and began building it with stones from the valley, which Quraysh carried on their necks. They raised it twenty cubits high. While the Prophet ﷺ was carrying stones from Ajyād and wearing a striped cloak, the cloak became tight on him, so he went to place it on his shoulder. Because of the smallness of the cloak, his nakedness appeared, so he was called: “O Muhammad! Cover your nakedness.” He was never seen naked after that. Between the rebuilding of the Kaʿbah and the revelation Allah sent down to him were five years, and between his departure from Makkah and its rebuilding were fifteen years."],[62,"Zayd ibn Ḥārithah said: I circumambulated with the Prophet ﷺ one day, and I touched some of the idols. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “Do not touch them.” I said: “I will do it again so that I may see what he says.” Then I touched them, and he said: “Were you not forbidden from this?” He said: By the One who honored him and sent down the Book upon him, he never touched any idol until Allah honored him and sent down the Book upon him."],[63,"ʿUrwah said: A neighbor of Khadījah bint Khuwaylid narrated to me that he heard the Prophet ﷺ saying to Khadījah: “O Khadījah, by Allah, I will never worship al-Lāt; by Allah, I will never worship al-ʿUzzā.” He said: Khadījah would say: “Leave al-Lāt; leave al-ʿUzzā.” He said: “Their idols which they used to worship, then lie down.”"],[64,"Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ before revelation was sent down to him, standing upon a camel of his at ʿArafāt with the people until he departed from it with them, by Allah’s guidance for him."],[65,"The Mother of the Believers ʿĀ’ishah said: Quraysh and those who followed their religious practice — and they were the ḥums — used to stand on the evening of ʿArafah at Muzdalifah, saying: “We are the dwellers of the House.” The rest of the people and the Arabs would stand at ʿArafāt. So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down: “Then depart from where the people depart, and seek forgiveness from Allah” [al-Baqarah: 199]. So they moved forward and stood with the people at ʿArafāt."],[66,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: A woman would circumambulate the House while naked and would say: “Who will lend me a garment for circumambulation?” She would place it over her private part and say: “Today some of it or all of it may appear, but whatever appears of it I do not permit.” Then this verse was revealed: “O children of Adam, take your adornment at every mosque.” [al-Aʿrāf: 31]"],[67,"Ibn Isḥāq, may Allah have mercy on him, said: The Jewish rabbis, the Christian monks and the soothsayers of the Arabs began to mention matters that belonged to the events before the sending of the Prophet ﷺ. The Christian monks and Jewish rabbis, who knew these proofs and were familiar with the signs, kept giving news of the sending of the Prophet ﷺ and informing of the coming of the message, which they found in their books. As for the soothsayers of the Arabs, they received these reports through the devils among the jinn, because the devils used to go up to the heavens and eavesdrop, and at that time the command to strike them with stars had not yet been issued. So they used to tell the Arabs before the prophetic mission of the Prophet ﷺ.\nThen when the time of the sending of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ drew near, the devils were prevented from listening, and a barrier came between them and the places where they used to sit to listen. They were struck with stars, and the jinn came to know that this was a matter that Allah had decreed among His servants concerning His Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, when Allah the Exalted sent him. He was relating to them the news of the jinn at the time when the jinn were deprived of listening; so they knew what they had not known, and when they saw it they did not deny it."],[68,"ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar said: I never heard ʿUmar say about anything, “I think it is such-and-such,” except that it was as he thought. While ʿUmar was sitting, a handsome man passed by him. He said: “My thought has failed — or else this man is still upon his religion from the Jāhiliyyah, or he was their soothsayer. Bring the man to me.” He was summoned, and ʿUmar said that to him. The man said: “I have never seen such a reception given to a Muslim man as today.” He said: “I insist that you tell me.” He said: “I was their soothsayer in the Jāhiliyyah.” He said: “What was the most amazing thing your female jinn brought to you?” He said: “One day, while I was in the market, she came to me and I recognized fear in her. She said: Have you not seen the jinn and their despair, their hopelessness after their reversal, and their joining the small she-camels and their saddle-cloths?” ʿUmar said: “He spoke the truth. While I was near their deities, a man came with a calf and slaughtered it. Then a caller shouted, and I never heard any caller with a louder voice than him, saying: O Jalīḥ, a successful matter, an eloquent man, saying: There is no god but You. The people leapt up. I said: I will not leave until I know what is behind this. Then he called again: O Jalīḥ, a successful matter, an eloquent man, saying: There is no god but Allah. I stood up, and soon it was said: This is a prophet.”"],[69,"Salamah ibn Salām ibn Waqsh — and Salamah was one of the people of Badr — said: We had a Jewish neighbor among Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal. He said: One day, shortly before the mission of the Prophet ﷺ, he came out to us from his house and stood before the gathering of Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal. Salamah said: At that time I was the youngest among them in age, wearing a cloak and lying in it in the courtyard of my family. He mentioned resurrection, the Day of Judgment, reckoning, the scales, Paradise, and Hellfire. He said that to a people of polytheism, idol worshipers, who did not believe that any resurrection would occur after death. They said to him: “Woe to you, so-and-so! Do you think this will happen — that people will be resurrected after their deaths to an abode containing Paradise and Hellfire, and that they will be recompensed there for their deeds?” He said: “Yes, by the One by whom oaths are sworn. He would wish that he had, in place of his share of that Fire, the greatest oven in the world heated for him, then that he be put into it and it be closed over him, and that he thereby be saved from that Fire tomorrow.” They said to him: “Woe to you, what is the sign of that?” He said: “A prophet will be sent from the direction of these lands,” and he gestured with his hand toward Makkah and Yemen. They said: “When do you think he will appear?” He looked at me, while I was among the youngest of them in age, and said: “If this boy lives out his natural lifespan, he will see him.” Salamah said: By Allah, not much time passed before Allah Most High sent His Messenger ﷺ while that man was still alive among us. We believed in him, but he disbelieved in him out of transgression and envy. We said: “Woe to you, so-and-so! Are you not the one who said to us about him what you said?” He said: “Yes, but it is not him.”"],[70,"Ibn ʿUmar said: Banū al-Naḍīr and Qurayẓah fought the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He expelled Banū al-Naḍīr and left Qurayẓah in place and showed them favor, until Qurayẓah fought. Then he killed their men and divided their women, children, and wealth among the Muslims, except that some of them joined the Prophet ﷺ; he granted them safety and they accepted Islam. He expelled all the Jews of Medina: Banū Qaynuqāʿ, who were the people of ʿAbdullah ibn Salām, the Jews of Banū Ḥārithah, and all the Jews of Medina."],[71,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was sent from the best generations of the children of Adam, generation after generation, until I was from the generation in which I was.”"],[72,"Jābir ibn Samurah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, I know a stone in Makkah that used to greet me before I was sent. Indeed, I know it now.”"],[73,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib said: I went out with the Prophet ﷺ, and he did not pass by any stone or tree except that it greeted him."],[74,"ʿAbdullah ibn al-Zubayr said to ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr ibn Qatādah al-Laythī: “Narrate to us, O ʿUbayd, how the beginning of what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began with of prophethood occurred, when Jibrīl, peace be upon him, came to him.” He said: ʿUbayd said — while I was present as he narrated to ʿAbdullah ibn al-Zubayr and those with him among the people —: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would spend a month in seclusion at Ḥirā’ every year. That was among the acts of taḥannuth that Quraysh practiced in the Jāhiliyyah; and taḥannuth means devotion and righteousness.\nʿUbayd said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would spend that month in seclusion every year and would feed any poor person who came to him. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ completed his seclusion for that month, the first thing he would begin with when he left his seclusion was the Kaʿbah before entering his house. He would circumambulate it seven times, or however much Allah willed of that, then return to his house."],[75,"Ibn ʿUmar narrated that Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl went out to al-Shām, asking about the religion and seeking to follow it. He met a Jewish scholar and asked him about their religion, saying: “Perhaps I will adopt your religion, so inform me.” He said: “You will not be upon our religion until you take your share of Allah’s anger.” Zayd said: “I flee only from Allah’s anger, and I will never bear anything of Allah’s anger. How could I bear it? Can you direct me to something else?” He said: “I do not know of anything except that you be a ḥanīf.” Zayd said: “What is a ḥanīf?” He said: “The religion of Ibrahim; he was neither Jewish nor Christian, and he worshiped none but Allah.” Zayd went out and met a Christian scholar. He mentioned something similar, and the scholar said: “You will not be upon our religion until you take your share of Allah’s curse.” He said: “I flee only from Allah’s curse, and I will never bear anything of Allah’s curse or His anger. How could I bear it? Can you direct me to something else?” He said: “I do not know of anything except that you be a ḥanīf.” He said: “What is a ḥanīf?” He said: “The religion of Ibrahim; he was neither Jewish nor Christian, and he worshiped none but Allah.” When Zayd saw what they said about Ibrahim, peace be upon him, he left. When he emerged into the open, he raised his hands and said: “O Allah, I bear witness that I am upon the religion of Ibrahim.”"],[76,"ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar narrated that the Prophet ﷺ met Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl at the lower part of Baldaḥ before revelation came down to the Prophet ﷺ. A meal was presented to the Prophet ﷺ, but he refused to eat from it. Then Zayd said: “I do not eat from what you slaughter upon your stone altars, and I eat only what Allah’s name has been mentioned over.” Zayd ibn ʿAmr used to criticize Quraysh for their slaughtered animals, saying: “The sheep was created by Allah; He sent down water from the sky for it and caused vegetation to grow for it from the earth. Then you slaughter it upon other than the name of Allah,” rejecting that and magnifying it."],[77,"Asmā’ bint Abī Bakr said: I saw Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl leaning his back against the Kaʿbah, saying: “O people of Quraysh, none of you today is upon the religion of Ibrahim, peace be upon him, besides me.” He used to pray in the Kaʿbah and say: “My Allah is the Allah of Ibrahim, and my religion is the religion of Ibrahim, peace be upon him.” He used to save girls who were to be buried alive. When a man wanted to kill his daughter, he would say: “Do not kill her. Give her to me, and I will bear her expenses for you.” When she grew up, he would say: “If you wish, take her now; and if you wish, leave her, and I will bear her expenses for you.” The Prophet ﷺ was asked about him, and he said: “He will be resurrected on the Day of Resurrection as a nation by himself.”"],[78,"Zayd ibn Ḥārithah said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he had me riding behind him, on a hot day from the days of Makkah. We had with us a sheep that we had slaughtered and prepared, and we placed it in a travel food container. Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl met him, and each of them greeted the other with the greeting of the Jāhiliyyah. The Prophet ﷺ said: “O Zayd” — meaning Zayd ibn ʿAmr — “why do I see that your people have become hostile toward you?” He said: “By Allah, O Muhammad, that is not because of any blood-feud I have with them. Rather, I went out seeking this religion until I came to the rabbis of Khaybar. I found them worshiping Allah while associating partners with Him, so I said: This is not the religion I seek. Then I went until I came to the rabbis of al-Shām. I found them worshiping Allah while associating partners with Him, so I said: This is not the religion I seek. Then one of them said: You are asking about a religion by which we know no one worships Allah except a shaykh in al-Jazīrah. So I went until I came to him. When he saw me, he said: All whom you have seen are in misguidance. Where are you from? I said: I am from the people of the House of Allah, from the people of thorn-trees and acacia. He said: What you seek has appeared in your land. A prophet has been sent; his star has risen. But I have not yet perceived anything, O Muhammad.” He said: The food container was brought near to him, and he said: “What is this?” He said: “A sheep that we slaughtered for one of these stone altars.” He said: “I would not eat anything slaughtered for other than Allah.” Then they parted. Zayd ibn Ḥārithah said: Then the Prophet ﷺ came to the House, and I was with him. He circumambulated it. Near the House there were two idols, one of copper; one was called Isāf and the other Nā’ilah. When the polytheists circumambulated, they would touch them. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not touch them, for they are filth.” He said: I said to myself: I will touch them so that I may see what he says. So I touched them, and he said: “O Zayd, were you not forbidden?” He said: Revelation was sent down upon the Prophet ﷺ. He said: Zayd ibn ʿAmr died, and the Prophet ﷺ said: “He will be resurrected as a nation by himself.”"],[79,"Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl said: I and ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb asked — meaning, we asked the Prophet ﷺ — about Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl. He said: “He will come on the Day of Resurrection as a nation by himself.”"],[80,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sent at the age of forty. He remained in Makkah for thirteen years while revelation came to him. Then he was commanded to migrate, so he migrated and lived ten years, and he died when he was sixty-three years old."],[81,"Allah the Exalted informed about ʿĪsā, peace be upon him: “ʿĪsā son of Maryam said: O Children of Israel! Indeed I am the Messenger of Allah to you, confirming what came before me of the Torah and giving good news of a messenger who will come after me, whose name is Aḥmad. Then when he came to them with clear signs, they said: This is plain magic.” (al-Ṣaff: 6)\nAnd the Exalted said: ﴿وَ يَقُوْلُ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْا لَسْتَ مُرْسَلًا١ؕ قُلْ كَفٰى بِاللّٰهِ شَهِيْدًۢا بَيْنِيْ وَ بَيْنَكُمْ١ۙ وَ مَنْ عِنْدَهٗ عِلْمُ الْكِتٰبِؒ۰۰۴۳﴾ [al-Raʿd: 43]\nAnd He said: “Those who disbelieve say: You are not a messenger. Say: Allah is sufficient as a witness between me and you, and so is the one who has knowledge of the Book.” (al-Raʿd: 43)\nAnd the Exalted said: ﴿كَذٰلِكَ يُوْحِيْۤ اِلَيْكَ وَ اِلَى الَّذِيْنَ مِنْ قَبْلِكَ١ۙ اللّٰهُ الْعَزِيْزُ الْحَكِيْمُ۰۰۳﴾ [al-Shūrā: 3]\nAnd He said: “Thus He reveals to you and to those before you, Allah, the Almighty, the All-Wise.” (al-Shūrā: 3)\nAnd the Exalted said: ﴿اِنَّاۤ اَوْحَيْنَاۤ اِلَيْكَ كَمَاۤ اَوْحَيْنَاۤ اِلٰى نُوْحٍ وَّ النَّبِيّٖنَ مِنْۢ بَعْدِهٖ١ۚ وَ اَوْحَيْنَاۤ اِلٰۤى اِبْرٰهِيْمَ وَ اِسْمٰعِيْلَ وَ اِسْحٰقَ وَ يَعْقُوْبَ وَ الْاَسْبَاطِ وَ عِيْسٰى وَ اَيُّوْبَ وَ يُوْنُسَ وَ هٰرُوْنَ وَ سُلَيْمٰنَ١ۚ وَ اٰتَيْنَا دَاوٗدَ زَبُوْرًاۚ۰۰۱۶۳﴾ [al-Nisā’: 163]\nAnd He said: “Indeed We have revealed to you as We revealed to Nūḥ and the prophets after him, and We revealed to Ibrāhīm, Ismāʿīl, Isḥāq, Yaʿqūb and the tribes, and ʿĪsā, Ayyūb, Yūnus, Hārūn and Sulaymān, and We gave Dāwūd the Zabūr.” (al-Nisā’: 163)\nAnd the Exalted said: ﴿اَلَّذِيْنَ اٰتَيْنٰهُمُ الْكِتٰبَ يَعْرِفُوْنَهٗ كَمَا يَعْرِفُوْنَ اَبْنَآءَهُمْ١ۘ﴾ [al-Baqarah: 146]\nAnd He said: “Those to whom We gave the Book recognize it as they recognize their own sons.” (al-Baqarah: 146)"],[82,"To study the glad tidings about the Prophet ﷺ given in the Torah, the Gospel and the books of the Hindus, see my book Dirāsāt fī al-Yahūdiyyah wa al-Masīḥiyyah wa Adyān al-Hind."],[83,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The first thing with which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began of revelation was true dreams in sleep. He would not see any dream except that it came like the break of dawn… the full hadith."],[84,"ʿĀ’ishah, the Mother of the Believers, said: The first thing with which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began of revelation was righteous dreams in sleep. He would not see any dream except that it came like the break of dawn. Then seclusion was made beloved to him. He would seclude himself in the Cave of Ḥirā’, practicing taḥannuth there — meaning worship — for a number of nights before returning to his family. He would take provisions for that, then return to Khadījah and take provisions for a similar period, until the truth suddenly came to him while he was in the Cave of Ḥirā’. The angel came to him and said: “Read.” He said: “I am not one who reads.” He said: He took me and pressed me until I reached the limit of my strength, then released me and said: “Read.” I said: “I am not one who reads.” He took me and pressed me a second time until I reached the limit of my strength, then released me and said: “Read.” I said: “I am not one who reads.” He took me and pressed me a third time until I reached the limit of my strength, then released me and said: “Read in the name of your Lord who created; created man from a clinging clot. Read, and your Lord is the Most Generous.” [al-ʿAlaq: 1–3] The Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned with it, his shoulders trembling, until he entered upon Khadījah and said: “Cover me, cover me.” They covered him until the fear left him. Then he said to Khadījah: “O Khadījah, what is wrong with me?” He informed her of what had happened and said: “I feared for myself.” Khadījah said to him: “No. Rejoice, for by Allah, Allah will never disgrace you. By Allah, you maintain ties of kinship, speak the truth, bear the burdens of others, provide for the needy, honor the guest, and help in the calamities of truth.” Khadījah then took him to Waraqah ibn Nawfal ibn Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, who was the cousin of Khadījah, the brother of her father. He was a man who had become Christian in the Jāhiliyyah. He used to write the Arabic script and write from the Gospel in Arabic whatever Allah willed him to write. He was an old man who had become blind. Khadījah said to him: “O uncle, listen to your nephew.” Waraqah ibn Nawfal said to him: “O nephew, what do you see?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed him of what he had seen. Waraqah said to him: “This is the Namus that was sent down upon Musa, peace be upon him. If only I were young then! If only I were alive when your people drive you out!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Will they drive me out?” Waraqah said: “Yes. No man has ever brought what you have brought except that he was opposed. If your day reaches me, I will support you with strong support.”"],[85,"Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr said: I asked Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān about the first thing revealed from the Qur’an, and he said: “O you who is wrapped up” [al-Muddaththir: 1]. I said: “They say: ‘Read in the name of your Lord who created.’” Abū Salamah said: I asked Jābir ibn ʿAbdullah about that and said to him what you said. Jābir said: I will only narrate to you what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ narrated to us. He said: “I secluded myself in Ḥirā’. When I completed my seclusion, I descended. Then I was called. I looked to my right and saw nothing; I looked to my left and saw nothing; I looked in front of me and saw nothing; I looked behind me and saw nothing. Then I raised my head and saw something. I came to Khadījah and said: Wrap me up, and pour cold water over me.” He said: They wrapped me up and poured cold water over me. He said: Then it was revealed: “O you who is wrapped up, arise and warn, and magnify your Lord.” [al-Muddaththir: 1–3]"],[86,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullah said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ narrating about the period in which revelation stopped. In his hadith he said: “While I was walking, I heard a voice from the sky. I raised my head, and there was the angel who had come to me at Ḥirā’, sitting on a chair between heaven and earth. I was terrified of him, so I returned and said: Cover me, cover me. They wrapped me up, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down: ‘O you who is wrapped up’ up to His saying: ‘And abandon idols’ [al-Muddaththir: 1–5] — before prayer was prescribed — and it refers to idols. Then revelation became intense and continued.”"],[87,"Jundub ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Sufyān said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became ill and did not stand for prayer for two or three nights. A woman came and said: “O Muhammad, I hope that your devil has left you; I have not seen him approach you for two or three nights.” So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down: “By the morning brightness, and by the night when it grows still, your Lord has not forsaken you, nor has He hated you.” [al-Ḍuḥā: 1–3]"],[88,"Ibn ʿAbbās said concerning the saying of Allah Most High: “Do not move your tongue with it to hasten with it” [al-Qiyāmah: 16]: The Prophet ﷺ used to experience hardship from the revelation, and he would move his lips. Saʿīd ibn Jubayr said: Ibn ʿAbbās said to me: “I will move them as the Prophet ﷺ used to move them.” Saʿīd said: “I will move them as Ibn ʿAbbās used to move them.” Then he moved his lips. So Allah Most High sent down: “Do not move your tongue with it to hasten with it. Indeed, upon Us is its collection and its recitation” [al-Qiyāmah: 16–17]. He said: Its collection in your chest, then that you recite it. “So when We recite it, follow its recitation” [al-Qiyāmah: 18]. He said: Listen and remain silent. Then it is upon Us that you recite it. So when Jibrīl came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he would listen, and when Jibrīl departed, the Prophet ﷺ would recite it as he had taught him."],[89,"Anas narrated that Allah Most High caused revelation to come successively to His Messenger ﷺ before his death until Allah took him, and revelation was at its greatest amount; then he died."],[90,"After the pause in revelation, this saying of the Lord was revealed:\n﴿يٰۤاَيُّهَا الْمُدَّثِّرُۙ۰۰ قُمْ فَاَنْذِرْ۪ۙ۰۰﴾ (al-Muddaththir: 1-2)\n“O you who are wrapped in your cloak! Arise and warn.”\n﴿وَ اَنْذِرْ عَشِيْرَتَكَ الْاَقْرَبِيْنَۙ۰۰﴾ (al-Shuʿarā’: 214)\n“And warn your closest relatives.”\nIbn Isḥāq says that after this, women and men began accepting Islam rapidly, until the call to Islam spread throughout Makkah and was talked about everywhere. Then Allah the Exalted commanded His Messenger to call openly. He was told to make the Lord’s message known to people publicly. The period of secret calling before his open calling was three years.\nDuring the period of secret calling, the Companions of the Messenger used to pray in hiding from their people; for prayer they would hide in a mountain pass outside the city. One day Sayyidunā Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, was praying in hiding with a few Muslims in a mountain pass of Makkah. The polytheists followed them, found them, and saw them praying in congregation. They heaped taunts and abuse on the Muslims, until it came to a fight. In self-defense Sayyidunā Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, picked up a camel’s bone and struck a polytheist with it, wounding him. This was the first blood shed because of Islam."],[91,"When this saying of Allah the Exalted was revealed: “So proclaim openly what you are commanded and turn away from the polytheists.” [al-Ḥijr: 94] And He said: “And warn your nearest relatives.” [al-Shuʿarā’: 214]"],[92,"Ibn Isḥāq, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Then people began entering Islam secretly, until the secret of Islam became known to everyone in Makkah and it began to be talked about. Then Allah the Exalted commanded the Prophet ﷺ to make known what had been given to him, to inform the people and to call them to (this religion). The period between the secret preaching of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the open preaching by Allah’s command was three years, as far as I have come to know. Then Allah the Exalted said: (al-Shuʿarā’ 214)"],[93,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: When this verse was revealed: “And warn your closest relatives” [al-Shuʿarā’: 214], and your sincere clan among them, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out until he climbed al-Ṣafā and called out: “O morning alarm!” They said: “Who is this calling out?” They said: “Muḥammad.” So they gathered to him. He said: “O Banū so-and-so! O Banū so-and-so! O Banū so-and-so! O Banū ʿAbd Manāf! O Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib!” They gathered to him, and he said: “Tell me, if I informed you that cavalry were coming out from the foot of this mountain, would you believe me?” They said: “We have never experienced any lie from you.” He said: “Then I am a warner to you before a severe punishment.” He said: Abū Lahab said: “May you perish! Did you gather us only for this?” Then he stood, and this surah was revealed: “Perish the two hands of Abū Lahab, and perish he” [al-Masad: 1]. Al-Aʿmash recited it this way to the end of the surah."],[94,"Abū Hurayrah said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had revealed to him: “And warn your closest relatives,” he said: “O people of Quraysh! Purchase yourselves from Allah; I cannot avail you against Allah at all. O Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib! I cannot avail you against Allah at all. O ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib! I cannot avail you against Allah at all. O Ṣafiyyah, aunt of the Messenger of Allah! I cannot avail you against Allah at all. O Fāṭimah, daughter of the Messenger of Allah! Ask me of my wealth whatever you wish; I cannot avail you against Allah at all.”"],[95,"Abū Hurayrah said: When this verse was revealed: “And warn your closest relatives,” the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called Quraysh, and they gathered. He addressed them generally and specifically, saying: “O Banū Kaʿb ibn Lu’ayy! Save yourselves from the Fire. O Banū Murrah ibn Kaʿb! Save yourselves from the Fire. O Banū ʿAbd Shams! Save yourselves from the Fire. O Banū ʿAbd Manāf! Save yourselves from the Fire. O Fāṭimah! Save yourself from the Fire, for I possess nothing for you against Allah, except that you have ties of kinship which I will maintain.”"],[96,"ʿĀ’ishah said: When “And warn your closest relatives” was revealed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood upon al-Ṣafā and said: “O Fāṭimah daughter of Muḥammad! O Ṣafiyyah daughter of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib! O Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib! I possess nothing for you against Allah. Ask me of my wealth whatever you wish.”"],[97,"Qabīṣah ibn al-Mukhāriq and Zuhayr ibn ʿAmr both said: When “And warn your closest relatives” was revealed, the Prophet of Allah ﷺ went to a rocky part of a mountain and climbed its highest stone. Then he called out: “O Banū ʿAbd Manāf! I am a warner. My example and your example is only like that of a man who saw the enemy, so he went to watch out for his people, but feared that they would reach them before he could, so he began calling out: O morning alarm!”"],[98,"ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿūd said: The first to openly display their Islam were seven: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr, ʿAmmār, his mother Sumayyah, Ṣuhayb, Bilāl, and al-Miqdād. As for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Allah protected him through his uncle Abū Ṭālib. As for Abū Bakr, Allah protected him through his people. As for the rest of them, the polytheists seized them, dressed them in iron armor, and left them to burn in the sun. There was not a single one of them except that he yielded to them in what they wanted, except Bilāl. He considered his own soul insignificant for the sake of Allah, and his people considered him insignificant, so they handed him over to the boys, and they dragged him around the mountain paths of Makkah while he was saying: “One, One.”"],[99,"ʿAmmār said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when none was with him except five slaves, two women, and Abū Bakr."],[100,"Five slaves: Zayd ibn Ḥārithah; ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah, freed by Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him; Abū Fukayhah, freed by Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him; and Shuqrān. It is also said that the fifth was ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir. Two women: one was the Prophet’s ﷺ noble wife Sayyidah Khadījah, may Allah be pleased with her, and the other was the Prophet’s ﷺ nurse Umm Ayman. It is also said that it was Umm ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, who was indeed among the first women martyred in Islam; Abū Jahl struck her and killed her. (al-Fatḥ 7/24)"],[101,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ said: I remember myself when I was one third of Islam."],[102,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ said: No one accepted Islam except on the day I accepted Islam; and I remained for seven days while I was one third of Islam."],[103,"Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ narrated from his father that verses of the Qur’an were revealed concerning him. He said: Sa‘d’s mother swore that she would never speak to him until he disbelieved in his religion, and that she would neither eat nor drink. She said: “You claim that Allah has instructed you regarding your parents, and I am your mother, and I command you with this.” He said: She remained for three days until she fainted from exhaustion. A son of hers named ʿUmārah stood and gave her drink. She began supplicating against Saʿd. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed in the Qur’an this verse: “But if they strive against you to make you associate with Me that of which you have no knowledge, then do not obey them,” and in it: “But accompany them in this world with kindness.” [Luqmān: 15]"],[104,"ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah al-Sulamī said: In the Jāhiliyyah, I used to think that the people were upon misguidance and that they were upon nothing while they worshiped idols. Then I heard of a man in Makkah who was reporting matters, so I mounted my riding animal and came to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in hiding, while his people were bold against him. I acted gently until I entered upon him in Makkah. I said to him: “What are you?” He said: “I am a prophet.” I said: “And what is a prophet?” He said: “Allah has sent me.” I said: “With what has He sent you?” He said: “He sent me with maintaining family ties, breaking idols, and that Allah be worshiped alone without anything being associated with Him.” I said to him: “Who is with you upon this?” He said: “A free man and a slave.” He said: With him that day were Abū Bakr and Bilāl among those who had believed in him. I said: “I will follow you.” He said: “You are not able to do that on this day of yours. Do you not see my condition and the condition of the people? Rather, return to your family, and when you hear that I have prevailed, then come to me.” He said: I went to my family, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to Medina while I was with my family. I began seeking news and asking people when he came to Medina, until a group from the people of Yathrib, the people of Medina, came to me. I said: “What happened to this man who came to Medina?” They said: “The people are rushing to him, and his people wanted to kill him but were unable to do so.” So I came to Medina and entered upon him. I said: “O Messenger of Allah, do you recognize me?” He said: “Yes, you are the one who met me in Makkah.” He said: I said: “Yes…”"],[105,"Abū Ḥamzah — a man from the Anṣār — said: I heard Zayd ibn Arqam saying: The first to accept Islam was ʿAlī. ʿAmr ibn Murrah said: I mentioned that to Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī, and he rejected it and said: The first to accept Islam was Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq."],[106,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: The first to pray was ʿAlī."],[107,"Ibn Isḥāq, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Some men from the chiefs of Quraysh went to Abū Ṭālib, among them the two sons of Rabīʿah, ʿUtbah and Shaybah, and Abū Sufyān and Abū Jahl. They said: O Abū Ṭālib, your nephew has insulted our gods, found fault with our religion, led our young men astray, and called our forefathers misguided. If you will not stop him, then leave him to us; and if you are against him as we are, we will suffice you against him. Abū Ṭālib reasoned with them in a very gentle manner and sent them back, until they went away. And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued giving his call in the same way. Then they came again to Abū Ṭālib and said: O Abū Ṭālib, you hold a great position and rank among us. We told you to restrain your nephew, but you did not stop him. By Allah, we will no longer be patient with the cursing of our forefathers, nor with the one who has led our young men astray and insulted our gods, until you yourself suffice us against him (that is, you stop him yourself), or hand him over to us and stand aside from him, until one of the two parties perishes, or as they said. Then they left. Abū Ṭālib found the separation from his people and their enmity distressing. For these reasons he neither believed in the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gladly, nor did he oppose him."],[108,"ʿAqīl ibn Abī Ṭālib said: Quraysh came to Abū Ṭālib and said: “Your nephew harms us in our gathering place and in our mosque, so stop him from harming us.” He said: “O ʿAqīl, bring Muḥammad to me.” I went and brought him to him. He said: “O my nephew, your cousins claim that you harm them in their gathering place and in their mosque, so stop doing that.” He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ lifted his gaze to the sky and said: “Do you see this sun?” They said: “Yes.” He said: “I am no more able to leave that for you than you are able to bring me a flame from it.” Abū Ṭālib said: “My nephew has not lied to us. Go back.”"],[109,"Anas narrated that the people of Makkah asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to show them a sign, so he showed them the splitting of the moon."],[110,"ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿūd said: The moon split during the time of the Prophet ﷺ and became two parts. He said to us: “Bear witness, bear witness.”"],[111,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: The people of Makkah asked the Prophet ﷺ to make al-Ṣafā gold for them and to remove the mountains from around them so that they could farm. It was said to him: “If you wish, you may delay and be patient with them; and if you wish, We will give them what they asked for, but if they disbelieve after that, they will be destroyed as those before them were destroyed.” He said: “No, rather I will be patient with them.” So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed this verse: “Nothing prevented Us from sending signs except that the former peoples denied them. And We gave Thamūd the she-camel as a visible sign.” [al-Isrā’: 59]"],[112,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: Quraysh said to the Prophet ﷺ: “Call upon your Lord for us to make al-Ṣafā gold for us, and we will believe in you.” He said: “Will you do so?” They said: “Yes.” He supplicated, and Jibrīl came to him and said: “Your Lord sends greetings of peace to you and says to you: If you wish, al-Ṣafā will become gold for them by morning, but whoever among them disbelieves after that, I will punish him with a punishment with which I have not punished anyone among the worlds. And if you wish, I will open for them the door of repentance and mercy.” He said: “Rather, the door of repentance and mercy.”"],[113,"ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿūd said: While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying near the House, and Abū Jahl and some of his companions were sitting, some of them said to others: “Which of you will bring the abdominal contents of a camel belonging to Banū so-and-so and place it on Muḥammad’s back when he prostrates?” The most wretched of the people went and brought it. He watched until the Prophet ﷺ prostrated, then placed it on his back between his shoulders. I was watching and could do nothing; if only I had strength. He said: They began laughing and leaning upon one another, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained prostrating and did not raise his head. Then he said: “O Allah, deal with Quraysh,” three times. This distressed them when he supplicated against them. He said: They used to believe that supplication in that land would be answered. Then he named them: “O Allah, deal with Abū Jahl; deal with ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah, Shaybah ibn Rabīʿah, al-Walīd ibn ʿUtbah, Umayyah ibn Khalaf, and ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ,” and he counted a seventh, but I did not remember him. He said: By the One in whose hand is my soul, I saw those whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counted lying dead in the well, the well of Badr."],[114,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr said: I said to ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ: “Tell me of the worst thing the polytheists did to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” He said: While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying in the courtyard of the Kaʿbah, ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ came, took the shoulder of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, twisted his garment around his neck, and strangled him severely. Abū Bakr came, took him by the shoulder, pushed him away from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and said: “Would you kill a man because he says, ‘My Lord is Allah,’ while he has brought you clear proofs from your Lord?” [Ghāfir: 28]"],[115,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated from ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ that he said: I said to him: “What was the worst you saw Quraysh inflict upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while they displayed hostility toward him?” He said: I was present with them one day when their nobles had gathered in the Ḥijr. They mentioned the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “We have never seen anything like what we have endured from this man. He has declared our minds foolish, insulted our fathers, criticized our religion, divided our unity, and reviled our gods. We have endured from him a grave matter,” or as they said. He said: While they were like that, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appeared before them. He came walking until he touched the Corner, then passed by them while circumambulating the House. When he passed by them, they made insulting remarks to him with some of what they were saying. He said: I recognized that in his face, then he continued. When he passed by them a second time, they made similar remarks, and I recognized that in his face, then he continued. Then he passed by them a third time, and they made similar remarks, so he said: “Do you hear, O people of Quraysh? By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, I have come to you with slaughter.” His words seized the people, until there was no man among them except that it was as though a bird had landed upon his head. Even the one who had been most severe against him before that began soothing him with the best words he could find, saying: “Go away, O Abū al-Qāsim. Go away rightly guided. By Allah, you were never ignorant.” He said: So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left. The next day, they gathered in the Ḥijr, and I was with them. Some of them said to others: “You mentioned what has reached you from him and what has reached him from you, until when he confronted you with what you dislike, you left him.” While they were like that, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appeared before them. They leapt toward him as one man, surrounded him, and said to him: “Are you the one who says such-and-such?” referring to what had reached them from him of criticism of their gods and religion. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Yes, I am the one who says that.” He said: I saw a man among them take hold of the gathered part of his cloak. Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq stood in front of him, saying while weeping: “Would you kill a man because he says, ‘My Lord is Allah’?” [Ghāfir: 28] Then they left him. That was the worst I ever saw Quraysh reach against him."],[116,"Anas said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was harmed for the sake of Allah when no one else was harmed, and I was frightened for the sake of Allah when no one else was frightened. Three days and nights passed over me, and neither I nor Bilāl had any food that a living creature with a liver could eat, except what could be hidden under Bilāl’s armpit.”"],[117,"Anas ibn Mālik said: They once beat the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until he lost consciousness. Abū Bakr stood and began calling out: “Woe to you! Would you kill a man because he says, ‘My Lord is Allah’?” [Ghāfir: 28] They said: “Who is this?” They said: “The mad son of Abū Quḥāfah.”"],[118,"Khabbāb said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was reclining on a cloak in the shade of the Kaʿbah, and we had met severe hardship from the polytheists. I said: “Will you not supplicate to Allah?” He sat up, his face red, and said: “Among those before you, a man would be combed with iron combs, stripping away the flesh or nerves beneath his bones, yet that would not turn him away from his religion. A saw would be placed on the parting of his head and he would be split in two, yet that would not turn him away from his religion. Allah will surely complete this matter until a rider travels from Ṣanʿā’ to Ḥaḍramawt fearing none but Allah.” Bayān added: “And the wolf regarding his sheep.”"],[119,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said to the Prophet ﷺ: “Did any day come upon you that was more severe than the day of Uḥud?” He said: “I met from your people what I met, and the severest thing I met from them was on the Day of al-ʿAqabah, when I presented myself to Ibn ʿAbd Yālīl ibn ʿAbd Kulāl, but he did not respond to what I wanted. I departed, distressed and not knowing where I was heading, and I did not regain awareness until I was at Qarn al-Thaʿālib. I raised my head, and there was a cloud shading me. I looked, and Jibrīl was in it. He called me and said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has heard what your people said to you and how they responded to you. He has sent to you the Angel of the Mountains so that you may command him concerning them with whatever you wish. He said: The Angel of the Mountains called me, greeted me with peace, then said: O Muḥammad, Allah has heard what your people said to you. I am the Angel of the Mountains, and your Lord has sent me to you so that you may command me with your command. What do you wish? If you wish, I will crush them between the two mountains. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: Rather, I hope that Allah will bring forth from their loins those who worship Allah alone and do not associate anything with Him.”"],[120,"Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rode a donkey with a Fadakī cloak beneath him and had Usāmah ibn Zayd riding behind him, going to visit Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah among Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj before the Battle of Badr. He said: He passed by a gathering in which was ʿAbdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl, and that was before ʿAbdullah ibn Ubayy accepted Islam. In the gathering were a mixture of Muslims, polytheist idol worshipers, Jews, and Muslims, and ʿAbdullah ibn Rawāḥah was in the gathering. When the dust of the animal covered the gathering, ʿAbdullah ibn Ubayy covered his nose with his cloak and said: “Do not raise dust over us.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ greeted them with peace, then stopped and dismounted. He called them to Allah and recited the Qur’an to them. ʿAbdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl said: “O man, there is nothing better than what you say if it is true, but do not annoy us with it in our gathering. Return to your camp, and whoever comes to you, then narrate to him.” ʿAbdullah ibn Rawāḥah said: “Rather, O Messenger of Allah, come to us with it in our gatherings, for we love that.” The Muslims, polytheists, and Jews began insulting one another until they almost fought. The Prophet ﷺ continued calming them until they became quiet. Then the Prophet ﷺ mounted his animal and went until he entered upon Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: “O Saʿd, did you not hear what Abū Ḥubāb said?” — meaning ʿAbdullah ibn Ubayy — “He said such-and-such.” Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah said: “O Messenger of Allah, pardon him and overlook him. By the One who sent down the Book upon you, Allah has brought the truth that He sent down upon you. The people of this settlement had agreed to crown him and bind the headband upon him. When Allah refused that by the truth He gave you, he was choked by that, and that is why he did what you saw.” So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pardoned him. The Prophet ﷺ and his Companions used to pardon the polytheists and the People of the Book as Allah commanded them, and they remained patient over harm. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: “You will surely hear much harm from those who were given the Book before you and from those who associate partners” [Āl ʿImrān: 186]. And Allah said: “Many of the People of the Book wish they could turn you back to disbelief after your faith, out of envy from themselves” [al-Baqarah: 109] to the end of the verse. The Prophet ﷺ would interpret the command as pardon, as Allah had commanded him, until Allah gave permission concerning them. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fought at Badr and Allah killed through it the chiefs of the disbelievers of Quraysh, Ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl and those with him from the polytheists and idol worshipers said: “This matter has now become established.” So they pledged allegiance to the Messenger ﷺ upon Islam, and they accepted Islam."],[121,"Ṭāriq ibn ʿAbdullah al-Muḥāribī said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the market of Dhū al-Majāz, wearing a red suit of garments. I heard him saying: “O people, say: There is no god but Allah, and you will succeed.” A man was following him, throwing stones at him, having made his heels and ankles bleed, saying: “O people, do not obey this man, for he is a liar.” I said: “Who is this?” It was said: “This is the young man of Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.” I said: “And who is this one following him and throwing stones at him?” He said: “This is ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, Abū Lahab.” He said: When Islam became manifest, we went out in that direction until we settled near Medina, and with us was a woman traveling in a litter. While we were sitting, a man came to us wearing two garments. He greeted us with peace and said: “From where have the people come?” We said: “From al-Rabadhah.” He said: We had a camel with us. He said: “Will you sell this camel?” We said: “Yes.” He said: “For how much?” We said: “For such-and-such ṣāʿs of dates.” He took it and did not bargain us down. He said: “I have taken it.” Then he disappeared among the walls of Medina. We began blaming one another, saying: “You gave your camel to a man you do not know.” The woman in the litter said: “Do not blame one another. I saw the face of a man who would not wrong you. I have never seen anything more resembling the full moon than his face.” He said: When evening came, a man came to us, greeted us with peace, and said: “I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He says: You may eat until you are full and measure out until you receive your full due.” He said: So we ate until we were full and measured out until we had received our due. He said: Then we came to Medina the next day, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was standing, delivering a sermon on the pulpit. He was saying: “The giving hand is the upper hand. Begin with those you support: your mother, your father, your sister, your brother, then the nearest and the nearest.” A man stood and said: “O Messenger of Allah, these Banū Thaʿlabah ibn Yarbuʿ killed so-and-so in the Jāhiliyyah, so take vengeance for us from him.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his hands until I saw the whiteness of his armpits and said: “Indeed, a mother does not bear guilt for her child. Indeed, a mother does not bear guilt for her child.”"],[122,"Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Do you not marvel at how Allah turns away from me the insults and curses of Quraysh? They insult Mudhammam and curse Mudhammam, while I am Muḥammad.”"],[123,"Among these accusations:\n§1- The madman:\n1: Madman\n﴿وَ يَقُوْلُوْنَ اِنَّهٗ لَمَجْنُوْنٌۘ۰۰﴾ [al-Qalam: 51]\nAnd they say: “Indeed he is surely a madman.” [al-Qalam: 51]\n﴿ثُمَّ تَوَلَّوْا عَنْهُ وَ قَالُوْا مُعَلَّمٌ مَّجْنُوْنٌۘ۰۰﴾ [al-Dukhān: 14]\nThen they turned away from him and said: “He is a taught one, a madman.” [al-Dukhān: 14]\n﴿وَ قَالُوْا يٰۤاَيُّهَا الَّذِيْ نُزِّلَ عَلَيْهِ الذِّكْرُ اِنَّكَ لَمَجْنُوْنٌؕ۰۰﴾ [al-Ḥijr: 6]\nAnd they said: “O you upon whom the reminder has been sent down! Indeed you are surely a madman.” [al-Ḥijr: 6]\n﴿وَ اِنْ يَّكَادُ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْا لَيُزْلِقُوْنَكَ۠ بِاَبْصَارِهِمْ لَمَّا سَمِعُوا الذِّكْرَ وَ يَقُوْلُوْنَ اِنَّهٗ لَمَجْنُوْنٌۘ۰۰﴾ [al-Qalam: 51]\nAnd those who disbelieve almost make you slip with their glares when they hear the reminder, and they say: “Indeed he is surely a madman.” [al-Qalam: 51]"],[124,"Allah’s reply to this:\n﴿مَاۤ اَنْتَ بِنِعْمَةِ رَبِّكَ بِمَجْنُوْنٍۚ۰۰۲﴾ [al-Qalam: 2]\nBy the grace of your Lord, you are not a madman. [al-Qalam: 2]\nAnd the Exalted said: ﴿وَ مَا صَاحِبُكُمْ بِمَجْنُوْنٍۚ۰۰۲۲﴾ [al-Takwīr: 22]\nAnd Allah the Exalted said: Your companion is not at all a madman. [al-Takwīr: 22]\n§2- Soothsayer: They accused the Prophet ﷺ of being a soothsayer; Allah replied to them with His saying:\n§2: Soothsayer. The accusation of being a soothsayer was made against the Prophet ﷺ. Allah the Exalted refuted them, saying:\n﴿فَذَكِّرْ فَمَاۤ اَنْتَ بِنِعْمَتِ رَبِّكَ بِكَاهِنٍ وَّ لَا مَجْنُوْنٍؕ۰۰۲۹﴾ [al-Ṭūr: 29]\nSo remind, for by the favor of your Lord you are not at all a soothsayer, nor a madman. [al-Ṭūr: 29]\nAnd He said: ﴿وَ لَا بِقَوْلِ كَاهِنٍ١ؕ قَلِيْلًا مَّا تَذَكَّرُوْنَؕ۰۰۴۲﴾ [al-Ḥāqqah: 42]\nAnd He said: Nor is it the word of a soothsayer; little do you remember. [al-Ḥāqqah: 42]\n§3- Poet:\n§3: Poet\n﴿وَ يَقُوْلُوْنَ اَىِٕنَّا لَتَارِكُوْۤا اٰلِهَتِنَا لِشَاعِرٍ مَّجْنُوْنٍؕ۰۰۳۶﴾ [al-Ṣāffāt: 36]\nAnd they used to say: “Are we really going to abandon our gods for a mad poet?” [al-Ṣāffāt: 36]"],[125,"Allah refuted them with this saying:\n﴿وَّ مَا هُوَ بِقَوْلِ شَاعِرٍ١ؕ قَلِيْلًا مَّا تُؤْمِنُوْنَۙ۰۰۴۱﴾ [al-Ḥāqqah: 41]\nAnd it is not the word of a poet; little do you believe. [al-Ḥāqqah: 41]\n﴿اَمْ يَقُوْلُوْنَ شَاعِرٌ نَّتَرَبَّصُ بِهٖ رَيْبَ الْمَنُوْنِۙ۰۰۳۰﴾ [al-Ṭūr: 30]\nOr do they say: “He is a poet for whom we await the misfortunes of time”? [al-Ṭūr: 30]\n§4- Sorcerer:\n§4: Magician\n﴿قَالَ الْكٰفِرُوْنَ اِنَّ هٰذَا لَسٰحِرٌ مُّبِيْنٌ۰۰۲﴾ [Yūnus: 2]\nThe disbelievers said: “Indeed this is surely a clear magician.” [Yūnus: 2]\n﴿كَذٰلِكَ مَاۤ اَتَى الَّذِيْنَ مِنْ قَبْلِهِمْ مِّنْ رَّسُوْلٍ اِلَّا قَالُوْا سَاحِرٌ اَوْ مَجْنُوْنٌۚ۰۰۵۲﴾ [al-Dhāriyāt: 52]\nThus no messenger came to those before them except that they said: “A magician, or a madman.” [al-Dhāriyāt: 52]\n﴿وَ عَجِبُوْۤا اَنْ جَآءَهُمْ مُّنْذِرٌ مِّنْهُمْ١ٞ وَ قَالَ الْكٰفِرُوْنَ هٰذَا سٰحِرٌ كَذَّابٌۖۚ۰۰۴﴾ [Ṣād: 4]\nAnd they wondered that a warner from among themselves had come to them, and the disbelievers said: “This is a magician, a great liar.” [Ṣād: 4]\n﴿اِنْ هٰذَاۤ اِلَّا سِحْرٌ يُّؤْثَرُۙ۰۰۲۴﴾ [al-Muddaththir: 24]\n“This is nothing but magic that is handed down.” [al-Muddaththir: 24]\n§5- They said: A man teaches him:\n§5: Some man teaches him\n﴿وَ لَقَدْ نَعْلَمُ اَنَّهُمْ يَقُوْلُوْنَ اِنَّمَا يُعَلِّمُهٗ بَشَرٌ١ؕ لِسَانُ الَّذِيْ يُلْحِدُوْنَ اِلَيْهِ اَعْجَمِيٌّ وَّ هٰذَا لِسَانٌ عَرَبِيٌّ مُّبِيْنٌ۰۰۱۰۳﴾ [al-Naḥl: 103]\nAnd indeed We know that they say: “It is only a man who teaches him.” The tongue of the one to whom they allude is foreign, while this is a clear Arabic tongue. [al-Naḥl: 103]\nSayyidunā ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, said regarding the saying of Allah the Exalted: ﴿اِنَّمَا يُعَلِّمُهٗ بَشَرٌ١ؕ لِسَانُ الَّذِيْ يُلْحِدُوْنَ اِلَيْهِ اَعْجَمِيٌّ وَّ هٰذَا لِسَانٌ عَرَبِيٌّ مُّبِيْنٌ۰۰۱۰۳﴾ [al-Naḥl: 103] “It is only a man who teaches him. The tongue of the one to whom they allude is foreign, while this is a clear Arabic tongue”: These people said that Muhammad ﷺ is taught by a slave of Ibn al-Ḥaḍramī, who is a man of the Book. So Allah the Exalted said: ﴿لِسَانُ الَّذِيْ يُلْحِدُوْنَ اِلَيْهِ اَعْجَمِيٌّ وَّ هٰذَا لِسَانٌ عَرَبِيٌّ مُّبِيْنٌ۰۰۱۰۳ اِنَّمَا يَفْتَرِي الْكَذِبَ الَّذِيْنَ لَا يُؤْمِنُوْنَ بِاٰيٰتِ اللّٰهِ١ۚ﴾ [Sūrat al-Naḥl: 103, 105] The tongue of the man they allude to is foreign, and the language of this Qur’an is clear Arabic (103). Only those who do not believe in the signs of Allah fabricate falsehood, and it is they who are the liars (105)."],[126,"Allah the Exalted said: ﴿وَ لَقَدِ اسْتُهْزِئَ بِرُسُلٍ مِّنْ قَبْلِكَ فَحَاقَ بِالَّذِيْنَ سَخِرُوْا مِنْهُمْ مَّا كَانُوْا بِهٖ يَسْتَهْزِءُوْنَ۠ؒ۰۰۱۰﴾ [al-Anʿām: 10]\nAllah the Exalted said: “And indeed messengers before you were mocked, but those who scoffed at them were overtaken by the very thing they used to mock.” [al-Anʿām: 10]\nAnd the Exalted said: ﴿وَ اِذَا رَاَوْكَ اِنْ يَّتَّخِذُوْنَكَ۠ اِلَّا هُزُوًا١ؕ اَهٰذَا الَّذِيْ بَعَثَ اللّٰهُ رَسُوْلًا۰۰۴۱﴾ [al-Furqān: 41]\nAnd Allah the Exalted said: “And when they see you, they take you only as a mockery: ‘Is this the one whom Allah has sent as a messenger?’” [al-Furqān: 41]"],[127,"Ibn Isḥāq, may Allah have mercy on him, said: The greatest of the mockers, as Yazīd ibn Rūmān related to me from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, were five men who were the elders and chiefs of their people: al-Aswad ibn al-Muṭṭalib, al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth, al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah, al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wā’il, and al-Ḥārith ibn Ṭulāṭilah."],[128,"Ibn ʿAbbās said regarding the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: “Indeed, We are sufficient for you against the mockers” [al-Ḥijr: 95]: The mockers were al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah, al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth al-Zuhrī, al-Aswad ibn al-Muṭṭalib Abū Zamʿah from Banū Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, al-Ḥārith ibn al-Ṭalāṭilah al-Sahmī, and al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wā’il. Jibrīl, peace be upon him, came to him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ complained to him about them. He showed him al-Walīd, Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Mughīrah, and Jibrīl, peace be upon him, pointed to his vein, and said: “What have you done?” He said: “I have sufficed you against him.” Then he showed him al-Aswad ibn al-Muṭṭalib, and Jibrīl pointed to his eyes and said: “What have you done?” He said: “I have sufficed you against him.” Then he showed him al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth al-Zuhrī, and he pointed to his head and said: “What have you done?” He said: “I have sufficed you against him.” Then he showed him al-Ḥārith ibn al-Ṭalāṭilah al-Sahmī, and he pointed to his head — or he said: to his belly — and said: “What have you done?” He said: “I have sufficed you against him.” Then al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wā’il passed by him, and he pointed to the hollow of his foot and said: “What have you done?” He said: “I have sufficed you against him.” As for al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah, he passed by a man from Khuzāʿah who was feathering arrows, and one struck his vein and severed it. As for al-Aswad ibn al-Muṭṭalib, he became blind. Some say he became blind in this way, and others say that he sat beneath an acacia tree and began saying: “O my sons, will you not defend me? I have been killed.” They kept saying: “We do not see anything.” He kept saying: “O my sons, will you not protect me? I have perished; here I am being stabbed with thorns in my eyes.” They kept saying: “We do not see anything,” and he remained like that until both his eyes became blind. As for al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth al-Zuhrī, sores appeared on his head and he died from them. As for al-Ḥārith ibn al-Ṭalāṭilah, yellow water afflicted his stomach until it came out of his mouth, and he died from it. As for al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wā’il, one day a thorny plant entered his head until it filled it, and he died from it. Others said in this report that he rode to al-Ṭā’if on a donkey, and it knelt upon a thorny plant, so a thorn entered the hollow of his foot and killed him."],[129,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ said: We were with the Prophet ﷺ, six people, and the polytheists said to the Prophet ﷺ: “Drive these people away so that they will not be bold toward us.” He said: I was among them, along with Ibn Masʿūd, a man from Hudhayl, Bilāl, and two men whom I will not name. Something occurred in the mind of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as Allah willed, and he thought to himself, so Allah revealed: “And do not drive away those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, seeking His Face. You are not accountable for them in anything” [al-Anʿām: 52]."],[130,"Masrūq said: We were sitting with ʿAbdullah while he was reclining among us. A man came to him and said: “O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, there is a storyteller at the gates of Kindah telling stories and claiming that the sign of the smoke will come and seize the breath of the disbelievers, while it will affect the believers like a cold.” ʿAbdullah sat up angrily and said: “O people! Fear Allah. Whoever among you knows something, let him speak according to what he knows; and whoever does not know, let him say: Allah knows best. For it is more knowledgeable for one of you, regarding what he does not know, to say: Allah knows best. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said to His Prophet ﷺ: ‘Say: I do not ask you for any reward for it, and I am not one of those who pretend’ [Ṣād: 86]. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw people turning away, he said: ‘O Allah, seven years like the seven of Yusuf.’ He said: A drought seized them and wiped out everything, until they ate hides and carrion from hunger. One of them would look toward the sky and see something like smoke. Abū Sufyān came to him and said: ‘O Muḥammad, you came commanding obedience to Allah and maintaining family ties, and your people have perished, so supplicate to Allah for them.’ Allah Most High said: ‘So watch for the Day when the sky will bring a clear smoke, covering the people. This is a painful punishment’ [al-Dukhān: 10–11], until His saying: ‘Indeed, you will return’ [al-Dukhān: 15]. He said: ‘Will the punishment of the Hereafter be removed?’ ‘The Day We will seize with the greatest seizure; indeed, We will take revenge’ [al-Dukhān: 16]. The seizure was the Day of Badr. The sign of the smoke, the seizure, the binding punishment, and the sign of the Romans have already passed.”"],[131,"Ibn Masʿūd said: Five have already passed: the binding punishment, the Romans, the seizure, the moon, and the smoke."],[132,"§1: You are a bearer of glad tidings and a warner.\n﴿اِنَّاۤ اَرْسَلْنٰكَ بِالْحَقِّ بَشِيْرًا وَّ نَذِيْرًا١ۙ وَّ لَا تُسْـَٔلُ عَنْ اَصْحٰبِ الْجَحِيْمِ۰۰۱۱۹﴾ [البقرة: ١١٩]\nWe have sent you with the truth as a bearer of glad tidings and a warner, and you will not be asked about the companions of the Hellfire. [al-Baqarah: 119]\n﴿قُلْ اِنَّمَاۤ اَنَا مُنْذِرٌ١ۖۗ وَّ مَا مِنْ اِلٰهٍ اِلَّا اللّٰهُ الْوَاحِدُ الْقَهَّارُۚ۰۰۶۵﴾ [ص:65]\nSay: “I am only a warner, and there is no god but Allah, the One, the Overpowering.” [Ṣād: 65]\n﴿اِنَّنِيْ لَكُمْ مِّنْهُ نَذِيْرٌ وَّ بَشِيْرٌۙ۰۰۲﴾ [هود: ٢]\n“I am from Him a warner and a bearer of glad tidings to you.” [Hūd: 2]\n﴿اِنَّمَاۤ اَنْتَ مُنْذِرٌ وَّ لِكُلِّ قَوْمٍ هَادٍؒ۰۰۷﴾ [الرعد: ٧]\nYou are only a warner, and for every people there is a guide. [al-Raʿd: 7]\n﴿يٰۤاَيُّهَا النَّبِيُّ اِنَّاۤ اَرْسَلْنٰكَ شَاهِدًا وَّ مُبَشِّرًا وَّ نَذِيْرًاۙ۰۰۴۵﴾ [الأحزاب: ٤٥]\nO Prophet! We have sent you as a witness, a bearer of glad tidings and a warner. [al-Aḥzāb: 45]\n§٢- أمر بتبليغ ما أنزل الله:\n§2: The command to convey what Allah has revealed\n﴿يٰۤاَيُّهَا الرَّسُوْلُ بَلِّغْ مَاۤ اُنْزِلَ اِلَيْكَ مِنْ رَّبِّكَ١ؕ وَ اِنْ لَّمْ تَفْعَلْ فَمَا بَلَّغْتَ رِسَالَتَهٗ١ؕ﴾ [المائدة: ٦٧]\nO Messenger! Convey what has been revealed to you from your Lord, and if you do not, then you have not conveyed His message. [al-Mā’idah: 67]\n§٣- ليس للنبي أن يبدّل شيئاً من القرآن:\n§3: It is not for the Prophet ﷺ to change anything of the Qur’an\n﴿وَ اِذَا تُتْلٰى عَلَيْهِمْ اٰيَاتُنَا بَيِّنٰتٍ١ۙ قَالَ الَّذِيْنَ لَا يَرْجُوْنَ لِقَآءَنَا ائْتِ بِقُرْاٰنٍ غَيْرِ هٰذَاۤ اَوْ بَدِّلْهُ١ؕ قُلْ مَا يَكُوْنُ لِيْۤ اَنْ اُبَدِّلَهٗ مِنْ تِلْقَآئِ نَفْسِيْ١ۚ اِنْ اَتَّبِعُ اِلَّا مَا يُوْحٰۤى اِلَيَّ١ۚ اِنِّيْۤ اَخَافُ اِنْ عَصَيْتُ رَبِّيْ عَذَابَ يَوْمٍ عَظِيْمٍ۰۰۱۵﴾ [يونس: ١٥]\nAnd when Our clear verses are recited to them, those who do not hope to meet Us say: “Bring a Qur’an other than this, or change it.” Say: “It is not for me to change it of my own accord. I only follow what is revealed to me. Indeed I fear, if I disobey my Lord, the punishment of a mighty Day.” [Yūnus: 15]\n§٤- أمر النبي صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم بالمباهلة من خالفه من المشركين:\n§4: The Prophet ﷺ was commanded to invoke mutual imprecation (mubāhalah) against the polytheists who opposed him\n﴿فَمَنْ حَآجَّكَ فِيْهِ مِنْۢ بَعْدِ مَا جَآءَكَ مِنَ الْعِلْمِ فَقُلْ تَعَالَوْا نَدْعُ اَبْنَآءَنَا وَ اَبْنَآءَكُمْ وَ نِسَآءَنَا وَ نِسَآءَكُمْ وَ اَنْفُسَنَا وَ اَنْفُسَكُمْ١۫ ثُمَّ نَبْتَهِلْ فَنَجْعَلْ لَّعْنَتَ اللّٰهِ عَلَى الْكٰذِبِيْنَ۰۰۶۱﴾ [آل عمران: ٦١]\nThen whoever disputes with you about him after the knowledge has come to you, say: “Come, let us gather our sons and your sons, our women and your women, ourselves and yourselves, then let us earnestly pray and invoke the curse of Allah upon the liars.” [Āl ʿImrān: 61]\n§٥- إنه صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم لا ينطق إلا ما يوحى إليه:\n§5: The Prophet ﷺ does not speak except by revelation\n﴿وَ مَا يَنْطِقُ عَنِ الْهَوٰى ؕ۰۰۳ اِنْ هُوَ اِلَّا وَحْيٌ يُّوْحٰى ۙ۰۰۴ عَلَّمَهٗ شَدِيْدُ الْقُوٰى ۙ۰۰۵﴾ [النجم: ٣ - ٥]\nNor does he speak out of his own desire. It is only a revelation revealed to him. He was taught by one mighty in power (the angel Jibrīl). [al-Najm: 3-5]\n§٦- إن النبي صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم يهدي إلى صراط مستقيم:\n§6: The Prophet ﷺ guides to the straight path\n﴿وَ اِنَّكَ لَتَهْدِيْۤ اِلٰى صِرَاطٍ مُّسْتَقِيْمٍۙ۰۰۵۲﴾ [الشورى: ٥٢]\nAnd indeed you guide people to a straight path. [al-Shūrā: 52]\n§٧- النبي صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم كان ينذر بالوحي:\n§7: The Prophet ﷺ warned by means of revelation\n﴿قُلْ اِنَّمَاۤ اُنْذِرُكُمْ بِالْوَحْيِ١ۖٞ وَ لَا يَسْمَعُ الصُّمُّ الدُّعَآءَ اِذَا مَا يُنْذَرُوْنَ۰۰۴۵﴾ [الأنبياء: ٤٥]\nSay, O My Messenger, to the disbelievers: “I warn you only by the revelation of Allah.” But the deaf do not hear the call when they are warned. [al-Anbiyā’: 45]\n§٨- حرص النبي صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم على إيمان قومه:\n§8: The Prophet’s ﷺ eagerness for his people to believe\n﴿فَلَعَلَّكَ بَاخِعٌ نَّفْسَكَ عَلٰۤى اٰثَارِهِمْ اِنْ لَّمْ يُؤْمِنُوْا بِهٰذَا الْحَدِيْثِ اَسَفًا۰۰۶﴾ [الكهف: ٦]\nThen perhaps you would destroy yourself with grief over their footsteps if they do not believe in this message. [al-Kahf: 6]\n§٩- سألوه عن علم الساعة:\n§9: He ﷺ was asked about the Hour\n﴿قُلْ اِنَّمَا عِلْمُهَا عِنْدَ اللّٰهِ١ؕ﴾ [الأحزاب: ٦٣]\nSay: “Its knowledge is only with Allah.” [al-Aḥzāb: 63]\n§١٠- إن اليهود والنصارى لا يرضون حتى تتبع ملتهم\n§10: The Jews and Christians will never be pleased with the Prophet ﷺ until he follows their way\n﴿وَ لَنْ تَرْضٰى عَنْكَ الْيَهُوْدُ وَ لَا النَّصٰرٰى حَتّٰى تَتَّبِعَ مِلَّتَهُمْ١ؕ قُلْ اِنَّ هُدَى اللّٰهِ هُوَ الْهُدٰى١ؕ﴾ [البقرة: ١٢٠]\nAnd the Jews and the Christians will never be pleased with you until you follow their religion. Say: “The guidance of Allah is the only guidance.” [al-Baqarah: 120]\n§١١- أهل الكتاب يعرفونه أنه رسول الله\n§11: The People of the Book know that he ﷺ is the Messenger of Allah\n﴿اَلَّذِيْنَ اٰتَيْنٰهُمُ الْكِتٰبَ يَعْرِفُوْنَهٗ كَمَا يَعْرِفُوْنَ اَبْنَآءَهُمْ١ؕ﴾ [البقرة:١٤٦]\nThose to whom We have given the Book recognize him (the Messenger of Allah ﷺ) just as they recognize their own sons. [al-Baqarah: 146]\n§١٢- النبي لا يعلم علم الغيب إلا ما علمه الله تعالى\n§12: The Prophet ﷺ does not know the unseen except what Allah the Exalted informs him of\n﴿وَ لَوْ كُنْتُ اَعْلَمُ الْغَيْبَ لَا سْتَكْثَرْتُ مِنَ الْخَيْرِ١ۛۖۚ وَ مَا مَسَّنِيَ السُّوْٓءُ١ۛۚ اِنْ اَنَا اِلَّا نَذِيْرٌ وَّ بَشِيْرٌ لِّقَوْمٍ يُّؤْمِنُوْنَؒ۰۰۱۸۸﴾ [الأعراف: ١٨٨]\nAnd if I had knowledge of the unseen, I would have acquired much good, and no harm would have touched me. I am only a warner and a bearer of glad tidings to a people who believe, warning them of Hell and giving glad tidings of Paradise. [al-Aʿrāf: 188]\n§١٣- المفلحون من اليهود والنصارى الذين يتبعون الرسول النبي الأمي.\n§13: Among the Jews and Christians, the successful will be those who follow the Messenger, the unlettered Prophet ﷺ\n﴿اَلَّذِيْنَ يَتَّبِعُوْنَ الرَّسُوْلَ النَّبِيَّ الْاُمِّيَّ الَّذِيْ يَجِدُوْنَهٗ مَكْتُوْبًا عِنْدَهُمْ فِي التَّوْرٰىةِ وَ الْاِنْجِيْلِ١ٞيَاْمُرُهُمْ بِالْمَعْرُوْفِ وَ يَنْهٰىهُمْ عَنِ الْمُنْكَرِ وَ يُحِلُّ لَهُمُ الطَّيِّبٰتِ وَ يُحَرِّمُ عَلَيْهِمُ الْخَبٰٓىِٕثَ وَ يَضَعُ عَنْهُمْ اِصْرَهُمْ وَ الْاَغْلٰلَ الَّتِيْ كَانَتْ عَلَيْهِمْ١ؕ فَالَّذِيْنَ اٰمَنُوْا بِهٖ وَ عَزَّرُوْهُ وَ نَصَرُوْهُ وَ اتَّبَعُوا النُّوْرَ الَّذِيْۤ اُنْزِلَ مَعَهٗۤ١ۙ اُولٰٓىِٕكَ هُمُ الْمُفْلِحُوْنَؒ۰۰۱۵۷﴾ [الأعراف: ١٥٧]\nFor those who follow our Messenger, the unlettered Prophet, whom they find written about in their Torah and Gospel, who commands them to what is right and forbids them from what is wrong, makes lawful for them the good things and prohibits for them the filthy things, and relieves them of their burden and the shackles that were upon them. So those who believe in him, honor him, help him and follow the light that was sent down with him, it is they who are the successful. [al-Aʿrāf: 157]\n§١٤- من وظائف الرسول تعليم الكتاب والحكمة وتزكيتهم\n§14: Among the duties of the Messenger ﷺ are teaching people the Book and the Sunnah and purifying them\n﴿هُوَ الَّذِيْ بَعَثَ فِي الْاُمِّيّٖنَ رَسُوْلًا مِّنْهُمْ يَتْلُوْا عَلَيْهِمْ اٰيٰتِهٖ وَ يُزَكِّيْهِمْ وَ يُعَلِّمُهُمُ الْكِتٰبَ وَ الْحِكْمَةَ١ۗ وَ اِنْ كَانُوْا مِنْ قَبْلُ لَفِيْ ضَلٰلٍ مُّبِيْنٍۙ۰۰۲﴾ [الجمعة:2]\nIt is He who sent among the unlettered people a messenger from among themselves, reciting to them His verses, purifying them (from the filth of disbelief and polytheism), and teaching them the Qur’an and the Sunnah, although they were before that in clear misguidance. [al-Jumuʿah: 2]\n§١٥- النبي لم يكن يعرف القراءة والكتابة\n§15: The Prophet ﷺ did not know how to read and write\n﴿وَ مَا كُنْتَ تَتْلُوْا مِنْ قَبْلِهٖ مِنْ كِتٰبٍ وَّ لَا تَخُطُّهٗ بِيَمِيْنِكَ اِذًا لَّارْتَابَ الْمُبْطِلُوْنَ۰۰۴۸﴾ [العنكبوت: ٤٨]\nYou did not recite any book before this, nor did you write one with your right hand; otherwise those who follow falsehood would have had cause for doubt. [al-ʿAnkabūt: 48]\n§١٦- اتباع النبي صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم من محبة الله:\n§16: Following the Prophet ﷺ is proof of love for Allah\n﴿قُلْ اِنْ كُنْتُمْ تُحِبُّوْنَ اللّٰهَ فَاتَّبِعُوْنِيْ۠ يُحْبِبْكُمُ اللّٰهُ وَ يَغْفِرْ لَكُمْ ذُنُوْبَكُمْ١ؕ وَ اللّٰهُ غَفُوْرٌ رَّحِيْمٌ۰۰۳۱﴾ [آل عمران: ٣١]\nSay: “If you love Allah, then follow me, and Allah will love you and forgive you your sins. And Allah is Forgiving and Merciful.” [Āl ʿImrān: 31]\n§١٧- إطاعة الرسول هو إطاعة الله:\n§17: Obeying the Messenger ﷺ is obeying Allah\n﴿مَنْ يُّطِعِ الرَّسُوْلَ فَقَدْ اَطَاعَ اللّٰهَ١ۚ﴾ [النساء: ٨٠]\nWhoever obeys the Messenger has obeyed Allah. [al-Nisā’: 80]\n﴿يٰۤاَيُّهَا الَّذِيْنَ اٰمَنُوْۤا اَطِيْعُوا اللّٰهَ وَ رَسُوْلَهٗ وَ لَا تَوَلَّوْا عَنْهُ وَ اَنْتُمْ تَسْمَعُوْنَۚۖ۰۰۲۰﴾ [الأنفال: ٢٠]\nO you who believe! Obey Allah and His Messenger, and do not turn away from him while you hear. [al-Anfāl: 20]\n§١٨- التحذير من مخالفة أمر رسول الله صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم\n§18: One should beware of disobeying the Messenger ﷺ\n﴿فَلْيَحْذَرِ الَّذِيْنَ يُخَالِفُوْنَ عَنْ اَمْرِهٖۤ اَنْ تُصِيْبَهُمْ فِتْنَةٌ اَوْ يُصِيْبَهُمْ عَذَابٌ اَلِيْمٌ۰۰۶۳﴾ [النور:٦٣]\nLet those who oppose his command beware lest a great trial strike them or a painful punishment afflict them. [al-Nūr: 63]\n﴿وَ مَا كَانَ لِمُؤْمِنٍ وَّ لَا مُؤْمِنَةٍ اِذَا قَضَى اللّٰهُ وَ رَسُوْلُهٗۤ اَمْرًا اَنْ يَّكُوْنَ لَهُمُ الْخِيَرَةُ مِنْ اَمْرِهِمْ١ؕ وَ مَنْ يَّعْصِ اللّٰهَ وَ رَسُوْلَهٗ فَقَدْ ضَلَّ ضَلٰلًا مُّبِيْنًاؕ۰۰۳۶﴾ [الأحزاب: ٣٦]\nAnd it is not for a believing man or a believing woman, when Allah and His Messenger have decided a matter, to have any choice in their affair. And whoever disobeys Allah and His Messenger has clearly gone astray. [al-Aḥzāb: 36]\n§١٩- بعثه الله رحمة للعالمين\n§19: Allah sent him ﷺ as a mercy to the worlds\n﴿وَ مَاۤ اَرْسَلْنٰكَ اِلَّا رَحْمَةً لِّلْعٰلَمِيْنَ۰۰۱۰۷﴾ [الأنبياء: ١٠٧]\nAnd We have not sent you except as a mercy to all the worlds. [al-Anbiyā’: 107]\n§٢٠- إنه رسول العالمين\n§20: Indeed he ﷺ is the Messenger to all the worlds\n﴿قُلْ يٰۤاَيُّهَا النَّاسُ اِنِّيْ رَسُوْلُ اللّٰهِ اِلَيْكُمْ جَمِيْعَا ا۟لَّذِيْ لَهٗ مُلْكُ السَّمٰوٰتِ وَ الْاَرْضِ١ۚ لَاۤ اِلٰهَ اِلَّا هُوَ يُحْيٖ وَ يُمِيْتُ١۪﴾ [الأعراف:١٥٨]\nSay: “O people! I am the Messenger of Allah to you all, of Him to whom belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth. There is no god but He; He gives life and causes death.” [al-Aʿrāf: 158]\n﴿وَ مَاۤ اَرْسَلْنٰكَ اِلَّا كَآفَّةً لِّلنَّاسِ بَشِيْرًا وَّ نَذِيْرًا﴾ [سبأ:٢٨]\nAnd We have not sent you except to all of mankind as a bearer of glad tidings and a warner. [Sabaʾ: 28]\n§٢١- أنه رسول الله صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم\n§21: Indeed he ﷺ is the Messenger of Allah\n﴿وَ مَا مُحَمَّدٌ اِلَّا رَسُوْلٌ١ۚ قَدْ خَلَتْ مِنْ قَبْلِهِ الرُّسُلُ١ؕ﴾ [آل عمران:١٤٤]\nAnd Muhammad ﷺ is only a messenger; messengers have passed away before him. [Āl ʿImrān: 144]\n﴿مَا كَانَ مُحَمَّدٌ اَبَاۤ اَحَدٍ مِّنْ رِّجَالِكُمْ وَ لٰكِنْ رَّسُوْلَ اللّٰهِ وَ خَاتَمَ النَّبِيّٖنَ١ؕ وَ كَانَ اللّٰهُ بِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ \nعَلِيْمًاؒ۰۰۴۰﴾ [الأحزاب: ٤٠]\nMuhammad ﷺ is not the father of any of your men, but he is the Messenger of Allah and the Seal of the Prophets, and Allah is All-Knowing of everything. [al-Aḥzāb: 40]\n﴿يٰسٓۚ۰۰۱ وَ الْقُرْاٰنِ الْحَكِيْمِۙ۰۰۲ اِنَّكَ لَمِنَ الْمُرْسَلِيْنَۙ۰۰۳ عَلٰى صِرَاطٍ مُّسْتَقِيْمٍؕ۰۰۴﴾ [يس: ١ - ٤]\nYā Sīn. By the Qur’an full of wisdom! Indeed you are among the messengers, on a straight path. [Yā Sīn: 1-4]\n§٢٢- إن الله أنزل عليه الكتاب:\n§22: Indeed Allah sent down the Book upon him ﷺ\n﴿وَ نَزَّلْنَا عَلَيْكَ الْكِتٰبَ تِبْيَانًا لِّكُلِّ شَيْءٍ وَّ هُدًى وَّ رَحْمَةً وَّ بُشْرٰى لِلْمُسْلِمِيْنَ۠ؒ۰۰۸۹﴾ [النحل: ٨٩]\nAnd We have sent down to you the Book as an explanation of everything, and as guidance, mercy and glad tidings for the Muslims. [al-Naḥl: 89]\n﴿وَ كَذٰلِكَ اَوْحَيْنَاۤ اِلَيْكَ قُرْاٰنًا عَرَبِيًّا لِّتُنْذِرَ اُمَّ الْقُرٰى وَ مَنْ حَوْلَهَا﴾ [الشورى: ٧]\nAnd thus We have revealed to you an Arabic Qur’an, so that you may warn the people of Makkah and those around it. [al-Shūrā: 7]\n§٢٣- إنه بشر يأكل ويشرب ويموت:\n§23: Indeed he ﷺ is a human being; he ate, drank, and departed from this world\n﴿قُلْ اِنَّمَاۤ اَنَا بَشَرٌ مِّثْلُكُمْ يُوْحٰۤى اِلَيَّ اَنَّمَاۤ اِلٰهُكُمْ اِلٰهٌ وَّاحِدٌ١ۚ﴾ [فصلت: ٦]\nSay, O My Prophet: “I am only a human being like you; it is revealed to me that your God is only one God.” [Fuṣṣilat: 6]\n﴿وَ قَالُوْا مَالِ هٰذَا الرَّسُوْلِ يَاْكُلُ الطَّعَامَ وَ يَمْشِيْ فِي الْاَسْوَاقِ١ؕ﴾ [الفرقان: ٧]\nAnd the disbelievers said: “What is the matter with this messenger, that he eats food and walks in the markets?” [al-Furqān: 7]\n﴿سُبْحَانَ رَبِّيْ هَلْ كُنْتُ اِلَّا بَشَرًا رَّسُوْلًاؒ۰۰۹۳﴾ [الإسراء:٩٣]\nAnswer: “Glory be to my Lord! Am I anything but a human being, a messenger?” [al-Isrā’: 93]\n﴿وَ مَا مَنَعَ النَّاسَ اَنْ يُّؤْمِنُوْۤا اِذْ جَآءَهُمُ الْهُدٰۤى اِلَّاۤ اَنْ قَالُوْۤا اَبَعَثَ اللّٰهُ بَشَرًا رَّسُوْلًا۰۰۹۴﴾ [الإسراء: ٩٤]\nAnd nothing prevented people from believing when the guidance came to them except that they said: “Has Allah sent a human being as a messenger?” [al-Isrā’: 94]\n§٢٤- إنه خاتم النبيين ليس بعده نبي\n§24: Indeed he ﷺ is the Seal of the Prophets, and there is no prophet after him ﷺ\n﴿مَا كَانَ مُحَمَّدٌ اَبَاۤ اَحَدٍ مِّنْ رِّجَالِكُمْ وَ لٰكِنْ رَّسُوْلَ اللّٰهِ وَ خَاتَمَ النَّبِيّٖنَ١ؕ﴾ [الأحزاب: ٤٠]\nMuhammad ﷺ is not the father of any of your men, but he is the Messenger of Allah and the Seal of the Prophets. [al-Aḥzāb: 40]\n§٢٥- الرسول لا يأتي بآية إلا بإذن الله\n§25: The Messenger does not bring any sign except by Allah’s permission\n﴿مَا كَانَ لِرَسُوْلٍ اَنْ يَّاْتِيَ بِاٰيَةٍ اِلَّا بِاِذْنِ اللّٰهِ١ؕ﴾ [الرعد: ٣٨]\nAnd it was not for any messenger to bring a sign except by Allah’s permission. [al-Raʿd: 38]\n§٢٦- النهي عن رفع الأصوات فوق صوت النبي\n§26: It is forbidden to raise one’s voice above the voice of the Prophet ﷺ\n﴿يٰۤاَيُّهَا الَّذِيْنَ اٰمَنُوْا لَا تَرْفَعُوْۤا اَصْوَاتَكُمْ فَوْقَ صَوْتِ النَّبِيِّ وَ لَا تَجْهَرُوْا لَهٗ بِالْقَوْلِ كَجَهْرِ بَعْضِكُمْ لِبَعْضٍ اَنْ تَحْبَطَ اَعْمَالُكُمْ وَ اَنْتُمْ لَا تَشْعُرُوْنَ۰۰۲﴾ [الحجرات: ٢]\nO you who believe! Do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet, and do not speak loudly to him as you speak loudly to one another, lest your deeds become void while you do not perceive it. [al-Ḥujurāt: 2]\n﴿لَا تَجْعَلُوْا دُعَآءَ الرَّسُوْلِ بَيْنَكُمْ كَدُعَآءِ بَعْضِكُمْ بَعْضًا١ؕ﴾ [النور: ٦٣]\n(O Muslims!) Do not regard the calling of the Messenger among you as you call one another. [al-Nūr: 63]\n§٢٧- إن الله يعصم النبي صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم من الكفار\n§27: Indeed Allah protected him from the disbelievers\n﴿وَ اللّٰهُ يَعْصِمُكَ مِنَ النَّاسِ١ؕ﴾ [المائدة: ٦٧]\nAnd Allah will protect you from the people. [al-Mā’idah: 67]\n﴿وَ اِذْ يَمْكُرُ بِكَ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْا لِيُثْبِتُوْكَ اَوْ يَقْتُلُوْكَ اَوْ يُخْرِجُوْكَ١ؕ وَ يَمْكُرُوْنَ وَ يَمْكُرُ اللّٰهُ١ؕ وَ اللّٰهُ خَيْرُ الْمٰكِرِيْنَ۰۰۳۰﴾ [الأنفال: ٣٠]\nAnd when the disbelievers of Quraysh were plotting against you to imprison you, or kill you, or expel you; they were plotting, and Allah was planning, and Allah is the best of planners. [al-Anfāl: 30]\n§٢٨- الرسول لا يطلب من أحد أجر الرسالة والنبوة\n§28: The Messenger does not ask anyone for a reward for his prophethood and messengership\n﴿قُلْ مَاۤ اَسْـَٔلُكُمْ عَلَيْهِ مِنْ اَجْرٍ﴾ [ص: ٨٦]\nSay, O My Prophet: “O people! I do not ask you for any reward for the call to monotheism.” [Ṣād: 86]\n﴿قُلْ لَّاۤ اَسْـَٔلُكُمْ عَلَيْهِ اَجْرًا١ؕ اِنْ هُوَ اِلَّا ذِكْرٰى لِلْعٰلَمِيْنَؒ۰۰۹۰﴾ [الأنعام: ٩٠]\nSay: “I do not ask you for any payment for conveying the Qur’an. It is only a reminder for all the worlds.” [al-Anʿām: 90]\n﴿فَخَرَاجُ رَبِّكَ خَيْرٌ١ۖۗ وَّ هُوَ خَيْرُ الرّٰزِقِيْنَ۰۰۷۲﴾ [المؤمنون: ٧٢]\nBut the reward of your Lord is better for you, and He is the best of providers. [al-Mu’minūn: 72]\n﴿قُلْ مَاۤ اَسْـَٔلُكُمْ عَلَيْهِ مِنْ اَجْرٍ وَّ مَاۤ اَنَا مِنَ الْمُتَكَلِّفِيْنَ۰۰۸۶﴾ [ص: ٨٦]\nSay, O My Prophet: “O people! I do not ask you for any reward for the call to monotheism, and I am not one of those who pretend in the call of prophethood.” [Ṣād: 86]\n§٢٩- إن الله وصف النبي صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم بأنه على خلق عظيم\n§29: Indeed Allah described the Prophet ﷺ as being of a great character\n﴿وَ اِنَّكَ لَعَلٰى خُلُقٍ عَظِيْمٍ۰۰۴﴾ [القلم: ٤]\nAnd indeed you are of a great moral character. [al-Qalam: 4]\n§٣٠- منهم من يلمز النبي صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم\n§30: Some people find fault with the Prophet ﷺ\n﴿وَ مِنْهُمْ مَّنْ يَّلْمِزُكَ فِي الصَّدَقٰتِ١ۚ فَاِنْ اُعْطُوْا مِنْهَا رَضُوْا وَ اِنْ لَّمْ يُعْطَوْا مِنْهَاۤ اِذَا هُمْ يَسْخَطُوْنَ۰۰۵۸﴾ [التوبة: ٥٨]\nAnd among them are some who find fault with you concerning the distribution of charity; if they are given some of it they are pleased, but if they are not given some of it, at once they become angry.\n[al-Tawbah: 58]\n§٣١- ومنهم من يؤذي النبي صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم بقوله: هو أذن\n§31: And some of them used to hurt the Prophet ﷺ by saying: He is an ear\n﴿وَ مِنْهُمُ الَّذِيْنَ يُؤْذُوْنَ النَّبِيَّ وَ يَقُوْلُوْنَ هُوَ اُذُنٌ١ؕ قُلْ اُذُنُ خَيْرٍ لَّكُمْ﴾ [التوبة: ٦١]\nAnd among these hypocrites are some who hurt the Prophet and say: “He is an ear (47)” (he listens to everyone). Say: “An ear of good for you.” [al-Tawbah: 61]\n§٣٢- باب النهي عن النجوى بين يدي رسول الله صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم\n§32: Chapter: It is forbidden to whisper in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ\n﴿اَلَمْ تَرَ اِلَى الَّذِيْنَ نُهُوْا عَنِ النَّجْوٰى ثُمَّ يَعُوْدُوْنَ لِمَا نُهُوْا عَنْهُ وَ يَتَنٰجَوْنَ بِالْاِثْمِ وَ الْعُدْوَانِ وَ مَعْصِيَتِ الرَّسُوْلِ١ٞ وَ اِذَا جَآءُوْكَ حَيَّوْكَ بِمَا لَمْ يُحَيِّكَ بِهِ اللّٰهُ١ۙ وَ يَقُوْلُوْنَ فِيْۤ اَنْفُسِهِمْ لَوْ لَا يُعَذِّبُنَا اللّٰهُ بِمَا نَقُوْلُ١ؕ حَسْبُهُمْ جَهَنَّمُ١ۚ يَصْلَوْنَهَا١ۚ فَبِئْسَ الْمَصِيْرُ۰۰۸﴾ [المجادلة: ٨]\nO My Prophet! Have you not seen those who were forbidden from secret whispering, then return to what they were forbidden, and whisper together for sin, aggression and disobedience to the Messenger? And when they come to you they greet you with a greeting with which Allah has not greeted you, and they say within themselves: “Why does Allah not punish us for what we say?” Hell is sufficient for them, in which they will burn, and what an evil destination. [al-Mujādalah: 8]\n§٣٣- نصرة الله للنبي صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم في الغار\n§33: Allah’s sending help to the Prophet ﷺ in the cave\n﴿اِلَّا تَنْصُرُوْهُ فَقَدْ نَصَرَهُ اللّٰهُ اِذْ اَخْرَجَهُ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْا ثَانِيَ اثْنَيْنِ اِذْ هُمَا فِي الْغَارِ اِذْ يَقُوْلُ لِصَاحِبِهٖ لَا تَحْزَنْ اِنَّ اللّٰهَ مَعَنَا١ۚ﴾ [التوبة: ٤٠]\nIf you do not help the Messenger of Allah (it makes no difference), Allah did help him when the disbelievers drove him out, being the second of two, when they were both in the cave, when he said to his companion: “Do not grieve; indeed Allah is with us.” [al-Tawbah: 40]\n§٣٤- المؤذي لرسول صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم يستحق العذاب الأليم\n§34: Whoever harms the Messenger of Allah ﷺ deserves a painful punishment\n﴿وَ الَّذِيْنَ يُؤْذُوْنَ رَسُوْلَ اللّٰهِ لَهُمْ عَذَابٌ اَلِيْمٌ۰۰۶۱﴾ [التوبة: ٦١]\nAnd those who harm the Messenger of Allah, for them is a painful punishment. [al-Tawbah: 61]\n§٣٥- ليس للنبي أن يحرم ما أحل الله\n§35: It is not for the Prophet ﷺ to forbid what Allah has made lawful\n﴿يٰۤاَيُّهَا النَّبِيُّ لِمَ تُحَرِّمُ مَاۤ اَحَلَّ اللّٰهُ لَكَ١ۚ تَبْتَغِيْ مَرْضَاتَ اَزْوَاجِكَ١ؕ وَ اللّٰهُ غَفُوْرٌ رَّحِيْمٌ۰۰۱﴾ [التحريم: ١]\nO Prophet! Why do you forbid what Allah has made lawful for you, seeking the pleasure of your wives? And Allah is Forgiving and Merciful. [al-Taḥrīm: 1]\n§٣٦- إن الله أرسل محمداً صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم يظهر الحق على الدين كله\n§36: Indeed Allah sent Muhammad ﷺ as a messenger so that he would make the truth manifest over all religions\n﴿هُوَ الَّذِيْۤ اَرْسَلَ رَسُوْلَهٗ بِالْهُدٰى وَ دِيْنِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهٗ عَلَى الدِّيْنِ كُلِّهٖ١ؕ وَ كَفٰى بِاللّٰهِ شَهِيْدًاؕ۰۰۲۸﴾ [الفتح: ٢٨]\nIt is He who sent His Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth, to make it prevail over all religions, and Allah is sufficient as a witness. [al-Fatḥ: 28]\n§٣٧- أمر النبي صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم بالصبر على أذى المشركين\n§37: The Prophet ﷺ was commanded to be patient with the harm of the polytheists\n﴿فَاصْبِرْ لِحُكْمِ رَبِّكَ وَ لَا تَكُنْ كَصَاحِبِ الْحُوْتِ١ۘ اِذْ نَادٰى وَ هُوَ مَكْظُوْمٌؕ۰۰۴۸﴾ [القلم: ٤٨]\nSo be patient with the decision of your Lord, and do not be like the Companion of the Fish (Yūnus ibn Mattā), when he called out while he was filled with grief. [al-Qalam: 48]\n﴿فَاصْبِرْ كَمَا صَبَرَ اُولُوا الْعَزْمِ مِنَ الرُّسُلِ وَ لَا تَسْتَعْجِلْ لَّهُمْ١ؕ﴾ [الأحقاف: ٣٥]\nSo be patient, O My Prophet, as the messengers of strong resolve were patient, and do not seek to hasten the punishment for them. [al-Aḥqāf: 35]"],[133,"These are some of the attributes of the Prophet ﷺ that are mentioned in the Qur’an. It is a difficult task to encompass all his attributes that are described in the Qur’an and the Sunnah."],[134,"Ibn Isḥāq said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw his Companions suffering at the hands of the disbelievers, and that they were safe only because of Allah the Exalted and because of his uncle Abū Ṭālib, but that he had no power to ward off their persecution from them, he said to them: “If you migrate to Abyssinia, no one is wronged in the land of that king. It is a land of truth and honesty, and after you go to that land, Allah the Exalted will open doors of ease for you.”\nHearing this saying of the Prophet ﷺ, his Muslim Companions, fearing this trial and preserving their religion for the sake of Allah, set out for Abyssinia. This was the first migration in Islam."],[135,"And those whom Ibn Isḥāq, may Allah have mercy on him, named are these:\n١- ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān and his wife Ruqayyah, daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ\n1: ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān and his wife, the daughter of the Prophet ﷺ, Ruqayyah, may Allah be pleased with her\n٢- Abū Ḥudhayfah ibn ʿUtbah and his wife Sahlah bint Suhayl ibn ʿAmr, who bore him Muhammad ibn Abī Ḥudhayfah in the land of Abyssinia\n2: Abū Ḥudhayfah ibn ʿUtbah and his wife Sahlah bint Suhayl ibn ʿAmr. Muhammad ibn Abī Ḥudhayfah was born to them in Abyssinia\n٣- al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām ibn Khuwaylid ibn Asad\n3: al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām ibn Khuwaylid ibn Asad\n٤- Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr ibn Hāshim\n4: Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr ibn Hāshim\n٥- ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf\n5: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf\n٦- Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Asad al-Makhzūmī and his wife Umm Salamah bint Abī Umayyah\n6: Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Asad al-Makhzūmī and his wife Umm Salamah bint Abī Umayyah\n٧- ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn\n7: ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn\n٨- ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah\n8: ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah\n٩- ʿAnazah ibn Asad ibn Rabīʿah and his wife Laylā bint Abī Khaythamah\n9: ʿAnazah ibn Asad ibn Rabīʿah and his wife Laylā bint Abī Khaythamah\n١٠- Abū Sabrah ibn Abī Ruhm\n10: Abū Sabrah ibn Abī Ruhm"],[136,"Ibn Isḥāq, may Allah have mercy on him, says: According to what has reached me, these ten among the Muslims were the first to set out for Abyssinia."],[137,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: The Prophet ﷺ prostrated during Sūrat al-Najm, and the Muslims, the polytheists, the jinn, and mankind prostrated with him; meaning when the saying of Allah Most High was revealed: “So prostrate to Allah and worship” [al-Najm: 62]."],[138,"Umm Salamah, daughter of Abū Umayyah ibn al-Mughīrah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said: When we settled in the land of Abyssinia, we lived under the best neighbor there, the Negus. We were safe in our religion, we worshiped Allah Most High without being harmed, and we did not hear anything we disliked. When that reached Quraysh, they plotted to send two strong men to the Negus concerning us, and to send gifts to the Negus from the rare goods of Makkah. Among the things he most admired from it was leather, so they gathered much leather for him and did not leave any of his bishops without giving him a gift. Then they sent that with ʿAbdullah ibn Abī Rabīʿah ibn al-Mughīrah al-Makhzūmī and ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wā’il al-Sahmī, and instructed them with their plan. They said to them: “Give every bishop his gift before you speak to the Negus about them, then present the Negus his gifts, then ask him to hand them over to you before he speaks to them.” She said: So they left and came to the Negus, while we were with him in the best dwelling and with the best neighbor. They did not leave any of his bishops except that they gave him his gift before speaking to the Negus. Then they said to every bishop among them: “Some foolish young men from us have fled to the king’s land. They have left the religion of their people and have not entered your religion. They have come with an invented religion that neither we nor you know. The nobles of their people have sent us to the king concerning them so that we may return them to them. When we speak to the king about them, advise him to hand them over to us and not speak to them, for their people have better insight about them and know best what they blame them for.” They said to the two of them: “Yes.” Then the two of them brought their gifts to the Negus, and he accepted them. Then they spoke to him and said: “O king, some foolish young men from us have fled to your land. They have left the religion of their people and have not entered your religion. They have come with an invented religion that neither we nor you know. The nobles of their people — their fathers, uncles, and clansmen — have sent us to you concerning them so that you may return them to them. They have better insight about them and know best what they blame and reproach them for.”\nShe said: Nothing was more hated to ʿAbdullah ibn Abī Rabīʿah and ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ than that the Negus should hear their speech. The bishops around him said: “They have spoken truthfully, O king. Their people have better insight about them and know best what they blame them for, so hand them over to the two of them, and let them return them to their land and their people.” She said: The Negus became angry and said: “No, by Allah, then I will not hand them over to them. I will not nearly hand over a people who have sought my protection, settled in my land, and chosen me over others, until I call them and ask them about what these two say concerning them. If they are as the two say, I will hand them over to them and return them to their people. But if they are otherwise, I will protect them from the two and treat them well as long as they remain under my protection.” She said: Then he sent for the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and summoned them. When his messenger came to them, they gathered and said to one another: “What will you say to the man when you come to him?” They said: “By Allah, we will say what we know and what our Prophet ﷺ commanded us, whatever may happen.” When they came to him, the Negus had summoned his bishops, and they had spread their scriptures around him. He asked them: “What is this religion by which you have separated from your people, while you have not entered my religion nor the religion of any of these nations?”\nShe said: The one who spoke to him was Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib. He said to him: “O king, we were a people of ignorance. We worshiped idols, ate carrion, committed immoral deeds, cut off family ties, treated neighbors badly, and the strong among us consumed the weak. We remained upon that until Allah sent to us a Messenger from among us, whose lineage, truthfulness, trustworthiness, and chastity we knew. He called us to Allah Most High, to single Him out and worship Him, and to abandon what we and our fathers used to worship besides Him of stones and idols. He commanded us to speak truthfully, fulfill trusts, maintain family ties, treat neighbors well, and refrain from forbidden things and bloodshed. He forbade us from immoralities, false speech, consuming the wealth of orphans, and slandering chaste women. He commanded us to worship Allah alone, not associating anything with Him, and he commanded us to pray, give zakah, and fast.” She said: He listed to him the matters of Islam. “So we believed him, had faith in him, and followed him in what he brought. We worshiped Allah alone and did not associate anything with Him. We made unlawful what he made unlawful for us, and lawful what he made lawful for us. Then our people attacked us, tortured us, and tried to tempt us away from our religion, to return us from the worship of Allah to the worship of idols, and to make us consider lawful what we used to consider lawful of foul things. When they overpowered us, wronged us, made things difficult for us, and came between us and our religion, we came out to your land, chose you over others, desired your protection, and hoped that we would not be wronged in your presence, O king.”\nShe said: The Negus said to him: “Do you have anything with you from what he brought from Allah?” Jaʿfar said: “Yes.” The Negus said: “Recite it to me.” So he recited to him the beginning of “Kāf Hā Yā ʿAyn Ṣād” [Maryam: 1]. She said: By Allah, the Negus wept until his beard became wet, and his bishops wept until their scriptures became wet when they heard what was recited to them. Then the Negus said: “Indeed, this and what Mūsā brought come from one lamp.” He said to the two men: “Go, for by Allah, I will never hand them over to you, nor will I come close to doing so.”\nUmm Salamah said: When they left him, ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ said: “By Allah, tomorrow I will inform him of something about them that will disgrace them in his sight, and by it I will uproot their foundation.” She said: ʿAbdullah ibn Abī Rabīʿah, who was the more mindful of the two men regarding us, said to him: “Do not do that, for they have family ties, even if they have opposed us.” He said: “By Allah, I will tell him that they claim that ʿĪsā ibn Maryam is a slave.” She said: The next morning he went to him and said: “O king, they say a grave statement about ʿĪsā ibn Maryam, so send for them and ask them what they say about him.”\nShe said: He sent for them and asked them about him, and nothing like that had ever come upon us. The people gathered and said to one another: “What will you say about ʿĪsā if he asks you about him?” They said: “By Allah, we will say about him what Allah said and what our Prophet brought, whatever may happen.” When they entered upon him, he said to them: “What do you say about ʿĪsā ibn Maryam?” Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib said to him: “We say about him what our Prophet brought: he is the servant of Allah, His Messenger, His spirit, and His word which He cast to Maryam, the virgin and pure one.” She said: The Negus struck his hand to the ground, picked up a stick from it, and said: “ʿĪsā ibn Maryam did not exceed what you have said by the measure of this stick.” His bishops snorted around him when he said what he said.\nHe said: “Even if you snort, by Allah. Go, for you are safe in my land.” The word “suyūm” means “safe.” “Whoever insults you will be fined; whoever insults you will be fined; whoever insults you will be fined. I would not love to have a mountain of gold while I harm a man among you.” In the language of Abyssinia, “dabr” means mountain. “Return their gifts to the two of them; we have no need of them. By Allah, Allah did not take a bribe from me when He returned my kingdom to me, so should I take a bribe concerning it? And the people did not obey concerning me, so should I obey them concerning it?” She said: They left him disgraced, with what they had brought returned to them, and we remained with him in the best dwelling and with the best neighbor. She said: By Allah, while we were upon that, someone came against him, meaning someone who contested him in his kingdom. She said: By Allah, we never experienced any grief more severe than the grief we felt at that time, fearing that this person might prevail over the Negus, and that a man would come who would not know our right as the Negus knew it. She said: The Negus went out, and between them was the width of the Nile. She said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Who is a man who will go out and attend the battle of the people, then bring us the news?” She said: al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām said: “I will.” She said: He was among the youngest of the people. They inflated a waterskin for him, and he placed it on his chest, then swam upon it until he reached the side of the Nile where the two groups were meeting. Then he went on until he attended them. She said: We supplicated to Allah for the Negus to prevail over his enemy and to be established in his land, and the affair of Abyssinia became firm for him. We remained with him in the best lodging until we came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was in Makkah."],[139,"Ibn Masʿūd said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us to the Negus, and we were about eighty men. Among them were ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿūd, Jaʿfar, ʿAbdullah ibn ʿArfaṭah, ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn, and Abū Mūsā. They came to the Negus, and Quraysh sent ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ and ʿUmārah ibn al-Walīd with a gift. When the two entered upon the Negus, they prostrated to him, then hastened to sit on his right and left. Then they said to him: “A group from our cousins have settled in your land, so send for them.” He sent for them. Jaʿfar said: “I am your spokesman today.” So they followed him. He greeted him with peace and did not prostrate. They said to him: “Why do you not prostrate to the king?” He said: “We do not prostrate except to Allah, Mighty and Majestic.” He said: “What is that?” He said: “Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent to us His Messenger ﷺ and commanded us not to prostrate to anyone except Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and commanded us with prayer and zakah.” ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ said: “They oppose you regarding ʿĪsā ibn Maryam!” He said: “What do you say about ʿĪsā ibn Maryam and his mother?” They said: “We say as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: He is the word of Allah and His spirit which He cast to the virgin and pure one, whom no human being had touched and from whom no child had been born before.” He lifted a stick from the ground and said: “O people of Abyssinia, priests, and monks, by Allah, they do not add to what we say about him even the measure of this.” Then he said: “Welcome to you and to the one from whom you have come. I testify that he is the Messenger of Allah. He is the one we find in the Gospel, and he is the Messenger about whom ʿĪsā ibn Maryam gave glad tidings. Settle wherever you wish. By Allah, were it not for the kingdom I am in, I would go to him until I myself carried his sandals and poured water for his ablution.” He ordered that the gift of the two others be returned to them. Then ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿūd hurried back until he caught Badr. He claimed that the Prophet ﷺ sought forgiveness for the Negus when news of his death reached him."],[140,"Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī said: News of the Prophet’s ﷺ emergence reached us while we were in Yemen, so we set out migrating to him — myself and two brothers of mine, I being the youngest of them: one was Abū Burdah and the other Abū Ruhm — either he said among a few, or he said among fifty-three or fifty-two men from my people. We boarded a ship, and our ship took us to the Negus in Abyssinia. We found Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib and his companions with him. Jaʿfar said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us here and commanded us to remain, so stay with us.” We stayed with him until we all came together, and we met the Prophet ﷺ when he conquered Khaybar. He assigned us shares — or he said: he gave us from it. He did not divide anything from Khaybar for anyone who was absent from its conquest except those who had witnessed it with him, except for the people of our ship along with Jaʿfar and his companions; he gave them shares with them."],[141,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said: I never knew my parents except that they were practicing the religion, and no day would pass except that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would come to us at both ends of the day: morning and evening. When the Muslims were tested, Abū Bakr set out as a migrant toward the land of Abyssinia. When he reached Bark al-Ghimād, Ibn al-Daghinah — the chief of al-Qārah — met him and said: “Where are you going, O Abū Bakr?” Abū Bakr said: “My people have driven me out, so I want to travel through the earth and worship my Lord.” Ibn al-Daghinah said: “A man like you, O Abū Bakr, should not leave and should not be driven out. You earn for the destitute, maintain family ties, bear burdens, host guests, and assist in calamities of truth. I am your protector, so return and worship your Lord in your town.” So he returned, and Ibn al-Daghinah traveled back with him. That evening, Ibn al-Daghinah went around to the nobles of Quraysh and said to them: “A man like Abū Bakr should not leave and should not be driven out. Will you drive out a man who earns for the destitute, maintains family ties, bears burdens, hosts guests, and assists in calamities of truth?” Quraysh did not reject the protection of Ibn al-Daghinah, and they said to Ibn al-Daghinah: “Tell Abū Bakr to worship his Lord in his house, to pray there, and to recite whatever he wishes, but not to harm us with that and not to do it openly, for we fear he will put our women and children to trial.” Ibn al-Daghinah told that to Abū Bakr. Abū Bakr remained like that, worshiping his Lord in his house, not making his prayer public and not reciting outside his house. Then something occurred to Abū Bakr, so he built a small mosque in the courtyard of his house. He would pray in it and recite the Qur’an. The women and children of the polytheists would crowd around him, amazed by him and looking at him. Abū Bakr was a man who wept often; he could not control his eyes when he recited the Qur’an. This alarmed the nobles of Quraysh among the polytheists, so they sent for Ibn al-Daghinah, and he came to them. They said: “We granted Abū Bakr protection through your protection on the condition that he worship his Lord in his house, but he has gone beyond that. He built a mosque in the courtyard of his house and openly performs prayer and recitation in it. We fear that he will put our women and children to trial, so stop him. If he wants to limit himself to worshiping his Lord in his house, let him do so. But if he refuses except to do that openly, then ask him to return your covenant of protection to you. We dislike violating your protection, but we will not allow Abū Bakr to practice openly.” ʿĀ’ishah said: Ibn al-Daghinah came to Abū Bakr and said: “You know what I covenanted for you. Either limit yourself to that, or return my covenant of protection to me, for I do not want the Arabs to hear that I have been betrayed regarding a man for whom I made a covenant.” Abū Bakr said: “Then I return your protection to you, and I am content with the protection of Allah, Mighty and Majestic.” The Prophet ﷺ was in Makkah at that time, and he said to the Muslims: “I have been shown the land of your migration: a place of palm trees between two lava plains,” and they were the two ḥarrahs. So whoever migrated before Medina migrated, and most of those who had migrated to Abyssinia returned to Medina. Abū Bakr prepared himself to go to Medina, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: “Wait, for I hope that permission will be granted to me.” Abū Bakr said: “Do you hope for that, may my father be sacrificed for you?” He said: “Yes.” So Abū Bakr kept himself waiting for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ so that he could accompany him, and he fed two riding animals that he had with the leaves of the samur tree — meaning khabaṭ — for four months. Ibn Shihāb said: ʿUrwah said: ʿĀ’ishah said: While we were sitting one day in the house of Abū Bakr at the height of midday, someone said to Abū Bakr: “This is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, veiled, at an hour when he would not usually come to us.” Abū Bakr said: “May my father and mother be sacrificed for him. By Allah, nothing has brought him at this hour except an important matter.” She said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and asked permission to enter, and permission was granted to him. He entered, and the Prophet ﷺ said to Abū Bakr: “Send out whoever is with you.” Abū Bakr said: “They are only your family, may my father be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “I have been granted permission to leave.” Abū Bakr said: “Companionship, may my father be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Yes.” Abū Bakr said: “Then take, may my father be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah, one of these two riding animals of mine.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “For a price.” ʿĀ’ishah said: We prepared them as quickly and completely as possible, and we prepared provisions for them in a leather bag. Asmā’ bint Abī Bakr cut a piece from her waistband and tied the mouth of the bag with it, and for that she was called Dhāt al-Niṭāq. She said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr reached a cave in Mount Thawr and hid in it for three nights. ʿAbdullah ibn Abī Bakr, a clever and perceptive young man, would spend the night with them, then leave them before dawn and wake up in Makkah among Quraysh as though he had spent the night there. He would not hear any plot they were devising except that he memorized it, then he would bring them the news when darkness mixed in. ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah, the freedman of Abū Bakr, would graze a milch flock of sheep for them and bring it to them when some time of the night had passed, so they would spend the night with fresh milk — the milk of their milch animals and what was warmed with hot stones — until ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah would drive it away before dawn. He did that every night of those three nights. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr hired a man from Banū al-Dīl, from Banū ʿAbd ibn ʿAdī, as a skilled guide; “khirrīt” means one expert in guidance. He had entered into a covenant with the family of al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wā’il al-Sahmī, and he was upon the religion of the disbelievers of Quraysh. They trusted him and handed over their two riding animals to him, agreeing to meet him at the cave of Thawr after three nights with their two mounts on the morning of the third. ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah and the guide departed with them, and he took them along the coastal route."],[142,"Abū Hurayrah said: The Prophet ﷺ said the next day, the Day of Sacrifice, while he was at Minā: “Tomorrow we will camp at the valley of Banū Kinānah, where they made a pact upon disbelief.” By this he meant al-Muḥaṣṣab. That was because Quraysh and Kinānah had allied themselves against Banū Hāshim and Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib — or Banū al-Muṭṭalib — that they would not marry into them, nor buy and sell with them, until they surrendered the Prophet ﷺ to them."],[143,"Ibn Isḥāq says: When Quraysh learned that the Companions of the Messenger were living in safety and security in Abyssinia, that the Negus was unwilling to listen to anyone against them, that ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, had also accepted Islam, that ʿUmar and Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, may Allah be pleased with them, had begun to defend the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions vigorously, and that Islam had begun spreading rapidly among the tribes, Quraysh gathered and resolved on a plan against him. In consultation it was agreed that a pact be written among them, stating that no one would marry from Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib or give them in marriage, and that no buying or selling would be done with them. After agreement was reached on this, the pact was formally written down. Then, as further emphasis, this pact was hung inside the Kaʿbah. The writer of the pact was Manṣūr ibn ʿIkrimah ibn ʿĀmir ibn Hāshim ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn ʿAbd al-Dār ibn Quṣayy. Ibn Hishām says that it was al-Naḍr ibn al-Ḥārith. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ invoked a curse against him, as a result of which some of his fingers became paralyzed.\nThose who supported Abū Ṭālib and those who separated from him:\nAfter this pact of Quraysh, Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib supported Abū Ṭālib ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib. They went with Abū Ṭālib into the Valley of Abū Ṭālib (Shiʿb Abī Ṭālib) and stayed with him. Of Banū Hāshim only Abū Lahab ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib sided with Quraysh and cooperated with them in every way.\nMūsā ibn ʿUqbah said that Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib were besieged in the valley for three years and faced severe hardship and distress. If anything reached them for their sustenance, it was only in secret.\nThis boycott continued from the seventh year of the prophethood until the tenth year of the prophethood, three years before the Hijrah. The people of biography mention that after three years several people of Makkah resolved to end the pact. Prominent among them were these: Hishām ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith, Zuhayr ibn Abī Umayyah, Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdī, Zamʿah ibn al-Aswad and Abū al-Bakhtarī ibn Hishām ibn al-Ḥārith. Out of family ties and kinship they supported Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib."],[144,"Ibn Isḥāq, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Then Sayyidah Khadījah, may Allah be pleased with her, and Abū Ṭālib died in the same year, and mountains of calamity fell upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Khadījah, may Allah be pleased with her, was like a minister to him, who had believed in his Islam; and with the death of his uncle Abū Ṭālib, who was his strong arm, a source of safety for him, and his protector and helper. These events took place three years before his migration to Madinah. After the death of Abū Ṭālib he faced from Quraysh such harms as had not been possible in his lifetime. Until one of the fools of Quraysh confronted him and even threw dust on his head."],[145,"al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib said to the Prophet ﷺ: “What benefit did you give your uncle, for he used to protect you and become angry on your behalf?” He said: “He is in a shallow part of the Fire. Were it not for me, he would have been in the lowest depth of the Fire.”"],[146,"al-Musayyib ibn Ḥazn said: When death approached Abū Ṭālib, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to him and found Abū Jahl ibn Hishām and ʿAbdullah ibn Abī Umayyah ibn al-Mughīrah with him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Abū Ṭālib: “O my uncle, say: There is no god but Allah — a word by which I will testify for you before Allah.” Abū Jahl and ʿAbdullah ibn Abī Umayyah said: “O Abū Ṭālib, will you turn away from the religion of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued presenting it to him, and the two of them kept repeating that statement, until the last thing Abū Ṭālib said to them was: “He is upon the religion of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib,” and he refused to say: “There is no god but Allah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “By Allah, I will surely seek forgiveness for you as long as I am not forbidden from doing so.” Then Allah Most High revealed: “It is not for the Prophet and those who believe to seek forgiveness for the polytheists, even if they are near relatives, after it has become clear to them that they are the companions of the Blaze” [al-Tawbah: 113]. And He revealed regarding Abū Ṭālib, saying to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “Indeed, you do not guide whom you love, but Allah guides whom He wills, and He knows best those who are guided” [al-Qaṣaṣ: 56]."],[147,"Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to his uncle: “Say: There is no god but Allah, and I will testify for you by it on the Day of Resurrection.” He said: “Were it not that Quraysh would shame me, saying: ‘Only panic drove him to that,’ I would have comforted your eye with it.” So Allah revealed: “Indeed, you do not guide whom you love, but Allah guides whom He wills, and He knows best those who are guided” [al-Qaṣaṣ: 56]."],[148,"Abū Saʿīd heard the Prophet ﷺ, when his uncle was mentioned in his presence, say: “Perhaps my intercession will benefit him on the Day of Resurrection, so he will be placed in a shallow part of the Fire reaching his ankles, from which his brain will boil.”"],[149,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The lightest of the people of the Fire in punishment is Abū Ṭālib. He will be wearing two sandals from which his brain will boil.”"],[150,"ʿUrwah narrated that ʿĀ’ishah told him that she said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “O Messenger of Allah, has there ever come upon you a day more severe than the Day of Uḥud?” He said: “I certainly faced from your people what I faced, and the most severe of what I faced from them was on the Day of al-ʿAqabah, when I presented myself to Ibn ʿAbd Yālīl ibn ʿAbd Kulāl, but he did not respond to what I wanted. I departed, overwhelmed with grief, not knowing where I was going, and I did not regain awareness until I reached Qarn al-Thaʿālib. I raised my head and there was a cloud shading me. I looked and there was Jibrīl in it. He called me and said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has heard what your people said to you and how they responded to you, and He has sent to you the Angel of the Mountains so that you may command him regarding them as you wish. Then the Angel of the Mountains called me, greeted me with salām, and said: O Muḥammad, Allah has heard what your people said to you. I am the Angel of the Mountains, and your Lord has sent me to you so that you may command me with your command. What do you wish? If you wish, I will crush them between the two mountains.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: “Rather, I hope that Allah will bring forth from their loins those who will worship Allah alone and not associate anything with Him.”"],[151,"ʿAbdullah ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib said: When Abū Ṭālib died, the Prophet ﷺ went out walking on his feet to al-Ṭā’if and called them to Allah, but they did not respond to him. He came to the shade of a tree, prayed two rakʿahs, then said: “O Allah, to You I complain of my weakness, my lack of means, and my lowliness before the people. O Most Merciful of the merciful, You are the Lord of the weak, and You are my Lord. To whom will You entrust me? To a distant person who receives me harshly? Or to an enemy whom You have given control over my affair? If You are not angry with me, then I do not mind; but Your protection is broader for me. I seek refuge in the light of Your Face, by which darknesses shine and the affairs of this world and the Hereafter are set right, from Your anger descending upon me or Your displeasure coming down upon me. I seek to please You until You are pleased. There is no might and no power except through You.”"],[152,"Allah the Exalted said: ﴿سُبْحٰنَ الَّذِيْۤ اَسْرٰى بِعَبْدِهٖ لَيْلًا مِّنَ الْمَسْجِدِ الْحَرَامِ اِلَى الْمَسْجِدِ الْاَقْصَا الَّذِيْ بٰرَكْنَا حَوْلَهٗ لِنُرِيَهٗ مِنْ اٰيٰتِنَا١ؕ اِنَّهٗ هُوَ السَّمِيْعُ الْبَصِيْرُ۰۰۱﴾ [al-Isrāʾ: 1]\nAllah the Exalted said: Glorified is He (free from all faults and defects) who took His servant (Muhammad) by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque, whose surroundings We have blessed, so that We might show him some of Our signs. Indeed, He is the All-Hearing, the All-Seeing. [al-Isrāʾ: 1]"],[153,"Imam Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was taken on the journey to Bayt al-Maqdis one year before he went to Madinah. Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah narrates the same from Ibn Shihāb, and this is also the statement of ʿUrwah (al-Dalāʾil by al-Bayhaqī). This is the correct view. Some of them have also mentioned that it took place before the Hijrah, on the twenty-seventh of Rabīʿ al-Awwal. The Isrāʾ and the Miʿrāj of the Prophet ﷺ took place in a single night, and only once. It took place with both the soul and the body, while he was awake and not asleep, as is the position of the majority of the scholars.\nImam al-Qurṭubī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: This is indicated by the apparent meaning of the Book (the texts), by the authentic hadiths, and by the denial of the Quraysh, which makes the matter clear. Had it been in a dream, why would they have denied it?! And when they were put to the test, (they said:) many people see very strange things in their dreams, so this would have been no difficulty in sleep. What they considered far-fetched was seeing it while awake."],[154,"Abū Dharr used to narrate that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The roof of my house was opened while I was in Makkah, and Jibrīl, peace be upon him, descended. He opened my chest, then washed it with the water of Zamzam. Then he brought a basin of gold filled with wisdom and faith, poured it into my chest, then closed it. Then he took my hand and ascended with me to the heaven. When we came to the lowest heaven, Jibrīl, peace be upon him, said to the gatekeeper of the lowest heaven: ‘Open.’ He said: ‘Who is this?’ He said: ‘This is Jibrīl.’ He said: ‘Is anyone with you?’ He said: ‘Yes, Muḥammad ﷺ is with me.’ He said: ‘Has he been sent for?’ He said: ‘Yes.’ So he opened. When we rose into the lowest heaven, there was a man, with dark multitudes on his right and dark multitudes on his left. When he looked to his right, he laughed, and when he looked to his left, he wept. He said: ‘Welcome to the righteous Prophet and righteous son.’ I said: ‘O Jibrīl, who is this?’ He said: ‘This is Ādam, peace be upon him, and these dark multitudes on his right and left are the souls of his children. The people of the right are the people of Paradise, and the dark multitudes on his left are the people of the Fire. When he looks to his right he laughs, and when he looks to his left he weeps.’ Then Jibrīl ascended with me until he came to the second heaven. He said to its gatekeeper: ‘Open.’ Its gatekeeper said to him the same as the gatekeeper of the lowest heaven had said, and he opened.” Anas ibn Mālik said: He mentioned that he found in the heavens Ādam, Idrīs, ʿĪsā, Mūsā, and Ibrāhīm, may the prayers of Allah be upon them all, but he did not establish exactly how their stations were, except that he mentioned that he found Ādam, peace be upon him, in the lowest heaven, and Ibrāhīm in the sixth heaven. He said: When Jibrīl and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by Idrīs, may Allah’s prayers be upon him, he said: “Welcome to the righteous Prophet and righteous brother.” He said: Then he passed on, and I said: “Who is this?” He said: “This is Idrīs.” Then I passed by Mūsā, peace be upon him, and he said: “Welcome to the righteous Prophet and righteous brother.” I said: “Who is this?” He said: “This is Mūsā.” Then I passed by ʿĪsā, and he said: “Welcome to the righteous Prophet and righteous brother.” I said: “Who is this?” He said: “This is ʿĪsā ibn Maryam.” Then I passed by Ibrāhīm, peace be upon him, and he said: “Welcome to the righteous Prophet and righteous son.” I said: “Who is this?” He said: “This is Ibrāhīm.”\nIbn Shihāb said: Ibn Ḥazm informed me that Ibn ʿAbbās and Abū Ḥabbah al-Anṣārī used to say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Then I was ascended until I reached a level where I heard the scratching of the pens.” Ibn Ḥazm and Anas ibn Mālik said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Allah obligated upon my Ummah fifty prayers.” He said: “I returned with that until I passed by Mūsā, and Mūsā, peace be upon him, said: ‘What has your Lord obligated upon your Ummah?’ I said: ‘He has obligated upon them fifty prayers.’ Mūsā, peace be upon him, said to me: ‘Return to your Lord, for your Ummah will not be able to bear that.’ So I returned to my Lord, and He removed half of it. Then I returned to Mūsā, peace be upon him, and informed him. He said: ‘Return to your Lord, for your Ummah will not be able to bear that.’ So I returned to my Lord, and He said: ‘They are five, and they are fifty. The word is not changed with Me.’ I returned to Mūsā, and he said: ‘Return to your Lord.’ I said: ‘I have become shy before my Lord.’ Then Jibrīl took me until he came to Sidrat al-Muntahā, and it was covered with colors that I did not know. Then I was admitted into Paradise, and there were domes of pearl, and its soil was musk.”"],[155,"Mālik ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah, a man from his people, said: The Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: “While I was at the House, between sleep and wakefulness, I heard someone saying: ‘One of the three, between the two men.’ I was brought and taken away, and a basin of gold containing water from Zamzam was brought. My chest was opened to such-and-such.” Qatādah said: I said to the one with me: “What does he mean?” He said: “To the bottom of his abdomen.” “My heart was taken out and washed with Zamzam water, then returned to its place, then filled with faith and wisdom. Then I was brought a white beast called al-Burāq, larger than a donkey and smaller than a mule, whose step would land at the farthest point of its sight, and I was carried upon it. Then we set out until we came to the lowest heaven. Jibrīl, peace be upon him, asked for it to be opened. It was said: ‘Who is this?’ He said: ‘Jibrīl.’ It was said: ‘Who is with you?’ He said: ‘Muḥammad ﷺ.’ It was said: ‘Has he been sent for?’ He said: ‘Yes.’ It was opened for us, and it was said: ‘Welcome to him, and what an excellent arrival he has made.’ Then we came upon Ādam, peace be upon him.” He then narrated the story of the ḥadīth and mentioned that in the second heaven he met ʿĪsā and Yaḥyā, peace be upon them both; in the third, Yūsuf; in the fourth, Idrīs; in the fifth, Hārūn — may the prayers and peace of Allah be upon them. He said: “Then we set out until we reached the sixth heaven. I came upon Mūsā, peace be upon him, and greeted him with salām. He said: ‘Welcome to the righteous brother and righteous Prophet.’ When I passed him, he wept, and he was called: ‘What makes you weep?’ He said: ‘My Lord, this young man whom You sent after me — more of his Ummah will enter Paradise than of my Ummah!’ Then we set out until we reached the seventh heaven, and I came upon Ibrāhīm.” In the ḥadīth, the Prophet of Allah ﷺ narrated that he saw four rivers emerging from its base: two outward rivers and two inward rivers. I said: “O Jibrīl, what are these rivers?” He said: “As for the inward rivers, they are two rivers in Paradise, and as for the outward ones, they are the Nile and the Euphrates.” Then al-Bayt al-Maʿmūr was raised for me. I said: “O Jibrīl, what is this?” He said: “This is al-Bayt al-Maʿmūr. Every day seventy thousand angels enter it, and when they leave it, they never return to it again; that is the last obligation upon them.” Then I was brought two vessels: one containing wine and the other milk. They were presented to me, and I chose the milk. It was said: “You have chosen correctly; through you, Allah has made your Ummah upon the fiṭrah.” Then fifty prayers every day were obligated upon me. Then he mentioned the story until the end of the ḥadīth."],[156,"Anas narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Sidrat al-Muntahā was raised for me, and there were four rivers: two outward rivers and two inward rivers. As for the outward rivers, they are the Nile and the Euphrates, and as for the inward rivers, they are two rivers in Paradise. I was brought three cups: a cup containing milk, a cup containing honey, and a cup containing wine. I took the one containing milk and drank it, and it was said: You and your Ummah have attained the fiṭrah.”"],[157,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was brought al-Burāq, a tall white animal, larger than a donkey and smaller than a mule, placing its hoof at the farthest point of its sight. I rode it until I came to Bayt al-Maqdis. I tied it to the ring to which the Prophets tie their mounts. Then I entered the mosque and prayed two rakʿahs in it. Then I came out, and Jibrīl, peace be upon him, brought me a vessel of wine and a vessel of milk, and I chose the milk. Jibrīl, peace be upon him said: ‘You have chosen the fiṭrah.’ Then we were taken up to the heaven, and Jibrīl asked for it to be opened. It was said: ‘Who are you?’ He said: ‘Jibrīl.’ It was said: ‘Who is with you?’ He said: ‘Muḥammad.’ It was said: ‘Has he been sent for?’ He said: ‘He has been sent for.’ It was opened for us, and there I was with Ādam, who welcomed me and supplicated good for me. Then we ascended to the second heaven, and Jibrīl, peace be upon him, asked for it to be opened. It was said: ‘Who are you?’ He said: ‘Jibrīl.’ It was said: ‘Who is with you?’ He said: ‘Muḥammad.’ It was said: ‘Has he been sent for?’ He said: ‘He has been sent for.’ It was opened for us, and there I was with the two maternal cousins: ʿĪsā ibn Maryam and Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā, may Allah’s prayers be upon them both. They welcomed me and supplicated good for me. Then I was ascended to the third heaven, and Jibrīl asked for it to be opened. It was said: ‘Who are you?’ He said: ‘Jibrīl.’ It was said: ‘Who is with you?’ He said: ‘Muḥammad.’ It was said: ‘Has he been sent for?’ He said: ‘He has been sent for.’ It was opened for us, and there I was with Yūsuf ﷺ, who had been given half of beauty. He welcomed me and supplicated good for me. Then we ascended to the fourth heaven, and Jibrīl, peace be upon him, asked for it to be opened. It was said: ‘Who is this?’ He said: ‘Jibrīl.’ It was said: ‘Who is with you?’ He said: ‘Muḥammad.’ It was said: ‘Has he been sent for?’ He said: ‘He has been sent for.’ It was opened for us, and there I was with Idrīs, who welcomed me and supplicated good for me. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: ‘And We raised him to a high place’ [Maryam: 57]. Then we ascended to the fifth heaven, and Jibrīl asked for it to be opened. It was said: ‘Who is this?’ He said: ‘Jibrīl.’ It was said: ‘Who is with you?’ He said: ‘Muḥammad.’ It was said: ‘Has he been sent for?’ He said: ‘He has been sent for.’ It was opened for us, and there I was with Hārūn ﷺ, who welcomed me and supplicated good for me. Then we ascended to the sixth heaven, and Jibrīl, peace be upon him, asked for it to be opened. It was said: ‘Who is this?’ He said: ‘Jibrīl.’ It was said: ‘Who is with you?’ He said: ‘Muḥammad.’ It was said: ‘Has he been sent for?’ He said: ‘He has been sent for.’ It was opened for us, and there I was with Mūsā ﷺ, who welcomed me and supplicated good for me. Then we ascended to the seventh heaven, and Jibrīl asked for it to be opened. It was said: ‘Who is this?’ He said: ‘Jibrīl.’ It was said: ‘Who is with you?’ He said: ‘Muḥammad ﷺ.’ It was said: ‘Has he been sent for?’ He said: ‘He has been sent for.’ It was opened for us, and there I was with Ibrāhīm ﷺ, reclining his back against al-Bayt al-Maʿmūr. Every day seventy thousand angels enter it and never return to it. Then I was taken to Sidrat al-Muntahā, and its leaves were like the ears of elephants, and its fruits were like large jars. When there covered it from the command of Allah what covered it, it changed, and no one from the creation of Allah could describe its beauty. Allah then revealed to me what He revealed, and He obligated upon me fifty prayers every day and night. I descended to Mūsā ﷺ, and he said: ‘What has your Lord obligated upon your Ummah?’ I said: ‘Fifty prayers.’ He said: ‘Return to your Lord and ask Him to lighten it, for your Ummah will not be able to bear that. I tested and experienced the Children of Israel.’ So I returned to my Lord and said: ‘My Lord, lighten it for my Ummah.’ He removed five from me. I returned to Mūsā and said: ‘He removed five from me.’ He said: ‘Your Ummah will not be able to bear that, so return to your Lord and ask Him to lighten it.’ I continued going back and forth between my Lord, Blessed and Most High, and Mūsā, peace be upon him, until He said: ‘O Muḥammad, they are five prayers every day and night. For every prayer there are ten, so that is fifty prayers. Whoever intends a good deed but does not do it, one good deed is written for him; if he does it, ten are written for him. Whoever intends an evil deed but does not do it, nothing is written; if he does it, one evil deed is written.’ Then I descended until I reached Mūsā and informed him. He said: ‘Return to your Lord and ask Him to lighten it.’ The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘I said: I have returned to my Lord until I have become shy before Him.’”"],[158,"Anas ibn Mālik said: On the night when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was taken by night from the Mosque of the Kaʿbah, three individuals came to him before revelation had come to him, while he was sleeping in al-Masjid al-Ḥarām. The first of them said: “Which of them is he?” The middle one said: “He is the best of them.” The last one said: “Take the best of them.” That was that night, and he did not see them again until they came to him on another night, while his heart was seeing, though his eyes slept and his heart did not sleep; and likewise the Prophets, their eyes sleep but their hearts do not sleep. They did not speak to him until they carried him and placed him by the well of Zamzam. Jibrīl took charge of him from among them and split from his throat to his lower chest until he finished his chest and abdomen. He washed him with Zamzam water by his hand until he purified his inside. Then he brought a basin of gold containing a vessel of gold filled with faith and wisdom, and he filled his chest and his throat veins with it, then closed it. Then he ascended with him to the lowest heaven and knocked on one of its gates. The people of heaven called out to him: “Who is this?” He said: “Jibrīl.” They said: “Who is with you?” He said: “Muḥammad ﷺ is with me.” They said: “Has he been sent?” He said: “Yes.” They said: “Welcome to him.” The people of heaven rejoiced at him, and the people of heaven do not know what Allah intends for him on earth until He informs them. He found Ādam in the lowest heaven, and Jibrīl said to him: “This is your father, so greet him with salām.” He greeted him, and Ādam returned the greeting and said: “Welcome to my son; what an excellent son you are.” Then in the lowest heaven there were two rivers flowing. He said: “What are these two rivers, O Jibrīl?” He said: “These are the source of the Nile and Euphrates.” Then he took him onward in heaven, and there was another river upon which was a palace of pearl and emerald. He struck it with his hand, and it was fragrant musk. He said: “What is this, O Jibrīl?” He said: “This is al-Kawthar, which your Lord has stored for you.” Then he ascended with him to the second heaven, and the angels said to him the same as the first had said: “Who is this?” He said: “Jibrīl.” They said: “Who is with you?” He said: “Muḥammad ﷺ.” They said: “Has he been sent to?” He said: “Yes.” They said: “Welcome to him.” Then he ascended with him to the third heaven, and they said to him the same as the first and second. Then he ascended with him to the fourth, and they said the same. Then he ascended with him to the fifth heaven, and they said the same. Then he ascended with him to the sixth heaven, and they said the same. Then he ascended with him to the seventh heaven, and they said the same. In every heaven there were Prophets whom he named. I remembered among them: Idrīs in the second, Hārūn in the fourth, another in the fifth whose name I did not preserve, Ibrāhīm in the sixth, and Mūsā in the seventh, due to the virtue of Allah speaking to him. Mūsā said: “My Lord, I did not think that anyone would be raised above me.” Then He took him above that to what none knows except Allah, until he came to Sidrat al-Muntahā, and the Almighty, Lord of Might, drew near and descended until he was at the distance of two bow-lengths or nearer. Allah revealed among what He revealed to him: fifty prayers upon your Ummah every day and night. Then he descended until he reached Mūsā, and Mūsā detained him and said: “O Muḥammad, what covenant did your Lord give you?” He said: “He gave me the covenant of fifty prayers every day and night.” He said: “Your Ummah cannot bear that, so return and let your Lord lighten it for you and for them.” The Prophet ﷺ turned to Jibrīl as though consulting him about that, and Jibrīl indicated to him: “Yes, if you wish.” He took him up to the Almighty, and he said, while still in his place: “My Lord, lighten it for us, for my Ummah cannot bear this.” He removed ten prayers from him. Then he returned to Mūsā, who detained him, and Mūsā continued sending him back to his Lord until they became five prayers. Then Mūsā detained him at the five and said: “O Muḥammad, by Allah, I urged the Children of Israel, my people, regarding something less than this, but they weakened and abandoned it. Your Ummah is weaker in bodies, hearts, bodies, sight, and hearing, so return and let your Lord lighten it for you.” Each time, the Prophet ﷺ would turn to Jibrīl for his counsel, and Jibrīl did not dislike that. He raised him at the fifth, and he said: “My Lord, my Ummah are weak in their bodies, hearts, hearing, and bodies, so lighten it for us.” The Almighty said: “O Muḥammad.” He said: “At Your service and obedience.” He said: “The word is not changed with Me, as I prescribed it for you in the Mother of the Book. Every good deed is multiplied tenfold, so they are fifty in the Mother of the Book, though they are five upon you.” He returned to Mūsā, who said: “What did you do?” He said: “He lightened it for us and gave us tenfold for every good deed.” Mūsā said: “By Allah, I urged the Children of Israel regarding less than that and they abandoned it. Return to your Lord and let Him lighten it for you also.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Mūsā, by Allah, I have become shy before my Lord because of how often I went back to Him.” He said: “Then descend in the name of Allah.” He said: He woke up while he was in al-Masjid al-Ḥarām."],[159,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “When my Lord caused me to ascend, I passed by people who had nails with which they were scratching their faces and chests. I said: ‘Who are these, O Jibrīl?’ He said: ‘These are the ones who ate the flesh of people and attacked their honor.’”"],[160,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “On the night I was taken by night, I passed by people whose lips were being cut with shears of fire. I said: ‘Who are these?’ They said: ‘Preachers from the people of the world who used to command people to righteousness and forget themselves, while they recited the Book. Do they not understand?’”"],[161,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Prophet ﷺ was brought al-Burāq on the night he was taken by night, saddled and bridled for him to ride, but it became difficult for him. Jibrīl said to it: “What makes you do this? By Allah, no one more honored by Allah than him has ever ridden you.” Then it flowed with sweat."],[162,"Abū Hurayrah said: The Prophet ﷺ said: “When I was taken by night, I met Mūsā, peace be upon him, and he was a man — I think he said: tall and lean — with straight hair, as though he were from the men of Shanū’ah. He said: I met ʿĪsā, and the Prophet ﷺ described him: he was of medium height, reddish, as though he had just come out of a dīmās, meaning a bathhouse. He said: I saw Ibrāhīm, may the prayers of Allah be upon him, and I am the child of his who most resembles him. He said: I was brought two vessels, one containing milk and the other containing wine. It was said to me: Take whichever you wish. I took the milk and drank it. It was said to me: You have been guided to the fiṭrah, or you have attained the fiṭrah. If you had taken the wine, your Ummah would have gone astray.”"],[163,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “On the night I was taken by night, I saw Mūsā as a dark-complexioned, tall, sturdy man, as though he were from the men of Shanū’ah. I saw ʿĪsā as a man of medium stature and build, inclined to reddishness and whiteness, with straight hair. And I saw Mālik, the keeper of the Fire, and the Dajjāl, among the signs that Allah showed him.” Allah Most High said: “So do not be in doubt concerning meeting him” [al-Sajdah: 23]."],[164,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: They mentioned the Dajjāl, and someone present said: “It is written between his eyes: disbeliever.” Ibn ʿAbbās said: “I did not hear him say that, but he said: ʿAs for Ibrāhīm, look at your companion. As for Mūsā, he was a dark-complexioned, sturdy man upon a red camel bridled with palm fiber; it is as though I can see him descending into the valley reciting the talbiyah.’”"],[165,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by Wādī al-Azraq and said: “What valley is this?” They said: “This is Wādī al-Azraq.” He said: “It is as though I can see Mūsā, peace be upon him, descending from the mountain pass, raising his voice to Allah with the talbiyah.” Then he came to the mountain pass of Harshā and said: “What mountain pass is this?” They said: “The mountain pass of Harshā.” He said: “It is as though I can see Yūnus ibn Mattā, peace be upon him, upon a red, sturdy she-camel, wearing a wool cloak, the halter of his camel made of palm fiber, while he is reciting the talbiyah.” Ibn Ḥanbal said in his narration: Hushaym said: meaning palm fiber, “while he is reciting the talbiyah.” Thus it appears in the Musnad."],[166,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “When it was the night I was taken by night, and I awoke in Makkah, I became distressed about my affair, and I knew that the people would deny me.” So he sat apart, saddened. He said: The enemy of Allah, Abū Jahl, passed by and came until he sat with him and said mockingly: “Did anything happen?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Yes.” He said: “What?” He said: “I was taken by night tonight.” He said: “To where?” He said: “To Bayt al-Maqdis.” He said: “Then you woke up among us?” He said: “Yes.” He did not show that he was denying him, fearing that he would deny the report if he called his people to him. He said: “What do you think, if I call your people, will you tell them what you told me?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Yes.” He said: “Come, O Banū Kaʿb ibn Lu’ayy!” The gatherings stirred and they came until they sat with the two of them. He said: “Tell your people what you told me.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was taken by night tonight.” They said: “To where?” He said: “To Bayt al-Maqdis.” They said: “Then you woke up among us?” He said: “Yes.” Some clapped, and some placed their hands on their heads, out of amazement at what they claimed was a lie. They said: “Can you describe the mosque to us?” Among the people were those who had traveled to that land and seen the mosque. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I began describing it, and I continued describing it until some of the description became unclear to me.” He said: “Then the mosque was brought while I was looking until it was placed near the house of ʿAqīl, and I described it while looking at it.” He said: “Along with this there was a description that I did not preserve.” He said: The people said: “As for the description, by Allah, he has spoken accurately.”"],[167,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “On the night in which I was taken by night, a pleasant fragrance came upon me. I said: ‘O Jibrīl, what is this pleasant fragrance?’ He said: ‘This is the fragrance of the hairdresser of Pharaoh’s daughter and her children.’ I said: ‘What was her story?’ He said: ‘One day, while she was combing the hair of Pharaoh’s daughter, the comb fell from her hand, and she said: In the name of Allah. Pharaoh’s daughter said to her: My father? She said: No, but my Lord and the Lord of your father is Allah. She said: Shall I inform him of that? She said: Yes. So she informed him, and he summoned her and said: O so-and-so, do you have a lord other than me? She said: Yes, my Lord and your Lord is Allah. He ordered a copper cauldron to be heated, then ordered that she and her children be thrown into it. She said to him: I have a request from you. He said: What is your request? She said: I would like you to gather my bones and the bones of my children in one cloth and bury us. He said: That is your right upon us. He ordered her children to be thrown in before her, one by one, until it came to a nursing infant of hers, and it was as though she hesitated because of him. He said: O my mother, plunge in, for the punishment of this world is easier than the punishment of the Hereafter. So she plunged in.’” Ibn ʿAbbās said: Four infants spoke: ʿĪsā ibn Maryam, peace be upon him; the companion of Jurayj; the witness of Yūsuf; and the son of the hairdresser of Pharaoh’s daughter."],[168,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: The Prophet ﷺ was taken by night to Bayt al-Maqdis, then returned that same night and told them about his journey, the sign of Bayt al-Maqdis, and their camel. Some people said — Ḥasan said: “Are we to believe Muḥammad in what he says?” — and they apostatized as disbelievers, so Allah struck their necks along with Abū Jahl. Abū Jahl said: “Muḥammad frightens us with the tree of Zaqqūm! Bring dates and butter and eat zaqqūm.” He saw the Dajjāl in his actual form, with the vision of the eye, not a dream vision; and he saw ʿĪsā, Mūsā, and Ibrāhīm, may Allah’s prayers be upon them. The Prophet ﷺ was asked about the Dajjāl, and he said: “He is white and bright — Ḥasan said: He said: I saw him as huge-bodied, white and bright — one of his eyes protruding, as if it were a shining star, and the hair of his head like the branches of a tree. I saw ʿĪsā as a young man, white, with curly hair, sharp sight, and a lean body. I saw Mūsā as dark brown, with much hair — Ḥasan said: hair — and a strong build. I looked at Ibrāhīm, and I did not look at any limb of his except that I saw it resembling mine, as though he were your companion. Jibrīl, peace be upon him, said: Greet Mālik with salām, so I greeted him.”"],[169,"Ibn ʿAbbās said regarding the saying of Allah Most High: “And We did not make the vision which We showed you except as a trial for the people” [al-Isrā’: 60]: It was a vision of the eye that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was shown on the night he was taken by night to Bayt al-Maqdis. He said regarding His saying: “And the cursed tree in the Qur’an” [al-Isrā’: 60]: It is the tree of Zaqqūm."],[170,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: On the night the Prophet of Allah ﷺ was taken by night, he entered Paradise and heard a faint sound at one side of it. He said: “O Jibrīl, what is this?” He said: “This is Bilāl the mu’adhdhin.” When the Prophet of Allah ﷺ came to the people, he said: “Bilāl has succeeded; I saw for him such-and-such.” He said: He met Mūsā, peace be upon him, who welcomed him and said: “Welcome to the unlettered Prophet.” He said: “Who is this, O Jibrīl?” He said: “This is Mūsā, peace be upon him.” He continued and met ʿĪsā, who welcomed him. He said: “Who is this, O Jibrīl?” He said: “This is ʿĪsā.” He continued and met a venerable, awe-inspiring old man, who welcomed him and greeted him with salām; all of them greeted him with salām. He said: “Who is this, O Jibrīl?” He said: “This is your father Ibrāhīm.” He then looked into the Fire and saw people eating carcasses. He said: “Who are these, O Jibrīl?” He said: “These are those who eat the flesh of people.” He also saw a reddish, blue-eyed, curly-haired, disheveled man, and when he saw him, he said: “Who is this, O Jibrīl?” He said: “This is the hamstringer of the she-camel.” He said: When the Prophet ﷺ entered al-Masjid al-Aqṣā, he stood to pray, then turned and saw all the Prophets praying with him. When he finished, two cups were brought, one on the right and one on the left. In one was milk and in the other honey. He took the milk and drank from it, and the one who was with the cup said: “You have attained the fiṭrah.”"],[171,"Jābir narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The Prophets were shown to me. Mūsā was a lean, tall man, as though he were from the men of Shanū’ah. I saw ʿĪsā ibn Maryam, peace be upon him, and the person I have seen who most resembled him was ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd. I saw Ibrāhīm, may Allah’s prayers be upon him, and the one I have seen who most resembled him was your companion,” meaning himself. “And I saw Jibrīl, peace be upon him, and the one I have seen who most resembled him was Diḥyah.” In another narration: “Diḥyah ibn Khalīfah.”"],[172,"Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh said: I came upon Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān while he was narrating about the night Muḥammad ﷺ was taken by night, and he was saying: “Then I set out — or we set out — until we came to Bayt al-Maqdis.” But they did not enter it. He said: I said: “Rather, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered it that night and prayed in it.” He said: “What is your name, O bald one? I recognize your face but do not know your name.” I said: “I am Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh.” He said: “What is your evidence that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in it that night?” I said: “The Qur’an informs me of that.” He said: “Whoever speaks with the Qur’an has prevailed. Recite.” So I recited: “Glory be to the One who took His servant by night from al-Masjid al-Ḥarām…” [al-Isrā’: 1]. He said: “I did not find in it that he prayed there.” He said: “O bald one, do you find that he prayed there?” I said: “No.” He said: “By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not pray there that night. Had he prayed there, prayer there would have been prescribed upon you just as prayer at the Ancient House was prescribed upon you. By Allah, they did not leave al-Burāq until the gates of heaven were opened for them, and they saw Paradise, the Fire, and the promise of the Hereafter altogether, then they returned along the same route by which they had begun.” He said: Then he laughed until I saw his molars. He said: “They narrate that he tied it — so that it would flee from him?! Rather, the Knower of the unseen and the witnessed had subjected it to him.” He said: I said: “Abū ʿAbdullah, what sort of animal was al-Burāq?” He said: “A tall white animal; its stride was as far as the eye could see.”"],[173,"As for what Sayyidunā Ḥudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, said, that had the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed there, then it would have been obligatory upon his Ummah as well to come to that place and pray there, just as the Prophet ﷺ did, there is no proof in it for Sayyidunā Ḥudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him. This is because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to many places and prayed there, yet he did not make it obligatory upon us to go to those places and pray there. Rather, Sayyidunā ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, forbade intending those places and praying there."],[174,"Buraydah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “When we reached Bayt al-Maqdis, Jibrīl indicated with his finger, pierced the stone with it, and tied al-Burāq to it.”"],[175,"Shaddād ibn Aws said: We said: “O Messenger of Allah, how were you taken by night?” He said: “I prayed the night prayer in Makkah after darkness had set in, and Jibrīl, peace be upon him, came to me with a white animal, larger than a donkey and smaller than a mule. He said: ‘Ride.’ It became difficult for me, so he twisted its ear, then carried me upon it. It set off swiftly with us, its hoof landing wherever its sight reached, until we reached a land of palm trees. He made me dismount and said: ‘Pray.’ So I prayed. Then we rode, and he said: ‘Do you know where you prayed?’ I said: ‘Allah knows best.’ He said: ‘You prayed in Yathrib; you prayed in Ṭaybah.’ It set off swiftly with us, its hoof landing wherever its sight reached. Then we reached a land, and he said: ‘Dismount.’ I dismounted, then he said: ‘Pray.’ So I prayed. Then we rode, and he said: ‘Do you know where you prayed?’ I said: ‘Allah knows best.’ He said: ‘You prayed in Madyan; you prayed near the tree of Mūsā, peace be upon him.’ Then it set off swiftly with us, its hoof landing wherever its sight reached. Then we reached a land in which palaces appeared to us. He said: ‘Dismount.’ I dismounted, and he said: ‘Pray.’ So I prayed. Then we rode. He said: ‘Do you know where you prayed?’ I said: ‘Allah knows best.’ He said: ‘You prayed in Bethlehem, where ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, the Messiah son of Maryam, was born.’ Then he took me until we entered the city from its southern gate. He came to the qiblah of the mosque and tied his animal there. We entered the mosque through a gate where the sun and moon incline. I prayed in the mosque wherever Allah willed. I was overcome by extreme thirst, and two vessels were brought to me: one containing milk and the other honey. Both were sent to me. I considered between them, then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, guided me, so I took the milk and drank until I struck my forehead with it. Before me was an old man reclining on his cushion, and he said: ‘Your companion has taken the fiṭrah; indeed, he is guided.’ Then he took me until we came to the valley in the city, and behold, Hell was being unveiled like carpets. I said: ‘O Messenger of Allah, how did you find it?’ He said: ‘Like a hot, black spring.’ Then he returned with me, and we passed by a caravan of Quraysh at such-and-such place. They had lost a camel, and so-and-so had gathered it. I greeted them, and some of them said: ‘This is the voice of Muḥammad.’ Then I came to my Companions before dawn in Makkah, and Abū Bakr came to me and said: ‘O Messenger of Allah, where were you tonight? I looked for you in your place.’ He said: ‘You knew that I came to Bayt al-Maqdis tonight.’ He said: ‘O Messenger of Allah, it is a month’s journey, so describe it to me.’ He said: A path was opened for me, as though I were looking into it; he did not ask me about anything except that I informed him of it. Abū Bakr said: ‘I bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah.’ The polytheists said: ‘Look at Ibn Abī Kabshah; he claims that he came to Bayt al-Maqdis tonight.’ He said: ‘Among the signs of what I say to you is that I passed by a caravan of yours at such-and-such place. They had lost a camel, and so-and-so gathered it. Their route is that they will stop at such-and-such, then such-and-such, and they will come to you on such-and-such day, led by a dark camel carrying a black covering and two black sacks.’ When that day came, the people looked out waiting, until it was near midday, and then the caravan approached, led by that camel which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had described.”"],[176,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said: When the Prophet ﷺ was taken by night to al-Masjid al-Aqṣā, the people began speaking about it in the morning. Some of those who had believed in him and affirmed him apostatized, and they went with that to Abū Bakr. They said: “What do you think of your companion? He claims that he was taken by night to Bayt al-Maqdis.” He said: “Did he say that?” They said: “Yes.” He said: “If he said that, then he has spoken the truth.” They said: “Do you believe him that he went tonight to Bayt al-Maqdis and returned before morning?” He said: “Yes, I believe him in what is farther than that. I believe him regarding news from the heaven in the morning or evening.” For that reason Abū Bakr was called al-Ṣiddīq."],[177,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullah heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “When Quraysh denied me, I stood in al-Ḥijr, and Allah made Bayt al-Maqdis clear to me, so I began informing them of its signs while I was looking at it.”"],[178,"Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I saw myself in al-Ḥijr while Quraysh were asking me about my night journey. They asked me about things in Bayt al-Maqdis that I had not memorized, and I became distressed with a distress unlike any I had ever felt. Then Allah raised it up for me so that I could look at it, and they did not ask me about anything except that I informed them of it. I also saw myself among a group of Prophets. Mūsā was standing and praying; he was a lean, sturdy man, as though he were from the men of Shanū’ah. ʿĪsā ibn Maryam, peace be upon him, was standing and praying; the person who most resembled him was ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd al-Thaqafī. Ibrāhīm, peace be upon him, was standing and praying; the one who most resembled him was your companion,” meaning himself. “Then the prayer time came, and I led them in prayer. When I finished the prayer, someone said: ‘O Muḥammad, this is Mālik, the keeper of the Fire, so greet him with salām.’ I turned toward him, and he began by greeting me with salām.”"],[179,"ʿAbdullah said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was taken by night, he was brought to Sidrat al-Muntahā. It is in the sixth heaven. To it ends whatever ascends from the earth and is taken from it, and to it ends whatever descends from above it and is taken from it. He said regarding: “When there covered the lote tree that which covered it” [al-Najm: 16]: “Moths of gold.” He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was given three things: he was given the five prayers, he was given the concluding verses of Sūrat al-Baqarah, and the major destructive sins were forgiven for those of his Ummah who did not associate anything with Allah."],[180,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Sidrat al-Muntahā was raised for me in the seventh heaven. Its fruits were like the jars of Hajar, and its leaves were like the ears of elephants. From its trunk emerge two outward rivers and two inward rivers. I said: ‘O Jibrīl, what are these?’ He said: ʿAs for the inward rivers, they are in Paradise, and as for the outward rivers, they are the Nile and the Euphrates.’”"],[181,"Imam al-Qurṭubī said in al-Mufhim (1/394): In the hadith of Sayyidunā Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, there is the indication that Sidrat al-Muntahā is in the seventh heaven or above it, because the hadith mentions: Then I was taken to Sidrat al-Muntahā, after the gate of the seventh heaven had been opened and the Prophet ﷺ had entered it.\nAnd in the hadith of Sayyidunā ʿAbdullāh, may Allah be pleased with him, it is stated that Sidrat al-Muntahā is in the sixth heaven. There is no doubt that there is a clear contradiction between these two narrations. But the narration of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, is more authentic, and this is the view of the majority.\nAccording to the description given of Sidrat al-Muntahā, it is fitting that it is the place where the knowledge of every angel brought near and every sent prophet comes to an end, as Sayyidunā Kaʿb, may Allah be pleased with him, said; and he also said that whatever lies beyond it is the unseen, which only Allah knows. Likewise, al-Khalīl ibn Aḥmad said that it is the place where the souls of the martyrs reach.\nSayyidunā Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, said that it is on the right side of the Throne. Furthermore, the hadith of Sayyidunā Anas is marfūʿ, whereas the hadith of ʿAbdullāh, may Allah be pleased with him, is mawqūf, limited to his own statement, and the marfūʿ hadith is given preference. End of quote.\nImam al-Nawawī tried to reconcile these two hadiths, and according to them Ibn Ḥajar also said the same, namely that its root is in the sixth heaven and its branch is in the seventh heaven, but there is room for objection in this."],[182,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullah said: The Prophet ﷺ used to present himself to the people at the standing place, saying: “Is there any man who will take me to his people? For Quraysh have prevented me from conveying the speech of my Lord.” A man from Hamdān came to him, and he said: “Who are you from?” The man said: “From Hamdān.” He said: “Do your people have strength and protection?” He said: “Yes.” Then the man feared that his people would break their pledge to him, so he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “I will go to them and inform them, then come to you next year.” He said: “Yes.” So he departed, and the delegation of the Anṣār came in Rajab."],[183,"Jābir said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained in Makkah for ten years, following the people in their camps at ʿUkāẓ and Majannah, and during the seasons at Minā, saying: “Who will shelter me? Who will support me so that I may convey the message of my Lord — and he will have Paradise?” A man would even come from Yemen or from Egypt — so he said — and his people would come to him and say: “Beware of the young man of Quraysh; do not let him tempt you.” He would walk among their men while they pointed at him with their fingers, until Allah sent us to him from Yathrib. We sheltered him and believed him. A man from us would go out, believe in him, and he would recite the Qur’an to him; then he would return to his family and they would embrace Islam because of his Islam, until there remained no house among the houses of the Anṣār except that in it was a group of Muslims openly manifesting Islam. Then they all consulted together, and we said: “How long will we leave the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to be driven through the mountains of Makkah and to live in fear?” So seventy men from us traveled to him until they came to him during the season, and we made an appointment with him at the mountain pass of al-ʿAqabah. We gathered with him, one man and two men at a time, until we all came together. We said: “O Messenger of Allah, upon what shall we pledge allegiance to you?” He said: “You shall pledge allegiance to me upon hearing and obeying in energy and reluctance, spending in hardship and ease, commanding what is right and forbidding what is wrong, speaking for Allah without fearing the blame of any blamer, and supporting me by protecting me, when I come to you, from what you protect yourselves, your wives, and your children from — and you will have Paradise.” He said: We stood to him and pledged allegiance. Asʿad ibn Zurārah, one of the youngest of them, took his hand and said: “Slowly, O people of Yathrib. We did not strike the livers of camels except knowing that he is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Taking him out today means separating from all the Arabs, the killing of your best men, and that swords will bite you. If you are a people who will be patient upon that, then your reward is with Allah. But if you are a people who fear cowardice from yourselves, then make that clear now, for that is more excusable for you before Allah.” They said: “Move away from us, O Asʿad. By Allah, we will never abandon this pledge, nor will we ever ask to be released from it.” He said: We stood to him and pledged allegiance. He took conditions from us and stipulated them, and in return he gave us Paradise."],[184,"Ashʿath said: An elderly man from Banū Mālik ibn Kinānah narrated to me: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the market of Dhū al-Majāz, moving through it and saying: “O people, say: There is no god but Allah, and you will succeed.” He said: Abū Jahl was throwing dust upon him and saying: “O people, do not let this man deceive you away from your religion. He only wants you to leave your gods and leave al-Lāt and al-ʿUzzā.” He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not turn toward him. He said: We said: “Describe the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to us.” He said: He was wearing two red cloaks, of medium stature, with much flesh, handsome in face, intensely black hair, very white, and with full hair."],[185,"Rabīʿah ibn ʿIbād al-Dīlī said: I saw Abū Lahab at ʿUkāẓ following the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and saying: “O people, this man has gone astray, so do not let him lead you away from the gods of your fathers.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would move away from him, while he followed behind him, and we were following as boys. It is as though I can still see him: cross-eyed, with two braided locks, the whitest and most handsome of people."],[186,"Rabīʿah ibn ʿIbād al-Dīlī, who had lived in the pre-Islamic period and then embraced Islam, said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with my own eyes in the market of Dhū al-Majāz, saying: “O people, say: There is no god but Allah, and you will succeed,” while entering its pathways, and the people gathered around him. I did not see anyone saying anything while he did not remain silent; he kept saying: “O people, say: There is no god but Allah, and you will succeed.” Behind him was a cross-eyed man with a bright face and two braided locks, saying: “He is a Ṣābi’ and a liar.” I said: “Who is this?” They said: “Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullah, and he is mentioning prophethood.” I said: “Who is this man who is calling him a liar?” They said: “His uncle, Abū Lahab.” I said: “You were young that day.” He said: “No, by Allah, I understood that day.”"],[187,"Rabīʿah ibn ʿIbād narrated from his father, who said: I was a young boy with my father at Minā, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was saying: “O Banū so-and-so, I am the Messenger of Allah to you. He commands you to worship Allah and not associate anything with Him, to renounce those rivals that you worship besides Him, to shelter me, to believe me, and to protect me until I make clear from Allah what He has sent me with.” He said: Behind him was a cross-eyed, bright-faced man with two braided locks, wearing an Adeni garment. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished his words and what he was calling to, that man would say: “O Banū so-and-so, this man only calls you to strip al-Lāt and al-ʿUzzā from your necks, and your allies from the jinn of Banū Mālik ibn Aqyas, for what he has brought of innovation and misguidance. So do not obey him and do not listen to him.” He said: I said to my father: “My father, who is this man who follows him and refutes what he says?” He said: “This is his uncle, ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, Abū Lahab.”"],[188,"Ṭāriq ibn ʿAbdullah al-Muḥāribī said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the market of Dhū al-Majāz wearing a red garment and saying: “O people, say: There is no god but Allah, and you will succeed.” A man was following him, throwing stones at him, having bloodied his heels and ankles, while saying: “O people, do not obey him, for he is a liar.” I said: “Who is this?” It was said: “This is the young man of Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.” I said: “Who is this man following him and throwing stones at him?” He said: “This is ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, Abū Lahab.”"],[189,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The Day of Buʿāth was a day that Allah had brought forward for His Messenger ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived when their leaders had become divided, their noblemen had been killed, and they had been wounded. Allah brought it forward for His Messenger ﷺ as a cause for their entering Islam."],[190,"Maḥmūd ibn Labīd, the brother of Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal, said: When Abū al-Ḥaysar Anas ibn Rāfiʿ came to Makkah with young men from Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal, among them Iyās ibn Muʿādh, seeking an alliance from Quraysh against their people from al-Khazraj, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard of them, came to them, and sat with them. He said to them: “Would you like something better than what you came for?” They said: “What is that?” He said: “I am the Messenger of Allah. He sent me to the servants, calling them to worship Allah and not associate anything with Him, and a Book has been revealed to me.” Then he mentioned Islam and recited the Qur’an to them. Iyās ibn Muʿādh, who was a young boy, said: “O people, by Allah, this is better than what you came for.” Abū al-Ḥaysar Anas ibn Rāfiʿ took a handful of gravel from the plain and struck the face of Iyās ibn Muʿādh with it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then rose from them, and they returned to Medina. Then the battle of Buʿāth occurred between al-Aws and al-Khazraj. He said: Soon after, Iyās ibn Muʿādh died. Maḥmūd ibn Labīd said: Those of my people who were present at his death informed me that they continued hearing him declaring Allah’s oneness, magnifying Him, praising Him, and glorifying Him until he died. They had no doubt that he died as a Muslim, for he had felt Islam enter his heart in that gathering when he heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what he heard."],[191,"Ibn Isḥāq said: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah narrated to me from elders of his people, who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met them, he said to them: “Who are you?” They said: “A group from al-Khazraj.” He said: “From the allies of the Jews?” They said: “Yes.” He said: “Will you not sit so that I may speak to you?” They said: “Yes.” They sat with him, and he called them to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, presented Islam to them, and recited the Qur’an to them. He said: Among what Allah had prepared for them in Islam was that the Jews lived with them in their land, and they were people of Scripture and knowledge, while they were people of shirk and idol worship. They had fought them in their land, and whenever something occurred between them, the Jews would say to them: “A Prophet is now being sent; his time has drawn near. We will follow him and kill you with him as ʿĀd and Iram were killed.” When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke to those men and called them to Allah, some of them said to others: “O people, by Allah, you know that he is the Prophet with whom the Jews have threatened you, so do not let them precede you to him.” They responded to what he called them to, believed him, accepted from him what he presented of Islam, and said: “We have left our people, and no people have between them the enmity and evil that they have. Perhaps Allah will unite them through you. We will go to them, call them to your matter, and present to them what we have responded to from this religion. If Allah unites them upon it, then no man will be dearer than you.” Then they departed from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, returning to their land, having believed and affirmed."],[192,"These were the six persons whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met at al-ʿAqabah (Minā), and all of them belonged to the tribe of Khazraj:\nAsʿad ibn Zurārah (Abū Umāmah), ʿAwf ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Rifāʿah\nRāfiʿ ibn Mālik ibn ʿAjlān, Quṭbah ibn ʿĀmir ibn Ḥadīdah\nʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir ibn Nābī, Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Riʾāb\nWhen these men returned to Madinah, they mentioned the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to their people and invited them to Islam, until Islam spread in Madinah, and there remained no house in which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was not mentioned."],[193,"ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, who had witnessed Badr and was one of the naqībs on the night of al-ʿAqabah, narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said while a group of his Companions were around him: “Pledge allegiance to me that you will not associate anything with Allah, that you will not steal, that you will not commit fornication, that you will not kill your children, that you will not bring a slander you fabricate between your hands and feet, and that you will not disobey in what is right. Whoever among you fulfills this, his reward is with Allah. Whoever commits any of that and is punished for it in this world, it is an expiation for him. Whoever commits any of that and Allah conceals him, then his matter is with Allah: if He wills, He will pardon him, and if He wills, He will punish him.” So we pledged allegiance to him upon that."],[194,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained for ten years following the pilgrims in their camps, during the season, at Majannah and ʿUkāẓ, and in their camps at Minā, saying: “Who will shelter me? Who will support me so that I may convey the messages of my Lord — and he will have Paradise?” He found no one to support him and shelter him. A man would even travel from Muḍar or from Yemen to his relative, and his people would come to him and say: “Beware of the young man of Quraysh; do not let him tempt you.” He would walk among their camps calling them to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, while they pointed at him with their fingers, until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent us to him from Yathrib. A man would come to him, believe in him, and he would recite the Qur’an to him; then he would return to his family, and they would embrace Islam because of his Islam, until there remained no house among the houses of Yathrib except that in it was a group of Muslims openly manifesting Islam. Then Allah sent us, and we consulted together, seventy men from us gathered, and we said: “How long will we leave the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to be driven through the mountains of Makkah and to live in fear?” We traveled until we came to him during the season and arranged to meet him at the mountain pass of al-ʿAqabah. His uncle al-ʿAbbās said: “My nephew, I do not know who these people are who have come to you, and I have knowledge of the people of Yathrib.” We gathered with him, one man and two men at a time. When al-ʿAbbās looked at our faces, he said: “These are people I do not know; these are young men.” We said: “O Messenger of Allah, upon what shall we pledge allegiance to you?” He said: “You shall pledge allegiance to me upon hearing and obeying in energy and reluctance, upon spending in hardship and ease, upon commanding what is right and forbidding what is wrong, upon speaking for Allah without the blame of any blamer taking hold of you, and upon supporting me when I come to Yathrib by protecting me from what you protect yourselves, your wives, and your children from — and you will have Paradise.” We stood to pledge allegiance to him, and Asʿad ibn Zurārah, the youngest of the seventy, took his hand and said: “Slowly, O people of Yathrib. We did not strike the livers of the riding camels to come to him except knowing that he is the Messenger of Allah. Taking him out today means separating from all the Arabs, the killing of your best men, and that swords will bite you. If you are a people who will be patient upon swords when they touch you, upon the killing of your best men, and upon separating from all the Arabs, then take him, and your reward is with Allah. But if you fear something within yourselves, then leave him, for that is more excusable before Allah.” They said: “O Asʿad ibn Zurārah, remove your hand from us. By Allah, we will not abandon this pledge, nor will we ask to be released from it.” So we stood to him one man at a time. He took from us according to the condition of al-ʿAbbās, and in exchange gave us Paradise."],[195,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullah said: My two maternal uncles attended al-ʿAqabah on my behalf. Abū ʿAbdullah — al-Bukhārī — said: Ibn ʿUyaynah said: One of them was al-Barā’ ibn Maʿrūr."],[196,"ʿAbdullah ibn Kaʿb, who used to guide Kaʿb when he became blind, said: I heard Kaʿb ibn Mālik narrating at length about when he remained behind from the Prophet ﷺ during the expedition of Tabūk. Ibn Bukayr said in his narration: “I indeed witnessed with the Prophet ﷺ the night of al-ʿAqabah, when we pledged ourselves upon Islam, and I would not prefer to have witnessed Badr instead of it, even though Badr is more remembered among the people than it.”"],[197,"Kaʿb ibn Mālik said: We set out among the pilgrims of our people from the polytheists, though we had prayed and gained understanding. With us was al-Barā’ ibn Maʿrūr, our chief and elder. When we set out on our journey and departed from Medina, al-Barā’ said: “O people, I have formed an opinion, and by Allah I do not know whether you will agree with me or not.” We said: “What is it?” He said: “I think that I should not place this structure behind my back,” meaning the Kaʿbah, “and that I should pray toward it.” We said: “By Allah, we have not heard that our Prophet ﷺ prays except toward Syria, and we do not wish to oppose him.” He said: “I will pray toward it.” We said to him: “But we will not do so.” Whenever prayer time came, we prayed toward Syria, while he prayed toward the Kaʿbah, until we arrived in Makkah. We had criticized him for what he did, but he refused except to persist in that. When we arrived in Makkah, he said to me: “My nephew, come with us to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ so that I may ask him about what I did on this journey of mine, for something has entered my heart because I saw you disagreeing with me concerning it.” We went out asking about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we did not know him and had not seen him before. We met a man from the people of Makkah and asked him about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: “Do you know him?” We said: “No.” He said: “Do you know al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, his uncle?” We said: “Yes.” We used to know al-ʿAbbās, for he often came to us as a merchant. He said: “When you enter the mosque, he is the man sitting with al-ʿAbbās.” We entered the mosque, and there was al-ʿAbbās sitting, with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sitting with him. We greeted them with salām and then sat with him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to al-ʿAbbās: “Do you know these two men, Abū al-Faḍl?” He said: “Yes. This is al-Barā’ ibn Maʿrūr, the chief of his people, and this is Kaʿb ibn Mālik.” He said: By Allah, I will never forget the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: “The poet?” He said: “Yes.” Al-Barā’ ibn Maʿrūr then said to him: “O Prophet of Allah, I set out on this journey after Allah Most High had guided me to Islam. I thought that I should not place this structure behind my back, so I prayed toward it. My companions disagreed with me about that, until something entered my heart about it. What do you think, O Messenger of Allah?” He said: “You had been upon a qiblah, if only you had remained patient upon it.” So al-Barā’ returned to the qiblah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and prayed with us toward Syria. His family claims that he prayed toward the Kaʿbah until he died, but that is not as they say; we know him better than they do. Kaʿb ibn Mālik said: Then we went out for Ḥajj, and we arranged to meet the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at al-ʿAqabah in the middle of the Days of Tashrīq. When we finished the Ḥajj, on the night for which we had arranged to meet the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, we had with us ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥarām, Abū Jābir, one of our leaders and nobles. We took him aside, while we were concealing our affair from the polytheists among our people who were with us. We spoke to him and said: “O Abū Jābir, you are one of our leaders and one of our nobles, and we dislike for you to remain in your present state and become fuel for the Fire tomorrow.” Then we called him to Islam and informed him of our appointment with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at al-ʿAqabah. He embraced Islam, witnessed al-ʿAqabah with us, and became a naqīb. He said: We slept that night with our people in our camps. When a third of the night had passed, we left our camps for our appointment with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, slipping away quietly like sandgrouse, concealing ourselves, until we gathered in the mountain pass at al-ʿAqabah. We were seventy-three men, and with us were two women: Nusaybah bint Kaʿb, Umm ʿUmārah, one of the women of Banū Māzin ibn al-Najjār; and Asmā’ bint ʿAmr ibn ʿAdī ibn Nābī, one of the women of Banū Salamah, who was Umm Manīʿ.\nHe said: We gathered in the mountain pass waiting for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until he came to us, accompanied by al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib. At that time, al-ʿAbbās was still upon the religion of his people, but he wished to attend the affair of his nephew and secure assurance for him. When he sat, the first to speak was al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib. He said: “O people of al-Khazraj” — he said: the Arabs used to refer to this group of the Anṣār as al-Khazraj, both their Khazraj and their Aws — “Muḥammad is from us, as you know. We have protected him from our people, those who share our view concerning him. He is in honor among his people and protection in his land. Yet he has refused except to join you and attach himself to you. If you believe that you will fulfill for him what you have called him to, and protect him from those who oppose him, then you and what you have undertaken are as you are. But if you believe that you will surrender him and abandon him after he has gone out to you, then leave him now, for he is in honor and protection among his people and in his land.” We said to him: “We have heard what you said. Speak, O Messenger of Allah, and take for yourself and your Lord whatever you wish.”\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke, recited the Qur’an, called to Allah, encouraged Islam, then said: “I pledge with you that you will protect me from what you protect your women and children from.” Al-Barā’ ibn Maʿrūr took his hand and said: “Yes, by the One who sent you with the truth as a Prophet, we will surely protect you from what we protect our families from. So accept our pledge, O Messenger of Allah, for by Allah, we are sons of wars and people of armor; we inherited it elder from elder.”\nThen Abū al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihān interrupted while al-Barā’ was speaking to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, between us and men there are ties, and we will be cutting them,” meaning the Jews. “Do you think that if we do this and then Allah gives you victory, you will return to your people and leave us?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled, then said: “Rather, blood is blood, and destruction is destruction. I am from you and you are from me. I will fight those you fight and make peace with those you make peace with.”\nKaʿb said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Bring forth from among you twelve naqībs, so that they may be responsible over their people regarding what is among them.” So they brought forth twelve naqībs: nine from al-Khazraj and three from al-Aws."],[198,"§The names of the twelve naqībs (chiefs):\nThese were the twelve naqībs (leaders) who were appointed as representatives of the Muslims at the time of the pledge of the Second ʿAqabah:\nNine (9) persons from the tribe of Khazraj:\n1- Abū Umāmah Asʿad ibn Zurārah 2- Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn ʿAmr\n3- ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah ibn Thaʿlabah 4- Rāfiʿ ibn Mālik ibn ʿAjlān\n5- al-Barāʾ ibn Maʿrūr 6- ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥarām\n7- ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit ibn Qays 8- Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah ibn Dulaym\n9- al-Mundhir ibn ʿAmr ibn Khunays\nThree (3) persons from the tribe of Aws:\n10- Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr ibn Simāk 11- Saʿd ibn Khaythamah ibn al-Ḥārith 12- Rifāʿah ibn ʿAbd al-Mundhir ibn Zubayr\nThese twelve chiefs helped in strengthening Islam in Madinah, guiding the Muslims, and spreading the religion."],[199,"Abū Mūsā narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: “I saw in a dream that I was migrating from Makkah to a land containing palm trees, and my mind first went to al-Yamāmah or Hajar, but it was Medina, Yathrib. In this dream of mine, I saw that I shook a sword and its front part broke; that represented what befell the believers on the Day of Uḥud. Then I shook it again and it returned better than it had been; that represented what Allah brought of victory and the gathering of the believers. I also saw cows in it — and Allah is good — and they were the group of believers on the Day of Uḥud; and the good was what Allah brought afterward of good and the reward of truthfulness that Allah gave us after the Day of Badr.”"],[200,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to the Muslims: “I have been shown the land of your migration: a place with palm trees between two lava fields,” meaning the two ḥarrahs. So those who migrated set out toward Medina, and most of those who had migrated to Abyssinia returned to Medina."],[201,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited me during the Farewell Ḥajj because of an illness from which I had nearly died. He mentioned the story of the will, then I said: “O Messenger of Allah, will I be left behind after my Companions?” He said: “You will not be left behind and then do any deed seeking the Face of Allah except that you will increase thereby in rank and elevation. Perhaps you will be left behind so that some people benefit through you and others are harmed through you. O Allah, complete the migration of my Companions and do not turn them back on their heels. But the unfortunate one is Saʿd ibn Khawlah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ lamented for him because he died in Makkah."],[202,"ʿĀ’ishah said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Medina, Abū Bakr and Bilāl fell ill with fever. She said: I entered upon them and said: “My father, how do you feel? Bilāl, how do you feel?” When the fever seized Abū Bakr, he would say: Every person wakes in the morning among his family, while death is closer than the strap of his sandal. And when Bilāl’s fever subsided, he would raise his voice and say: Would that I knew whether I shall spend a night in a valley with idhkhir and jalīl around me; and whether one day I shall return to the waters of Majannah, and whether Shāmah and Ṭafīl will appear to me. ʿĀ’ishah said: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him, so he said: “O Allah, make Medina beloved to us as we love Makkah, or even more. Make it healthy, bless us in its ṣāʿ and its mudd, and transfer its fever and place it in al-Juḥfah.”"],[203,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to the Muslims, while he was in Makkah at that time: “I have been shown the land of your migration. I was shown a salty land with palm trees between two lava fields,” meaning the two ḥarrahs. So whoever migrated set out toward Medina after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned that, and some of those who had migrated to Abyssinia returned to Medina. Abū Bakr prepared to migrate, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: “Wait calmly, for I hope that permission will be granted to me.” Abū Bakr said: “Do you hope for that, may my father be sacrificed for you?” He said: “Yes.” So Abū Bakr held himself back for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ so that he could accompany him, and he fed two riding camels that he had with him on the leaves of the samur tree for four months."],[204,"al-Barā’ ibn ʿĀzib said: The first to come to us were Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr and Ibn Umm Maktūm. Then ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir and Bilāl, may Allah be pleased with them, came to us."],[205,"al-Barā’ ibn ʿĀzib said: The first to come to us were Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr and Ibn Umm Maktūm, and they used to teach the people Qur’an. Then Bilāl, Saʿd, and ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir came. Then ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb came with twenty of the Prophet’s Companions. Then the Prophet ﷺ came. I never saw the people of Medina rejoice over anything as they rejoiced over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, until even the slave girls were saying: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has arrived.” He did not arrive until I had recited, “Glorify the Name of your Lord, the Most High” [al-Aʿlā: 1], among sūrahs from the Mufaṣṣal."],[206,"Umm Salamah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “No Muslim is afflicted by a calamity and then says, ‘Indeed we belong to Allah and indeed to Him we return. O Allah, reward me in my calamity and replace it for me with something better than it,’ except that Allah will replace it for him with something better than it.” She said: When Abū Salamah died, I said: “Which Muslim is better than Abū Salamah? The first household to migrate to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” Then I said it, and Allah replaced him for me with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[207,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb said: When we wanted to migrate to Medina, I, ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah, and Hishām ibn al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wā’il al-Sahmī agreed to meet at al-Tanāḍub, near the pool of Banū Ghifār above Sarif. We said: “Whoever does not arrive there by morning has been detained, so his two companions should go on.” He said: In the morning, I and ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah were at al-Tanāḍub, while Hishām had been detained from us and was put to trial, and he succumbed to the trial. When we arrived in Medina, we stayed among Banū ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf in Qubā’. Abū Jahl ibn Hishām and al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām went out to ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah — he was their cousin and their brother through their mother — until they came to us in Medina, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was still in Makkah. They spoke to him and said: “Your mother has vowed that no comb will touch her head until she sees you, and that she will not shade herself from the sun until she sees you.” So he felt pity for her. I said to him: “O ʿAyyāsh, by Allah, these people only want to tempt you away from your religion, so beware of them. By Allah, if lice were to harm your mother, she would comb her hair; and if the heat of Makkah became severe upon her, she would seek shade.” He said: “I will fulfill my mother’s oath, and I have wealth there which I will take.” I said: “By Allah, you know that I am among the wealthiest of Quraysh, so you may have half my wealth, but do not go with them.” But he refused except to go out with them. When he refused except to do that, I said to him: “Since you have done what you have done, take this she-camel of mine. It is a noble, docile camel. Stay on its back, and if anything suspicious from the people alarms you, escape on it.” He set out on it with them, until they were on part of the road. Abū Jahl said to him: “My nephew, by Allah, I find my camel too rough. Will you let me ride behind you on your camel?” He said: “Yes.” He made it kneel, and they both made their mounts kneel so he could transfer to it. When they were all on the ground, they attacked him, bound him tightly, tied him up, and brought him into Makkah, where they put him to trial and he succumbed to the trial. He said: We used to say: “Allah will not accept from anyone who has been put to trial any compensation, ransom, or repentance; they were people who knew Allah, then returned to disbelief because of an affliction that struck them.” They used to say that about themselves as well. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Medina, Allah Most High revealed concerning them, and concerning what we said and what they said about themselves: “Say: O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, do not despair of the mercy of Allah. Indeed, Allah forgives all sins. Indeed, He is the Forgiving, the Merciful. And turn in repentance to your Lord and submit to Him before the punishment comes to you, then you will not be helped. And follow the best of what has been revealed to you from your Lord before the punishment comes upon you suddenly while you do not perceive” [al-Zumar: 53–55]. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb said: I wrote it with my own hand on a sheet and sent it to Hishām ibn al-ʿĀṣ. Hishām ibn al-ʿĀṣ said: When it reached me, I began reading it at Dhū Ṭuwā, going up and down with it and not understanding it, until I said: “O Allah, make me understand it.” He said: Allah Most High cast into my heart that it had been revealed concerning us, and concerning what we had been saying about ourselves and what was being said about us. He said: I returned to my camel, mounted it, and caught up with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was in Medina."],[208,"ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAbbās informed him that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf returned to his family while he was at Minā during the last Ḥajj that ʿUmar performed, and he found me. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: I said: “O Commander of the Believers, the pilgrimage season gathers the rabble and the common masses. I think you should wait until you arrive in Medina, for it is the land of migration and the Sunnah, and you will be with the people of understanding, the nobles, and those of sound judgment.” ʿUmar said: “I will surely stand in the first place where I stand in Medina.”"],[209,"Allah the Exalted said: ﴿وَ قُلْ رَّبِّ اَدْخِلْنِيْ مُدْخَلَ صِدْقٍ وَّ اَخْرِجْنِيْ مُخْرَجَ صِدْقٍ وَّ اجْعَلْ لِّيْ مِنْ لَّدُنْكَ سُلْطٰنًا نَّصِيْرًا۰۰۸۰﴾ [al-Isrāʾ: 80]"],[210,"Allah the Exalted said: (And pray:) My Lord, cause me to enter with a truthful entrance, and cause me to exit with a truthful exit, and grant me from Yourself a supporting authority. [al-Isrāʾ: 80]\nAllah the Exalted instructed the Prophet ﷺ to make this supplication so that the way would open for him ﷺ to be delivered quickly from the present circumstances and to migrate in the best manner. Allah accepted this supplication and granted permission to migrate to Madinah."],[211,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: The Prophet ﷺ was sent at the age of forty. He remained in Makkah for thirteen years receiving revelation, then he was commanded to migrate. He then lived for ten years after migration, and died at the age of sixty-three."],[212,"ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAdī ibn al-Ḥamrā’ said that he heard the Prophet ﷺ while he was standing at al-Ḥazwarah in the market of Makkah: “By Allah, you are the best land of Allah and the most beloved land of Allah to Allah, Mighty and Majestic. Were it not that I was expelled from you, I would not have left.”"],[213,"ʿĀ’ishah said: Saʿd said: “O Allah, You know that there is no one whom I would love more to fight in Your cause than a people who denied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and expelled him. O Allah, I think that You have ended the war between us and them.”"],[214,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said: I never knew my parents except that they followed the religion, and not a day passed except that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would come to us at both ends of the day: morning and evening. When the Muslims were put to trial, Abū Bakr set out as a migrant toward Abyssinia, until he reached Bark al-Ghimād. There he met Ibn al-Dughnah, the chief of al-Qārah, who said: “Where are you going, Abū Bakr?” Abū Bakr said: “My people have driven me out, so I want to travel through the land and worship my Lord.” Ibn al-Dughnah said: “A man like you, Abū Bakr, should neither leave nor be driven out. You earn for the one who has nothing, maintain ties of kinship, carry the burden of the weak, host guests, and help in calamities of truth. I will grant you protection. Return and worship your Lord in your own town.” So he returned, and Ibn al-Dughnah traveled back with him. That evening Ibn al-Dughnah went around among the nobles of Quraysh and said to them: “A man like Abū Bakr should neither leave nor be driven out. Will you drive out a man who earns for the one who has nothing, maintains ties of kinship, carries the burden of the weak, hosts guests, and helps in calamities of truth?” Quraysh did not reject Ibn al-Dughnah’s protection, but they said to Ibn al-Dughnah: “Tell Abū Bakr to worship his Lord inside his house. Let him pray there and recite whatever he wishes, but he must not harm us by that or do it openly, for we fear that he will tempt our women and children.” Ibn al-Dughnah said that to Abū Bakr. Abū Bakr remained like that, worshiping his Lord in his house, not performing the prayer openly and not reciting anywhere other than his house. Then something occurred to Abū Bakr, so he built a small mosque in the courtyard of his house. He would pray in it and recite the Qur’an, and the women and children of the polytheists would gather around him, amazed by him and looking at him. Abū Bakr was a man who wept much and could not hold back his tears when he recited the Qur’an. This alarmed the noble polytheists of Quraysh, so they sent for Ibn al-Dughnah, and he came to them. They said: “We granted protection to Abū Bakr on the condition that he worship his Lord in his house, but he has gone beyond that and built a mosque in the courtyard of his house, publicly performing prayer and reciting in it. We fear that he will tempt our women and children, so forbid him. If he wishes to restrict himself to worshiping his Lord in his house, let him do so. But if he refuses except to do it openly, then ask him to return your covenant of protection to you. We dislike breaking your protection, but we will not allow Abū Bakr to act publicly.” ʿĀ’ishah said: Ibn al-Dughnah came to Abū Bakr and said: “You know the covenant upon which I gave you protection. Either restrict yourself to that, or return my covenant of protection to me, for I do not want the Arabs to hear that I was betrayed regarding a man for whom I had made a covenant.” Abū Bakr said: “I return your protection to you, and I am content with the protection of Allah, Mighty and Majestic.” The Prophet ﷺ was then in Makkah, and the Prophet ﷺ said to the Muslims: “I have been shown the land of your migration: a place with palm trees between two lava fields,” meaning the two ḥarrahs. So those who migrated set out toward Medina, and most of those who had migrated to Abyssinia returned to Medina. Abū Bakr prepared to go toward Medina, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: “Wait calmly, for I hope that permission will be granted to me.” Abū Bakr said: “Do you hope for that, may my father be sacrificed for you?” He said: “Yes.” So Abū Bakr held himself back for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ so that he could accompany him, and he fed two riding camels that he had with him on the leaves of the samur tree — which is al-khabaṭ — for four months. Ibn Shihāb said: ʿUrwah said: ʿĀ’ishah said: One day, while we were sitting in the house of Abū Bakr at the hottest part of midday, someone said to Abū Bakr: “This is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ coming, covering his head, at an hour when he did not usually come to us.” Abū Bakr said: “May my father and mother be sacrificed for him. By Allah, nothing has brought him at this hour except an important matter.” She said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and sought permission, and permission was granted to him. He entered, and the Prophet ﷺ said to Abū Bakr: “Remove whoever is with you.” Abū Bakr said: “They are only your family, may my father be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “Permission has been granted to me to leave.” Abū Bakr said: “Companionship, may my father be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Yes.” Abū Bakr said: “Then take, may my father be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah, one of these two riding camels of mine.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “For its price.” ʿĀ’ishah said: We prepared them as quickly and thoroughly as possible, and we prepared for them a food-bag in a leather pouch. Asmā’ bint Abī Bakr cut a piece from her waist-belt and tied the mouth of the pouch with it, and for that reason she was called Dhāt al-Niṭāq. She said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr went to the cave in Mount Thawr and hid in it for three nights. ʿAbdullah ibn Abī Bakr, a young, intelligent, quick-witted boy, would spend the night with them. He would leave them before dawn, then wake up among Quraysh in Makkah as though he had slept there, and he would not hear any plot being planned against them except that he would memorize it, until he brought them the news when darkness mixed in. ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah, the freedman of Abū Bakr, would graze a milch flock of sheep for them, and would bring it to them when part of the night had passed after ‘ishā’. They would spend the night with fresh milk from their sheep and heated milk, until ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah would drive the sheep away before dawn. He did that each night of those three nights. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr hired a man from Banū al-Dīl, from Banū ʿAbd ibn ʿAdī, as a skilled guide — and a khirrīt is an expert guide. He had entered into an alliance with the family of al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wā’il al-Sahmī and was upon the religion of the disbelievers of Quraysh. They trusted him, gave him their two riding camels, and arranged to meet him at the cave of Thawr after three nights, in the morning of the third night, with their two camels. ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah and the guide set out with them, and he took them along the coastal route."],[215,"Asmā’ bint Abī Bakr said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ departed and Abū Bakr departed with him, Abū Bakr carried all of his wealth with him: five thousand dirhams or six thousand dirhams. She said: He set out with it. She said: Our grandfather Abū Quḥāfah entered upon us, and he had lost his sight. He said: “By Allah, I think he has distressed you by taking his wealth along with himself.” She said: I said: “No, my father, he has left us much good.” She said: I took some stones and placed them in the niche of the house where my father used to put his wealth, then I placed a cloth over them. Then I took his hand and said: “My father, place your hand on this wealth.” She said: He placed his hand on it and said: “There is no harm. If he has left this for you, then he has done well, and this is enough for you.” She said: No, by Allah, he had left us nothing, but I only wanted to calm the old man by that."],[216,"Asmā’ said: I prepared a food-bag for the Prophet ﷺ and Abū Bakr when they intended to go to Medina. I said to my father: “I cannot find anything to tie it with except my waist-belt.” He said: “Tear it in two.” So I did that, and I was called Dhāt al-Niṭāqayn, the woman of the two waist-belts."],[217,"Asmā’ was pregnant with ʿAbdullah ibn al-Zubayr, and she said: I set out while I was near full term. I came to Medina and stopped at Qubā’, and I gave birth to him at Qubā’. Then I brought him to the Prophet ﷺ, and he placed him in his lap. He then called for a date, chewed it, and put some of his saliva into his mouth. So the first thing to enter his stomach was the saliva of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then he performed taḥnīk for him with a date, supplicated for him, and blessed him. He was the first child born in Islam."],[218,"Allah the Exalted said: ﴿وَ اِذْ يَمْكُرُ بِكَ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْا لِيُثْبِتُوْكَ اَوْ يَقْتُلُوْكَ اَوْ يُخْرِجُوْكَ١ؕ وَ يَمْكُرُوْنَ وَ يَمْكُرُ اللّٰهُ١ؕ وَ اللّٰهُ خَيْرُ الْمٰكِرِيْنَ۰۰۳۰﴾ [al-Anfāl: 30]"],[219,"The Exalted Lord says:\n﴿وَ اِذْ يَمْكُرُ بِكَ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْا لِيُثْبِتُوْكَ اَوْ يَقْتُلُوْكَ اَوْ يُخْرِجُوْكَ١ؕ وَ يَمْكُرُوْنَ وَ يَمْكُرُ اللّٰهُ١ؕ وَ اللّٰهُ خَيْرُ الْمٰكِرِيْنَ۰۰۳۰﴾ [al-Anfāl: 30]\nAnd (O Prophet, remember) when those who disbelieved were plotting against you to imprison you, or kill you, or expel you (from Makkah); and they were plotting, and Allah was plotting, and Allah is the best of plotters.\nIbn Isḥāq said that after the Muslims migrated, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained in Makkah. He was waiting for the divine permission to migrate. Now in Makkah, besides him, there were only Sayyidunā ʿAlī and Sayyidunā Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both, or those who were enduring imprisonment and hardship, or those who were disabled and helpless. Sayyidunā Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, kept asking him again and again for permission to migrate, but he would say: “Do not hurry. Perhaps Allah will grant you a companion.” Sayyidunā Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, hoped that it would be the Messenger of Allah ﷺ himself. When the Quraysh sensed that a large group had been prepared around the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that many people in other regions had joined the ranks of his Companions, and that his followers from here were also migrating to them, and that they had found safety and security there and their defense had become strong, the Quraysh were now afraid of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ going to Madinah. They feared that he would raise a force against them. In view of all these reasons, the Quraysh gathered in Dār al-Nadwah. This was the mansion of Quṣayy of Banū Kilāb, where the Quraysh would conduct all their collective affairs. In this very place, fearing him, they held a council concerning him."],[220,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: A group of Quraysh, from the nobles of every tribe, gathered to enter Dār al-Nadwah. Iblīs came across them in the form of a venerable old man. When they saw him, they said: “Who are you?” He said: “An old man from Najd. I heard that you had gathered, so I wished to attend with you, and you will not lack opinion and advice from me.” They said: “Yes, enter.” So he entered with them and said: “Consider the matter of this man. By Allah, he is soon to leap upon you in your affairs with his affair.” One person said: “Bind him in chains, then wait for the calamity of death to overtake him until he perishes as those poets before him perished, like Zuhayr and al-Nābighah. He is only like one of them.” The enemy of Allah, the Najdī old man, cried out and said: “By Allah, this is not a good opinion for you. By Allah, his Lord will surely bring him out of his prison to his Companions, and they will soon pounce upon you until they take him from your hands and protect him from you. I do not feel safe that they will expel you from your land.” They said: “Then consider something else.” Another person said: “Expel him from among you and be relieved of him. Once he leaves, whatever he does and wherever he goes will not harm you. When his harm is absent from you, you will be relieved, and his affair will be among others.” The Najdī old man said: “By Allah, this is not a good opinion for you. Have you not seen the sweetness of his speech, the eloquence of his tongue, and how hearts are seized by what they hear of his words? By Allah, if you do that and he then presents himself to the Arabs, they will surely unite against you, then come to you until they expel you from your land and kill your nobles.” They said: “He has spoken the truth, by Allah. So consider an opinion other than this.” Abū Jahl said: “By Allah, I will suggest to you an opinion which I do not think you have yet considered, and I see no other.” They said: “What is it?” He said: “We take from every tribe a young, strong, middle-ranking man, then each young man is given a sharp sword, and they strike him as one man. When they kill him, his blood will be dispersed among all the tribes. I do not think this clan of Banū Hāshim will be able to fight all of Quraysh. When they see that, they will accept blood money, and we will be relieved and cut off his harm from us.” The Najdī old man said: “This, by Allah, is the opinion. The correct word is what the young man has said; I see no other.” So they dispersed upon that, having agreed upon it. He said: Jibrīl came to the Prophet ﷺ and commanded him not to sleep in the bed in which he usually slept that night. Allah then permitted him to depart. After his arrival in Medina, Allah revealed al-Anfāl to him, reminding him of His blessing upon him and His favor with him: “And when those who disbelieved plotted against you to restrain you, kill you, or expel you. They plot, and Allah plots, and Allah is the best of plotters” [al-Anfāl: 30]. And He revealed concerning their saying, “Wait for the calamity of death to overtake him until he perishes as the poets before him perished”: “Or do they say: A poet for whom we await the calamity of time?” [al-Ṭūr: 30]. That day was called the Day of al-Zaḥmah because of the plan upon which they gathered."],[221,"Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: ﴿اِلَّا تَنْصُرُوْهُ فَقَدْ نَصَرَهُ اللّٰهُ اِذْ اَخْرَجَهُ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْا ثَانِيَ اثْنَيْنِ اِذْ هُمَا فِي الْغَارِ اِذْ يَقُوْلُ لِصَاحِبِهٖ لَا تَحْزَنْ اِنَّ اللّٰهَ مَعَنَا١ۚ﴾ [al-Tawbah: 40]\nAllah the Exalted said: If you do not help the Messenger (it makes no difference), for Allah helped him when those who disbelieved drove him out, as one of two, when they were both in the cave, when he said to his companion: Do not grieve; indeed Allah is with us. [al-Tawbah: 40]"],[222,"Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I said to the Prophet ﷺ while I was in the cave: “If one of them were to look beneath his feet, he would see us.” He said: “What do you think, O Abū Bakr, of two when Allah is their third?”"],[223,"Jundub ibn Sufyān said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in a cave, and his finger was struck."],[224,"It is reported from Sayyidunā Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, in the explanation of the Quranic verse: ﴿وَ اِذْ يَمْكُرُ بِكَ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْا﴾ [al-Anfāl: 30] (And when those who disbelieved were plotting secretly against you), that one night the Quraysh consulted one another in Makkah. Some said: “As soon as it is morning, bind Muhammad ﷺ in chains.” Some said: “Kill him.” And some advised expelling him. Allah the Exalted informed the Prophet ﷺ of this consultation of the polytheists. So that night Sayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, slept in the bed of the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ left from there and went to pray. All night the polytheists kept guard, thinking that ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, was the Prophet ﷺ, and as soon as it was morning they rushed in, but when they looked, it was Sayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, who was there. In this way Allah turned their plot back upon them. They said: “Where is your companion?” Sayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: “I do not know.” Then they set out following the footprints of the Prophet ﷺ. When they reached that mountain, they became confused. They even climbed the mountain and passed by that very cave (where the Prophet ﷺ had taken refuge), but they saw a spider’s web at the mouth of the cave, and on seeing it they said: “If he were here, there would be no spider’s web at the mouth of this cave.” The Prophet ﷺ stayed in that cave for three days.\nImam Aḥmad narrated it as no. 3251. In its chain is ʿUthmān al-Jazarī. He is not the one mentioned in al-Tahdhīb. Rather, he is the one about whom Imam al-Bukhārī, may Allah have mercy on him, mentioned in al-Tārīkh al-Kabīr (6/258) that he is unknown (majhūl). Imam Aḥmad, may Allah have mercy on him, was also asked about him, and he said that he used to narrate objectionable (munkar) hadiths. It was also supposed that his book had been lost by him.\nSome have supposed that he is ʿUthmān ibn ʿAmr al-Jazarī, who is mentioned in al-Tahdhīb with a chain described as ḥasan. But the correct view is that he is ʿUthmān al-Jazarī, and the name of his father is not known. And he is a narrator of objectionable reports, as Imam Aḥmad stated.\nBesides, this narration also contradicts the statement of Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, when he said that if they looked down, their eyes would fall upon our feet. If the spider had spun a web, then what would be the point of speaking about their eyes falling upon the feet? Moreover, the presence of a spider’s web would imply that there was no one inside. That is why the scholar Ibn ʿUthaymīn, may Allah have mercy on him, said that this story is false. The sign of Allah and His protection is precisely that the mouth of the cave was open, and nothing prevented them from peering into the cave, yet they still did not see. As for the story of the pigeon’s nest and the spider’s web, it is very far-fetched, and it also contradicts the statement that if they looked down their eyes would fall upon our feet."],[225,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Prophet ﷺ approached Medina with Abū Bakr riding behind him. Abū Bakr was an older man who was recognized, while the Prophet of Allah ﷺ was a young man who was not recognized. A man would meet Abū Bakr and say: “Abū Bakr, who is this man in front of you?” He would say: “This man guides me along the way.” The listener would think he meant the road, while he meant the way of goodness."],[226,"Ibn Shihāb reported: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mālik al-Mudlijī—the nephew of Surāqah ibn Mālik ibn Juʿshum—informed me that his father told him that he heard Surāqah ibn Juʿshum say: Messengers from the disbelievers of Quraysh came to us offering the blood-money of each of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr to anyone who killed or captured either of them. While I was sitting in one of the gatherings of my people, Banū Mudlij, a man from among them came and stood over us while we were seated. He said: “O Surāqah, I have just seen some dark figures near the coast, and I think they are Muḥammad and his companions.” Surāqah said: I knew that it was them, but I said to him: “They are not them. You only saw so-and-so and so-and-so, who set out before our eyes.” I remained in the gathering for a while, then got up and entered my house. I ordered my slave girl to take my horse out and keep it ready for me behind a hillock. I took my spear and went out through the back of the house, dragging its lower end along the ground and keeping its upper end lowered, until I reached my horse. I mounted it and urged it onward until I drew near them. My horse stumbled and I fell from it. I stood up, reached into my quiver, took out the divining arrows, and sought a decision through them as to whether I should harm them or not. The result came out with what I disliked. I mounted my horse again and disobeyed the divining arrows. It carried me closer until I could hear the recitation of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He did not turn around, while Abū Bakr frequently looked back. Then the forelegs of my horse sank into the earth up to the knees, and I fell from it. I urged it, and it rose, barely managing to pull out its forelegs. Once it stood upright, a cloud of dust rose from the marks of its forelegs into the sky like smoke. I again sought a decision through the divining arrows, and again the result came out with what I disliked. So I called out to them offering safety, and they stopped. I rode until I reached them. Because of what had happened to me in being prevented from reaching them, it occurred to me that the cause of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would prevail. I said to him: “Your people have placed a blood-price upon you.” I informed them of what the people intended against them and offered them provisions and goods, but they took nothing from me and asked nothing of me except that he said: “Keep our whereabouts concealed.” I asked him to write me a document granting me safety. He instructed ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah, who wrote it upon a piece of tanned leather. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued on his journey."],[227,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mālik ibn Juʿshum al-Mudlijī narrated that his father Mālik told him that his brother Surāqah ibn Juʿshum told him: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left Makkah migrating to Medina, Quraysh offered one hundred camels to whoever returned him to them. He said: While I was sitting in the gathering of my people, a man from us came and said: “By Allah, I just saw three riders pass by me, and I think it is Muḥammad.” He said: I signaled to him with my eye to be quiet and said: “It is only Banū so-and-so, seeking a lost animal of theirs.” He said: “Perhaps.” Then he became silent. He said: I remained for a short while, then I rose and entered my house. I ordered my horse, and it was led to me in the middle of the valley. I took out my weapon from behind my rooms, then took my arrows to seek a decision with them. Then I put on my armor and went out with my arrows, seeking a decision with them. The arrow I disliked came out: “Do not harm him.” I had hoped to return him and take the one hundred camels. He said: I rode after him. While my horse was carrying me along, it stumbled and I fell from it. He said: I took out my arrows and sought a decision with them, and the arrow I disliked came out: “Do not harm him.” But I refused except to follow him. I mounted again, and when the people appeared to me and I fixed my gaze upon them, my horse stumbled with me, and its forelegs sank into the ground. I fell from it, then it pulled out its forelegs, and smoke like dust followed them. I knew then that he had been protected from me and that he would prevail. So I called out to them and said: “Wait for me. By Allah, I will not harm you, and nothing you dislike will come from me.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Say to him: What do you seek?” I said: “Write for me a document that will be a sign between me and you.” He said: “Write for him, Abū Bakr.” He said: So he wrote it for me and threw it to me. I returned and kept silent, not mentioning anything of what had happened, until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, opened Makkah and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had finished with the people of Khaybar. I went out to meet the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, carrying the document he had written for me. While I was heading toward him, I entered between the two sides of one of the battalions of the Anṣār. They began striking me with their spears and saying: “Away, away,” until I came near the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was on his she-camel. I could see his shin in the stirrup, like the pith of a palm tree. I raised my hand with the document and said: “O Messenger of Allah, this is your document.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “A day of fulfillment and righteousness. Bring him near.” He said: So I embraced Islam. Then I remembered something to ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about. Ibn Shihāb said: He only asked him about stray animals and something he had done during the journey he had been on. I do not remember anything except that I said: “O Messenger of Allah, stray animals come to my water-basins, which I have filled for my camels. Is there reward for me if I give them water?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Yes, in every moist liver there is reward.” He said: Then I went back and drove my charity to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[228,"al-Barā’ ibn ʿĀzib said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came from Makkah to Medina, Surāqah ibn Mālik ibn Juʿshum pursued him. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated against him, and his horse sank into the ground. He said: “Supplicate to Allah for me, and I will not harm you.” He said: So he supplicated to Allah. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became thirsty, and they passed by a shepherd. Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq said: I took a cup and milked into it a small amount of milk for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I brought it to him, and he drank until I was satisfied."],[229,"al-Barā’ ibn ʿĀzib said: Abū Bakr bought a saddle from ʿĀzib for thirteen dirhams. Abū Bakr said to ʿĀzib: “Tell al-Barā’ to carry it to my house.” He said: “No, not until you tell us what you did when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left and you were with him.” Abū Bakr said: We set out by night and pushed on through our day and night until midday came and the sun stood high. I looked around to see whether I could find shade to take refuge in, and I saw a rock. I went toward it and found some shade remaining beside it. I prepared it for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, spread out a fur cloak for him, and said: “Lie down, O Messenger of Allah.” So he lay down. Then I went out to see whether I could see anyone pursuing us, and I saw a shepherd. I said: “Whose are you, young man?” He said: “I belong to a man from Quraysh.” He named him, and I recognized him. I said: “Is there milk in your sheep?” He said: “Yes.” I said: “Will you milk for me?” He said: “Yes.” I commanded him, so he restrained one of the sheep. Then I commanded him to wipe the dust from its udder. Then I commanded him to wipe the dust from his hands. I had with me a waterskin with a cloth tied over its mouth. He milked a small amount of milk for me, and I poured water over the cup until its lower part became cool. Then I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and found that he had awakened. I said: “Drink, O Messenger of Allah.” He drank until I was satisfied. Then I said: “Has the time to depart come?” So we set out while the people were searching for us. None of them caught up with us except Surāqah ibn Mālik ibn Juʿshum on a horse of his. I said: “O Messenger of Allah, this pursuer has caught up with us.” He said: “Do not grieve; indeed Allah is with us.” When he came close to us, and the distance between us and him was about one spear-length or two — or he said two or three — I said: “O Messenger of Allah, this pursuer has caught up with us,” and I cried. He said: “Why are you crying?” I said: “By Allah, I am not crying for myself, but I am crying for you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated against him and said: “O Allah, suffice us against him however You will.” The legs of his horse sank up to its belly in hard ground. He jumped off and said: “Muḥammad, I know that this is your doing. Supplicate to Allah to save me from what I am in. By Allah, I will mislead those behind me who are pursuing you. This is my quiver; take an arrow from it, for you will pass by my camels and sheep at such-and-such place, so take from them whatever you need.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have no need of them.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for him, and he was released. He returned to his companions, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued on, and I was with him, until we arrived in Medina. The people met him, coming out into the roads and onto the rooftops. The servants and children ran quickly through the road saying: “Allah is greatest! The Messenger of Allah has come! Muḥammad has come!” The people disputed about who he would stay with. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Tonight I will stay with Banū al-Najjār, the maternal uncles of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, to honor them by that.” When morning came, he went where he was commanded. al-Barā’ said: The first of the Muhājirīn to come to us was Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr, the brother of Banū ʿAbd al-Dār. Then Ibn Umm Maktūm, the blind man, one of Banū Fihr, came to us. Then ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb came with twenty riders. We said: “What has happened to the Messenger of Allah?” He said: “He is following after me.” Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived, with Abū Bakr accompanying him. al-Barā’ said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not arrive until I had recited several sūrahs from the Mufaṣṣal."],[230,"al-Barā’ narrated that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq said: When we set out with the Prophet ﷺ from Makkah to Medina, we passed by a shepherd, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had become thirsty. He said: So I milked a small amount of milk for him and brought it to him, and he drank until I was satisfied."],[231,"Abū Bakr said: I set out and found a shepherd driving his sheep. I said: “Whose are you?” He said: “I belong to a man from Quraysh,” and he named him, so I recognized him. I said: “Is there milk in your sheep?” He said: “Yes.” I said: “Will you milk for me?” He said: “Yes.” So I ordered him to restrain a sheep from his flock. Then I ordered him to wipe the dust from its udder. Then I ordered him to wipe his hands, and he did like this: he struck one palm against the other. He milked a small amount of milk. I had prepared for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a waterskin with a cloth tied over its mouth, so I poured water over the milk until its lower part became cool. Then I came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “Drink, O Messenger of Allah.” He drank until I was satisfied."],[232,"Ibn Masʿūd said: I was a young boy tending sheep for ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ when the Prophet ﷺ and Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came, having fled from the polytheists. They said: “Young boy, do you have any milk to give us to drink?” I said: “I am entrusted, and I will not give you drink.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Do you have a young she-goat that no male has mounted?” I said: “Yes.” I brought it to them. The Prophet ﷺ restrained it, wiped the udder, and supplicated. The udder filled with milk. Then Abū Bakr brought him a bowl from a hollowed stone, and he milked into it. He drank, Abū Bakr drank, and then I drank. Then he said to the udder: “Shrink.” So it shrank. I came to him after that and said: “Teach me from this speech.” He said: “You are a boy who will be taught.” He said: I took from his mouth seventy sūrahs concerning which no one disputed with me."],[233,"Qays ibn al-Nuʿmān said: When the Prophet ﷺ and Abū Bakr set out in concealment, they passed by a slave tending sheep and asked him for milk to drink. He said: “I do not have any sheep that can be milked, except that there is here a young she-goat that became pregnant at the beginning of winter, then gave birth prematurely, and no milk remains for it.” He said: “Call it.” So he called it. The Prophet ﷺ restrained it, wiped its udder, and supplicated until it produced milk. Abū Bakr came with a shield, and he milked into it. He gave Abū Bakr to drink, then milked and gave the shepherd to drink, then milked and drank himself. The shepherd said: “By Allah, who are you? By Allah, I have never seen anyone like you.” He said: “Will you keep my matter secret if I tell you?” He said: “Yes.” He said: “I am Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah.” He said: “Are you the one Quraysh claim is a Ṣābi’?” He said: “They do say that.” He said: “I bear witness that you are a Prophet, and I bear witness that what you have brought is true, and no one does what you have done except a Prophet. I will follow you.” He said: “You will not be able to do that today. When news reaches you that I have prevailed, then come to us.”"],[234,"Ḥubaysh ibn Khālid al-Khuzāʿī said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left Makkah, along with Abū Bakr, the freedman of Abū Bakr, ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah, and their Laythī guide ʿAbdullah ibn Urayqiṭ, they passed by the tent of Umm Maʿbad al-Khuzāʿiyyah. She was a strong, visible woman who would sit wrapped in her garment in the courtyard of the tent, giving drink and food. They asked her for meat and dates to buy from her, but they found nothing of that with her. The people were exhausted and afflicted by drought. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at a sheep in the corner of the tent and said: “What is this sheep, Umm Maʿbad?” She said: “A sheep that weakness has kept behind from the flock.” He said: “Does it have any milk?” She said: “It is too weak for that.” He said: “Do you permit me to milk it?” She said: “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you; if you see any milk in it, then milk it.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated, wiped its udder with his hand, mentioned the Name of Allah, Blessed and Most High, and supplicated for her sheep. It spread its legs for him, gave milk, and began chewing. He called for a vessel large enough to satisfy the group, and he milked into it abundantly until foam rose over it. Then he gave her to drink until she was satisfied, and he gave his Companions to drink until they were satisfied. He drank last, and they were all fully satisfied. Then he milked into it a second time after the first until he filled the vessel, left it with her, pledged with her, and departed from her. Not long after, her husband Abū Maʿbad came driving lean goats that were swaying from thinness, with little marrow in them. When Abū Maʿbad saw the milk, he was amazed and said: “Where did you get this, Umm Maʿbad, when the sheep are far away in the pasture and there is no milking animal in the house?” She said: “No, by Allah, except that a blessed man passed by us, of such-and-such condition.” He said: “Describe him to me, Umm Maʿbad.” She said: “I saw a man radiant in brightness, with a luminous face, fine in form. He was not marred by a large belly, nor disfigured by a small head. He was handsome and well-proportioned. His eyes had deep blackness, his eyelashes were long, his voice had a slight hoarseness, his neck was long, his beard was thick, his eyebrows were arched and joined. If he was silent, dignity rested upon him; and if he spoke, beauty rose over him and crowned him. He was the most beautiful and splendid of people from afar, and the finest and sweetest of them from near. His speech was sweet, clear, neither too little nor too much, as though his words were a string of pearls descending. He was of medium height: not disliked for being too tall, nor looked down upon for being short. He was like a branch between two branches, the most radiant of the three in appearance and the finest of them in stature. He had companions surrounding him. If he spoke, they listened attentively to his words; if he commanded, they hastened to his command. He was served and surrounded, neither frowning nor foolish in speech.” Abū Maʿbad said: “By Allah, this is the man of Quraysh whose matter has been mentioned to us in Makkah. I had wished to accompany him, and I will certainly do so if I find a way.” In the morning, a loud voice was heard in Makkah. They heard the voice but did not know who its owner was, and it was saying: May Allah, Lord of mankind, reward with His best reward two companions who stopped at the tent of Umm Maʿbad. They descended upon her with guidance, so she was guided by it; successful indeed is the one who becomes the companion of Muḥammad. O people of Quṣayy, what Allah withheld from you through him of deeds unmatched and noble rank! Congratulations to Banū Kaʿb for the place of their young woman, and her seat as a watch-post for the believers. Ask your sister about her sheep and her vessel, for if you ask the sheep, it will testify. He called it, though it was barren, and it poured forth milk for him, pure and abundant from the udder of the sheep. He left it with her as a pledge for the milker, who would return to it at each watering and return. When Ḥassān ibn Thābit al-Anṣārī, the poet of the Prophet ﷺ, heard of that, he composed verses answering the unseen caller and said: A people have failed whose Prophet departed from them; blessed is the one to whom he travels by night and by morning. He moved away from a people whose minds were deficient, and settled among a people with renewed light. Their Lord guided them through him after misguidance, and whoever follows the truth is rightly guided.\nCan the misguidance of foolish, blind people be equal to guided ones who are guided by a guide? From him there descended upon the people of Yathrib riders of guidance who came down upon them with good fortune. A Prophet who sees what people around him do not see, and recites the Book of Allah in every gathering. If he says a statement one day about what is absent, its confirmation comes that day or the next morning. Congratulations to Abū Bakr for the good fortune of his destiny, by companionship with him; whoever Allah makes happy is happy.\nCongratulations to Banū Kaʿb for the place of their young woman, and her seat as a watch-post for the believers."],[235,"ʿĀ’ishah said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left the cave as a migrant, accompanied by Abū Bakr and ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah, with Abū Bakr riding behind him and ʿAbdullah ibn Urayqiṭ al-Laythī behind him, he took them along the lower part of Makkah. Then he continued until he brought them down to the coast below ʿUsfān. Then he passed with them by the lower part of Amaj. Then he crossed the route after passing with them through Qudayd. Then he took them through al-Ḥijāz, then passed through Thaniyyat al-Marār, then took them through al-Ḥafyā’, then passed with them through Mudlijat Liqf, then took them into the interior of Mudlijat Ṣiḥāḥ, then took them through Madhḥij, then through the valley of Madhḥij from Dhī al-Ghaḍwayn, then through the valley of Kashd, then he took al-Jabājib, then Dhā Salam from the upper valley of Mudlijah, then took al-Qāḥah, then descended al-ʿArj, then took Thaniyyat al-Ghā’ir to the right of Rakūbah, then descended the valley of Rīm, and arrived at Qubā’ among Banū ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf."],[236,"Ibn Shihāb said: ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met al-Zubayr among a group of Muslims who were merchants returning from Syria. al-Zubayr clothed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr in white garments. The Muslims in Medina heard that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had left Makkah, so every morning they would go out to the lava field and wait for him until the heat of midday drove them back. One day, after they had waited a long time, they returned. When they had gone back to their houses, a Jewish man climbed one of their forts for something he was looking at, and he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions clothed in white, the mirage moving around them. The Jew could not restrain himself and cried out at the top of his voice: “O assembly of Arabs, this is your fortune that you have been waiting for!” The Muslims rushed to their weapons and met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the back of the lava field. He turned with them to the right until he stopped among Banū ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf, and that was on a Monday in the month of Rabīʿ al-Awwal. Abū Bakr stood for the people, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat silently. Those of the Anṣār who came and had not seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began greeting Abū Bakr, until the sun struck the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then Abū Bakr came forward and shaded him with his cloak, and the people then recognized the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained among Banū ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf for more than ten nights. He founded the mosque that was founded upon taqwā, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in it. Then he mounted his riding animal and traveled on while the people walked with him, until it knelt at the place of the Messenger’s mosque in Medina. At that time, some Muslim men used to pray there. It had been a drying-ground for dates belonging to Sahl and Suhayl, two orphan boys under the guardianship of Asʿad ibn Zurārah. When his riding animal knelt, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “This, if Allah wills, is the place of residence.” Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called the two boys and negotiated with them regarding the drying-ground so that he could take it as a mosque. They said: “Rather, we give it to you as a gift, O Messenger of Allah.” But the Messenger of Allah ﷺ refused to accept it from them as a gift until he purchased it from them. Then he built it as a mosque. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began carrying bricks with them during its construction, saying while carrying the bricks: This load is not the load of Khaybar; this is more righteous, our Lord, and purer. And he would say: O Allah, the true reward is the reward of the Hereafter; so have mercy on the Anṣār and the Muhājirah. He recited the poetry of a Muslim man whose name was not mentioned to me. Ibn Shihāb said: It has not reached us in the ḥadīths that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited a complete line of poetry other than this line."],[237,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Prophet ﷺ approached Medina with Abū Bakr riding behind him. Abū Bakr was an older man who was recognized, while the Prophet of Allah ﷺ was a young man who was not recognized. A man would meet Abū Bakr and say: “Abū Bakr, who is this man in front of you?” He would say: “This man guides me along the way.” The listener would think he meant the road, while he meant the way of goodness. Then Abū Bakr turned and saw a horseman who had caught up with them. He said: “O Messenger of Allah, this horseman has caught up with us.” The Prophet of Allah ﷺ turned and said: “O Allah, throw him down.” The horse threw him down, then stood neighing. He said: “O Prophet of Allah, command me with whatever you wish.” He said: “Stand in your place, and do not let anyone catch up with us.” He said: At the beginning of the day, he was striving against the Prophet of Allah ﷺ, and by the end of the day he was a guard for him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stopped by the side of the lava field, then sent to the Anṣār. They came to the Prophet of Allah ﷺ, greeted both of them, and said: “Ride safely and obeyed.” So the Prophet of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr rode, while they surrounded them with weapons. It was said in Medina: “The Prophet of Allah has come! The Prophet of Allah ﷺ has come!” The people looked out, saying: “The Prophet of Allah has come! The Prophet of Allah ﷺ has come!” He continued until he stopped near the house of Abū Ayyūb. While he was speaking to his family, ʿAbdullah ibn Salām heard of him. He was in his family’s palm grove harvesting dates for them, so he hurried to put down what he was harvesting, came while carrying it, and listened to the Prophet of Allah ﷺ. Then he returned to his family. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: “Which of our family’s houses is closest?” Abū Ayyūb said: “I am, O Prophet of Allah. This is my house, and this is my door.” He said: “Go and prepare for us a resting place.” He said: “Rise, upon the blessing of Allah.” When the Prophet of Allah ﷺ came, ʿAbdullah ibn Salām came and said: “I bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah and that you have come with the truth. The Jews know that I am their chief and the son of their chief, and the most knowledgeable of them and the son of the most knowledgeable of them. Call them and ask them about me before they know that I have embraced Islam, for if they know that I have embraced Islam, they will say about me what is not true.” The Prophet of Allah ﷺ sent for them. They came and entered upon him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: “O assembly of Jews, woe to you, fear Allah. By Allah, besides Whom there is no deity, you know that I am truly the Messenger of Allah and that I have come to you with the truth, so embrace Islam.” They said: “We do not know that.” They said this to the Prophet ﷺ three times. He said: “What kind of man among you is ʿAbdullah ibn Salām?” They said: “He is our chief and the son of our chief, the most knowledgeable of us and the son of the most knowledgeable of us.” He said: “What do you think if he has embraced Islam?” They said: “Allah forbid! He would never embrace Islam.” He said: “What do you think if he has embraced Islam?” They said: “Allah forbid! He would never embrace Islam.” He said: “What do you think if he has embraced Islam?” They said: “Allah forbid! He would never embrace Islam.” He said: “ʿIbn Salām, come out to them.” He came out and said: “O assembly of Jews, fear Allah. By Allah, besides Whom there is no deity, you know that he is the Messenger of Allah and that he has come with the truth.” They said: “You lie.” So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ expelled them."],[238,"Ibn Isḥāq relates that in Qubāʾ the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed with Kulthūm ibn Hidm of Banū ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf. He stayed in Qubāʾ with Banū ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf for four days: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday, and there he laid the foundation of Masjid Qubāʾ.\nHe ﷺ set out from there towards Madinah on Friday. On the way, the time of Friday prayer came upon him in the quarter of Banū Sālim ibn ʿAwf. There, in Wādī Rānūnāʾ, he prayed the Friday prayer with the Muslims, at the place where the mosque now stands. This was his first Friday prayer in Madinah.\nThis was his first Friday prayer absolutely. In Makkah it was not possible for the Muslims to gather all together for the Friday sermon and for exhortation and advice, because such a thing was unthinkable in an atmosphere of intense opposition and persecution from the polytheists. Friday prayer had already been made obligatory in Makkah itself, as is the opinion of Imam Aḥmad and of most of the people of knowledge among the Shāfiʿīs and Mālikīs. There were several obstacles to his not having prayed Friday in Makkah, as Imam Ibn Kathīr has mentioned.\nIbn Jarīr al-Ṭabarī mentioned his blessed sermon at the first Friday prayer in his Tārīkh (2/394-396). Imam al-Bayhaqī also mentioned it in Dalāʾil al-Nubuwwah (2/524-525). There is a slight difference between the two in the wording of the Friday sermon. The chains of both are mursal, and because their sources differ, each strengthens the other.\nIbn Abī Shaybah mentioned in Tārīkh al-Madīnah (1/68) that when the Prophet ﷺ came to Madinah, he prayed his first Friday prayer in the mosque of ʿĀtikah of Banū Sālim. In the chain of this narration is the narrator Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Yaḥyā, who is a liar.\nBefore the arrival of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Madinah, the honor of initiating the Friday prayer there went to Sayyidunā Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr, may Allah be pleased with him. Imam al-Bayhaqī quoted from al-Zuhrī in al-Sunan al-Kubrā (3/189): Before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to Madinah, Muṣʿab was the first to pray Friday prayer there. This narration is mursal. Reports have been narrated continuously that, before the blessed arrival of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Madinah, the honor of leading the first Friday prayer there went to Sayyidunā Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr, may Allah be pleased with him."],[239,"al-Barā’ ibn ʿĀzib said: The first to come to us were Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr and Ibn Umm Maktūm, and they used to teach the people Qur’an. Then Bilāl, Saʿd, and ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir came. Then ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb came with twenty of the Prophet’s Companions. Then the Prophet ﷺ came. I never saw the people of Medina rejoice over anything as they rejoiced over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, until even the slave girls were saying: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has arrived.”"],[240,"Anas ibn Mālik said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Medina, the Abyssinians played with their spears out of joy at his arrival."],[241,"Anas said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ migrated, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was riding and Abū Bakr was behind him. Abū Bakr was known along the road because of his frequent travel to Syria. He would pass by people, and they would say: “Who is this in front of you, Abū Bakr?” He would say: “A guide who guides me.” When they drew near Medina, they sent to the people of the Anṣār who had embraced Islam, to Abū Umāmah and his companions, so they came out to the two of them and said: “Enter, safe and obeyed.” So they entered. Anas said: I never saw any day brighter or more beautiful than the day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr entered Medina. And I witnessed his death, and I never saw any day darker or uglier than the day on which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died."],[242,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met al-Zubayr among a group of Muslims. Then he mounted his riding animal and traveled on while the people walked with him, until it knelt at the place of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ mosque in Medina."],[243,"ʿAbdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Medina, his riding animal was made to kneel between the house of Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī and the house of al-Ḥasan ibn Zayd. The people came to him and said: “O Messenger of Allah, the lodging!” Then his riding animal moved on with him, and he said: “Leave it, for it has been commanded.” It continued with him until it brought him to the place of the pulpit, where it knelt. Then it shifted slightly, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dismounted from his riding animal and took shelter in the shade, where he settled. Abū Ayyūb came to him and said: “O Messenger of Allah, my house is the closest of houses.” Then another man came and said: “O Messenger of Allah, stay with me.” He said: “A man remains with his belongings wherever they are.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained in the shelter until he prayed there with the people for twelve nights, until the mosque was built."],[244,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stopped by the side of the lava field, then sent to the Anṣār. They came to the Prophet of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr, greeted both of them, and said: “Ride, safe and obeyed.” So the Prophet of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr rode, while they surrounded them with weapons. It was said in Medina: “The Prophet of Allah has come! The Prophet of Allah ﷺ has come!” The people looked out, saying: “The Prophet of Allah has come!” He continued until he stopped near the house of Abū Ayyūb. While he was speaking to his family, ʿAbdullah ibn Salām heard of him. He was in his family’s palm grove harvesting dates for them, so he hurried to put down what he was harvesting, came while carrying it, and listened to the Prophet of Allah ﷺ. Then he returned to his family. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: “Which of our family’s houses is closest?” Abū Ayyūb said: “I am, O Prophet of Allah. This is my house, and this is my door.” He said: “Go and prepare for us a resting place.”"],[245,"Abū Ayyūb narrated that the Prophet ﷺ stayed with him. The Prophet ﷺ stayed on the lower floor, while Abū Ayyūb was on the upper floor. He said: One night Abū Ayyūb awoke and said: “Are we walking above the head of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” So they moved aside and spent the night on one side. Then he spoke to the Prophet ﷺ about that, and the Prophet ﷺ said: “The lower floor is more convenient.” He said: “I will not be above a ceiling while you are beneath it.” So the Prophet ﷺ moved to the upper floor, and Abū Ayyūb moved to the lower floor. He used to prepare food for the Prophet ﷺ, and when it was brought back to him, he would ask about the place where his fingers had touched and follow the place of his fingers. He prepared food for him that contained garlic, and when it was returned to him he asked about the place where the Prophet’s ﷺ fingers had touched. He was told: “He did not eat.” He became alarmed, went up to him, and said: “Is it forbidden?” The Prophet ﷺ said: “No, but I dislike it.” He said: “Then I dislike what you dislike,” or “what you disliked.” He said: The Prophet ﷺ used to receive revelation."],[246,"Abū Ayyūb said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed with me, I said: “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you. I dislike being above you while you are below me.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “It is more convenient for me to be on the lower floor because of the people who come to us.” I remember that a jar of ours broke and its water spilled. I and Umm Ayyūb rose with a cloak of ours — we had no blanket other than it — and used it to soak up the water out of fear that anything of it might reach the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and harm him."],[247,"Anas ibn Mālik said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Medina, he stayed in the upper part of Medina among a clan called Banū ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf. He said: He stayed among them for fourteen nights. Then he sent for the leaders of Banū al-Najjār. He said: They came wearing their swords. He said: It is as though I am looking at the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on his riding animal, with Abū Bakr riding behind him and the leaders of Banū al-Najjār around him, until he stopped at the courtyard of Abū Ayyūb. He said: He would pray wherever the prayer reached him, and he would pray in the sheep pens. He said: Then he commanded that the mosque be built. He sent for the leaders of Banū al-Najjār, and they came. He said: “O Banū al-Najjār, name a price for me for this garden of yours.” They said: “No, by Allah, we seek its price only from Allah.” He said: In it was what I will tell you: there were graves of polytheists, ruins, and palm trees. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that the graves of the polytheists be dug up, that the ruins be leveled, and that the palm trees be cut down. He said: They arranged the palm trees toward the qiblah of the mosque. He said: They made its two doorposts from stones. He said: They began carrying those stones while chanting, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was with them, saying: O Allah, there is no good except the good of the Hereafter; so support the Anṣār and the Muhājirah."],[248,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī said: We were carrying one brick at a time, while ʿAmmār was carrying two bricks at a time. The Prophet ﷺ saw him, wiped the dust from him, and said: “Woe to ʿAmmār. He will be killed by the rebellious group. He calls them to Paradise, while they call him to the Fire.”"],[249,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that when ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām heard that the Prophet ﷺ had arrived in Madinah, he came to him to ask him about certain matters. He said, “I will ask you about three things which no one knows except a prophet: What is the first of the signs of the Hour? What is the first food the people of Paradise will eat? And why does a child resemble his father or his mother?” He said, “Jibrīl has just informed me of them.” ʿAbdullāh said, “He is the enemy of the Jews among the angels.” The Prophet ﷺ said, “As for the first of the signs of the Hour, it is a fire that will gather them from the east to the west. As for the first food the people of Paradise will eat, it is the extra lobe of the liver of the fish. As for the child, when the man’s fluid precedes the woman’s fluid, the child resembles his father; and when the woman’s fluid precedes the man’s fluid, the child resembles his mother.” He said, “I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that you are the Messenger of Allah.” Then he said, “O Messenger of Allah, the Jews are a people of slander, so ask them about me before they learn of my Islam.” The Jews came, and the Prophet ﷺ said, “What kind of man is ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām among you?” They said, “He is the best of us and the son of the best of us, and the most virtuous of us and the son of the most virtuous of us.” The Prophet ﷺ said, “What do you think if ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām embraces Islam?” They said, “May Allah protect him from that.” He repeated the question, and they said the same. Then ʿAbdullāh came out to them and said, “I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.” They said, “He is the worst of us and the son of the worst of us,” and they disparaged him. He said, “This is what I feared, O Messenger of Allah.”"],[250,"Allah the Exalted said: ﴿وَ الَّذِيْنَ تَبَوَّؤُ الدَّارَ وَ الْاِيْمَانَ مِنْ قَبْلِهِمْ يُحِبُّوْنَ مَنْ هَاجَرَ اِلَيْهِمْ وَ لَا يَجِدُوْنَ فِيْ صُدُوْرِهِمْ حَاجَةً مِّمَّاۤ اُوْتُوْا وَ يُؤْثِرُوْنَ عَلٰۤى اَنْفُسِهِمْ وَ لَوْ كَانَ بِهِمْ خَصَاصَةٌ١۫ؕ وَ مَنْ يُّوْقَ شُحَّ نَفْسِهٖ فَاُولٰٓىِٕكَ هُمُ الْمُفْلِحُوْنَۚ۰۰۹﴾ [al-Ḥashr: 9]\nAllah the Exalted said: And (that wealth is) for those who were settled in Madinah and had believed before the arrival of the Muhājirūn of Makkah. They love those who migrated to them, and they do not find in their hearts any need or envy for what the Muhājirūn were given of the spoils, and they prefer them over themselves even though they themselves are in need. And whoever is protected from the stinginess and miserliness of his own soul, it is those who are the successful. [al-Ḥashr: 9]"],[251,"Ibn ʿAbbās said regarding the verse, “And for everyone We have appointed heirs” [al-Nisāʾ: 33], that it means inheritors. Regarding the verse, “And those with whom your right hands have made a covenant” [al-Nisāʾ: 33], he said: When the Muhājirūn came to Madinah, a Muhājir would inherit from an Anṣārī instead of the Anṣārī’s blood relatives, because of the brotherhood that the Prophet ﷺ had established between them. When the verse, “And for everyone We have appointed heirs,” was revealed, it abrogated that. Then he said that the verse, “And those with whom your right hands have made a covenant,” refers to support, assistance, and sincere counsel; inheritance has passed away, but one may make a bequest for him."],[252,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf arrived, and the Prophet ﷺ established brotherhood between him and Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Anṣārī. Saʿd offered to divide his family and wealth with him equally, but ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said, “May Allah bless your family and wealth for you; show me the marketplace.” He earned some dried yogurt and clarified butter. The Prophet ﷺ saw him with traces of yellow perfume on him and said, “What is this, O ʿAbd al-Raḥmān?” He said, “O Messenger of Allah, I married a woman from the Anṣār.” He said, “What dowry did you give her?” He said, “The weight of a date-stone in gold.” The Prophet ﷺ said, “Hold a wedding feast, even if only with a sheep.”"],[253,"Anas ibn Mālik was asked, “Has it reached you that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ‘There is no alliance in Islam’?” Anas replied, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ made an alliance between Quraysh and the Anṣār in my house.”"],[254,"Khārijah ibn Zayd al-Anṣārī narrated that Umm al-ʿAlāʾ, a woman of theirs who had pledged allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ, told him that ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn’s lot had fallen to her household when the Anṣār cast lots to host the Muhājirūn. Umm al-ʿAlāʾ said: ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn stayed with us, then he became ill, and we nursed him until he died. When we had shrouded him in his garments, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon us, and I said, “May Allah have mercy on you, Abū al-Sā’ib. My testimony concerning you is that Allah has honored you.” The Prophet ﷺ said to me, “How do you know that Allah has honored him?” I said, “I do not know—may my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “As for ʿUthmān, by Allah, certainty has come to him, and I hope for good for him. But by Allah, I do not know, though I am the Messenger of Allah, what will be done with him.” She said, “By Allah, I will never declare anyone pure after him.” That saddened me. She said: Then I slept and saw for ʿUthmān a flowing spring. I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and told him, and he said, “That is his deed.”"],[255,"Anas narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ established brotherhood between Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ and Abū Ṭalḥah."],[256,"Anas narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ made an alliance between Quraysh and the Anṣār in his house in Madinah."],[257,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: When the Muhājirūn came to Madinah from Makkah, they had nothing in their hands, while the Anṣār owned land and property. So the Anṣār shared with them, giving them the fruits of their properties each year, while the Anṣār would spare them the labor and expenses. His mother, Umm Anas—Umm Sulaym, the mother of ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah—had given the Messenger of Allah ﷺ some date-palms, and the Prophet ﷺ gave them to Umm Ayman, his freedwoman, the mother of Usāmah ibn Zayd. Ibn Shihāb said: Anas ibn Mālik informed me that when the Prophet ﷺ finished fighting the people of Khaybar and returned to Madinah, the Muhājirūn returned to the Anṣār the grants of fruit they had been given. The Prophet ﷺ returned to Anas’s mother her date-palms, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave Umm Ayman in their place from his own orchard. Aḥmad ibn Shabīb said: My father informed us from Yūnus with this narration, and he said: “in their place from his private share.”"],[258,"Anas narrated: When the Prophet ﷺ came to Madinah, the Muhājirūn came to him and said, “O Messenger of Allah, we have never seen any people more generous with much, nor better in sharing when they have little, than the people among whom we have settled. They have spared us the expenses and shared with us the fruits and comforts, until we feared that they would take all the reward.” The Prophet ﷺ said, “No, so long as you supplicate to Allah for them and praise them.”"],[259,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated: The Prophet ﷺ wrote down for every clan their blood-money responsibilities, then he wrote: “It is not lawful for a Muslim to attach himself as a client to the client of another Muslim without his permission.” Then I was informed that in his document he cursed whoever did that."],[260,"Anas narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ established brotherhood among his Companions. He established brotherhood between Salmān and Abū al-Dardāʾ, and between ʿAwf ibn Mālik and Ṣaʿb ibn Jaththāmah."],[261,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ established brotherhood between al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām and ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd."],[262,"Anas narrated: The Prophet ﷺ established brotherhood between Ibn Masʿūd and al-Zubayr."],[263,"Zayd ibn Ḥārithah narrated: I said, “O Messenger of Allah, you established brotherhood between me and Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.”"],[264,"ʿAmr ibn Qays ibn Thawr ibn Māzin ibn Khaythamah narrated that his grandfather Māzin ibn Khaythamah and Hubayl ibn Kaʿb, the grandfather of Zaml, were sent by Muʿādh ibn Jabal when he settled between al-Sakūn and al-Sakāsik and fought until the people embraced Islam. He then sent the two of them as delegates to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ established brotherhood between al-Sakūn and al-Sakāsik."],[265,"Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: ﴿اُذِنَ لِلَّذِيْنَ يُقٰتَلُوْنَ بِاَنَّهُمْ ظُلِمُوْا١ؕ وَ اِنَّ اللّٰهَ عَلٰى نَصْرِهِمْ لَقَدِيْرُۙ۰۰۳۹ ا۟لَّذِيْنَ اُخْرِجُوْا مِنْ دِيَارِهِمْ بِغَيْرِ حَقٍّ اِلَّاۤ اَنْ يَّقُوْلُوْا رَبُّنَا اللّٰهُ١ؕ وَ لَوْ لَا دَفْعُ اللّٰهِ النَّاسَ بَعْضَهُمْ بِبَعْضٍ لَّهُدِّمَتْ صَوَامِعُ وَ بِيَعٌ وَّ صَلَوٰتٌ وَّ مَسٰجِدُ يُذْكَرُ فِيْهَا اسْمُ اللّٰهِ كَثِيْرًا١ؕ وَ لَيَنْصُرَنَّ اللّٰهُ مَنْ يَّنْصُرُهٗ١ؕ اِنَّ اللّٰهَ لَقَوِيٌّ عَزِيْزٌ۰۰۴۰ اَلَّذِيْنَ اِنْ مَّكَّنّٰهُمْ فِي الْاَرْضِ اَقَامُوا الصَّلٰوةَ وَ اٰتَوُا الزَّكٰوةَ وَ اَمَرُوْا بِالْمَعْرُوْفِ وَ نَهَوْا عَنِ الْمُنْكَرِ١ؕ وَ لِلّٰهِ عَاقِبَةُ الْاُمُوْرِ۰۰۴۱﴾ [Sūrat al-Ḥajj: 39-41]\nAllah the Exalted said: Permission (to fight) has now been given to those believers against whom war is being waged, because they have been wronged, and indeed Allah is able to give them victory. Those who were expelled from their homes without right only because they said, “Our Lord is Allah.” And if Allah did not repel some people by means of others, the monasteries of Christian monks, the churches, the synagogues of the Jews, and the mosques in which the name of Allah is much remembered would have been demolished. And Allah will surely help those who help His religion; indeed Allah is Powerful and Mighty. Those who, when We establish them in the land, establish prayer, give zakah, enjoin what is good and forbid what is evil; and with Allah rests the outcome of all matters. [Sūrat al-Ḥajj: 39-41]"],[266,"Fighting was legislated to put an end to injustice and to establish justice among people, because the Muslims were oppressed and persecuted. When they migrated to Madinah and found security there, Allah permitted them to defend themselves against injustice and tyranny."],[267,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās narrated: When the Prophet ﷺ was expelled from Makkah, Abū Bakr said, “They have expelled their Prophet. Indeed, we belong to Allah and indeed to Him we will return. They will surely perish.” Then the verse was revealed: “Permission has been given to those who are fought because they have been wronged, and indeed Allah is capable of giving them victory” [al-Ḥajj: 39]. I then knew that fighting would occur. Ibn ʿAbbās said: This was the first verse revealed concerning fighting."],[268,"Al-Zuhrī said: The first verse revealed concerning fighting, as ʿUrwah informed me from ʿĀ’ishah, was: “Permission has been given to those who are fought because they have been wronged, and indeed Allah is capable of giving them victory,” up to His saying: “Indeed, Allah is Powerful, Mighty” [al-Ḥajj: 39-40]. Thereafter, permission to fight was given in many verses of the Qur’an."],[269,"Jābir narrated: I participated with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in nineteen expeditions. Jābir said: I did not witness Badr or Uḥud; my father prevented me. But when ʿAbdullāh was killed on the Day of Uḥud, I never stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in any expedition."],[270,"Abū Isḥāq said: I was beside Zayd ibn Arqam, and he was asked, “How many expeditions did the Prophet ﷺ undertake?” He said, “Nineteen.” He was asked, “How many did you participate in with him?” He said, “Seventeen.” I said, “Which of them was first?” He said, “Al-ʿUsayrah or al-ʿUshayr.”"],[271,"Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb narrated that he participated with the Prophet ﷺ in sixteen expeditions."],[272,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib narrated: I participated with the Prophet ﷺ in fifteen expeditions."],[273,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ narrated: I participated with the Prophet ﷺ in seven expeditions, and I went out in nine of the detachments he sent: once Abū Bakr was our commander, and once Usāmah ibn Zayd was our commander."],[274,"Ibn Isḥāq relates that twelve months after the Hijrah to Madinah, in Ṣafar, he ﷺ set out for the expedition of Waddān. This is the same as the expedition of al-Abwāʾ. His ﷺ aim was to intercept a caravan of the Quraysh and of Banū Ḍamrah ibn Bakr ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn Kinānah. In this expedition Banū Ḍamrah made a treaty with him ﷺ. The one who made the treaty was Makhshī ibn ʿAmr al-Ḍamrī, who was then the chief of his tribe. After that he ﷺ returned to Madinah. There was no clash with anyone. In Madinah he ﷺ stayed for some days of Ṣafar and Rabīʿ al-Awwal and set out on a new campaign. Ibn Hishām says that this was his first expedition. Al-Abwāʾ is one of the settlements in the surroundings of Madinah, and the distance between it and al-Juḥfah is 23 miles."],[275,"Ibn Isḥāq relates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent from Madinah the expedition (sariyyah) of ʿUbaydah ibn al-Ḥārith ibn al-Muṭṭalib ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn Quṣayy. About sixty or eighty Muhājirūn took part in this campaign. There was not a single one of the Anṣār. The force set out until it reached a watering place in the Hijaz, in the lower side of Thaniyyat al-Murrah. There they met a very large force of the Quraysh. There was no fighting between them, but Sayyidunā Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, shot arrows at them. This was the first arrow shot for Islam. In this expedition Sayyidunā al-Miqdād ibn ʿAmr al-Bahrānī and ʿUtbah ibn Ghazwān ibn Jābir al-Māzinī, may Allah be pleased with them both, fled from the army of the disbelievers and joined the Muslims. Both were Muslims who had gone out with the disbelievers so that, under this pretext, they could reach the Muslims. The commander of the Quraysh force at that time was ʿIkrimah ibn Abī Jahl."],[276,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib ibn Hāshim, may Allah be pleased with him, towards the coast of the Red Sea, to the region of al-ʿĪṣ, and with him were thirty horsemen from the Muhājirūn, and none of the Anṣār was among them. On that coast they met Abū Jahl ibn Hishām, who was with three hundred horsemen from Makkah. Majdī ibn ʿAmr al-Juhanī brought about peace between the two groups. He was at peace with both groups, so both sides turned back from each other, and no fighting took place between them.\nAl-Wāqidī narrated in al-Maghāzī (1/10) from Ibn al-Musayyab and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿīd ibn Yarbūʿ, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not send any of the Anṣār on any expedition until he himself marched to Badr. The reason was that it was assumed that they would help him ﷺ only in their own home (in Madinah).\nThere is a disagreement among the biographers and historians about which of these two expeditions came first. Ibn Isḥāq said that the banner of ʿUbaydah ibn al-Ḥārith was the first that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tied. He added: Some people say that the banner of Ḥamzah was the first banner that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tied for any of the Muslims. The reason was that the expeditions of Ḥamzah and ʿUbaydah took place together, so the matter became unclear to people."],[277,"Ibn Isḥāq said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went on an expedition in the month of Rabīʿ al-Awwal, intending the Quraysh, until he reached Buwāṭ, which is in the region of Raḍwā. Then he returned to Madinah, and no enemy was met. Then he stayed in Madinah for the remaining days of Rabīʿ al-Ākhir and some of Jumādā al-Ūlā. (Sīrat Ibn Hishām, 1/598).\nBuwāṭ: it is permissible to read the letter bāʾ with either fatḥah or ḍammah. It is one of the mountains of Juhaynah, located near Yanbuʿ."],[278,"Jābir narrated in a long ḥadīth: We traveled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the expedition of Baṭn Buwāṭ. A man from the Anṣār had a water-camel, so he made it kneel, mounted it, and then urged it forward, but it hesitated somewhat. He said to it, “Move! May Allah curse you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Who is this cursing his camel?” He said, “I am, O Messenger of Allah.” He said, “Dismount from it, for one that has been cursed must not accompany us. Do not supplicate against yourselves, do not supplicate against your children, and do not supplicate against your wealth, lest you coincide with an hour in which Allah is asked for a gift and He answers you.”"],[279,"Abū Isḥāq al-Sabīʿī narrated: I was beside Zayd ibn Arqam, and he was asked, “How many expeditions did the Prophet ﷺ undertake?” He said, “Nineteen.” He was asked, “How many did you participate in with him?” He said, “Seventeen.” I said, “Which of them was first?” He said, “Al-ʿUshayr or al-ʿUsayrah.” I mentioned that to Qatādah, and he said, “Al-ʿUshayrah.”"],[280,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Sayyidunā Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, towards al-Kharrār. Al-Kharrār is a place in al-Juḥfah near Khumm. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Sayyidunā Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him: “Saʿd! Reach al-Kharrār with your companions. The caravan of the Quraysh will pass by there.” With Saʿd there were eight Muhājir Muslims; it has also been said that they were twenty men. They reached al-Kharrār. They would hide and rest during the day and travel at night. When they arrived there, they learned that the caravan of the Quraysh had passed the day before. Ibn Hishām says that some people of knowledge have mentioned that Saʿd was sent after the expedition of Sayyidunā Ḥamzah, may Allah be pleased with them both."],[281,"According to Ibn Saʿd, this expedition took place in Rabīʿ al-Awwal, thirteen months after the Hijrah. Ibn Isḥāq has reported that this campaign was carried out after the expedition of Dhū al-ʿUshayrah, which took place in Jumādā al-Ākhirah. The event was that Kurz ibn Jābir al-Fihrī raided the pasture of Madinah and drove off camels. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out in pursuit of him and chased him towards Badr as far as Wādī Safwān. He managed to escape. No fighting took place.\nIbn Isḥāq said that this is the first expedition of Badr.\nKurz ibn Jābir later became a Muslim and proved to be a good Muslim. He was honored with martyrdom at the conquest of Makkah. All these details have been mentioned by al-Ḥāfiẓ Ibn Ḥajar, may Allah have mercy on him, in al-Iṣābah."],[282,"Jundub ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he sent a small group and appointed Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ, or ʿUbaydah, over them. When he was about to set out, he wept out of longing for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and sat down. So the Prophet ﷺ appointed ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaḥsh in his place, wrote a letter for him, and ordered him not to read it until he reached such-and-such place. He said, “Do not force any of your companions to travel with you.” When he read the letter, he said, “Indeed we belong to Allah and indeed to Him we will return,” then said, “Hearing and obedience to Allah and His Messenger.” He informed them of the matter and read the letter to them. Two men returned, while the rest continued. They met Ibn al-Ḥaḍramī and killed him, not knowing whether that day was in Rajab or Jumādā. The polytheists said to the Muslims, “You have killed during the sacred month!” So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: “They ask you about the sacred month—fighting therein…” [al-Baqarah: 217]. Some said, “If they did not incur sin, then they have no reward.” So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: “Indeed, those who believed and those who emigrated and strove in the path of Allah—those hope for the mercy of Allah, and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful” [al-Baqarah: 218]."],[283,"Allah the Exalted said: ﴿وَ لَقَدْ نَصَرَكُمُ اللّٰهُ بِبَدْرٍ وَّ اَنْتُمْ اَذِلَّةٌ١ۚ فَاتَّقُوا اللّٰهَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُوْنَ۰۰۱۲۳ اِذْ تَقُوْلُ لِلْمُؤْمِنِيْنَ۠ اَلَنْ يَّكْفِيَكُمْ اَنْ يُّمِدَّكُمْ رَبُّكُمْ بِثَلٰثَةِ اٰلٰفٍ مِّنَ الْمَلٰٓىِٕكَةِ مُنْزَلِيْنَؕ۰۰۱۲۴ بَلٰۤى١ۙ اِنْ تَصْبِرُوْا وَ تَتَّقُوْا وَ يَاْتُوْكُمْ مِّنْ فَوْرِهِمْ هٰذَا يُمْدِدْكُمْ رَبُّكُمْ بِخَمْسَةِ اٰلٰفٍ مِّنَ الْمَلٰٓىِٕكَةِ مُسَوِّمِيْنَ۰۰۱۲۵ وَ مَا جَعَلَهُ اللّٰهُ اِلَّا بُشْرٰى لَكُمْ وَ لِتَطْمَىِٕنَّ قُلُوْبُكُمْ بِهٖ١ؕ وَ مَا النَّصْرُ اِلَّا مِنْ عِنْدِ اللّٰهِ الْعَزِيْزِ الْحَكِيْمِۙ۰۰۱۲۶ لِيَقْطَعَ طَرَفًا مِّنَ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْۤا اَوْ يَكْبِتَهُمْ فَيَنْقَلِبُوْا خَآىِٕبِيْنَ۰۰۱۲۷﴾ [Sūrat Āl ʿImrān: 123-127]\nThe statement of Allah the Exalted:\nAllah helped you at Badr at the very time when you were weak. So fear Allah, so that you may be enabled to give thanks. (O Prophet!) When you were saying to the believers: Is it not sufficient for you that your Lord should reinforce you with three thousand angels sent down from the heavens? Yes indeed! If you are patient and fear Allah, and the enemy come upon you at once, then at that very moment your Lord will reinforce you with five thousand angels bearing (distinctive) marks. Allah made it only glad tidings for you, so that your hearts might be reassured by it. Victory comes only from Allah, the Mighty, the Wise. (Allah’s purpose was) that He might cut off a part of those who disbelieved or humiliate them, so that they would turn back disappointed."],[284,"Kaʿb ibn Mālik narrated: I did not stay behind from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in any expedition he undertook except the expedition of Tabūk. However, I had stayed behind from the expedition of Badr, and no one who stayed behind from it was reproached, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had only gone out intending the caravan of Quraysh, until Allah brought them together with their enemy without prior appointment. The ḥadīth continues."],[285,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard that Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb was returning from Syria with forty riders from Quraysh, merchants among whom were Makhramah ibn Nawfal and ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called the Muslims to go out and said to them, “This is Abū Sufyān returning with the trade goods of Quraysh, so go out to intercept it; perhaps Allah will grant it to you as spoils.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the Muslims set out. Some men went quickly with him, while others were slow, because the call was only for wealth they might obtain, and they did not think they would meet battle. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out with a little over three hundred riders, though most of his Companions were on foot. They had eighty camels and one horse, and some people claim that it belonged to al-Miqdād. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out, and he, ʿAlī, and Marthad ibn Abī Marthad al-Ghanawī shared one camel. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ left through the pass of Banū Dīnār from the lava-field toward al-ʿAqīq. The narrator mentioned his route, until when he was at ʿIrq al-Ẓabyah, he met a man from the Bedouins. They asked him about the people but found no information with him. When Abū Sufyān drew near to the Ḥijāz, he began seeking news and asking about it until he received information from some riders. He then hired Ḍamḍam ibn ʿAmr al-Ghifārī and sent him to Quraysh to call them to protect their wealth and to inform them that Muhammad had set out against it with his Companions. Ḍamḍam hastened until he reached Quraysh in Makkah and said, “O Quraysh, the trade caravan has been targeted by Muhammad and his Companions”—and al-laṭīmah means the trade goods—“Help! Help! I do not think you will catch it.” Quraysh said, “Does Muhammad and his Companions think this will be like the caravan of Ibn al-Ḥaḍramī?” So they went out, with both the strong and the weak among them, and none of their nobles stayed behind except Abū Lahab, who stayed back and sent in his place al-ʿĀṣ ibn Hishām ibn al-Mughīrah. Quraysh went out as nine hundred and fifty fighters, with two hundred horses that they led, and they brought singing girls with them who beat the tambourines and sang verses mocking the Muslims."],[286,"Anas narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Busaysah as a scout to see what the caravan of Abū Sufyān was doing. He returned, and there was no one in the house except me and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He told him the news. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out and spoke, saying, “We have a pursuit; whoever has his mount ready should ride with us.” Some men began asking his permission to go to their mounts in the upper parts of Madinah, but he said, “No, only whoever has his mount ready.”"],[287,"Ibn ʿAbbās and ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated: Three nights before Ḍamḍam reached Makkah, ʿĀtikah saw a dream that frightened her. She sent for her brother al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib and said to him, “My brother, by Allah, tonight I saw a dream that terrified me, and I fear that evil and calamity will enter upon your people because of it. So keep secret what I am telling you.” He said to her, “What did you see?” She said, “I saw a rider approaching on his camel until he stopped at al-Abṭaḥ. Then he cried out at the top of his voice: ‘Go forth, O people of treachery, to your places of death in three days!’ I saw the people gather around him. Then he entered the mosque while the people followed him. While they were around him, his camel was made to stand on top of the Kaʿbah, and he cried out the same: ‘Go forth, O people of treachery, to your places of death in three days!’ Then his camel was made to stand on top of Abū Qubays, and he cried out the same. Then he took a rock and threw it, and it came crashing down until, when it reached the base of the mountain, it shattered. There was no house or dwelling in Makkah except that a fragment of it entered it.” Al-ʿAbbās said, “By Allah, this is certainly a dream. Keep it secret, and do not mention it to anyone.” Then al-ʿAbbās went out and met al-Walīd ibn ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah, who was a friend of his. He mentioned it to him and asked him to keep it secret. Al-Walīd mentioned it to his father ʿUtbah, and the report spread through Makkah until Quraysh were discussing it in their gatherings. Al-ʿAbbās said: I went early to circumambulate the House, while Abū Jahl ibn Hishām was sitting with a group of Quraysh discussing ʿĀtikah’s dream. When Abū Jahl saw me, he said, “O Abū al-Faḍl, when you finish your ṭawāf, come to us.” When I finished, I came and sat with them. Abū Jahl said to me, “O Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, when did this prophetess arise among you?” I said, “What is that?” He said, “The dream that ʿĀtikah saw.” I said, “What did she see?” He said, “O Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, were you not satisfied that your men claim prophethood until your women claim it too? ʿĀtikah claims in her dream that someone said, ‘Go forth in three days.’ We will wait these three days for you. If what she says is true, then it will happen; but if three days pass and nothing happens, we will write a document against you that you are the most lying household among the Arabs.” Al-ʿAbbās said: By Allah, I did not respond to him much except that I denied it and denied that she had seen anything. Then we dispersed.\nWhen evening came, every woman from Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib came to me and said, “Did you accept that this wicked sinner should speak against your men, then turn to speaking about the women while you heard him, and you had no response to anything you heard?” I said, “By Allah, I did that. I did not respond to him much. By Allah, I will confront him, and if he returns to it, I will deal with him.” He said: I went early on the third day after ʿĀtikah’s dream, angry and sharp, thinking that I had missed an opportunity with him that I wanted to seize. I entered the mosque and saw him. By Allah, I was walking toward him, seeking to provoke him so that he would repeat some of what he had said and I could attack him.\nHe was a nimble man, sharp-faced, sharp-tongued, and keen-eyed. Suddenly, he rushed toward the door of the mosque. I said to myself, “What is wrong with him, may Allah curse him? Is all this out of fear that I will argue with him?” But he had heard what I had not heard: the voice of Ḍamḍam ibn ʿAmr al-Ghifārī shouting in the valley, standing on his camel, with his camel’s nose cut, his saddle reversed, and his shirt torn, saying, “O Quraysh, the trade caravan! The trade caravan! Your wealth with Abū Sufyān has been attacked by Muhammad and his Companions. I do not think you will catch it. Help! Help!” That distracted me from him, and distracted him from me because of the matter that had come.\nThe people prepared quickly, saying, “Does Muhammad and his Companions think this will be like the caravan of Ibn al-Ḥaḍramī? No, by Allah, he will learn otherwise.” They were of two types: either a man went out himself, or he sent another man in his place. Quraysh mobilized fully, and none of their nobles stayed behind except Abū Lahab ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib. He stayed behind and sent in his place al-ʿĀṣ ibn Hishām ibn al-Mughīrah, who owed him four thousand dirhams and had gone bankrupt. Abū Lahab hired him with that debt to go in his place, so he sent him, and he went out on his behalf, while Abū Lahab stayed behind.\nIbn Isḥāq said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Najīḥ narrated to me that Umayyah ibn Khalaf had resolved to stay behind. He was an old, dignified, heavy-bodied man. ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ came to him while he was sitting in the mosque among his people, carrying an incense burner containing fire and incense. He placed it in front of him and said, “O Abū ʿAlī, perfume yourself with incense, for you are only one of the women.” He said, “May Allah disgrace you and disgrace what you have brought.” Then he prepared and went out with the people."],[288,"Allah the Exalted said: ﴿كَمَاۤ اَخْرَجَكَ رَبُّكَ مِنْۢ بَيْتِكَ بِالْحَقِّ١۪ وَ اِنَّ فَرِيْقًا مِّنَ الْمُؤْمِنِيْنَ لَكٰرِهُوْنَۙ۰۰۵ يُجَادِلُوْنَكَ فِي الْحَقِّ بَعْدَ مَا تَبَيَّنَ كَاَنَّمَا يُسَاقُوْنَ اِلَى الْمَوْتِ وَ هُمْ يَنْظُرُوْنَؕ۰۰۶ وَ اِذْ يَعِدُكُمُ اللّٰهُ اِحْدَى الطَّآىِٕفَتَيْنِ اَنَّهَا لَكُمْ وَ تَوَدُّوْنَ اَنَّ غَيْرَ ذَاتِ الشَّوْكَةِ تَكُوْنُ لَكُمْ وَ يُرِيْدُ اللّٰهُ اَنْ يُّحِقَّ الْحَقَّ بِكَلِمٰتِهٖ وَ يَقْطَعَ دَابِرَ الْكٰفِرِيْنَۙ۰۰۷﴾ [al-Anfāl: 5-7]\nThe statement of Allah the Exalted: Just as (on the occasion of Badr) your Lord brought you out of your home (Madinah) with the truth (the best plan), though indeed a group of the believers (at that time) disliked (this going out). They disputed with you about the truth (the matter) after it had become clear, as though they were being driven towards death while they looked on. And when Allah was promising you that one of the two groups would be yours, you wished that the unarmed one (the trade caravan) would be yours, while Allah intended to establish the truth by His words and to cut off the root of the disbelievers.\nThe two groups refer to the caravan of Abū Sufyān on one side and the army of the Quraysh on the other."],[289,"Anas narrated that when news reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Abū Sufyān was approaching, he consulted the people. Abū Bakr spoke, but he turned away from him. Then ʿUmar spoke, but he turned away from him. Then Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah stood and said, “Do you mean us, O Messenger of Allah? By the One in Whose hand is my soul, if you commanded us to plunge into the sea, we would plunge into it; and if you commanded us to drive our mounts to Bark al-Ghimād, we would do so.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then called the people to go out, and they set out until they encamped at Badr. The water-camels of Quraysh came to them, and among them was a black slave belonging to Banū al-Ḥajjāj. They seized him, and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began asking him about Abū Sufyān and his companions. He would say, “I have no knowledge of Abū Sufyān, but here are Abū Jahl, ʿUtbah, Shaybah, and Umayyah ibn Khalaf.” When he said that, they would beat him. So he would say, “Yes, I will tell you; this is Abū Sufyān.” When they left him and then asked him again, he would say, “I have no knowledge of Abū Sufyān, but here are Abū Jahl, ʿUtbah, Shaybah, and Umayyah ibn Khalaf among the people.” When he said that again, they beat him, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was standing in prayer. When he saw that, he turned away from prayer and said, “By the One in Whose hand is my soul, you beat him when he tells you the truth and leave him when he lies to you.” Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “This is the place where so-and-so will fall,” placing his hand on the ground here and here. Anas said: Not one of them moved away from the place where the hand of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had indicated."],[290,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out toward Badr, he consulted the people. Abū Bakr gave him his opinion, then he consulted them again and ʿUmar gave him his opinion. Then he remained silent. A man from the Anṣār said: “He is referring to you.” They said: “O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, we will not be like the Children of Israel who said to Mūsā: ‘So go, you and your Lord, and fight. Indeed, we are sitting right here.’ [al-Māʾidah: 24] Rather, by Allah, even if you were to drive the camels until you reached Bark al-Ghimād, we would certainly be with you.”"],[291,"Ibn Masʿūd narrated: I witnessed from al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad a scene that I would love to have been the one who did it more than anything equal to it. He came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was supplicating against the polytheists and said, “We will not say as the people of Mūsā said: ‘So go, you and your Lord, and fight; indeed, we are sitting right here.’ Rather, we will fight on your right, on your left, in front of you, and behind you.” I saw the face of the Prophet ﷺ brighten, and he was pleased by what he said."],[292,"Thābit narrated from Anas ibn Mālik: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Busaysah as a scout to see what the caravan of Abū Sufyān was doing. He came back, and there was no one in the house except me and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Anas said: I do not know whether he made an exception for some of his wives. He said: Busaysah told him the news. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out, spoke, and said, “We have a pursuit; whoever has his mount ready should ride with us.” Some men began asking his permission to go to their mounts in the upper parts of Madinah, but he said, “No, only whoever has his mount ready.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions set out until they reached ahead of the polytheists. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “None of you should advance toward anything until I am before him.” When the polytheists drew near, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Rise toward a Paradise whose width is the heavens and the earth.” ʿUmayr ibn al-Ḥumām al-Anṣārī said, “O Messenger of Allah, a Paradise whose width is the heavens and the earth?” He said, “Yes.” He said, “Bakh, bakh!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “What made you say ‘bakh, bakh’?” He said, “Nothing, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, except the hope that I will be among its people.” He said, “You are among its people.” So he took some dates from his quiver and began eating from them, then said, “If I live until I finish eating these dates, that would be a long life.” He threw away the dates he had with him, then fought them until he was killed."],[293,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered the bells to be cut from the necks of the camels on the Day of Badr."],[294,"Ibn Isḥāq relates that Sayyidunā al-Ḥubāb ibn al-Mundhir ibn al-Jamūḥ, may Allah be pleased with him, said: “O Messenger of Allah! Is this place where the camp has been set up something revealed by Allah the Exalted? If so, we will not move one step forward or backward from here. Or is the choice of this place merely a war tactic, a personal opinion and a strategy?” He said: “It is an opinion, a war tactic and a strategy.” He said: “O Messenger of Allah! Then this place is not suitable for fighting. The people should be made to rise from here and camp near the water. Staying there, we will fill in the other wells. We will build a basin over our well and fill it with water. When we fight the people, we will have water, and the enemy will be deprived of water.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “You have given the best opinion.”\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the fighters rose from the former place and went and camped at the well near the enemy. The other wells were closed by his order. A basin was built over one well and filled with water. Then the vessels used for drawing water were also thrown into the well, so that the enemy could not draw water.\nIn some narrations, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ formally asked: “What is your opinion about this place?” Sayyidunā al-Ḥubāb ibn al-Mundhir al-Sulamī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: “O Messenger of Allah! I know this area and its wells. If you permit, we will go and camp near one well. I know that the water of this well is plentiful and sweet. We will reach there before the enemy and camp. We will close all the wells except one.” He said: “Let us go. Allah the Exalted has promised victory over one of the two groups.” Hearing this, the people were quite afraid.\nThe Muslims and the polytheists made every possible effort to obtain water. Allah the Exalted sent down rain that very night. This rain became a cause of severe distress and hardship for the polytheists. Walking on the wet mud became extremely difficult for them. Light rain fell on the Muslims’ side, which made the ground firm and made walking easy for them. The Muslims reached the well first. They stayed there all night. The well was gradually filled in. Its water became cool and rose to the top. A basin was built beside the well and filled with water, and the wells were closed.\nImam al-Ḥākim narrated that al-Ḥubāb ibn al-Mundhir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: “At the Battle of Badr I gave the Messenger of Allah ﷺ my opinion on two matters. He accepted both opinions. I was also in the army with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He camped far from the water. I said: ‘O Messenger of Allah! Is this based on revelation or is it only an opinion?’ He said: ‘Ḥubāb! It is my opinion.’ I said: ‘If it is an opinion, then let us go beyond the water and camp. If there is need of water, it will be with us.’ He accepted my opinion. In the meantime, Jibrīl, peace be upon him, descended upon him ﷺ. He said to him: ‘Which of the two matters do you prefer? Do you wish to remain in the world with your Companions, or do you wish to go to your Lord and benefit from the blessings of Paradise that have been promised?’ He consulted his Companions. The fighters said: ‘O Messenger of Allah! Your being with us is dearer to us than the whole world. Tell us about the weak points of the enemy, pray for our help and victory over them, and inform us of the revelation that comes from the heavens.’ The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘Ḥubāb! You are not saying anything.’ Then I said: ‘O Messenger of Allah! Choose what Allah the Exalted has chosen.’ He approved my opinion and accepted it.” Imam al-Dhahabī, may Allah be pleased with him, called it munkar in his Talkhīṣ."],[295,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās narrated that the Prophet ﷺ spoke while he was in a tent on the Day of Badr, and he mentioned the supplication."],[296,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated: Then during the night a light rain fell upon us, so we went under the trees and shields to take shelter from the rain. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ spent the night supplicating to his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, saying, “O Allah, if You destroy this group, You will not be worshiped.”"],[297,"Al-Barāʾ narrated: The Companions of Muhammad ﷺ who witnessed Badr told me that they were the same number as the companions of Ṭālūt who crossed the river with him: three hundred and a little over ten. Al-Barāʾ said: No, by Allah, none crossed the river with him except a believer."],[298,"Al-Barāʾ narrated: Ibn ʿUmar and I were deemed too young on the Day of Badr. The Muhājirūn on the Day of Badr were a little over sixty, and the Anṣār were a little over two hundred and forty."],[299,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb narrated: On the Day of Badr, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at the polytheists, and they were one thousand, while his Companions were three hundred and nineteen men."],[300,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out on the Day of Badr with three hundred and fifteen men. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “O Allah, they are barefoot, so provide mounts for them. O Allah, they are unclothed, so clothe them. O Allah, they are hungry, so feed them.” Allah granted him victory on the Day of Badr, and when they returned, not one of them returned except with one or two camels; they were clothed and fed."],[301,"Abū Mūsā narrated: The number of the people of Badr was the same as the number of the companions of Ṭālūt on the day of Jālūt: three hundred and seventeen."],[302,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd narrated: On the Day of Badr, every three of us shared one camel. Abū Lubābah and ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib were the two who shared with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: It was the turn of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They said, “We will walk for you.” He said, “You two are not stronger than me, and I am not less in need of reward than you.”"],[303,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated: There was no horseman among us on the Day of Badr except al-Miqdād. I saw us all asleep, except the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who was beneath a tree praying and weeping until morning."],[304,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib said to him: We had only two horses with us: a horse belonging to al-Zubayr and a horse belonging to al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad, meaning on the Day of Badr."],[305,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave the white banner to Sayyidunā Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr, may Allah be pleased with him, and gave two black banners to Sayyidunā ʿAlī and Sayyidunā Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, may Allah be pleased with them both. Ibn Isḥāq mentioned this. It is narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entrusted the standard to ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, on the day of Badr, when he was twenty years old."],[306,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib narrated: Ibn ʿUmar and I were deemed too young on the Day of Badr."],[307,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ narrated that the Prophet ﷺ looked at ʿUmayr ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and deemed him too young when he set out for Badr, then later permitted him. Saʿd said: It is said that his sword failed him. ʿAbdullāh said: He was killed on the Day of Badr."],[308,"Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān narrated: Nothing prevented me from witnessing Badr except that I went out with my father Ḥusayl, and the disbelievers of Quraysh seized us. They said, “You intend to go to Muhammad?” We said, “We do not intend him; we only intend Madinah.” They took from us Allah’s covenant and pledge that we would go to Madinah and not fight alongside him. We came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him of what happened. He said, “Go back. We will fulfill their covenant, and we will seek Allah’s help against them.”"],[309,"ʿAlī narrated: We captured two men on the Day of Badr: a man from Quraysh and a freedman of ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ. As for the Qurashī, he escaped, but we captured the freedman of ʿUqbah. We kept saying to him, “How many are the people?” He would say, “By Allah, they are many in number and strong in might.” Whenever he said that, the Muslims would beat him, until they brought him to the Prophet ﷺ. He said, “How many are the people?” He said, “By Allah, they are many in number and strong in might.” The Prophet ﷺ tried hard to get him to tell how many they were, but he refused. Then the Prophet ﷺ asked him, “How many camels do they slaughter?” He said, “Ten every day.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “The people are one thousand; every camel is for one hundred and those who follow them.”"],[310,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated: I saw us on the Day of Badr taking refuge close to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while he was the nearest of us to the enemy, and he was among the fiercest of the people that day in battle."],[311,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to al-Zubayr on the Day of al-Yarmūk, “Will you not charge so that we may charge with you?” He said, “If I charge, you will not follow through.” They said, “We will.” So he charged against them until he split their ranks, passed beyond them with no one with him, then returned facing them. They grabbed the reins of his horse and struck him twice on his shoulder, between which was a wound he had received on the Day of Badr. ʿUrwah said: When I was small, I used to put my fingers into those wounds while playing. ʿUrwah said: ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr was with him that day, and he was ten years old, so he placed him on a horse and assigned a man to watch over him."],[312,"ʿUrwah narrated: Al-Zubayr had three sword wounds, one of them on his shoulder. He said: I used to put my fingers into it. He said: He received two wounds on the Day of Badr and one on the Day of al-Yarmūk. ʿUrwah said: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Marwān said to me when ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr was killed, “O ʿUrwah, do you recognize the sword of al-Zubayr?” I said, “Yes.” He said, “What is in it?” I said, “It has a notch that was made on the Day of Badr.” He said, “You have spoken the truth.” “It bears notches from striking against battalions.” Then he returned it to ʿUrwah. Hishām said: We valued it among ourselves at three thousand, and one of us took it. I wish I had taken it."],[313,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated: I will be the first to kneel before the Most Merciful for dispute on the Day of Resurrection. Qays ibn ʿUbād said: Concerning them was revealed: “These are two opposing parties who disputed concerning their Lord” [al-Ḥajj: 19]. He said: They are those who engaged in single combat on the Day of Badr: Ḥamzah, ʿAlī, and ʿUbaydah, or Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Ḥārith; and Shaybah ibn Rabīʿah, ʿUtbah, and al-Walīd ibn ʿUtbah."],[314,"Qays ibn ʿUbād narrated: I heard Abū Dharr swear emphatically that this verse: “These are two opposing parties who disputed concerning their Lord” [al-Ḥajj: 19] was revealed concerning those who came out for single combat on the Day of Badr: Ḥamzah, ʿAlī, ʿUbaydah ibn al-Ḥārith, ʿUtbah and Shaybah the two sons of Rabīʿah, and al-Walīd ibn ʿUtbah."],[315,"Abū Isḥāq narrated: A man asked al-Barāʾ while I was listening, “Did ʿAlī witness Badr?” He said, “He fought in single combat and wore armor over armor.”"],[316,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated: He advanced, meaning ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah, and his son and brother followed him. He called out, “Who will come out for single combat?” Some young men from the Anṣār stepped forward to face him. He said, “Who are you?” They told him. He said, “We have no need of you; we only want our cousins.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Stand, O Ḥamzah; stand, O ʿAlī; stand, O ʿUbaydah ibn al-Ḥārith.” Ḥamzah advanced toward ʿUtbah, and I advanced toward Shaybah. Between ʿUbaydah and al-Walīd there were two blows, and each severely wounded the other. Then we turned against al-Walīd, killed him, and carried ʿUbaydah away."],[317,"Usayd narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to us on the Day of Badr, “When they come close to you, shoot them, and preserve your arrows.” In another wording: The Prophet ﷺ said on the Day of Badr when we lined up against Quraysh and they lined up against us, “When they come close to you, then use arrows.”"],[318,"Abū ʿImrān al-Tujībī narrated that he heard Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī say: We lined up on the Day of Badr, and a group from us advanced ahead of the row. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at them and said, “With me, with me,” like this. Maʿmar said: “A group from us rushed forward.” And he said: “We lined up on the Day of Badr.”"],[319,"Allah the Exalted said: ﴿وَ لَقَدْ نَصَرَكُمُ اللّٰهُ بِبَدْرٍ وَّ اَنْتُمْ اَذِلَّةٌ١ۚ فَاتَّقُوا اللّٰهَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُوْنَ۰۰۱۲۳ اِذْ تَقُوْلُ لِلْمُؤْمِنِيْنَ۠ اَلَنْ يَّكْفِيَكُمْ اَنْ يُّمِدَّكُمْ رَبُّكُمْ بِثَلٰثَةِ اٰلٰفٍ مِّنَ الْمَلٰٓىِٕكَةِ مُنْزَلِيْنَؕ۰۰۱۲۴ بَلٰۤى١ۙ اِنْ تَصْبِرُوْا وَ تَتَّقُوْا وَ يَاْتُوْكُمْ مِّنْ فَوْرِهِمْ هٰذَا يُمْدِدْكُمْ رَبُّكُمْ بِخَمْسَةِ اٰلٰفٍ مِّنَ الْمَلٰٓىِٕكَةِ مُسَوِّمِيْنَ۰۰۱۲۵ وَ مَا جَعَلَهُ اللّٰهُ اِلَّا بُشْرٰى لَكُمْ وَ لِتَطْمَىِٕنَّ قُلُوْبُكُمْ بِهٖ١ؕ وَ مَا النَّصْرُ اِلَّا مِنْ عِنْدِ اللّٰهِ الْعَزِيْزِ الْحَكِيْمِۙ۰۰۱۲۶﴾ [Āl ʿImrān: 123-126]\nThe Exalted Lord says: And Allah helped you at Badr at the very time when you were weak. So fear Allah, so that you may be enabled to give thanks. (O Prophet!) When you were saying to the believers: Is it not sufficient for you that your Lord should reinforce you with three thousand angels sent down from the heavens? Yes indeed! If you are patient and fear Allah, and the enemy come upon you at once, then at that very moment your Lord will reinforce you with five thousand angels bearing (distinctive) marks. And Allah made it only glad tidings for you, so that your hearts might be reassured by it. And victory comes only from Allah, the Mighty, the Wise.\nAnd the Exalted said: ﴿اِذْ تَسْتَغِيْثُوْنَ۠ رَبَّكُمْ فَاسْتَجَابَ لَكُمْ اَنِّيْ مُمِدُّكُمْ بِاَلْفٍ مِّنَ الْمَلٰٓىِٕكَةِ مُرْدِفِيْنَ۰۰۹ وَ مَا جَعَلَهُ اللّٰهُ اِلَّا بُشْرٰى وَ لِتَطْمَىِٕنَّ بِهٖ قُلُوْبُكُمْ١ۚ وَ مَا النَّصْرُ اِلَّا مِنْ عِنْدِ اللّٰهِ١ؕ اِنَّ اللّٰهَ عَزِيْزٌ حَكِيْمٌؒ۰۰۱۰ اِذْ يُغَشِّيْكُمُ النُّعَاسَ اَمَنَةً مِّنْهُ وَ يُنَزِّلُ عَلَيْكُمْ مِّنَ السَّمَآءِ مَآءً لِّيُطَهِّرَكُمْ۠ بِهٖ وَ يُذْهِبَ عَنْكُمْ رِجْزَ الشَّيْطٰنِ وَ لِيَرْبِطَ عَلٰى قُلُوْبِكُمْ وَ يُثَبِّتَ بِهِ الْاَقْدَامَؕ۰۰۱۱ اِذْ يُوْحِيْ رَبُّكَ اِلَى الْمَلٰٓىِٕكَةِ اَنِّيْ مَعَكُمْ فَثَبِّتُوا الَّذِيْنَ اٰمَنُوْا١ؕ سَاُلْقِيْ فِيْ قُلُوْبِ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوا الرُّعْبَ فَاضْرِبُوْا فَوْقَ الْاَعْنَاقِ وَ اضْرِبُوْا مِنْهُمْ كُلَّ بَنَانٍؕ۰۰۱۲ ذٰلِكَ بِاَنَّهُمْ شَآقُّوا اللّٰهَ وَ رَسُوْلَهٗ١ۚ وَ مَنْ يُّشَاقِقِ اللّٰهَ وَ رَسُوْلَهٗ فَاِنَّ اللّٰهَ شَدِيْدُ الْعِقَابِ۰۰۱۳﴾ [al-Anfāl: 9-13]"],[320,"The divine statement: (Remember) when you were seeking help from your Lord, and He responded to your plea (and said) that: Indeed, I will reinforce you with a thousand angels, following one another. And Allah made this (help) only glad tidings so that your hearts might be reassured by it, and help is only with Allah. Indeed, Allah is Mighty, All-Wise. (Remember) when He (Allah) was causing drowsiness to overcome you as a reassurance and security from Him, and was sending down upon you rain from the sky to purify you with it and remove from you the whisperings of Satan, and to strengthen your hearts, and to keep (you) firm by it. (O Prophet!) When your Lord was revealing to the angels: Indeed, I am with you, so keep firm those who believe. I will cast terror into the hearts of those who disbelieved, so strike them upon the necks, and strike every fingertip of theirs. That is because they opposed Allah and His Messenger, and whoever opposes Allah and His Messenger, then indeed Allah is severe in punishment.\nIn the divine statement ﴿وَّ اَنْتُمْ اَذِلَّةٌ﴾ (while you were weak), the word adhillah is the plural of dhalīl, just as aʿizzah is the plural of ʿazīz. Allah the Exalted described the Muslims with the word adhillah because of their small number, since they were about three hundred and ten, whereas the disbelievers numbered between nine hundred and a thousand.\nThe divine statement: ﴿اِذْ تَقُوْلُ لِلْمُؤْمِنِيْنَ۠ اَلَنْ يَّكْفِيَكُمْ اَنْ يُّمِدَّكُمْ رَبُّكُمْ بِثَلٰثَةِ اٰلٰفٍ مِّنَ الْمَلٰٓىِٕكَةِ مُنْزَلِيْنَؕ۰۰۱۲۴﴾ (Āl ʿImrān: 124). The correct view concerning this is that it refers to the Battle of Badr. Allah the Exalted helped the Muslims with a thousand angels, then they became three thousand, and then five thousand."],[321,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said while he was in a tent on the Day of Badr, “I implore You by Your covenant and Your promise. O Allah, if You will, You will not be worshiped after today ever again.” Abū Bakr took his hand and said, “Enough, O Messenger of Allah, for you have persisted in imploring your Lord,” while he was wearing armor. He then came out reciting: “The group will be defeated, and they will turn their backs. Rather, the Hour is their appointment, and the Hour is more disastrous and more bitter.” [al-Qamar: 45-46]"],[322,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb narrated: On the Day of Badr, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at the polytheists, and they were one thousand, while his Companions were three hundred and nineteen men. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ faced the qiblah, stretched out his hands, and began calling upon his Lord: “O Allah, fulfill for me what You promised me. O Allah, bring what You promised me. O Allah, if You destroy this group from the people of Islam, You will not be worshiped on earth.” He continued calling upon his Lord, stretching out his hands and facing the qiblah, until his cloak fell from his shoulders. Abū Bakr came to him, took his cloak, placed it back on his shoulders, then embraced him from behind and said, “O Prophet of Allah, enough of your imploring your Lord, for He will surely fulfill for you what He promised you.” Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: “When you were seeking help from your Lord, and He answered you: ‘I will reinforce you with a thousand angels following one another’” [al-Anfāl: 9]. So Allah reinforced him with the angels. Abū Zumayl said: Ibn ʿAbbās narrated to me: While a Muslim man that day was pursuing a polytheist in front of him, he heard the crack of a whip above him and the voice of a rider saying, “Advance, Ḥayzūm!” He looked at the polytheist in front of him, and he had fallen flat on his back. He looked at him and saw that his nose had been struck and his face split as if by the blow of a whip, and the whole of that had turned green. The Anṣārī came and told that to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said, “You have spoken the truth. That was from the reinforcement of the third heaven.” They killed seventy that day and captured seventy. Abū Zumayl said: Ibn ʿAbbās said: When they captured the prisoners, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, “What do you think concerning these prisoners?” Abū Bakr said, “O Prophet of Allah, they are our cousins and kin. I think you should take ransom from them, so it may be a source of strength for us against the disbelievers, and perhaps Allah will guide them to Islam.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “What do you think, O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb?” I said, “No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah. I do not see what Abū Bakr sees. Rather, I think you should give us power over them so we strike their necks. Give ʿAlī power over ʿAqīl so he may strike his neck, and give me power over so-and-so, a relative of ʿUmar, so I may strike his neck, for these are the leaders and chiefs of disbelief.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ inclined to what Abū Bakr said and did not incline to what I said.\nThe next day, I came and found the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr sitting and weeping. I said, “O Messenger of Allah, tell me what makes you and your companion weep. If I find reason to weep, I will weep; and if I do not find reason to weep, I will make myself weep because of your weeping.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “I weep because of what was presented to me regarding your companions taking ransom. Their punishment was presented to me closer than this tree,” a tree near the Prophet of Allah ﷺ. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: “It is not for a prophet to have captives until he has thoroughly subdued the enemy in the land,” up to His saying: “So eat of what you have taken as spoils, lawful and good” [al-Anfāl: 67-69]. Thus Allah made the spoils lawful for them."],[323,"ʿAlī narrated: When we came to Madinah, we ate from its fruits, but the climate did not suit us and we were afflicted with fever. The Prophet ﷺ was seeking news about Badr. When news reached us that the polytheists had advanced, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ marched to Badr, and Badr was a well. We reached it before the polytheists. We found two men from them there: a man from Quraysh and a freedman of ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ. As for the Qurashī, he escaped; as for the freedman of ʿUqbah, we captured him. We kept saying to him, “How many are the people?” He would say, “By Allah, they are many in number and strong in might.” Whenever he said that, the Muslims would beat him, until they brought him to the Prophet ﷺ. He said to him, “How many are the people?” He said, “By Allah, they are many in number and strong in might.” The Prophet ﷺ tried hard to get him to tell how many they were, but he refused. Then the Prophet ﷺ asked him, “How many camels do they slaughter?” He said, “Ten every day.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “The people are one thousand; every camel is for one hundred and those who follow them.” Then during the night a light rain fell upon us, so we went under the trees and shields to shelter from the rain. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ spent the night supplicating to his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, saying, “O Allah, if You destroy this group, You will not be worshiped.” When dawn rose, he called out, “Prayer, servants of Allah.” The people came out from beneath the trees and shields, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer and urged us to fight. Then he said, “The gathering of Quraysh is beneath this red side of the mountain.” When the people drew near to us and we formed ranks against them, a man among them on a red camel was moving among the people. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “O ʿAlī, call Ḥamzah for me,” and he was the closest of them to the polytheists, “Who is the owner of the red camel, and what is he saying to them?” Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “If there is anyone among the people commanding good, perhaps it is the owner of the red camel.” Ḥamzah came and said, “It is ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah. He is forbidding them from fighting and saying to them: ‘O people, I see a people who are ready to die. You will not reach them while any good remains among you. O people, pin this matter on me today, and say: ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah became cowardly; and you know that I am not the most cowardly among you.’” Abū Jahl heard that and said, “Are you saying this? By Allah, if someone else had said this, I would have made him bite on it. Your lungs have filled your belly with fear.” ʿUtbah said, “Are you mocking me, O one whose backside is yellowed? Today you will know which of us is the coward.” Then ʿUtbah, his brother Shaybah, and his son al-Walīd came out in pride and said, “Who will fight in single combat?” Six young men from the Anṣār came out, but ʿUtbah said, “We do not want these; rather, let those from our cousins, from Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, fight us.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Stand, O ʿAlī; stand, O Ḥamzah; stand, O ʿUbaydah ibn al-Ḥārith ibn al-Muṭṭalib.” Allah Most High caused ʿUtbah and Shaybah, the two sons of Rabīʿah, and al-Walīd ibn ʿUtbah to be killed, while ʿUbaydah was wounded. We killed seventy of them and captured seventy. A short man from the Anṣār brought al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib as a prisoner. Al-ʿAbbās said, “O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, this man did not capture me. Rather, I was captured by a bald-fronted man with one of the most beautiful faces among people, riding a piebald horse. I do not see him among the people.” The Anṣārī said, “I captured him, O Messenger of Allah.” He said, “Be silent, for Allah Most High supported you with a noble angel.” ʿAlī said: We captured from Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib: al-ʿAbbās, ʿAqīl, and Nawfal ibn al-Ḥārith."],[324,"Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ al-Zuraqī narrated: Jibrīl came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “How do you regard the people of Badr among you?” He said, “Among the best of the Muslims,” or a similar phrase. He said, “And likewise are those angels who witnessed Badr.”"],[325,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said on the Day of Badr, “This is Jibrīl, holding the head of his horse, wearing the equipment of war.”"],[326,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me and to Abū Bakr on the Day of Badr, “With one of you is Jibrīl, and with the other is Mīkāʾīl, and Isrāfīl is a mighty angel who witnesses the battle, or is present in the battle.”"],[327,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Thaʿlabah ibn Ṣuʿayr narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dozed briefly in the shelter, then woke and said, “Receive glad tidings, O Abū Bakr. This is Jibrīl, wearing his turban, holding the reins of his horse and leading it, with dust upon its front teeth. Allah’s victory and His promise have come to you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then commanded, took a handful of pebbles in his hand, came out, faced the people, and said, “May the faces be disfigured.” Then he threw it at them and said to his Companions, “Charge.” It was then only defeat. Allah killed those He killed among their chiefs and caused those who were captured to be captured."],[328,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his hand on the Day of Badr and said, “O Lord, if You destroy this group, You will never be worshiped on earth.” Jibrīl, peace be upon him, said to him, “Take a handful of dust.” So he took a handful of dust and threw it into their faces. There was not a single one of the polytheists except that dust from that handful struck his eyes, nostrils, and mouth, so they turned away fleeing."],[329,"Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām narrated: On the Day of Badr, we heard a sound falling from the sky to the earth, like the sound of a pebble falling into a basin. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ threw those pebbles, and they were defeated. That is the saying of Allah Most High: “And you did not throw when you threw, but Allah threw, so that He might test the believers with a good test from Him.”"],[330,"Abū Ṭalḥah narrated: Drowsiness overcame us while we were in our battle rows on the Day of Badr. Abū Ṭalḥah said: I was among those overcome by drowsiness that day. My sword kept falling from my hand, and I would pick it up; it would fall, and I would pick it up again."],[331,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: Abū Jahl said: “O Allah, if this is indeed the truth from You, then rain down upon us stones from the sky or bring us a painful punishment.” [al-Anfāl: 32] Then the following was revealed: “But Allah would not punish them while you are among them, nor would Allah punish them while they seek forgiveness. And why should Allah not punish them while they prevent people from al-Masjid al-Ḥarām?” [al-Anfāl: 33–34]"],[332,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Thaʿlabah ibn Ṣuʿayr narrated that Abū Jahl said when the two groups met: “O Allah, whoever among us has severed the ties of kinship more and has come with what is more unknown, destroy him this morning.” Thus he was the one seeking judgment."],[333,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: When the Muslims encamped at Badr and the polytheists approached, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah while he was upon a red camel and said, “If there is any good with anyone among the people, it is with the owner of the red camel; if they obey him, they will be rightly guided.” He was saying, “O people, obey me regarding these people. If you do this, it will remain in your hearts: each man will look at the killer of his brother and the killer of his father. So put its cowardice upon my head and return.” Abū Jahl said, “By Allah, his lungs have swollen with fear when he saw Muhammad and his Companions. Muhammad and his Companions are only like the meal of one slaughtered camel if we meet.” ʿUtbah said, “You will know who is the coward who ruins his people. By Allah, I see a people who will strike you with blows. Do you not see that their heads are like serpents and their faces are like swords?” Then he called his brother and his son, went out walking between them, and called for single combat."],[334,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ spent the night supplicating and saying, “O Allah, if You destroy this group, You will not be worshiped on earth.” When dawn rose, he said, “Prayer, servants of Allah.” So we came from beneath the trees and shields. He urged us to fight and said, “It is as if I am looking at the places where they will fall dead.” When the people drew near, there was a man moving among them on a red camel. The Prophet ﷺ said to al-Zubayr, “Call one of your companions and ask him who the owner of the red camel is; if there is anyone among the people commanding good, it is he.” Al-Zubayr asked, “Who is the owner of the red camel?” They said, “ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah. He is forbidding them from fighting and saying: ‘O people, I see a people ready to die. By Allah, I do not think you will reach them until you are destroyed.’” When what he was saying reached Abū Jahl, he came to him and said, “Your lungs have filled with terror when you saw Muhammad and his Companions.” ʿUtbah said to him, “Do you mean me, O one whose backside is yellowed? You will know which of us is more cowardly.” Then he dismounted from his camel, and his brother Shaybah and his son al-Walīd followed him, and they called for single combat. Then he mentioned the full ḥadīth."],[335,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf narrated: While I was standing in the row on the Day of Badr, I looked to my right and left, and I found myself between two young boys from the Anṣār, both very young in age. I wished I had been between men stronger than them. One of them nudged me and said, “Uncle, do you know Abū Jahl?” I said, “Yes. What do you need from him, my nephew?” He said, “I have been told that he insults the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, if I see him, my body will not part from his body until the quicker of us dies.” I was amazed at that. Then the other nudged me and said the same thing. It was not long before I saw Abū Jahl moving among the people, so I said, “Do you not see? This is the one you are asking about.” They rushed toward him and struck him with their swords until they killed him. Then they returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him. He said, “Which of you killed him?” Each one said, “I killed him.” He said, “Have you wiped your swords?” They said, “No.” He looked at the two swords and said, “Both of you killed him.” He awarded his spoils to Muʿādh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Jamūḥ. The two men were Muʿādh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Jamūḥ and Muʿādh ibn ʿAfrāʾ."],[336,"Anas narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Who will go and see for us what happened to Abū Jahl?” Ibn Masʿūd went and found that the two sons of ʿAfrāʾ had struck him until he had become cold. He took hold of his beard and said, “Are you Abū Jahl?” He said, “Is there anyone greater than a man whom you have killed?” Or he said, “whom his people killed?” Abū Mijlaz said: Abū Jahl said, “If only someone other than a farmer had killed me.”"],[337,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd narrated that he came to Abū Jahl while there was still some life in him on the Day of Badr. Abū Jahl said, “Is there any man greater than a man whom you have killed?”"],[338,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated: Al-Zubayr said: On the Day of Badr I met ʿUbaydah ibn Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ. He was fully armored, and nothing could be seen of him except his eyes. He was known as Abū Dhāt al-Karsh. He said, “I am Abū Dhāt al-Karsh.” I charged at him with a short spear and stabbed him in the eye, and he died. Hishām said: I was informed that al-Zubayr said: I placed my foot on him and then pulled with effort, and it was difficult for me to pull it out, while its two ends had bent. ʿUrwah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him for it, and he gave it to him. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, he took it. Then Abū Bakr requested it, and he gave it to him. When Abū Bakr passed away, ʿUmar asked him for it, and he gave it to him. When ʿUmar passed away, he took it. Then ʿUthmān requested it from him, and he gave it to him. When ʿUthmān was killed, it came into the possession of the family of ʿAlī. Then ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr requested it, and it remained with him until he was killed."],[339,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf narrated: I made a written agreement with Umayyah ibn Khalaf that he would protect my property and dependents in Makkah, and I would protect his property and dependents in Madinah. When I mentioned “al-Raḥmān,” he said, “I do not know al-Raḥmān. Write to me with your name that you had in the Jāhiliyyah.” So I wrote to him as “ʿAbd ʿAmr.” When it was the Day of Badr, I went out toward a mountain to protect him while the people were asleep. Bilāl saw him and went out until he stood before a gathering of the Anṣār and said, “Umayyah ibn Khalaf! May I not survive if Umayyah survives.” A group from the Anṣār went out with him following us. When I feared they would catch us, I left his son behind for them to occupy them, so they killed him. Then they refused except to continue following us, and Umayyah was a heavy man. When they caught up with us, I said to him, “Kneel down.” He knelt, and I threw myself over him to protect him. They covered him with swords from beneath me until they killed him, and one of them struck my foot with his sword. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf used to show us that scar on the top of his foot."],[340,"Ibn Masʿūd narrated: The first sūrah in which a prostration was revealed was “By the star” [al-Najm]. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrated, and those behind him prostrated, except for one man whom I saw take a handful of dust and prostrate upon it. I later saw him killed as a disbeliever, and he was Umayyah ibn Khalaf."],[341,"Ibrāhīm narrated: Al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Qays wanted to appoint Masrūq to a position, so ʿUmārah ibn ʿUqbah said to him, “Will you appoint a man from the remnants of the killers of ʿUthmān?” Masrūq said to him, “ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd narrated to us—and in our view he was reliable in ḥadīth—that when the Prophet ﷺ wanted to kill your father, he said, ‘Who will take care of the children?’ He said, ‘The Fire.’ So I am pleased for you with what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was pleased for you.”"],[342,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ accepted ransom for the prisoners of Badr, and the ransom for each man among them was four thousand. He killed ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ before the ransom. ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib stood over him and killed him as a captive. He said, “Who will take care of the children, O Messenger of Allah?” He said, “The Fire.”"],[343,"What is narrated from Sayyidunā Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, that the disbelievers were overtaken by a fierce, violent wind at the Battle of Badr, this statement is weak.\nImam Aḥmad narrated it in his Musnad (22762) through the route of Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr, from Sayyidunā ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allah be pleased with him. Imam Ibn Isḥāq relates that Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I asked ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit about Sūrat al-Anfāl. He said: It was revealed about us, the people of Badr, when we disagreed about the spoils of war and the disagreement became intense, so that wealth of spoils was handed over to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He divided it equally among all the Muslims."],[344,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated: We were with ʿUmar between Makkah and Madinah, and we looked for the crescent moon. I was a man with sharp eyesight, so I saw it, while no one else claimed to have seen it. I kept saying to ʿUmar, “Do you not see it?” But he did not see it. ʿUmar said, “I will see it while reclining on my bed.” Then he began telling us about the people of Badr and said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had shown us the places where the people of Badr would fall the day before, saying, “This is the place where so-and-so will fall tomorrow, if Allah wills.” ʿUmar said: By the One Who sent him with the truth, they did not miss the boundaries that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had marked. He said: They were placed in a well, one upon another. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went until he reached them and said, “O so-and-so son of so-and-so, and O so-and-so son of so-and-so, have you found what Allah and His Messenger promised you to be true? Indeed, I have found what Allah promised me to be true.” ʿUmar said, “O Messenger of Allah, how do you speak to bodies that have no souls?” He said, “You do not hear what I say better than they do, except that they are unable to reply to me.”"],[345,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ faced the House and supplicated against six men from Quraysh, among them Abū Jahl, Umayyah ibn Khalaf, ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah, Shaybah ibn Rabīʿah, and ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ. By Allah, I saw them lying dead at Badr, changed by the sun, and it was a hot day."],[346,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: The chiefs of Quraysh gathered in al-Ḥijr and made a pact by al-Lāt, al-ʿUzzā, Manāt the third, the other, Nāʾilah, and Isāf: “If we see Muhammad, we will rise against him as one man and will not leave him until we kill him.” His daughter Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her, came crying until she entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “These chiefs of Quraysh have made a pact against you. If they see you, they will rise against you and kill you. There is not a man among them except that he has known his share of your blood.” He said, “My daughter, bring me water for wuḍūʾ.” He performed wuḍūʾ, then entered the mosque upon them. When they saw him, they said, “There he is.” They lowered their gazes, their chins fell to their chests, and they were fixed in their seats. None of them raised his gaze to him, and not one of them stood up to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came until he stood over their heads, took a handful of dust, and said, “May the faces be disfigured,” then threw it at them. No man among them was struck by a pebble from that dust except that he was killed as a disbeliever on the Day of Badr."],[347,"Abū Ṭalḥah narrated that on the Day of Badr, the Prophet of Allah ﷺ commanded that twenty-four men from the chiefs of Quraysh be thrown into one of the wells of Badr, an evil and foul well. Whenever he prevailed over a people, he would remain in the open ground for three nights. When it was the third day at Badr, he ordered his mount to be prepared and its saddle tied. Then he walked, and his Companions followed him, saying, “We do not think he is setting out except for some need of his.” He came to the edge of the well and began calling them by their names and the names of their fathers: “O so-and-so son of so-and-so, and O so-and-so son of so-and-so, would it have pleased you if you had obeyed Allah and His Messenger? Indeed, we have found what our Lord promised us to be true, so have you found what your Lord promised to be true?” ʿUmar said, “O Messenger of Allah, what are you saying to bodies that have no souls?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, you do not hear what I say better than they do.” Qatādah said: Allah brought them to life until He made them hear his statement as rebuke, humiliation, punishment, regret, and remorse."],[348,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left the dead of Badr for three days. Then he came to them, stood over them, and called them, saying, “O Abū Jahl ibn Hishām, O Umayyah ibn Khalaf, O ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah, O Shaybah ibn Rabīʿah, have you not found what your Lord promised to be true? Indeed, I have found what my Lord promised me to be true.” ʿUmar heard the Prophet’s ﷺ statement and said, “O Messenger of Allah, how can they hear and how can they answer when they have decayed?” He said, “By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, you do not hear what I say better than they do, but they are unable to answer.” Then he ordered that they be dragged and thrown into the well of Badr."],[349,"Abū Isḥāq narrated: I heard al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, may Allah be pleased with him, narrating: On the Day of Uḥud, the Prophet ﷺ appointed ʿAbdullāh ibn Jubayr over the foot soldiers, and they were fifty men. He said, “If you see birds snatching us away, do not leave this place of yours until I send for you. And if you see that we have defeated the people and trampled them, do not leave until I send for you.” They defeated them. He said: By Allah, I saw the women running quickly, their anklets and legs visible, lifting their garments. The companions of Ibn Jubayr said, “The spoils, O people! The spoils! Your companions have prevailed, so what are you waiting for?” ʿAbdullāh ibn Jubayr said, “Have you forgotten what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to you?” They said, “By Allah, we will go to the people and take from the spoils.” When they went to them, their faces were turned back, and they came fleeing in defeat. That was when the Messenger was calling them from behind them, and no one remained with the Prophet ﷺ except twelve men. They inflicted upon us seventy casualties. The Prophet ﷺ and his Companions had inflicted one hundred and forty upon the polytheists on the Day of Badr: seventy prisoners and seventy killed. The ḥadīth."],[350,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on the Day of Badr, “Whoever among Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib you are able to capture, then capture them, for they came out unwillingly.”"],[351,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated: A short man from the Anṣār came with al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib as a prisoner. Al-ʿAbbās said, “O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, this man did not capture me. Rather, I was captured by a bald-fronted man with one of the most beautiful faces among people, riding a piebald horse. I do not see him among the people.” The Anṣārī said, “I captured him, O Messenger of Allah.” He said, “Be silent, for Allah Most High supported you with a noble angel.” ʿAlī said: We captured from Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib: al-ʿAbbās, ʿAqīl, and Nawfal ibn al-Ḥārith."],[352,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: When they captured the prisoners, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, “What do you think concerning these prisoners?” Abū Bakr said, “O Prophet of Allah, they are our cousins and kin. I think you should take ransom from them, so it may be a source of strength for us against the disbelievers, and perhaps Allah will guide them to Islam.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “What do you think, O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb?” I said, “No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah. I do not see what Abū Bakr sees. Rather, I think you should give us power over them so we strike their necks. Give ʿAlī power over ʿAqīl so he may strike his neck, and give me power over so-and-so, a relative of ʿUmar, so I may strike his neck, for these are the leaders and chiefs of disbelief.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ inclined to what Abū Bakr said and did not incline to what I said. The next day, I came and found the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr sitting and weeping. I said, “O Messenger of Allah, tell me what makes you and your companion weep. If I find reason to weep, I will weep; and if I do not find reason to weep, I will make myself weep because of your weeping.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “I weep because of what was presented to me regarding your companions taking ransom. Their punishment was presented to me closer than this tree,” a tree near the Prophet of Allah ﷺ. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: “It is not for a prophet to have captives until he has thoroughly subdued the enemy in the land” [al-Anfāl: 67] up to His saying: “So eat of what you have taken as spoils, lawful and good” [al-Anfāl: 69]. Thus Allah made the spoils lawful for them."],[353,"Ibn ʿUmar narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ consulted Abū Bakr concerning the prisoners, and he said, “They are your people and your kin, so let them go.” He consulted ʿUmar, and he said, “Kill them.” He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ accepted ransom for them. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: “It is not for a prophet to have captives until he has thoroughly subdued the enemy in the land,” up to His saying: “So eat of what you have taken as spoils, lawful and good” [al-Anfāl: 67-69]. He said: The Prophet ﷺ met ʿUmar and said, “A calamity nearly struck us because of differing from you.”"],[354,"Anas narrated, mentioning a man narrating from al-Ḥasan, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ consulted the people concerning the prisoners on the Day of Badr. He said, “Allah has given you power over them.” ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb stood and said, “O Messenger of Allah, strike their necks.” The Prophet ﷺ turned away from him. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned and said, “O people, Allah has given you power over them, and they were only your brothers yesterday.” ʿUmar stood and said, “O Messenger of Allah, strike their necks.” The Prophet ﷺ turned away from him. Then the Prophet ﷺ returned and said the same to the people. Abū Bakr stood and said, “O Messenger of Allah, perhaps you should pardon them and accept ransom from them.” Then the grief that had been on the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ disappeared. He pardoned them and accepted ransom from them. Then Allah revealed: “Had it not been for a decree from Allah that preceded, you would have been touched for what you took by a great punishment” [al-Anfāl: 68]."],[355,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās narrated: The Prophet ﷺ accepted ransom for the prisoners of Badr, and the ransom for each one of them was four thousand."],[356,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr narrated: Quraysh had mourned loudly over their dead, then regretted it and said, “Do not mourn over them, lest Muhammad and his Companions hear of that and rejoice over your misfortune.” Among the prisoners was Abū Wadāʿah ibn Ṣubayrah al-Sahmī. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “He has in Makkah a son who is a clever merchant and wealthy. It is as if you will soon see him coming to you with the ransom of his father.” When Quraysh said what they said regarding ransom, al-Muṭṭalib said, “By Allah, you have spoken the truth. If you do that, he will gain the upper hand over you.” Then he slipped away at night, came to Madinah, and ransomed his father for four thousand dirhams."],[357,"Anas narrated that some men from the Anṣār asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for permission and said, “Permit us to leave the ransom of our sister’s son, al-ʿAbbās.” He said, “By Allah, you will not leave even a single dirham of it.”"],[358,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated: Al-ʿAbbās said, “I was a Muslim, O Messenger of Allah.” He said, “Allah knows best about your Islam. If it is as you say, Allah will reward you. So ransom yourself, your two nephews Nawfal ibn al-Ḥārith and ʿAqīl ibn Abī Ṭālib, and your ally ʿUtbah ibn ʿAmr.” He said, “I do not have that, O Messenger of Allah.” He said, “Then where is the wealth that you and Umm al-Faḍl buried, when you said to her: ‘If I am struck down, this wealth is for my sons: al-Faḍl, ʿAbdullāh, and Qutham’?” He said, “By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I testify that you are the Messenger of Allah. This is something no one knew except me and Umm al-Faḍl. So count for me, O Messenger of Allah, the twenty ounces of wealth that you took from me.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “I will not.” So al-ʿAbbās ransomed himself, his two nephews, and his ally. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: “O Prophet, say to whoever is in your hands of the prisoners: If Allah knows any good in your hearts, He will give you better than what was taken from you, and He will forgive you. And Allah is Forgiving, Merciful” [al-Anfāl: 70]. He said: In place of the twenty ounces, He gave me in Islam twenty slaves, each of whom had wealth in his hand with which he traded, along with what I hope for of the forgiveness of Allah, Mighty and Majestic."],[359,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: Some of the prisoners on the Day of Badr had no ransom, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made their ransom that they teach the children of the Anṣār writing. He said: One day a boy came crying to his father. He said, “What is the matter with you?” He said, “My teacher hit me.” He said, “The wicked one is seeking revenge for Badr. By Allah, you will never go to him again.”"],[360,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated: When the people of Makkah sent ransom for their prisoners, Zaynab sent wealth as ransom for Abū al-ʿĀṣ, and she sent with it a necklace of hers that had belonged to Khadījah, which she had given her when she married Abū al-ʿĀṣ. She said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw it, he felt deep tenderness for her and said, “Would you consider releasing her prisoner for her and returning to her what belongs to her?” They said, “Yes.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had taken from him, or he had promised him, that he would allow Zaynab to come to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Zayd ibn Ḥārithah and a man from the Anṣār and said, “Be in the valley of Yaʾjaj until Zaynab passes by you, then accompany her until you bring her to me.”"],[361,"Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said concerning the prisoners of Badr: “If al-Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdī had been alive and then spoken to me regarding these foul ones, I would have released them for him.”"],[362,"It is narrated from Sayyidunā ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We set out with the Prophet ﷺ and I took part with him at Badr. When the people faced one another, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, defeated the enemy. One group went after them, driving them off and killing them, while one group bent over the equipment of the army, seizing it and gathering it, and one group remained around the Messenger of Allah ﷺ so that the enemy could not attack him suddenly.\nUntil, when night came, the people gathered with one another. Those who had gathered the spoils said: “We took possession of it and gathered it; no one else has a share in it.” And those who had gone to pursue the enemy said: “You have no more right to it than us; we kept the enemy away from it and defeated them.” And those who had remained around the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “You have no more right to it than us; we guarded around the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and feared that the enemy might attack him, and we were occupied with protecting him.”\nThen this verse was revealed: ﴿يَسْـَٔلُوْنَكَ عَنِ الْاَنْفَالِ١ؕ قُلِ الْاَنْفَالُ لِلّٰهِ وَ الرَّسُوْلِ١ۚ فَاتَّقُوا اللّٰهَ وَ اَصْلِحُوْا ذَاتَ بَيْنِكُمْ١۪﴾ [al-Anfāl: 1] (They ask you about the spoils of war. Say: The spoils belong to Allah and the Messenger. So fear Allah and set right the relations between you.) So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ divided it among the Muslims with justice.\nSayyidunā ʿUbādah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raided enemy land, he would give a quarter of the spoils (to the soldiers), and when he returned and the people were all together, he would give a third of the spoils. He ﷺ disliked giving extra rewards and bounties (that is, an additional share of the spoils) and would say:\n“Let the strong believer be generous to the weak believer” (that is, let him give the benefit of his strength and resources to the weak). Imam Aḥmad narrated it (22762), from Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr, from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah, from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, from Abū Sallām, from Abū Umāmah, from ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, and he mentioned this hadith.\nMuḥammad ibn Isḥāq also narrated it and said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith and others of our companions related to me, from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, from Makḥūl, from Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: I asked ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allah be pleased with him, about the anfāl, and he said: This verse was revealed about us, the Companions of Badr, when we fell into disagreement over the spoils and our manners deteriorated in this matter, so Allah took the spoils out of our hands and placed them under the authority of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ divided it among all the Muslims equally, that is, with equality.\nSīrat Ibn Hishām (1/642) (this narration is found in Sīrat Ibn Hishām, volume 1, page 642).\nAnd this same hadith was also narrated by al-Tirmidhī (hadith no. 1561), Ibn Mājah (hadith no. 2852), Ibn Ḥibbān (hadith no. 4855), and al-Ḥākim (volume 2, page 135) with this same chain through ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith, and some of them abridged it.\nAl-Tirmidhī said: The hadith of ʿUbādah is ḥasan (in grade).\nAl-Ḥākim, when he narrated the hadith of Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar, said: This hadith is ṣaḥīḥ according to the condition of Muslim, and it is also supported by the hadith of Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Qurashī.\nAnd all of them included Makḥūl in the chain between Sulaymān ibn Mūsā and Abū Umāmah.\nImam al-Bukhārī declared this hadith weak because Sulaymān ibn Mūsā is munkar al-ḥadīth. He said: I do not narrate any hadith from him, because most of the hadiths of Sulaymān ibn Mūsā are munkar (unacceptable). And he said: This hadith is not ṣaḥīḥ. In reality, this hadith was narrated by Dāwūd ibn ʿAmr through Abū Sallām from the Prophet ﷺ as a mursal report.\nAl-ʿIlal al-Kabīr (2/665-666) (this reference is taken from al-ʿIlal al-Kabīr, volume 2, pages 665-666)."],[363,"Al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām narrated: On the Day of Badr, one hundred shares were allocated for the Muhājirūn."],[364,"Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated: The stipend of the people of Badr was five thousand, and ʿUmar said, “I will certainly give them preference over those who came after them.”"],[365,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated: I had a she-camel from my share of the spoils on the Day of Badr, and the Prophet ﷺ had given me from what Allah had granted him from the khums that day. The full ḥadīth."],[366,"It is narrated from Sayyidunā Dhū al-Jawshan, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ after the battle against the people of Badr had ended, with a foal of mine, and said: “O Muhammad! I have brought you a foal of the qarḥāʾ (a breed of) horse so that you may keep it.” He ﷺ said: “I do not need it, but if you wish, I will give you in exchange for it the finest coats of mail of Badr.” I said: “Today I will not make a bargain with you for goods.” He ﷺ said: “Then I do not need it.”\nThen he ﷺ said: “O Dhū al-Jawshan! Will you not accept Islam so that you may be among the first of this affair?” I said: “No.” He ﷺ asked: “Why?” I said: “I saw that your people are set against you.” He ﷺ said: “So how did you come to know of their fate at Badr?” I said: “I received news.” Then I said: “What if you overcome Makkah and settle there?” He ﷺ said: “It is possible that if you live, you will see that sight.”\nThen he ﷺ said: “O Bilāl! Take this man’s belongings and provide him with ʿajwah dates as provisions.”\nWhen I turned to go back, he ﷺ said: “This is one of the best of Banū ʿĀmir.”\nBy Allah! I was in Ghawr with my family when a rider came. I asked: “Where are you coming from?” He said: “From Makkah.” I asked: “How are the people?” He replied: “Muhammad ﷺ has overcome Makkah.” I said: “May my mother lose me! By Allah, had I accepted Islam on that very day and asked him for al-Ḥīrah, he would have granted it to me.”\nAḥmad narrated it (hadith no. 15965), and his son ʿAbdullāh in his additions (hadith no. 15966), and Abū Dāwūd (hadith no. 2786).\nIt was narrated through various routes from ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Hamdānī, from his father, from his grandfather, and from him through Dhū al-Jawshan. The wording is that of Imam Aḥmad, while Abū Dāwūd has only a portion of it.\nSufyān al-Thawrī narrated something similar from Abū Isḥāq, from Dhū al-Jawshan. And he said: The son of Dhū al-Jawshan was a neighbor of Abū Isḥāq, and I think he must have heard this narration from him. The son of Dhū al-Jawshan was Shimr, who was not fit to narrate, as Imam al-Dhahabī said.\nAl-Mundhirī said in his book Mukhtaṣar al-Sunan (vol. 4, p. 90): Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī said: I do not know of any hadith from Dhū al-Jawshan other than this hadith. And it is said that Abū Isḥāq heard this hadith from Shimr ibn Dhī al-Jawshan, through his father (Dhū al-Jawshan), and Allah knows best. This is the end of his words. And this hadith is not established, because it is either a case of a break in the chain or a narration of a narrator who cannot be relied upon. The words of al-Mundhirī end here."],[367,"Abū Ṭalḥah narrated: Whenever the Prophet ﷺ prevailed over a people, he would remain in the open ground for three nights. When it was the third day at Badr, he ordered that his mount be prepared and its saddle tied. Then he walked, and his Companions followed him, saying, “We do not think he is setting out except for some need of his,” until he stood at the edge of the well and began calling them. Then he mentioned the ḥadīth."],[368,"Abū Umāmah ibn Sahl narrated: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished at Badr, he sent two bearers of glad tidings to the people of Madinah. He sent Zayd ibn Ḥārithah to the people of the lower part, and he sent ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah to the people of the upper part, giving them glad tidings of Allah’s victory for His Prophet ﷺ. Zayd ibn Ḥārithah arrived upon his son Usāmah while he was leveling the soil over Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. It was said to him, “That is your father who has arrived.” Usāmah said: I came, and he was standing among the people saying, “ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah, Shaybah ibn Rabīʿah, Abū Jahl ibn Hishām, Nabīh, Munabbih, and Umayyah ibn Khalaf have been killed.” I said, “My father, is this true?” He said, “Yes, by Allah, my son.”"],[369,"Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated that the Prophet ﷺ left ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān and Usāmah ibn Zayd behind to look after Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, during the days of Badr. Then Zayd ibn Ḥārithah came on al-ʿAḍbāʾ, the camel of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, with the glad tidings. Usāmah said: I heard the cry of alarm, so I went out, and there was Zayd, who had come with the glad tidings. By Allah, I did not believe it until I saw the prisoners. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ allocated a share for ʿUthmān."],[370,"Abū ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmayr, the brother of Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr, narrated: I was among the prisoners on the Day of Badr, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Treat the prisoners well.” I was with a group of the Anṣār, and whenever they brought their lunch and dinner, they would eat dates and feed me bread because of the instruction of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to them."],[371,"Miqsam, the freedman of ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith, narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās that he heard him say regarding: “Not equal are those believers who sit behind” [al-Nisāʾ: 95]: this refers to Badr and those who went out to Badr."],[372,"Ḥumayd narrated: I heard Anas say: Ḥārithah was struck on the Day of Badr while he was a young boy. His mother came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of Allah, you know the status of Ḥārithah with me. If he is in Paradise, I will be patient and seek reward; but if it is otherwise, you will see what I do.” He said, “Woe to you—or may you be bereaved! Is it only one Paradise? It is many Gardens, and he is in the Garden of al-Firdaws.”"],[373,"Ḥafṣah reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I truly hope that, if Allah Most High wills, none of those who witnessed Badr and al-Ḥudaybiyyah will enter the Fire.” She said: I asked: “O Messenger of Allah, has Allah not said: ‘And there is none of you except that he will come to it. This is upon your Lord an inevitable decree.’ [Maryam: 71]?” He replied: “Have you not heard Him say: ‘Then We will save those who were mindful of Allah and leave the wrongdoers therein on their knees.’\n[Maryam: 72]?”"],[374,"Shaqīq narrated that Ibn Masʿūd told him: The eighteen Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who were killed on the Day of Badr—Allah placed their souls in Paradise inside green birds that roam freely in Paradise. While they were in that state, your Lord looked upon them and said, “My servants, what do you desire?” They said, “Our Lord, is there anything above this?” He said again, “My servants, what do you desire?” On the fourth time they said, “That our souls be returned to our bodies so that we may be killed as we were killed.”"],[375,"From Banū al-Najjār: Sayyidunā ʿAwf ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Rifāʿah ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Suwād, may Allah be pleased with him. He is Ibn ʿAfrāʾ, who is the brother of Sayyidunā Muʿādh and Sayyidunā Muʿawwidh, may Allah be pleased with them both. All three took part in the Battle of Badr. Ibn Isḥāq relates that Ibn ʿAfrāʾ asked: “O Messenger of Allah! At what deed of His servant does the Lord smile?” He said: “His plunging into the enemy without military and protective equipment.” He took off the coat of mail he was wearing and threw it away. Then he took his sword, fell upon the enemy, and was martyred fighting. ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAmr ibn Qatādah is a reliable narrator and a scholar of the military campaigns, but he narrates mursal reports.\nIt is said that fourteen Muslims drank the cup of martyrdom. Six were from the Quraysh and eight from the Anṣār. Ibn Kathīr mentioned this in al-Bidāyah wa al-Nihāyah."],[376,"Ibn Shihāb narrated: When the defeated remnants of the polytheists returned to Makkah, ʿUmayr ibn Wahb came and sat with Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah in al-Ḥijr. Ṣafwān said, “May Allah make life ugly after those killed at Badr.” He said, “Yes. By Allah, there is no good in life after them. Were it not for a debt upon me that I cannot repay, and dependents whom I would leave with nothing, I would travel to Muhammad and kill him if my eyes could fill with sight of him. I have an excuse with him by which I can approach him: I will say, ‘I came because of this son of mine who is a prisoner.’” Ṣafwān rejoiced and said to him, “Your debt is upon me, and your dependents will be like my dependents in maintenance. I will not be able to provide anything for mine while being unable to provide for them.” They agreed. Ṣafwān mounted him, prepared him, and ordered that a sword of Ḥimyar be polished and poisoned. ʿUmayr said to Ṣafwān, “Conceal my news for some days.” ʿUmayr came to Madinah, dismounted at the door of the mosque, tied his mount, took the sword, and headed toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. ʿUmar saw him while he was among a group of the Anṣār, became alarmed, and entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying, “O Prophet of Allah, do not trust him with anything.” He said, “Bring him in to me.” ʿUmar went out and ordered his companions to enter upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and guard against ʿUmayr.\nʿUmar and ʿUmayr came forward until they entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while ʿUmayr had his sword. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿUmar, “Step back from him.” When ʿUmayr drew near, he said, “May your morning be pleasant.” This was the greeting of the people of Jāhiliyyah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has honored us above your greeting, and has made our greeting the greeting of the people of Paradise, and it is salām.” ʿUmayr said, “Your time with it is recent.” He said to him, “What brought you, O ʿUmayr?” He said, “I came because of my prisoner who is with you, so deal kindly with us concerning our prisoners, for you are kin and family.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “What is the matter with the sword around your neck?” He said, “May Allah make these swords ugly. Did they benefit us at all? I only forgot it around my neck when I dismounted.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Tell me the truth: what brought you?” He said, “I came only to seek my prisoner.” He said, “Then what did you stipulate with Ṣafwān in al-Ḥijr?” ʿUmayr was startled and said, “What did I stipulate with him?” He said, “You undertook to kill me for him on condition that he support your children and pay off your debt. Allah will come between you and that.” ʿUmayr said, “I testify that you are the Messenger of Allah, and I testify that there is no god but Allah. O Messenger of Allah, we used to deny you regarding revelation and what came to you from heaven. This conversation was between me and Ṣafwān in al-Ḥijr exactly as you said; no one knew of it, and Allah informed you of it. Praise be to Allah who brought me to this path.” The Muslims rejoiced over him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him, “Sit, O ʿUmayr, so we may keep you company.” He said to his Companions, “Teach your brother the Qur’an.” And he released his prisoner for him. ʿUmayr said, “Permit me, O Messenger of Allah, to return to Quraysh and call them to Allah and to Islam, perhaps Allah will guide them.” He permitted him, and he returned to Makkah. Ṣafwān had been saying to Quraysh, “Rejoice in a victory that will make you forget the event of Badr.” He kept asking every rider who came from Madinah, “Has anything happened there?” until a man came to him and said, “ʿUmayr has accepted Islam.” The polytheists cursed him. Ṣafwān said, “It is upon me before Allah that I will never speak to him and will never benefit him with anything.” Then ʿUmayr came and called them to Islam and advised them with all his effort, and many people accepted Islam because of him."],[377,"Al-Wāqidī mentioned in his book al-Maghāzī: ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith narrated from his father that ʿAṣmāʾ bint Marwān, who was from Banū Umayyah ibn Zayd ibn Ḥiṣn al-Khaṭmī, used to harm the Prophet ﷺ, find fault with Islam, and incite people against the Prophet ﷺ. And she would recite these verses:\nHow wretched are Banū Mālik and Banū Nabīt * and how wretched are ʿAwf and Banū Khazraj\nI seek refuge with others, without your help * neither with the tribe of Murād nor with Madhḥij\nYou hope for it after the killing * like one hoping for leftover broth\nWhen ʿUmayr ibn ʿAdī ibn Kharashah ibn Umayyah al-Khaṭmī heard these verses of hers and her incitement, he said: “O Allah, I have a covenant with You that if You bring the Messenger of Allah ﷺ back to Madinah, I will kill her” - and at that time the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was at Badr. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from Badr, ʿUmayr ibn ʿAdī came to her house at night while she was sleeping among her children, one of whom was nursing. ʿUmayr removed the child and placed his sword on her chest and drove it through her. Then he came with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to the mosque of Madinah to pray the Fajr prayer. When the Prophet ﷺ finished the prayer, he saw ʿUmayr and asked: “Did you kill the daughter of Marwān?” ʿUmayr replied: “Yes, O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you.” ʿUmayr feared that perhaps he had killed her without the command of the Prophet ﷺ. He asked: “O Messenger of Allah, is there anything further upon me concerning this?” The Prophet ﷺ said: “No, two goats will butt each other over her.” The killing of ʿAṣmāʾ took place after the return from Badr, at the beginning of the nineteenth month after the Hijrah, on the 25th of Ramaḍān."],[378,"At the beginning of Shawwāl, in the twentieth month after the Hijrah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, took place the expedition of Sālim ibn ʿUmayr al-ʿUmayrī against Abū ʿAfak the Jew. Abū ʿAfak was a very old man of Banū ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf, a hundred years old, a Jew, who would incite people against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and compose verses.\nSayyidunā Sālim ibn ʿUmayr, may Allah be pleased with him, who was one of those who wept much and had been present at Badr, said: “I vow that either I will kill Abū ʿAfak or I will die trying.” He waited for a moment when he would be unaware. On a hot night Abū ʿAfak slept in the open, and Sayyidunā Sālim ibn ʿUmayr, may Allah be pleased with him, learned of it. He came forward, placed his sword on his liver and pressed down, standing over it until it went through into his bed. The enemy of Allah screamed, and his followers came running, carried the corpse to his house and buried it."],[379,"Al-Wāqidī said: This event took place in the second year after the Hijrah, on the middle (fifteenth) day of Shawwāl, on a Saturday."],[380,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ defeated Quraysh on the Day of Badr and returned to Madinah, he gathered the Jews in the market of Banū Qaynuqāʿ and said, “O assembly of Jews, accept Islam before there strikes you the like of what struck Quraysh.” They said, “O Muhammad, do not be deceived by yourself because you killed a group of Quraysh who were inexperienced and did not know fighting. If you fought us, you would know that we are truly the people, and that you have not met the like of us.” So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed concerning that: “Say to those who disbelieve: You will be overcome,” up to His saying: “One group fighting in the path of Allah and another disbelieving” [Āl ʿImrān: 12-13], regarding Badr."],[381,"Ibn Hishām says: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Miswar ibn Makhramah narrates from Abū ʿAwn: The beginning of this war was that an Arab woman came to the market of Banū Qaynuqāʿ with something, sold it, and sat down at a goldsmith’s shop. The Jews wanted to have the woman’s face unveiled. The woman refused. The goldsmith secretly tied a knot in her garment at the back. When the woman stood up, her private parts were exposed. The Jews began to laugh and mocked the woman greatly. The woman cried out. A Muslim came and killed that goldsmith, who was a Jew. The Jews crowded around and killed that Muslim. When the relatives of that Muslim cried out for help, many Muslims gathered against the Jews, and the fighting grew intense.\nIbn Isḥāq says: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed me, saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ besieged these Jews until they accepted submission to him ﷺ. At that time ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl came into the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “O Muhammad ﷺ, be kind to my allies,” and all of them were allies of Banū Khazraj. He gave him no reply. He said it again. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned his face away from him. He took hold of the edge of his coat of mail. The name of that coat of mail was Dhāt al-Fuḍūl. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Let go,” and his blessed face turned red with anger. And he said: “Woe to you, let go of my garment.” He said: “I will never let go until you spare the lives of my allies and be kind to them. Among them are four hundred without armor and three hundred with mail, and they are such brave men that they fear no battle. Yet you would kill them in one day; this is what I fear.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Go, I have granted them to you.”\nIbn Isḥāq says: And ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed me: Banū Qaynuqāʿ were the first of the Jews to break the agreement that existed between them and Muhammad ﷺ, and to fight, between Badr and Uḥud."],[382,"Al-Ḥāfiẓ Ibn al-Qayyim says: The Prophet ﷺ had four campaigns against the Jews.\nThe first: Banū Qaynuqāʿ after the Battle of Badr, the second: Banū al-Naḍīr after Uḥud, the third: Qurayẓah after the Trench, and the fourth: Khaybar after al-Ḥudaybiyah.\nIbn Saʿd says: The siege of Banū Qaynuqāʿ lasted fifteen days until the new moon of Dhū al-Qaʿdah. They were the first of the Jews to break the covenant and to fight, and they secured themselves in their fortress. He ﷺ besieged them with great severity. Allah the Exalted cast terror into their hearts, and they agreed to the decision of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that their property would be for the Messenger of Allah, and the women and children for them. He ﷺ gave the command, and their hands were bound.\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed al-Mundhir ibn Qudāmah al-Sulamī, of Banū al-Salm of the tribe of Saʿd ibn Khaythamah, to bind them. ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to spare their lives and insisted greatly, so he ﷺ said: “Leave them; may Allah curse them, and may He curse along with them (ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl).” He ﷺ spared their lives and ordered that they be expelled from Madinah. ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allah be pleased with him, was appointed for this task. The Jews went to Adhriʿāt, but they were not able to remain there for long either."],[383,"Ibn Isḥāq says: Then Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb set out for the expedition of Sawīq in Dhū al-Ḥijjah, in the twenty-second month after the Hijrah.\nWhen the polytheists returned from Badr, Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb forbade oil to himself until vengeance was taken on Muhammad ﷺ and his Companions. According to the hadith of al-Zuhrī he set out with two horsemen, and according to the hadith of Ibn Kaʿb with forty horsemen. Abū Sufyān reached al-Najdiyyah, went at night to Banū al-Naḍīr, and knocked at the door of Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab to inquire about the Messenger of Allah and his Companions, but he refused to open the door. He knocked at the door of Sallām ibn Mishkam, and he opened it, hosted them, gave them wine to drink, and also told them about the affairs of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When dawn broke, Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb went out and reached al-ʿUraydʿ, which is about three miles between Madinah and al-ʿUraydʿ. There he killed a man of the Anṣār who was his hired worker, and burned some houses and grass. He thought that his oath had been fulfilled and turned his back and fled. When this news reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he ﷺ called out to his Companions and set out, with two hundred men of the Muhājirūn and Anṣār, following their tracks. Abū Sufyān and his companions fled swiftly, dropping sacks of sawīq (roasted barley flour), which was generally their provision, and the Muslims would pick them up. From this it became known as the expedition of Sawīq (sawīq meaning roasted barley flour). The Muslims could not catch up with him, and the Messenger ﷺ returned to Madinah. He ﷺ was away from Madinah for five days."],[384,"Ibn Isḥāq said: Then when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned to Madinah, he stayed there only seven nights, until he ﷺ himself set out on an expedition intending Banū Sulaym.\nIbn Hishām said: And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed Sibāʿ ibn ʿUrfuṭah al-Ghifārī or Ibn Umm Maktūm, may Allah be pleased with him, as governor over Madinah.\nIbn Isḥāq said: Then he ﷺ reached one of their watering places, called al-Kudr, and stayed there three nights. This is the expedition of Qarqarat al-Kudr (also called Qarārat al-Kudr).\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ carried out the expedition of Qarqarat al-Kudr (or Qarārat al-Kudr) at the completion of 23 months after the Hijrah, in the middle of Muḥarram. This place is located in the mining region of Banū Sulaym, near Arḥaḍiyyah and behind Sadd Maʿūnah. The distance from Madinah to the mine is eight stages (burud). The banner of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the hand of Sayyidunā ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, and he ﷺ appointed ʿAbdullāh ibn Umm Maktūm, may Allah be pleased with him, as his deputy over Madinah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ received word that people of Banū Sulaym and Ghaṭafān were gathering in that region. He ﷺ set out towards them, but found no one in that area. He ﷺ sent some of his Companions to the upper part of the valley, and he himself came down to the lower part of the valley, where there were shepherds, among them a slave who was called Yasār. He ﷺ asked him about the people, and he said: “I have no knowledge of them; I only bring (the camels) to give them water, and today my turn is on the fourth day. The people have gone to the upper waters, and we are a few people with the livestock.”\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned to Madinah, bringing the livestock with him. Then he ﷺ divided the spoils at Ṣirār near Madinah, which was three miles from Madinah. The number of camels was 500. He ﷺ took out the fifth and divided the remaining four parts among the Muslims. Each Muslim received two camels, and the army consisted of 200 men.\nThe slave Yasār fell to the share of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he ﷺ freed him, because he had seen him praying. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was away from Madinah for 15 nights on account of this expedition."],[385,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the expedition of Sawīq, he spent the rest of Dhū al-Ḥijjah in Madinah. Then he set out towards Najd, intending to fight Banū Ghaṭafān, and appointed ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān as his deputy in Madinah. This is what Ibn Hishām said.\nIbn Isḥāq says: He stayed in Najd for the entire month of Ṣafar and then returned to Madinah. No fighting took place on this journey, and all or part of the month of Rabīʿ al-Awwal passed.\nIt is mentioned in Ṭabaqāt Ibn Saʿd: A group of Banū Thaʿlabah and Muḥārib of Dhū Amarr gathered and intended to cause some harm in the surroundings of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Their chief was Daʿthūr ibn al-Ḥārith, who was from Banū Muḥārib. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned the Muslims, and they set out on the eleventh night of Rabīʿ al-Awwal with four hundred and fifty men, and horses were also with them. When these enemies heard of the coming of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, they fled to the mountain peaks. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not meet any enemy, but he saw them on the mountain peaks."],[386,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Anṣārī narrated: I saw the Prophet ﷺ in the Battle of Anmār praying voluntary prayer on his mount while facing toward the east."],[387,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Anṣārī narrated: We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the Battle of Banū Anmār. Jābir said: While I was resting beneath a tree, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came. I said, “O Messenger of Allah, come to the shade.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dismounted. I went to a sack of ours and searched in it for something. I found in it a small cucumber, broke it, and brought it near to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said, “Where did you get this?” I said, “We brought it with us from Madinah, O Messenger of Allah.” Jābir said: We had a companion with us whom we had prepared to go and graze our riding animals. I prepared him, and he turned away to go to the riding animals, wearing two old worn cloaks of his. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at him and said, “Does he not have two garments other than these?” I said, “Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He has two garments in the chest; I clothed him with them.” He said, “Call him and tell him to wear them.” I called him, and he wore them. Then he turned away to go. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “What is the matter with him? May Allah strike his neck. Is this not better for him?” The man heard him and said, “O Messenger of Allah, in the path of Allah?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “In the path of Allah.” He said: The man was then killed in the path of Allah."],[388,"Ibn Hishām says: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ marched out of Madinah intending to fight the Quraysh and appointed Ibn Umm Maktūm, may Allah be pleased with him, as his deputy in Madinah. Ibn Isḥāq says: Until he reached the place of Buḥrān, which is located in the Hijaz at the edge of al-Furuʿ. There he stayed for two full months, Rabīʿ al-Ākhir and Jumādā al-Ūlā, and then came to Madinah. No fighting took place on this journey either. And this occurred in Jumādā al-Ūlā, after twenty-seven months had passed.\nI say: Buḥrān is the name of a mountain located ninety kilometers east of the city of Rābigh."],[389,"The expedition of Sayyidunā Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, towards al-Qaradah took place in the twenty-eighth month after the Hijrah, at the beginning of Jumādā al-Ākhirah. It was the very first expedition in which Zayd went out as commander. Al-Qaradah is in the land of Najd, between al-Zubdah and al-Ghamrah, in the vicinity of Dhāt ʿIrq. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent them to intercept a caravan of the Quraysh, in which were Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah, Ḥuwayṭib ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā and ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Rabīʿah. With them was a great deal of wealth, gold and silver coins, vessels and silver, which weighed thirty thousand dirhams. Their guide was Furāt ibn Ḥayyān al-ʿIjlī, who led them by the route of Iraq towards Dhāt ʿIrq.\nWhen the news reached the Messenger ﷺ, he ﷺ sent Zayd ibn Ḥārithah with a hundred horsemen. They intercepted it and caught up with the caravan. The chiefs of the people escaped. They brought all the wealth to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he ﷺ divided it into fifths. One fifth of it amounted to twenty thousand dirhams, and what remained he ﷺ distributed among the members of the expedition. Furāt ibn Ḥayyān was taken prisoner and brought to the Prophet ﷺ. He was told: “If you accept Islam, you will be released!” Upon this he accepted Islam, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pardoned him from being killed.\nIbn Isḥāq mentioned that Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb was also with them, and they had a great deal of silver, which was a large part of their trade."],[390,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Who will deal with Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf? He has harmed Allah and His Messenger.” Muḥammad ibn Maslamah stood and said, “O Messenger of Allah, would you like me to kill him?” He said, “Yes.” He said, “Then permit me to say something.” He said, “Say it.” Muḥammad ibn Maslamah came to him and said, “This man has asked us for charity, and he has burdened us. I have come to you seeking a loan.” He said, “And more than that, by Allah, you will grow tired of him.” He said, “We have followed him, and we do not wish to leave him until we see where his matter will end. We want you to lend us a wasq or two.” ʿAmr narrated to us more than once and did not mention “a wasq or two,” or I said to him, “Does it contain ‘a wasq or two’?” He said, “I think it contains ‘a wasq or two.’” He said, “Yes, give me collateral.” They said, “What do you want?” He said, “Pledge your women to me.” They said, “How can we pledge our women to you when you are the most handsome of the Arabs?” He said, “Then pledge your sons to me.” They said, “How can we pledge our sons to you, then one of them would be insulted and it would be said: He was pledged for a wasq or two? This would be shame upon us. But we will pledge our weapons to you.” Sufyān said: Meaning weapons. He made an appointment to come to him. He came to him at night with Abū Nāʾilah, who was Kaʿb’s brother through nursing. He called them to the fortress, and Kaʿb came down to them. His wife said to him, “Where are you going out at this hour?” He said, “It is only Muḥammad ibn Maslamah and my brother Abū Nāʾilah.” Someone other than ʿAmr said: She said, “I hear a voice as if blood is dripping from it.” He said, “It is only my brother Muḥammad ibn Maslamah and my foster brother Abū Nāʾilah. If a noble man were called to a stab at night, he would answer.” Muḥammad ibn Maslamah entered with two men. It was said to Sufyān: Did ʿAmr name them? He said: He named some of them. ʿAmr said: Two men came with him. Someone other than ʿAmr said: Abū ʿAbs ibn Jabr, al-Ḥārith ibn Aws, and ʿAbbād ibn Bishr. ʿAmr said: Two men came with him. He said, “When he comes, I will take hold of his hair and smell it. When you see that I have gained control of his head, then strike him.” And he said once: “Then I will let you smell it.” Kaʿb came down to them wearing his garment, and the scent of perfume was coming from him. He said, “I have never smelled a scent better than today’s.” Someone other than ʿAmr said: He said, “I have the most fragrant and most perfect women of the Arabs.” ʿAmr said: He said, “Do you permit me to smell your head?” He said, “Yes.” He smelled it, then let his companions smell it. Then he said, “Do you permit me?” He said, “Yes.” When he had gained control of him, he said, “Now!” So they killed him. Then they came to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him."],[391,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ walked with them to Baqīʿ al-Gharqad, then sent them forth and said, “Go in the name of Allah.” And he said, “O Allah, help them.” Meaning the group he sent to Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf."],[392,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik narrated from his father, who was one of the three whose repentance was accepted: Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf used to satirize the Prophet ﷺ and incite the disbelievers of Quraysh against him. When the Prophet ﷺ came to Madinah, its people were mixed: among them were Muslims, polytheists who worshiped idols, and Jews. They used to harm the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded His Prophet to be patient and pardon. Concerning them Allah revealed: “And you will surely hear from those who were given the Scripture before you…” [Āl ʿImrān: 186]. When Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf refused to stop harming the Prophet ﷺ, the Prophet ﷺ ordered Saʿd ibn Muʿādh to send a group to kill him. So he sent Muḥammad ibn Maslamah, and he mentioned the story of his killing. When they killed him, the Jews and polytheists were terrified, and they came to the Prophet ﷺ in the morning and said, “Our companion was attacked at night and killed.” The Prophet ﷺ mentioned to them what he used to say, and the Prophet ﷺ called them to write a document between him and them to which they would adhere. So the Prophet ﷺ wrote a document between himself, them, and the Muslims in general."],[393,"Muhammad ibn Isḥāq said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote a document between the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār, in which he also included the Jews. He made a covenant with them, confirmed them in their religion and their property, and imposed conditions on them and set terms for them. These are the clauses of that document, as Ibn Isḥāq reported them:\n1۔ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِيمِ ، هَذَا كِتَابٌ مِنْ مُحَمَّدٍ النَّبِيِّ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ ، بَيْنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ وَالْمُسْلِمِينَ مِنْ قُرَيْشٍ وَيَثْرِبَ ، وَمَنْ تَبِعَهُمْ ، فَلَحِقَ بِهِمْ ، وَجَاهَدَ مَعَهُمْ ، إنَّهُمْ أُمَّةٌ وَاحِدَةٌ مِنْ دُونِ النَّاسِ.\n2۔ الْمُهَاجِرُونَ مِنْ قُرَيْشٍ عَلَى رِبْعَتِهِمْ يَتَعَاقَلُونَ بَيْنَهُمْ ، وَهُمْ يَفْدُونَ عَانِيَهُمْ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَالْقِسْطِ بَيْنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ.\n3۔ وَبَنُو عَوْفٍ عَلَى رِبْعَتِهِمْ يَتَعَاقَلُونَ مَعَاقِلَهُمْ الْأُولَى ، كُلُّ طَائِفَةٍ تَفْدِي عَانِيَهَا بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَالْقِسْطِ بَيْنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ.\n4۔ وَبَنُو سَاعِدَةَ عَلَى رِبْعَتِهِمْ يَتَعَاقَلُونَ مَعَاقِلَهُمْ الْأُولَى ، وَكُلُّ طَائِفَةٍ مِنْهُمْ تَفْدِي عَانِيَهَا بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَالْقِسْطِ بَيْنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ.\n5۔ وَبَنُو الْحَارِثِ عَلَى رِبْعَتِهِمْ يَتَعَاقَلُونَ مَعَاقِلَهُمْ الْأُولَى ، وَكُلُّ طَائِفَةٍ تَفْدِي عَانِيَهَا بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَالْقِسْطِ بَيْنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ.\n6۔ وَبَنُو جُشَمٍ عَلَى رِبْعَتِهِمْ يَتَعَاقَلُونَ مَعَاقِلِهِمْ الْأُولَى ، وَكُلُّ طَائِفَةٍ مِنْهُمْ تَفْدِي عَانِيَهَا بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَالْقِسْطِ بَيْنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ.\n7۔ وَبَنُو النَّجَّارِ عَلَى رِبْعَتِهِمْ يَتَعَاقَلُونَ مَعَاقِلَهُمْ الْأُولَى ، وَكُلُّ طَائِفَةٍ مِنْهُمْ تَفْدِي عَانِيَهَا بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَالْقِسْطِ بَيْنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ.\n8۔ وَبَنُو عَمْرِو بْنِ عَوْفٍ عَلَى رِبْعَتِهِمْ يَتَعَاقَلُونَ مَعَاقِلَهُمْ الْأُولَى ، وَكُلُّ طَائِفَةٍ تَفْدِي عَانِيَهَا بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَالْقِسْطِ بَيْنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ.\n9۔ وَبَنُو النَّبِيتِ عَلَى رِبْعَتِهِمْ يَتَعَاقَلُونَ مَعَاقِلَهُمْ الْأُولَى ، وَكُلُّ طَائِفَةٍ تَفْدِي عَانِيَهَا بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَالْقِسْطِ بَيْنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ.\n10۔ وَبَنُو الْأَوْسِ عَلَى رِبْعَتِهِمْ يَتَعَاقَلُونَ مَعَاقِلَهُمْ الْأُولَى ، وَكُلُّ طَائِفَةٍ مِنْهُمْ تَفْدِي عَانِيَهَا بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَالْقِسْطِ بَيْنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ.\n11۔ وَإِنَّ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ لَا يَتْرُكُونَ مُفْرَحًا بَيْنَهُمْ أَنْ يُعْطُوهُ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ فِي فِدَاءٍ أَوْ عَقْلٍ، وَأَنْ لَا يُحَالِفَ مُؤْمِنٌ مَوْلَى مُؤْمِنٍ دُونَهُ.\n12۔ وَإِنَّ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ الْمُتَّقِينَ عَلَى مَنْ بَغَى مِنْهُمْ ، أَوْ ابْتَغَى دَسِيعَةَ ظُلْمٍ ، أَوْ إثْمٍ ، أَوْ عُدْوَانٍ ، أَوْ فَسَادٍ بَيْنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ ؛ وَإِنَّ أَيْدِيَهُمْ عَلَيْهِ جَمِيعًا ، وَلَوْ كَانَ وَلَدَ أَحَدِهِمْ.\n13۔ وَلَا يَقْتُلُ مُؤْمِنٌ مُؤْمِنًا فِي كَافِرٍ.\n14۔ وَلَا يَنْصُرُ كَافِرًا عَلَى مُؤْمِنٍ.\n15۔ وَإِنَّ ذِمَّةَ اللَّهِ وَاحِدَةٌ ، يُجِيرُ عَلَيْهِمْ أَدْنَاهُمْ.\n16۔ وَإِنَّ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ بَعْضُهُمْ مَوَالِي بَعْضٍ دُونَ النَّاسِ.\n17۔ وَإِنَّهُ مَنْ تَبِعَنَا مِنْ يَهُودَ فَإِنَّ لَهُ النَّصْرَ وَالْأُسْوَةَ ، غَيْرَ مَظْلُومِينَ وَلَا مُتَنَاصَرِينَ عَلَيْهِمْ.\n18۔ وَإِنَّ سِلْمَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ وَاحِدَةٌ ، لَا يُسَالَمُ مُؤْمِنٌ دُونَ مُؤْمِنٍ فِي قِتَالٍ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ ، إلَّا عَلَى سَوَاءٍ وَعَدْلٍ بَيْنَهُمْ.\n19۔ وَإِنَّ كُلَّ غَازِيَةٍ غَزَتْ مَعَنَا يُعْقِبُ بَعْضُهَا بَعْضًا.\n20۔ وَإِنَّ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ يُبِئْ بَعْضُهُمْ عَلَى بَعْضٍ بِمَا نَالَ دِمَاءَهُمْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ.\n21۔ وَإِنَّ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ الْمُتَّقِينَ عَلَى أَحْسَنِ هُدًى وَأَقْوَمِهِ.\n22۔ وَإِنَّهُ لَا يُجِيرُ مُشْرِكٌ مَالًا لِقُرَيْشٍ وَلَا نَفْسَهَا ، وَلَا يَحُولُ دُونَهُ عَلَى مُؤْمِنٍ.\n23۔ وَإِنَّهُ مَنْ اعْتَبَطَ مُؤْمِنًا قَتْلًا عَنْ بَيِّنَةٍ فَإِنَّهُ قَوَدٌ بِهِ إلَّا أَنْ يَرْضَى وَلِيُّ الْمَقْتُولِ.\n24۔ وَإِنَّ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ عَلَيْهِ كَافَّةٌ ، وَلَا يَحِلُّ لَهُمْ إلَّا قِيَامٌ عَلَيْهِ.\n25۔ وَإِنَّهُ لَا يَحِلُّ لِمُؤْمِنٍ أَقَرَّ بِمَا فِي هَذِهِ الصَّحِيفَةِ ، وَآمَنَ بِاَللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ ، أَنْ يَنْصُرَ مُحْدِثًا وَلَا يُؤْوِيهِ.\n26۔ وَأَنَّهُ مَنْ نَصَرَهُ أَوْ آوَاهُ ، فَإِنَّ عَلَيْهِ لَعْنَةَ اللَّهِ وَغَضَبَهُ يَوْمَ الْقِيَامَةِ، وَلَا يُؤْخَذُ مِنْهُ صَرْفٌ وَلَا عَدْلٌ.\n27۔ وَإِنَّكُمْ مَهْمَا اخْتَلَفْتُمْ فِيهِ مِنْ شَيْءٍ ، فَإِنَّ مَرَدَّهُ إلَى اللَّهِ عَزَّ وَجَلَّ ، وَإِلَى مُحَمَّدٍ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ.\n28۔ وَإِنَّ الْيَهُودَ يُنْفِقُونَ مَعَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ مَا دَامُوا مُحَارَبِينَ.\n29۔ وَإِنَّ يَهُودَ بَنِي عَوْفٍ أُمَّةٌ مَعَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ.\n30۔ لِلْيَهُودِ دِينُهُمْ ، وَلِلْمُسْلِمَيْنِ دِينُهُمْ ، مَوَالِيهِمْ وَأَنْفُسُهُمْ ، إلَّا مَنْ ظَلَمَ وَأَثِمَ ، فَإِنَّهُ لَا يُوتِغُ إلَّا نَفْسَهُ ، وَأَهْلَ بَيْتِهِ.\n31۔ وَإِنَّ لِيَهُودِ بَنِي النَّجَّارِ مِثْلَ مَا لِيَهُودِ بَنِي عَوْفٍ.\n32۔ وَإِنَّ لِيَهُودِ بَنِي الْحَارِثِ مِثْلَ مَا لِيَهُودِ بَنِي عَوْفٍ.\n33۔ وَإِنَّ لِيَهُودِ بَنِي سَاعِدَةَ مَا لِيَهُودِ بَنِي عَوْفٍ.\n34۔ وَإِنَّ لِيَهُودِ بَنِي جُشَمٍ مِثْلَ مَا لِيَهُودِ بَنِي عَوْفٍ.\n35۔ وَإِنَّ لِيَهُودِ بَنِي الْأَوْسِ مِثْلَ مَا لِيَهُودِ بَنِي عَوْفٍ.\n36۔ وَإِنَّ لِيَهُودِ بَنِي ثَعْلَبَةَ مِثْلَ مَا لِيَهُودِ بَنِي عَوْفٍ ، إلَّا مَنْ ظَلَمَ وَأَثِمَ ، فَإِنَّهُ لَا يُوتِغُ إلَّا نَفْسَهُ وَأَهْلَ بَيْتِهِ.\n37۔ وَإِنَّ جَفْنَةَ بَطْنٌ مِنْ ثَعْلَبَةَ كَأَنْفُسِهِمْ.\n38۔ وَإِنَّ لِبَنِي الشَّطِيبَةِ مِثْلَ مَا لِيَهُودِ بَنِي عَوْفٍ.\n39۔ وَإِنَّ الْبِرَّ دُونَ الْإِثْمِ.\n40۔ وَإِنَّ مَوَالِيَ ثَعْلَبَةَ كَأَنْفُسِهِمْ.\n41۔ وَإِنَّ بِطَانَةَ يَهُودَ كَأَنْفُسِهِمْ.\n42۔ وَإِنَّهُ لَا يَخْرَجُ مِنْهُمْ أَحَدٌ إلَّا بِإِذْنِ مُحَمَّدٍ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ .\n43۔ وَإِنَّهُ لَا يُنْحَجَزُ عَلَى ثَأْرٍ جُرْحٌ.\n44۔ وَإِنَّهُ مَنْ فَتَكَ فَبِنَفْسِهِ فَتَكَ ، وَأَهْلِ بَيْتِهِ ، إلَّا مِنْ ظَلَمَ ؛ وَإِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلَى أَبَرِّ هَذَا.\n45۔ وَإِنَّ عَلَى الْيَهُودِ نَفَقَتَهُمْ.\n46۔ وَعَلَى الْمُسْلِمِينَ نَفَقَتَهُمْ.\n47۔ وَإِنَّ بَيْنَهُمْ النَّصْرَ عَلَى مَنْ حَارَبَ أَهْلَ هَذِهِ الصَّحِيفَةِ.\n48۔ وَإِنَّ بَيْنَهُمْ النُّصْحَ وَالنَّصِيحَةَ ، وَالْبِرَّ دُونَ الْإِثْمِ.\n49۔ وَإِنَّهُ لَمْ يَأْثَمْ امْرُؤٌ بِحَلِيفِهِ.\n50۔ وَإِنَّ النَّصْرَ لِلْمَظْلُومِ.\n51۔ وَإِنَّ الْيَهُودَ يُنْفِقُونَ مَعَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ مَا دَامُوا مُحَارَبِينَ.\n52۔ وَإِنَّ يَثْرِبَ حَرَامٌ جَوْفُهَا لِأَهْلِ هَذِهِ الصَّحِيفَةِ.\n53۔ وَإِنَّ الْجَارَ كَالنَّفْسِ غَيْرَ مُضَارٍّ وَلَا آثِمٌ.\n54۔ وَإِنَّهُ لَا تُجَارُ حُرْمَةٌ إلَّا بِإِذْنِ أَهْلِهَا.\n55۔ وَإِنَّهُ مَا كَانَ بَيْنَ أَهْلِ هَذِهِ الصَّحِيفَةِ مِنْ حَدَثٍ أَوْ اشْتِجَارٍ يُخَافُ فَسَادُهُ ، فَإِنَّ مَرَدَّهُ إلَى اللَّهِ عَزَّ وَجَلَّ ، وَإِلَى مُحَمَّدٍ رَسُولِ اللَّهِ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ.\n56۔ وَإِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلَى أَتْقَى مَا فِي هَذِهِ الصَّحِيفَةِ وَأَبَرِّهِ.\n57۔ وَإِنَّهُ لَا تُجَارُ قُرَيْشٌ وَلَا مَنْ نَصَرَهَا.\n58۔ وَإِنَّ بَيْنَهُمْ النَّصْرَ عَلَى مَنْ دَهَمَ يَثْرِبَ.\n59۔ وَإِذَا دُعُوا إلَى صُلْحٍ يُصَالِحُونَهُ وَيَلْبَسُونَهُ ، فَإِنَّهُمْ يُصَالِحُونَهُ وَيَلْبَسُونَهُ.\n60۔ وَإِنَّهُمْ إذَا دُعُوا إلَى مِثْلِ ذَلِكَ فَإِنَّهُ لَهُمْ عَلَى الْمُؤْمِنِينَ ، إلَّا مَنْ حَارَبَ فِي الدِّينِ.\n61۔ عَلَى كُلِّ أُنَاسٍ حِصَّتُهُمْ مِنْ جَانِبِهِمْ الَّذِي قِبَلَهُمْ.\n62 ۔ وَإِنَّ يَهُودَ الْأَوْسِ ، مَوَالِيَهُمْ وَأَنْفُسَهُمْ ، عَلَى مِثْلِ مَا لِأَهْلِ هَذِهِ الصَّحِيفَةِ . مَعَ الْبِرِّ الْمَحْضِ؟ مِنْ أَهْلِ هَذِهِ الصَّحِيفَةِ.\n63۔ وَإِنَّ الْبِرَّ دُونَ الْإِثْمِ ، لَا يَكْسِبُ كَاسِبٌ إلَّا عَلَى نَفْسِهِ.\n64۔ وَإِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلَى أَصْدَقِ مَا فِي هَذِهِ الصَّحِيفَةِ وَأَبَرِّهِ.\n65۔ وَإِنَّهُ لَا يَحُولُ هَذَا الْكِتَابُ دُونَ ظَالِمٍ وَآثِمٍ.\n66۔ وَإِنَّهُ مَنْ خَرَجَ آمِنٌ.\n67۔ وَمَنْ قَعَدَ آمِنٌ بِالْمَدِينَةِ ، إلَّا مَنْ ظَلَمَ أَوْ أَثِمَ.\n68۔ وَإِنَّ اللَّهَ جَارٌ لِمَنْ بَرَّ وَاتَّقَى ، وَمُحَمَّدٌ رَسُولُ اللَّهِ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ.\n1. In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. This is a document from Muhammad the Prophet ﷺ, between the believers and the Muslims of Quraysh and Yathrib, and those who follow them, join them, and strive alongside them. Indeed, they are one community (ummah) to the exclusion of other people.\n2. The Muhājirūn of Quraysh, according to their former condition, shall pay and receive blood-money among themselves, and they shall ransom their captives with kindness and justice among the believers.\n3. Banū ʿAwf, according to their former condition, shall pay and receive blood-money as before, and each group shall ransom its captives with kindness and justice among the believers.\n4. Banū Sāʿidah, according to their former condition, shall pay and receive blood-money as before, and each group of them shall ransom its captives with kindness and justice among the believers.\n5. Banū al-Ḥārith, according to their former condition, shall pay and receive blood-money as before, and each group shall ransom its captives with kindness and justice among the believers.\n6. Banū Jusham, according to their former condition, shall pay and receive blood-money as before, and each group of them shall ransom its captives with kindness and justice among the believers.\n7. Banū al-Najjār, according to their former condition, shall pay and receive blood-money as before, and each group of them shall ransom its captives with kindness and justice among the believers.\n8. Banū ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf, according to their former condition, shall pay and receive blood-money as before, and each group shall ransom its captives with kindness and justice among the believers.\n9. Banū al-Nabīt, according to their former condition, shall pay and receive blood-money as before, and each group shall ransom its captives with kindness and justice among the believers.\n10. Banū al-Aws, according to their former condition, shall pay and receive blood-money as before, and each group of them shall ransom its captives with kindness and justice among the believers.\n11. The believers shall never leave anyone among them who is destitute and burdened with debt without giving him, with kindness, what is due for ransom or blood-money. And no believer shall make an alliance with the freed slave of another believer without his permission.\n12. The God-fearing believers shall stand against whoever among them rebels, or seeks to obtain something through injustice, sin, aggression, or corruption among the believers. Their hands shall all be against such a person, even if he is the son of one of them.\n13. No believer shall kill a believer in retaliation for a disbeliever.\n14. Nor shall he help a disbeliever against a believer.\n15. The protection of Allah is one; even the least of the believers may grant protection on their behalf, and all the believers are bound by it.\n16. The believers are allies of one another, to the exclusion of other people.\n17. Whoever of the Jews follows us shall have help and equal treatment; they shall not be wronged, nor shall others be aided against them.\n18. The peace of the believers is one. No believer shall make peace apart from another believer in fighting in the path of Allah, except on the basis of equality and justice among them.\n19. Every expedition that fights with us shall take turns, one after another.\n20. The believers shall exact retribution from one another for the blood shed in the path of Allah.\n21. The God-fearing believers are on the best and straightest guidance.\n22. No polytheist shall grant protection to any property or person of Quraysh, nor shall he stand in the way of a believer in this regard.\n23. Whoever kills a believer unjustly, with clear proof, shall be killed in retaliation, unless the guardian of the slain person agrees to accept compensation.\n24. All the believers shall stand against him, and nothing is lawful for them except to rise against him.\n25. It is not lawful for a believer who has affirmed what is in this document, and who believes in Allah and the Last Day, to help a wrongdoer or to give him shelter.\n26. Whoever helps him or gives him shelter, upon him shall be the curse of Allah and His wrath on the Day of Resurrection, and no obligatory or voluntary act shall be accepted from him.\n27. Whatever you differ about, its reference is to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and to Muhammad ﷺ.\n28. The Jews shall bear expenses together with the believers as long as they are at war.\n29. The Jews of Banū ʿAwf are a community together with the believers.\n30. The Jews have their religion and the Muslims have their religion, both their allies and themselves, except for those who commit injustice and sin, for he only brings ruin upon himself and his household.\n31. The Jews of Banū al-Najjār have the same as the Jews of Banū ʿAwf.\n32. The Jews of Banū al-Ḥārith have the same as the Jews of Banū ʿAwf.\n33. The Jews of Banū Sāʿidah have the same as the Jews of Banū ʿAwf.\n34. The Jews of Banū Jusham have the same as the Jews of Banū ʿAwf.\n35. The Jews of Banū al-Aws have the same as the Jews of Banū ʿAwf.\n36. The Jews of Banū Thaʿlabah have the same as the Jews of Banū ʿAwf, except for whoever commits injustice and sin, for he only brings ruin upon himself and his household.\n37. Jafnah, a branch of Thaʿlabah, is treated as themselves.\n38. Banū al-Shuṭaybah have the same as the Jews of Banū ʿAwf.\n39. Loyalty to the covenant is a barrier against sin.\n40. The freed slaves of Thaʿlabah are treated as themselves.\n41. The close associates of the Jews are treated as themselves.\n42. None of them shall go out except with the permission of Muhammad ﷺ.\n43. No one shall be barred from retaliation for a wound.\n44. Whoever strikes treacherously, strikes only at himself and his household, except one who has been wronged; and Allah is with the most faithful in this.\n45. The Jews shall bear their expenses.\n46. And the Muslims shall bear their expenses.\n47. Between them there shall be mutual help against whoever fights the people of this document.\n48. Between them there shall be sincere goodwill and sound counsel, and righteousness rather than sin.\n49. No one commits a sin because of his ally.\n50. Help is due to the one who is wronged.\n51. The Jews shall bear expenses together with the believers as long as they are at war.\n52. The interior of Yathrib is sacred for the people of this document.\n53. The neighbor is like oneself, not to be harmed and not to be sinned against.\n54. No woman shall be given protection except with the permission of her people.\n55. Whatever incident or dispute arises among the people of this document from which corruption is feared, its reference is to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and to Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\n56. Allah is with the most God-fearing and most righteous of what is in this document.\n57. Neither Quraysh nor those who help them shall be given protection.\n58. Between them there shall be mutual help against whoever attacks Yathrib.\n59. If they are called to a peace which they make and join, they shall make it and join it.\n60. And if they are called to the like of that, then they have the same right over the believers, except for those who fight over the religion.\n61. Each group bears its share from the side that faces them.\n62. The Jews of al-Aws, their allies and themselves, have the same as the people of this document, along with sincere righteousness from the people of this document.\n63. Righteousness is a barrier against sin; no one earns anything except against himself.\n64. Allah is with the most truthful and most righteous of what is in this document.\n65. This document shall not protect a wrongdoer or a sinner.\n66. Whoever goes out is safe,\n67. and whoever stays in Madinah is safe, except one who commits injustice or sin.\n68. Allah is the protector of whoever is righteous and God-fearing, and Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, is as well."],[394,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a group to Abū Rāfiʿ. ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAtīk entered his house at night while he was sleeping and killed him."],[395,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent some men from the Anṣār to Abū Rāfiʿ the Jew and appointed ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAtīk over them. Abū Rāfiʿ used to harm the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and support others against him, and he had a fortress in the land of the Ḥijāz. When they came near him, the sun had set and the people had returned with their livestock. ʿAbdullāh said to his companions, “Sit in your place. I am going and will act gently with the gatekeeper; perhaps I will be able to enter.” He came forward until he drew near to the gate, then covered himself with his garment as if relieving himself, while the people had entered. The gatekeeper called out to him, “O ʿAbdullāh, if you want to enter, then enter, for I intend to close the gate.” So I entered and hid. When the people had entered, he closed the gate and hung the keys on a peg. He said: I went to the keys, took them, and opened the gate. Abū Rāfiʿ used to have people sit with him at night, and he was in an upper chamber of his. When those who had spent the evening with him left, I climbed up to him, and every time I opened a door, I locked it behind me from the inside. I said: If the people become aware of me, they will not reach me until I have killed him. I reached him, and he was in a dark room among his family. I did not know where he was in the room, so I said, “O Abū Rāfiʿ.” He said, “Who is this?” I moved toward the voice and struck him with the sword while I was startled, but I did not do anything effective. He cried out, so I left the room and stayed not far away. Then I entered upon him again and said, “What is that sound, O Abū Rāfiʿ?” He said, “May your mother be bereaved; a man in the house struck me with a sword just now.” He said: I struck him with a blow that wounded him severely but did not kill him. Then I placed the tip of the sword in his belly until it reached his back, and I knew that I had killed him. I began opening the doors one by one until I reached a stairway of his. I placed my foot down, thinking I had reached the ground, but I fell on a moonlit night and broke my leg. I tied it with a turban, then went and sat at the gate. I said: I will not leave tonight until I know whether I killed him. When the rooster crowed, the announcer of death stood on the wall and said, “I announce the death of Abū Rāfiʿ, the merchant of the people of the Ḥijāz.” I went to my companions and said, “Escape, for Allah has killed Abū Rāfiʿ.” I came to the Prophet ﷺ and told him. He said, “Stretch out your leg.” I stretched out my leg, and he wiped it, and it was as though I had never complained of it."],[396,"Kaʿb ibn Mālik narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed us, while we were at Khaybar, not to kill a child or a woman."],[397,"Ibn Isḥāq said: After returning from Najrān, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed in Madinah during the months of Jumādā al-Ākhirah, Rajab, Shaʿbān and Ramaḍān, and Quraysh fought the Battle of Uḥud in Shawwāl of the third year of the Hijrah."],[398,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “I saw as if I were inside a fortified coat of armor, and I saw cattle being slaughtered. I interpreted the fortified armor as Madinah, and the cattle as—by Allah, good.” He said: He said to his Companions, “If we remain in Madinah, then if they enter upon us there, we will fight them.” They said, “O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, they never entered upon us there in Jāhiliyyah, so how could they enter upon us there in Islam?” ʿAffān said in his narration: He said, “Then it is your affair.” He put on his armor. The Anṣār then said, “We have rejected the opinion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” They came and said, “O Messenger of Allah, then do as you wish.” He said, “It is not for a prophet, once he has put on his armor, to take it off until he fights.”"],[399,"Abū Mūsā narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I saw in a dream that I was emigrating from Makkah to a land filled with date palms. At first I thought it was al-Yamāmah or Hajar, but it turned out to be Madinah, Yathrib. In that same dream I shook my sword, and its blade broke. I interpreted that as what befell the believers on the Day of Uḥud. Then I shook it again, and it returned even better than before. I interpreted that as the victory Allah granted and the reunion of the believers. I also saw cattle—and Allah is the source of all good. They represented the believers on the Day of Uḥud, while the good referred to the goodness Allah later brought and the reward for truthfulness that He granted us after Badr.”"],[400,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ awarded himself the sword Dhū al-Fiqār on the Day of Badr. Ibn ʿAbbās said: This was the sword concerning which he saw the dream before Uḥud. When the polytheists came for the Battle of Uḥud, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preferred to remain inside Madinah and fight them there. But some people who had not witnessed Badr said: “O Messenger of Allah, take us out to meet them at Uḥud so that we may attain the virtue attained by the people of Badr.” They continued urging the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until he put on his armor. Then they regretted it and said: “O Messenger of Allah, remain here, for the decision is yours.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “It is not appropriate for a prophet to remove his armor after putting it on until Allah judges between him and his enemy.” He also said: “I saw myself inside a fortified coat of armor, and I interpreted it as Madinah. I saw that I had a ram riding behind me, and I interpreted it as the commander of the army. I saw that my sword Dhū al-Fiqār had a notch in it, and I interpreted it as a calamity that would befall you. I also saw cattle being slaughtered—and the cattle, by Allah, are good.”"],[401,"Allah the Exalted said: “Allah would not leave the believers in the state in which you now are, until He separates the impure from the pure.” (Āl ʿImrān 3:179)\nAnd Allah the Exalted said: “And so that He may know those who acted hypocritically, and who were told: ‘Come, fight in the way of Allah, or defend,’ they said: ‘If we knew of any fighting, we would certainly follow you.’ On that day they were nearer to disbelief than to faith; they say with their mouths what is not in their hearts, and Allah knows best what they conceal.” (Āl ʿImrān 3:167)\nMuhammad ibn Isḥāq reported: Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihāb, Muhammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān, ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah, Ḥuṣayn ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAmr ibn Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, and other scholars of ours told me that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out toward Uḥud, a thousand of his Companions were with him. When he reached al-Shawṭ (which lies between Uḥud and Madinah), ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl withdrew with a third of the people and said: “He listened to them and disobeyed me. By Allah, for what are we giving our lives here, people?” Then he took back with him those who were with him, among them the hypocrites of his tribe and people afflicted with doubt and suspicion. ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥarām, a brother of Banū Salimah, followed them and said: “O my people! Remember Allah, and do not abandon your Prophet and your people when their enemy is before them.” They said: “If we knew that you would fight, we would not leave you, but we do not think there will be a battle here.” When they would not listen to him and insisted on going back, he said: “May Allah keep you far away, O enemies of Allah! Allah will manage His affair without you.” Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went on ahead."],[402,"Al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām narrated: The Prophet ﷺ wore two coats of armor on the Day of Uḥud. When he attempted to climb a large rock, he was unable to do so. Ṭalḥah sat beneath him, allowing the Prophet ﷺ to step upon him until he reached the top of the rock. Al-Zubayr said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “Ṭalḥah has secured Paradise.”"],[403,"Al-Sā’ib ibn Yazīd narrated: The Prophet ﷺ wore two coats of armor together on the Day of Uḥud."],[404,"Ibn Isḥāq said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prepared for battle with seven hundred men. The disbelieving army numbered three thousand, of whom two hundred were cavalry and the rest infantry. Khālid ibn al-Walīd commanded the right wing of the disbelievers’ army, while ʿIkrimah ibn Abī Jahl was the commander of the left wing.\nAnd the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded the archers, who were fifty men, to shower arrows on the horses coming toward us so that they could not come at us from behind, whether the battle went in our favor or against us."],[405,"Anas narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took a sword on the Day of Uḥud and said: “Who will take this sword from me with its due right?” Many stretched out their hands, each saying: “I will! I will!” He then asked: “Who will take it with its due right?” The people hesitated. Then Simāk ibn Kharashah, Abū Dujānah, said: “I will take it with its due right.” He took it and split open the heads of the polytheists with it."],[406,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: ʿAlī came carrying his sword on the Day of Uḥud after it had become bent. He said to Fāṭimah: “Take this sword with praise, for it has satisfied me.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “If you have fought well with your sword, then Sahl ibn Ḥunayf, Abū Dujānah, ʿĀṣim ibn Thābit al-Aflaḥ, and al-Ḥārith ibn al-Ṣimmah have also fought excellently.”"],[407,"Abū ʿUqbah, a freedman of Persian origin, said: I fought with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Uḥud. I struck one of the polytheists and said: “Take that from me—I am the Persian youth!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned toward me and said: “Why did you not say: ‘Take that from me—I am the Anṣārī youth’?”"],[408,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated: When the Day of Uḥud came, the polytheists were initially defeated. Then Iblīs—may Allah curse him—shouted: “O servants of Allah, those behind you!” So the front ranks of the Muslims turned back toward those behind them, and they began fighting one another. Ḥudhayfah then saw that one of those being attacked was his own father, al-Yamān. He cried out: “O servants of Allah! My father! My father!” But, by Allah, they did not stop until they killed him. Ḥudhayfah then said: “May Allah forgive you.” ʿUrwah said: By Allah, there remained in Ḥudhayfah a remnant of goodness until he met Allah."],[409,"Al-Zubayr said: “By Allah, that day I could see the women’s anklets as they ran with their garments raised after the enemy had been routed. I saw nothing standing between us and capturing them. We thought that the enemy had been killed and that none of them would ever return to us. The standard-bearers had been struck down, [but they remained steadfast around the standard until it passed to an Abyssinian slave of theirs named Sawab. Then Sawab was killed and the standard fell]. No one among Allah Most High’s creation would approach it until ʿAmrah bint ʿAlqamah al-Harithiyyah sprang toward it, raised it for them, and the people rallied around it.\n“By Allah, we remained in that condition, having gained the upper hand over them and defeated them, when the archers disobeyed the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They went down toward the camp when they saw it left exposed after we had driven the enemy away from it. They desired the spoils and abandoned their covenant with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and began taking the belongings. The cavalry then came upon us from behind and crushed our ranks. The people turned back in flight, and someone—whom they believed to be Satan—shouted, ‘Muhammad has been killed!’ The people were struck with tremendous alarm and trampled over one another. They became three groups: one-third wounded, one-third killed, and one-third fleeing. The battle had reached its height. “The archers had disagreed among themselves. One group, seeing the people collecting the spoils after Allah Most High had defeated the polytheists and the Muslims had taken the booty, said, ‘What are you waiting for?’ Another group said, ‘The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave you explicit instructions and forbade you from leaving your position, whether the battle went against him or in his favor.’ They disputed over this. Then the first group of archers insisted on joining the army, so the men dispersed and abandoned their position. It was then that the cavalry of the polytheists charged.”"],[410,"Al-Baraʾ ibn ʿAzib reported that on the day of Uhud, the Prophet ﷺ appointed ʿAbdullah ibn Jubayr as commander over the infantry, who numbered fifty men. He said: “Even if you see birds snatching us away, do not leave this position until I send for you. And if you see us defeat and trample the enemy, do not leave until I send for you.” The Muslims defeated them. Al-Baraʾ said: “By Allah, I saw the women running swiftly with their garments raised, their anklets and lower legs exposed. The men under Ibn Jubayr said, ‘The spoils, people! The spoils! Your companions have prevailed, so what are you waiting for?’ ʿAbdullah ibn Jubayr said, ‘Have you forgotten what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told you?’ They replied, ‘By Allah, we will go to the people and obtain a share of the spoils.’ “When they reached them, the tide was turned against them, and they began fleeing. That was when the Messenger was calling to them from behind. Only twelve men remained with the Prophet ﷺ. The enemy killed seventy of us, whereas the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions had inflicted one hundred and forty casualties upon the polytheists at Badr: seventy captives and seventy killed. “Abū Sufyan then called three times, ‘Is Muhammad among the people?’ The Prophet ﷺ forbade them from answering him. He then asked three times, ‘Is the son of Abū Quhafah among the people?’ Then he asked three times, ‘Is the son of al-Khattab among the people?’ He returned to his companions and said, ‘These men have been killed.’ “ʿUmar could no longer restrain himself and said, ‘You have lied, by Allah, enemy of Allah! All those whom you named are alive, and there still remains for you that which will distress you.’ Abū Sufyan said, ‘A day in return for the day of Badr; war has its alternating fortunes. You will find mutilation among your dead. I did not order it, but it did not displease me.’ “He then began chanting, ‘Rise high, Hubal! Rise high, Hubal!’ The Prophet ﷺ said, ‘Will you not answer him?’ They asked, ‘Messenger of Allah, what should we say?’ He said, ‘Say: Allah is higher and more majestic.’ Abū Sufyan said, ‘We have al-ʿUzza, whereas you have no ʿUzza.’ The Prophet ﷺ said, ‘Will you not answer him?’ They asked, ‘Messenger of Allah, what should we say?’ He said, ‘Say: Allah is our Protector, and you have no protector.’”"],[411,"Ibn ʿAbbas said: “Allah, Blessed and Exalted, did not grant victory at any place as He granted victory on the day of Uhud.” We found that difficult to accept, so Ibn ʿAbbas said: “The Book of Allah, Blessed and Exalted, will judge between me and whoever rejects this. Allah Almighty says concerning the day of Uhud: ‘Allah certainly fulfilled His promise to you when you were killing them by His permission.’ “Ibn ʿAbbas said that al-hass means killing. Allah then says: ‘Until, when you lost courage…’ through His saying: ‘He has already pardoned you, and Allah is the Possessor of bounty toward the believers.’ [Ali ʿImran: 152] “This referred specifically to the archers. The Prophet ﷺ stationed them in a particular position and said, ‘Protect our rear. If you see us being killed, do not come to our aid; and if you see us taking spoils, do not join us.’ “When the Prophet ﷺ gained the spoils and the camp of the polytheists was laid open, all the archers rushed down and entered the camp to plunder it. The ranks of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had become intermingled like this”—and he interlaced the fingers of his hands—“and they became confused. When the archers abandoned the opening where they had been stationed, the cavalry entered through that position and attacked the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ. They struck one another, became confused, and many Muslims were killed. “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions had held the advantage at the beginning of the day, to the point that seven or nine of the polytheists’ standard-bearers were killed. The Muslims then recoiled toward the mountain. They did not reach the cave, as people claim; rather, they were beneath al-Mihras. Satan cried out, ‘Muhammad has been killed!’ No one doubted that it was true. We continued in that condition, having no doubt that he had been killed, until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appeared between the two Saʿds. We recognized him by the swaying motion of his walk. “We rejoiced as though nothing of what had befallen us had happened. He climbed toward us, saying, ‘The wrath of Allah has become severe against a people who bloodied the face of His Messenger.’ On another occasion, he said, ‘O Allah, it is not for them to rise above us,’ until he reached us. “After some time, Abū Sufyan began shouting from the foot of the mountain, ‘Rise high, Hubal! Rise high, Hubal!’—referring to his deity—‘Where is the son of Abū Kabshah? Where is the son of Abū Quhafah? Where is the son of al-Khattab?’ “ʿUmar said, ‘Messenger of Allah, may I answer him?’ He said, ‘Yes.’ When Abū Sufyan said, ‘Rise high, Hubal,’ ʿUmar replied, ‘Allah is higher and more majestic.’ Abū Sufyan said, ‘Son of al-Khattab, you have certainly gladdened its eye, so leave it—or refrain from it.’ He then asked, ‘Where is the son of Abū Kabshah? Where is the son of Abū Quhafah? Where is the son of al-Khattab?’ “ʿUmar replied, ‘Here is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, here is Abū Bakr, and here I am, ʿUmar.’ “Abū Sufyan said, ‘A day in return for the day of Badr. Days alternate, and war has its changing fortunes.’ ʿUmar replied, ‘They are not equal. Our dead are in Paradise, whereas your dead are in the Fire.’ Abū Sufyan said, ‘If that is what you claim, then we have indeed failed and lost.’ “Abū Sufyan then said, ‘You will find mutilation among your dead, but it was not done by the decision of our leaders.’ Then the zeal of the Age of Ignorance overtook him, and he said, ‘It did happen, and we did not dislike it.’”"],[412,"Anas ibn Malik reported that on the day of Uhud, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say: “O Allah! If You so will, You will not be worshipped upon the earth.”"],[413,"قال الله تعالى: ﴿ثُمَّ اَنْزَلَ عَلَيْكُمْ مِّنْۢ بَعْدِ الْغَمِّ اَمَنَةً نُّعَاسًا يَّغْشٰى طَآىِٕفَةً مِّنْكُمْ١ۙ وَ طَآىِٕفَةٌ قَدْ اَهَمَّتْهُمْ اَنْفُسُهُمْ يَظُنُّوْنَ بِاللّٰهِ غَيْرَ الْحَقِّ ظَنَّ الْجَاهِلِيَّةِ١ؕ﴾ [آل عمران: ١٥٤]\nAllah the Exalted says: Then, after the distress, He sent down upon you security, a drowsiness that overcame a group of you, while another group was concerned only about themselves, thinking of Allah other than the truth, the thoughts of the Days of Ignorance. (Āl ʿImrān 3:154)"],[414,"Anas reported that Abū Talhah said: “Drowsiness overcame us while we were standing in our battle lines on the day of Uhud. My sword kept falling from my hand; I would pick it up, then it would fall again, and I would pick it up.”"],[415,"Anas reported that Abū Talhah said: “Drowsiness overcame us while we were in our battle lines on the day of Uhud.” He related that he was among those overcome by drowsiness that day, saying: “My sword kept falling from my hand and I would pick it up; then it would fall from my hand and I would pick it up again. The other group were the hypocrites, whose only concern was themselves—the most cowardly of people, the most panic-stricken, and the most forsaking of the truth.”"],[416,"Al-Zubayr said: “I remember being with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of Uhud when fear became intense upon us and sleep was sent over us. Every one of us had his chin—or he said, his chin—upon his chest. By Allah, I could hear, as though in a dream, Muʿattib ibn Qushayr saying: ‘If we had possessed any authority in this matter, we would not have been killed here.’ “I memorized those words, and Allah Almighty revealed concerning them: ‘Then, after the distress, He sent down upon you a sense of security—a drowsiness,’ through His saying, ‘We would not have been killed here,’ because of the words of Muʿattib ibn Qushayr. He then said: ‘Say: Even if you had remained in your homes,’ until He reached: ‘And Allah knows well what is within the breasts.’” [Ali ʿImran: 154]"],[417,"قال الله عز وجل: ﴿اِنَّ الَّذِيْنَ تَوَلَّوْا مِنْكُمْ يَوْمَ الْتَقَى الْجَمْعٰنِ١ۙ اِنَّمَا اسْتَزَلَّهُمُ الشَّيْطٰنُ بِبَعْضِ مَا كَسَبُوْا١ۚ وَ لَقَدْ عَفَا اللّٰهُ عَنْهُمْ١ؕ اِنَّ اللّٰهَ غَفُوْرٌ حَلِيْمٌؒ۰۰۱۵۵﴾ [سورة آل عمران: ١٥٥]\nAllah the Mighty and Majestic said: Indeed, those of you who turned away on the day the two armies met, it was only Satan who made them slip because of some of what they had earned; and Allah has certainly pardoned them. Indeed, Allah is Forgiving and Forbearing. (Āl ʿImrān 3:155)"],[418,"ʿUthman ibn Mawhab reported: A man who had performed the pilgrimage came and saw a group of people sitting together. He asked, “Who are these people sitting here?” They replied, “They are Quraysh.” He asked, “Who is the elder?” They replied, “Ibn ʿUmar.” He approached him and said, “I am going to ask you about something. Will you answer me? I adjure you by the sanctity of this House: do you know that ʿUthman fled on the day of Uhud?” Ibn ʿUmar replied, “Yes.” He asked, “Do you know that he was absent from Badr and did not attend it?” He replied, “Yes.” He asked, “Do you know that he remained behind from the Pledge of al-Ridwan and did not attend it?” He replied, “Yes.” The man then proclaimed, “Allahu Akbar!” Ibn ʿUmar said: “Come here, so that I may inform you and clarify the matters about which you asked me. As for his flight on the day of Uhud, I testify that Allah pardoned him. As for his absence from Badr, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was his wife, and she was ill. The Prophet ﷺ therefore told him, ‘You will receive the reward and the share of a man who attended Badr.’ “As for his absence from the Pledge of al-Ridwan, had there been anyone in the valley of Makkah possessing greater influence and protection than ʿUthman ibn ʿAffan, the Prophet ﷺ would have sent that person in his place. Instead, he sent ʿUthman. The Pledge of al-Ridwan took place after ʿUthman had gone to Makkah. The Prophet ﷺ indicated his right hand and said, ‘This is the hand of ʿUthman.’ He then struck it against his other hand and said, ‘This pledge is for ʿUthman.’ Now take this explanation with you.”"],[419,"ʿAbdullah ibn al-Zubayr said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, “Talhah has secured Paradise,” after Talhah had done what he did in defense of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The people had fled from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until some of them reached al-Munaqqa, short of al-Aʿwas. ʿUthman ibn ʿAffan fled, as did ʿUqbah ibn ʿUthman and Saʿd ibn ʿUthman—the latter two being men of the Ansar from Banu Zurayq—until they reached al-Jalʿab, a mountain in the vicinity of Madinah. They remained there for three days and then returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “You certainly went very far in your flight.”"],[420,"Shaqiq reported: ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn ʿAwf met al-Walid ibn ʿUqbah. Al-Walid said to him, “Why do I see that you have distanced yourself from the Commander of the Believers, ʿUthman?” ʿAbd al-Rahman replied, “Convey to him that I did not flee on the day of ʿAynayn”—ʿAsim said that he meant the day of Uhud—“I did not remain behind on the day of Badr, and I did not abandon the way of ʿUmar.” Al-Walid went and informed ʿUthman of this. ʿUthman said: “As for his statement, ‘I did not flee on the day of ʿAynayn,’ how can he reproach me for a sin that Allah has already pardoned?” Allah said: “Indeed, those of you who turned away on the day the two armies met were only caused to slip by Satan because of some of what they had earned. Yet Allah has certainly pardoned them.” [Ali ʿImran: 155] “As for his statement that I remained behind on the day of Badr, I was nursing Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, until she died. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ assigned me my share, and anyone to whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ assigned a share is counted among those who attended. “As for his statement, ‘I did not abandon the way of ʿUmar,’ neither I nor he is capable of following it fully. Go to him and relate this to him.”"],[421,"ʿAbdullah ibn Kaʿb ibn Malik reported that Kaʿb was the first person to recognize the Messenger of Allah ﷺ after the defeat, when the people were saying, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has been killed.” Kaʿb said: “I recognized his eyes gleaming beneath his helmet, so I called out at the top of my voice, ‘Muslims, rejoice! This is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ!’ He motioned to me to remain silent. “When the Muslims recognized the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, they rose with him and headed toward the mountain pass. Accompanying him were Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿAli, Talhah, al-Zubayr, al-Harith ibn al-Simmah, and a group of Muslims. “When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took shelter in the mountain pass, Ubayy ibn Khalaf caught up with him, saying, ‘Muhammad, may I not survive if you survive!’ The men asked, ‘Messenger of Allah, should one of us turn upon him?’ He replied, ‘Leave him.’ “When Ubayy drew near, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took the spear from al-Harith ibn al-Simmah. One of the men, according to what was related to me, said: ‘When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took it, he shook himself with such force that we scattered away from him like biting flies scattering from the back of a camel when it shakes itself. He then faced Ubayy and struck him with the spear, causing him repeatedly to tumble from the back of his horse.’”"],[422,"Al-Baraʾ ibn ʿAzib reported: “On the day of Uhud, the Prophet ﷺ placed ʿAbdullah ibn Jubayr in command of the archers. The polytheists killed seventy of us, whereas the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions had inflicted one hundred and forty casualties upon the polytheists on the day of Badr: seventy captives and seventy killed. Abū Sufyan said, ‘A day in return for the day of Badr; war has its alternating fortunes.’”"],[423,"Qatadah said: “We do not know of any clan among the Arab clans having more martyrs, or being more honored on the Day of Resurrection, than the Ansar.” Qatadah said: “Anas ibn Malik related to us that seventy of them were killed on the day of Uhud, seventy on the day of Bi’r Maʿunah, and seventy on the day of al-Yamamah. Bi’r Maʿunah occurred during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while the day of al-Yamamah occurred during the caliphate of Abū Bakr al-Siddiq, on the occasion of Musaylimah the Liar.”"],[424,"Jabir reported: “Some people drank wine on the morning of Uhud and were all killed as martyrs that same day. This occurred before wine was prohibited.” Among them were Hamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muttalib, al-Yaman—the father of Hudhayfah—Anas ibn al-Nadr, and Musʿab ibn ʿUmayr."],[425,"Food was brought to ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn ʿAwf while he was fasting. He said: “Musʿab ibn ʿUmayr was killed, and he was better than I. He was shrouded in a cloak: when his head was covered, his feet became exposed, and when his feet were covered, his head became exposed.” The narrator said: “I think he also said, ‘Hamzah was killed, and he was better than I.’” ʿAbd al-Rahman continued: “Then the worldly provisions that were spread out for us were spread out—or he said: we were given of this world what we were given—and we fear that the rewards for our good deeds may have been hastened for us in this world.” He then began to weep until he left the food untouched."],[426,"Khabbab ibn al-Aratt reported: “We emigrated with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the cause of Allah, seeking the countenance of Allah, and our reward became assured with Allah. Some of us passed away without consuming anything of their reward in this world. Among them was Musʿab ibn ʿUmayr, who was killed on the day of Uhud. Nothing could be found in which to shroud him except a short striped cloak. Whenever we placed it over his head, his feet became exposed, and whenever we placed it over his feet, his head became exposed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ therefore said, ‘Place it over the side of his head and cover his feet with idhkhir grass.’ “Others among us lived until their fruits ripened, and they are now gathering them.”"],[427,"Jabir ibn ʿAbdullah reported that on the day of Uhud, a man asked the Prophet ﷺ, “Tell me: if I am killed, where will I be?” He replied, “In Paradise.” The man threw away the dates he was holding and fought until he was killed."],[428,"Anas reported: “My paternal uncle Anas ibn al-Nadr was absent from the fighting at Badr. He said, ‘Messenger of Allah, I was absent from the first battle in which you fought the polytheists. If Allah allows me to witness another battle against the polytheists, Allah will certainly see what I shall do.’ “When the day of Uhud came and the Muslims retreated, he said, ‘O Allah, I apologize to You for what these people have done’—meaning his companions—‘and I disassociate myself before You from what these people have done’—meaning the polytheists. “He then advanced and encountered Saʿd ibn Muʿadh. He said, ‘Saʿd ibn Muʿadh! Paradise, by the Lord of al-Nadr! I can smell its fragrance from this side of Uhud.’ “Saʿd later said, ‘Messenger of Allah, I could not accomplish what he accomplished.’” Anas continued: “We found more than eighty wounds upon him—blows from swords, thrusts from spears, and shots from arrows. We found that he had been killed and that the polytheists had mutilated him. No one could recognize him except his sister, who recognized him by his fingertips. “We used to believe—or suppose—that the following verse was revealed concerning him and others like him: ‘Among the believers are men who have been true to the covenant they made with Allah.’” [Al-Ahzab: 23]"],[429,"Anas ibn Malik reported: “That day, we found seventy wounds upon Anas ibn al-Nadr. No one recognized him except his sister, who recognized him by his fingertips.”"],[430,"Jabir reported: “My father was brought on the day of Uhud after he had been mutilated. He was placed before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and covered with a cloth. I went to uncover him, but my people stopped me. I went again to uncover him, but my people stopped me. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then ordered that he be carried away. “He heard the voice of a woman crying and asked, ‘Who is this?’ They replied, ‘The daughter of ʿAmr’—or: ‘the sister of ʿAmr.’ He said, ‘Why is she weeping?’—or: ‘Do not weep. The angels continued shading him with their wings until he was lifted away.’”"],[431,"Jabir reported: “When the time of Uhud approached, my father called me during the night and said, ‘I believe that I will be among the first of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ to be killed. Apart from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ himself, I leave behind no one dearer to me than you. I have a debt, so repay it, and treat your sisters well.’ “When morning came, he was the first person killed. Another man was buried with him in the same grave. My heart was not content to leave him with the other man, so I exhumed him six months later. He was just as he had been on the day I buried him, except for a slight change near his ear.”"],[432,"Abū Qatadah, who was present on that occasion, reported: ʿAmr ibn al-Jamuh came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked, “Messenger of Allah, if I fight in the cause of Allah until I am killed, will I walk in Paradise with this leg of mine restored to health?” His leg was lame. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied, “Yes.” He, his nephew, and one of their freedmen were killed on the day of Uhud. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by him and said, “It is as though I can see you walking in Paradise with this leg of yours restored to health.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered that he and his freedman be placed together in one grave."],[433,"Anas ibn Malik reported that on the day of Uhud, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was left isolated with seven men from the Ansar and two men from Quraysh. When the enemy closed in upon him, he said, “Who will repel them from us in return for Paradise—or so that he may be my companion in Paradise?” A man from the Ansar advanced and fought until he was killed. The enemy then closed in upon them again, and the Prophet ﷺ said, “Who will repel them from us in return for Paradise—or so that he may be my companion in Paradise?” Another man from the Ansar advanced and fought until he was killed. This continued until all seven were killed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said to his two companions, “We did not deal fairly with our companions.”"],[434,"Ubayy ibn Kaʿb reported: “On the day of Uhud, sixty-four men from the Ansar and six from the Muhajirun were killed, among them Hamzah. The polytheists mutilated their dead. “The Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ‘If we are ever given a day against the polytheists like this day, we will certainly inflict more upon them.’ “When the day of the Conquest came, an unidentified man said, ‘There will be no Quraysh after today!’ The herald of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ then proclaimed, ‘Everyone, black and white, has been granted safety except for so-and-so and so-and-so,’ naming certain people. “Allah Almighty then revealed: ‘If you retaliate, then retaliate with the equivalent of what you were made to suffer. But if you remain patient, that is certainly better for those who are patient.’ [Al-Nahl: 126] “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ‘We shall remain patient and shall not retaliate.’”"],[435,"Abū Hurayrah reported that ʿAmr ibn Uqaysh had money owed to him through usury during the Age of Ignorance, and he was unwilling to embrace Islam until he had collected it. On the day of Uhud, he came and asked, “Where are my cousins?” They replied, “At Uhud.” He asked, “Where is so-and-so?” They replied, “At Uhud.” He asked, “Where is so-and-so?” They replied, “At Uhud.” He put on his armor, mounted his horse, and headed toward them. When the Muslims saw him, they said, “Keep away from us, ʿAmr!” He replied, “I have believed.” He fought until he was wounded and was carried back to his family. Saʿd ibn Muʿadh came and told his sister, “Ask him whether he fought out of tribal zeal for his people, out of anger on their behalf, or out of anger for Allah.” He replied, “Rather, it was out of anger for Allah and His Messenger!” He died, entered Paradise, and had never performed a single prayer for Allah."],[436,"Abū Hurayrah would say: “Tell me about a man who entered Paradise without ever having performed a prayer.” When the people did not know the answer, they would ask, “Who was he?” He would reply, “The Usayrim of Banu ʿAbd al-Ashhal: ʿAmr ibn Thabit ibn Waqsh.” Al-Husayn said: “I asked Mahmud ibn Labid, ‘What was the story of al-Usayrim?’ He replied: “ʿHe had refused to accept Islam despite his people having done so. When the day of Uhud came and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ departed for Uhud, Islam became appealing to him and he embraced it. He took his sword, went out in the morning until he reached the people, entered the midst of the fighting, and fought until his wounds incapacitated him. “ʿWhile the men of Banu ʿAbd al-Ashhal were searching for their dead on the battlefield, they suddenly found him. They said, “By Allah, this is al-Usayrim! What brought him here? We left him behind while he was still opposed to this matter.”\n“ʿThey asked him what had brought him. They said, “What brought you here, ʿAmr? Was it concern for your people, or a desire for Islam?” “ʿHe replied, “Rather, it was a desire for Islam. I believed in Allah and His Messenger and embraced Islam. I then took my sword, went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and fought until what has befallen me befell me.” “ʿHe died in their arms shortly afterward. They mentioned him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said, “He is indeed among the people of Paradise.”’”"],[437,"ʿA’ishah reported: “On the day of Uhud, the polytheists were defeated. Iblis—may Allah’s curse be upon him—then cried out, ‘Servants of Allah, beware of those behind you!’ The foremost ranks turned back, and they began fighting with those behind them. “Hudhayfah looked and saw that the man was his father, al-Yaman. He cried out, ‘Servants of Allah, my father! My father!’ But by Allah, they did not stop until they had killed him. Hudhayfah said, ‘May Allah forgive you.’” ʿUrwah said: “By Allah, a lasting remnant of goodness continued to remain in Hudhayfah until he returned to Allah.” “Basurtu” means “I knew or recognized,” deriving from insight into a matter. “Absartu” refers to seeing with the eye. It is also said that basurtu and absartu have the same meaning."],[438,"Saʿd ibn Abi Waqqas reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a woman from Banu Dinar whose husband, brother, and father had been killed while fighting alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Uhud. When she was informed of their deaths, she asked, “What happened to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” They replied, “He is well, mother of so-and-so. Praise be to Allah, he is as you would wish him to be.” She said, “Show him to me so that I may look at him.” They pointed him out to her, and when she saw him, she said, “Every calamity after seeing you safe is insignificant!” By jalal, she meant something small and insignificant."],[439,"Jaʿfar ibn ʿAmr ibn Umayyah al-Damri reported: “I travelled with ʿUbaydullah ibn ʿAdi ibn al-Khiyar. When we reached Hims, ʿUbaydullah ibn ʿAdi said to me, ‘Would you like to visit Wahshi and ask him about the killing of Hamzah?’ I replied, ‘Yes.’ “Wahshi was living in Hims, so we asked about him. We were told, ‘There he is, sitting in the shade of his palace, looking like a full waterskin.’ “We approached until we stood a short distance from him. We greeted him, and he returned the greeting. ʿUbaydullah had wrapped his turban around his head and face, so Wahshi could see nothing of him except his eyes and feet. “ʿUbaydullah asked, ‘Wahshi, do you recognize me?’ “Wahshi looked at him and said, ‘No, by Allah. However, I know that ʿAdi ibn al-Khiyar married a woman named Umm Qital bint Abi al-ʿIs. She bore him a boy in Makkah, and I used to seek a wet nurse for him. I carried that boy with his mother and handed him to her. It is as though I can still see your feet.’ “ʿUbaydullah uncovered his face and said, ‘Will you not tell us about the killing of Hamzah?’ “He replied, ʿYes. Hamzah had killed Tuʿaymah ibn ʿAdi ibn al-Khiyar at Badr. My master, Jubayr ibn Mutʿim, said to me, “If you kill Hamzah in retaliation for my uncle, you will be free.” “ʿWhen the people set out in the year of ʿAynayn—ʿAynayn being a mountain opposite Uhud, with a valley between them—I went out with the people to fight. When they formed their battle lines, Sibaʿ came forward and called, “Is there anyone who will duel me?” “ʿHamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muttalib came out against him and said, “Sibaʿ, son of Umm Anmar, the circumciser of women! Do you oppose Allah and His Messenger ﷺ?” He then charged him, and Sibaʿ became like yesterday that had passed away. “ʿI concealed myself from Hamzah beneath a rock. When he came near me, I threw my spear at him and lodged it in his lower abdomen until it emerged from between his hips. That was the end of him. “ʿWhen the people returned, I returned with them. I remained in Makkah until Islam became widespread there, after which I departed for al-Ta’if. Its people sent an envoy to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I was told that he did not harm envoys. I therefore went with them until I arrived before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. “ʿWhen he saw me, he asked, “Are you Wahshi?” “ʿI replied, “Yes.” “ʿHe asked, “Did you kill Hamzah?” “ʿI replied, “What occurred is as you have been informed.” “ʿHe said, “Can you keep your face out of my sight?” “ʿI then left. After the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away and Musaylimah the Liar emerged, I said, “I will certainly go out against Musaylimah. Perhaps I will kill him and thereby compensate for Hamzah.” “‘I went out with the people, and what happened took place. I suddenly saw a man standing in a breach in a wall, looking like an ash-colored camel, with dishevelled hair. I threw my spear at him and lodged it between his breasts until it emerged from between his shoulder blades. A man from the Ansar then sprang upon him and struck him on the crown of his head with his sword.’” ʿAbdullah ibn al-Fadl said: Sulayman ibn Yasar informed me that he heard ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar say: “A slave girl standing upon the roof of a house cried out, ‘Alas, Commander of the Believers! The black slave has killed him!’”"],[440,"Anas ibn Malik reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by Hamzah after his body had been mutilated. He said, “Were it not that Safiyyah would be deeply grieved, I would have left him until the beasts and birds of prey consumed him, so that he would be resurrected from their bellies.”"],[441,"It is narrated from Sayyiduna Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, that on the day of Uḥud the women were behind the Muslims, tending to the wounded of the polytheists. He said: “If I were to swear an oath, my oath would be true (and I would not be in breach of it) that on that day none of us wanted this world, until Allah revealed this verse: ﴿مِنْكُمْ مَّنْ يُّرِيْدُ الدُّنْيَا وَ مِنْكُمْ مَّنْ يُّرِيْدُ الْاٰخِرَةَ١ۚ ثُمَّ صَرَفَكُمْ عَنْهُمْ لِيَبْتَلِيَكُمْ۠١ۚ﴾ Some of you want this world and some of you want the Hereafter; then He turned you away from them to test you. When the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ disobeyed the Prophet’s command and did not carry it out, the Prophet ﷺ was left alone among only nine men, seven of them Anṣār and two of them Qurashī, with the Prophet ﷺ himself being the tenth. When the polytheists swarmed upon the Prophet ﷺ, the Prophet ﷺ said: “May Allah have mercy on the man who drives them away from us.” Hearing this, one of the Anṣār came forward, fought for a while, and was martyred. In the same way, one by one, all seven Anṣārī Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, were martyred. Seeing this, the Prophet ﷺ said: “We have not been fair to our companions.” After a short while Abū Sufyān came (he had not yet accepted Islam at that time) and began to shout the slogan of praise for Hubal. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Answer him: Allah alone is Most High and Most Majestic.” Abū Sufyān said: “We have al-ʿUzzā, and you have no ʿUzzā.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Answer him: Allah is our Protector, and the disbelievers have no protector.” Then Abū Sufyān said: “Today is the repayment for the Battle of Badr; the days alternate: one day for us and one day against us, one day we suffer and one day we rejoice. Ḥanẓalah for Ḥanẓalah, so-and-so for so-and-so, and so-and-so for so-and-so.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “There is still no equality between us and you: our slain are alive and receive provision, while your slain are being punished in the Fire of Hell.” Then Abū Sufyān said: “Some of the dead have been mutilated. This is not the work of our leaders; I neither ordered it nor forbade it, I neither liked it nor disliked it, it neither displeased me nor pleased me.” When the Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, looked, they found that the belly of Sayyiduna Ḥamzah, may Allah be pleased with him, had been cut open, and Abū Sufyān’s wife Hind had taken out his liver and chewed it, but she had not been able to swallow it. When the Prophet ﷺ saw his body, he asked: “Did she eat any of it?” The Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, said: “No.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Allah does not will that any part of Ḥamzah’s body enter the Fire.” Then the Prophet ﷺ placed his body before him and prayed the funeral prayer over him. Then the body of one of the Anṣār was brought and placed beside Sayyiduna Ḥamzah, may Allah be pleased with him, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed the funeral prayer over him too. Then the Anṣārī’s body was taken away and the body of Sayyiduna Ḥamzah was left there. Then another body was brought and placed beside Sayyiduna Amīr Ḥamzah, may Allah be pleased with him, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed the funeral prayer over it as well; then that body was taken away and Sayyiduna Ḥamzah’s body was left there. In this way, the funeral prayer was offered over Sayyiduna Ḥamzah, may Allah be pleased with him, seventy times that day.”\nIt is a well-known story in the books of sīrah and history that Hind bint ʿUtbah chewed the liver of Sayyiduna Ḥamzah, may Allah be pleased with him. Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah reported that Waḥshī cut open the chest of Sayyiduna Ḥamzah, may Allah be pleased with him, and gave his liver to Hind bint ʿUtbah. She tried to swallow it but could not. Ibn Isḥāq reported in his Sīrah, p. 516, that Hind herself cut open the chest of Sayyiduna Ḥamzah, may Allah be pleased with him, and took out the liver. She chewed it and tried to swallow it but could not. In this version there is the additional statement that Hind cut off the ears and noses of the martyrs and made anklets and a necklace out of them for herself.\nAl-Wāqidī said that after killing Sayyiduna Ḥamzah, may Allah be pleased with him, Waḥshī took out his liver and carried it with him to Makkah so that his master Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim could see it. Although these several reports are not narrated through sound chains, they indicate the fact that the chest of Sayyiduna Ḥamzah, may Allah be pleased with him, was cut open. As for what is related about Waḥshī ibn Ḥarb, that he said: “I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He asked: ‘Are you Waḥshī?’ I said: ‘Yes.’ He asked: ‘Was it you who killed Ḥamzah?’ I said: ‘Yes. Praise be to Allah, who honored Sayyiduna Ḥamzah, may Allah be pleased with him, and favored him at my hands, and did not disgrace me at his hands. Quraysh said: How can we love the killer of Ḥamzah? That cannot be.’ I said: ‘O Messenger of Allah, ask forgiveness for me.’ He spat lightly three times and placed his blessed hand on my chest three times and said: ‘Waḥshī, just as you fought the Muslims to keep people from the path of truth, go and fight in the same way for Allah, in His path.’” This report is weak. Al-Ṭabarānī narrated it in al-Muʿjam al-Kabīr, 22/139.\nImam al-Dhahabī considered Waḥshī ibn Ḥarb to be lenient (layyin) in terms of transmitting ḥadīth. Ḥāfiẓ Ibn Ḥajar counted him as unknown in status (mastūr). There is also something objectionable (nakārah) in the text of the above report. The Prophet ﷺ had told him to leave Madinah, and he came to Madinah only after the Prophet’s death. Al-Haythamī, in Majmaʿ al-Zawāʾid, 6/121, called its chain ḥasan; he relied on Ibn Ḥibbān’s declaration of him as trustworthy."],[442,"قال تعالى: ﴿وَ لَا تَهِنُوْا وَ لَا تَحْزَنُوْا وَ اَنْتُمُ الْاَعْلَوْنَ اِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُّؤْمِنِيْنَ۰۰۱۳۹ اِنْ يَّمْسَسْكُمْ قَرْحٌ فَقَدْ مَسَّ الْقَوْمَ قَرْحٌ مِّثْلُهٗ١ؕ وَ تِلْكَ الْاَيَّامُ نُدَاوِلُهَا بَيْنَ النَّاسِ١ۚ وَ لِيَعْلَمَ اللّٰهُ الَّذِيْنَ اٰمَنُوْا وَ يَتَّخِذَ مِنْكُمْ شُهَدَآءَ١ؕ وَ اللّٰهُ لَا يُحِبُّ الظّٰلِمِيْنَۙ۰۰۱۴۰ وَ لِيُمَحِّصَ اللّٰهُ الَّذِيْنَ اٰمَنُوْا وَ يَمْحَقَ الْكٰفِرِيْنَ۰۰۱۴۱ اَمْ حَسِبْتُمْ اَنْ تَدْخُلُوا الْجَنَّةَ وَ لَمَّا يَعْلَمِ اللّٰهُ الَّذِيْنَ جٰهَدُوْا مِنْكُمْ وَ يَعْلَمَ الصّٰبِرِيْنَ۰۰۱۴۲ وَ لَقَدْ كُنْتُمْ تَمَنَّوْنَ الْمَوْتَ مِنْ قَبْلِ اَنْ تَلْقَوْهُ١۪ فَقَدْ رَاَيْتُمُوْهُ وَ اَنْتُمْ تَنْظُرُوْنَؒ۰۰۱۴۳﴾ [آل عمران: ١٣٩ - ١٤٣]\nAllah the Exalted said: Do not become weak and do not grieve, and you will be the superior ones, if you are believers. (Āl ʿImrān 3:139)\nIf a wound has touched you, a similar wound has touched the others; and We alternate these days among the people, so that Allah may know those who believe and take martyrs from among you; and Allah does not love the wrongdoers. (Āl ʿImrān 3:140)\nAnd so that Allah may purify those who believe and wipe out the disbelievers. (Āl ʿImrān 3:141) Or do you think that you will enter Paradise while Allah has not yet made evident those among you who strive and has not yet made evident the steadfast? (Āl ʿImrān 3:142) And you used to wish for death before you met it; now you have seen it, while you were looking on. (Āl ʿImrān 3:143)"],[443,"ʿUqbah ibn ʿAmir reported: “Eight years after the battle, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed for those killed at Uhud, like one bidding farewell to both the living and the dead. He then ascended the pulpit and said, ‘I will precede you, and I shall be a witness over you…’” The remainder of the lengthy hadith follows."],[444,"Jabir ibn ʿAbdullah reported: “When news of Hamzah’s death reached the Prophet ﷺ, he wept. When he saw him, he sobbed.”"],[445,"Ibn ʿUmar reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by the women of ʿAbd al-Ashhal as they wept for those of their families who had died on the day of Uhud. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “But Hamzah has no women to weep for him.” The women of the Ansar then came and wept for Hamzah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ awoke and said, “Woe to them! Have they still not returned home? Tell them to return, and they must not wail over anyone who dies after today.”"],[446,"Yahya ibn ʿAbbad ibn al-Zubayr reported from his father, from his grandfather, who said: “I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say—after the people had fled from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until some of them reached a mountain near Madinah, short of al-Aʿrad, and then returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “ʿHanzalah ibn Abi ʿAmir encountered Abū Sufyan ibn Harb. When Hanzalah overpowered him, Shaddad ibn al-Aswad saw him and struck Hanzalah from above with his sword until he killed him. Hanzalah had nearly killed Abū Sufyan. “ʿThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “The angels are washing your companion Hanzalah. Ask his wife about him.” “ʿShe said, “He went out while in a state of major ritual impurity when he heard the alarm calling the people to battle.” “ʿThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “That is why the angels washed him.”’”"],[447,"Zayd ibn Thabit reported: “When the Prophet ﷺ set out for Uhud, some of those accompanying him turned back. One group said, ‘We should kill them,’ while another group said, ‘We should not kill them.’ Allah then revealed: ‘Why are you divided into two groups concerning the hypocrites?’ [Al-Nisaʾ: 88] “The Prophet ﷺ said, ‘It expels men just as fire removes impurities from iron.’”"],[448,"Saʿd ibn Abi Waqqas reported: “On the day of Uhud, I saw two men dressed in white on the right and left of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, fighting in his defense with the fiercest fighting. I never saw them before that day or afterward.”"],[449,"Anas reported: “On the day of Uhud, the people fled from the Prophet ﷺ, while Abū Talhah stood before him, shielding him with a leather shield. “Abū Talhah was a powerful archer with a strong draw. He broke two or three bows that day. Whenever a man passed carrying a quiver of arrows, the Prophet ﷺ would say, ‘Empty them out for Abū Talhah.’ “The Prophet ﷺ would raise himself to look toward the enemy, and Abū Talhah would say, ‘May my father and mother be sacrificed for you! Do not raise yourself, lest an arrow from the enemy strike you. Let my neck be struck instead of yours.’ “I saw ʿA’ishah bint Abi Bakr and Umm Sulaym with their garments raised. I could see the anklets upon their lower legs as they carried waterskins upon their backs and poured water into the mouths of the men. They would then return, refill the waterskins, come back, and pour the water into the mouths of the men. “The sword fell from Abū Talhah’s hands twice or three times.”"],[450,"ʿAli ibn Abi Talib reported: “I never heard the Prophet ﷺ offer both his parents as a ransom for anyone except Saʿd ibn Malik. I heard him say on the day of Uhud, ‘Saʿd, shoot! May my father and mother be sacrificed for you.’”"],[451,"Saʿd ibn Abi Waqqas reported: “On the day of Uhud, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered both of his parents as a ransom for me.” He meant the occasion when the Prophet ﷺ said, “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you,” while Saʿd was fighting."],[452,"Saʿd ibn Abi Waqqas reported that on the day of Uhud, the Prophet ﷺ offered both of his parents as a ransom for him. Saʿd said: “A man from among the polytheists had inflicted severe harm upon the Muslims. The Prophet ﷺ said to me, ‘Shoot! May my father and mother be sacrificed for you.’ “I drew an arrow that had no arrowhead and struck the man in his side. He fell, and his private parts became exposed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed until I could see his molar teeth.”"],[453,"Saʿd ibn Abi Waqqas reported: “On the day of Uhud, the Prophet ﷺ emptied his quiver out for me and said, ‘Shoot! May my father and mother be sacrificed for you.’”"],[454,"Abū ʿUthman—Abū ʿUthman al-Nahdi, ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn Mull—reported: “During one of those days on which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fought, no one remained with him except Talhah and Saʿd.” This was reported on their own authority."],[455,"Al-Sa’ib ibn Yazid reported: “I accompanied ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn ʿAwf, Talhah ibn ʿUbaydillah, al-Miqdad, and Saʿd—may Allah be pleased with them. I did not hear any of them narrate from the Prophet ﷺ, except that I heard Talhah narrating concerning the day of Uhud.”"],[456,"Qays ibn Abi Hazim reported: “I saw that Talhah’s hand was paralyzed. He had used it to shield the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Uhud.”"],[457,"Anas ibn Malik reported that on the day of Uhud, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was left isolated with seven men from the Ansar and two men from Quraysh. When the enemy closed in upon him, he said, “Who will repel them from us in return for Paradise—or so that he may be my companion in Paradise?” A man from the Ansar advanced and fought until he was killed. The enemy then closed in upon them again, and the Prophet ﷺ said, “Who will repel them from us in return for Paradise—or so that he may be my companion in Paradise?” Another man from the Ansar advanced and fought until he was killed. This continued until all seven were killed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said to his two companions, “We have not dealt fairly with our companions.”"],[458,"Jabir ibn ʿAbdullah reported: “When the people turned away on the day of Uhud, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was standing to one side with twelve men from the Ansar, among whom was Talhah ibn ʿUbaydillah. The polytheists caught up with them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned and asked, ‘Who will confront these people?’ “Talhah said, ‘I will.’ “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ‘Remain where you are.’ “A man from the Ansar said, ‘I will, Messenger of Allah.’ “He said, ‘Then you go.’ The man fought until he was killed. “The Prophet ﷺ turned again and saw the polytheists. He asked, ‘Who will confront these people?’ “Talhah said, ‘I will.’ “He said, ‘Remain where you are.’ “Another man from the Ansar said, ‘I will.’ “He said, ‘Then you go.’ The man fought until he was killed. “The Prophet ﷺ continued asking the same question, and each time a man from the Ansar went out and fought as the man before him had fought until he was killed. Eventually, only the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Talhah ibn ʿUbaydillah remained. “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked, ‘Who will confront these people?’ “Talhah replied, ‘I will.’ “Talhah then fought with the strength of all eleven men. His hand was struck and his fingers were severed, causing him to exclaim, ‘Hass!’ The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ‘Had you said, “In the name of Allah,” the angels would have lifted you while the people watched.’ “Allah then repelled the polytheists.”"],[459,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab distributed woolen garments among some of the women of Madinah. One fine garment remained, so some of those with him said, “Commander of the Believers, give this to the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who is married to you.” They meant Umm Kulthum bint ʿAli. ʿUmar replied, “Umm Sulayt has a greater right to it.” Umm Sulayt was one of the women of the Ansar who had pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. ʿUmar said, “She used to carry the waterskins for us on the day of Uhud.”"],[460,"Anas reported: “On the day of Uhud, the people fled from the Prophet ﷺ, while Abū Talhah stood before him, shielding him with a leather shield.” He then related the hadith. It contains the following: “I saw ʿA’ishah bint Abi Bakr and Umm Sulaym with their garments raised. I could see the anklets upon their lower legs as they carried waterskins upon their backs and poured water into the mouths of the men. They would then return, refill the waterskins, and come back to pour the water into the mouths of the men.”"],[461,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “The wrath of Allah became severe against a people who did this to His Prophet”—and he pointed to his incisor tooth—“and the wrath of Allah became severe against a man whom the Messenger of Allah kills in the cause of Allah.”"],[462,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Prophet ﷺ said, “The wrath of Allah became severe against a people who shattered the helmet upon the head of their Prophet while he was calling them to Allah.”"],[463,"ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud reported: “It is as though I can see the Messenger of Allah ﷺ relating the story of a prophet whose people struck him and made him bleed. As he wiped the blood from his face, he said, ‘O Allah, forgive my people, for they do not know.’”"],[464,"Ibn ʿAbbas reported: “The wrath of Allah became severe against the person whom the Prophet ﷺ killed in the cause of Allah, and the wrath of Allah became severe against the people who bloodied the face of Allah’s Prophet ﷺ.”"],[465,"Al-Zubayr reported: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed ʿAli ibn Abi Talib to go to al-Mihras. ʿAli brought him water in his shield. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to drink from it, but he detected an odor in it and found it distasteful. He therefore used it to wash from his face the blood that had struck him, while saying, ‘The wrath of Allah became severe against the one who bloodied the face of the Messenger of Allah.’ “The person who bloodied him that day was ʿUtbah ibn Abi Waqqas.”"],[466,"Sahl ibn Saʿd was asked about the wound of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He replied: “By Allah, I know who washed the wound of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who poured the water, and what was used to treat him. “Fatimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, washed the wound, while ʿAli poured water from a shield. When Fatimah saw that the water only caused the bleeding to increase, she took a piece of matting, burned it, and pressed it against the wound, and the bleeding stopped. His incisor tooth was broken that day, his face was wounded, and the helmet upon his head was shattered.”"],[467,"Regarding the verse: “Those who responded to Allah and the Messenger after suffering injury—for those among them who did good and remained mindful of Allah there is a magnificent reward.” [Ali ʿImran: 172] ʿA’ishah said to ʿUrwah: “Son of my sister, both of your fathers were among them: al-Zubayr and Abū Bakr. After what befell the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of Uhud and the polytheists withdrew from him, he feared that they might return. He therefore asked, ‘Who will pursue them?’ Seventy men volunteered, among whom were Abū Bakr and al-Zubayr.”"],[468,"Anas reported that the incisor tooth of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was broken on the day of Uhud and his head was wounded. As he wiped the blood from himself, he said, “How can a people succeed when they have wounded the head of their Prophet and broken his incisor tooth while he is calling them to Allah?” Allah Almighty then revealed: “You have no authority whatsoever in this matter.” [Ali ʿImran: 128]"],[469,"Jabir ibn ʿAbdullah reported: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would place two of those killed at Uhud together in a single cloth. He would then ask, ‘Which of them had learned more of the Qur’an?’ When one of them was pointed out, he would place him first in the grave. “He said, ‘I shall be a witness over these people on the Day of Resurrection.’ He ordered that they be buried with their blood upon them. They were neither washed nor was the funeral prayer performed over them.”"],[470,"ʿUbayd ibn Rifaʿah al-Zuraqi reported from his father: “When the day of Uhud came and the polytheists withdrew, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ‘Stand in orderly rows so that I may praise my Lord Almighty.’ “They formed rows behind him, and he said: “ʿO Allah, all praise belongs entirely to You. O Allah, none can withhold what You have expanded, none can bring near what You have placed far away, none can distance what You have brought near, none can give what You have withheld, and none can withhold what You have given. “ʿO Allah, spread over us Your blessings, mercy, bounty, and provision. O Allah, I ask You for lasting bliss that neither changes nor disappears. O Allah, I ask You for bliss on the day of destitution and security on the day of war. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the evil of what You have given us and the evil of what You have withheld from us. “ʿO Allah, make faith beloved to us and beautify it within our hearts. Make disbelief, wickedness, and disobedience hateful to us, and make us among those who are rightly guided. “ʿO Allah, cause us to die as Muslims, keep us alive as Muslims, and join us with the righteous, neither disgraced nor subjected to trials. “‘O Allah, fight the disbelievers who turn others away from Your path and deny Your messengers, and place upon them Your punishment and torment. O Allah, fight the disbelievers who were given the Scripture, O Allah of truth.’” ʿAli said: “I also heard it from Muhammad ibn Bishr, who narrated it with a connected chain, but I will not relate it here.”"],[471,"Regarding the verse: “Those who responded to Allah and the Messenger after suffering injury—for those among them who did good and remained mindful of Allah there is a magnificent reward.” [Ali ʿImran: 172] ʿA’ishah said to ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr: “Son of my sister, both of your fathers were among them: al-Zubayr and Abū Bakr. After what befell the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of Uhud and the polytheists withdrew from him, he feared that they might return. He therefore asked, ‘Who will pursue them?’ Seventy men volunteered, among whom were Abū Bakr and al-Zubayr.”"],[472,"Before returning to Madinah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had Muʿāwiyah ibn al-Mughīrah ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ ibn Umayyah ibn ʿAbd Shams brought before him, and Abū ʿAzzah was also presented. Abū ʿAzzah had been taken prisoner at the Battle of Badr, and the Prophet ﷺ had released him as a favor. Now he began to say: “O Messenger of Allah, pardon me.” He said: “Not now. By Allah, you will not be able to go to Makkah now. If you went, you would cry out that you had deceived Muhammad a second time. Zubayr! Strike off his neck.” Sayyiduna Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, severed his head from his body.\nIbn Hishām said that Muʿāwiyah ibn al-Mughīrah was killed by Sayyiduna Zayd ibn Ḥārithah and Sayyiduna ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, may Allah be pleased with them both, after the Ḥamrāʾ al-Asad expedition."],[473,"Abū Hurayrah reported: “The Prophet ﷺ sent a reconnaissance detachment and appointed ʿAsim ibn Thabit as its commander. He was the maternal grandfather of ʿAsim ibn ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab. “They travelled until they reached a place between ʿUsfan and Makkah. News of them reached a clan of Hudhayl known as Banu Lihyan, who pursued them with nearly one hundred archers. They followed their tracks until they reached a campsite at which the Muslims had stopped. There they found date stones from provisions the Muslims had brought from Madinah. They said, ‘These are dates from Yathrib,’ and continued following their tracks until they caught up with them. “When ʿAsim and his companions realized that they had been overtaken, they sought refuge upon a raised piece of ground. The enemy surrounded them and said, ‘You have our solemn covenant and pledge that if you come down to us, we will not kill any one of you.’ “ʿAsim replied, ʿAs for me, I will not descend under the protection of a disbeliever. O Allah, inform Your Prophet about us.’ “They shot arrows at them until they killed ʿAsim and seven other men. Khubayb, Zayd, and one other man remained. The enemy gave them their solemn covenant and pledge, so they descended to them. Once they had gained control of them, they unstrung their bows and tied the three men with the bowstrings. “The third man said, ‘This is the first act of treachery.’ He refused to accompany them. They dragged him and tried to force him to go with them, but when he continued to refuse, they killed him. “They took Khubayb and Zayd away and sold them in Makkah. Banu al-Harith ibn ʿAmir ibn Nawfal purchased Khubayb, because Khubayb had killed al-Harith on the day of Badr. “Khubayb remained their prisoner until they unanimously decided to kill him. He borrowed a razor from one of the daughters of al-Harith so that he could remove his pubic hair, and she lent it to him. “She later said: ʿI became distracted from one of my young children, who crawled over to Khubayb. Khubayb placed him upon his thigh. When I saw him there, I became so terrified that Khubayb recognized my fear, for the razor was still in his hand. “ʿHe said, “Do you fear that I will kill him? I would never do that, Allah willing.” “‘I never saw a prisoner better than Khubayb. I once saw him eating from a bunch of grapes when there was no fruit in Makkah at that time, while he was bound in iron chains. It could only have been provision that Allah had granted him.’ “They took him outside the Sacred Precinct to kill him. He said, ‘Allow me to pray two units of prayer.’ When he finished, he returned to them and said, ‘Were it not that you might think I was prolonging the prayer out of fear of death, I would have prayed more.’ He was the first person to establish the practice of praying two units before being killed. “He then said, ‘O Allah, count them one by one.’ He recited: “I do not care, when I am killed as a Muslim, On which side, for Allah, my death may fall. “That is entirely for the sake of Allah; and if He wills, He will bless the severed limbs of a body torn apart.” “ʿUqbah ibn al-Harith then approached and killed him. “Quraysh sent men to retrieve a recognizable part of ʿAsim’s body, because ʿAsim had killed one of their great leaders on the day of Badr. Allah sent over his body a swarm of hornets resembling a protective canopy. They protected him from the men Quraysh had sent, and they were unable to take anything from his body.”"],[474,"The expedition of Sayyiduna Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Asad al-Makhzūmī took place toward Qaṭan. Qaṭan is a mountain in the area of Fayd, where Banū Asad ibn Khuzaymah had a spring. This expedition took place at the new moon of Muḥarram, in the thirty-fifth month after the Hijrah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. News reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Ṭulayḥah and Salamah, the sons of Khuwaylid, together with their followers, had gone to their people and were calling them to war against the Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned Abū Salamah, appointed a banner for him, and one hundred and twenty-five men from the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār set out with him. He said to them: “Go until you reach the territory of Banū Asad, and attack them before their groups can confront you.”\nThey set out and quickened their pace. They left the usual road, passed through al-Akhbār, and came near Qaṭan. They attacked together and took control of it, captured three slave shepherds, and the rest escaped.\nThe shepherds came to their group and informed them, and all the people scattered in different directions. Abū Salamah divided his companions into three groups to search for the camels and sheep. They returned safe and sound with the camels and sheep; no one was found who would resist. Abū Salamah brought them all to Madinah."],[475,"The expedition of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Unays took place against Sufyān ibn Khālid ibn Nubayḥ al-Hudhalī at ʿUranah. He set out from Madinah on Monday, the 5th of Muḥarram, in the thirty-fifth month after the Hijrah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. News reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Sufyān ibn Khālid al-Hudhalī al-Liḥyānī, who used to camp at ʿUranah and in its surroundings, had gathered some groups, together with his people and others, against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent ʿAbd Allāh ibn Unays, may Allah be pleased with him, to kill him.\nHe said: “O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, describe him to me.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “When you see him, you will be afraid of him and disturbed by him, and you will be reminded of Satan.” ʿAbd Allāh said: “I do not fear men.” He asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for permission to speak deceptively, and it was granted.\nHe said: I took my sword and set out, ascribing myself to Banū Khuzāʿah. When I reached Baṭn ʿUranah, I met him as he was walking, with people from various tribes behind him who had gathered around him. I recognized him from the description the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had given, and I became afraid. Such fear came over me that I broke into a sweat. I became certain that Allah and His Messenger are truthful. He asked me who I was, and I said: “I am a man of Khuzāʿah. I heard about your group against Muhammad ﷺ and came here so that I may be with you.” He said: “Indeed, I am preparing to confront them.”\nI walked along with him talking; he found my words pleasing, and as we talked we reached his tent. His companions parted from him, the people dispersed and went to sleep, and I killed him by deception and took his head.\nI entered a cave in the mountain, and a spider spun its web over me. They searched a great deal but found nothing, and turned back to return. I came out and walked all night and hid by day until I reached Madinah. I found the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the mosque. When he saw me, he said: “May your face prosper.” I said: “And may yours, O Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” I placed his head before him and informed him of what had happened. He was away eighteen days and came back on Saturday, the 23rd of Muḥarram.\nThe Prophet ﷺ gave me a staff and said: “Hold on to this and go into Paradise with it.” The staff remained with him. When the time of his death drew near, he instructed his family to put the staff in his shroud, and they did so."],[476,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned me and said: “I have been informed that Khālid ibn Sufyān ibn Nubayḥ al-Hudhalī is gathering people to attack me. He is at ʿUranah, so go to him and kill him.” I said, “O Messenger of Allah, describe him to me so that I may recognize him.” He said: “When you see him, you will experience a shudder.” He said: I set out with my sword strapped across me until I came upon him at ʿUranah, accompanied by women traveling in litters, while he was searching for a place for them to camp. It was then the time of the afternoon prayer. When I saw him, I experienced the shudder that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had described. I advanced toward him, fearing that an encounter between us might distract me from the prayer, so I prayed while walking toward him, indicating the bowing and prostration by motions of my head. When I reached him, he asked, “Who are you?” I replied, “A man of the Arabs who heard about you and the forces you are gathering against this man, and so has come to you for that purpose.” He said, “Yes, I am engaged in that.” I walked with him for a while. When I gained the opportunity, I attacked him with my sword until I killed him. I then departed, leaving his women bent over him. When I returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he saw me, he said: “The face has prospered.” I said, “I killed him, O Messenger of Allah.” He replied: “You have spoken the truth.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then rose with me, took me into his house, and gave me a staff, saying: “Keep this with you, O ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays.” I went out among the people carrying it, and they asked, “What is this staff?” I replied, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave it to me and instructed me to keep it.” They said, “Why do you not return to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and ask him about it?” I returned and said, “O Messenger of Allah, why did you give me this staff?” He replied: “It will be a sign between you and me on the Day of Resurrection, for few people on that day will be carrying staffs.” ʿAbdullāh therefore fastened it alongside his sword, and it remained with him. When he died, he instructed that it be placed with him inside his shroud, and the two were buried together."],[477,"Anas reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent seventy men on a mission. They were known as “the Reciters.” Two clans of Banū Sulaym—Riʿl and Dhakwān—intercepted them at a well known as Bi’r Maʿūnah. The men said, “By Allah, we did not come for you; we are merely passing through on a mission for the Prophet ﷺ.” Nevertheless, they killed them. The Prophet ﷺ supplicated against them for a month during the dawn prayer. That was the beginning of the qunūt supplication, as we had not previously performed it."],[478,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that Riʿl, Dhakwān, ʿUṣayyah, and Banū Liḥyān asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for reinforcements against an enemy. He supported them with seventy men of the Anṣār whom we called “the Reciters” in their time. They would gather firewood by day and pray by night. When they reached Bi’r Maʿūnah, those people treacherously attacked and killed them. When the news reached the Prophet ﷺ, he performed qunūt for a month, supplicating during the dawn prayer against several Arab clans: Riʿl, Dhakwān, ʿUṣayyah, and Banū Liḥyān. Anas said: We used to recite concerning them a passage of the Qur’an that was subsequently withdrawn: “Convey to our people on our behalf that we have met our Lord; He is pleased with us and has made us pleased.” Qatādah also related from Anas ibn Mālik that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ performed qunūt for a month during the dawn prayer, supplicating against several Arab clans: Riʿl, Dhakwān, ʿUṣayyah, and Banū Liḥyān."],[479,"Anas reported that the Prophet ﷺ sent his maternal uncle—the brother of Umm Sulaym—among seventy mounted men. The leader of the polytheists was ʿĀmir ibn al-Ṭufayl. He was given a choice of three options, and said: “The people of the plains will be yours and the people of the settled highlands will be mine; or I will be your successor; or I will attack you with one thousand and another thousand men of Ghaṭafān.” ʿĀmir was then afflicted by a plague-swelling in the house of a certain woman. He exclaimed: “A swelling like that of a young camel, and in the house of a woman from such-and-such a family! Bring me my horse.” He died upon the back of his horse. Ḥarām, the brother of Umm Sulaym, then went forward, together with a lame man and another man from a certain clan. He said to them, “Remain nearby while I approach them. If they grant me safety, remain where you are; but if they kill me, return to your companions.” He asked the enemy, “Will you grant me safety so that I may convey the message of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” He began speaking to them, but they signaled to a man who approached him from behind and stabbed him—Hammām said, “I think”—until the spear passed through him. Ḥarām exclaimed: “Allah is the Greatest! I have triumphed, by the Lord of the Kaʿbah!” The enemy then caught up with the men and killed all of them except the lame man, who was on the top of a mountain. Allah revealed concerning them a passage that was later withdrawn: “We have met our Lord; He is pleased with us and has made us pleased.” The Prophet ﷺ supplicated against them for thirty mornings—against Riʿl, Dhakwān, Banū Liḥyān, and ʿUṣayyah, who had disobeyed Allah and His Messenger ﷺ."],[480,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: When Ḥarām ibn Milḥān—who was his maternal uncle—was stabbed on the day of Bi’r Maʿūnah, he took some of his blood like this and splashed it over his face and head. He then exclaimed: “I have triumphed, by the Lord of the Kaʿbah!”"],[481,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: When the persecution of Abū Bakr became severe, he sought the Prophet’s ﷺ permission to depart. The Prophet ﷺ told him: “Remain.” Abū Bakr asked, “O Messenger of Allah, do you hope that permission will be granted to you?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say: “I do indeed hope for that.” She said: Abū Bakr therefore waited for him. One day at noon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to him and called out to him, saying: “Have everyone with you leave.” Abū Bakr replied, “They are only my two daughters.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Do you realize that I have been granted permission to depart?” Abū Bakr asked, “O Messenger of Allah, may I accompany you?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “You may accompany me.” Abū Bakr said, “O Messenger of Allah, I have two she-camels that I prepared for the journey.” He gave one of them—the she-camel called al-Jadʿāʾ—to the Prophet ﷺ. They mounted and departed until they reached the cave at Mount Thawr, where they concealed themselves. ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah had been an enslaved servant of ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ṭufayl ibn Sakhbarah, ʿĀ’ishah’s maternal half-brother. Abū Bakr had a milch animal, which ʿĀmir would take out in the evening and bring to them in the morning. He would travel to them during the latter part of the night, then take the animal out to pasture, so that none of the shepherds noticed him. When the Prophet ﷺ and Abū Bakr departed, ʿĀmir went with them, riding behind them in turns, until they reached Madinah. ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah was later killed on the day of Bi’r Maʿūnah. Abū Usāmah also reported that Hishām ibn ʿUrwah said: My father informed me that when the men at Bi’r Maʿūnah were killed and ʿAmr ibn Umayyah al-Ḍamrī was taken captive, ʿĀmir ibn al-Ṭufayl pointed to one of the slain men and asked him, “Who is this?” ʿAmr ibn Umayyah replied, “This is ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah.” He said, “After he was killed, I saw him raised toward the sky, until I could see the sky between him and the earth; then he was set down.” The news reached the Prophet ﷺ, and he announced their deaths, saying: “Your companions have been slain. They asked their Lord, saying: ‘Our Lord, inform our brothers on our behalf that we are pleased with You and You are pleased with us.’ So He informed them concerning their brothers.” Among those killed that day was ʿUrwah ibn Asmāʾ ibn al-Ṣalt, after whom ʿUrwah was named; and also Mundhir ibn ʿAmr, after whom al-Mundhir was named."],[482,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: The Prophet ﷺ supplicated for thirty mornings against those who had killed his Companions at Bi’r Maʿūnah. He would supplicate against Riʿl and Liḥyān, saying: “And ʿUṣayyah disobeyed Allah and His Messenger ﷺ.” Anas said: Allah Most High revealed to His Prophet ﷺ concerning those who were killed—the Companions of Bi’r Maʿūnah—a passage of the Qur’an that we recited until it was later abrogated: “Convey to our people that we have met our Lord; He is pleased with us and we are pleased with Him.”"],[483,"ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal reported: I asked Anas ibn Mālik about qunūt in prayer, and he said, “Yes.” I asked, “Was it before bowing or after it?” He replied, “Before it.” I said, “But a certain person informed me that you said it was after it.” He replied, “He has lied. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed qunūt after bowing only for one month. He had sent some men known as the Reciters, numbering seventy, to a group of polytheists. Some people who had a treaty with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ betrayed them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ therefore performed qunūt after bowing for one month, supplicating against them.”"],[484,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed qunūt for one continuous month during the noon, afternoon, sunset, night, and dawn prayers. He did so at the end of each prayer, after saying, “Allah hears the one who praises Him,” in the final unit. He supplicated against a clan of Banū Sulaym—Riʿl, Dhakwān, and ʿUṣayyah—while those behind him said, “Āmīn.” He had sent people to them to invite them to Islam, but they killed them."],[485,"Ibn Isḥāq mentioned that this event took place after Biʾr Maʿūnah and Uḥud. ʿAmr ibn Umayyah returned and killed two men of Banū ʿĀmir, although they held a covenant from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. At this the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “You have killed these two men; I will pay their blood-money.”\nIbn Isḥāq says that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to Banū al-Naḍīr to speak about the blood-money of these two slain men whom ʿAmr ibn Umayyah al-Ḍamrī had killed. Those slain men were from Banū ʿĀmir, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had informed Banū ʿĀmir, and Banū al-Naḍīr were allies of Banū ʿĀmir; for this reason he spoke to them. They said: “Yes, O Abū al-Qāsim, that is fine; we are with you as you wish.” Then the people of Banū al-Naḍīr consulted secretly. They said: “You will never get such an opportunity again.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting near a wall. They said: “Who will climb this wall and drop a stone on him (the Messenger of Allah) and relieve us of him?” ʿAmr ibn Jaḥḥāsh ibn Kaʿb took responsibility for this task and said: “I will do it.” Then he climbed up (the wall) in order to drop a stone on him, as had been said. At that time the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting with a few of his Companions, among them Abū Bakr, ʿUmar and ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with them. News of this event was given to him from heaven, and at that very moment, without saying anything to anyone, he set out toward Madinah. The Companions began to look for him. Then they saw a man coming from the direction of Madinah, and he said: “I saw the Messenger ﷺ entering Madinah.”\nHearing this, the Companions also came to Madinah. Then the Messenger ﷺ told them about the treachery and plot of Banū al-Naḍīr and ordered preparation for war against Banū al-Naḍīr. Ibn Hishām says: Then they spent six nights in siege, and at that very time the ruling on the prohibition of wine was revealed.\nIbn Isḥāq says: When they had shut themselves in their fortresses, the Prophet ﷺ ordered that their orchards be cut down and their fields set on fire. At that, Banū al-Naḍīr cried out: “O Muhammad ﷺ, you forbid corruption and you condemn the corrupter. Now what is the reason that you have our gardens cut down and burned?”\nSome of the hypocrites from Banū ʿAwf and Banū al-Khazraj, among them ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl, Widāʿah ibn Mālik ibn Abī Qawqal, Dāʿis, Suwayd and others, sent word to Banū al-Naḍīr: “If you fight the Muslims, we will take part in the fighting with you; and if you leave your homes and go elsewhere, we will go with you.” Relying on this, they waited for several days, but no help was given to them, and Allah cast fear of the Muslims into their hearts. They sent word to him ﷺ: “If you spare our lives and allow us to take with us as much of our property as camels can carry, we will leave from here.”\nHe ﷺ gave permission, and they loaded all their belongings on camels and left, even taking the doors and the door frames of their houses, and they demolished the houses with their own hands. Some of them settled in Khaybar while others went to Syria.\nAmong those who migrated to Khaybar: their great chiefs set out for Khaybar, namely Sallām ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq, Kinānah ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq and Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab. When they arrived there, the people of Khaybar accepted their authority."],[486,"Anas reported: A man would assign some palm trees from his land to the Prophet ﷺ. When the lands of Qurayẓah and al-Naḍīr were conquered for him, the Prophet ﷺ began returning to each man what he had given him. Anas said: My family instructed me to go to the Prophet ﷺ and ask him to return what they had given him, or part of it. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ had given it to Umm Ayman, but when I went to him, he gave the palm trees back to me. Umm Ayman came, placed a cloth around my neck, and said, “By Allah, we shall not give them to you after he has given them to me!” The Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: “Umm Ayman, let him go, and you shall have such-and-such.” She replied, “No, by Him besides whom there is no deity!” He continued increasing his offer until he gave her ten times their value, or nearly ten times their value."],[487,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: When the Emigrants arrived in Madinah from Makkah, they came possessing nothing, whereas the Anṣār owned land and palm groves. The Anṣār entered into an arrangement with them whereby they would give them half the annual produce of their properties and spare them the labor and expenses. The mother of Anas ibn Mālik, known as Umm Sulaym, was also the mother of ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, Anas’s maternal brother. She had given the Messenger of Allah ﷺ some of her palm trees, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave them to his freedwoman Umm Ayman, the mother of Usāmah ibn Zayd. Ibn Shihāb said: Anas ibn Mālik informed me that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had finished fighting the people of Khaybar and returned to Madinah, the Emigrants returned to the Anṣār the grants of fruit that the Anṣār had provided them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ therefore returned my mother’s palm trees to her and gave Umm Ayman others in their place from his own orchard. Ibn Shihāb said: Umm Ayman, the mother of Usāmah ibn Zayd, had been an enslaved servant of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib and was originally from Abyssinia. When Āminah gave birth to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ after his father had died, Umm Ayman cared for him until he grew up. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then emancipated her and married her to Zayd ibn Ḥārithah. She died five months after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[488,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb reported: The property of Banū al-Naḍīr was among what Allah bestowed upon His Messenger ﷺ as spoils for which the Muslims had spurred neither horses nor riding camels. It therefore belonged exclusively to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. From it, he would provide his family with their maintenance for a year, then place what remained into weapons and riding animals as equipment in the cause of Allah."],[489,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: The Prophet ﷺ fought Banū al-Naḍīr and Qurayẓah. He expelled Banū al-Naḍīr but allowed Qurayẓah to remain and showed them favor. Later, when Qurayẓah fought, he had their fighting men killed and divided their women, children, and property among the Muslims. Some of them, however, joined the Prophet ﷺ, who granted them safety, and they embraced Islam. He expelled all the Jews of Madinah: Banū Qaynuqāʿ—the clan of ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām—the Jews of Banū Ḥārithah, and all the other Jews of Madinah."],[490,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar reported: The Prophet ﷺ burned and cut down the palm trees of Banū al-Naḍīr at al-Buwayrah. Then the following was revealed: “Whatever palm trees you cut down or left standing upon their trunks was by Allah’s permission.” [Al-Ḥashr 59:5]"],[491,"Ibn ʿUmar reported that the Prophet ﷺ burned the palm trees of Banū al-Naḍīr. He said: Concerning this, Ḥassān ibn Thābit composed: How lightly the chiefs of Banū Lu’ayy took the sweeping blaze at al-Buwayrah! Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith replied: May Allah perpetuate that deed of yours, and may flames consume its surrounding lands. You will come to know which of us remains far from its harm, and which of our two lands is truly injured."],[492,"Mālik ibn Aws ibn al-Ḥadathān reported: While I was sitting with my family after the day had advanced, a messenger from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb came to me and said, “Answer the summons of the Commander of the Faithful.” I went with him and entered upon ʿUmar. He was sitting upon the woven cords of a bed with no mattress between him and it, leaning against a leather cushion. I greeted him and sat down. He said, “Mālik, some needy households from your people have come to us, and I have ordered that they be given a small allotment. Take it and distribute it among them.” I said, “Commander of the Faithful, if only you would assign that to someone else.” He replied, “Take it, man.” While I was sitting with him, his chamberlain Yarfāʾ came and asked, “Will you receive ʿUthmān, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, al-Zubayr, and Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, who are seeking permission to enter?” He replied, “Yes.” Permission was given, and they entered, greeted him, and sat down. After a short while, Yarfāʾ asked, “Will you receive ʿAlī and al-ʿAbbās?” He replied, “Yes.” He permitted them to enter, and they greeted him and sat down. Al-ʿAbbās said, “Commander of the Faithful, judge between me and this man.” They were disputing over the property of Banū al-Naḍīr that Allah had bestowed upon His Messenger ﷺ. The group—ʿUthmān and his companions—said, “Commander of the Faithful, judge between them and relieve each of the other.” ʿUmar said, “Wait! I adjure you by Allah, by whose permission heaven and earth remain standing: Do you know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ‘We prophets are not inherited from; whatever we leave behind is charity,’ referring to himself?” They replied, “He did say that.” ʿUmar turned to ʿAlī and al-ʿAbbās and said, “I adjure you both by Allah: Do you know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that?” They replied, “He did say that.” ʿUmar said: “I shall inform you concerning this matter. Allah granted His Messenger ﷺ an exclusive right regarding this fayʾ that He granted to no one else.” He then recited: “Whatever Allah bestowed upon His Messenger from them—you spurred neither horses nor riding camels for it. But Allah gives His messengers authority over whomever He wills, and Allah has power over all things.” [Al-Ḥashr 59:6] “This property belonged exclusively to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. By Allah, he neither seized it for himself to your exclusion nor kept it from you. He gave it to you and distributed it among you until only this property remained. From it, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would provide his family with their maintenance for the year, then take what remained and place it where the wealth of Allah was to be placed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ followed this practice throughout his life. I adjure you by Allah: Do you know this?” They replied, “Yes.” He then said to ʿAlī and al-ʿAbbās, “I adjure you both by Allah: Do you know this?” They replied, “Yes.” ʿUmar continued: “Allah then took His Prophet ﷺ in death, and Abū Bakr said, ‘I am the successor entrusted with the affairs of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.’ Abū Bakr took possession of it and administered it as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had done. Allah knows that in doing so he was truthful, righteous, rightly guided, and a follower of the truth. “Allah then took Abū Bakr in death, and I became Abū Bakr’s successor. I administered it during the first two years of my rule according to the practice of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr. Allah knows that in doing so I was truthful, righteous, rightly guided, and a follower of the truth. “Then you both came to speak to me. Your claim was one and your matter was the same. You, al-ʿAbbās, came asking me for your share from your nephew, while this man”—meaning ʿAlī—“came seeking his wife’s share from her father. I told you both that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘We prophets are not inherited from; whatever we leave behind is charity.’ “When I decided to entrust it to you, I said, ‘If you wish, I shall entrust it to you on the condition that you accept Allah’s covenant and pledge to administer it as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ administered it, as Abū Bakr administered it, and as I have administered it since assuming responsibility for it.’ You said, ‘Entrust it to us.’ It was upon that condition that I delivered it to you.” He then said, “I adjure you by Allah: Did I entrust it to them on that condition?” The group replied, “Yes.” He turned to ʿAlī and al-ʿAbbās and said, “I adjure you both by Allah: Did I entrust it to you on that condition?” They replied, “Yes.” He said: “Do you now seek from me a judgment other than that? By Allah, by whose permission heaven and earth remain standing, I shall give no judgment concerning it other than this. If you are unable to administer it, return it to me and I shall relieve you of its responsibility.”"],[493,"A man among the Prophet’s Companions reported that, before the Battle of Badr, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in Madinah, the disbelievers of Quraysh wrote to ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl and the idolaters among the Aws and Khazraj, saying: “You have given refuge to our man. We swear by Allah that you must fight him or expel him; otherwise, we shall march against you in our entirety, kill your fighting men, and violate your women.” When this reached ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy and the idolaters with him, they assembled to fight the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions. When the Prophet ﷺ learned of this, he met them and said: “The threat of Quraysh has profoundly affected you. They could not harm you more than you intend to harm yourselves. Do you mean to fight your own sons and brothers?” When they heard this from the Prophet ﷺ, they dispersed. News of this reached the disbelievers of Quraysh. After the Battle of Badr, they wrote to the Jews: “You possess weapons, armor, and fortresses. You must fight our man, or we shall do such-and-such to you, and nothing will stand between us and the anklets of your women.” When news of their letter reached the Prophet ﷺ, Banū al-Naḍīr resolved upon treachery. They sent word to the Prophet ﷺ: “Come out to us with thirty of your Companions, and we shall come out with thirty of our rabbis. We shall meet at a place midway between us so that they may hear from you. If they affirm your truthfulness and believe in you, we shall all believe.” The Prophet ﷺ set out with thirty of his Companions, and thirty Jewish rabbis came out to meet him. When they reached an open tract of land, some of the Jews said to the others, “How can you get to him when thirty of his Companions are with him, every one of whom would love to die before him?” They therefore sent word to him: “How can we understand one another while we are sixty men? Come out with three of your Companions, and three of our scholars will come to you. Let them listen to you; if they believe in you, we shall all believe and affirm your truthfulness.” The Prophet ﷺ set out with three of his Companions, while three Jews came out with daggers concealed beneath their garments, intending to assassinate the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. A sincere woman from Banū al-Naḍīr sent word to her brother, a Muslim man of the Anṣār, and informed him of Banū al-Naḍīr’s intended betrayal of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Her brother hurried forward until he caught up with the Prophet ﷺ. He privately informed him of their plot before the Prophet ﷺ reached them, so the Prophet ﷺ returned. The following morning, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ marched against them with military detachments and besieged them. He said: “By Allah, you will have no security with me unless you enter into a covenant with me.” They refused to give him a covenant, so he and the Muslims fought them that day. The following morning, he marched with cavalry and military detachments against Banū Qurayẓah, leaving Banū al-Naḍīr. He called upon Qurayẓah to enter into a covenant with him, and they did so. He then withdrew from them and marched against Banū al-Naḍīr with his detachments. He fought them until they agreed to depart, with permission to take whatever their camels could carry except weapons. Banū al-Naḍīr departed, carrying whatever their camels could bear of their belongings, the doors of their houses, and their timber. They demolished their own houses and carried away whatever timber suited them. Their expulsion was the first gathering and removal of people toward Syria. Banū al-Naḍīr belonged to one of the tribes of the Children of Israel and had not suffered expulsion since Allah decreed exile upon the Children of Israel. For this reason, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ expelled them. Had Allah not decreed exile for them, He would have punished them in this world as Banū Qurayẓah were punished. Allah then revealed: “Whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth glorifies Allah, and He is the Almighty, the All-Wise,” continuing until: “And Allah has power over all things.” [Al-Ḥashr 59:1–6] The palm groves of Banū al-Naḍīr belonged exclusively to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; Allah bestowed them upon him and reserved them for him. He said: “Whatever Allah bestowed upon His Messenger from them—you spurred neither horses nor riding camels for it.” [Al-Ḥashr 59:6] That is, they were acquired without battle. The narrator said: The Prophet ﷺ gave most of it to the Emigrants and divided it among them. He also gave a share to two needy men of the Anṣār but to no other Anṣārī. The remainder became the charitable endowment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that remained in the hands of the descendants of Fāṭimah."],[494,"In this chapter there is a narration from al-Zuhrī, who said regarding the words of Allah: “You did not urge on any horses or camels for it” — that is, wealth obtained without fighting (Sūrat al-Ḥashr 59:6) — that its story is that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made peace with the people of Fadak and some other villagers, whose names al-Zuhrī mentioned but the narrator did not remember. At that time he was besieging another people, and these people had sent him wealth by way of peace. About this wealth Allah said: this wealth came without fighting (and Allah gave it to His Messenger). Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī says: The property of Banū al-Naḍīr also belonged exclusively to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; the people had not conquered it by force (that is, by fighting), but it was taken through a peace settlement. So he distributed it among the Muhājirūn and gave nothing from it to the Anṣār, except two men who were in need."],[495,"Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah narrated from Ibn Shihāb: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fought Banū al-Muṣṭaliq and Banū Liḥyān in Shaʿbān of the fifth year of the Hijrah. What al-Bukhārī mentioned from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah is the fourth year; this is a scribal error. The intention was to write the fifth year, but the fourth was written. This is because in all the narrations in Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah’s Maghāzī through various routes, which al-Ḥākim, Abū Saʿīd al-Naysābūrī and al-Bayhaqī related in al-Dalāʾil, the year is the fifth year of the Hijrah. Ḥāfiẓ Ibn Ḥajar mentioned this in Fatḥ al-Bārī. And Abū Isḥāq said that it was in the sixth year of the Hijrah, but the first view is more correct.\n§ The reason for the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ fight against them (Banū al-Muṣṭaliq):\nIbn Isḥāq reported: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr, and Muhammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān told me some of the circumstances of Banū al-Muṣṭaliq. They said: News reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Banū al-Muṣṭaliq were gathering against him, and their leader was al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Ḍirār, the father of Juwayriyah bint al-Ḥārith, who later became a wife of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard about them, he set out toward them and met them at a place called al-Muraysīʿ, near Qudayd on the coastal side. The two groups faced each other and fighting took place. Allah defeated Banū al-Muṣṭaliq; those of them who were killed were killed, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed their children, women and property among the Muslims as war booty."],[496,"Ibn ʿAwn reported: I wrote to Nāfiʿ asking him about calling people to Islam before fighting them. He wrote back: “That was only during the early period of Islam. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ raided Banū al-Muṣṭaliq while they were unaware, as their livestock were being watered. He killed their fighting men and took their captives. Juwayriyyah bint al-Ḥārith was captured that day. ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, who was present with that army, related this to me.”"],[497,"Ibn Muḥayrīz reported: I entered the mosque and saw Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, so I sat with him and asked him about coitus interruptus. Abū Saʿīd said: We accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the campaign of Banū al-Muṣṭaliq and captured some Arab women. We desired women, celibacy had become difficult for us, and we wished to practice coitus interruptus. We intended to do so but said, “Shall we practice it while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is among us without first asking him?” We therefore asked him, and he replied: “There is no harm if you do not do so, for every soul destined to exist until the Day of Resurrection will inevitably come into existence.”"],[498,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to travel, he would draw lots among his wives. Whichever wife’s lot was drawn would accompany him. On one campaign he drew lots among us, and my lot was selected. I therefore accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ after the command of veiling had been revealed. I was carried inside my litter and would remain in it when it was set down. We traveled until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ completed that campaign and began the return journey. When we had drawn close to Madinah, orders were given one night to depart. I rose when the departure was announced and walked beyond the army to relieve myself. When I had finished, I returned toward my mount and touched my chest, only to discover that a necklace of black onyx from Ẓafār had broken and fallen. I went back to search for it, and the search delayed me. The men responsible for preparing my mount came and lifted my litter onto the camel I had been riding, assuming that I was inside it. Women at that time were light and had not become heavy with flesh, since they ate only small amounts of food. The men therefore did not find the litter’s lightness unusual when they raised and carried it. I was also a young girl. They sent the camel forward and departed. I found my necklace after the army had moved on. I returned to their campsite, but there was no one there to call or answer. I remained at the place where I had been, thinking that when they discovered my absence they would return for me. While I was sitting there, sleep overcame me. Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal al-Sulamī, of Dhakwān, had remained behind the army. In the morning he reached my stopping place and saw the dark figure of a sleeping person. When he saw me, he recognized me, for he had seen me before the command of veiling. I awoke when he recognized me and said, “Indeed, we belong to Allah, and to Him we shall return.” I covered my face with my outer garment. By Allah, we did not speak a single word, nor did I hear anything from him other than his words of return to Allah. He lowered himself, made his camel kneel, and placed his foot upon its foreleg. I rose and mounted it. He then led the camel on foot until we reached the army during the intense heat of midday, while they were encamped. Some people were ruined by what followed, and the one who assumed the greatest share of the slander was ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl. ʿUrwah said: I was informed that the slander would be circulated and discussed in his presence, and he would approve of it, listen to it, and encourage its spread. ʿUrwah also said: Among those involved in the slander, no one was specifically named except Ḥassān ibn Thābit, Misṭaḥ ibn Uthāthah, and Ḥamnah bint Jaḥsh, together with others whose names I do not know. They were a group, as Allah Most High stated. The man said to have borne its greatest share was ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl.\nʿUrwah said: ʿĀ’ishah disliked anyone insulting Ḥassān in her presence, and would say that he was the one who composed: My father, his father, and my honor are a shield protecting Muhammad’s honor from you. ʿĀ’ishah continued: When we reached Madinah, I fell ill for a month. The people were engrossed in circulating the slander, but I knew nothing of it. What made me uneasy during my illness was that I did not see from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ the tenderness he usually showed me when I was ill. He would enter, greet me, and merely ask, “How is that one?” He would then leave. This made me uneasy, though I still knew nothing of the evil being said.\nWhen I had begun to recover, I went out with Umm Misṭaḥ toward al-Manāṣiʿ, which was where we relieved ourselves. We would go out only from night to night. This was before privies were constructed close to our houses. We followed the custom of the early Arabs of going into open country to relieve ourselves, and we disliked having privies beside our homes. I went with Umm Misṭaḥ, who was the daughter of Abū Ruhm ibn al-Muṭṭalib ibn ʿAbd Manāf. Her mother was the daughter of Ṣakhr ibn ʿĀmir and the maternal aunt of Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq. Her son was Misṭaḥ ibn Uthāthah ibn ʿAbbād ibn al-Muṭṭalib. When Umm Misṭaḥ and I had finished and were returning toward my house, she stumbled over her cloak and exclaimed, “May Misṭaḥ perish!” I said to her, “What a terrible thing you have said! Are you insulting a man who witnessed Badr?” She replied, “My dear girl, have you not heard what he said?” I asked, “What did he say?” She then told me what the slanderers were saying, and my illness became even worse. When I returned home, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered, greeted me, and asked, “How is that one?” I said, “Will you permit me to go to my parents?” I wanted to verify the news through them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted me permission. I asked my mother, “Mother, what are the people saying?” She replied, “My daughter, take it easy. By Allah, there is hardly ever a beautiful woman beloved by her husband who has co-wives without them speaking much against her.” I exclaimed, “Glory be to Allah! Have the people truly been saying this?” I wept throughout that night until morning. My tears did not cease, nor did I taste sleep. When morning came, I continued weeping. As revelation had been delayed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib and Usāmah ibn Zayd to ask their opinion about separating from his wife. Usāmah advised the Messenger of Allah ﷺ according to what he knew of his wife’s innocence and the affection he knew the Prophet ﷺ had for her. He said, “They are your family, and we know nothing but good about them.” ʿAlī said, “Messenger of Allah, Allah has not restricted matters for you, and there are many women besides her. Ask the maidservant, and she will tell you the truth.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned Barīrah and asked: “Barīrah, have you ever seen anything about her that made you suspicious?” Barīrah replied: “By Him who sent you with the truth, I have never seen anything in her that I could fault, except that she is a young girl who sometimes falls asleep while watching her family’s dough, so a domestic animal comes and eats it.” That same day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood upon the pulpit and publicly sought support against ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy. He said: “Muslims, who will excuse me in dealing with a man whose abuse of my family has reached me? By Allah, I know nothing but good concerning my family. They have also mentioned a man about whom I know nothing but good and who never entered upon my family except in my company.” Saʿd ibn Muʿādh of Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal rose and said, “Messenger of Allah, I shall excuse you and deal with him. If he belongs to the Aws, I will strike off his head; and if he belongs to our brothers of the Khazraj, command us and we shall carry out your order.” A man of the Khazraj then stood up. He was Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah, the leader of the Khazraj, and Umm Ḥassān was his cousin from the same clan. He had previously been a righteous man, but tribal zeal overcame him. He said to Saʿd, “You have lied! By Allah, you will not kill him and cannot kill him. Had he belonged to your own clan, you would not have wanted him killed.” Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr, Saʿd ibn Muʿādh’s cousin, then rose and said to Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah, “You have lied! By Allah, we shall certainly kill him. You are behaving like a hypocrite, arguing in defense of the hypocrites.” The two tribes, the Aws and Khazraj, became inflamed until they nearly fought one another, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained standing upon the pulpit. He continued calming them until they settled down and fell silent. I wept throughout that entire day. My tears did not stop, nor did I taste sleep. My parents remained with me the next morning. I had wept for two nights and a day without my tears ceasing or tasting sleep, until I thought that my weeping would split my liver. While my parents were sitting with me and I was weeping, a woman of the Anṣār sought permission to enter. I allowed her in, and she sat and wept with me. While we were in that state, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered, greeted us, and sat down. He had not sat with me since the rumors began, and a month had passed without any revelation concerning me. After sitting down, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praised Allah and recited the testimony of faith, then said: “To proceed: ʿĀ’ishah, such-and-such has reached me concerning you. If you are innocent, Allah will declare your innocence. If you have committed a sin, seek Allah’s forgiveness and repent to Him, for when a servant acknowledges a sin and repents, Allah accepts the servant’s repentance.” When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished speaking, my tears stopped so completely that I could not feel a single drop. I said to my father, “Answer the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on my behalf.” My father replied, “By Allah, I do not know what to say to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” I said to my mother, “Answer the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning what he has said.” She replied, “By Allah, I do not know what to say to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” Being a young girl who had not memorized much of the Qur’an, I said: “By Allah, I know that you have heard this story until it has settled in your minds and you have believed it. If I tell you that I am innocent, you will not believe me; but if I confess to something—and Allah knows that I am innocent of it—you will believe me. “By Allah, I can find no comparison for my situation with you except the father of Yūsuf when he said: ‘So beautiful patience is most fitting, and Allah alone is whose help is sought against what you describe.’” [Yūsuf 12:18] I turned away and lay down upon my bed. Allah knew that I was innocent and that He would declare my innocence. Yet, by Allah, I never imagined that Allah would reveal recited revelation concerning me. In my own estimation, my affair was too insignificant for Allah to speak about me in revelation. I merely hoped that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would see a dream through which Allah would declare my innocence. By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had not left his seat, nor had anyone departed from the house, when revelation descended upon him. The severe condition that accompanied revelation overtook him, and drops of perspiration like pearls streamed from him despite the coldness of the day, because of the weight of the words being revealed. When the revelation ended, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was smiling. His first words were: “ʿĀ’ishah, Allah has declared you innocent.” My mother said, “Rise and go to him.” I replied, “By Allah, I will not rise to go to him. I shall praise none but Allah Almighty.” Allah Most High revealed: “Indeed, those who came with the slander are a group from among you.” [Al-Nūr 24:11] He revealed the ten verses declaring my innocence. Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, who used to support Misṭaḥ ibn Uthāthah because of their kinship and Misṭaḥ’s poverty, said: “By Allah, I shall never again spend anything upon Misṭaḥ after what he said about ʿĀ’ishah.” Allah then revealed: “Let not those among you who possess virtue and wealth swear not to give…” through His words: “…and Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” [Al-Nūr 24:22] Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq said: “Yes, by Allah, I certainly love that Allah should forgive me.” He resumed the support he had provided to Misṭaḥ and said: “By Allah, I shall never withdraw it from him.” ʿĀ’ishah continued: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had asked Zaynab bint Jaḥsh concerning me, saying: “What do you know or what have you seen?” She replied: “Messenger of Allah, I guard my hearing and sight from speaking without knowledge. By Allah, I know nothing but good.” ʿĀ’ishah said: Among the Prophet’s wives, she was the one who competed with me in status, but Allah protected her through piety. Her sister Ḥamnah, however, began defending her and became ruined among those who were ruined by the slander. Ibn Shihāb said: This is what reached me from the reports of those narrators. ʿUrwah then related that ʿĀ’ishah said: By Allah, the man about whom those things were said used to declare: “Glory be to Allah! By Him in whose hand is my soul, I have never uncovered the garment of any woman.” She said: He was later killed in the cause of Allah."],[499,"Umm Rūmān, the mother of ʿĀ’ishah, reported: While ʿĀ’ishah and I were sitting together, a woman of the Anṣār entered and exclaimed, “May Allah do such-and-such to so-and-so!” Umm Rūmān asked, “What has happened?” She replied, “My son is among those who have repeated the story.” She asked, “What story?” The woman then related what had been said. ʿĀ’ishah asked, “Has the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard it?” She replied, “Yes.” She asked, “Has Abū Bakr heard it?” She replied, “Yes.” ʿĀ’ishah collapsed unconscious. When she regained consciousness, she had a fever accompanied by shivering, so I placed her garments over her and covered her. The Prophet ﷺ came and asked: “What is wrong with her?” I replied, “Messenger of Allah, she has been seized by a shivering fever.” He asked: “Is it perhaps because of a story being circulated?” She replied, “Yes.” ʿĀ’ishah sat up and said: “By Allah, if I swear an oath, you will not believe me, and if I offer an explanation, you will not excuse me. My situation with you is like that of Yaʿqūb with his sons: ‘Allah alone is whose help is sought against what you describe.’” The Prophet ﷺ departed without saying anything. Allah then revealed her vindication. She said: “It came by Allah’s praise, not through the praise of anyone else, nor through yours.”"],[500,"Masrūq reported: We entered upon ʿĀ’ishah, while Ḥassān ibn Thābit was with her reciting poetry and praising her in his verses. He said: Chaste and dignified, never accused of anything suspicious; she rises hungry, having never consumed the flesh of heedless women. ʿĀ’ishah said to him, “But you were not like that.” Masrūq said: I asked her, “Why do you permit him to enter upon you when Allah Most High says: ‘The one among them who assumed the greatest share of it will suffer a tremendous punishment’?”\n[Al-Nūr 24:11] She replied: “What punishment could be more severe than blindness?” She also said: “He used to defend the Messenger of Allah ﷺ—or engage in poetic combat on his behalf.”"],[501,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reported: I began insulting Ḥassān in the presence of ʿĀ’ishah, but she said: “Do not insult him, for he used to defend the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” ʿĀ’ishah also related: Ḥassān sought the Prophet’s ﷺ permission to satirize the polytheists. The Prophet ﷺ asked: “What about my ancestry among them?” Ḥassān replied: “I shall extract you from among them as a hair is drawn out of dough.” ʿUrwah said: I insulted Ḥassān because he had been among those who spoke extensively against her."],[502,"ʿĀ’ishah, Mother of the Believers, reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed the captives of Banū al-Muṣṭaliq, Juwayriyyah bint al-Ḥārith fell to the share of Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās, or to one of his cousins. She concluded a contract with him to purchase her freedom. She was a beautiful and attractive woman whom no one could see without being captivated by her. She came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seeking his assistance in paying her emancipation contract. ʿĀ’ishah said: By Allah, as soon as I saw her at the door of my apartment, I disliked her presence, for I knew that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would notice the same beauty in her that I had noticed. She entered and said: “Messenger of Allah, I am Juwayriyyah, daughter of al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Ḍirār, the chief of his people. The hardship that has befallen me is not hidden from you. I fell to the share of Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās—or one of his cousins—and entered into an emancipation contract with him. I have come to seek your assistance in paying it.” He asked: “Would you like something better than that?” She asked, “What is it, Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “I shall pay your emancipation contract and marry you.” She said, “Yes, Messenger of Allah.” He replied: “I have done so.” News spread among the people that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had married Juwayriyyah bint al-Ḥārith. The people said, “They are now the in-laws of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ!” They therefore released the captives in their possession. ʿĀ’ishah said: Through his marriage to her, one hundred households of Banū al-Muṣṭaliq were set free. I know of no woman who brought a greater blessing to her people than she did."],[503,"ʿĀ’ishah, Mother of the Believers, reported: As for Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, Allah protected her through her religion, and she said nothing but good. Her sister Ḥamnah, however, was ruined among those who were ruined. Those who spoke about the matter included Misṭaḥ, Ḥassān ibn Thābit, and the hypocrite ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl. He was the one who encouraged and gathered the slander, and he assumed its greatest share, together with Ḥamnah."],[504,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: When the revelation vindicating me descended, the Prophet ﷺ stood upon the pulpit, mentioned the matter, and recited the Qur’an concerning it. When he descended from the pulpit, he ordered that the two men and the woman receive their prescribed punishment, and it was carried out upon them."],[505,"Al-Zuhrī reported: Al-Walīd ibn ʿAbd al-Malik asked me, “Has it reached you that ʿAlī was among those who accused ʿĀ’ishah?” I replied, “No. However, two men from your people—Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān and Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith—have informed me that ʿĀ’ishah told them that ʿAlī had remained silent and did not involve himself in the accusation concerning her.”"],[506,"It is related from Muhammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī that Ḥassān ibn Thābit, may Allah be pleased with him, had joined those who spread the propaganda of the slander (ifk) against Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal and Sayyidah ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her. Then he composed a verse in which he mocked Ṣafwān and people like him:\nNow the simple and good people have become honored, and their number has grown,\nAnd Ibn al-Furayʿah (Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal) has become the distinguished man of the city.\nOne night Sayyiduna Ḥassān, may Allah be pleased with him, met Ṣafwān on the road. He was going to his relatives, Banū Sāʿidah. Ṣafwān struck Sayyiduna Ḥassān on the head with his sword and wounded him severely. Sayyiduna Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās, may Allah be pleased with him, seized Ṣafwān, tied his hands behind his neck with a black rope, and set off with him to Banū Ḥārithah. On the way he met Sayyiduna ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rawāḥah, may Allah be pleased with him. He asked what was the matter. Sayyiduna Thābit said: “It is most astonishing that he has wounded Sayyiduna Ḥassān with a sword. By Allah! I think he has killed Sayyiduna Ḥassān.” Sayyiduna ʿAbd Allāh asked: “Does the Messenger of Allah ﷺ know of what you have done?” Sayyiduna Thābit said: “No.” Sayyiduna ʿAbd Allāh said: “Then you are being very bold in doing this. Release him. In the morning, both of you go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and explain the whole situation.” They came into his presence and told him about the incident. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Where is Ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal?” He stood up and said: “I am here, O Messenger of Allah!” He ﷺ asked: “Why did you do all this?” He said: “O Messenger of Allah! He harmed me, even composing verses of blame about me. I could not control myself because of anger. Now I am present before you. Whatever my punishment is, give it to me.” He said: “Bring Ḥassān to me.” When Sayyiduna Ḥassān came, he asked: “Ḥassān! Allah the Exalted has honored and distinguished your people through their acceptance of Islam; do you now want to spoil it? In the hurt that has reached you, choose kindness (iḥsān).” Sayyiduna Ḥassān said: “O Messenger of Allah! My matter is in your hands.” He ﷺ gave him Coptic slave women, from one of whom ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥassān was born. He also granted him a plot of land, which had belonged to Ḥaḍrat Abū Ṭalḥah, who had given it as a gift to him.\nRegarding this, Sayyidah ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, relates: Inquiry was made about Ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal, and it was found that he had been deprived of carnal desire. He kept completely apart from women. After that he was granted the rank of martyrdom. This is a mursal narration."],[507,"﴿يٰۤاَيُّهَا الَّذِيْنَ اٰمَنُوا اذْكُرُوْا نِعْمَةَ اللّٰهِ عَلَيْكُمْ اِذْ جَآءَتْكُمْ جُنُوْدٌ فَاَرْسَلْنَا عَلَيْهِمْ رِيْحًا وَّ جُنُوْدًا لَّمْ تَرَوْهَا١ؕ وَ كَانَ اللّٰهُ بِمَا تَعْمَلُوْنَ بَصِيْرًاۚ۰۰۹ اِذْ جَآءُوْكُمْ مِّنْ فَوْقِكُمْ وَ مِنْ اَسْفَلَ مِنْكُمْ وَ اِذْ زَاغَتِ الْاَبْصَارُ وَ بَلَغَتِ الْقُلُوْبُ الْحَنَاجِرَ وَ تَظُنُّوْنَ بِاللّٰهِ الظُّنُوْنَا۰۰۱۰﴾ [الأحزاب: ٩-١٠]"],[508,"Allah the Exalted says: (Sūrat al-Aḥzāb 33:9–10)\nO you who believe! Remember the favor of Allah upon you when armies came against you, and We sent upon them a wind and armies you did not see; and Allah is ever Seeing of what you do. When they came upon you from above you and from below you, and when the eyes turned away and the hearts reached the throats, and you thought of Allah various thoughts.\nThe majority of the people of knowledge have stated that this took place in Shawwāl of the fifth year of the Hijrah. This is the opinion of Ibn Isḥāq, ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, Qatādah, al-Bayhaqī and others. And those who said that it took place in the fourth year meant that it took place after four years were complete and before five years were complete."],[509,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: The Prophet ﷺ inspected him for participation on the day of Uḥud, when he was fourteen years old, but did not permit him to fight. He inspected him on the day of the Trench, when he was fifteen, and permitted him."],[510,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar reported: The first battle I witnessed was the Battle of the Trench."],[511,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: I entered upon Ḥafṣah while her braids were dripping. I said, “You see what has happened among the people, yet I have been given no part in the matter.” She replied, “Go and join them, for they are waiting for you. I fear that your remaining away from them may cause division.” She continued urging him until he went. When the people dispersed, Muʿāwiyah delivered an address and said: “Whoever wishes to speak concerning this matter should show himself to us, for we have a greater right to it than he and his father.” Ḥabīb ibn Maslamah asked Ibn ʿUmar, “Why did you not answer him?” ʿAbdullāh replied: I loosened the cloth binding my legs and was about to say, “The one who fought you and your father for the sake of Islam has a greater right to this matter than you.” But I feared uttering a statement that would divide the community, lead to bloodshed, and be interpreted as meaning something other than what I intended. I therefore remembered what Allah has prepared in Paradise. Ḥabīb said: “You were protected and preserved.” Maḥmūd related from ʿAbd al-Razzāq the wording: “her braids.”"],[512,"Ibn Isḥāq said: Then the Battle of the Trench took place in Shawwāl of the fifth year of the Hijrah. Yazīd ibn Rūmān, ʿUrwah, and—one whom I do not doubt—ʿAbd Allāh ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik, Muhammad ibn Kaʿb al-Quraẓī, al-Zuhrī, ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr and other scholars of ours told me, each relating some events that others do not. They said that the story of the Trench is as follows: A number of Jews—among them Sallām ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq al-Naḍrī, Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab al-Naḍrī, Kinānah ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq, Hawdhah ibn Qays al-Wāʾilī, and Abū ʿAmmār al-Wāʾilī, some individuals of Banū al-Naḍīr and Banū Wāʾil—went to Quraysh of Makkah and called them to war against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They said: “We will join you to finish off Muhammad ﷺ.” Quraysh said to them: “O company of Jews, you are people of the Book and you know what the dispute is between us and Muhammad. Is our religion better, or his?” They replied: “Your religion is better than his religion, and you are closer to the truth.” These are the people about whom Allah said: “Have you not seen those who were given a portion of the Book? They believe in jibt and ṭāghūt, and they say about the disbelievers: ‘These are better guided than the believers.’ Those are the ones whom Allah has cursed, and whomever Allah curses, you will never find for him any helper.” (al-Nisāʾ 4:51–52). When they said this to Quraysh, Quraysh were very pleased and prepared for war against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then those Jews went to Banū Ghaṭafān and also called them to war against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, telling them that Quraysh had also agreed to support them.\nQuraysh were led by Abū Sufyān; Ghaṭafān were led by ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn ibn Ḥudhayfah ibn Badr with Banū Fazārah, al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAwf ibn Abī Ḥārithah al-Murrī with Banū Murrah, and Misʿar ibn Rukhaylah ibn Nuwayrah ibn Ṭarīf ibn Saḥmah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hilāl ibn Khalāwah ibn Ashjaʿ ibn Rayth ibn Ghaṭafān with some of his people. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ received news of their planning, he ﷺ ordered a trench to be dug around Madinah.\nIbn Hishām said: It is said that Salmān al-Fārisī advised the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to dig the trench."],[513,"ʿĀ’ishah, Mother of the Believers, said concerning Allah’s words: “They came upon you from above and from below; your eyes grew wild, your hearts rose into your throats, and you entertained various thoughts about Allah.” [Al-Aḥzāb 33:10] “That occurred on the day of the Trench.”"],[514,"Yazīd ibn Sharīk al-Tamīmī reported: We were with Ḥudhayfah when a man said, “Had I lived in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I would have fought alongside him and distinguished myself.” Ḥudhayfah asked, “Would you really have done that?” “I saw us with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the night of the Confederates, when we were struck by a fierce wind and bitter cold. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘Will any man bring me news of the enemy, so that Allah may place him with me on the Day of Resurrection?’ We remained silent, and none of us answered him. “He repeated: ‘Will any man bring me news of the enemy, so that Allah may place him with me on the Day of Resurrection?’ We remained silent, and no one answered. He said it a third time, but we remained silent and no one answered. “He then said: ‘Rise, Ḥudhayfah, and bring us news of the enemy.’ Once he had called me by name, I had no choice but to rise. He said: ‘Go and bring me news of them, but do nothing to alarm them against me.’ “When I departed from him, it was as though I were walking inside a heated bath, until I reached them. I saw Abū Sufyān warming his back at the fire. I placed an arrow at the center of my bow and intended to shoot him, but then I remembered the Messenger of Allah’s words: ‘Do nothing to alarm them against me.’ Had I shot, I would certainly have struck him. “I returned, again feeling as though I were walking in a heated bath. When I reached the Prophet ﷺ, I informed him of the enemy’s situation. Once I had finished, I became cold, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ covered me with the extra portion of a cloak in which he had been praying. I remained asleep until morning. When morning came, he said: ‘Rise, sleepyhead!’”"],[515,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr reported: On the day of the Trench, ʿUmar ibn Abī Salamah and I were with the women inside Ḥassān’s fortified tower."],[516,"Ibn Isḥāq said: When the hardship on the people intensified, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ—as ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah and others whom I do not doubt informed me, through Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī—sent a message to ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn ibn Ḥudhayfah ibn Badr and al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAwf ibn Abī Ḥārithah al-Murrī, who were the leaders of Ghaṭafān. He promised them a third of the date harvest of Madinah on condition that they and their followers go back and not attack the Muslims.\nPeace talks took place between the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and them, until a document was written, but it was not witnessed, and no firm assurance of peace was given; there were only preliminary talks. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to do this, he called Saʿd ibn Muʿādh and Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah, may Allah be pleased with them both, spoke to them about it and consulted them. They said: “O Messenger of Allah, is this something you like and wish to do, or is it a command from Allah that we must carry out, or is it something you intend to do for us?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “It is something I am doing for you. By Allah, I am doing it only because I saw that the Arabs have shot at you with one bow and surrounded you from every side, so I want to reduce their sharpness against you somewhat.”\nSaʿd ibn Muʿādh, may Allah be pleased with him, said: “O Messenger of Allah, we and these people, when we associated partners with Allah and worshipped idols, neither worshipped Allah nor knew Him, they did not even hope to eat our dates except as guests or by buying them. Now that Allah has honored us with Islam, guided us through you, and given us honor, will you give them our wealth? By Allah, we have no need of that. By Allah, they will be given nothing but the sword, until Allah decides between us and them.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Then it is up to you.”\nThen Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, may Allah be pleased with him, took the document, erased what had been written in it, and said: “Now let them try their strength against us.”"],[517,"Abū Hurayrah reported: Al-Ḥārith al-Ghaṭafānī came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “Muhammad, give us half the dates of Madinah, or we shall fill it against you with cavalry and men.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Let me first consult the Saʿds”—meaning Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah and Saʿd ibn Muʿādh. They said: “No, by Allah! We did not accept humiliation during the pre-Islamic period, so how could we accept it now that Allah has brought Islam?” Al-Ḥārith returned and was informed of their response, so he said, “You have betrayed us, Muhammad!” Ḥassān responded: Ḥārith, whoever betrays the protection granted his neighbor is one of you—for Muhammad never betrays. If you betray, betrayal is among your customs, and baseness grows deep within the roots of al-sakhbar. The pledge of a man of Murrah, wherever you encounter it, is like shattered glass whose fracture can never be repaired. Al-Ḥārith said: “Muhammad, restrain Ḥassān’s tongue from us! If his words were mixed with the water of the sea, they would permeate it.”"],[518,"Sahl ibn Saʿd reported: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the Trench. They were digging while we carried the soil upon our backs. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, there is no true life except the life of the Hereafter, so forgive the Emigrants and the Anṣār.”"],[519,"Anas reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to the Trench and found the Emigrants and the Anṣār digging on a cold morning. They had no servants to perform that labor for them. When he saw their exhaustion and hunger, he said: “O Allah, the true life is the life of the Hereafter, so forgive the Anṣār and the Emigrants.” They answered him: We are the ones who pledged allegiance to Muhammad to strive as long as we remain alive."],[520,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: The Emigrants and the Anṣār began digging the trench around Madinah and carrying soil upon their backs while chanting: We are the ones who pledged allegiance to Muhammad to remain upon Islam for as long as we live. The Prophet ﷺ answered them: “O Allah, there is no true good except the good of the Hereafter, so bless the Anṣār and the Emigrants.” A quantity of barley filling two cupped hands would be brought and prepared for them with rancid fat. It would be placed before the men while they were hungry, though it tasted repulsive in the throat and had a foul odor."],[521,"Anas reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, there is no true life except the life of the Hereafter, so forgive the Anṣār and the Emigrants.”"],[522,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: On the day of the Trench, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say: By Allah, had Allah not guided us, we would never have found guidance, nor given charity, nor performed prayer. So send tranquility down upon us."],[523,"Umm Salamah reported: I have never forgotten what he said on the day of the Trench as he handed the workers bricks, while the hair upon his chest had become covered with dust. He was saying: “O Allah, the true good is the good of the Hereafter, so forgive the Anṣār and the Emigrants.” He then saw ʿAmmār and said: “Alas for you, son of Sumayyah! The rebellious faction will kill you.” The narrator said: I mentioned this report to Muḥammad—meaning Ibn Sīrīn—and he asked, “Did he narrate it from his mother?” I replied, “Yes. She used to associate closely with Umm Salamah and enter upon her.”"],[524,"Al-Barāʾ reported: I saw the Prophet ﷺ on the day of the Trench carrying soil until the dust covered the hair of his chest—and he was a man with abundant body hair. He was chanting the martial verses of ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah: O Allah, had it not been for You, we would never have found guidance, nor given charity, nor performed prayer. So send tranquility down upon us, and make our feet firm when we meet the enemy. The enemies have indeed transgressed against us; whenever they desire sedition, we refuse. He raised his voice while reciting it. Another narration reads: “Those people have indeed transgressed against us.”"],[525,"There is considerable disagreement among the scholars of sīrah and maghāzī about how long the digging of the trench took. Ibn Saʿd says that the Muslims completed the digging of the trench in six days. Al-Qasṭallānī mentioned this in al-Mawāhib al-Laduniyyah, 1/451–452. Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah stated that the digging of the trench took the Muslims about twenty days. According to al-Wāqidī it was completed in twenty-four days. Imam al-Nawawī gave fifteen days in Rawḍat al-Ṭālibīn. Imam Ibn al-Qayyim gave a duration of one month in Zād al-Maʿād.\nIn my view, the opinion of Ḥāfiẓ Ibn al-Qayyim is correct. The length of the trench was twelve thousand cubits. It is unlikely that a trench of such length could have been dug in less than a month."],[526,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: While the trench was being dug, I noticed that the Prophet ﷺ was suffering from severe hunger. I returned to my wife and asked, “Do you have anything? I have seen that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is suffering from intense hunger.” She brought out a leather bag containing one ṣāʿ of barley. We also had a young domesticated animal, so I slaughtered it while she ground the barley. She finished at the same time as I did, and I cut the meat and placed it in a cooking pot. When I was about to go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, my wife said: “Do not disgrace me before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and those with him by bringing more people than the food can feed.” I went to him and spoke privately, saying: “Messenger of Allah, we have slaughtered a young animal and ground a ṣāʿ of barley that we had. Come with a few people.” The Prophet ﷺ called aloud: “People of the Trench! Jābir has prepared a meal, so come along quickly!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then instructed me: “Do not remove your pot from the fire, and do not bake your dough until I arrive.” I returned home while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came leading the people. When I reached my wife, she rebuked me, saying, “What have you done!” I replied, “I did exactly what you told me: I spoke to him privately.” She brought the dough to the Prophet ﷺ. He spat lightly into it and invoked Allah’s blessing. He then went to the cooking pot, spat lightly into it, and invoked Allah’s blessing. He said: “Call another woman to help you bake. Ladle food from your pot, but do not take it off the fire.” They numbered one thousand. By Allah, they continued eating until they were satisfied and departed. Our pot continued bubbling as before, and our dough continued being baked as though none had been taken from it."],[527,"Jābir reported: While we were digging on the day of the Trench, we encountered a mass of extremely hard earth. The men came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “A hard obstruction has appeared in the trench.” He replied: “I shall go down.” He rose with a stone tied against his stomach. We had remained for three days without tasting any food. The Prophet ﷺ took the pickaxe and struck the obstruction, whereupon it became a loose mound of sand that flowed apart. I said, “Messenger of Allah, permit me to go home.” I went to my wife and said, “I have seen the Prophet ﷺ in a condition that I cannot patiently bear. Do you have anything?” She replied, “I have some barley and a young goat.” I slaughtered the goat, and she ground the barley. We then placed the meat in the cooking pot. I then went to the Prophet ﷺ after the dough had become ready and the pot, set upon its three stones, was nearly cooked. I said, “I have prepared a small meal. Come, Messenger of Allah, with one or two men.” He asked: “How much is there?” I told him, and he said: “That is abundant and good.” He instructed me: “Tell her not to remove the pot from the fire or the bread from the oven until I arrive.” He then said to the people: “Rise.” The Emigrants and the Anṣār all rose. When Jābir entered upon his wife, he said, “What shall we do! The Prophet ﷺ has come with the Emigrants, the Anṣār, and those accompanying them.” She asked, “Did he ask you how much food we had?” I replied, “Yes.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Enter without crowding one another.” He began breaking the bread, placing meat upon it, and covering the pot and oven each time he took food from them. He presented it to his Companions, then returned for more. He continued breaking bread and ladling food until everyone was satisfied, and some food still remained. He then said to Jābir’s wife: “Eat from this and give some away as gifts, for the people have been afflicted by hunger.”"],[528,"A man among the Prophet’s Companions reported: When the Prophet ﷺ ordered that the trench be dug, the workers encountered a rock that prevented them from continuing. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose, took the pickaxe, placed his cloak beside the trench, and recited: “The word of your Lord has been perfected in truth and justice. None can alter His words, and He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.” [Al-Anʿām 6:115] A third of the rock broke away. Salmān al-Fārisī was standing and watching, and a flash of light appeared with the Messenger of Allah’s blow. He struck a second time and recited: “The word of your Lord has been perfected in truth and justice. None can alter His words, and He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.” Another third of the rock broke away, and a flash appeared that Salmān saw. He struck a third time and recited the same verse. The remaining third broke away. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ climbed out, took his cloak, and sat down. Salmān said, “Messenger of Allah, whenever you struck, I saw a flash accompanying the blow.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Salmān, did you see that?” He replied, “Yes, by Him who sent you with the truth, Messenger of Allah.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “When I struck the first blow, the cities of Chosroes and their surroundings, along with many other cities, were displayed before me until I saw them with my own eyes.” The Companions present said: “Messenger of Allah, pray that Allah will grant us their conquest, give us their lands as spoils, and bring about the destruction of their power at our hands.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for that. He continued: “When I struck the second blow, the cities of Caesar and their surroundings were displayed before me until I saw them with my own eyes.” They said, “Messenger of Allah, pray that Allah will grant us their conquest, give us their lands as spoils, and bring about the destruction of their power at our hands.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for that. He continued: “When I struck the third blow, the cities of Abyssinia and the surrounding villages were displayed before me until I saw them with my own eyes.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “Leave the Abyssinians alone as long as they leave you alone, and leave the Turks alone as long as they leave you alone.”"],[529,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: The Prophet ﷺ was unable to sleep one night and said: “If only a righteous man from among my Companions would guard me tonight.” We then heard the sound of weapons. He asked: “Who is there?” The man replied, “Saʿd, Messenger of Allah. I have come to guard you.” The Prophet ﷺ then slept until we could hear him breathing deeply in his sleep."],[530,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: On the day of the Confederates, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Who will bring us news of the people?” Al-Zubayr said, “I will.” He asked a second time: “Who will bring us news of the people?” Al-Zubayr again said, “I will.” He asked a third time, and al-Zubayr again volunteered. The Prophet ﷺ then said: “Every prophet has a devoted disciple, and my devoted disciple is al-Zubayr.”"],[531,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated against the Confederates, saying: “O Allah, Revealer of the Book and Swift in reckoning, defeat the Confederates. O Allah, defeat them and shake them.”"],[532,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib reported that the Prophet ﷺ said on the day of the Trench: “May Allah fill their houses and graves with fire, just as they kept us occupied from the middle prayer until the sun set.”"],[533,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: On the day of the Trench, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb came after sunset, denouncing the disbelievers of Quraysh. He said, “Messenger of Allah, I was barely able to perform the afternoon prayer before the sun was about to set.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “By Allah, I have not yet performed it.” We went down with the Prophet ﷺ to Buṭḥān. He performed ablution for prayer, and we did likewise. He performed the afternoon prayer after sunset, then performed the sunset prayer afterward."],[534,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported: On the day of the Trench, when hardship had reached its limit for us, we asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, “Is there anything we should say?” He replied: “Say: O Allah, conceal our vulnerabilities and calm our fears.” Allah then defeated the enemy by means of the wind."],[535,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say: “There is no deity except Allah alone. He strengthened His army, granted victory to His servant, and defeated the Confederates by Himself. Nothing remains after Him.”"],[536,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar reported: Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from a military expedition, pilgrimage, or ʿumrah, he would begin by proclaiming Allah’s greatness three times and then say: “There is no deity except Allah alone, without partner. To Him belongs all sovereignty and praise, and He has power over all things. We return repentant, worshipping, prostrating, and praising our Lord. Allah fulfilled His promise, granted victory to His servant, and defeated the Confederates by Himself.”"],[537,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I was granted victory by the east wind, while ʿĀd were destroyed by the west wind.”"],[538,"Anas reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was granted victory by the east wind, while ʿĀd were destroyed by the west wind.”"],[539,"Sulaymān ibn Ṣurad reported: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say when the Confederates had been driven away from him: “Now we shall campaign against them, and they will not campaign against us. We shall march toward them.”"],[540,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: On the day of the Confederates, after they had assembled vast forces against him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “They will never campaign against you after this; rather, we shall campaign against them.”"],[541,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: Saʿd was wounded on the day of the Trench. A man of Quraysh named Ḥibbān ibn al-ʿAriqah shot him in the medial vein of his arm. The Prophet ﷺ had a tent erected for him inside the mosque so that he could visit him nearby. His wound continued discharging blood, and he eventually died from it."],[542,"The Battle of Banū Qurayẓah took place in the month of Dhū al-Ḥijjah of the fifth year of the Hijrah, after the Battle of al-Aḥzāb. Its cause was the breaking of the covenant by Banū Qurayẓah, which they did with regard to the Prophet ﷺ when Quraysh fought at Madinah. So Allah the Exalted issued to him ﷺ the command to fight them."],[543,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: When the Prophet ﷺ returned from the Trench, laid down his weapons, and bathed, Gabriel came to him and said: “Have you laid down your weapons? By Allah, we have not laid ours down! March against them.” The Prophet ﷺ asked: “Where?” Gabriel replied, “Over there,” pointing toward Banū Qurayẓah. The Prophet ﷺ therefore set out against them."],[544,"Anas reported: It is as though I can still see the dust rising through the street of Banū Ghanm from Gabriel’s procession when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ marched against Banū Qurayẓah."],[545,"Kaʿb ibn Mālik reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from pursuing the Confederates, laid down his armor, and perfumed himself with incense—as Duḥaym added in his narration—the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Gabriel descended and said: ‘What excuse can there be for laying aside battle? I see that you have removed your armor, while we have not yet laid ours down.’” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sprang up in alarm and firmly instructed the people not to perform the afternoon prayer until they reached Banū Qurayẓah. They put on their weapons and departed, but did not reach Banū Qurayẓah until after sunset. The people differed regarding the afternoon prayer. Some said: “Perform the prayer, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not intend that you should omit it.” Others said: “He firmly instructed us not to pray until we reached Banū Qurayẓah. We are carrying out the Messenger of Allah’s explicit command, so there is no sin upon us.” One group performed the afternoon prayer on time, acting in faith and seeking Allah’s reward. The other group did not pray until they reached Banū Qurayẓah after sunset, likewise acting in faith and seeking Allah’s reward. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not reprimand either group."],[546,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: On the day of the Confederates, the Prophet ﷺ said: “None of you should perform the afternoon prayer until he reaches Banū Qurayẓah.” The time of the afternoon prayer reached some of them while they were still traveling. Some said, “We shall not pray until we reach Banū Qurayẓah.” Others said, “We shall pray now; the Prophet ﷺ did not intend that we delay the prayer itself.” The matter was later mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ, and he did not reprimand either group."],[547,"Al-Barāʾ reported: The Prophet ﷺ said to Ḥassān: “Satirize them—or engage them in poetic combat—for Gabriel is with you.”"],[548,"Abū Saʿīd reported: Banū Qurayẓah agreed to submit to the judgment of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh. The Prophet ﷺ sent for Saʿd, who arrived riding a donkey. When he drew near the mosque, the Prophet ﷺ said to the Anṣār: “Rise to receive your leader—or the best among you.” He then said to Saʿd: “These people have agreed to submit to your judgment.” Saʿd said: “Their fighting men are to be killed and their women and children taken captive.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “You have judged according to Allah’s judgment.” On another occasion, the narrator said: “According to the judgment of the Sovereign.”"],[549,"ʿĀ’ishah, Mother of the Believers, reported: Saʿd was wounded on the day of the Trench. A man of Quraysh named Ḥibbān ibn al-ʿAriqah shot him in the medial vein of his arm. The Prophet ﷺ had a tent erected for him inside the mosque so that he could visit him nearby. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the Trench, he laid down his weapons and bathed. Gabriel came to him, shaking dust from his head, and said: “Have you laid down your weapons? By Allah, I have not laid mine down! March against them.” The Prophet ﷺ asked: “Where?” Gabriel pointed toward Banū Qurayẓah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to them, and they agreed to submit to his judgment. He referred the judgment to Saʿd, who said: “My judgment is that their fighting men be killed, their women and children taken captive, and their property divided.” Hishām said: My father related to me from ʿĀ’ishah that Saʿd prayed: “O Allah, You know that there are no people whom I would rather strive against for Your sake than those who rejected Your Messenger ﷺ and drove him out. O Allah, I believe that You have brought the warfare between us and them to an end. If any fighting against Quraysh remains, preserve me so that I may strive against them for Your sake. But if You have ended the war, cause my wound to burst and make my death result from it.”\nThe wound burst open near his upper chest. The people in a tent belonging to Banū Ghifār inside the mosque were suddenly startled by blood flowing toward them. They asked, “People of the tent, what is this coming toward us from your direction?” They discovered that Saʿd’s wound was pouring blood, and he died from it."],[550,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: On the day of the Trench, I went out following the people’s tracks. I heard the heavy sound of the ground behind me—that is, the sound of someone walking. I turned and saw Saʿd ibn Muʿādh accompanied by his nephew al-Ḥārith ibn Aws, who was carrying Saʿd’s shield. I sat down upon the ground. Saʿd passed wearing an iron coat of armor from which his limbs protruded, and I feared that his exposed limbs might be struck. Saʿd was among the largest and tallest of men. As he passed, he chanted: Wait but a little—Ḥamal will reach the raging battle; how beautiful death is when the appointed time has come. I rose and entered an enclosed garden. A group of Muslims was inside, including ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb and a man wearing a helmet that covered his face. ʿUmar asked: “What brought you here? By my life, you are certainly bold! What makes you feel safe from a calamity or a forced retreat?” He continued reproaching me until I wished that the ground would split open at that moment and swallow me. The man lifted the helmet from his face, and it was Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydullāh. He said: “ʿUmar, you have gone too far today! Where can anyone seek refuge or flee except to Allah Almighty?”\nA polytheist of Quraysh known as Ibn al-ʿAriqah shot an arrow at Saʿd, saying, “Take that! I am Ibn al-ʿAriqah.” It struck and severed the medial vein in Saʿd’s arm. Saʿd prayed: “O Allah, do not cause me to die until You grant me satisfaction concerning Banū Qurayẓah.” They had been his allies and confederates during the pre-Islamic period. His wound stopped bleeding. Allah Almighty sent the wind against the polytheists and spared the believers from fighting; Allah is All-Strong, Almighty. Abū Sufyān and those with him withdrew toward Tihāmah, while ʿUyaynah ibn Badr and his forces returned to Najd. Banū Qurayẓah returned and fortified themselves inside their strongholds.\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned to Madinah and laid down his weapons. He ordered that a leather tent be erected for Saʿd inside the mosque. Gabriel came with dust visible upon his front teeth and said: “Have you laid down your weapons? By Allah, the angels have not yet laid down theirs! March against Banū Qurayẓah and fight them.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ put on his armor and announced to the people that they were to depart. He set out and passed by Banū Ghanm, who lived around the mosque. He asked: “Who passed by you?” They replied, “Diḥyah al-Kalbī passed us.” Gabriel resembled Diḥyah al-Kalbī in his beard, teeth, and facial appearance.\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached Banū Qurayẓah and besieged them for twenty-five nights. When the siege and hardship became severe, they were told: “Submit to the judgment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” They consulted Abū Lubābah ibn ʿAbd al-Mundhir, who gestured toward his throat to indicate slaughter. They said, “We shall submit to the judgment of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told them: “Then submit to the judgment of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh,” and they did so.\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for Saʿd ibn Muʿādh. He was brought riding a donkey fitted with a saddle made of palm fiber, having been helped onto it. His people surrounded him and said: “Abū ʿAmr, consider your allies and confederates—people who have fought alongside you and whose position you know.” He gave them no answer and did not turn toward them. When he approached the dwellings of Banū Qurayẓah, he turned to his people and said: “The time has come for me not to care, for Allah’s sake, about the criticism of any critic.”\nAbū Saʿīd said: When Saʿd came into view, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Rise to receive your leader and help him dismount.” ʿUmar said, “Our Master is Allah Almighty.” The Prophet ﷺ repeated: “Help him dismount,” so they did. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Pass judgment concerning them.” Saʿd replied: “My judgment is that their fighting men be killed, their women and children taken captive, and their property divided.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “You have judged according to the judgment of Allah Almighty and the judgment of His Messenger.”\nSaʿd then prayed: “O Allah, if any warfare against Quraysh remains for Your Prophet ﷺ, preserve me for it. But if You have ended the war between him and them, then take me to Yourself.” His wound burst open after having healed until only a ring-like scar could be seen. He was returned to the tent that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had erected for him. ʿĀ’ishah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar were present with him. By Him in whose hand is Muhammad’s soul, I could distinguish ʿUmar’s weeping from Abū Bakr’s while I was inside my apartment. They were as Allah Almighty described them: “Compassionate among themselves.”\n[Al-Fatḥ 48:29] ʿAlqamah said: I asked, “Mother, what would the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do?” She replied: “His eyes would not shed tears over anyone, but when grief affected him, he would grasp his beard.”"],[551,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: Saʿd ibn Muʿādh was shot in the medial vein of his arm. The Prophet ﷺ cauterized it with his own hand using a broad-headed arrowhead. When the arm swelled, he cauterized it a second time."],[552,"Jābir reported: On the day of the Confederates, Saʿd ibn Muʿādh was shot and the medial vein of his arm—or another major vein—was severed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ cauterized it with fire. His arm swelled, so he left it untreated for a time, but when the bleeding resumed, he cauterized it again, and the arm swelled once more. When Saʿd saw this, he prayed: “O Allah, do not take my soul until You grant me satisfaction concerning Banū Qurayẓah.” His vein then closed and did not release another drop until they submitted to the judgment of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh. The Prophet ﷺ sent for him. Saʿd judged that their men should be killed and their women kept alive so that the Muslims could benefit from their service. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “You have reached Allah’s judgment concerning them.” They numbered four hundred. When their execution was completed, Saʿd’s vein opened again and he died."],[553,"ʿAṭiyyah al-Quraẓī reported: I was among the captives of Banū Qurayẓah. They examined the captives: anyone whose pubic hair had grown was killed, while anyone whose hair had not grown was not killed. I was among those whose hair had not grown."],[554,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: Only one of their women—meaning the women of Banū Qurayẓah—was killed. She was sitting with me, speaking and laughing heartily, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was having their men put to the sword. Then someone called out her name: “Where is so-and-so?” She replied, “Here I am.” I asked, “What is the matter with you?” She replied, “It is because of something I did.” She was taken away and beheaded. I shall never forget my astonishment at her: she was laughing heartily despite knowing that she was going to be killed."],[555,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar reported: The Prophet ﷺ fought Banū al-Naḍīr and Banū Qurayẓah. He expelled Banū al-Naḍīr but allowed Qurayẓah to remain and showed them favor. Later, when Qurayẓah fought, he had their men killed and divided their women, children, and property among the Muslims. Some of them, however, joined the Prophet ﷺ, who granted them safety, and they embraced Islam. He expelled all the Jews of Madinah: Banū Qaynuqāʿ—the clan of ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām—the Jews of Banū Ḥārithah, and all the other Jews of Madinah."],[556,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: A man would assign some palm trees from his land to the Prophet ﷺ. When the lands of Qurayẓah and al-Naḍīr were conquered for him, the Prophet ﷺ began returning to each person what he had given. Anas said: My family instructed me to go to the Prophet ﷺ and ask him to return what they had given him, or part of it. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ had given it to Umm Ayman. I went to the Prophet ﷺ, and he gave the palm trees back to me.\nUmm Ayman came, placed a cloth around my neck, and said: “No, by Allah! We shall not give them to you after he has given them to me.” The Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: “Umm Ayman, let him go, and you shall receive such-and-such.” She replied: “No, by Him besides whom there is no deity!” He continued increasing his offer until he gave her ten times their value, or nearly ten times their value."],[557,"This event took place in Muḥarram al-Ḥarām of the sixth year of the Hijrah, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may Allah be pleased with him, with a detachment of thirty horsemen to attack the tribe of Qurṭāʾ. This tribe was a branch of Banū Abī Bakr ibn Kilāb, who lived in an area called Bakarāt near Ḍarīyah. The distance from Madinah to Ḍarīyah was about seven days’ travel.\nThe Prophet ﷺ ordered Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may Allah be pleased with him, to attack them suddenly. So he traveled by night and hid by day. When the right opportunity came, they attacked Qurṭāʾ. In this attack some people were killed, while the rest fled. Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may Allah be pleased with him, and his companions seized the camels and sheep of the tribe, but did not harm the women. Then they returned to Madinah with the spoils of war.\nAfter reaching Madinah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, as usual, set aside one-fifth of the spoils and distributed the rest among the army. In this distribution one camel was counted as equal to ten sheep. In this expedition 150 camels and 3,000 sheep were obtained. Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may Allah be pleased with him, was away from Madinah for 19 days on this expedition and returned to Madinah one day before the last night of Muḥarram.\nDuring this same expedition, Thumāmah ibn Uthāl, the chief of Yamāmah, was also taken captive, as Ibn Ḥibbān mentioned in his book al-Thiqāt. Further details of the story of Thumāmah are given in the next ḥadīth."],[558,"Abū Hurayrah reported: The Prophet ﷺ sent a cavalry detachment toward Najd. They returned with a man from Banū Ḥanīfah named Thumāmah ibn Uthāl and tied him to one of the pillars of the mosque. The Prophet ﷺ came to him and asked: “What do you have to say, Thumāmah?” He replied: “I have good to say, Muhammad. If you kill me, you will kill a man whose blood will be avenged. If you show me favor, you will show favor to one who is grateful. If you desire wealth, ask for whatever amount you wish.” The Prophet ﷺ left him until the following day, then asked: “What do you have to say, Thumāmah?” He replied: “What I told you: if you show me favor, you will show favor to one who is grateful.” He left him until the next day and again asked: “What do you have to say, Thumāmah?” He replied: “I have what I already told you.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Release Thumāmah.” Thumāmah went to a grove near the mosque and bathed. He then entered the mosque and declared: “I testify that there is no deity except Allah, and I testify that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.” He continued: “Muhammad, by Allah, there was no face upon the earth more hateful to me than yours, but now your face has become the most beloved of all faces to me. By Allah, no religion was more hateful to me than your religion, but now your religion has become the most beloved religion to me. By Allah, no land was more hateful to me than your land, but now your land has become the most beloved land to me.” “Your cavalry captured me while I was intending to perform ʿumrah. What do you advise?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him glad tidings and instructed him to perform his ʿumrah. When he reached Makkah, someone asked him, “Have you abandoned your religion?” He replied: “No, but I have embraced Islam with Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. By Allah, not a single grain of wheat will come to you from al-Yamāmah until the Prophet ﷺ permits it.”"],[559,"Muhammad ibn Isḥāq says: After the battle with Banū Qurayẓah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed in Madinah for five months, Dhū al-Ḥijjah, Muḥarram, Ṣafar, Rabīʿ al-Awwal and Rabīʿ al-Thānī; then in the sixth month he set out for the battle against Banū Liḥyān. He intended to fight in retaliation for the people of al-Rajīʿ, that is, Khubayb ibn ʿAdī and his companions. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ made it appear that he intended to go to Syria, so that he could attack the enemy suddenly."],[560,"Abū ʿAyyāsh al-Zuraqī reported: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at ʿUsfān when the polytheists confronted us under the command of Khālid ibn al-Walīd. They were positioned between us and the direction of prayer. The Prophet ﷺ led us in the noon prayer. They said: “They were in a position in which we could have taken them by surprise.” They then said: “A prayer is now approaching that is dearer to them than their children and their own lives.” Gabriel descended between the noon and afternoon prayers with the verses beginning: “When you are among them and lead them in prayer…” [Al-Nisāʾ 4:102] When the afternoon prayer arrived, the Prophet ﷺ instructed them to take up their weapons. We formed two rows behind him. He bowed and we all bowed; he rose and we all rose. The Prophet ﷺ prostrated together with the row immediately behind him, while the other row remained standing on guard. When the first row had prostrated and stood, the other row prostrated in its place. The two rows then exchanged positions. The Prophet ﷺ then bowed and they all bowed; he rose and they all rose. He prostrated with the row directly behind him while the others remained standing on guard. When he sat, the others sat and prostrated. He then pronounced the salutation to all of them and completed the prayer. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed the fear prayer in this manner twice: once at ʿUsfān and once in the territory of Banū Sulaym."],[561,"Abū Hurayrah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ encamped between Ḍajnān and ʿUsfān. The polytheists said: “These people have a prayer that is dearer to them than their fathers and children—the afternoon prayer. Unite upon your plan and attack them in one concerted assault.” Gabriel came to the Prophet ﷺ and instructed him to divide his Companions into two groups. He was to pray with one group while the other stood behind them on guard, taking their precautions and carrying their weapons. The second group would then come and pray with him while the first took its precautions and weapons. In this way, each group would perform one unit with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while he would perform two units."],[562,"This expedition took place in the month of Rabīʿ al-Awwal, in the sixth year after the Hijrah of the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ sent ʿUkkāshah ibn Miḥṣan with forty men toward al-Ghamr. ʿUkkāshah set out immediately and traveled swiftly, and the people were alerted that he was there. The people fled and reached a high place in their territory. Their homes were found empty. ʿUkkāshah sent Shujāʿ ibn Wahb ahead, and he saw the tracks of livestock. They seized the livestock and also took the livestock of their tribe. They returned to Madinah with two hundred camels and came to the Prophet ﷺ, having met no resistance of any kind."],[563,"This expedition took place in the month of Rabīʿ al-Ākhir, in the sixth year after the Hijrah of the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ sent Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may Allah be pleased with him, to Dhū al-Qaṣṣah, which was located in the territory of Banū Thaʿlabah and Banū ʿAwāl. This place was twenty-four miles from Madinah. Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may Allah be pleased with him, and his ten companions arrived there at night, but the people there, who were one hundred men, surrounded them. The fighting continued all night, and then the Bedouins attacked with spears, as a result of which Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may Allah be pleased with him, was wounded and his foot could not move. The enemy stripped him of his clothes. A Muslim lifted Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may Allah be pleased with him, and brought him to Madinah. The Prophet ﷺ sent Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ, may Allah be pleased with him, with forty men to the bodies of those people, but no one was found alive; only livestock and sheep were found, which Abū ʿUbaydah, may Allah be pleased with him, brought back."],[564,"This expedition took place in the month of Rabīʿ al-Ākhir, in the sixth year after the Hijrah of the Prophet ﷺ. Banū Muḥārib, Banū Thaʿlabah and Banū Anmār together intended to attack the livestock of Madinah, which was grazing at a place seven miles from Madinah. The Prophet ﷺ sent Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ, may Allah be pleased with him, taking forty Muslims with him. They set out after the Maghrib prayer and reached Dhū al-Qaṣṣah near dawn. They attacked and drove the enemy toward the mountains, and captured one man who became Muslim, so they released him. They took the enemy’s livestock and returned to Madinah and presented it before the Prophet ﷺ, who took out the fifth and distributed the rest of the property."],[565,"This expedition took place in the month of Rabīʿ al-Ākhir, in the sixth year after the Hijrah of the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ sent Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, toward the territory of Banū Sulaym. Zayd ibn Ḥārithah and his companions reached al-Jumūm, which was four barīds (miles) from Madinah. There they met a woman of the tribe of Muzaynah, Ḥalīmah, who told them the location of livestock and captives at a place belonging to Banū Sulaym. Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, seized the livestock and captives there, among whom was Ḥalīmah’s husband. After returning to Madinah, the Prophet ﷺ freed Ḥalīmah and her husband."],[566,"This expedition took place in the month of Jumādā al-Ūlā, in the sixth year after the Hijrah of the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ heard that a caravan of Quraysh was coming from Syria, so he sent Zayd ibn Ḥārithah with seventy riders to intercept that caravan. Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, captured the caravan and took possession of the wealth in it, including a great deal of silver. Some captives were also taken, among them Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ. Abū al-ʿĀṣ asked for protection from Zaynab, the daughter of the Prophet ﷺ, and she gave him protection. While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying the Fajr prayer, she called out among the people: “I have given protection to Abū al-ʿĀṣ.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “We did not know of this, but we too grant protection to the one to whom you have granted protection.” And the Prophet ﷺ returned everything that had been taken from them."],[567,"This expedition took place in the month of Jumādā al-Ākhirah, in the sixth year after the Hijrah of the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ sent Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, toward a place thirty-six miles from Madinah. Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, and his fifteen companions reached the territory of Banū Thaʿlabah and seized the livestock there. The Bedouins fled, and Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, returned to Madinah with the livestock, which numbered twenty camels, without any resistance. They were absent for four nights, and their watchword was “Amit, amit.”"],[568,"This expedition took place in the month of Rajab, in the sixth year after the Hijrah of the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ sent Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, as commander to the territory of Banū Fazārah. Some Companions were wounded in the fighting.\nZayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, vowed that he would not wash his head clean of impurity (janābah) until he had attacked Banū Fazārah. When Zayd recovered, he attacked Banū Fazārah with an army, confronted them in Wādī al-Qurā, and defeated them. Qays ibn Musḥir al-Yaʿmurī killed Masʿadah ibn Ḥakamah ibn Mālik ibn Ḥudhayfah ibn Badr. And Umm Qirfah Fāṭimah bint Rabīʿah ibn Badr was also captured. Umm Qirfah was an old woman who had been with Mālik ibn Ḥudhayfah ibn Badr. Her daughter and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿadah were also captured. Zayd ibn Ḥārithah ordered Qays ibn Musḥir to kill Umm Qirfah, and Qays killed her very cruelly. Then they presented Umm Qirfah’s daughter and Ibn Masʿadah before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, was the one who had captured Umm Qirfah’s daughter. Umm Qirfah’s daughter held an honored position in her tribe. The Arabs used to say: “Even if you were more honorable than Umm Qirfah, you would not gain much honor.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked Salamah, may Allah be pleased with him, for Umm Qirfah’s daughter, and he gave her to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave Umm Qirfah’s daughter as a gift to his maternal cousin Ḥazn ibn Abī Wahb, from whom ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥazn was born.\nI say: Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, captured the daughter of Umm Qirfah, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave her to his maternal cousin Ḥazn ibn Abī Wahb. But the correct view is that this event took place under the leadership of Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, in the seventh year of the Hijrah, and that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ obtained in exchange for this woman the release of some Muslims who were imprisoned in Makkah."],[569,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent an expedition toward the coast, appointing Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ as its commander. They numbered three hundred men, and I was among them. We set out, but while we were partway through the journey, our provisions ran out. Abū ʿUbaydah ordered that all the army’s remaining provisions be gathered together. They amounted to two provision bags of dates. He rationed them to us little by little each day until they were exhausted, and eventually each man received only one date. I asked, “What benefit could a single date provide?” Jābir replied: “We certainly felt its loss when even that ran out.” We eventually reached the sea and found an enormous fish resembling a hill. The army ate from it for eighteen nights. Abū ʿUbaydah ordered that two of its ribs be erected, then had a camel saddled and led beneath them without touching them."],[570,"Jābir reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us to intercept a caravan of Quraysh and appointed Abū ʿUbaydah as our commander. He supplied us with one leather bag of dates, having nothing else to give us. Abū ʿUbaydah distributed them one date at a time. I asked, “What did you do with a single date?” He replied: “We sucked it as a child sucks something, then drank water afterward. It sustained us from morning until night. We would also beat the leaves from the trees with our staffs, soak them in water, and eat them.” We continued along the seacoast until something resembling an enormous sandhill appeared before us on the shore. When we approached it, we found that it was a sea creature called al-ʿAnbar. Abū ʿUbaydah initially said: “It is carrion.” He then said: “No; we are the messengers of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, traveling in Allah’s cause, and necessity has overtaken you, so eat.” We remained there for a month, although we numbered three hundred, until we regained our weight. I saw us scooping fat from its eye socket with large pitchers and cutting pieces of its flesh as large as an ox. Abū ʿUbaydah seated thirteen of our men inside its eye socket. He erected one of its ribs, then had the largest camel with us saddled and led underneath it. We took dried strips of its flesh as provisions. When we returned to Madinah, we went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and told him what had happened. He said: “It was provision that Allah brought forth for you. Do you have any of its flesh with you so that you may feed us?” We sent some of it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he ate it."],[571,"ʿAmr ibn Dīnār reported: I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent three hundred mounted men under the command of Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ to watch for a caravan of Quraysh. We remained on the coast for half a month and suffered such severe hunger that we ate leaves beaten from the trees. The expedition therefore became known as the Army of al-Khabaṭ. The sea cast out for us a creature called al-ʿAnbar. We ate from it for half a month and rubbed its fat upon our bodies until our physical strength and fullness returned. Abū ʿUbaydah took one of its ribs and erected it. He then selected the tallest man with him—Sufyān once narrated: “He erected a bone from its body”—and had the man ride a camel beneath it. Jābir said: One of the men slaughtered three camels for the army, then another three, and then another three. Abū ʿUbaydah subsequently prohibited him from slaughtering more. ʿAmr would also relate: Abū Ṣāliḥ informed us that Qays ibn Saʿd told his father: “I was with the army when they became hungry.” His father said, “Slaughter a camel.” Qays said, “I did.” He said, “Then they became hungry again.” His father said, “Slaughter another.” He replied, “I did.” He said, “Then they became hungry again.” His father said, “Slaughter another.” He replied, “I did.” He said, “Then they became hungry once more.” His father said, “Slaughter another.” Qays replied, “I was prohibited from doing so.”"],[572,"This event took place in Shaʿbān of the sixth year of the Hijrah. When he ﷺ was about to send him off, he called ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, made him sit in front of him, tied the turban on him himself, and gave instructions: “Fight in the name of Allah; fight whoever disbelieves in Allah; do not commit treachery, do not break covenants, and do not kill children.” Sending him to Dūmat al-Jandal, to Banū Kalb, he said: “If they accept what you say, marry the daughter of their chief.” When he arrived there, he stayed three days and invited them to Islam. Aṣbagh ibn ʿAmr al-Kalbī, who was the chief and had previously been a Christian, became Muslim, and many other people of the tribe also became Muslim, and some agreed to pay the jizyah. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf married Aṣbagh’s daughter Tumāḍir and brought her to Madinah, and Abū Salamah was born from her. (A brief account.)"],[573,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar reported in a lengthy hadith that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf to prepare for an expedition over which he had appointed him commander. He came in the morning wearing a turban made of black cotton cloth. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ drew him close, unwrapped the turban, then wrapped it around his head himself, allowing approximately four finger-widths of it to hang down behind him. He said: “This is how you should wear your turban, Ibn ʿAwf, for it is more distinctive and more becoming.” He then instructed Bilāl to give him the banner. Bilāl handed it to him, and the Prophet ﷺ praised Allah and invoked blessings upon His Prophet. He then said: “Take it, Ibn ʿAwf. Campaign together in Allah’s cause and fight those who disbelieve in Allah. Do not steal from the spoils, betray anyone, mutilate the dead, or kill a child. This is Allah’s covenant and the way of your Prophet among you.”"],[574,"This event took place in Shaʿbān of the sixth year of the Hijrah, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ received news that a group of Banū Saʿd was preparing to help the Jews of Khaybar. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, toward them with one hundred men. ʿAlī and his companions traveled by night and hid by day until they reached a place called Hamj, a watering place between Khaybar and Fadak. Between Fadak and Madinah was a journey of six days. There they met a man, whom they asked about Banū Saʿd. The man said: “If you give me protection, I will give you information about them.” ʿAlī and his companions gave him protection, and he guided them toward Banū Saʿd. They attacked Banū Saʿd and seized five hundred camels and one thousand sheep. The people of Banū Saʿd, whose chief was Wabar ibn ʿUlaym, fled. Sayyiduna ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, set aside a special she-camel for the Prophet ﷺ, named al-Ḥafdah, then set aside the fifth (khums) of the spoils and distributed the remaining spoils among his companions. After that they returned to Madinah, and they suffered no loss."],[575,"This event took place in Shawwāl, 6 AH. After the killing of Abū Rāfiʿ Sallām ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq, the Jews appointed Usayr ibn Zārim as their leader. Usayr began gathering people from Ghaṭafān and other tribes to fight against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When this news reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he sent ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah, may Allah be pleased with him, with three men to gather intelligence. ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah, may Allah be pleased with him, learned about their plan and informed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned the people about this danger, and thirty men volunteered to take part in the mission. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah, may Allah be pleased with him, as their commander. They came to Usayr ibn Zārim and said to him: “We are safe with you as long as you hear what we have to say.” Usayr also gave them a guarantee of safety. They said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has sent us to you so that you may come to him, and he will make you the ruler of Khaybar and treat you well.” Usayr trusted this and set out with them. There were thirty Jews with Usayr, and each Muslim had a Jewish man riding with him. When they reached a place called Qarqarat Thubār, Usayr became suspicious and looked toward ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays\nʿAbdullāh ibn Unays reached for his sword and struck Usayr, cutting off his thigh and most of his leg, and he fell from his camel. Usayr struck ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays on the head with a stick of thorny wood and wounded him. The rest of the Muslims attacked Usayr’s companions and killed them all except one man who escaped. None of the Muslims was wounded. They returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and told him the whole story. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Allah has saved you from the wrongdoing people.”\nThe name of Usayr is given differently in different narrations: Usayr ibn Zārim, Ibn Rāzim, Ibn Raqrām, and al-Yusayr ibn Rizām."],[576,"This event took place in Shawwāl, 6 AH. It is said that eight men of the tribe of ʿUraynah came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Madinah, accepted Islam and stayed there. But the climate of Madinah did not suit them, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent them to his milch camels that were grazing at Dhū al-Jadr, near Qubāʾ, close to ʿAyr, six miles from Madinah. They stayed there until they recovered their health and put on weight. Then, in the morning, they stole those camels. When Yasār, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and a few men with him pursued them, the men of ʿUraynah cut off Yasār’s hands and feet and thrust thorns into his tongue and eyes, so that Yasār died. When this news reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he sent twenty horsemen after them and appointed Kurz ibn Jābir al-Fihrī as their commander. They captured those criminals, bound them, placed them on horses and brought them to Madinah. At that time the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was at al-Ghābah, so they took them to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered that their hands and feet be cut off, that their eyes be branded with hot iron, and that they be hung on crosses there. Then Allah revealed this verse: ﴿اِنَّمَا جَزٰٓؤُا الَّذِيْنَ يُحَارِبُوْنَ اللّٰهَ وَ رَسُوْلَهٗ وَ يَسْعَوْنَ فِي الْاَرْضِ فَسَادًا﴾ [al-Māʾidah: 33] “The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger and strive to spread corruption in the land...” After that, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ never again branded anyone’s eyes. The number of camels was fifteen, and they were brought back to Madinah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked about one of them, a she-camel named al-Ḥināʾ, which was missing. He was told that it had been slaughtered."],[577,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: Some men from ʿUkl and ʿUraynah came to the Prophet ﷺ in Madinah and professed Islam. They said: “Prophet of Allah, we are people accustomed to livestock, not agricultural settlements.” They found the climate of Madinah unhealthy. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ provided them with a herd of camels and a shepherd, instructing them to go out with the herd and drink from its milk and urine. They departed, but when they reached the region of al-Ḥarrah, they renounced Islam, killed the Prophet’s shepherd, and drove away the camels. When the Prophet ﷺ learned of this, he sent men in pursuit. After they were captured, he ordered that their eyes be seared and their hands cut off. They were left in the region of al-Ḥarrah until they died in that state."],[578,"This event took place in Shawwāl, 6 AH. The reason was that Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb said to some men of Quraysh: “Is there anyone who will kill Muhammad (peace be upon him) by stealth? He walks about in the markets.” A Bedouin man came to him and said: “I have found a man with the strongest heart, the strongest hands, and the swiftest pace. If you help me, I will go and kill him by stealth. I have a dagger like the talon of an eagle; I will strike him with it, then join some caravan and be the first to flee. I know the roads well and am an excellent guide.” Abū Sufyān said: “You are our man.” So he gave him a camel and some provisions and said: “Keep your matter secret.” The man set out at night, traveled on his mount for five days, and on the sixth day, in the morning, reached the edge of the Ḥarrah of Madinah. Then he began asking about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until he was told where he was. He tied up his mount and made his way toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who was at that time in the mosque of Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal. When the Prophet ﷺ saw him, he said: “This man intends treachery.” When he moved forward to attack him ﷺ, Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr seized him from inside his cloak and pulled him, and the dagger appeared. Seeing this, the man was left bewildered and distressed. The man said: “My blood! My blood!” Sayyidunā Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr took hold of the back of his neck and gripped him firmly. Then the Prophet ﷺ said: “Tell me the truth, who are you?” He asked: “Do I have safety?” He ﷺ said: “Yes, you have safety.” Then the man told about his plan and the reward promised by Abū Sufyān. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ let him go, and he became Muslim. Afterwards the Prophet ﷺ sent ʿAmr ibn Umayyah al-Ḍamrī and Salamah ibn Aslam against Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb and said: “If you find any opportunity to overpower him, kill him.”\nThe two reached Makkah. ʿAmr ibn Umayyah began circumambulating the Kaʿbah at night, but Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān saw him and recognized him. Muʿāwiyah informed Quraysh of his presence, so Quraysh felt alarmed and went out searching for him.\nʿAmr ibn Umayyah had been a dangerous fighter in the Jāhiliyyah, so Quraysh said: “ʿAmr has not come for any good!” So the people of Makkah gathered against him. ʿAmr and Salamah fled from there. On the way ʿAmr met ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mālik ibn ʿUbaydullāh al-Taymī and killed him. Then he killed a man of Banū Dīl who was chanting this verse:\nAs long as I live, I will not become Muslim, and I will not accept the religion of the Muslims\nIn the meantime, he met two messengers of Quraysh whom Quraysh had sent to gather news. He killed one of them and took the other captive, then returned with him to Madinah. On reaching Madinah, ʿAmr began telling the Prophet ﷺ the details of his journey, and the Prophet ﷺ was smiling as he listened to him."],[579,"Allah the Exalted said: “Indeed, Allah was pleased with the believers when they were pledging allegiance to you under the tree.” (al-Fatḥ 18)\nThe scholars of biography and military expeditions agree that this took place in Dhū al-Qaʿdah, 6 AH."],[580,"Allah the Exalted said: “Indeed, Allah has fulfilled for His Messenger the vision in truth: you will surely enter al-Masjid al-Ḥarām, if Allah wills, in safety, with your heads shaved and hair cut short, not fearing. So He knew what you did not know, and He placed before that a near conquest.” (al-Fatḥ 27)"],[581,"Anas reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed four ʿumrahs, all of them in Dhū al-Qaʿdah except the one performed together with his pilgrimage: the ʿumrah of al-Ḥudaybiyyah in Dhū al-Qaʿdah; the ʿumrah of the following year in Dhū al-Qaʿdah; the ʿumrah from al-Jiʿrānah, where he distributed the spoils of Ḥunayn, in Dhū al-Qaʿdah; and the ʿumrah performed with his pilgrimage."],[582,"Marwān and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah reported: In the year of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, the Prophet ﷺ set out with between thirteen and fifteen hundred of his Companions. When he reached Dhū al-Ḥulayfah, he placed identifying garlands upon the sacrificial animals, marked them, and entered the state of iḥrām from there. The narrator said: I cannot count how many times I heard it from Sufyān until I heard him say: “I do not reliably remember the words concerning marking and garlanding the sacrificial animals from al-Zuhrī.” The narrator was unsure whether Sufyān meant only the passage concerning marking and garlanding or the entire hadith."],[583,"Nāfiʿ reported: During the period of civil strife, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar set out intending to perform ʿumrah. He said: “If I am prevented from reaching the Sacred House, we shall do as we did with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” He therefore entered iḥrām for ʿumrah because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had entered iḥrām for ʿumrah in the year of al-Ḥudaybiyyah."],[584,"Nāfiʿ reported that ʿUbayd Allah ibn ʿAbd Allah and Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allah told him that they spoke to ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿUmar and said, “If only you would remain here this year, for we fear that you may not reach the Sacred House.” He replied, “We set out with the Prophet ﷺ, but the disbelievers of Quraysh prevented him from reaching the House. The Prophet ﷺ therefore slaughtered his sacrificial animals and shaved his head, while his Companions shortened their hair.” He then said, “I call you to witness that I have entered into ʿumrah. If I am allowed to reach the House, I shall circumambulate it; but if I am prevented from reaching it, I shall do as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did.” After travelling for a while, he said, “I consider the matter of the two rites to be the same. I call you to witness that I have entered into ḥajj together with my ʿumrah.” He performed one circumambulation and one saʿy, and did not leave the state of consecration until he had completed both rites."],[585,"Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever ascends the mountain pass—the Pass of al-Marār—will have his sins removed as they were removed from the Children of Israel.” He said: “The first to ascend it were our horsemen, the horsemen of Banū al-Khazraj, and then the people followed one after another.”"],[586,"Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever climbs the Pass of al-Marār—or al-Marār…” He then related a report similar to the narration of Muʿādh, except that he said: “There was a Bedouin who had come looking for a lost animal.”"],[587,"Abū Saʿīd reported: We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When we reached ʿUsfān, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to us: “The scouts of Quraysh are now at Ḍajnān. Which of you knows the road of Dhāt al-Ḥanẓal?” When evening came, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Is there a man who will dismount and go ahead of the mounted party?” A man said, “I will, Messenger of Allah.” He dismounted, but the stones kept striking him and the branches caught his clothing, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Mount again.” Another man then dismounted, but the stones struck him and the branches caught his clothing, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him: “Mount again.” We eventually found the road and travelled through a pass called al-Ḥanẓal. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The likeness of this pass is that of the gate through which the Children of Israel entered. They were told: ‘Enter the gate prostrating and say, “Relieve us of our burdens,” and We shall forgive you your sins’ [al-Baqarah 2:58]. No one will cross this pass tonight without being forgiven.” The people began hurrying across it. The last to cross was Qatādah ibn al-Nuʿmān, who was at the rear of the company. The people crowded upon one another until we had all caught up. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then dismounted, and we dismounted."],[588,"Zayd ibn Khālid reported: We accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the year of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, and rain fell upon us one night. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in the dawn prayer and then turned toward us and said: “Do you know what your Lord has said?” We replied, “Allah and His Messenger know best.” He said: “Allah said: ‘This morning some of My servants became believers in Me and others became disbelievers in Me. Whoever said, “We were given rain through Allah’s mercy, provision and grace,” is a believer in Me and a disbeliever in the stars. But whoever said, “We were given rain because of such-and-such a star,” is a believer in the stars and a disbeliever in Me.’”"],[589,"Al-Barāʾ said: You consider the conquest to be the conquest of Makkah. The conquest of Makkah was indeed a conquest, but we considered the Pledge of al-Riḍwān on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah to be the conquest. We were fourteen hundred men with the Prophet ﷺ. Al-Ḥudaybiyyah had a well, and we drew out all its water until not a single drop remained. When this reached the Prophet ﷺ, he came to the well and sat at its edge. He called for a vessel of water, performed ablution, rinsed his mouth, supplicated, and then poured the water into the well. We left it for a short while, after which it supplied all the water that we and our riding animals required."],[590,"Mirdās al-Aslamī reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “The righteous will depart, one after another, and there will remain only dregs like the husks of barley or the refuse of dates. Allah will give them no consideration.”"],[591,"Zayd ibn Aslam reported from his father: I went with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, to the marketplace. A young woman caught up with him and said, “Commander of the Faithful, my husband has died and left behind small children. By Allah, they cannot even cook a sheep’s trotter, and they possess neither crops nor milk-producing animals. I fear that a hyena may devour them. I am the daughter of Khafāf ibn Īmāʾ al-Ghifārī, and my father witnessed al-Ḥudaybiyyah with the Prophet ﷺ.” ʿUmar stopped and went no farther. He said, “Welcome to one of close lineage.” He then went to a strong pack camel that was tethered in the house, loaded it with two sacks filled with food, and placed money and clothing between them. He handed her its halter and said, “Lead it away. This provision will not be exhausted before Allah brings you relief.” A man said, “Commander of the Faithful, you have given her a great deal.” ʿUmar replied, “May your mother lose you! By Allah, I can still picture this woman’s father and brother besieging a fortress for a long time until they conquered it, and afterward we benefited from the shares they had earned in it.”"],[592,"Ṭāriq ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: I set out to perform the pilgrimage and passed by some people who were praying. I asked, “What is this place of prayer?” They replied, “This is the tree beneath which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ received the Pledge of al-Riḍwān.” I went to Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib and told him about it. Saʿīd said: “My father told me that he was among those who pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ beneath the tree. When we returned the following year, however, we had forgotten its location and could not identify it.” Saʿīd then remarked: “The Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ did not know where it was, but you know it—you must therefore know better!”"],[593,"ʿAbd Allah ibn Abī Awfā, who was one of the Companions of the Tree, reported: Whenever people brought their charitable contributions to the Prophet ﷺ, he would say: “O Allah, bestow Your blessings upon them.” When my father brought him his charitable contribution, he said: “O Allah, bestow Your blessings upon the family of Abū Awfā.”"],[594,"Al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Musayyib reported from his father: I met al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, may Allah be pleased with him, and said, “Blessed are you! You accompanied the Prophet ﷺ and pledged allegiance to him beneath the tree.” He replied, “My nephew, you do not know what we introduced after him.”"],[595,"Abū Qilābah reported that Thābit ibn al-Ḍaḥḥāk told him that he had pledged allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ beneath the tree. The remainder of the report follows."],[596,"Zāhir al-Aslamī, who was among those who witnessed the Pledge beneath the tree, said: I was tending the fire beneath a cooking pot containing donkey meat when the herald of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbids you to eat donkey meat.”"],[597,"Suwayd ibn al-Nuʿmān, who was one of the Companions of the Tree, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions were brought sawīq—a preparation of roasted grain—and they chewed and ate it."],[598,"Abū Jamrah said: I asked ʿĀʾidh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him, who was one of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ and one of the Companions of the Tree, “Should the witr prayer be repeated?” He replied, “If you have performed witr at the beginning of the night, do not perform another witr at its end.”"],[599,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reported from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam, each adding details to the other’s account, that they said: The Prophet ﷺ set out during the year of al-Ḥudaybiyyah with more than fourteen hundred of his Companions. When he reached Dhū al-Ḥulayfah, he garlanded and marked the sacrificial animals and entered into the state of consecration for ʿumrah. He also sent a scout from the tribe of Khuzāʿah. The Prophet ﷺ continued until he reached the pond of al-Ashṭāṭ, where his scout came to him and said, “Quraysh have assembled their forces against you and gathered the allied tribes. They intend to fight you, turn you away from the Sacred House and prevent you from reaching it.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Advise me, people. Do you think that I should turn against the families and children of those who intend to prevent us from reaching the House? If they come to confront us, Allah Almighty will have cut off a force from among the polytheists; and if they do not come, we shall leave them stripped and ruined.” Abū Bakr said, “Messenger of Allah, you set out intending to reach this House. You do not desire to kill or fight anyone. Proceed toward it, and we shall fight whoever prevents us from reaching it.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Proceed in the name of Allah.”"],[600,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported that on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah the Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not light a fire at night.” Later, he said: “Light your fires and prepare what you need, for no people who come after you will equal even your ṣāʿ or your mudd.”"],[601,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ reported: We arrived at al-Ḥudaybiyyah with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we numbered fourteen hundred. Its well contained so little water that it could not provide enough for fifty sheep. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat at the edge of the well and either supplicated or spat into it. The water then surged forth, so we drank and drew as much as we needed."],[602,"Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwān reported: The people complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of thirst. He took an arrow from his quiver and instructed them to place it in the well. By Allah, it continued surging with abundant water for them until they had all finished and departed."],[603,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib reported that they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, numbering fourteen hundred or more. They stopped beside a well and drew out all its water. They went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who came to the well, sat at its edge and said: “Bring me a bucket of its water.” It was brought to him, and he spat into it and supplicated. He then said: “Leave it for a while.” Thereafter, they fully quenched themselves and their riding animals before departing."],[604,"Al-Barāʾ reported: We were fourteen hundred men with the Prophet ﷺ. Al-Ḥudaybiyyah had a well, and we drew out all its water until we left not a single drop in it. When this reached the Prophet ﷺ, he came to the well and sat at its edge. He called for a vessel of water, performed ablution, rinsed his mouth and supplicated, and then poured the water into it. We left it for a short while, after which it supplied all the water that we and our riding animals required."],[605,"Jābir reported: The people became thirsty on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a small leather vessel of water before him, from which he performed ablution. The people then came toward him, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “What is the matter with you?” They replied, “Messenger of Allah, we have no water with which to perform ablution or drink except what is in your vessel.” The Prophet ﷺ placed his hand in the vessel, and water began gushing from between his fingers like springs. We drank and performed ablution. I asked Jābir, “How many were you that day?” He replied, “Had we been one hundred thousand, it would have sufficed us. We were fifteen hundred.”"],[606,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ reported: The people’s provisions became scarce and they fell into poverty, so they came to the Prophet ﷺ seeking permission to slaughter their camels, and he permitted them to do so. ʿUmar met them and they informed him, whereupon he said, “How will you survive after your camels are gone?” He went to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “Messenger of Allah, how will they survive after their camels are gone?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Call upon the people to bring whatever remains of their provisions.” A leather sheet was spread out, and the provisions were placed upon it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood, supplicated and invoked blessings over the food. He then called upon the people to bring their containers, and they took handfuls until they had all finished. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “I testify that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, and that I am the Messenger of Allah.”"],[607,"Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to us on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah: “You are the best people on earth.” We numbered fourteen hundred. Had I still possessed my sight today, I would have shown you the location of the tree."],[608,"Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allah reported: Umm Mubashshir informed me that she heard the Prophet ﷺ say in the presence of Ḥafṣah: “Allah willing, none of the Companions of the Tree—those who pledged allegiance beneath it—will enter the Fire.” Ḥafṣah said, “Indeed they will, Messenger of Allah.” He rebuked her, so she quoted: “There is none of you who will not pass over it” [Maryam 19:71]. The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Allah Almighty also said: ‘Then We shall save those who were mindful of Allah and leave the wrongdoers there upon their knees’ [Maryam 19:72].”"],[609,"Maʿqil ibn Yasār reported: I remember myself on the day of the Tree, while the Prophet ﷺ was receiving the people’s pledges and I was holding one of its branches above his head. We numbered fourteen hundred. He said: “We did not pledge to die; rather, we pledged that we would not flee.”"],[610,"Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd reported: I asked Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ, “For what did you pledge allegiance to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah?” He replied, “To face death.”"],[611,"ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm reported: On the day of al-Ḥarrah, when the people were pledging allegiance to ʿAbd Allah ibn Ḥanẓalah, Ibn Zayd asked, “For what is Ibn Ḥanẓalah receiving the people’s pledge?” He was told, “To face death.” He replied, “I shall never give such a pledge to anyone after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” He had witnessed al-Ḥudaybiyyah with him."],[612,"Nāfiʿ reported: People say that Ibn ʿUmar embraced Islam before ʿUmar, but that is not so. On the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, ʿUmar sent ʿAbd Allah to retrieve a horse of his that was being kept by a man of the Anṣār, so that he could ride it in battle. Meanwhile, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was receiving the people’s pledges beneath the tree, though ʿUmar was unaware of it. ʿAbd Allah pledged allegiance to him and then went to retrieve the horse. He brought it to ʿUmar, who was putting on his armour for battle, and informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was receiving pledges beneath the tree. ʿUmar immediately set out with him and pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. This is the incident that people misconstrued when they said that Ibn ʿUmar embraced Islam before ʿUmar."],[613,"Ibn ʿUmar reported that the people were with the Prophet ﷺ on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah and had dispersed beneath the shade of the trees. They then gathered closely around the Prophet ﷺ. ʿUmar said, “ʿAbd Allah, go and see what has happened to the people, for they have gathered around the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” He found them pledging allegiance, so he gave his pledge and then returned to ʿUmar. ʿUmar subsequently went out and pledged allegiance as well."],[614,"ʿUthmān ibn Mawhab reported: A man came to perform pilgrimage at the Sacred House and saw a group of people sitting together. He asked, “Who are these people?” They replied, “They are Quraysh.” He asked, “Who is the elder among them?” They replied, “Ibn ʿUmar.” He approached him and said, “I wish to ask you about something. Will you answer me?” He continued, “I adjure you by the sanctity of this House: do you know that ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān fled on the day of Uḥud?” Ibn ʿUmar replied, “Yes.” He asked, “Do you know that he was absent from Badr and did not witness it?” He replied, “Yes.” He asked, “Do you know that he was absent from the Pledge of al-Riḍwān and did not witness it?” He replied, “Yes.” The man exclaimed, “Allah is the Greatest!” Ibn ʿUmar said: “Come here, and I shall explain the matters about which you asked me. As for his flight on the day of Uḥud, I testify that Allah pardoned him. As for his absence from Badr, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was his wife, and she was ill. The Prophet ﷺ therefore told him: ‘You shall receive the reward and the share of a man who witnessed Badr.’ “As for his absence from the Pledge of al-Riḍwān, had there been anyone in Makkah more respected and influential than ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, the Prophet ﷺ would have sent that person in his place. Instead, he sent ʿUthmān. The Pledge of al-Riḍwān took place after ʿUthmān had departed for Makkah. The Prophet ﷺ indicated his right hand and said: ‘This is ʿUthmān’s hand.’ He then struck it against his other hand and said: ‘This pledge is for ʿUthmān.’ Now take this explanation with you.”"],[615,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ reported: We arrived at al-Ḥudaybiyyah with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, numbering fourteen hundred. Its well contained so little water that it could not provide enough for fifty sheep. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat at the edge of the well and either supplicated or spat into it. The water then surged forth, so we drank and drew what we needed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then summoned us to pledge allegiance beneath the tree. I was the first person to pledge to him. He continued receiving people’s pledges until he reached the middle of the company, when he said: “Pledge to me, Salamah!” I said, “I pledged to you, Messenger of Allah, among the first people.” He said: “And again.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw that I was unarmed, so he gave me a leather shield. He continued receiving pledges until he reached the last of the people, when he said: “Will you not pledge to me, Salamah?” I replied, “I pledged to you, Messenger of Allah, among the first people and again in the middle.” He said: “And again.” I therefore pledged to him a third time. He then asked me: “Salamah, where is the shield I gave you?” I replied, “Messenger of Allah, my uncle ʿĀmir met me while he was unarmed, so I gave it to him.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed and said: “You are like the man of old who said: ‘O Allah, grant me a beloved companion who is dearer to me than my own self.’”"],[616,"Abū al-Zubayr reported that he heard Jābir being asked, “How many were you on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah?” He replied: “We were fourteen hundred. We pledged allegiance to him beneath the tree while ʿUmar held his hand. It was an acacia tree. All of us pledged except al-Jadd ibn Qays al-Anṣārī, who hid beneath his camel.”"],[617,"Ṭāriq ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: I set out to perform pilgrimage and passed by people who were praying. I asked, “What is this place of prayer?” They replied, “This is the tree beneath which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ received the Pledge of al-Riḍwān.” I went to Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib and informed him. Saʿīd said: “My father told me that he was among those who pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ beneath the tree. When we returned the following year, however, we had forgotten its location and could not identify it.” Saʿīd then remarked: “The Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ did not know where it was, but you know it—you must therefore know better!”"],[618,"Ṭāriq reported: The tree was mentioned in the presence of Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, and he laughed. He then said: “My father, who had witnessed the pledge beneath it, informed me…”"],[619,"Nāfiʿ reported: People used to visit the tree they called the Tree of al-Riḍwān and pray beside it. When this reached ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, he threatened them with punishment and ordered that it be cut down, so it was cut down."],[620,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib reported that they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, numbering fourteen hundred or more. They stopped beside a well and drew out all its water. They went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who came to the well, sat at its edge and said: “Bring me a bucket of its water.” It was brought to him, and he spat into it and supplicated. He then said: “Leave it for a while.” Thereafter, they fully quenched themselves and their riding animals before departing."],[621,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ reported: We arrived at al-Ḥudaybiyyah with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, numbering fourteen hundred. Its well contained so little water that it could not provide enough for fifty sheep. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat at the edge of the well and either supplicated or spat into it. The water then surged forth, so we drank and drew what we needed."],[622,"Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd reported from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allah: I remember being with the Prophet ﷺ when the time for the afternoon prayer arrived. We had only a small amount of water, which was placed in a vessel and brought to the Prophet ﷺ. He placed his hand in it, spread his fingers and said: “Come, people of ablution! The blessing is from Allah.” I saw water springing from between his fingers. The people performed ablution and drank. I spared no effort in filling my stomach with it, for I knew that it was blessed. I asked Jābir, “How many were you that day?” He replied, “Fourteen hundred.”"],[623,"Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allah reported: The people became thirsty on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a small leather vessel of water before him, from which he performed ablution. The people then came toward him, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “What is the matter with you?” They replied, “Messenger of Allah, we have no water with which to perform ablution or drink except what is in your vessel.” The Prophet ﷺ placed his hand in the vessel, and water began gushing from between his fingers like springs. We drank and performed ablution. I asked Jābir, “How many were you that day?” He replied, “Had we been one hundred thousand, it would have sufficed us. We were fifteen hundred.”"],[624,"Qatādah reported: I said to Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, “I have been informed that Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allah used to say that they numbered fourteen hundred.” Saʿīd replied, “Jābir told me that those who pledged allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ numbered fifteen hundred.”"],[625,"ʿAbd Allah ibn Abī Awfā reported: The Companions of the Tree numbered thirteen hundred, and the tribe of Aslam constituted one-eighth of the migrating force."],[626,"Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out in the year of al-Ḥudaybiyyah intending to visit the Sacred House, not to fight. He brought seventy sacrificial camels, while the people numbered seven hundred, so each camel was offered on behalf of ten men."],[627,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib reported: Two enslaved men came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah before the treaty was concluded. Their masters wrote to him, saying, “Muḥammad, by Allah, they did not come to you out of a desire for your religion; they came only to escape enslavement.” Some people said, “They are telling the truth, Messenger of Allah. Return them to their masters.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became angry and said: “I do not think you will desist, people of Quraysh, until Allah sends against you someone who will strike your necks over this matter.” He refused to return them and said: “They are those whom Allah Almighty has set free.”"],[628,"Anas reported that eighty armed men from Makkah descended from Mount al-Tanʿīm toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, seeking to take the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions by surprise. They were captured without fighting, but he spared their lives. Allah Almighty then revealed: “He is the One who restrained their hands from you and your hands from them in the valley of Makkah after giving you victory over them” [al-Fatḥ 48:24]."],[629,"ʿAbd Allah ibn Mughaffal al-Muzanī reported: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at al-Ḥudaybiyyah beneath the tree mentioned by Allah in the Qur’an. Some of its branches were hanging upon the back of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib and Suhayl ibn ʿAmr sat before him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿAlī: “Write: ‘In the name of Allah, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful.’” Suhayl ibn ʿAmr restrained his hand and said, “We do not acknowledge ‘the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful.’ Write in our agreement what we recognise.” He said: “Write: ‘In Your name, O Allah.’” ʿAlī wrote: “This is the treaty concluded by Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, with the people of Makkah.” Suhayl again restrained his hand and said, “We would have wronged you if you were truly His Messenger. Write in our agreement what we recognise.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Write: ‘This is the treaty concluded by Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib’—and I am indeed the Messenger of Allah.” So he wrote it. While we were in that state, thirty armed young men came out against us and rushed toward us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated against them, and Allah Almighty deprived them of their sight. We went forward and captured them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Have you come under anyone’s covenant, or has anyone granted you protection?” They replied, “No.” He nevertheless released them. Allah Almighty then revealed: “He is the One who restrained their hands from you and your hands from them in the valley of Makkah after giving you victory over them. Allah sees all that you do” [al-Fatḥ 48:24]."],[630,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concluded the treaty with the people of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib wrote the agreement between them and wrote: “Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah.” The polytheists said, “Do not write ‘Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah.’ Had you been a messenger, we would not have fought you.” The Prophet ﷺ told ʿAlī: “Erase it.” ʿAlī replied, “I am not the one who will erase it.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ therefore erased it with his own hand. He concluded the treaty on the condition that he and his Companions could enter Makkah for three days and would enter carrying no weapons except those kept in their sheaths. They asked what this meant, and he replied: “The scabbard together with what it contains.”"],[631,"Ibn ʿUmar reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out to perform ʿumrah, but the disbelievers of Quraysh prevented him from reaching the Sacred House. He slaughtered his sacrificial animals and shaved his head at al-Ḥudaybiyyah. He concluded a treaty with them stipulating that he would perform ʿumrah the following year, carry no weapons against them other than sheathed swords, and remain in Makkah only for the period they allowed. He performed ʿumrah the following year and entered Makkah in accordance with the treaty. After he had remained there for three days, they instructed him to leave, so he departed."],[632,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reported that he heard Marwān and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah relate from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: Among the conditions imposed by Suhayl ibn ʿAmr when he concluded the treaty that day was: “No man from among us may come to you—even if he follows your religion—without your returning him to us and allowing us to take custody of him.” The believers disliked this condition and were deeply distressed by it, but Suhayl refused to accept anything else, so the Prophet ﷺ concluded the treaty on that basis. That day he returned Abū Jandal to his father, Suhayl ibn ʿAmr. During the treaty period, no man came to him from Quraysh without being returned, even if he was a Muslim. Believing women, however, came as emigrants. Umm Kulthūm bint ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ was among those who came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at that time, and she was a young unmarried woman. Her family came to the Prophet ﷺ asking him to return her, but he did not do so, because Allah revealed concerning the women: “Believers, when believing women come to you as emigrants, test their faith—Allah knows their faith best…” through His words: “…nor are the disbelievers lawful husbands for them” [al-Mumtaḥanah 60:10]."],[633,"Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwān, each confirming the other’s account, reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out at the time of al-Ḥudaybiyyah. When they had travelled part of the way, the Prophet ﷺ said: “Khālid ibn al-Walīd is at al-Ghamīm with a cavalry unit serving as the vanguard of Quraysh, so take the road to the right.” By Allah, Khālid did not become aware of them until he saw the dust raised by the army. He galloped away to warn Quraysh. The Prophet ﷺ continued until he reached the pass from which he would descend toward them, whereupon his she-camel knelt down. The people called, “Ḥal! Ḥal!” but she remained obstinate. They said, “Al-Qaṣwāʾ has become stubborn.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Al-Qaṣwāʾ has not become stubborn, nor is stubbornness part of her nature. Rather, she has been restrained by the One who restrained the elephant.” He then said: “By the One in whose hand is my soul, they will not propose any plan by which they seek to honour the sacred ordinances of Allah without my accepting it.” He urged the camel onward, and she rose. He turned away from Quraysh and camped at the far end of al-Ḥudaybiyyah beside a shallow pool containing little water. The people drew from it sparingly, but they soon exhausted it. They complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of thirst. He removed an arrow from his quiver and instructed them to place it in the water. By Allah, it continued surging with abundant water until they had all finished and departed from it. While they were in this state, Budayl ibn Warqāʾ al-Khuzāʿī arrived with several men of Khuzāʿah. Among the people of Tihāmah, they were the trusted confidants and sincere advisers of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Budayl said, “I left Kaʿb ibn Lu’ayy and ʿĀmir ibn Lu’ayy encamped beside the abundant waters of al-Ḥudaybiyyah. They have brought nursing she-camels with their young, and they intend to fight you and prevent you from reaching the Sacred House.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “We have not come to fight anyone; we have come only to perform ʿumrah. War has exhausted and harmed Quraysh. If they wish, I shall conclude a truce with them for a fixed period, provided they allow me to deal freely with the other people. If I prevail, they may enter the faith that the others have entered, should they wish; otherwise, they will at least have regained their strength. But if they refuse, then by the One in whose hand is my soul, I shall fight them for this cause until my neck is severed, and Allah will surely accomplish His command.” Budayl said, “I shall convey your words to them.” He went to Quraysh and said, “We have come to you from this man and heard him make a proposal. If you wish, we shall present it to you.” Their foolish men said, “We have no need for you to tell us anything about him.” Those of sound judgment among them said, “Tell us what you heard him say.” Budayl then related the Prophet’s ﷺ proposal to them. ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd stood and said, “My people, are you not like parents to me?” They replied, “Yes.” He asked, “And am I not like a son to you?” They replied, “Yes.” He asked, “Do you have any reason to mistrust me?” They replied, “No.” He said, “Do you not know that I called upon the people of ʿUkāẓ to support you, and when they refused me, I came to you with my family, children and all who obeyed me?” They replied, “Yes.” He said, “This man has offered you a sound proposal. Accept it, and allow me to go to him.” They said, “Go to him.” He came to the Prophet ﷺ and began speaking with him. The Prophet ﷺ gave him essentially the same reply he had given Budayl. ʿUrwah then said, “Muḥammad, suppose you completely destroy your own people: have you ever heard of an Arab before you who exterminated his own family? If the opposite occurs, by Allah, I see no prominent men around you; I see only a mixed collection of people who are likely to flee and abandon you.” Abū Bakr said to him, “Go suck al-Lāt’s clitoris! Would we flee from him and abandon him?” ʿUrwah asked, “Who said that?” They replied, “Abū Bakr.” He said, “By the One in whose hand is my soul, were it not for a favour you once did me which I have not yet repaid, I would answer you.” ʿUrwah continued speaking with the Prophet ﷺ, and whenever he spoke he reached for the Prophet’s beard. Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah was standing over the Prophet ﷺ, carrying a sword and wearing a helmet. Whenever ʿUrwah reached for the Prophet’s beard, al-Mughīrah struck his hand with the lower part of the sword’s sheath and said, “Keep your hand away from the beard of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” ʿUrwah raised his head and asked, “Who is this?” They replied, “Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah.” He said, “You treacherous man! Am I not still working to settle the consequences of your treachery?” Before Islam, al-Mughīrah had accompanied a group of men, killed them and taken their property. He then came and embraced Islam. The Prophet ﷺ told him: “As for your Islam, I accept it; but as for the property, I will have nothing to do with it.” ʿUrwah began carefully observing the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ. By Allah, whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spat, it fell into the hand of one of them, who rubbed it over his face and skin. Whenever he commanded them, they hastened to carry out his command. Whenever he performed ablution, they almost fought over the remaining water. When he spoke, they lowered their voices in his presence, and out of reverence they did not stare directly at him. ʿUrwah returned to his people and said, “My people, by Allah, I have visited kings. I have visited Caesar, Chosroes and the Negus. By Allah, I have never seen any king whose followers revere him as the Companions of Muḥammad revere Muḥammad ﷺ. “By Allah, whenever he spits, it falls into the hand of one of them, who rubs it over his face and skin. Whenever he commands them, they hasten to carry out his command. Whenever he performs ablution, they almost fight over the remaining water. When he speaks, they lower their voices in his presence, and out of reverence they do not stare directly at him. He has offered you a sound proposal, so accept it.” A man from Banū Kinānah said, “Allow me to go to him.” They said, “Go.” When he appeared before the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “This man belongs to a people who venerate sacrificial animals, so send them out for him to see.” The sacrificial animals were brought before him, while the people received him reciting the pilgrimage response. When he saw this, he exclaimed, “Glory be to Allah! These people should not be prevented from reaching the Sacred House.” When he returned to his companions, he said, “I saw the sacrificial animals garlanded and marked. I do not believe they should be prevented from reaching the House.” A man named Mikraz ibn Ḥafṣ then stood and said, “Allow me to go to him.” They said, “Go.” When he appeared before them, the Prophet ﷺ said: “This is Mikraz, and he is a wicked man.” Mikraz began speaking with the Prophet ﷺ. While he was speaking, Suhayl ibn ʿAmr arrived. Maʿmar said that Ayyūb reported to him from ʿIkrimah that when Suhayl ibn ʿAmr arrived, the Prophet ﷺ said: “Your affair has now been made easy for you.” Maʿmar reported that al-Zuhrī’s narration continued: Suhayl ibn ʿAmr said, “Come, write an agreement between us and you.” The Prophet ﷺ summoned the scribe and dictated: “In the name of Allah, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful.” Suhayl said, “By Allah, I do not know who ‘the Most Compassionate’ is. Write instead: ‘In Your name, O Allah,’ as you used to write.” The Muslims said, “By Allah, we shall write only: ‘In the name of Allah, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful.’” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Write: ‘In Your name, O Allah.’” He then dictated: “This is the treaty concluded by Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah.” Suhayl said, “By Allah, had we known that you were the Messenger of Allah, we would neither have prevented you from reaching the House nor fought you. Write instead: ‘Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allah.’” The Prophet ﷺ said: “By Allah, I am truly the Messenger of Allah even though you deny me. Write: ‘Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allah.’” Al-Zuhrī said that he accepted this because of his earlier statement: “They will not propose any plan by which they seek to honour the sacred ordinances of Allah without my accepting it.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “On condition that you allow us access to the Sacred House so that we may circumambulate it.” Suhayl replied, “By Allah, we will not allow the Arabs to say that we yielded under pressure. You may do so next year.” This was written into the agreement. Suhayl then said, “And on condition that no man from among us may come to you—even if he follows your religion—without your returning him to us.” The Muslims exclaimed, “Glory be to Allah! How can a man be returned to the polytheists after coming as a Muslim?” While they were in that state, Abū Jandal ibn Suhayl ibn ʿAmr appeared, dragging himself in his chains. He had escaped from the lower part of Makkah and threw himself among the Muslims. Suhayl said, “Muḥammad, this is the first person concerning whom I demand that you fulfil our agreement: return him to me.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “We have not yet completed the written agreement.” Suhayl replied, “Then, by Allah, I shall never conclude any treaty with you.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Grant him to me as an exception.” He replied, “I will not grant him to you.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Please, do so.” He replied, “I will not.” Mikraz said, “We have granted him to you,” but Suhayl did not agree. Abū Jandal cried, “Muslims! Am I to be returned to the polytheists after coming to you as a Muslim? Do you not see what I have suffered?” He had been severely tortured for the sake of Allah. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb said: I went to the Prophet of Allah ﷺ and asked, “Are you not truly Allah’s Prophet?” He replied: “Indeed I am.” I asked, “Are we not upon the truth and our enemy upon falsehood?” He replied: “Indeed.” I asked, “Why, then, should we accept humiliation in our religion?” He said: “I am the Messenger of Allah. I do not disobey Him, and He will grant me victory.” I asked, “Did you not tell us that we would reach the Sacred House and circumambulate it?” He replied: “Indeed. But did I tell you that we would reach it this year?” I said, “No.” He said: “You will certainly reach it and circumambulate it.” I then went to Abū Bakr and asked, “Abū Bakr, is he not truly Allah’s Prophet?” He replied, “Indeed he is.” I asked, “Are we not upon the truth and our enemy upon falsehood?” He replied, “Indeed.” I asked, “Why, then, should we accept humiliation in our religion?” He said, “Man, he is truly the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He does not disobey his Lord, and Allah will grant him victory. Hold firmly to his stirrup, for by Allah, he is upon the truth.” I said, “Did he not tell us that we would reach the House and circumambulate it?” He replied, “Indeed. But did he tell you that you would reach it this year?” I said, “No.” He said, “You will certainly reach it and circumambulate it.” Al-Zuhrī reported that ʿUmar said: “Because of what I had said that day, I later performed many righteous deeds in expiation.” When the treaty had been completed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told his Companions: “Rise, slaughter your sacrificial animals and then shave your heads.” By Allah, not one of them rose, even after he repeated the instruction three times. When none of them responded, he entered the tent of Umm Salamah and told her how the people had reacted. Umm Salamah said, “Prophet of Allah, would you like them to comply? Go out and do not speak a word to anyone until you slaughter your own sacrificial animal and summon your barber to shave your head.” He went out and spoke to no one until he had done so. He slaughtered his sacrificial animal and summoned his barber, who shaved his head. When the Companions saw this, they rose, slaughtered their animals and began shaving one another’s heads. In their grief, they nearly killed one another. Believing women then came to him, so Allah revealed: “Believers, when believing women come to you as emigrants, test their faith—Allah knows their faith best. If you determine that they are believers, do not return them to the disbelievers. These women are not lawful for the disbelievers, nor are the disbelievers lawful for them. Return to their former husbands whatever bridal gifts they spent. There is no blame upon you if you marry these women, provided that you give them their bridal gifts. Do not retain marital bonds with disbelieving women” [al-Mumtaḥanah 60:10]. That day, ʿUmar divorced two wives who had remained upon polytheism. Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān married one of them, and Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah married the other. The Prophet ﷺ then returned to Madinah. Abū Baṣīr, a Muslim man from Quraysh, came to him. Quraysh sent two men to retrieve him, saying, “Remember the covenant you made with us.” The Prophet ﷺ handed him over to them. They departed with him until they reached Dhū al-Ḥulayfah, where they stopped to eat some dates. Abū Baṣīr said to one of the men, “By Allah, I see that this sword of yours is excellent.” The other man drew it and said, “Yes, by Allah, it is excellent. I have tested it time and again.” Abū Baṣīr said, “Let me look at it.” The man handed it to him, whereupon Abū Baṣīr struck him with it until he died. The other man fled until he reached Madinah and ran into the mosque. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him, he said: “This man has certainly witnessed something terrifying.” When the man reached the Prophet ﷺ, he cried, “By Allah, my companion has been killed, and I shall be killed as well!” Abū Baṣīr then arrived and said, “Prophet of Allah, by Allah, Allah has fully discharged your obligation. You returned me to them, and then Allah rescued me from them.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “May his mother lose him! What an inciter of war he would be, if only he had others with him.” When Abū Baṣīr heard this, he understood that the Prophet ﷺ would return him to Quraysh, so he left and travelled to the seacoast. Abū Jandal ibn Suhayl also escaped from Quraysh and joined Abū Baṣīr. Thereafter, every man who embraced Islam and escaped from Quraysh joined Abū Baṣīr, until they formed a sizeable band. By Allah, whenever they heard of a Quraysh caravan travelling toward Syria, they intercepted it, killed its guards and seized its property. Quraysh therefore sent word to the Prophet ﷺ, imploring him by Allah and by the bonds of kinship to summon these men, declaring that anyone who thereafter came to him from Makkah would be safe and would not be returned. The Prophet ﷺ then sent for them. Allah revealed: “He is the One who restrained their hands from you and your hands from them in the valley of Makkah after giving you victory over them…” through His words: “…the zealotry—the zealotry of the Age of Ignorance” [al-Fatḥ 48:24–26]. Their ignorant zealotry consisted of refusing to acknowledge that he was Allah’s Prophet, refusing the words “In the name of Allah, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful,” and preventing the Muslims from reaching the Sacred House. ʿUqayl reported from al-Zuhrī that ʿUrwah said: ʿĀ’ishah informed me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to test the faith of the emigrating women. We have also been informed that when Allah commanded the Muslims to return to the polytheists what they had spent as bridal gifts upon wives who emigrated, and ordered the Muslims not to retain marital bonds with disbelieving women, ʿUmar divorced two wives: Qarībah bint Abī Umayyah and the daughter of Jarwal al-Khuzāʿī. Muʿāwiyah married Qarībah, while Abū Jahm married the other. When the disbelievers refused to reimburse the Muslims for what they had spent upon wives who defected to them, Allah revealed: “If any of your wives go over to the disbelievers and you subsequently obtain compensation…” [al-Mumtaḥanah 60:11]. The compensation referred to what the Muslims paid to a disbeliever whose wife had emigrated. The Muslim whose wife had gone over to the disbelievers was therefore to be reimbursed for the bridal gift he had paid, using the bridal gifts left behind by the disbelieving husbands of women who had emigrated. We do not know of any emigrating woman who renounced her faith after believing. We have also been informed that Abū Baṣīr ibn Usayd al-Thaqafī came to the Prophet ﷺ as a believing emigrant during the treaty period. Al-Akhnas ibn Sharīq wrote to the Prophet ﷺ requesting that Abū Baṣīr be returned, and the remainder of the account follows."],[634,"Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out during the year of al-Ḥudaybiyyah intending to visit the Sacred House, not to fight. He brought seventy sacrificial camels, while the people numbered seven hundred, so each camel was offered on behalf of ten men. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached ʿUsfān, Bishr ibn Sufyān al-Kaʿbī met him and said, “Messenger of Allah, Quraysh have heard of your approach. They have come out with nursing she-camels and their young, dressed in leopard skins and pledging before Allah that you will never enter Makkah against their will. Khālid ibn al-Walīd has been sent ahead with their cavalry to Kurāʿ al-Ghamīm.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “How unfortunate Quraysh are! War has consumed them. What harm would it do them to leave me to deal with the rest of the people? If they defeat me, that will be what they desire. But if Allah grants me victory, they may enter Islam with their strength intact. If they choose not to, they may then fight while still possessing strength. What do Quraysh imagine? By Allah, I shall continue striving against them for the cause with which Allah sent me until Allah grants me victory or this neck is severed.” He then instructed the people to take the route to the right through al-Ḥumḍ, along a road leading to the Pass of al-Marār and al-Ḥudaybiyyah from below Makkah. He led the army along that road. When the Quraysh cavalry saw the dust raised by the army and realised that the Muslims had changed their route, they galloped back to Quraysh. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued until his she-camel knelt down at the Pass of al-Marār. The people said, “She has become stubborn.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “She has not become stubborn, nor is stubbornness part of her nature. Rather, she has been restrained from Makkah by the One who restrained the elephant. By Allah, Quraysh will not invite me today to any proposal involving the maintenance of kinship without my accepting it.” He then told the people: “Dismount.” They said, “Messenger of Allah, there is no water in the valley beside which the people can camp.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took an arrow from his quiver and gave it to one of his Companions. The man descended into one of the wells and planted the arrow in it, whereupon the water surged forth until the people and their animals gathered abundantly around it. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had settled at the campsite, Budayl ibn Warqāʾ arrived with some men of Khuzāʿah. The Prophet ﷺ told them essentially what he had told Bishr ibn Sufyān. They returned to Quraysh and said, “People of Quraysh, you are acting hastily against Muḥammad. Muḥammad has not come to fight; he has come only to visit this House and honour its sanctity.” Quraysh distrusted them. Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq reported that al-Zuhrī said: Khuzāʿah—both its polytheists and Muslims—were trusted confidants and sincere advisers of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They concealed from him nothing that occurred in Makkah. Quraysh said, “Even if that is his sole purpose, by Allah, he will never enter Makkah against our will, lest the Arabs speak of it.” They sent Mikraz ibn Ḥafṣ, a man of Banū ʿĀmir ibn Lu’ayy, to him. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him, he said: “This is a treacherous man.” When Mikraz reached him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him essentially the same message he had given the previous envoys. Mikraz returned to Quraysh and conveyed the Prophet’s words. They then sent al-Ḥulays ibn ʿAlqamah al-Kinānī, who was the leader of the allied tribes at that time. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him, he said: “This man belongs to a people who are deeply devoted to religious observances. Send the sacrificial animals out before him.” The animals were sent toward him, flowing across the breadth of the valley with their garlands upon them. Their hair had become worn from being detained so long from the place of sacrifice. Out of reverence for what he saw, al-Ḥulays turned back without reaching the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\nHe told Quraysh, “I have seen something that it is not lawful to prevent: sacrificial animals wearing their garlands, their hair worn away from being detained so long from their place of sacrifice.” They replied, “Sit down. You are merely a Bedouin with no knowledge.” They then sent ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd al-Thaqafī. He said, “People of Quraysh, I have seen how harshly and abusively you treat the men whom you send to Muḥammad when they return. You know that you are like parents to me and I am like a son to you. When I heard of the difficulty that had befallen you, I gathered those of my people who obeyed me and came to support you personally.” They replied, “You have spoken truthfully. We do not mistrust you.” He went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, sat before him and said, “Muḥammad, you have gathered the rabble and brought them against your own family to destroy it! Quraysh have come out with nursing she-camels and their young, dressed in leopard skins and pledging before Allah that you will never enter Makkah against their will. By Allah, I can almost see these people abandoning you tomorrow.”\nAbū Bakr was sitting behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “Go suck al-Lāt’s clitoris! Would we abandon him?” ʿUrwah asked, “Who is this, Muḥammad?” He replied: “This is the son of Abū Quḥāfah.” ʿUrwah said, “By Allah, were it not for a favour you once did me, I would repay your words in kind. Let that favour settle this.” ʿUrwah then reached for the beard of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah was standing over the Prophet ﷺ in armour, and he struck ʿUrwah’s hand, saying, “Remove your hand from the beard of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ before, by Allah, it is prevented from returning to you.” ʿUrwah said, “Woe to you! How harsh and coarse you are!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled. ʿUrwah asked, “Who is this, Muḥammad?” He replied: “This is your nephew, al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah.” ʿUrwah said, “You treacherous man! Was it not only yesterday that I settled the disgrace of your treachery?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him the same message he had given the previous envoys and informed him that he had not come seeking war. ʿUrwah then departed, having observed how the Prophet’s Companions treated him: whenever he performed ablution they rushed for the remaining water; whenever he spat they rushed to receive it; and whenever a hair fell from him they picked it up. He returned to Quraysh and said, “People of Quraysh, I have visited Chosroes in his kingdom, and I have visited Caesar and the Negus in their kingdoms. By Allah, I have never seen any king among his subjects like Muḥammad among his Companions. I have seen people who will never surrender him for anything. Consider carefully what you will do.” Before that, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had sent Khirāsh ibn Umayyah al-Khuzāʿī to Makkah, mounted upon one of his camels, to inform its chiefs of the purpose for which he had come. Quraysh hamstrung the Messenger’s camel and intended to kill Khirāsh, but the allied tribes protected him. They released him, and he returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ then summoned ʿUmar to send him to Makkah. ʿUmar said, “Messenger of Allah, I fear that Quraysh may kill me. There is no one from Banū ʿAdī in Makkah to protect me, and Quraysh know my hostility and severity toward them. But I can direct you to a man who possesses greater protection there than I do: ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned ʿUthmān and sent him to Quraysh to inform them that he had not come to fight anyone, but only to visit the Sacred House and honour its sanctity.\nʿUthmān travelled to Makkah, where Abān ibn Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ met him. Abān dismounted, seated ʿUthmān before him on his mount and rode behind him, granting him protection until he could deliver the Messenger’s message. ʿUthmān went to Abū Sufyān and the leading men of Quraysh and conveyed the message of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They told him, “If you wish to circumambulate the House, you may do so.” He replied, “I would never do so before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ circumambulates it.” Quraysh detained him, and word reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that ʿUthmān had been killed. Muḥammad reported that al-Zuhrī told him: Quraysh sent Suhayl ibn ʿAmr, of Banū ʿĀmir ibn Lu’ayy, saying, “Go to Muḥammad and conclude a treaty with him, but the treaty must require him to turn back this year. By Allah, the Arabs must never be allowed to say that he entered Makkah against our will.” When Suhayl ibn ʿAmr came, the Prophet ﷺ said: “The people now desire peace, since they have sent this man.” When Suhayl reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, they spoke at length and exchanged proposals until they agreed upon peace. When the matter had been settled and only the written document remained, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb went to Abū Bakr and asked, “Abū Bakr, is he not the Messenger of Allah? Are we not Muslims? Are they not polytheists?”\nHe replied, “Indeed.” ʿUmar asked, “Why, then, should we accept humiliation in our religion?” Abū Bakr said, “ʿUmar, hold firmly to his stirrup wherever he leads, for I testify that he is the Messenger of Allah.” ʿUmar replied, “And I testify to that.” ʿUmar then went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked, “Messenger of Allah, are we not Muslims? Are they not polytheists?” He replied: “Indeed.” ʿUmar asked, “Why, then, should we accept humiliation in our religion?” He replied: “I am Allah’s servant and Messenger. I shall never oppose His command, and He will never abandon me.” ʿUmar later said: “Because of what I had done and the words I had spoken that day, I continued fasting, praying, giving charity and emancipating slaves until I hoped that the matter had been turned to good.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib and said: “Write: ‘In the name of Allah, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful.’” Suhayl ibn ʿAmr said, “I do not acknowledge this. Write instead: ‘In Your name, O Allah.’” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Write: ‘In Your name, O Allah. This is the treaty concluded by Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah, with Suhayl ibn ʿAmr.’” Suhayl said, “Had I acknowledged that you were the Messenger of Allah, I would not have fought you. Write instead: ‘This is the agreement concluded by Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allah and Suhayl ibn ʿAmr.’” They agreed to suspend warfare for ten years, during which the people would enjoy security and refrain from attacking one another. Any man of Quraysh who came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ without his guardian’s permission would be returned, whereas anyone who left the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for Quraysh would not be returned. Relations between them would be sincere and free of hidden hostility, with neither theft nor treachery.\nThe written conditions also stipulated that any tribe wishing to enter the alliance and covenant of Muḥammad could do so, and any tribe wishing to enter the alliance and covenant of Quraysh could do so. Khuzāʿah immediately declared, “We enter the alliance and covenant of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” Banū Bakr declared, “We enter the alliance and covenant of Quraysh.” The treaty further stipulated: “You will turn back this year and not enter Makkah. Next year we will withdraw from the city, and you may enter it with your Companions and remain for three days. You may carry only a traveller’s weapons—swords kept in their scabbards.” While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was having the agreement written, Abū Jandal ibn Suhayl ibn ʿAmr arrived in chains, having escaped to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Companions had set out with no doubt that victory would occur because of a vision the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had seen. When they witnessed the treaty, the return without entering Makkah and the concessions the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had accepted, they were overwhelmed by such distress that they nearly perished from grief. When Suhayl saw Abū Jandal, he went to him, struck him in the face and said, “Muḥammad, our agreement was concluded before this man came to you.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “You have spoken truthfully.” Suhayl seized Abū Jandal by the front of his clothing. Abū Jandal cried out at the top of his voice, “Muslims! Will you return me to the polytheists so that they may persecute me for my religion?” This only intensified the people’s anguish. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Abū Jandal, remain patient and seek your reward from Allah. Allah will provide relief and a way out for you and the other oppressed Muslims with you. We have concluded a treaty with these people, giving them our covenant and receiving theirs in return. We will not betray them.” ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb went to Abū Jandal and walked beside him, saying, “Be patient, Abū Jandal. They are only polytheists, and the blood of any one of them is merely the blood of a dog.” ʿUmar brought the sword’s hilt close to him, hoping that he would seize it and strike his father, but Abū Jandal could not bring himself to kill his father. The agreement was carried into effect. When they had finished writing it—the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying within the sacred precinct while his camp stood outside it—he arose and said: “People, slaughter your sacrificial animals and shave your heads.” No one stood.\nHe repeated the command, but no one stood. He repeated it a third time, yet still no one rose. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the tent of Umm Salamah and asked: “Umm Salamah, what is the matter with the people?” She replied, “Messenger of Allah, they have been overwhelmed by the grief you have seen. Do not speak to any of them. Go directly to your sacrificial animal, slaughter it and shave your head. Once you have done so, the people will follow.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out without speaking to anyone. He approached his sacrificial animal and slaughtered it, then sat down and had his head shaved. The people immediately arose, slaughtered their animals and shaved their heads. When they reached the middle of the road between Makkah and Madinah, Sūrat al-Fatḥ was revealed."],[635,"Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah reported that he stood over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ carrying a sword, with his face covered, while ʿUrwah was present. As ʿUrwah spoke to the Prophet ﷺ, he repeatedly reached for his beard. Al-Mughīrah said to him, “Keep your hand away from his beard, or it will not return to you.” ʿUrwah asked, “Who is this?” He was told, “This is your nephew, al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah.” ʿUrwah said, “You treacherous man! You have not yet cleansed your head of your treachery.”"],[636,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ reported: We arrived at al-Ḥudaybiyyah with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, numbering fourteen hundred. Its well contained so little water that it could not provide enough for fifty sheep. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat at the edge of the well and either supplicated or spat into it. The water then surged forth, so we drank and drew what we needed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned us to pledge allegiance beneath the tree. I was the first to pledge. When he reached the middle of the people, he said: “Pledge to me, Salamah!” I replied, “I pledged to you, Messenger of Allah, among the first people.” He said: “And again.” Seeing that I was unarmed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave me a shield. When he reached the last of the people, he said: “Will you not pledge to me, Salamah?” I replied, “I pledged to you among the first people and again in the middle.” He said: “And again.” I therefore pledged a third time. He then asked: “Salamah, where is the shield I gave you?” I replied, “Messenger of Allah, my uncle ʿĀmir met me while he was unarmed, so I gave it to him.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed and said: “You are like the man of old who said: ‘O Allah, grant me a beloved companion who is dearer to me than my own self.’” The polytheists then entered into peace negotiations with us. We began moving freely among one another and concluded the treaty. I was serving Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allah: watering and grooming his horse, attending to his needs and eating from his food. I had left my family and property behind, emigrating to Allah and His Messenger ﷺ. When we concluded peace with the people of Makkah and began mixing with one another, I came to a tree, cleared away its thorns and lay beneath it. Four polytheists from Makkah came and began insulting the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so I detested them. I moved to another tree. They hung up their weapons and lay down. While they were resting, a caller shouted from the lower part of the valley, “Emigrants! Ibn Zunaym has been killed!” I drew my sword and rushed upon the four men while they were lying down. I gathered their weapons into a bundle in my hand and said, “By the One who has honoured the face of Muḥammad, none of you will raise his head without my striking the place where his eyes are.” I drove them before me to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. My uncle ʿĀmir also brought a man of al-ʿAbalāt named Mikraz, leading him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon an armoured horse, together with seventy polytheists. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at them and said: “Release them, so that both the beginning and the recurrence of aggression will be attributed to them.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ pardoned them. Allah then revealed the complete verse: “He is the One who restrained their hands from you and your hands from them in the valley of Makkah after giving you victory over them” [al-Fatḥ 48:24]. We then departed for Madinah and camped at a place separated from the polytheist tribe of Banū Liḥyān by a mountain. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed for forgiveness for whoever would climb that mountain during the night and serve as a lookout for the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions. I climbed it two or three times that night. We then reached Madinah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent his riding camels out with Rabāḥ, his servant, and I accompanied him. I also took Ṭalḥah’s horse to exercise it among the camels. The following morning, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Fazārī raided the Messenger’s herd, drove away all the camels and killed their herdsman. I said, “Rabāḥ, take this horse to Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allah and inform the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the polytheists have raided his grazing animals.” I climbed a hillock, faced Madinah and cried three times, “Alarm! Alarm!” I then pursued the raiders, shooting arrows at them and chanting: I am the son of al-Akwaʿ; today is the day the cowardly will perish. I caught up with one of them and shot an arrow through his saddle until its point reached his shoulder. I said: Take that! I am the son of al-Akwaʿ; today is the day the cowardly will perish. By Allah, I continued shooting at them and hamstringing their mounts. Whenever a horseman turned back toward me, I took cover beneath a tree, shot at him and brought down his mount. When they entered a narrow mountain pass, I climbed above them and began pelting them with stones. I continued pursuing them until I had recovered every camel from the herd of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and left it behind me. They abandoned the camels to me. I continued chasing and shooting at them until, to lighten their loads, they discarded more than thirty cloaks and thirty spears. Whenever they threw something down, I marked it with a pile of stones so that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions could identify it. When they reached a narrow mountain pass, a man of the family of Badr al-Fazārī came to them. They sat down to eat their late-morning meal, while I positioned myself at the top of a mountain spur. The Fazārī asked, “Who is that man I see?” They replied, “We have suffered terribly from him! By Allah, he has not left us since the darkness before dawn. He has kept shooting at us until he has stripped everything from our hands.” He said, “Let four of you go up against him.” Four men climbed the mountain toward me. When they came within speaking distance, I asked, “Do you know me?” They replied, “No. Who are you?” I said, “I am Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ. By the One who has honoured the face of Muḥammad, I will catch any one of you whom I pursue, while none of you who pursues me will catch me.” One of them said, “I believe he is telling the truth.” They then returned. I remained in my position until I saw the horsemen of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ weaving through the trees. The first was al-Akhram al-Asadī, followed by Abū Qatādah al-Anṣārī, and then al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad al-Kindī. I seized al-Akhram’s reins as the raiders turned and fled. I said, “Akhram, beware of them! Do not let them cut you off before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions catch up.”\nHe replied, “Salamah, if you believe in Allah and the Last Day and know that Paradise and Hell are real, do not stand between me and martyrdom.” I therefore released him. He met ʿAbd al-Raḥmān and brought down his horse, but ʿAbd al-Raḥmān stabbed and killed him, then mounted his horse. Abū Qatādah, the horseman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, caught ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, stabbed him and killed him. By the One who honoured the face of Muḥammad, I pursued them on foot until I could no longer see the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ or even the dust they raised behind me. Shortly before sunset, the raiders turned toward a ravine containing water called Dhū Qarad, intending to drink because they were thirsty. When they saw me running behind them, I drove them away from it, and they did not taste a single drop. They fled rapidly up a pass. I ran after them, caught one of them and struck him with an arrow in the ridge of his shoulder. I said: Take that! I am the son of al-Akwaʿ; today is the day the cowardly will perish. He exclaimed, “May his mother lose him! Is this the same al-Akwaʿ who pursued us this morning?” I replied, “Yes, enemy of yourself—the very same al-Akwaʿ from this morning.” They abandoned two horses on the pass, and I brought them back, driving them toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. ʿĀmir caught up with me carrying a leather vessel containing diluted milk and another containing water. I performed ablution and drank, then went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who had reached the water from which I had driven the raiders. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had recovered the camels and everything I had rescued from the polytheists, including every spear and cloak. Bilāl had slaughtered one of the recovered she-camels and was roasting some of its liver and hump for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I said, “Messenger of Allah, allow me to select one hundred men and pursue the raiders. I will kill every one of them so that none remains to carry the news.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed until his molar teeth appeared in the firelight. He asked: “Salamah, do you really think you could do that?”\nI replied, “Yes, by the One who has honoured you!” He said: “They are now being entertained as guests in the territory of Ghaṭafān.” A man from Ghaṭafān then arrived and said, “So-and-so slaughtered a camel for them. As they were skinning it, they saw dust rising and said, ‘The pursuing force has reached you!’ They fled immediately.” The following morning, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The best of our horsemen today was Abū Qatādah, and the best of our foot soldiers was Salamah.”\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave me two shares of the spoils: the share of a horseman and that of a foot soldier. He combined both shares for me, then seated me behind him upon al-ʿAḍbāʾ as we returned to Madinah. While we were travelling, a man of the Anṣār who was considered unbeatable in running kept calling, “Will anyone race me to Madinah? Is there anyone who will race?” He repeated it several times. When I heard him, I asked, “Do you not show respect to an honourable man or stand in awe of a noble one?” He replied, “No—unless it is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” I said, “Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you, allow me to race this man.” He said: “If you wish.” I told the man, “Go ahead.” I bent my legs, sprang forward and began running. I let him gain one or two rises in the ground while conserving my strength, then ran after him. I again allowed him one or two rises. I then accelerated until I caught him, struck him between the shoulders and said, “By Allah, I have overtaken you!” He replied, “I believe you have.” I beat him to Madinah. By Allah, we remained only three nights before setting out for Khaybar with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. My uncle ʿĀmir began chanting to the company: By Allah, had Allah not guided us, we would not be guided, nor would we give charity or pray. We can never dispense with Your favour; make our feet firm when we meet the enemy, and send tranquillity down upon us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Who is this?” He replied, “I am ʿĀmir.” He said: “May your Lord forgive you.” Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ specifically prayed for someone’s forgiveness in this manner, that person attained martyrdom. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, who was riding a camel, called out, “Prophet of Allah, if only you had allowed us to enjoy ʿĀmir’s company longer!” When we reached Khaybar, their champion Marḥab came out, strutting with his sword and chanting: Khaybar knows that I am Marḥab, fully armed, a tested warrior, when wars advance in blazing fury.\nMy uncle ʿĀmir came out against him and chanted: Khaybar knows that I am ʿĀmir, fully armed, a daring warrior. They exchanged two blows. Marḥab’s sword struck ʿĀmir’s shield, and ʿĀmir attempted to strike him from below. His sword rebounded upon himself and severed the artery in his forearm, causing his death. Salamah said: I came upon several Companions of the Prophet ﷺ saying, “ʿĀmir’s deeds have become void; he killed himself.” I went to the Prophet ﷺ weeping and asked, “Messenger of Allah, have ʿĀmir’s deeds become void?”\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Who said that?” I replied, “Some of your Companions.” He said: “Whoever said that has lied. Rather, he will receive a double reward.” He then sent me to fetch ʿAlī, who was suffering from inflamed eyes, saying: “I shall give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger—or whom Allah and His Messenger love.” I went to ʿAlī and led him by the hand because of his eyes until I brought him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He spat into his eyes, and they were cured. He then gave him the banner.\nMarḥab came out chanting: Khaybar knows that I am Marḥab, fully armed, a tested warrior, when wars advance in blazing fury. ʿAlī replied: I am the one whose mother named him Ḥaydarah, like a lion of the forests, fearsome to behold; I repay them measure for measure, in a mighty measure. ʿAlī struck Marḥab on the head and killed him, and the conquest was then achieved at his hands."],[637,"Anas reported that Quraysh concluded a treaty with the Prophet ﷺ, represented by Suhayl ibn ʿAmr. The Prophet ﷺ told ʿAlī: “Write: ‘In the name of Allah, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful.’” Suhayl said, “We do not know this phrase, ‘In the name of Allah, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful.’ Write what we recognise: ‘In Your name, O Allah.’” The Prophet ﷺ then said: “Write: ‘From Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah.’” They said, “Had we known that you were the Messenger of Allah, we would have followed you. Write instead your name and your father’s name.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Write: ‘From Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allah.’” They imposed the condition that anyone who left the Muslims for Quraysh would not be returned, whereas anyone who left Quraysh for the Muslims would be returned to them. The Companions asked, “Messenger of Allah, should we write this?” He replied: “Yes. Whoever leaves us for them—may Allah keep him far away. As for anyone who comes to us from them, Allah will provide him relief and a way out.”"],[638,"ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿAbbās reported: When the Ḥarūriyyah seceded and isolated themselves, I said to them: “On the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concluded a treaty with the polytheists. He told ʿAlī: ʿWrite, ʿAlī: “This is the treaty concluded by Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah.”’ “They said, ‘Had we known that you were the Messenger of Allah, we would not have fought you.’ “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ʿErase it, ʿAlī. O Allah, You know that I am Your Messenger. Erase it, ʿAlī, and write: “This is the treaty concluded by Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allah.”’ “By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was better than ʿAlī, yet he erased his own title, and that erasure did not remove him from prophethood. Have I answered this objection of yours?” They replied, “Yes.”"],[639,"Abū Wā’il reported: On the day of Ṣiffīn, Sahl ibn Ḥunayf stood and said: “People, distrust your own opinions. We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, and had we considered fighting appropriate, we would have fought. This occurred during the treaty between the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the polytheists. “ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked, ‘Messenger of Allah, are we not upon the truth and they upon falsehood?’ “He replied: ‘Indeed.’ “ʿUmar asked, ‘Are not our dead in Paradise and their dead in the Fire?’ “He replied: ‘Indeed.’ “ʿUmar asked, ‘Why, then, should we accept humiliation in our religion and turn back before Allah has judged between us and them?’ “He replied: ‘Son of al-Khaṭṭāb, I am the Messenger of Allah, and Allah will never abandon me.’” Still deeply agitated, ʿUmar left and went to Abū Bakr. He asked, “Abū Bakr, are we not upon the truth and they upon falsehood?” He replied, “Indeed.” ʿUmar asked, “Are not our dead in Paradise and their dead in the Fire?” He replied, “Indeed.” ʿUmar asked, “Why, then, should we accept humiliation in our religion and turn back before Allah has judged between us and them?” Abū Bakr replied, “Son of al-Khaṭṭāb, he is the Messenger of Allah, and Allah will never abandon him.” The revelation announcing the victory then descended upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He sent for ʿUmar and recited it to him. ʿUmar asked, “Messenger of Allah, is this truly a victory?” He replied: “Yes.” ʿUmar’s heart then became content and he withdrew his objection."],[640,"Sahl ibn Ḥunayf said: “Distrust personal opinion. I remember myself on the day of Abū Jandal: had I been able to reject the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I would have done so. Yet Allah and His Messenger know best. “Before this present conflict, whenever we placed our swords upon our shoulders to confront a terrifying situation, those swords led us toward an outcome we understood. But in this affair, whenever we close one breach, another bursts open before us, and we do not know how to deal with it.”"],[641,"Sahl ibn Ḥunayf said at Ṣiffīn: “People, distrust your own opinions. By Allah, I remember myself on the day of Abū Jandal: had I been able to reject the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I would have done so. By Allah, whenever we placed our swords upon our shoulders for any cause, they led us toward an outcome we understood—except in this conflict of yours.”"],[642,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reported that he heard Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah relate an account of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the ʿumrah of al-Ḥudaybiyyah. Part of their report was that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concluded the fixed-term treaty with Suhayl ibn ʿAmr, Suhayl imposed the following condition: “No man from among us may come to you—even if he follows your religion—without your returning him to us and allowing us to take custody of him.” Suhayl refused to conclude the treaty except upon this condition. The believers disliked it, were deeply distressed and voiced their objections. When Suhayl refused to accept anything else, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concluded the agreement accordingly. That day he returned Abū Jandal ibn Suhayl to his father, Suhayl ibn ʿAmr. During the treaty period, no man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from Quraysh without being returned, even if he was a Muslim. Believing women, however, came as emigrants. Umm Kulthūm bint ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ was among those who came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and she was a young unmarried woman. Her family came asking him to return her, whereupon Allah revealed what He revealed concerning the believing women."],[643,"Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam reported: While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was having the treaty written, Abū Jandal ibn Suhayl ibn ʿAmr arrived in chains, having escaped to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Companions had set out with no doubt that victory would occur because of a vision the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had seen. When they witnessed the treaty, the return without entering Makkah and the concessions the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had accepted, they were overwhelmed by such distress that they nearly perished from grief. When Suhayl saw Abū Jandal, he went to him, struck him in the face and said, “Muḥammad, our agreement was settled before this man came to you.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “You have spoken truthfully.” Suhayl seized Abū Jandal by the front of his clothing. Abū Jandal cried at the top of his voice, “Muslims! Will you return me to the polytheists so that they may persecute me for my religion?” This only intensified the people’s anguish. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Abū Jandal, remain patient and seek your reward from Allah. Allah will provide relief and a way out for you and the other oppressed Muslims with you. We have concluded a treaty with these people, giving them our covenant and receiving theirs in return. We will not betray them.”"],[644,"Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwān, each confirming the other’s account, reported: The Prophet ﷺ returned to Madinah. Abū Baṣīr, a Muslim man from Quraysh, came to him. Quraysh sent two men to retrieve him, saying, “Remember the covenant you made with us.” The Prophet ﷺ handed him over to them. They departed with him until they reached Dhū al-Ḥulayfah, where they stopped to eat some dates. Abū Baṣīr said to one of the men, “By Allah, I see that this sword of yours is excellent.” The man drew it and said, “Yes, by Allah, it is excellent. I have tested it time and again.” Abū Baṣīr said, “Let me look at it.” The man handed it to him, whereupon Abū Baṣīr struck him with it until he died. The other man fled to Madinah and ran into the mosque. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him, he said: “This man has certainly witnessed something terrifying.” When the man reached the Prophet ﷺ, he cried, “By Allah, my companion has been killed, and I shall be killed as well!” Abū Baṣīr then arrived and said, “Prophet of Allah, by Allah, Allah has fully discharged your obligation. You returned me to them, and then Allah rescued me from them.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “May his mother lose him! What an inciter of war he would be, if only he had others with him.” When Abū Baṣīr heard this, he understood that the Prophet ﷺ would return him to Quraysh, so he left and travelled to the seacoast. Abū Jandal ibn Suhayl also escaped from Quraysh and joined Abū Baṣīr. Thereafter, every man who embraced Islam and escaped from Quraysh joined Abū Baṣīr, until they formed a sizeable band. By Allah, whenever they heard of a Quraysh caravan travelling toward Syria, they intercepted it, killed its guards and seized its property. Quraysh therefore sent word to the Prophet ﷺ, imploring him by Allah and by the bonds of kinship to summon these men, declaring that anyone who thereafter came to him from Makkah would be safe and would not be returned. The Prophet ﷺ then sent for them. Allah revealed: “He is the One who restrained their hands from you and your hands from them in the valley of Makkah after giving you victory over them…” through His words: “…the zealotry—the zealotry of the Age of Ignorance” [al-Fatḥ 48:24–26]. Their ignorant zealotry consisted of refusing to acknowledge that he was Allah’s Prophet, rejecting the words “In the name of Allah, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful,” and preventing the Muslims from reaching the Sacred House."],[645,"Zayd ibn Aslam reported from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was travelling on one of his journeys, with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb riding beside him at night. ʿUmar asked him about something, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not answer. He asked a second time, but received no answer, and then asked a third time without receiving a reply. ʿUmar said to himself, “May your mother lose you, ʿUmar! You importuned the Messenger of Allah ﷺ three times, and on each occasion he did not answer you.” ʿUmar continued: I urged my camel onward and rode ahead of the Muslims, fearing that revelation might descend concerning me. Before long, I heard someone calling loudly for me. I thought, “I feared that Qur’anic revelation would descend concerning me.” I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and greeted him. He said: “Tonight a sūrah was revealed to me that is dearer to me than everything upon which the sun rises.” He then recited: “Indeed, We have granted you a manifest victory” [al-Fatḥ 48:1]."],[646,"Abū Wā’il reported: On the day of Ṣiffīn, Sahl ibn Ḥunayf stood and said: “People, distrust your own opinions. We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, and had we considered fighting appropriate, we would have fought. This occurred during the treaty between the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the polytheists. “ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked, ‘Messenger of Allah, are we not upon the truth and they upon falsehood?’ “He replied: ‘Indeed.’ “ʿUmar asked, ‘Are not our dead in Paradise and their dead in the Fire?’ “He replied: ‘Indeed.’ “ʿUmar asked, ‘Why, then, should we accept humiliation in our religion and turn back before Allah has judged between us and them?’ “He replied: ‘Son of al-Khaṭṭāb, I am the Messenger of Allah, and Allah will never abandon me.’” Still deeply agitated, ʿUmar went to Abū Bakr and asked, “Abū Bakr, are we not upon the truth and they upon falsehood?” He replied, “Indeed.” ʿUmar asked, “Are not our dead in Paradise and their dead in the Fire?” He replied, “Indeed.” ʿUmar asked, “Why, then, should we accept humiliation in our religion and turn back before Allah has judged between us and them?” Abū Bakr replied, “Son of al-Khaṭṭāb, he is the Messenger of Allah, and Allah will never abandon him.” The revelation announcing the victory then descended upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He sent for ʿUmar and recited it to him. ʿUmar asked, “Messenger of Allah, is this truly a victory?” He replied: “Yes.” ʿUmar’s heart then became content and he withdrew his objection."],[647,"Anas ibn Mālik explained the verse, “Indeed, We have granted you a manifest victory” [al-Fatḥ 48:1], saying: “It refers to al-Ḥudaybiyyah.” The Companions said, “May it bring you happiness and blessing, but what has been granted to us?” Allah then revealed: “So that He may admit believing men and believing women into gardens…” [al-Fatḥ 48:5]. Shuʿbah said: I went to Kūfah and related this entire account from Qatādah. When I later returned and mentioned it to him, he said: “The explanation of ‘Indeed, We have granted you a manifest victory’ is from Anas, while the words ‘May it bring you happiness and blessing’ are from ʿIkrimah.”"],[648,"Imam al-Bukhārī stated: This is the expedition in which the camels of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were seized, and it took place three months before Khaybar.\nI say: Other statements have also been reported about this, but what Imam al-Bukhārī stated is the most correct."],[649,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ reported: I went out before the call to the dawn prayer. The milch camels of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were grazing at Dhū Qarad. A servant of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf met me and said, “The milch camels of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ have been taken.” I asked, “Who took them?” He replied, “Ghaṭafān.” I cried three times, “Alarm! Alarm!” making myself heard throughout the area between the two lava fields of Madinah. I then rushed forward until I caught them as they were drawing water. I began shooting arrows at them, for I was a skilled archer, while chanting: I am the son of al-Akwaʿ; today is the day the cowardly will perish. I continued chanting and fighting until I recovered the camels and seized thirty cloaks from them. The Prophet ﷺ and the people arrived. I said, “Prophet of Allah, I have kept them away from the water while they are thirsty. Send a force against them immediately.” He replied: “Son of al-Akwaʿ, now that you have prevailed, show leniency.”\nWe then returned, with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seating me behind him upon his she-camel until we entered Madinah."],[650,"al-Wāqidī stated that this series began in Dhū al-Ḥijjah of 6 AH, after the ʿumrah of al-Ḥudaybiyah.\nMuḥammad ibn Isḥāq stated that this series continued during the period between al-Ḥudaybiyah and the death of the Prophet ﷺ.\nI say: The people of knowledge have no real disagreement that this series began before the conquest of Makkah. Because when Heraclius asked Abū Sufyān whether anyone had left him, he said: “No. There is a truce between us and him for a period, and we do not know what he will do during it.”\nAnd the Prophet ﷺ also wrote the letter to Heraclius before the conquest of Makkah, the details of which are coming ahead."],[651,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ wrote to Khosrow, Caesar, the Negus, and every powerful ruler, inviting them to Allah Most High. This was not the same Negus over whom the Prophet ﷺ performed the funeral prayer. Another narration adds: “And Ukaydir of Dūmah.”"],[652,"Anas ibn Mālik said: When the Prophet ﷺ intended to write to the Romans, he was told: “They do not read a letter unless it is sealed.” He therefore had a silver signet ring made. It is as though I can still see its whiteness upon his hand. Engraved upon it were the words: “Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah.” Another narration states: “He intended to write to the non-Arabs.” A third narration states: “He intended to write to Khosrow, Caesar, and the Negus.”"],[653,"Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb reported that Heraclius sent for him while he was travelling with a group of Quraysh. They were merchants in Greater Syria during the period in which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had concluded a truce with Abū Sufyān and the disbelievers of Quraysh. They came to Heraclius while he was in Aelia—Jerusalem. He summoned them to his court while the Roman dignitaries were gathered around him. He then called for his interpreter and asked: “Which of you is most closely related to the man who claims to be a prophet?” Abū Sufyān said: I replied, “I am the closest of them to him in lineage.” Heraclius said: “Bring him close to me, and bring his companions near and place them behind him.” He then told his interpreter: “Tell them that I am going to question this man about the one who claims to be a prophet. If he lies to me, they must expose his lie.” By Allah, had I not been ashamed that they would later report me as a liar, I would have lied about him. The first thing he asked me was: “What is his lineage among you?” I replied: “He possesses a noble lineage among us.” He asked: “Has anyone among you ever made this claim before him?” I replied: “No.” He asked: “Was any of his forefathers a king?” I replied: “No.” He asked: “Are the nobles of the people following him, or are their weak following him?” I replied: “Rather, their weak are following him.” He asked: “Are they increasing or decreasing?” I replied: “They are increasing.” He asked: “Does anyone among them renounce his religion out of displeasure with it after embracing it?” I replied: “No.” He asked: “Did you ever accuse him of lying before he made this claim?” I replied: “No.” He asked: “Does he act treacherously?” I replied: “No. We currently have a truce with him, and we do not know what he will do during it.” Abū Sufyān said: That was the only statement into which I was able to insert something against him. Heraclius asked: “Have you fought against him?” I replied: “Yes.” He asked: “How has your warfare against him been?” I replied: “The war between us and him has alternated; sometimes he gains the advantage over us, and sometimes we gain the advantage over him.” He asked: “What does he command you to do?” I replied: “He says: ‘Worship Allah alone and associate nothing with Him, and abandon what your forefathers used to say.’ He commands us to pray, give zakāh, speak truthfully, remain chaste, and maintain the ties of kinship.” Heraclius then said to his interpreter: “Tell him: I asked you about his lineage, and you stated that he possesses a noble lineage among you. In the same way, messengers are sent from the noble lineages of their people. “I asked whether anyone among you had made this claim before him, and you said no. I reasoned that had someone made this claim before him, I would have said that he was merely imitating a statement made before him. “I asked whether any of his forefathers had been a king, and you said no. Had one of his forefathers been a king, I would have said that he was seeking to reclaim the kingdom of his father. “I asked whether you had accused him of lying before he made this claim, and you said no. I therefore know that a man who would not lie about people would never lie about Allah. “I asked whether the nobles or the weak among the people followed him, and you stated that their weak followed him. Such are the followers of the messengers. “I asked whether their numbers were increasing or decreasing, and you stated that they were increasing. Such is the nature of faith until it reaches completion. “I asked whether anyone renounced his religion out of displeasure with it after embracing it, and you stated that no one did. Such is faith when its delight permeates the heart. “I asked whether he acted treacherously, and you stated that he did not. Likewise, the messengers do not act treacherously. “I asked what he commanded you to do, and you stated that he commanded you to worship Allah without associating anything with Him, that he forbade you from worshipping idols, and that he commanded you to pray, speak truthfully, and remain chaste. “If what you have said is true, he will soon rule the very ground beneath these two feet of mine. I knew that he was about to appear, but I did not think that he would be from among you. If I knew that I could reach him, I would endure every hardship to meet him. If I were with him, I would wash his feet.” Heraclius then called for the letter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he had sent with Diḥyah to the ruler of Buṣrā, who had delivered it to Heraclius. Heraclius read it, and it contained: “In the name of Allah, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful. “From Muḥammad, the servant of Allah and His Messenger, to Heraclius, the great ruler of the Romans: “Peace be upon whoever follows the guidance. “To proceed: I invite you with the call of Islam. Embrace Islam and you will be safe. Allah will grant you your reward twice. If you turn away, you will bear the sin of the Arīsiyyīn. “ʿO People of the Scripture, come to a word that is equitable between us and you: that we worship none but Allah, associate nothing with Him, and do not take one another as lords besides Allah. But if they turn away, then say, “Bear witness that we are Muslims.”’” [Āl ʿImrān: 64] Abū Sufyān said: When Heraclius had finished speaking and the reading of the letter had ended, a great uproar arose around him and voices were raised. We were then expelled. When we were taken out, I said to my companions: “The affair of the son of Abū Kabshah has indeed become momentous! Even the king of the Romans fears him.” From that time onward, I remained certain that he would prevail until Allah brought Islam into my heart. Ibn al-Nāẓūr, the governor of Aelia, and Heraclius’s bishop over the Christians of Greater Syria, used to relate that when Heraclius arrived in Aelia, he awoke one morning in a disturbed state of mind. Some of his patriarchs said to him: “We find your appearance unusual today.” Ibn al-Nāẓūr said: Heraclius was skilled in astrology and used to examine the stars. When they questioned him, he said: “Last night, while examining the stars, I saw that the king of those who practise circumcision had appeared. Who among the people of this age practises circumcision?” They replied: “No one practises circumcision except the Jews. Do not let their affair trouble you. Write to the cities of your kingdom, ordering that the Jews living within them be killed.” While they were deliberating, a man was brought to Heraclius whom the king of Ghassān had sent with news concerning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. After Heraclius questioned him, he ordered: “Go and examine whether he is circumcised.” They examined him and informed Heraclius that he was circumcised. Heraclius then asked him about the Arabs, and the man replied: “They practise circumcision.” Heraclius said: “The king of this nation has now appeared.” Heraclius then wrote to a companion of his in Rome who was his equal in knowledge. Heraclius travelled to Ḥimṣ and had scarcely reached it when a letter arrived from his companion agreeing with Heraclius’s conclusion that the Prophet ﷺ had appeared and that he was indeed a prophet. Heraclius summoned the Roman dignitaries to a palace of his in Ḥimṣ. He then ordered its doors to be locked. Looking down upon them, he said: “O assembly of Romans! Do you desire success, right guidance, and the preservation of your kingdom? Then pledge allegiance to this Prophet.” They fled wildly toward the doors like startled wild donkeys, but found them locked. When Heraclius witnessed their aversion and despaired of their accepting faith, he said: “Bring them back to me.” He then told them: “I made that statement only to test the firmness of your adherence to your religion, and I have now seen it.” They prostrated themselves before him and became pleased with him. That was the end of the affair of Heraclius."],[654,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote to Caesar, saying: “If you turn away, you will bear the sin of the Arīsiyyīn.”"],[655,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote to Caesar, inviting him to Islam. He sent the letter with Diḥyah al-Kalbī and instructed him to deliver it to the ruler of Buṣrā, who would then deliver it to Caesar. When Allah had removed the Persian armies from Caesar, he walked from Ḥimṣ to Aelia in gratitude for the favour Allah had granted him. When the letter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached Caesar and he read it, he said: “Find someone from this man’s people who is present here so that I may question him concerning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[656,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Who will take this letter of mine to Caesar? He shall have Paradise.” A man from among the people asked: “Even if I am not killed?” He replied: “Even if you are not killed.” The man departed with the letter and encountered Caesar as he was approaching Jerusalem. A special carpet had been laid out for Caesar upon which no one else was permitted to walk. The man threw the letter onto the carpet and stepped aside. When Caesar reached the letter, he picked it up. He then summoned the chief Catholicos and had him read it. The Catholicos said: “My knowledge concerning this letter is no greater than yours.” Caesar then proclaimed: “Whoever brought this letter shall be granted safety.” The man came forward, and Caesar said to him: “When I return, come to me.” When Caesar returned, the man came to him. Caesar ordered the doors of his palace to be locked. He then instructed a herald to proclaim: “Behold! Caesar has followed Muḥammad ﷺ and abandoned Christianity!” His soldiers advanced fully armed until they surrounded his palace. Caesar said to the messenger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “You can see that I fear for my kingdom.” He then instructed a herald to proclaim: “Behold! Caesar is pleased with you. He merely tested you to see how firmly you would adhere to your religion. You may now return.” They therefore dispersed. Caesar wrote to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, claiming: “I am a Muslim.” He also sent him some gold dinars. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ read the letter, he said: “The enemy of Allah has lied. He is not a Muslim; he remains upon Christianity.” The Prophet ﷺ then distributed the dinars."],[657,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent his letter to Khosrow with ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥudhāfah al-Sahmī and instructed him to deliver it to the ruler of Bahrain. The ruler of Bahrain then delivered it to Khosrow. When Khosrow read it, he tore it apart. Al-Zuhrī said: I believe that Ibn al-Musayyib said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ then supplicated against them, asking that they be utterly torn apart."],[658,"Abū Bakrah reported: Allah benefited me through a statement that I heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the days of the Battle of the Camel, after I had nearly joined the army of the Camel and fought alongside them. When news reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the people of Persia had appointed the daughter of Khosrow as their ruler, he said: “A people who entrust their affairs to a woman will never prosper.”"],[659,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Qārī reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah to al-Muqawqis, the ruler of Alexandria, carrying his letter to him. Al-Muqawqis kissed the letter, honoured Ḥāṭib, and provided him with gracious hospitality. He then sent him back to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, sending with him as gifts a garment, a grey mule together with its saddle, and two bondwomen. One of them was the mother of Ibrāhīm. The other was given to Jahm ibn Qays al-ʿAbdarī; she was the mother of Zakariyyā ibn Jahm, who served as ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ’s deputy over Egypt."],[660,"Yazīd ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: We were at al-Mirbad when a man with dishevelled hair arrived, holding a piece of red leather in his hand. We said: “You appear to be from the desert.” He replied: “Yes.” We said: “Hand us the piece of leather that you are holding.” He handed it to us, and we read it. It contained: “From Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Banū Zuhayr ibn Uqaysh: If you testify that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah, establish the prayer, give zakāh, deliver one-fifth of the spoils, along with the Prophet’s ﷺ share and the selected share, then you shall be secure under the protection of Allah and His Messenger.” We asked: “Who wrote this document for you?” He replied: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[661,"Anas reported that the Prophet ﷺ wrote to Bakr ibn Wā’il: “From Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Bakr ibn Wā’il: Embrace Islam and you will be safe.” We could find no one able to read it except a man from Banū Ḍubayʿah; consequently, they came to be known as Banū al-Kātib—the descendants of the scribe."],[662,"Ibn Isḥāq reported: This is the letter from the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ to the Negus:\nIn the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. This letter is from Muhammad, the Prophet of Allah, to the Negus Aṣḥam, king of Abyssinia. Peace be upon whoever follows guidance, believes in Allah and His Prophet, and bears witness that Allah is One, without partner, and the true God, who has neither wife nor child, and bears witness that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. I invite you to accept the divine message. I am the true Prophet of Allah. Accept Islam and you will be safe.\n﴿يٰۤاَهْلَ الْكِتٰبِ تَعَالَوْا اِلٰى كَلِمَةٍ سَوَآءٍۭ بَيْنَنَا وَ بَيْنَكُمْ اَلَّا نَعْبُدَ اِلَّا اللّٰهَ وَ لَا نُشْرِكَ بِهٖ شَيْـًٔا وَّ لَا يَتَّخِذَ بَعْضُنَا بَعْضًا اَرْبَابًا مِّنْ دُوْنِ اللّٰهِ١ؕ﴾ [Āl ʿImrān: 64]\nSay: O People of the Book! Come to a word that is equal between us and you: that we worship none but Allah, that we associate nothing with Him, and that none of us takes others as lords besides Allah.\nIf you refuse, then the sin of all your subjects will also be upon you. (Mustadrak al-Ḥākim: 2/623, and Dalāʾil by al-Bayhaqī: 2/380) Ibn Isḥāq reported that the name of the Negus was Maṣḥamah, which in Arabic means gift and present. “Negus” is the title of the king, like Kisrā and Heraclius. Imam al-Ḥākim said that no one has supported Ibn Isḥāq al-Qurashī in giving the name of the Negus as Maṣḥamah. In the authentic and clear narrations in books such as the Two Ṣaḥīḥs, the name of the Negus is mentioned as Aṣḥamah, with an alif.\nI say: What Imam al-Ḥākim said is correct. This is not the Negus for whom he prayed the funeral prayer, as has already passed from Imam Muslim. This Negus took the throne after him, and some narrators of hadith confused the matter and wrote that the earlier Negus, who had died, was the Muslim one.\nSaʿīd ibn al-Musayyab reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote one and the same letter to Kisrā, Caesar and the Negus. Its text is as follows:\nIn the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. From Muhammad, the Prophet of Allah ﷺ, to Kisrā, Caesar and the Negus.\nTo proceed:\n﴿قُلْ يٰۤاَهْلَ الْكِتٰبِ تَعَالَوْا اِلٰى كَلِمَةٍ سَوَآءٍۭ بَيْنَنَا وَ بَيْنَكُمْ اَلَّا نَعْبُدَ اِلَّا اللّٰهَ وَ لَا نُشْرِكَ بِهٖ شَيْـًٔا وَّ لَا يَتَّخِذَ بَعْضُنَا بَعْضًا اَرْبَابًا مِّنْ دُوْنِ اللّٰهِ١ؕ فَاِنْ تَوَلَّوْا فَقُوْلُوا اشْهَدُوْا بِاَنَّا مُسْلِمُوْنَ۰۰۶۴﴾ [Āl ʿImrān: 64]\nSay: O People of the Book! Come to a word that is equal between us and you: that we worship none but Allah, that we associate nothing with Him, and that none of us takes others as lords besides Allah. Then if they turn away, say: Bear witness that we are Muslims, submitting to Allah.\nKisrā tore up the letter of the Prophet ﷺ without even opening it to look. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “He has torn the letter. His kingdom will be torn to pieces.” Caesar said that this letter contained “In the name of Allah,” and that he had never seen it before this, since the time of Sayyidunā Solomon, peace be upon him. He summoned Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb and al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, who had gone to Syria for trade. Caesar asked the two of them questions about the Prophet ﷺ. In the end he said: “May my father be sacrificed for him. If I could reach him, I would wash his blessed feet. A time will come when my kingdom too will be subject to him.” The Prophet ﷺ said about him: “He has been given respite.” The Negus believed in him. The Companions of the Messenger who were with him were granted peace and security. He sent fine clothing to the Prophet ﷺ as a gift. He said: “As long as he keeps away from you, do not say anything to him either.” (al-Amwāl by Abū ʿUbayd: 60 and Sunan Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr: 2480)\nThe correct view is that this Negus is different from the earlier one. He became king after the Muslim Negus."],[663,"Ibn Saʿd reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Sayyidunā Shujāʿ ibn Wahb al-Asadī, may Allah be pleased with him, with a letter to al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Shamir al-Ghassānī. Sayyidunā Shujāʿ was one of the six messengers of the Prophet ﷺ. al-Ḥārith was invited to Islam, and a letter was also sent to him. Sayyidunā Shujāʿ narrates: I went to al-Ḥārith. He was in the Ghūṭah of Damascus, busy with arrangements for the arrival and lodging of Caesar. He had come from Ḥimṣ to Īliyāʾ. I stayed in his palace for two or three days and said to his doorkeeper: “I am the messenger of the Prophet of Allah to Caesar.” The doorkeeper said: “You cannot meet him except on such and such a day, when he comes out.” al-Ḥārith’s envoy was a Roman named Murī. The envoy began asking me about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I informed him of his qualities and his call. He softened until he even began to weep. He said: “I have read the Gospel, and I myself read in it the description of this Prophet. I believe in him and affirm him. I fear that al-Ḥārith may kill me. He honors and respects me greatly.” One day al-Ḥārith came out of the palace, sat on the throne and placed the royal crown on his head. He gave me permission to come to him. I gave him the letter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He read the letter and threw it down and said: “Who can take my kingdom from me? I myself will go to him with an army. Even if he is in Yemen, I will seize him and bring him.” He issued an order to gather the army. Before he stood up, he spent a long time informing people of their responsibilities. He also gave the order to shoe the horses. Then he said to me: “Go and inform your master of everything you have seen here.” He wrote a letter to Caesar informing him about me and about his plan. Caesar sent him a reply: “Do not go anywhere; stay completely away from him, and meet me in Īliyāʾ.” After receiving Caesar’s letter, al-Ḥārith called me and said: “When will you go back to your master?” I said: “In the morning.” He ordered his envoy to give me one hundred mithqāls of silver. The envoy Murī treated me well. He also gave me provisions and clothing. He further said: “Convey my greetings to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” I came to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him of the whole event. He said: “His kingdom will be destroyed.” I conveyed to him the greeting of Murī and also informed him of his feelings. He said: “He has spoken the truth.” al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Shamir died in the year of the conquest of Makkah.\nImam al-Ṭabarī mentioned this text of his letter in al-Tārīkh (2/652): “Peace be upon the one who follows guidance and the one who believes in me. I also invite you to believe that Allah is One, without partner, and your kingdom will remain yours.”"],[664,"Sayyidunā Sulayṭ ibn ʿAmr al-ʿĀmirī, may Allah be pleased with him, was sent by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a letter to Hawdhah ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥanafī. He was invited to Islam and a letter was also sent to him. He honored the Prophet’s messenger and treated him with respect. He replied to the letter of the Prophet ﷺ in a very fine manner. He praised the call and message of the Prophet ﷺ. He said: “I am the poet and orator of my people. The Arabs know my position and rank well and also fear me. Command me to do certain things and I will surely carry them out.” He honored Sayyidunā Sulayṭ ibn ʿAmr with gifts and rewards. He gave him a cloth made in Hajar. Sayyidunā Sulayṭ came to the Prophet ﷺ with all this and told him the whole situation. He read out his letter to him. The Prophet ﷺ said: “If he had asked me for even a piece of land, I would not have given it to him at all. He himself will perish and his kingdom will also be plundered and ruined.” When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the conquest of Makkah, Jibrīl came and informed him of the death of this Hawdhah.\nʿAllāmah al-Zaylaʿī, in Naṣb al-Rāyah (4/525), has reported the text of the Prophet’s letter regarding him as follows: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. A letter from Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, to Hawdhah ibn ʿAlī. Peace be upon the one who follows the path of guidance. Know that the message I have brought will prevail over the whole world. Accept Islam and you will be safe. Whatever you have will remain yours.\nSayyidunā Sulayṭ ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him, was of Quraysh and of the tribe of ʿĀmir. He was the brother of Sayyidunā Suhayl ibn ʿAmr. The Prophet ﷺ sent him with a letter to Hawdhah ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥanafī. Ibn Hishām has added Thumāmah ibn Uthāl al-Ḥanafī as well, saying that he was sent to both. Both were chiefs of al-Yamāmah. Sayyidunā Sulayṭ was martyred in 14 AH."],[665,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Sayyidunā ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, in Dhū al-Qaʿdah, 8 AH, to Jayfar and ʿAbd, the sons of al-Julandā. Both were of the tribe of Azd, and the one with authority was Jayfar. They were invited to Islam. A letter was written to them and the prophetic seal was also affixed to it. Sayyidunā ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him, narrates: When I reached ʿUmān, I went first to ʿAbd. He was the better mannered and more dignified of the two. I said: “I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to you and to your brother.” He said: “My brother is greater than me in authority. I will take you to him. Read the letter to him.” I stayed with him a few days, then he called me, and I gave him the sealed letter. He opened the letter and read it calmly to the end, and then he gave the letter to his brother. He also read it completely. But in my view his brother was better mannered and more tender-hearted. He said: “Give me one day to think. Come to me tomorrow.” In the morning I went to him. He said: “I have thought a great deal about your message and have reached the conclusion that I am the ruler of the weakest of all Arabs.” I said: “I wish to go back in the morning.” The gathering ended. When he became sure of my return, he sent word and called me. I went to him, and he and his brother had accepted Islam and testified to the truthfulness of the Prophet ﷺ. They gave me general permission to collect the zakāh wealth from there. They helped me fully against those who opposed. I took wealth from their rich and distributed it among their poor. I stayed with them until the news of the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached me.\nʿAllāmah al-Zaylaʿī, in Naṣb al-Rāyah (4/423), has reported this text of his letter:\nIn the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. From Muhammad ibn ʿAbdullāh to Jayfar and ʿAbd, the sons of al-Julandā. Peace be upon the one who follows guidance. To proceed: I invite you both to Islam. Accept Islam and you will remain safe. I am the Messenger of Allah to all people, so that I may warn the living and the case may be established against the disbelievers. If you accept Islam, I will appoint you as rulers. If you refuse to declare Islam, your rule will be destroyed and my horsemen will trample your land. The message of my prophethood will prevail in your land as well.\nSayyidunā Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote this letter and sealed it."],[666,"Abū Bakr ibn Sulaymān ibn Abī Ḥathmah narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the expedition of Tabūk when a few days of Rajab, 9 AH, remained. He sent Sayyidunā al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥaḍramī, may Allah be pleased with him, to al-Mundhir ibn Sāwā al-ʿAbdī of Bahrain. This letter of his contained the following:\nIn the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. A letter from Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to al-Mundhir ibn Sāwā. Peace be upon the one who follows guidance. To proceed:\nI invite you to Islam. Accept Islam and you will be entitled to safety. Come into the fold of Islam, and Allah the Exalted will leave your kingdom with you. Know that my religion will prevail over the whole world. At the end of the letter he also affixed his seal.\nSayyidunā al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥaḍramī, may Allah be pleased with him, set out toward al-Mundhir carrying his letter. With him were a few other companions, among them Sayyidunā Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. He said to Sayyidunā al-ʿAlāʾ: be gentle with your companions and take care of them. He further said: if al-Mundhir accepts your invitation, then stay there and wait for my order. Take ʿushr and zakāh wealth from their rich and distribute it among their poor.\nSayyidunā al-ʿAlāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, says: “O Messenger of Allah! Write for me something of the teachings which will remain with me.” He wrote for him in detail the zakāh on camels, cattle, sheep and goats, crops, and gold and silver. Sayyidunā al-ʿAlāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, went to al-Mundhir with his letter and read the letter to him. He declared: “I bear witness that your message is true, that there is no god but Allah, and that Muhammad ﷺ is the servant and Messenger of Allah.” He entertained Sayyidunā al-ʿAlāʾ generously. Sayyidunā al-ʿAlāʾ returned and related the full account to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He was very pleased.\nSayyidunā ʿIkrimah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrates that after the death of Sayyidunā al-ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, a letter was found among his documents. Its words were: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Sayyidunā al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥaḍramī with a letter to al-Mundhir ibn Sāwā. In reply to the Prophet’s letter, al-Mundhir wrote a letter to him, whose words were: To proceed!\nO Messenger of Allah! I have read your blessed letter regarding the people of Bahrain. Some people liked the call to Islam and have accepted Islam. Some have turned away. In my land there are also Magians and Jews. What do you command me concerning them?\nIn reply, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a letter which stated:\nIn the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, to al-Mundhir ibn Sāwā. Peace be upon you. I praise Allah with you, besides Whom there is no god. I bear witness that there is no god but He and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. I advise you for the sake of Allah. Whoever takes advice, his advice benefits himself. Whoever obeys my messengers and follows their commands has obeyed me. Whoever is sincere toward them has been sincere toward me. My messengers have praised you. I will accept your recommendation regarding your people. Let those who were owners of something when they became Muslim keep it. I have pardoned the wrongdoers. You too accept their excuse. As long as you remain on the right path, you will not be removed from your position. Whoever wishes to remain on Judaism or Magianism must pay the jizyah.\nal-Mundhir read the letter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and became Muslim and proved to be a good Muslim. He died before the people of Bahrain apostatized. This is a necessary summary of the discussion; otherwise the reports are very many, as Ibn Saʿd has mentioned. (1/258-290)\nWhat we know with an authentic chain of transmission is very brief, and we have mentioned it. But this does not mean that no other letters were sent; rather the correct view is that other letters were also written and sent, but they have not reached us through authentic chains according to the conditions of the hadith scholars, because the historians paid little attention to this. As I have explained more than once.\nFrom the letters the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote to the kings of the Arabs and non-Arabs, it is perfectly clear that his message is universal and for all the world. Allah, Glorified and Exalted, has Himself said this about it, at a time when this religion was still only among the weak Muslims of Makkah. The Lord’s statement is:\n﴿وَ مَاۤ اَرْسَلْنٰكَ اِلَّا رَحْمَةً لِّلْعٰلَمِيْنَ۰۰۱۰۷ ﴾ [Sūrat al-Anbiyāʾ: 107]\nAnd (O Prophet!) We have sent you only as a mercy to all the worlds."],[667,"Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned during the year of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, and Sūrat al-Fatḥ was revealed to him between Makkah and Madinah. In it, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, granted him Khaybar: “Allah has promised you abundant spoils that you will obtain, and He has hastened this for you” [al-Fatḥ 48:20]. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached Madinah in Dhū al-Ḥijjah and remained there until he set out for Khaybar in Muḥarram. He camped at al-Rajīʿ, a valley between Khaybar and Ghaṭafān, fearing that Ghaṭafān might reinforce the people of Khaybar. He spent the night there and advanced against them the following morning."],[668,"ʿIrāk ibn Mālik al-Ghifārī reported that Abū Hurayrah arrived in Madinah with a group from his tribe while the Prophet ﷺ was at Khaybar, having appointed Sibāʿ ibn ʿUrfuṭah as his deputy over Madinah. Abū Hurayrah said: I reached him while he was leading the dawn prayer. In the first unit he recited: “Kāf Hā Yā ʿAyn Ṣād” [Maryam 19:1], and in the second: “Woe to those who give short measure” [al-Muṭaffifīn 83:1]. I said to myself, “Woe to so-and-so! When he receives a measure, he takes it in full, but when he gives a measure, he gives less.” After the prayer, Sibāʿ provided us with some provisions, and we travelled to Khaybar. The Prophet ﷺ had already conquered it. He spoke to the Muslims on our behalf, and they allowed us to share in their allotted portions."],[669,"Suwayd ibn al-Nuʿmān reported that he went with the Prophet ﷺ during the year of Khaybar. When they reached al-Ṣahbāʾ, near Khaybar, the Prophet ﷺ performed the afternoon prayer and called for provisions. Nothing was brought except sawīq, a preparation of roasted grain. He ordered that it be moistened, and he and the Companions ate it. He then rose for the sunset prayer, rinsed his mouth, and the Companions did likewise. He prayed without renewing his ablution."],[670,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ reported: We set out with the Prophet ﷺ for Khaybar and travelled by night. A man asked ʿĀmir, “ʿĀmir, will you not recite some of your verses for us?” ʿĀmir was a camel-driver who chanted rhythmically, so he began chanting: O Allah, had You not guided us, we would not be guided, nor would we give charity or pray. Forgive, as a ransom for You, the sins we have committed; send tranquillity down upon us, and make our feet firm when we meet the enemy. When we are called to battle, we stand firm, though with cries they have summoned forces against us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Who is this driver?” They replied, “ʿĀmir ibn al-Akwaʿ.” He said: “May Allah have mercy upon him.” A man among the company said, “His martyrdom has now become certain, Prophet of Allah. If only you had allowed us to enjoy his company longer!” We reached Khaybar and besieged its people until we suffered severe hunger. Allah, Exalted, then granted us victory over them. On the evening of the day of conquest, the people lit many fires. The Prophet ﷺ asked: “What are these fires? What are you cooking?” They replied, “Meat.” He asked: “What kind of meat?” They replied, “The meat of domesticated donkeys.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Pour it away and break the pots.” A man asked, “Messenger of Allah, may we pour it away and wash the pots instead?” He replied: “You may do that.” When the two forces formed their battle lines, ʿĀmir, whose sword was short, attempted to strike a Jewish man’s leg. The tip of his sword rebounded and struck the artery at his own knee, causing his death.\nAs the Muslims returned, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw me and took my hand. He asked: “What is troubling you?” I replied, “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you! They claim that ʿĀmir’s deeds have become void.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Whoever said that has lied. He will receive a double reward”—and he joined two fingers together—“for he was one who strove and exerted himself. Few Arabs have walked the earth like him.”"],[671,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿAbd Allah ibn Rawāḥah: “Why do you not urge our mounts onward with your chanting?” He replied, “I have abandoned composing poetry.” ʿUmar said to him, “Listen and obey.” He then chanted: O Allah, had You not guided us, we would not be guided, nor would we give charity or pray. Send tranquillity down upon us, and make our feet firm when we meet the enemy. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, have mercy upon him.” ʿUmar said, “It has become certain.”"],[672,"ʿAbd Allah ibn Mughaffal reported: While we were besieging Khaybar, someone threw down a leather bag containing fat. I sprang forward to take it, but then turned and saw the Prophet ﷺ, so I felt embarrassed."],[673,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: The Prophet ﷺ performed the dawn prayer near Khaybar while it was still dark, then said: “Allah is the Greatest! Khaybar is ruined. When we descend into the territory of a people, terrible is the morning of those who were warned.” The inhabitants came running through the streets. The Prophet ﷺ killed their fighting men and took their women and children captive. The remainder of the report follows."],[674,"Anas reported: The Prophet ﷺ reached Khaybar early in the morning, as its people came out carrying their spades. When they saw him, they cried, “Muḥammad and the army! Muḥammad and the army!” They turned and ran toward the fortress. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his hands, proclaimed Allah’s greatness three times and said: “Khaybar is ruined. When we descend into the territory of a people, terrible is the morning of those who were warned.” We captured some domesticated donkeys that had come out of the settlement and cooked their meat. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His Messenger forbid you to eat domesticated donkeys, for they are an impurity from the work of Satan.”"],[675,"Anas reported: When the Prophet ﷺ reached Khaybar, he encountered its people as they were going out to their fields carrying their spades. When they saw him with the army, they retreated to their fortress. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Allah is the Greatest! Khaybar is ruined. When we descend into the territory of a people, terrible is the morning of those who were warned.”"],[676,"Abū Ṭalḥah reported: When the Prophet of Allah ﷺ reached Khaybar in the morning, its people had taken their spades and gone out to their cultivated lands. When they saw the Prophet of Allah ﷺ with the army, they turned and fled. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: “Allah is the Greatest! Allah is the Greatest! When we descend into the territory of a people, terrible is the morning of those who were warned.”"],[677,"Anas reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ campaigned against Khaybar. We performed the dawn prayer near it while it was still dark. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ mounted, and Abū Ṭalḥah mounted with me riding behind him. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ rode rapidly through a narrow passage in Khaybar, and my knee touched his thigh. His lower garment shifted from his thigh, and I could see the whiteness of the Prophet’s ﷺ thigh. When he entered the settlement, he said: “Allah is the Greatest! Khaybar is ruined. When we descend into the territory of a people, terrible is the morning of those who were warned.” He repeated it three times. The inhabitants had gone out to their work and cried, “Muḥammad!” ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz said: Some of our companions added, “And the army!” We conquered Khaybar by force."],[678,"Anas reported: On the day of Khaybar, I rode behind Abū Ṭalḥah, and my foot touched the foot of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. We reached the inhabitants just as the sun rose. They had brought out their livestock and gone out carrying axes, baskets and spades. They cried, “Muḥammad and the army!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Khaybar is ruined. When we descend into the territory of a people, terrible is the morning of those who were warned.” Allah, Mighty and Majestic, defeated them. A beautiful captive woman fell to the share of Diḥyah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ acquired her in exchange for seven captives and entrusted her to Umm Sulaym to prepare her for him. I believe he also said that she was to observe her waiting period in Umm Sulaym’s house. She was Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ held her wedding feast with dates, dried curds and clarified butter. Hollows were dug in the ground and leather sheets were spread within them. The dried curds and butter were brought, and the people ate their fill. The people said, “We do not know whether he has married her or taken her as a bondwoman who may bear him a child. If he screens her, she is his wife; if he does not, she is a bondwoman.” When he prepared to depart, he screened her, and she sat upon the rear of the camel. They therefore knew that he had married her. As they approached Madinah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ urged his mount forward, and we did likewise. Al-ʿAḍbāʾ stumbled, throwing both the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Ṣafiyyah. He immediately stood and covered her. The women looked on and said, “May Allah keep the Jewish woman far away!” The narrator said: I asked, “Abū Ḥamzah, did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ really fall?” He replied, “Yes, by Allah, he did.”"],[679,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached Khaybar at night. Whenever he reached a people at night, he would not attack them until morning. At daybreak, the Jewish inhabitants came out carrying their spades and baskets. When they saw him, they cried, “Muḥammad, by Allah! Muḥammad and the army!” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Khaybar is ruined. When we descend into the territory of a people, terrible is the morning of those who were warned.”"],[680,"Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ campaigned against Khaybar—or when he set out toward it—the people overlooked a valley and raised their voices, proclaiming: “Allah is the Greatest! Allah is the Greatest! There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Be gentle with yourselves. You are not calling upon one who is deaf or absent. You are calling upon One who hears and is near, and He is with you.” I was behind the mount of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he heard me saying, “There is no power or might except through Allah.” He said: “ʿAbd Allah ibn Qays.” I replied, “At your service, Messenger of Allah.” He asked: “Shall I direct you to a phrase from one of the treasures of Paradise?” I replied, “Yes, Messenger of Allah; may my father and mother be sacrificed for you.” He said: “‘There is no power or might except through Allah.’”"],[681,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ reported: ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, initially remained behind when the Prophet ﷺ went to Khaybar because he was suffering from inflamed eyes. He then said, “Should I remain behind the Prophet ﷺ?” and caught up with him. On the night before the conquest, the Prophet ﷺ said: “Tomorrow I shall certainly give the banner—or the banner will certainly be taken—by a man whom Allah and His Messenger love, and victory will be granted through him.” We all hoped to receive it, but then it was said, “Here is ʿAlī.” The Prophet ﷺ gave him the banner, and victory was granted through him."],[682,"Sahl ibn Saʿd reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on the day of Khaybar: “Tomorrow I shall give this banner to a man through whose hands Allah will grant victory. He loves Allah and His Messenger, and Allah and His Messenger love him.” The people spent the night discussing which of them would receive it. The following morning, they all went early to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, each hoping to be given the banner. He asked: “Where is ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib?” They replied, “Messenger of Allah, his eyes are hurting.” He said: “Send for him.” ʿAlī was brought, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spat into his eyes and supplicated for him. He was cured as though he had never suffered any pain, and the Prophet ﷺ gave him the banner. ʿAlī asked, “Messenger of Allah, shall I fight them until they become like us?” He replied: “Proceed calmly until you reach their territory. Then invite them to Islam and inform them of the rights of Allah that become binding upon them within it. By Allah, for Allah to guide one man through you is better for you than possessing the finest red camels.”"],[683,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ reported: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say on the day of Khaybar: “I shall give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, and whom Allah and His Messenger love.” We stretched forward, hoping to receive it. He said: “Call ʿAlī for me.” ʿAlī was brought while suffering from inflamed eyes. The Prophet ﷺ spat into his eyes and gave him the banner, and Allah granted victory through him."],[684,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Tomorrow I shall give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, and through whose hands Allah will grant victory.” ʿUmar said: “I never desired leadership except on that day. I made myself conspicuous, hoping that I would be called to receive it.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, gave him the banner and said: “Go forward and do not turn around until Allah grants you victory.” ʿAlī walked some distance and then stopped without turning around. He called aloud, “Messenger of Allah, for what cause shall I fight the people?” He replied: “Fight them until they testify that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah. Once they do so, their lives and property are protected from you except when lawfully due, and their reckoning rests with Allah.”"],[685,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ related a lengthy report containing the following: By Allah, we remained only three nights before setting out for Khaybar with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. My uncle ʿĀmir began chanting to the company: By Allah, had Allah not guided us, we would not be guided, nor would we give charity or pray. We can never dispense with Your favour; make our feet firm when we meet the enemy, and send tranquillity down upon us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Who is this?” He replied, “I am ʿĀmir.” He said: “May your Lord forgive you.” Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ specifically prayed for someone’s forgiveness in this manner, that person attained martyrdom. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, who was riding a camel, called out, “Prophet of Allah, if only you had allowed us to enjoy ʿĀmir’s company longer!” When we reached Khaybar, their champion Marḥab came out, strutting with his sword and chanting: Khaybar knows that I am Marḥab, fully armed, a tested warrior, when wars advance in blazing fury.\nMy uncle ʿĀmir came out against him and chanted: Khaybar knows that I am ʿĀmir, fully armed, a daring warrior. They exchanged two blows. Marḥab’s sword struck ʿĀmir’s shield, and ʿĀmir attempted to strike him from below. His sword rebounded upon himself and severed the artery in his forearm, causing his death. Salamah said: I came upon several Companions of the Prophet ﷺ saying, “ʿĀmir’s deeds have become void; he killed himself.” I went to the Prophet ﷺ weeping and asked, “Messenger of Allah, have ʿĀmir’s deeds become void?”\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Who said that?” I replied, “Some of your Companions.” He said: “Whoever said that has lied. Rather, he will receive a double reward.” He then sent me to fetch ʿAlī, who was suffering from inflamed eyes, saying: “I shall give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger—or whom Allah and His Messenger love.” I went to ʿAlī and led him by the hand because of his eyes until I brought him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He spat into his eyes, and they were cured. He then gave him the banner.\nMarḥab came out chanting: Khaybar knows that I am Marḥab, fully armed, a tested warrior, when wars advance in blazing fury. ʿAlī replied: I am the one whose mother named him Ḥaydarah, like a lion of the forests, fearsome to behold; I repay them measure for measure, in a mighty measure. ʿAlī struck Marḥab on the head and killed him, and victory was then achieved at his hands."],[686,"ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿUmar reported: The Prophet ﷺ contracted with the people of Khaybar to cultivate its land in return for half of all the fruit and crops it produced."],[687,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: When Khaybar was conquered, its Jewish inhabitants asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to allow them to remain and cultivate the land in return for half of its fruit and crops. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “We shall allow you to remain upon these terms for as long as we wish.” The report also states that the produce from half of Khaybar was distributed according to the allotted shares, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took one-fifth."],[688,"Ibn ʿUmar reported that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb expelled the Jews and Christians from the land of the Ḥijāz. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Khaybar, he intended to expel its Jewish inhabitants. Upon its conquest, the land belonged to Allah, His Messenger and the Muslims. When the Prophet ﷺ sought to remove them, they asked him to allow them to remain and perform its agricultural work in return for half the produce. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “We shall allow you to remain upon these terms for as long as we wish.” They remained there until ʿUmar expelled them to Taymāʾ and Jericho."],[689,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: When the people of Khaybar assaulted ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿUmar and dislocated his limbs, ʿUmar stood to deliver a sermon and said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ contracted with the Jewish inhabitants of Khaybar concerning their lands and said: ‘We shall allow you to remain for as long as Allah allows you to remain.’ “ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿUmar went to his property there and was attacked at night, leaving his hands and feet dislocated. We have no enemy there other than them. They are our enemy and the ones we suspect, and I have therefore decided to expel them.” When ʿUmar resolved upon this, a man from Banū Abī al-Ḥuqayq came to him and said, “Commander of the Faithful, will you expel us even though Muḥammad ﷺ allowed us to remain, contracted with us concerning the land and guaranteed that condition to us?” ʿUmar replied, “Did you imagine that I had forgotten the words of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: ‘What will your condition be when you are expelled from Khaybar and your young she-camel carries you onward night after night?’” The man said, “That was merely a jest from Abū al-Qāsim.” ʿUmar replied, “You have lied, enemy of Allah.” ʿUmar expelled them and compensated them for the value of their share of the produce with money, camels and goods such as camel saddles, ropes and other items."],[690,"Ibn ʿUmar reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fought the people of Khaybar until he drove them into their fortress and gained control of their land, crops and date palms. They concluded a treaty with him stipulating that they would leave Khaybar, taking only what their mounts could carry, while all their gold and silver would belong to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He stipulated that they must neither conceal nor hide anything; if they did, they would no longer enjoy covenantal protection. They nevertheless concealed a leather bag containing the wealth and jewellery of Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab, which he had brought to Khaybar when Banū al-Naḍīr were expelled. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab’s uncle: “What happened to Ḥuyayy’s leather bag that he brought from al-Naḍīr?” He replied, “Expenses and warfare consumed its contents.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “The time has been too short, and the wealth was too great for that.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ handed him over to al-Zubayr, who subjected him to physical pressure during interrogation. The man had previously entered a ruined building, so he said, “I saw Ḥuyayy moving around a ruin over here.” They searched the place and found the leather bag in the ruin. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ executed the two sons of Abū al-Ḥuqayq, one of whom was the husband of Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab. Because they had violated the agreement, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took their women and children captive and divided their property. He intended to expel them from Khaybar, but they said, “Muḥammad, allow us to remain upon this land to cultivate and maintain it.” Neither the Messenger of Allah ﷺ nor his Companions had sufficient workers to maintain the land, nor were they free to undertake the work themselves. He therefore allowed the people of Khaybar to remain on the condition that they receive half of every crop, date harvest and other produce, for as long as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wished. ʿAbd Allah ibn Rawāḥah used to visit them every year, estimate the harvest and make them liable for half. One year they complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the strictness of his assessment and attempted to bribe him. He said, “Enemies of Allah, would you feed me unlawful gain? I have come to you from the person dearest to me, while you are more hateful to me than an equivalent number of apes and swine. Yet neither my hatred of you nor my love for him will cause me to act unjustly toward you.” They replied, “It is through such justice that the heavens and earth remain established.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ noticed a greenish bruise around Ṣafiyyah’s eyes and asked: “What is this mark around your eyes?” She replied, “My head was resting in the lap of Ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq while I slept, and I dreamed that the moon fell into my lap. When I told him, he struck me and said, ‘Do you desire the king of Yathrib?’” She said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was initially the most hateful of people to me, for my husband, father and brother had been killed. He continued explaining the circumstances to me, saying, ‘Your father incited the Arabs against me and did such-and-such,’ until those feelings left my heart.”\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to give each of his wives eighty wasqs of dates and twenty wasqs of barley every year. During the caliphate of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, the people of Khaybar acted treacherously toward the Muslims and threw Ibn ʿUmar from the roof of a house. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb said, “Whoever possesses a share in Khaybar should attend so that we may distribute it among them.” He then divided it among its Muslim shareholders. Their leader said, “Do not expel us. Allow us to remain as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr allowed us to remain.”\nʿUmar said to him, “Do you imagine that I have forgotten the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ words to you: ‘What will your condition be when your mount carries you toward Syria, one day after another?’” ʿUmar then divided Khaybar among the people of al-Ḥudaybiyyah who had participated in its conquest."],[691,"ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿUmar reported: Al-Zubayr, al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad and I travelled to our properties at Khaybar to inspect them. When we arrived, each of us went separately to his own property. During the night, while I was asleep upon my bed, I was attacked and both my arms were dislocated at the elbows. The following morning, my two companions were summoned to help me. They came and asked, “Who did this to you?” I replied, “I do not know.” They reset my arms and then brought me to ʿUmar. He said, “This is the work of the Jewish inhabitants.” He then addressed the people, saying: “People, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ contracted with the Jewish inhabitants of Khaybar on the condition that we could expel them whenever we wished. They have attacked ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿUmar and dislocated his arms, as you have heard, in addition to their earlier attack upon an Anṣārī. We have no doubt that they were responsible, for we have no enemy there other than them. “Whoever owns property in Khaybar should go to it, for I am expelling its Jewish inhabitants.” He then expelled them."],[692,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: On the day of Khaybar, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ allotted two shares for a horse and one share for a foot soldier. Nāfiʿ explained: A man who possessed a horse received three shares—two for the horse and one for himself. A man without a horse received one share."],[693,"Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim reported: ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān and I went to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “You gave Banū al-Muṭṭalib a share from the one-fifth of Khaybar but gave us nothing, even though our degree of kinship to you is the same.” He replied: “Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib are one and the same.” Jubayr said: The Prophet ﷺ did not allot anything to Banū ʿAbd Shams or Banū Nawfal."],[694,"Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī reported: We came to the Prophet ﷺ after he had conquered Khaybar. He allotted shares to us, although he gave no share to anyone else who had not witnessed the conquest."],[695,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb said: “By the One in whose hand is my soul, were it not that the later generations would otherwise be left destitute with nothing, I would distribute every territory conquered during my rule just as the Prophet ﷺ distributed Khaybar. Instead, I leave it as a common treasury for them, from which they may receive their shares.”"],[696,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ghanm reported: We were stationed on the frontier at the city of Qinnasrīn with Shuraḥbīl ibn al-Simṭ. When he conquered it, he acquired sheep and cattle. He distributed some of them among us and placed the remainder with the general spoils. I later met Muʿādh ibn Jabal and told him about it. Muʿādh said: “We campaigned at Khaybar with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and acquired sheep there. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed some of them among us and placed the remainder with the general spoils.”"],[697,"Sahl ibn Abī Ḥathmah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ divided Khaybar into two halves: one half was reserved for emergencies and other needs, while the other half was distributed among the Muslims in eighteen shares."],[698,"The Mother of the Believers ʿĀ’ishah reported that Fāṭimah, peace be upon her, the daughter of the Prophet ﷺ, sent a request to Abū Bakr for her inheritance from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: from the property Allah had granted him as fayʾ in Madinah and Fadak, and from what remained of the one-fifth of Khaybar. Abū Bakr replied: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘We prophets are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity. The family of Muḥammad ﷺ may only receive their sustenance from this property.’ By Allah, I shall not alter any part of the charity of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from the condition in which it existed during his lifetime. I shall administer it exactly as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ administered it.” Abū Bakr therefore declined to give Fāṭimah any of that property. Fāṭimah became displeased with Abū Bakr concerning the matter and withdrew from speaking to him until she died. She lived for six months after the Prophet ﷺ. When she died, her husband ʿAlī buried her at night without informing Abū Bakr of her death, and ʿAlī performed the funeral prayer over her. ʿAlī held a position of respect among the people during Fāṭimah’s lifetime. After she died, he perceived a change in the people’s attitude toward him, so he sought reconciliation with Abū Bakr and wished to pledge allegiance to him. He had not given the pledge during those months. He sent word to Abū Bakr: “Come to us, but let no one accompany you,” because he disliked the prospect of ʿUmar being present. ʿUmar said, “By Allah, you must not enter upon them alone.” Abū Bakr replied, “What could they possibly do to me? By Allah, I shall go to them.” Abū Bakr entered upon them. ʿAlī pronounced the testimony of faith and said: “We recognise your virtue and what Allah has granted you. We do not begrudge you any good that Allah has brought to you. However, you acted independently of us concerning the matter of leadership, while we believed that our kinship with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entitled us to some consideration.” Abū Bakr’s eyes overflowed with tears. When Abū Bakr spoke, he said: “By the One in whose hand is my soul, maintaining ties with the relatives of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is dearer to me than maintaining ties with my own relatives. “As for the disagreement that arose between us concerning this property, I have not fallen short in pursuing what is right. I have not omitted anything that I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do with it; I have done exactly as he did.” ʿAlī said to Abū Bakr, “I shall meet you this afternoon to give the pledge of allegiance.” After Abū Bakr performed the noon prayer, he ascended the pulpit, pronounced the testimony of faith, mentioned ʿAlī’s situation and his delay in giving the pledge, and related the explanation ʿAlī had given him. He then asked Allah for forgiveness. ʿAlī then pronounced the testimony of faith, affirmed the great rights of Abū Bakr and explained that his conduct had not arisen from jealousy toward Abū Bakr or from any denial of the distinction Allah had granted him. Rather, he said: “We believed that we were entitled to some consideration in this matter, but it was decided without consulting us, and this caused us to feel hurt.” The Muslims were pleased by this and said, “You have acted correctly.” The Muslims then drew close to ʿAlī again after he returned to the recognised course of unity and reconciliation."],[699,"Abū Mūsā reported: News of the Prophet’s ﷺ migration reached us while we were in Yemen, so we set out to emigrate to him. I travelled with two of my brothers, of whom I was the youngest: one was Abū Burdah and the other Abū Ruhm. We were accompanied by either a little over fifty—or specifically fifty-three or fifty-two—men from my tribe. We boarded a ship, but it carried us to the Negus in Abyssinia. There we met Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib and remained with him until we all travelled together and reached the Prophet ﷺ at the time he conquered Khaybar. Some people used to say to us—the people of the ship—“We preceded you in emigration.” Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays, who had arrived with us, visited Ḥafṣah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ. Asmāʾ had been among those who emigrated to the Negus. ʿUmar entered upon Ḥafṣah while Asmāʾ was with her. When he saw Asmāʾ, he asked, “Who is this?” Ḥafṣah replied, “Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays.” ʿUmar asked, “Is this the Abyssinian woman—the woman who travelled by sea?” Asmāʾ replied, “Yes.” ʿUmar said, “We preceded you in emigration, so we have a greater claim to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than you do.” Asmāʾ became angry and replied, “No, by Allah! You were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: he fed those of you who were hungry and instructed those of you who were ignorant. Meanwhile, we lived in the distant and hostile land of Abyssinia—and we endured that for the sake of Allah and His Messenger ﷺ.” “By Allah, I shall neither eat nor drink until I tell the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what you have said. We were persecuted and lived in fear. I shall mention this to the Prophet ﷺ and ask him about it. By Allah, I shall neither lie, distort your words nor add anything to them.” When the Prophet ﷺ arrived, Asmāʾ said, “Prophet of Allah, ʿUmar said such-and-such.” He asked: “What did you say to him?” She related her response. The Prophet ﷺ said: “He has no greater claim to me than you do. He and his companions have one emigration, while you—the people of the ship—have two emigrations.” Asmāʾ said: “I saw Abū Mūsā and the people of the ship coming to me in successive groups to ask about this report. Nothing in the world brought them greater happiness or was more significant to them than what the Prophet ﷺ had said about them.” Abū Burdah reported that Asmāʾ said: “I saw Abū Mūsā repeatedly asking me to relate this report to him again.”"],[700,"Abū Hurayrah reported: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Khaybar after its conquest and said, “Messenger of Allah, allot me a share.” A man from the family of Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ said, “Do not allot him a share, Messenger of Allah.” Abū Hurayrah said, “This is the man who killed Ibn Qawqal.” The son of Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ replied, “How astonishing that a small wild animal should descend upon us from the summit of Ḍaʾn and reproach me for killing a Muslim man whom Allah honoured at my hands, while He did not disgrace me at his hands!” The narrator said: I do not know whether the Prophet ﷺ allotted Abū Hurayrah a share or not."],[701,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Abān ibn Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ as commander of a detachment from Madinah toward Najd. Abān and his companions arrived before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Khaybar after its conquest, with their horses’ girths made of palm fibre. Abān said, “Messenger of Allah, allot us shares.” Abū Hurayrah said, “Messenger of Allah, do not allot them shares.” Abān replied, “You again, little wild animal, descending upon us from the summit of Ḍāl!” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Sit down, Abān.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not allot them shares."],[702,"ʿUmayr, the freedman of Ābī al-Laḥm, reported: I witnessed Khaybar with my masters. They spoke to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning me and informed him that I was enslaved. He ordered that I be equipped with a sword, but it was so long for me that I dragged it along the ground. He then ordered that I be given some miscellaneous household goods. I also presented to him an incantation that I used when treating those afflicted with insanity. He instructed me to omit part of it and retain the remainder. Another narration states: “He gave me some miscellaneous household goods but did not allot me a formal share.”"],[703,"Abū Laylā reported: I witnessed the conquest of Khaybar with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When its people were defeated, we entered their encampment, and the Muslims took whatever ordinary goods they found. Before long, the cooking pots began to boil. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered that the pots be overturned. He then distributed the animals among us, allotting one sheep to every ten people."],[704,"Thābit ibn al-Ḥārith al-Anṣārī reported: On the day of Khaybar, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave something from the spoils to Sahlah bint ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAdī and to a daughter who had been born to her."],[705,"ʿAbd Allah ibn Mughaffal reported: We were besieging a fortress at Khaybar when someone threw down a leather bag containing fat. I sprang forward to take it, but then turned and saw the Prophet ﷺ, so I felt embarrassed before him. Another wording states: “I turned and saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiling.”"],[706,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: During our military expeditions, we would acquire honey and grapes. We would eat them without submitting them for inclusion among the collected spoils."],[707,"Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Mujālid reported: The people of the mosque sent me to Ibn Abī Awfā to ask him what the Prophet ﷺ had done with the food acquired at Khaybar. I went to him and asked, “Did he take one-fifth of it?” He replied, “No, it was too insignificant for that. Whenever one of us needed some of it, he took what he required.” Another wording states: “A man would come, take the amount sufficient for his needs and then leave.”"],[708,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: When Khaybar was conquered, we said, “Now we shall eat our fill of dates.”"],[709,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: We did not eat our fill until we conquered Khaybar."],[710,"Anas reported: When the Emigrants arrived in Madinah from Makkah, they possessed nothing, whereas the Anṣār owned the land and date-palm properties. The Anṣār therefore entered into an arrangement with them: they would give the Emigrants half the annual produce of their properties, while undertaking the labour and expenses themselves. The mother of Anas, known as Umm Sulaym, was also the mother of ʿAbd Allah ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, Anas’s maternal brother. She gave the Messenger of Allah ﷺ some date palms of hers, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave them to his freedwoman Umm Ayman, the mother of Usāmah ibn Zayd. Ibn Shihāb said: Anas ibn Mālik informed me that after the Prophet ﷺ had completed the campaign against the people of Khaybar and returned to Madinah, the Emigrants returned to the Anṣār the grants of produce that the Anṣār had previously given them. The Prophet ﷺ returned the date palms of Anas’s mother to her and gave Umm Ayman replacement palms from his own orchard. Ibn Shihāb said: Umm Ayman, the mother of Usāmah ibn Zayd, had been a young female servant belonging to ʿAbd Allah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, and she was originally from Abyssinia.\nWhen Āminah gave birth to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ after his father’s death, Umm Ayman cared for him and helped raise him until he grew up. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ later emancipated her and arranged her marriage to Zayd ibn Ḥārithah. She died five months after the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[711,"Anas reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ campaigned against Khaybar. We performed the dawn prayer near it while it was still dark. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ mounted, and Abū Ṭalḥah mounted with me riding behind him. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ rode rapidly through a narrow passage in Khaybar, and my knee touched his thigh. His lower garment shifted from his thigh, and I could see the whiteness of the Prophet’s ﷺ thigh. When he entered the settlement, he said: “Allah is the Greatest! Khaybar is ruined. When we descend into the territory of a people, terrible is the morning of those who were warned.” He repeated it three times. The inhabitants had gone out to their work and cried, “Muḥammad, by Allah!” ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz said: Some of our companions added, “Muḥammad and the army!” He said: We conquered Khaybar by force, and the captives were assembled. Diḥyah came and said, “Messenger of Allah, give me a captive woman.” He replied: “Go and take a captive woman.” Diḥyah selected Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy. A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “Prophet of Allah, have you given Diḥyah Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy, the leading woman of Banū Qurayẓah and Banū al-Naḍīr? She is suitable for no one but you.”\nThe Prophet ﷺ said: “Call him and have him bring her.” Diḥyah brought her. When the Prophet ﷺ saw her, he told him: “Take another captive woman instead.” The Prophet ﷺ emancipated Ṣafiyyah and married her. Thābit asked Anas, “Abū Ḥamzah, what did he give her as a bridal gift?” He replied, “Her own freedom: he emancipated her and married her.” While they were travelling, Umm Sulaym prepared Ṣafiyyah for the Prophet ﷺ and presented her to him during the night. The following morning, the Prophet ﷺ was a bridegroom and said: “Whoever has any food should bring it.”\nHe spread out a leather sheet. One man brought dried curds, another brought dates and another brought clarified butter. They mixed them together to prepare ḥays. This became the wedding feast of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[712,"Anas reported: The Prophet ﷺ took Ṣafiyyah among the captives, emancipated her and married her. Thābit asked Anas, “What did he give her as a bridal gift?” He replied, “He gave her her own freedom as the bridal gift by emancipating her.”"],[713,"Anas reported: We reached Khaybar, and when Allah granted the Prophet ﷺ victory over the fortress, he was informed of the beauty of Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab. Her husband had been killed, and she was a newlywed. The Prophet ﷺ selected her for himself and departed with her. When we reached the embankment of al-Ṣahbāʾ, she became ritually eligible for marriage, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ consummated the marriage with her. He then prepared ḥays upon a small leather sheet and told me: “Invite those around you.” That was his wedding feast for Ṣafiyyah. We then departed for Madinah. I saw the Prophet ﷺ make a screened space for her behind him using a cloak. He would sit beside his camel and offer his knee, and Ṣafiyyah would place her foot upon it in order to mount."],[714,"Anas reported: Ṣafiyyah initially fell to Diḥyah as part of his allotted share. The people began praising her before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying, “We have never seen anyone among the captives like her.” The Prophet ﷺ sent for Diḥyah and compensated him with what he desired. He then entrusted Ṣafiyyah to my mother and said: “Prepare her.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then departed from Khaybar. When he had left Khaybar behind him, he stopped and pitched a tent for her. The following morning, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever possesses any surplus provisions should bring them to us.” The men began bringing their surplus dates and roasted grain until they had assembled a considerable quantity and made ḥays from it. They ate the ḥays and drank rainwater from pools beside them. Anas said: That was the wedding feast the Messenger of Allah ﷺ held for Ṣafiyyah. We continued travelling until we saw the walls of Madinah. We became eager to reach it and urged our mounts forward. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ also urged his mount onward, with Ṣafiyyah seated behind him. The mount of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stumbled, throwing both him and Ṣafiyyah to the ground. None of the people looked toward either of them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and covered Ṣafiyyah. When we approached him, he said: “We have suffered no harm.” When we entered Madinah, the young women belonging to his wives came out to look at Ṣafiyyah and expressed satisfaction at hearing that she had fallen."],[715,"Abū Hurayrah reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ consummated his marriage with Ṣafiyyah, Abū Ayyūb spent the night guarding the Prophet’s ﷺ doorway. When morning came and he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he proclaimed Allah’s greatness. Abū Ayyūb was carrying his sword. He said, “Messenger of Allah, she was a young woman who had only recently been married, and her father, brother and husband had been killed. I therefore did not feel that you were safe from her.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed and spoke words of goodness to him."],[716,"Anas reported: Ṣafiyyah learned that Ḥafṣah had called her “the daughter of a Jew.” The Prophet ﷺ entered upon Ṣafiyyah while she was weeping and asked: “What has made you cry?” She replied, “Ḥafṣah said that I am the daughter of a Jew.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “You are indeed descended from a prophet, your uncle was a prophet, and you are married to a prophet. What, then, does she have to boast about over you?” He then said: “Be mindful of Allah, Ḥafṣah.”"],[717,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entrusted the land of Khaybar to its cultivators in return for half of its fruit and crops. He used to give each of his wives one hundred wasqs every year: eighty wasqs of dates and twenty wasqs of barley. When ʿUmar became caliph, he divided Khaybar. He gave the wives of the Prophet ﷺ a choice: either portions of land and water rights would be assigned to them, or their annual quantities of produce would continue to be guaranteed. They made different choices. Some selected land and water rights, while others selected the guaranteed annual produce. ʿĀ’ishah and Ḥafṣah were among those who selected land and water rights."],[718,"Abū Hurayrah reported: When Khaybar was conquered, the Prophet ﷺ was presented with a sheep containing poison. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Gather before me all the Jewish people who are present here.” They were assembled before him. He said: “I shall ask you about something. Will you answer me truthfully?” They replied, “Yes.” The Prophet ﷺ asked them: “Who is your forefather?” They named a certain man. He said: “You have lied. Rather, your forefather is so-and-so.” They replied, “You have spoken the truth.” He asked: “Will you answer me truthfully if I ask you about something else?” They replied, “Yes, Abū al-Qāsim. If we lie, you will recognise our lie just as you recognised it concerning our forefather.” He asked them: “Who are the inhabitants of the Fire?” They replied, “We shall remain in it for only a short time, after which you will take our place there.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Remain there despised! By Allah, we shall never take your place in it.” He then asked: “Will you answer me truthfully if I ask you about something?” They replied, “Yes, Abū al-Qāsim.” He asked: “Did you place poison in this sheep?” They replied, “Yes.” He asked: “What caused you to do that?” They replied, “We intended that, if you were a liar, we would be rid of you; but if you were truly a prophet, it would not harm you.”"],[719,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that a Jewish woman brought the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a poisoned sheep, and he ate some of it. The woman was brought before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he questioned her about it. She said, “I intended to kill you.” He replied: “Allah would never grant you power to accomplish that”—or he said: “power over me.” The Companions asked, “Shall we kill her?” He replied: “No.” Anas said: I continued to recognise the effects of the poison upon the palate of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[720,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported that a Jewish woman presented the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a poisoned sheep. He summoned her and asked: “What caused you to do what you did?” She replied, “I wished—or intended—that if you were a prophet, Allah would inform you of it; but if you were not a prophet, I would rid the people of you.” Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ experienced any effect from the poison, he underwent cupping. On one journey, after entering the state of consecration, he felt some of its effects and underwent cupping."],[721,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that during the illness in which he died, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say: “ʿĀ’ishah, I continue to feel the pain caused by the food I ate at Khaybar. Now is the time when I feel my aorta being severed by that poison.”"],[722,"Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd reported: I saw the scar of a blow upon Salamah’s leg and asked, “Abū Muslim, what caused this wound?” He replied, “This blow struck me on the day of Khaybar. The people said, ‘Salamah has been wounded.’ “I went to the Prophet ﷺ, and he blew lightly upon the wound three times. I have not suffered any pain from it up to this very moment.”"],[723,"Abū Hurayrah reported: We witnessed Khaybar. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning a man in his company who claimed to be a Muslim: “This man is among the people of the Fire.” When battle began, the man fought with exceptional intensity until he sustained numerous wounds. Some of the people nearly began to doubt the Prophet’s statement. The man could no longer endure the pain of his wounds, so he reached into his quiver, removed some arrows and cut his own throat with them. Several Muslims hurried to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “Messenger of Allah, Allah has confirmed the truth of your statement. So-and-so committed suicide and killed himself.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Stand, so-and-so, and announce that none but a believer will enter Paradise. Allah may indeed support this religion through a wicked man.”"],[724,"Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq stated: About twenty men were martyred at Khaybar:\n1- Rabīʿah ibn Akthum ibn Sakhbarah al-Asadī    2- Thaqīf ibn ʿAmr\n3- Rifāʿah ibn Masrūḥ    4- ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Hubayb ibn Uhayb ibn Suḥaym ibn Ghayrah\n5- Bishr ibn al-Barāʾ ibn Maʿrūr (cause of martyrdom: he sat with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and ate the poisoned sheep)\n6- Fuḍayl ibn al-Nuʿmān    7- Masʿūd ibn Saʿd ibn Qays ibn Khalad al-Zuraqī\n8- Maḥmūd ibn Maslamah al-Ashhalī    9- Abū Ḍayyāḥ ibn Thābit ibn al-Nuʿmān al-Qamarī\n10- al-Ḥārith ibn Ḥāṭib    11- ʿUrwah ibn Murrah ibn Surāqah\n12- Aws ibn Qāʾid    13- Unayf ibn Ḥabīb\n14- Thābit ibn Athlah    15- Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā ibn Mulayl ibn Ḍamrah\n16- ʿUmārah ibn ʿUqbah (martyred by a spear)    17- ʿĀmir ibn al-Akwaʿ (his own sword struck his neck)\n18- Masʿūd ibn Rabīʿah    19- Aws ibn Qatādah    20- al-Aswad the shepherd of Aslam\nHe was from the people of Khaybar."],[725,"Ibn Isḥāq says: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was besieging one of the fortresses of Khaybar, al-Aswad the shepherd came to him ﷺ with his sheep. He was employed to graze the sheep of a Jew. He said to him ﷺ: “O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, make me a Muslim.” He ﷺ made him a Muslim. In making anyone a Muslim, he ﷺ did not consider whether the person was low or high; rather he brought everyone into Islam. al-Aswad said: “O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, what shall I do with these sheep?” He said: “Strike them and drive them away; they will go to their owner by themselves.” al-Aswad took a handful of pebbles, threw them at the sheep and drove them toward the fortress. The sheep went straight into the fortress. Then al-Aswad took part in jihad with the Muslims against that same fortress, and a stone from the fortress struck al-Aswad’s head so hard that he was martyred from the blow. The people brought his body to him ﷺ and covered the body with a cloth. He ﷺ came to his body with a few Companions, then he ﷺ turned his face away from him. One Companion said: “O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, why did you turn your face away from him?” He said: “A ḥūr, who is his wife, is sitting by him.” The narrator says that al-Aswad had not even prayed a single prayer. It is narrated that when a martyr falls, his wife from among the ḥūr wipes the dust from his face and says: “May Allah cover with dust the one who covered you with dust, and may Allah kill the one who killed you.”"],[726,"Anas reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Khaybar, al-Ḥajjāj ibn ʿIlāṭ said, “O Messenger of Allah, I have wealth and family in Makkah, and I wish to go to them. Will I be free of blame if I speak against you or say something?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ permitted him to say whatever was necessary. When he arrived, he went to his wife and said, “Gather everything you have for me. I intend to purchase some of the spoils taken from Muḥammad and his Companions, for they have been defeated and their property seized.” This news spread throughout Makkah. The Muslims became downcast, while the polytheists openly displayed joy and delight. When the news reached al-ʿAbbās, he was so distressed that he became unable to stand. Maʿmar said: ʿUthmān al-Jazarī informed me, from Miqsam, who said: Al-ʿAbbās took one of his sons, named Qutham, lay down and placed him upon his chest, while saying: My beloved Qutham, who resembles the man of the prominent nose— the Prophet, the possessor of blessings— whatever anyone may claim. Thābit reported from Anas: Al-ʿAbbās then sent one of his servants to al-Ḥajjāj ibn ʿIlāṭ, saying, “Woe to you! What news have you brought, and what is this you are saying? What Allah has promised is better than the news you have brought.” Al-Ḥajjāj ibn ʿIlāṭ told the servant, “Convey my greetings of peace to Abū al-Faḍl and tell him to arrange a private room for me to visit him, for the true news is something that will delight him.” When the servant reached the door of the house, he called out, “Receive glad tidings, O Abū al-Faḍl!” Al-ʿAbbās leapt up joyfully and kissed him between the eyes. The servant told him what al-Ḥajjāj had said, so al-ʿAbbās set him free.\nAl-Ḥajjāj then came to him and informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had conquered Khaybar, captured its people’s wealth, and distributed their property according to the shares ordained by Allah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had chosen Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy for himself and offered her the choice of being set free and becoming his wife or returning to her people. She chose to be set free and become his wife. He continued, “However, I came here because I have property in Makkah which I wished to gather and take away. I sought the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ permission, and he allowed me to say whatever was necessary. Keep my true news secret for three days; then disclose whatever you wish.”\nHis wife gathered all the jewellery and possessions she had and handed them over to him. He then departed swiftly with them. After three days, al-ʿAbbās went to al-Ḥajjāj’s wife and asked, “What has your husband done?” She informed him that he had departed on such-and-such a day. She then said, “May Allah not cause you grief, O Abū al-Faḍl. We were deeply saddened by the news that reached you.” He replied, “Indeed, may Allah not cause me grief! By Allah’s praise, nothing occurred except what we desired: Allah granted His Messenger ﷺ victory over Khaybar, its wealth was distributed according to Allah’s appointed shares, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ chose Ṣafiyyah for himself. If you have any need of your husband, then go and join him.”\nShe said, “By Allah, I believe you are telling the truth.” He replied, “I am indeed truthful, and the matter is exactly as I have told you.” Al-ʿAbbās then went to the gathering places of Quraysh. Whenever he passed them, they would say mockingly, “May nothing but good befall you, O Abū al-Faḍl!” He told them, “Nothing but good has befallen me, praise be to Allah! Al-Ḥajjāj ibn ʿIlāṭ informed me that Allah granted His Messenger ﷺ victory over Khaybar, its wealth was distributed according to Allah’s appointed shares, and he chose Ṣafiyyah for himself. Al-Ḥajjāj asked me to conceal the news for three days. He came only to collect his wealth and whatever property he had here, and then depart.”\nThus Allah transferred the sorrow that had afflicted the Muslims to the polytheists. The Muslims who had withdrawn sorrowfully into their homes came out and went to al-ʿAbbās, who told them the news. The Muslims rejoiced, while the grief, anger and sorrow fell upon the polytheists."],[727,"Ibn Isḥāq says: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished with the battle of Khaybar, Allah cast fear of the Messenger ﷺ into the hearts of the people of Fadak as well, and they sent their messenger to the Messenger ﷺ so that he would make peace with them for half of the produce. He ﷺ accepted. He ﷺ was at that time at Khaybar, or between Khaybar and Madinah, or had returned to Madinah. And since Fadak was conquered without fighting and without a military expedition, it was for that reason a special property of his ﷺ.\nFadak is located in eastern Khaybar and is today called al-Ḥāʾiṭ. It is an inhabited area where dates and crops are abundant."],[728,"Abū Hurayrah reported: We went with the Prophet ﷺ to Khaybar, and Allah granted us victory. We did not take gold or silver as spoils; rather, we took goods, food and clothing. We then proceeded to the valley. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had with him a servant who had been given to him by a man from Judhām named Rifāʿah ibn Zayd, from Banū al-Ḍubayb. When we stopped in the valley, the servant of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up to unload his saddle. He was struck by an arrow, which caused his death. We said, “May martyrdom be a blessing for him, O Messenger of Allah!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied, “No! By Him in Whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, the cloak he took from the spoils on the day of Khaybar before it was included in the distribution is blazing as fire upon him.” The people became alarmed. A man then brought one or two leather sandal straps and said, “O Messenger of Allah, I took these on the day of Khaybar.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “A strap of fire—or two straps of fire.”"],[729,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb reported: On the day of Khaybar, a group of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ came forward, saying, “So-and-so is a martyr, and so-and-so is a martyr,” until they passed by a man and said, “So-and-so is a martyr.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “No. I saw him in the Fire because of a cloak—or an outer garment—that he had secretly taken from the spoils.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said, “O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, go and proclaim among the people that none shall enter Paradise except the believers.” ʿUmar said: So I went out and proclaimed, “Indeed, none shall enter Paradise except the believers.”"],[730,"After Wādī al-Qurā, in Jumādā al-Ākhirah, 6 AH, took place the expedition of Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, toward Ḥisma. Diḥyah ibn Khalīfah al-Kalbī, may Allah be pleased with him, was coming back from Caesar, who had hosted him and given him a robe of honor. At Ḥisma he met Hunayd ibn ʿĀriḍ and his son ʿĀriḍ ibn Hunayd with a few men of the tribe of Judhām. They robbed Diḥyah and left him with nothing but old clothes. When some men of Banū Ḍubayb heard this, they set out toward them and took away Diḥyah’s belongings. Diḥyah came to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him of it. He sent Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, with 500 men and sent Diḥyah along with them. Zayd would travel by night and hide by day, and with them was a guide from the tribe of Banū ʿUdhrah. He brought them, and at dawn they attacked that people. They plundered them, shed blood and inflicted harm; they also killed Hunayd and his son, and seized livestock, property and women. They took captive one thousand camels, five thousand sheep and goats, and a hundred women and children. Zayd ibn Rifāʿah al-Judhāmī went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a group of his people and recited the decree that he ﷺ had written for him and his people in those nights when he had come to him. He accepted Islam and said: “O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, do not make the lawful unlawful for us, nor make the unlawful lawful for us.” He said: “What shall I do with those who were killed?” Abū Yazīd ibn ʿAmr said: “O Messenger of Allah, release whoever is alive, and whoever has been killed is under these two feet of mine.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Abū Zayd has spoken the truth.” He ﷺ sent ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, along with those men to Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, and ordered that they give them back their people and their property, and the women. ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, set out and met Rāfiʿ ibn Makīth al-Juhanī, the bearer of the good news of victory sent by Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, who was riding a she-camel belonging to that people. ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, returned that she-camel too to the same people. He met Zayd at al-Faḥlatayn, which is between Madinah and Dhū al-Marwah, and conveyed to him the order of the Messenger ﷺ, and they returned everything they had taken from the people."],[731,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ reported: We launched an expedition against Fazārah under the command of Abū Bakr, whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had appointed over us. When we were an hour’s journey from their water, Abū Bakr ordered us to stop and rest during the latter part of the night. He then launched the attack, reached the water and killed some of those present while taking others captive. I saw a group of people that included women and children, and I feared they would reach the mountain before me. I therefore shot an arrow between them and the mountain. When they saw the arrow, they stopped, and I drove them ahead of me. Among them was a woman from Banū Fazārah wearing a piece of tanned leather—he explained that al-qishʿ means a leather mat—and with her was a daughter who was among the most beautiful of the Arabs. I drove them onward until I brought them to Abū Bakr, and he awarded me her daughter as an additional share. We returned to Madinah, and I had not uncovered her garment. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ met me in the marketplace and said, “O Salamah, give me the woman.” I replied, “O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, she has greatly pleased me, but I have not uncovered her garment.” The following day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met me again in the marketplace and said, “O Salamah, give me the woman—may Allah bless your father!” I replied, “She is yours, O Messenger of Allah. By Allah, I have not uncovered her garment.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent her to the people of Makkah and used her to ransom several Muslims who had been held captive there."],[732,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed Abū Bakr as our commander, and we launched an expedition against a group of polytheists. We attacked them by night and fought them. Our battle cry that night was: “Put them to death! Put them to death!” Salamah said: That night, I personally killed seven men from different households of the polytheists."],[733,"In Ramaḍān, 7 AH, took place the expedition of Ghālib ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allah be pleased with him, toward al-Mayfaʿah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Ghālib ibn ʿAbdullāh to Banū al-Awāl and Banū ʿAbd ibn Thaʿlabah, who were at al-Mayfaʿah. This area is from Baṭn Nakhl toward Naqrah, on that side in the region of Najd. The distance between it and Madinah is eight barīds (96 miles). He sent them with 130 men; with them was Yasār, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They attacked everyone suddenly, fell upon the midst of their houses, killed whoever came before them, drove the camels and sheep and brought them to Madinah, and they did not take anyone captive. In this same expedition Usāmah ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with them both, killed a man who had said lā ilāha illā Allāh. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Why did you not split open his heart so that you could know whether he was truthful or lying?” Usāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: “I will not fight anyone who testifies lā ilāha illā Allāh.”\nThis is mentioned in the following hadith."],[734,"Usāmah ibn Zayd reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us against al-Ḥuraqah. We attacked the people in the morning and defeated them. A man from the Anṣār and I caught up with one of their men. When we closed in upon him, he said, “There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah.” The Anṣārī held back, but I stabbed the man with my spear and killed him. When we returned, news of this reached the Prophet ﷺ. He said, “O Usāmah, did you kill him after he said: ‘There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah’?” I replied, “He said it only to protect himself.” He continued repeating this question until I wished that I had not embraced Islam before that day."],[735,"It is reported from Jundab ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Bajalī: During the turmoil of Ibn al-Zubayr, he sent a message to ʿAsʿas ibn Salāmah saying, “Gather for me a group of your brothers so that I may speak to them.” He sent a messenger to them, and when they had assembled, Jundab came wearing a yellow hooded cloak. He said, “Continue discussing what you were discussing,” until it became his turn to speak. When it became his turn, he removed the hood from his head and said: I have come to you, though I did not intend to relate to you something concerning your Prophet. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a detachment of Muslims against a group of polytheists, and they met in battle. There was a man among the polytheists who would single out any Muslim he wished, attack him and kill him. One of the Muslims waited for an opportunity to catch him unawares—and we used to say that this Muslim was Usāmah ibn Zayd. When he raised his sword over the polytheist, the man declared, “There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah,” but he killed him. When the bearer of glad tidings came to the Prophet ﷺ, the Prophet questioned him, and he related what had happened, including what that man had done. The Prophet ﷺ summoned the man and asked him, “Why did you kill him?” He replied, “O Messenger of Allah, he inflicted great harm upon the Muslims and killed so-and-so and so-and-so,” naming several people. “I attacked him, and when he saw the sword, he said: ‘There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah!’” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked, “Did you kill him?” He replied, “Yes.” He said, “Then what will you do with ‘There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah’ when it comes on the Day of Resurrection?” He said, “O Messenger of Allah, seek forgiveness for me.” He repeated, “What will you do with ‘There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah’ when it comes on the Day of Resurrection?” He continued saying nothing more to him than: “What will you do with ‘There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah’ when it comes on the Day of Resurrection?”"],[736,"It is reported from Ibn ʿAbbās: The verse, “O you who believe, obey Allah, obey the Messenger and those in authority among you” [al-Nisāʾ 4:59], was revealed concerning ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥudhāfah al-Sahmī, whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent in command of an expedition."],[737,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib reported: The Prophet ﷺ sent out an expedition and appointed a man from the Anṣār as its commander, ordering the men to obey him. The commander became angry and asked, “Did the Prophet ﷺ not command you to obey me?” They replied, “Yes.” He said, “Gather firewood for me.” They gathered it, and he said, “Light a fire.” They lit it, and he then commanded, “Enter it.” They were about to do so, but then began restraining one another and saying, “We fled to the Prophet ﷺ to escape the Fire.” They remained like that until the fire died down and the commander’s anger subsided. When this reached the Prophet ﷺ, he said, “Had they entered it, they would not have emerged from it until the Day of Resurrection. Obedience is required only in what is right.”"],[738,"Abū Saʿīd reported: ʿAlqamah ibn Mujazziz was placed in command of an expedition in which I participated. When he reached the destination of our campaign—or while we were somewhere along the road—he gave a portion of the army permission to depart and appointed ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥudhāfah ibn Qays al-Sahmī as their commander. He was one of the participants at Badr and possessed a playful nature—that is, he liked to joke. I was among those who returned with him. We stopped at a place along the road, and the people lit a fire, either to prepare some food or to warm themselves. He asked them, “Do you not owe me hearing and obedience?” They replied, “Yes.” He asked, “Then whatever I command you to do, will you do it?” They replied, “Yes.” He said, “By the obedience you owe me, I command you to leap into this fire.” Some of them arose, while others held them back. When he thought they were about to leap into it, he said, “Restrain yourselves; I was only joking with you.” When they returned, they mentioned this to the Prophet ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “If any of those placed in authority over you commands you to disobey Allah, do not obey him.”"],[739,"In Shaʿbān, 7 AH, Sayyidunā ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with him, set out on the expedition toward Turabah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Sayyidunā ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with him, with 30 men toward that branch of the tribe of Hawāzin at Turabah, in the surroundings of al-ʿAbalāʾ, four nights’ distance from Makkah, on the highway of Thanāʾ Najrān. They set out, and with them was a guide from Banū Hilāl. They traveled by night and hid by day. Hawāzin learned of it and fled. Sayyidunā ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with him, came to their settlement but found no one there. He returned and came back to Madinah."],[740,"Ibn Shihāb narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Sayyidunā ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah, may Allah be pleased with him, with thirty horsemen to crush Yusayr ibn Rizām the Jew. Sayyidunā ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays, may Allah be pleased with him, was also part of this force. The mujāhidīn reached their target in Khaybar. News had reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the Jew had gathered Banū Ghaṭafān to fight the Muslims. The Muslims went to Ibn Rizām. They told him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had sent him a message that if he accepted obedience, he would be made governor of Khaybar. Discussion and talk continued for quite some time. In the end the Jew agreed to go to Madinah. He prepared thirty of his trusted men to go with him. With each man a Muslim also rode on the mount. When this caravan reached the area of Thubār, six miles from Khaybar, the king of Khaybar regretted his decision. With evil intent he tried to seize the sword of Sayyidunā ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah, may Allah be pleased with him. Sayyidunā ʿAbdullāh became aware of his intentions and he made his camel run. Sayyidunā ʿAbdullāh spurred his mount, seized Yusayr and struck with the sword, cutting off one of his legs. The Jew had a staff. He struck with the staff and wounded Sayyidunā ʿAbdullāh’s face severely. All the Muslims attacked their own disbelieving companions and put them to death. Only one Jew succeeded in escaping. The Muslims were all safe. The caravan came into the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They informed him of the whole situation. He applied his blessed saliva to the wounded face of Sayyidunā ʿAbdullāh. His pain ended forever and the wound also healed."],[741,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Fuḍayl narrates from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Sayyidunā Bashīr ibn Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, with three men toward Banū Murrah at Fadak. They set out and met some shepherds of sheep. He ﷺ asked: “Where are the people?” They told him: “They are in their valley.” In those days it was the cold season, and they did not come to the water sources. They drove the camels and sheep and returned toward Madinah. A man came out shouting and informed them, and their pursuit overtook him at night. During the night they began shooting arrows at them until the arrows of Bashīr’s companions ran out. In the morning Banū Murrah attacked them and killed some of Bashīr’s companions; some of them fled, and Sayyidunā Bashīr, may Allah be pleased with him, fought fiercely until his ankle was broken. Some said he was martyred. The people took their camels and sheep and went back. Sayyidunā Bashīr ibn Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, lay among the slain until, when evening came, someone lifted him and brought him to Fadak. He stayed a few days with that Jew in Fadak, then when his wounds healed, he returned to Madinah."],[742,"In Shawwāl, 7 AH, took place the expedition of Bashīr ibn Saʿd al-Anṣārī toward Yumn and Jabār. News reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn had taken a promise from a group of Ghaṭafān who were in al-Jinab that he would set out with them against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called Bashīr ibn Saʿd, tied a banner for him and sent him with 300 men. They would travel all night and hide all day until they came to Yumn and Jabār, which is on the side of al-Jinab, facing Jinab, Salaḥ, Khaybar and Wādī al-Qurā. They stopped at Salāḥ and came near that people. Bashīr obtained many camels from them, and the shepherds fled. They frightened the gathering and everyone fled and went to the top of the mountain. Bashīr set out with his companions in search of them and came to their houses but found no one. They returned with the camels; only two men were found, whom they took prisoner during the day and brought to the Prophet ﷺ. Both accepted Islam, so he sent them back."],[743,"Abū Mūsā reported: We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a military expedition. We were six men sharing one camel, which we rode in turns. Our feet became wounded. Both of my feet were torn and my toenails fell off. We used to wrap pieces of cloth around our feet, so it was called the Expedition of Dhāt al-Riqāʿ—“the Expedition of the Patches”—because of the pieces of cloth we bound around our feet. Abū Burdah said: Abū Mūsā narrated this report, but afterward he disliked having done so. It was as though he disliked disclosing any of his own deeds.\nAbū Usāmah said: Someone other than Burayd added for me: “Allah will reward him for it.”"],[744,"It is reported from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh: He took part with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in an expedition toward Najd. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began the return journey, Jābir returned with him. The time of the midday rest overtook them in a valley containing many thorny trees. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dismounted, and the people dispersed among the thorny trees, seeking shade beneath them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stopped beneath an acacia tree and hung his sword from it. Jābir said: We fell asleep for a while, and then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called us. We came to him and found a Bedouin sitting beside him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “This man drew my sword while I was asleep. I awoke to find it unsheathed in his hand. He asked me, ‘Who will protect you from me?’ I replied, ‘Allah.’ Here he is now, sitting before you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not punish him."],[745,"Jābir reported: We travelled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until we reached Dhāt al-Riqāʿ. Whenever we came upon a tree that provided plentiful shade, we would leave it for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. A man from among the polytheists came while the sword of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was hanging from a tree. He took the Prophet’s ﷺ sword, drew it from its sheath and asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, “Do you fear me?” He replied, “No.” The man asked, “Then who will protect you from me?” He replied, “Allah will protect me from you.” The Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ then threatened the man, so he sheathed the sword and hung it back on the tree. The call to prayer was then made. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed two units with one group, after which they withdrew, and he prayed two units with the other group. Thus, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed four units of prayer, while each group performed two."],[746,"Ṣāliḥ ibn Khawwāt reported from one who performed the Prayer of Fear with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of Dhāt al-Riqāʿ: One group formed rows with him while another faced the enemy. He prayed one unit with the group accompanying him and then remained standing while they completed the prayer by themselves. They then withdrew and formed ranks facing the enemy. The other group came, and he prayed with them the remaining unit of his prayer. He then remained seated while they completed their prayer by themselves, after which he concluded the prayer with them by giving the salutation."],[747,"It is reported from Sahl ibn Abī Ḥathmah: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led his Companions in the Prayer of Fear, arranging them in two rows behind him. He prayed one unit with those immediately behind him and then remained standing while they completed one unit by themselves. They then moved forward, and those who had previously been in front withdrew to the rear. He prayed one unit with them and then remained seated while those who had remained behind completed one unit by themselves. He then concluded the prayer with the salutation."],[748,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar reported: I participated with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in an expedition toward Najd. We came face-to-face with the enemy and formed ranks against them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood to lead us in prayer. One group stood and prayed with him, while another group faced the enemy. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ bowed with those accompanying him and made two prostrations. They then withdrew to the position of the group that had not yet prayed, and that group came forward. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed one unit with them and made two prostrations, after which he concluded his prayer with the salutation. Each of them then stood and completed one unit individually, bowing once and making two prostrations."],[749,"It is reported from Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam: He asked Abū Hurayrah, “Did you perform the Prayer of Fear with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” Abū Hurayrah replied, “Yes.” He asked, “When?” He replied, “During the year of the expedition to Najd.”"],[750,"Nāfiʿ says that this took place in Dhū al-Qaʿdah, 7 AH."],[751,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā reported: We were with the Prophet ﷺ when he performed ʿumrah. He circumambulated the Kaʿbah, and we circumambulated with him. He prayed, and we prayed with him. He walked between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah, while we shielded him from the people of Makkah so that no one could harm him."],[752,"It is reported from Ibn ʿUmar: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out intending to perform ʿumrah, but the disbelievers of Quraysh prevented him from reaching the Sacred House. He slaughtered his sacrificial animals and shaved his head at al-Ḥudaybiyah. He concluded a treaty with them permitting him to perform ʿumrah the following year, provided that he carried no weapons against them except swords and remained in Makkah only for as long as they permitted. The following year he performed ʿumrah and entered Makkah according to the terms of the treaty. After he had remained there for three days, they ordered him to leave, so he departed."],[753,"Mujāhid reported: ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr and I entered the mosque and found ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar sitting beside the apartment of ʿĀ’ishah. Some people were performing the forenoon prayer in the mosque. We asked him about their prayer, and he said, “It is an innovation.” ʿUrwah then asked him, “How many times did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform ʿumrah?” He replied, “Four times, one of them in Rajab.” We disliked contradicting him. We heard ʿĀ’ishah, the Mother of the Believers, brushing her teeth in her apartment. ʿUrwah called, “O Mother, O Mother of the Believers, do you not hear what Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān is saying?” She asked, “What is he saying?” He replied, “He says that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed four ʿumrahs, one of them in Rajab.” She said, “May Allah have mercy upon Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never performed an ʿumrah without him being present, yet he never performed ʿumrah in Rajab.”"],[754,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions arrived in Makkah after the fever of Yathrib had weakened them. The polytheists said, “Tomorrow, a people weakened by fever and severely afflicted by it will come to you.” They therefore sat in the area facing al-Ḥijr. The Prophet ﷺ instructed his Companions to walk briskly during three circuits and to walk normally between the two corners so that the polytheists could witness their strength. The polytheists said, “Are these the people whom you claimed had been weakened by fever? They are stronger than such-and-such!” Ibn ʿAbbās said: Nothing prevented the Prophet ﷺ from ordering them to walk briskly during every circuit except his concern to spare them hardship."],[755,"Anas reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed four ʿumrahs, all of them in Dhū al-Qaʿdah except the one associated with his pilgrimage: the ʿumrah of al-Ḥudaybiyah in Dhū al-Qaʿdah, the ʿumrah of the following year in Dhū al-Qaʿdah, the ʿumrah from al-Jiʿrānah—where he distributed the spoils of Ḥunayn—in Dhū al-Qaʿdah, and the ʿumrah associated with his pilgrimage."],[756,"Yazīd ibn al-Aṣamm reported: Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith informed me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married her while he was not in the state of consecration. He added: She was both my maternal aunt and the maternal aunt of Ibn ʿAbbās."],[757,"Maymūnah reported: The Prophet ﷺ married me at Sarif while neither of us was in the state of consecration."],[758,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: The Prophet ﷺ married Maymūnah while he was in the state of consecration."],[759,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: The Prophet ﷺ married Maymūnah while he was in the state of consecration, consummated the marriage with her after leaving that state, and she died at Sarif."],[760,"Ibn Isḥāq, may Allah have mercy on him, says: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed three days in Makkah. When the third day came, Quraysh sent Ḥuwayṭib ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Abī Qays ibn ʿAbd Wudd ibn Naṣr ibn Mālik ibn Ḥisl with a group of Quraysh to him ﷺ, saying that his period of stay was now complete, so he should now leave. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “There is no harm to you. We would hold the wedding here and invite the people to a meal, and you would also take part in it.” Quraysh said: “We do not want your invitation.” Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ himself set out with the Companions, and left his freed slave Abū Rāfiʿ with Sayyidah Maymūnah, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with her. So Abū Rāfiʿ brought her and met the Messenger ﷺ at the place Sarif, and there he ﷺ consummated the marriage, and he returned to Madinah in the month of Dhū al-Ḥijjah.\nIbn Hishām says that concerning this very event Allah the Exalted revealed this verse: “Indeed, Allah has fulfilled for His Messenger the vision in truth: you will surely enter al-Masjid al-Ḥarām, if Allah wills, in safety, with your heads shaved and hair cut short, not fearing. So He knew what you did not know, and He placed before that a near conquest.” (Sūrat al-Fatḥ 27)"],[761,"Al-Barāʾ reported: When the Prophet ﷺ set out to perform ʿumrah in Dhū al-Qaʿdah, the people of Makkah refused to allow him to enter until he concluded an agreement with them permitting him to remain there for three days. When they drew up the document, they wrote: “This is what Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah, has agreed upon.” They said, “We do not acknowledge this. Had we known that you were the Messenger of Allah, we would not have prevented you from anything. Rather, you are Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh.” He replied, “I am the Messenger of Allah, and I am Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh.” He then said to ʿAlī, “Erase the words ‘Messenger of Allah.’” ʿAlī replied, “No, by Allah, I shall never erase you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took the document, though he did not write proficiently, and wrote: “This is what Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh has agreed upon: he shall not enter Makkah bearing weapons except swords kept in their sheaths; he shall not take anyone from its inhabitants with him, even if that person wishes to follow him; and Quraysh shall not prevent any of his Companions from remaining there if he wishes to do so.” When the Prophet ﷺ entered Makkah and the appointed period had elapsed, they came to ʿAlī and said, “Tell your companion to leave us, for the appointed time has ended.” The Prophet ﷺ departed, and the daughter of Ḥamzah followed him, calling, “O uncle! O uncle!” ʿAlī reached for her, took her by the hand and said to Fāṭimah, “Take your cousin and care for her.” ʿAlī, Zayd and Jaʿfar then disputed over who should have custody of her. ʿAlī said, “I took her, and she is the daughter of my paternal uncle.” Jaʿfar said, “She is the daughter of my paternal uncle, and her maternal aunt is my wife.” Zayd said, “She is the daughter of my brother.” The Prophet ﷺ awarded her custody to her maternal aunt and said, “The maternal aunt holds the position of the mother.” He said to ʿAlī, “You are from me, and I am from you.” He said to Jaʿfar, “You resemble me in appearance and character.” He said to Zayd, “You are our brother and our freedman and ally.” ʿAlī asked, “Will you not marry the daughter of Ḥamzah?” He replied, “She is the daughter of my foster brother through nursing.”"],[762,"Imam al-Zuhrī says: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the ʿumrah of qaḍāʾ, it was Dhū al-Ḥijjah, 7 AH. He sent Ibn Abī al-ʿAwjāʾ al-Sulamī, may Allah be pleased with him, with 50 men. They set out toward Banū Sulaym. A spy of Banū Sulaym was also with them. When they left Madinah, that spy went to his people and made them cautious and informed. They prepared a large army to confront them. Ibn Abī al-ʿAwjāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, came to them. That spy had already prepared the group. When the noble Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, saw their huge army, they invited them to Islam. They received them with arrows and did not listen to a single word of the Companions. They said: “We have no need of what you are inviting us to.” Then they showered arrows on them for some time, and reinforcements kept coming to them from their side, until they surrounded the Muslims from every direction. Then a fierce battle took place until most of the people were martyred, and their commander Ibn Abī al-ʿAwjāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, also fell wounded among the slain. Then he was lifted and brought to the Prophet ﷺ."],[763,"It is narrated from Sayyidunā Jundub ibn Makīth, may Allah be pleased with him, that once the Prophet ﷺ sent Ghālib ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Kalbī, may Allah be pleased with him, to Banū al-Mulawwiḥ, who lived at Kudayd, and ordered him to make a night raid on them. They set out, and I was part of that detachment. We kept going, and when we reached the place Qudayd, we met al-Ḥārith ibn Mālik. We seized him. He said: “I was only coming to accept Islam.” Sayyidunā Ghālib said: “If you were truly coming to become Muslim, then this one day of confinement will not harm you at all; and if you were coming with some other intention, then we have bound you.” Saying this, they tied al-Ḥārith’s arms tightly, appointed an Abyssinian who was with us as guard over him and left him behind us, and told him: “Stay here until we return. If in the meantime he tries to resist you, cut off his head.” After that we set out, and reaching Baṭn Kudayd, we camped after the ʿAṣr prayer at the place ʿAshīshah. My companions sent me to a high place. I climbed a hill so that I could watch everyone coming and going. I climbed it at the time of Maghrib. A man of the enemy came out and saw me on the hill. He said to his wife: “I see a human shadow on this hill, which I did not see in the early part of the day. Look, perhaps the dogs have dragged your vessels out and thrown them.” She looked and said: “By Allah, I do not find anything missing.” He said: “Then bring me my bow and two arrows from my quiver.” She brought him these things, and he took aim and shot an arrow at me, which hit my side. I pulled it out and threw it aside, and did not move. He shot the second arrow at me too, which hit the base of my shoulder. I pulled that out too and threw it aside, and did not move. Seeing this, he said to his wife: “Both my arrows hit him. If this were a living thing, it would certainly have moved. So after morning comes, search for my arrows and bring them, so that the dogs do not use them against me.” We gave those people respite in this way until the night began to settle in. When they had milked the animals, tied the camels, and settled down to rest, and part of the night had passed, we made a night raid on them. Some people were killed at our hands, and after that we drove off the animals and set out to return. Meanwhile their herald began shouting, calling the people for help. We were going quickly until we reached al-Ḥārith ibn Mālik and his companion, and set out with them as well. In the meantime the sound of the people’s cries, full of fervor, began to be heard. When only the bed of the valley remained between us and them, suddenly rain began, which came between us and them, and which Allah had sent for us. We had never seen such rain before, nor has there been such rain since. No one could stand before it. We saw them standing and looking at us, but none had the courage to advance, while we went on quickly gathering our things, until we reached a place named Mushallal and descended from there, and made the enemy unable to recover the animals we held in our possession."],[764,"ʿUmar ibn al-Ḥakam, may Allah have mercy on him, says that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Sayyidunā Shujāʿ ibn Wahb, may Allah be pleased with him, with 24 men toward the army of Hawāzin, which was at the place Sī, and ordered him to attack them suddenly. So they set out, traveling by night and hiding by day, until in the morning they attacked them suddenly. He had informed all his companions not to feel secure from pursuit. They obtained many camels and sheep and drove all of them to Madinah and divided the spoils. Their shares came to fifteen camels, each man received one camel, and 10 sheep were counted as equal to one camel. This group was absent for fifteen (15) nights. Ibn Abī Sabrah says that they had also found women in the settlement and had brought them along as well."],[765,"Imam al-Zuhrī, may Allah have mercy on him, says: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Sayyidunā Kaʿb ibn ʿUmayr al-Ghifārī, may Allah the Exalted be pleased with him, with 15 men. When they reached Dhāt Aṭlāḥ in the region of Syria, they found a large army of theirs there. They invited them to Islam, but they did not accept the call and showered arrows upon them. When the noble Companions, may Allah be pleased with them all, saw this situation, they fought them fiercely until they were all martyred. One of the Companions, who was among the slain in a wounded state, when the night turned cool, mounted to escape from there and came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him. This news weighed extremely heavily on him, and a force was sent toward them. Then he learned that they had gone to another side, so he left them."],[766,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Sayyidunā Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, toward the town of Maqnā. It is located on the Red Sea near the settlement of Aylah, at the boundary between Hijaz and Syria. There he found the people of Subāyā, among whom was Ḍumayr, the freed slave of ʿAlī. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered them to pledge allegiance, saying that if they pledged, there would be brotherhood with them. Hearing this, they went to him weeping, and he asked: “Why are you weeping?” They replied: “Separation has been placed between us.” He said: “Do not make separation; rather all of you pledge allegiance.”"],[767,"Its reason, as al-Wāqidī mentioned, was that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Sayyidunā al-Ḥārith ibn ʿUmayr al-Azdī, may Allah be pleased with him, with a letter to the king of Buṣrā. On the way, at the place Mūtah, Sayyidunā al-Ḥārith, may Allah be pleased with him, met Shuraḥbīl ibn ʿAmr al-Ghassānī. He was the governor of the region of al-Balqāʾ in Syria on behalf of Caesar. He asked Sayyidunā al-Ḥārith: “Where are you headed?” He said: “Syria.” Shuraḥbīl said: “Perhaps you are the messenger of Muhammad ﷺ?” He said: “Yes, I am indeed the messenger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” By the order of Shuraḥbīl he was seized and bound and then martyred. This news reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He was deeply grieved. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had it announced that the Muslims should gather for fighting. He informed them of the martyrdom of Sayyidunā al-Ḥārith and of his killer.\nMūtah: the mīm has ḍammah and the wāw is sākin. It is also read with a hamzah in place of the wāw, Muʾtah. It is the name of a place in Syria.\nIbn Isḥāq reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent an army toward Mūtah in Jumādā al-Ūlā, 8 AH, and appointed Sayyidunā Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with him, as its commander. He said: “If Zayd is martyred, then Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib is the commander. If Jaʿfar is also martyred, then ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah will be the commander of the people.”\nThe people prepared for jihad. Their number was three thousand. They set out and camped at the place Maʿān in Syria. It became known that Heraclius was camped at the place Māʾab in al-Balqāʾ. With him were one hundred thousand Roman fighters. In addition, one hundred thousand fighters from Lakhm, Judhām, Qayn, Bahrāʾ and Balī had joined his army. This narration is mursal.\nIbn Isḥāq reported that the mujāhidīn set out, and at the place Mushārif near al-Balqāʾ they met a detachment of Heraclius, which included Roman and Arab fighters. The enemy began to advance, and the Muslims camped at the place Mūtah. Here the Muslims formed the army. On the right wing he appointed Sayyidunā Quṭbah ibn Qatādah, may Allah be pleased with him, from Banū ʿUdhrah. On the left wing he named ʿAbāyah ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Anṣār."],[768,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed Zayd ibn Ḥārithah as commander during the Battle of Muʾtah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “If Zayd is killed, then Jaʿfar shall command; and if Jaʿfar is killed, then ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah shall command.” ʿAbdullāh said: I was among them during that battle. We searched for Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib and found him among the slain. We found more than ninety wounds on his body from spear thrusts and arrows."],[769,"ʿAwf ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī reported: I went out with the Muslims who accompanied Zayd ibn Ḥārithah during the Battle of Muʾtah. A reinforcement soldier from Yemen accompanied me, and he possessed nothing besides his sword. One of the Muslims slaughtered a camel, and the reinforcement soldier asked him for a piece of its hide. He gave it to him, and the soldier fashioned it into something resembling a shield. We continued onward and encountered the Byzantine forces. Among them was a man riding a chestnut horse with a gilded saddle and gilded weapons. This Byzantine fighter began attacking and harassing the Muslims. The reinforcement soldier waited for him behind a rock. When the Byzantine passed by him, the soldier hamstrung his horse. The man fell, and the soldier overcame and killed him, taking possession of his horse and weapons. When Allah granted deliverance to the Muslims, Khālid ibn al-Walīd sent for the soldier and took the slain man’s spoils from him. ʿAwf said: I went to Khālid and asked, “O Khālid, do you not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ awarded the possessions of a slain combatant to the person who killed him?” He replied, “Yes, but I considered these spoils excessive.” I said, “You must return them to him, or I will certainly report the matter to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” But Khālid refused to return them. ʿAwf said: When we assembled before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I related the story of the reinforcement soldier and what Khālid had done. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked, “O Khālid, what caused you to do what you did?” He replied, “O Messenger of Allah, I considered the spoils excessive.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “O Khālid, return to him what you took from him.” ʿAwf said: I then said, “There you are, Khālid! Did I not fulfil what I promised you?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked, “What is this about?” I informed him of what had happened, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ became angry. He said, “O Khālid, do not return it to him. Will you not leave my commanders to me? You receive the benefits of their leadership, while they bear its hardships.”"],[770,"It is reported from Ibn ʿUmar: He stood over Jaʿfar that day after he had been killed and said: I counted fifty wounds upon him from spear thrusts and sword strikes, and none of them was in his rear—that is, in his back."],[771,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed Zayd ibn Ḥārithah as commander during the Battle of Muʾtah and said, “If Zayd is killed, then Jaʿfar shall command; and if Jaʿfar is killed, then ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah shall command.” ʿAbdullāh said: I was among them during that battle. We searched for Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib and found him among the slain. We found more than ninety wounds on his body from spear thrusts and arrows."],[772,"Ibn ʿUmar, whenever he greeted the son of Jaʿfar, would say: “Peace be upon you, O son of the possessor of the two wings.”"],[773,"ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr reported: My foster father, who belonged to Banū Murrah ibn ʿAwf and participated in the Battle of Muʾtah, narrated to me: By Allah, it is as though I can still see Jaʿfar when he leapt down from his chestnut mare and hamstrung it. He then fought the enemy until he was killed."],[774,"ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr reported: My foster father, who belonged to Banū Murrah ibn ʿAwf, narrated to me: When Jaʿfar was killed, ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah took the standard and advanced with it while mounted upon his horse. He began urging himself to dismount, although he hesitated somewhat, and then recited: I swear, O soul, you shall descend; You shall descend, willingly or be compelled. Though the people gather and raise the cry of war, Why do I see you disliking Paradise? For far too long you have lived in ease; What are you but a drop within an old waterskin? He also recited: O soul, if you are not killed, you will still die. You have now entered the very heat of death. What you wished for has now been granted; If you do as those two did, you will be rightly guided. He meant his two companions, Zayd and Jaʿfar. He then dismounted. After he had dismounted, one of his cousins brought him a meat-covered bone and said, “Strengthen yourself with this, for you have endured much during these days.”\nHe took it from his hand and bit off a mouthful. Then he heard the violent clash of battle among the people and said to himself, “And you are still occupied with this world!” He threw it from his hand, took his sword, advanced and fought until he was killed."],[775,"Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr reported: ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah once lost consciousness. His sister ʿAmrah began lamenting over him, saying, “O my mountain! O such-and-such!” enumerating his virtues. When he regained consciousness, he said, “You did not say anything about me except that I was asked: ‘Are you truly like that?’”"],[776,"Anas reported: Before news of their deaths reached the people, the Prophet ﷺ announced the deaths of Zayd, Jaʿfar and Ibn Rawāḥah. He said, “Zayd took the standard and was struck down. Then Jaʿfar took it and was struck down. Then Ibn Rawāḥah took it and was struck down.” His eyes were shedding tears. He continued, “Then one of the swords of Allah took the standard, and Allah granted them victory.”"],[777,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: The Prophet ﷺ delivered an address and said, “Zayd took the standard and was struck down. Jaʿfar then took it and was struck down. ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah then took it and was struck down. Khālid ibn al-Walīd then took it without having previously been appointed commander, and victory was granted through him.” He also said, “It would not please us for them to be here with us.” Ayyūb said: Or he said, “It would not please them to be here with us,” while his eyes were shedding tears."],[778,"Abū Qatādah, the horseman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched the Army of the Commanders and said, “Zayd ibn Ḥārithah shall command you. If Zayd is struck down, then Jaʿfar shall command; and if Jaʿfar is struck down, then ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah al-Anṣārī shall command.” Jaʿfar rose and said, “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Prophet of Allah! I did not expect that you would appoint Zayd over me.” He replied, “Proceed, for you do not know which of those arrangements is better.” He then mentioned the announcement of the deaths of Zayd, Jaʿfar and ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah and said, “Khālid ibn al-Walīd then took the standard. He had not been one of the appointed commanders; rather, he assumed command himself.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised two of his fingers and said, “O Allah, he is a sword from among Your swords, so grant him victory.”"],[779,"ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr reported: My foster father narrated to me: Thābit ibn Aqram, a man of Banū al-ʿAjlān, then took the standard and said, “O Muslims, agree upon a man from among yourselves to command you.” They said, “You.” He replied, “I will not do so.” The people agreed upon Khālid ibn al-Walīd. When he took the standard, he held the enemy back, manoeuvred the Muslims away from them and withdrew while the enemy also pulled back, until he safely led the people away."],[780,"Khālid ibn al-Walīd reported: Nine swords broke in my hand on the day of Muʾtah, and nothing remained in my hand except a broad Yemeni sword."],[781,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported: When news arrived of the deaths of Ibn Ḥārithah, Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib and ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah, may Allah be pleased with them, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat down with grief visibly apparent upon him. ʿĀ’ishah said: I was watching through an opening in the door—that is, through a crack in it. A man came to him and said, “O Messenger of Allah, the women of Jaʿfar…” and mentioned their weeping. He ordered the man to tell them to stop. The man went and then returned, saying, “I told them to stop,” but mentioned that they had not obeyed him. He ordered him again. The man went and returned, saying, “By Allah, they have overcome us.” She related that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Then throw dust into their mouths.” ʿĀ’ishah said: I said to the man, “May Allah rub your nose in the dust! By Allah, you will not do what you were instructed to do, nor have you spared the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from further trouble.”"],[782,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported: When news of Jaʿfar’s death arrived, we could see grief upon the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[783,"1- Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib    2- Zayd ibn Ḥārithah\n3- Masʿūd ibn al-Aswad ibn Ḥārithah    4- Wahb ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Sarḥ\n5- ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah    6- ʿAbbād ibn Qays\n7- al-Ḥārith ibn al-Nuʿmān ibn Usāmah    8- Surāqah ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAṭiyyah\n9- Abū Kulayb    10- Jābir ibn ʿAmr ibn Zayd\n11- and ʿAmr    12- ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd ibn al-Ḥārith"],[784,"This expedition took place one month after Mūtah, in Jumādā al-Ākhirah, 8 AH. Dhāt al-Salāsil was named after a spring called Salāsil, located in the land of Banū ʿUdhrah, in the land of Judhām.\nAs al-Wāqidī said, the reason for it is this: News reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that a group of the people of Balī and Quḍāʿah had gathered in order to reach the outskirts of the city of the Prophet ﷺ. He ﷺ intended to win their hearts through ʿAmr.\nSo the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called Sayyidunā ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, tied a white banner for him and also gave him black flags with it. He sent him off with a force of three hundred of the Muhājirūn and Anṣār. He ﷺ ordered him to seek help from those of Balī, ʿUdhrah and Balqayn whom they passed. This was because Sayyidunā ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ was kind to them and his grandmother belonged to the tribe of Balī. When they came near the enemy and saw their great numbers, he sent Sayyidunā Rāfiʿ ibn Makīth al-Juhanī to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to inform him of it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave a banner to Sayyidunā Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ and sent reinforcements of two hundred soldiers under his command, among whom were leading Muhājirūn, for example Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, and chiefs of the Anṣār. Abū ʿUbaydah was ordered to join ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and that the two should work together and not disagree. So ʿAmr went on with them until he reached the land of Balī. They reached a place where people had gathered. So they scattered, until they reached the lands of Balī, ʿUdhrah and Balqayn, where they encountered a small group. They fought them for some time. The Muslims attacked them and they were defeated and dispersed. Then the Muslims returned, having prevailed by the strength of Islam."],[785,"ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for me and said, “Put on your clothes and take your weapons, then come to me.” I came to him while he was performing ablution. He looked me over intently and then lowered his gaze. He said, “I wish to send you in command of an army. May Allah keep you safe and grant you spoils, and I shall give you a generous portion of wealth.” I replied, “O Messenger of Allah, I did not embrace Islam for the sake of wealth. Rather, I embraced Islam out of a desire for Islam and to be with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” He said, “O ʿAmr, how excellent is righteous wealth for a righteous man!”"],[786,"ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed him commander of the army of Dhāt al-Salāsil. He said: I came to him and asked, “Which person is most beloved to you?” He replied, “ʿĀ’ishah.” I asked, “Among men?” He replied, “Her father.” I asked, “Then who?” He replied, “ʿUmar.” He then named several other men, so I remained silent out of fear that he might place me last among them."],[787,"ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him on the expedition of Dhāt al-Salāsil. His companions asked his permission to light a fire, but he forbade them. They spoke to Abū Bakr, who then spoke to ʿAmr about it. ʿAmr said, “If any of them lights a fire, I will throw him into it.” They encountered the enemy and defeated them. The Muslims wished to pursue them, but ʿAmr prevented them. When the army returned, they mentioned these matters to the Prophet ﷺ and complained to him about ʿAmr. ʿAmr said, “O Messenger of Allah, I disliked permitting them to light fires because the enemy might see how few they were. I also disliked allowing them to pursue the enemy because reinforcements might have joined them, enabling them to turn back and attack the Muslims.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ praised his decision. ʿAmr then asked, “O Messenger of Allah, who is the person most beloved to you?” He asked, “Why do you ask?” ʿAmr replied, “So that I may love those whom you love.” He said, “ʿĀ’ishah.” ʿAmr asked, “Among men?” He replied, “Abū Bakr.”"],[788,"ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ reported: I experienced a nocturnal emission on a bitterly cold night during the expedition of Dhāt al-Salāsil. I feared that I might die if I performed the ritual bath, so I performed dry ablution and then led my companions in the dawn prayer. They mentioned this to the Prophet ﷺ, who asked, “O ʿAmr, did you lead your companions in prayer while you were in a state of major ritual impurity?” I informed him of what had prevented me from bathing and said, “I heard Allah say: ‘Do not kill yourselves. Indeed, Allah is ever Merciful to you.’ [al-Nisāʾ 4:29]” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled and said nothing."],[789,"Then a detachment under the leadership of Sayyidunā Abū Qatādah ibn Rabʿī al-Anṣārī, may Allah be pleased with him, set out toward Khaḍirah, which is in Najd, in the land of Muḥārib. This took place in Shaʿbān, 8 AH. It is narrated: Abū Qatādah had 15 men with him, whom he sent toward Ghaṭafān, ordering them to surround them from all sides. They would travel by night and hide by day. Abū Qatādah, may Allah be pleased with him, attacked a very large tribe. A man called out: “O Khaḍirah!” They fought, but whoever came before the Muslims was killed. They brought livestock as spoils, which were one hundred camels and two thousand sheep and goats, and they also took many prisoners. They gathered the spoils and took out the khumus, then set it apart, and distributed the remaining spoils among the participants of the expedition. Each man’s share was 12 camels. One camel was counted as equal to ten sheep. In Abū Qatādah’s share came a beautiful slave woman, whom the Messenger of Allah asked for and gifted to Muḥammiyah ibn Jazʾ. In this expedition these men were away for 15 days. al-Bukhārī mentioned it after the battle of Ṭāʾif, and as for the battles he mentioned, the point is that it was before the conquest of Makkah, but there is disagreement about the month, whether it was Shaʿbān, Ramaḍān or some other month."],[790,"Nāfiʿ reported from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a detachment toward Najd in which ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar participated. They captured many camels. Each man’s allotted share was twelve camels—or eleven camels—and each was given one additional camel."],[791,"In the eighth year of the Hijrah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, at the beginning of the blessed month of Ramaḍān, a detachment under the leadership of Abū Qatādah, may Allah be pleased with him, set out toward Baṭn Aḍam. It is narrated: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out for the conquest of Makkah, he sent Abū Qatādah ibn Rabʿī to Baṭn Aḍam with 8 men; it lies between Dhū al-Khushub and Dhū al-Marwah. The distance between it and Madinah is 36 miles. This expedition was sent so that it would be known that attention was also directed toward that region, and so that people would stay informed. Among them was a man, Muḥallim ibn Jaththāmah. On the way these men met a man, ʿĀmir ibn al-Aḍbaṭ. ʿĀmir greeted them in the proper Islamic manner, and the men held back their hands from him. But Muḥallim ibn Jaththāmah, seizing the opportunity, killed him and took his camel, belongings and the milk-skin he had with him. Then when they reached the Prophet ﷺ, it was revealed about them in the Qurʾān: (O you who believe! When you travel in the way of Allah, investigate carefully, and do not say to one who offers you greetings of peace: “You are not a believer.” You seek the goods of worldly life, but with Allah are many spoils.) [al-Nisāʾ: 94] to the end of the verse. Then they went on and did not meet any group; they kept going until they reached Dhū Khushub, where they received news that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had set out toward Makkah. So they went toward Bībin, until they joined the Prophet ﷺ at the place Suqyā."],[792,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Ḥadrad reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us to Iḍam. I departed with a group of Muslims that included Abū Qatādah al-Ḥārith ibn Ribʿī and Muḥallim ibn Jaththāmah ibn Qays. We continued until, while we were at Baṭn Iḍam, ʿĀmir al-Ashjaʿī passed us riding a young camel and carrying possessions and a leather container of milk. When he passed us, he greeted us with peace, so we refrained from harming him. Muḥallim ibn Jaththāmah, however, attacked and killed him because of a previous matter between them, and he took his camel and possessions. When we returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him of what had happened, the following Qur’anic verse was revealed concerning us: “O you who believe, when you go forth in the cause of Allah, investigate carefully, and do not say to one who offers you peace, ‘You are not a believer,’ seeking the temporary gains of worldly life, for with Allah are abundant spoils. You yourselves were like that before, but Allah bestowed His favor upon you; therefore, investigate carefully. Indeed, Allah is ever fully aware of what you do.” [al-Nisāʾ 4:94]"],[793,"And Allah the Exalted has mentioned this in several places in the Noble Qurʾān. Allah the Exalted says: (Those among you who spent and fought before the conquest are not equal. Those are greater in rank than those who spent and fought afterward. And Allah has promised the best reward to both.) [al-Ḥadīd: 10]\nIn another place Allah the Exalted said: When the help of Allah and the conquest have come, and (O Prophet ﷺ) you have seen the people entering the religion of Allah in multitudes, then glorify your Lord with praise and seek His forgiveness; indeed, He is ever Accepting of repentance. [al-Naṣr: 1-3]\nThis expedition took place in the month of Ramaḍān; there is no disagreement about this among anyone, though there is disagreement about the times of setting out from Madinah and entering Makkah."],[794,"Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah both reported: Among the terms of the treaty concluded between the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Quraysh on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyah was that whoever wished to enter the alliance and covenant of Muḥammad could do so, and whoever wished to enter the alliance and covenant of Quraysh could do so. Khuzāʿah immediately declared, “We shall enter the alliance and covenant of Muḥammad ﷺ,” while Banū Bakr declared, “We shall enter the alliance and covenant of Quraysh.” They remained under that truce for approximately seventeen or eighteen months. Banū Bakr, who had entered the alliance and covenant of Quraysh, then launched a night attack upon Khuzāʿah, who had entered the alliance and covenant of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The attack occurred at a water source belonging to Khuzāʿah called al-Watīr, near Makkah. Quraysh said, “Muḥammad will not learn of our involvement. It is night, and no one can see us.” They therefore assisted Banū Bakr with horses and weapons and fought alongside them, thereby violating their covenant with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. ʿAmr ibn Sālim rode to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ after what had occurred between Khuzāʿah and Banū Bakr at al-Watīr. He reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Madinah to inform him of the news, having composed several lines of poetry. When he arrived, he recited them before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: O Allah, I appeal to Muḥammad By the ancient alliance of our father and his father. You were once as our children, and we as your parents; Then we embraced Islam and never withdrew our loyalty. So grant the Messenger of Allah powerful victory, And summon Allah’s servants to come as reinforcements. Among them is the Messenger of Allah, fully prepared; If oppression is imposed upon him, his face darkens with resolve. Within an army surging like a foaming sea; Indeed, Quraysh has broken its promise to you. They have violated your solemn covenant And claimed that you would summon no one. Yet they are humbler and fewer in number; They have set an ambush for me at Kadāʾ. They attacked us at al-Watīr while we slept, And killed us while bowing and prostrating. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “You shall be supported, O ʿAmr ibn Sālim.” A cloud then appeared before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said, “This cloud is pouring forth with the victory of Banū Kaʿb.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered the people to prepare for military action but concealed his destination from them. He asked Allah to keep news of his movement hidden from Quraysh so that he could take them by surprise in their own territory."],[795,"Abū Hurayrah reported: The leader of Khuzāʿah recited: O Allah, I appeal to Muḥammad By the ancient alliance of our father and his father. May Allah guide you—grant us powerful victory, And summon Allah’s servants to come as reinforcements."],[796,"Jābir reported: During the year of the conquest, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out for Makkah in Ramaḍān and travelled until he reached Kurāʿ al-Ghamīm. The people were fasting, both those travelling on foot and those riding. He was told, “Fasting has become difficult for the people, and they are watching to see what you will do.” He called for a cup and raised it to his mouth where the people could see him. He then drank. Some of the people broke their fast, while others continued fasting. The Prophet ﷺ was told, “Some of them have continued fasting.” He said, “Those are the disobedient ones.” The Companions who were travelling on foot gathered and said, “Let us present ourselves for the supplications of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, for the journey has become severe and the hardship prolonged.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told them, “Seek assistance through nasal—walking at a quick pace—for it shortens the distance for you and makes your burden feel lighter.” Jābir said: We did so and became swift and light in our movement."],[797,"Ibn Isḥāq reported that Budayl ibn Warqāʾ set out toward Madinah with a few men of Khuzāʿah. Reaching the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Madinah, he told him of the wrongs they had suffered, and also told him how the Quraysh had backed Banū Bakr. Then they turned back. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to the people: “Abū Sufyān will soon come to reinforce the treaty and extend its term.” On the way, at ʿUsfān, Budayl ibn Warqāʾ met Abū Sufyān. The Quraysh had sent Abū Sufyān to Madinah to reinforce the treaty and extend its term; they were also afraid now about what they had done against Banū Khuzāʿah. Abū Sufyān asked Budayl, “Where are you coming from?” He thought they were coming from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. In answer Budayl said, “I went out strolling along the seashore with Banū Khuzāʿah.” Abū Sufyān asked, “Did you not go to Muḥammad?” He said, “No.” Budayl then headed for Makkah. Abū Sufyān thought: if Budayl had gone to Madinah, his mount must surely have eaten fodder containing date stones. He picked up the dung of Budayl’s camel and broke it open, and there was a date stone in it. He said, “By Allah! Budayl has been to Muḥammad.”\nAbū Sufyān urged his mount on from there and reached Madinah. He first went to the house of his daughter, Sayyidah Umm Ḥabībah, may Allah be pleased with her. When he went to sit on the bedding of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, she folded it up. Abū Sufyān said in astonishment, “My daughter! Do you think the bedding unfit for me, or do you dislike my sitting on it?” She said, “This is the bedding of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and you are impure because you are a polytheist. I do not think it proper for you to sit on the bedding of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” Abū Sufyān said, “Surely your mind has gone bad since you parted from me.”\nFrom there Abū Sufyān went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He spoke to him about what he wanted, but the Prophet ﷺ gave him no reply. Then Abū Sufyān went to Sayyidunā Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and asked him to speak to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He refused. Then Abū Sufyān went to Sayyidunā ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him. He said, “Shall I intercede for you with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? That cannot be. By Allah! If I saw even ants against you, I would help them.” Then Abū Sufyān went to Sayyidunā ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him. Sayyidah Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, was there, and Sayyidunā al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with them both, was a small boy playing. Abū Sufyān said, “ʿAlī! You are the dearest to me and the closest of my kin. I have come on a matter; do not disappoint me. Just intercede for me with the Messenger of Allah.” Sayyidunā ʿAlī said, “Abū Sufyān, woe to you! The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has made up his mind about this matter, and we cannot speak to him about it at all.” Abū Sufyān looked at Sayyidah Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her, and said, “Daughter of Muḥammad! Could you tell your son al-Ḥasan to announce before all the people our renewal of the pact? Through it he would be recognized as a leader among all the Arabs.” Sayyidah Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her, said, “My son is not yet old enough to grant protection to anyone, and no one can grant protection against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” Then Abū Sufyān said, “Abū al-Ḥasan! The matter has become very complicated, so at least give me some useful advice.” Sayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said, “By Allah! I can think of nothing that would benefit you. You are the chief of Banū Kinānah. Go and announce the pact yourself before all the people, and then return to your homeland.” Abū Sufyān asked, “Will that be of any use?” Sayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said, “By Allah, not at all. But I see no other solution for you.” Abū Sufyān stood in the mosque and said, “People! I am renewing the pact before all of you.”\nHaving said this, Abū Sufyān left the mosque, mounted his camel and set out for Makkah. He reached the Quraysh. They asked, “What have you done?” He said, “First I went and spoke to Muḥammad, and I got no reply. Then I spoke to Ibn Abī Quḥāfah, and it was of no use. Then I spoke to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, and he reacted severely. Then I went to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, who was the gentlest of them. He advised me to do something, and I did it. Now I do not know whether it will be of any use or not.” They asked what advice he had given. He said, “He told me to announce the pact before all the people, so I did just that.” The Quraysh asked, “Has Muḥammad confirmed this pact?” He said, “No.” They said, “By Allah, woe to you! You have been played with.” Abū Sufyān said, “I could think of nothing else.”\nʿAbd al-Razzāq explicitly mentioned in his Muṣannaf (9739) that Abū Sufyān came to Madinah, spoke to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “We renew our earlier pact.” He said: “We stand by our earlier pact. Has some new event occurred on your side?” Abū Sufyān said, “No.” He said: “Then we stand by the earlier pact we made with you.” When Abū Sufyān could not persuade the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to renew the pact, he went to Sayyidunā ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him.\nMūsā ibn ʿUqbah reported concerning the conquest of Makkah: Banū Nufāthah attacked Banū Kaʿb by night, while the treaty of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with the Quraysh had not yet ended. Banū Kaʿb were allied with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Banū Nufāthah with the Quraysh. Banū Bakr helped Banū Nufāthah, and the Quraysh also supported them with men and arms. Banū Mudlij stayed out of this bloodshed and kept to the treaty. Banū Wāʾil had two chiefs, Salm ibn al-Aswad and Kulthūm ibn al-Aswad. It is said that among those who helped the wrongdoers were also Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah, Shaybah ibn ʿUthmān and Suhayl ibn ʿAmr. Banū Wāʾil attacked Banū ʿAmr and showed mercy to none of them. They oppressed women, children and the elderly, and shed much blood. To save their lives, these people fled toward the house of Budayl ibn Warqāʾ. Some men of Banū Kaʿb fled and reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him of the event, and also informed him of the role of the Quraysh. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Separate from one another as you go back.” Abū Sufyān went from Makkah to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, fearing what he had done. He said, “Muḥammad! Strengthen the earlier pact further and extend its term as well.” He asked: “Is this why you have come? Has no incident occurred on your side?” Abū Sufyān said, “Allah forbid, nothing of the sort has happened.” He said: “We stand by our agreement, that of the Treaty of Ḥudaybiyah. We will not change anything in it.” After that Abū Sufyān went to Sayyidunā Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said, “Strengthen the pact further and extend its term as well.” Sayyidunā Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said, “My pact is the same as that of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. By Allah! If I saw ants fighting against you, I would help them.” Abū Sufyān then went to Sayyidunā ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, and spoke about renewing the pact. He said, “Whatever new pact we may have, may Allah not let it be completed, and may He create the means to end what has been made, and not let what has ended happen again.” Abū Sufyān said, “You have repaid the ties of kinship badly.” Then he went to Sayyidunā ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him. When he spoke to him, he said, “My pact and the pact of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ are one and the same.” Abū Sufyān went to many of the great leaders of the Quraysh. All said, “Our pact is the same as that of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” Despairing, he went to Sayyidah Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her, and spoke to her. She said, “I am only a woman. This matter is for the Messenger of Allah to decide.” Abū Sufyān said, “Ask your sons al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn to speak about it.” Sayyidah Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her, said, “They are still children.” When Abū Sufyān asked to speak to Sayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, Sayyidah Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her, said, “Speak to him yourself.” He spoke to Sayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said, “Abū Sufyān! None of the Companions of the Messenger ﷺ can give protection to anyone against the Messenger of Allah. You are the chief of the Quraysh. Do this: announce the renewal of the pact before your people.” Abū Sufyān said, “That is right.” He said in a loud voice, “Muḥammad! I announce the renewal of the pact before all the people. In my view no one will break this pact, and no one will reject it.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Abū Ḥanẓalah! It is you who are saying this.” Abū Sufyān made the announcement and left. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah! Deprive the people of Makkah of hearing and sight. Let them see us suddenly in their midst, and hear the news of our coming only suddenly.”\nAbū Sufyān reached Makkah, and the Quraysh asked, “What have you done? Has Muḥammad written a document for you, or has he renewed the pact?” Abū Sufyān said, “Muḥammad flatly refused to do so. I also went to his Companions. By Allah! I have never seen the followers of any king obey more than Muḥammad’s Companions obey him. ʿAlī gave one piece of advice: that no one can grant protection against Muḥammad, and you cannot do so either. You are the chief and elder of your people, so there is little chance that your pact will be violated. I announced the renewal of the pact before everyone and spoke of extending its term. Then I said to Muḥammad that he would not oppose what I had said, and he said, ‘Abū Ḥanẓalah! That is only your word.’” Hearing all this, the Quraysh said, “You accepted what Muḥammad said, and you have done something of no use. ʿAlī has joked with you. By Allah! Your announcing the pact is of no use at all. Violating it is no problem for them.” After this argument Abū Sufyān went home. He told his wife the whole matter. She said, “May Allah disgrace and shame you. You are the worst envoy of your people.”\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a cloud and said: “This cloud brings good news of help for Banū Kaʿb.” After Abū Sufyān left, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began preparing for the advance. He told Sayyidah ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, to prepare his provisions for the journey and to keep the matter secret. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out of the house on some errand, or went to the mosque. Sayyidunā Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came to his beloved daughter Sayyidah ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and saw her cleaning and grinding wheat for the journey. He asked, “Daughter! For whom are you preparing these provisions?” Sayyidah ʿĀʾishah stayed silent. He asked, “Does the Messenger of Allah ﷺ want to go on an expedition?” Sayyidah ʿĀʾishah again stayed silent. Sayyidunā Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, asked, “Perhaps he wants to take action against the Romans?” Sayyidah ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, still stayed silent. He asked, “Is a campaign against the people of Najd intended?” Still she gave no answer. He asked, “Is he intending to go toward the Quraysh?” This time Sayyidah ʿĀʾishah said nothing. Meanwhile the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came home. Sayyidunā Abū Bakr asked him, “O Messenger of Allah! Do you want to go on an expedition?” He said: “Yes.” He asked, “Are you preparing for a campaign against the Romans?” He said: “No.” He asked, “Is it the people of Najd that you intend?” He said: “No.” He asked, “Are the Quraysh the target?” He said: “Yes.” Sayyidunā Abū Bakr said, “O Messenger of Allah! Is there not a treaty between us and them?” He said: “Do you not know what they have done to Banū Kaʿb?” After that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a general announcement made among the people for the expedition.\nThe scholars of the Sīrah differ on whether Abū Sufyān went before the news of the Quraysh’s breach of the treaty reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He spoke to him about renewing the pact, and he rejected it, saying, “We still stand by our earlier pact.” Or whether Abū Sufyān went to Madinah after the news had reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He had promised ʿAmr ibn Sālim al-Khuzāʿī help, because he had come to Madinah seeking help. He said: “ʿAmr ibn Sālim! You will be helped.” as has already been mentioned. The Quraysh regretted what they had done and sent Abū Sufyān to Madinah to renew the pact. He spoke, to ask for intercession, to Sayyidunā Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, Sayyidah Fāṭimah and ʿAlī, and their sons Sayyidunā al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, may Allah be pleased with them. He begged them to speak to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the matter. They refused. Then he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He too gave no reply.\nThe apparent sense of the earlier reports shows that Abū Sufyān went to Madinah after the news had reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. That is why he did not deign to speak to him, but kept his plan for the matter hidden and did not inform him, just as he kept it secret from others."],[798,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: Abū Bakr entered while she was sifting some wheat. He asked, “What is this? Has the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered you to prepare for departure?” She replied, “Yes, so prepare yourself.” He asked, “Where are we going?” She replied, “He has not named any destination to us; he has only ordered us to prepare.”"],[799,"ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ, the scribe of ʿAlī, reported: I heard ʿAlī say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent al-Zubayr, al-Miqdād and me, saying, “Proceed until you reach Rawḍat Khākh. There you will find a woman carrying a letter. Take it from her.” We set out with our horses racing swiftly until we reached the meadow, where we found the woman. We said to her, “Produce the letter.” She replied, “I have no letter.” We said, “You will produce the letter, or we will remove your clothing.” She then removed it from the braids of her hair. We brought it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. It contained a message from Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah to some of the polytheists in Makkah, informing them of some of the plans of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked, “O Ḥāṭib, what is this?” He replied, “O Messenger of Allah, do not be hasty in judging me. I was a man attached to Quraysh”—meaning that he was their ally but not originally one of them—“whereas the Emigrants accompanying you have relatives in Makkah who protect their families and property. “Because I lacked such blood ties among them, I wished to perform a favor for them so that they might protect my relatives. I did not do it because I had abandoned my religion or become pleased with disbelief after accepting Islam.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “He has indeed spoken truthfully to you.” ʿUmar said, “O Messenger of Allah, allow me to strike the neck of this hypocrite.” He replied, “He participated at Badr. How do you know? Perhaps Allah looked upon those who participated at Badr and said, ‘Do whatever you wish, for I have forgiven you.’” Allah then revealed the sūrah: “O you who believe, do not take My enemies and your enemies as allies, offering them affection even though they have disbelieved in the truth that has come to you. They drive out the Messenger and yourselves merely because you believe in Allah, your Lord. If you have truly gone forth striving in My cause and seeking My pleasure, how can you secretly show them affection? I know best what you conceal and what you reveal. Whoever among you does this has certainly strayed from the straight path.” [al-Mumtaḥanah 60:1]"],[800,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah wrote to the people of Makkah, informing them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to march against them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was directed to the woman carrying the letter, so he sent men after her, and the letter was taken from her hair. He asked, “O Ḥāṭib, did you do this?” He replied, “Yes. I did not do it out of treachery toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ”—Yūnus’s wording was: “out of treachery, O Messenger of Allah”—“nor out of hypocrisy. “I knew that Allah would grant victory to His Messenger and complete his mission. However, I had once held an honored position among them, and my mother was living with them, so I wished to establish this favor with them.” ʿUmar said, “Shall I not strike off this man’s head?” The Prophet ﷺ replied, “Would you kill a man who participated at Badr? How do you know? Perhaps Allah looked upon the people of Badr and said, ‘Do whatever you wish.’”"],[801,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb reported: Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah wrote a letter to the people of Makkah. Allah informed His Prophet ﷺ of it, so he sent ʿAlī and al-Zubayr after the letter. They caught up with a woman riding a camel and removed the letter from one of the locks of her hair, exactly as the Prophet of Allah ﷺ had told them. He summoned Ḥāṭib and asked, “O Ḥāṭib, did you write this letter?” He replied, “Yes, O Messenger of Allah.” He asked, “What caused you to do that?” He replied, “By Allah, I am sincerely devoted to Allah and His Messenger. However, I was an outsider among the people of Makkah, while my family lived among them, and I feared for them. I therefore wrote a letter that could not harm Allah or His Messenger in any way, hoping that it might benefit my family.” ʿUmar said: I drew my sword and said, “O Messenger of Allah, place Ḥāṭib in my power, for he has disbelieved, and I will strike off his head.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied, “O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, how do you know? Perhaps Allah looked upon this group who participated at Badr and said, ‘Do whatever you wish, for I have forgiven you.’”"],[802,"It is reported from Ibn ʿUmar: Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah was brought before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked him, “Did you write this letter?” He replied, “Yes. By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, faith has not changed within my heart. But every man of Quraysh had close relatives and a household who would protect his family. I wrote the letter hoping that, through it, Allah would protect my family.” ʿUmar said, “Permit me to deal with him.” The Prophet ﷺ asked, “Would you kill him?” He replied, “Yes, if you permit me.” He said, “How do you know? Perhaps Allah looked upon the people of Badr and said, ‘Do whatever you wish.’”"],[803,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah reported: His father, who was accompanying the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and had participated at Badr, wrote a letter to the disbelievers of Quraysh. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned ʿAlī and al-Zubayr and said, “Proceed until you catch a woman carrying a letter, and bring it to me.” They set out until they met her and said, “Give us the letter you are carrying.” They informed her that they would not leave until they had removed every garment she was wearing. She asked, “Are you not two Muslim men?” They replied, “Yes, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has informed us that you are carrying a letter.” When she realized that she could not escape them, she untied the letter from her hair and handed it to them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned Ḥāṭib and read the letter to him. He asked, “Do you recognize this letter?” He replied, “Yes.” He asked, “What caused you to do this?” He replied, “My children and relatives are there, and I was an outsider among you, O people of Quraysh.” ʿUmar said, “Permit me to kill Ḥāṭib.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied, “No, because he participated at Badr. You do not know; perhaps Allah looked upon the people of Badr and said, ‘Do whatever you wish, for I shall forgive you.’”"],[804,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: The Prophet ﷺ departed from Madinah during Ramaḍān with ten thousand men. This was eight and a half years after his arrival in Madinah. He and the Muslims accompanying him travelled toward Makkah while he and they were fasting. When he reached al-Kadīd—a water source between ʿUsfān and Qudayd—he broke his fast, and they also broke theirs."],[805,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ proceeded after appointing Abū Ruhm Kulthūm ibn Ḥuṣayn al-Ghifārī as governor of Madinah. He departed after ten days of Ramaḍān had passed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fasted, and the people fasted with him, until he reached al-Kadīd, between ʿUsfān and Amaj, where he broke his fast. He continued until he camped at Marr al-Ẓahrān with ten thousand Muslims, including men from Muzaynah and Sulaym, while every tribe was represented by a number of Muslims. The Emigrants and the Anṣār came out in full force with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and not one of them remained behind. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ camped at Marr al-Ẓahrān. All reports of his movements had been concealed from Quraysh. No news concerning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached them, and they did not know what he intended to do. During those nights, Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb, Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām and Budayl ibn Warqāʾ went out to investigate and search for any news they might discover or hear. Al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib had already joined the Messenger of Allah ﷺ somewhere along the road. Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib and ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Umayyah ibn al-Mughīrah had also met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ somewhere between Makkah and Madinah and sought permission to enter his presence. Umm Salamah spoke to him on their behalf, saying, “O Messenger of Allah, one is your cousin, and the other is the son of your paternal aunt and your brother-in-law.” He replied, “I have no need of either of them. As for my cousin, he attacked my honor; and as for the son of my paternal aunt and my brother-in-law, he is the one who said to me in Makkah what he said.” When this response was conveyed to them—and Abū Sufyān had a young son with him—he said, “By Allah, he must permit me to enter, or I shall take this son of mine by the hand and wander through the earth until we die of thirst and hunger.” When this reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he felt compassion for them and granted them permission to enter. They came before him and embraced Islam. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ camped at Marr al-Ẓahrān, al-ʿAbbās said, “What a terrible morning awaits Quraysh! By Allah, if the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enters Makkah by force before they seek his protection, it will mean the ruin of Quraysh for all time.” He said: I mounted the white mule of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and rode until I reached the arāk trees. I thought: Perhaps I will encounter a woodcutter, a milk seller or someone travelling to Makkah on some errand, who can inform its people of the location of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ so that they may come out and seek his protection before he enters by force. He said: By Allah, while I was riding the mule and searching for what I had set out to find, I heard Abū Sufyān and Budayl ibn Warqāʾ conversing with one another. Abū Sufyān said, “I have never seen fires or an army like those of tonight.” Budayl replied, “By Allah, these are the fires of Khuzāʿah, stirred into action by war.” Abū Sufyān said, “By Allah, Khuzāʿah is too weak and too few in number for these to be its fires and army.” Al-ʿAbbās said: I recognized his voice and called, “O Abū Ḥanẓalah!” He recognized my voice and asked, “Abū al-Faḍl?” I replied, “Yes.” He asked, “What is happening? May my father and mother be sacrificed for you!” I said, “Woe to you, Abū Sufyān! The Messenger of Allah ﷺ is here among the people.” He exclaimed, “What a terrible morning awaits Quraysh, by Allah! What can be done? May my father and mother be sacrificed for you!” I said, “By Allah, if he captures you, he will surely have your neck struck. Mount this mule behind me so that I may bring you to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and seek his protection for you.” He mounted behind me, while his two companions returned. I rode onward with him. Whenever we passed one of the Muslims’ campfires, they asked, “Who is this?” When they recognized the mule of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, they said, “It is the uncle of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ riding his mule.” Eventually, I passed the fire of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. He asked, “Who is this?” and came toward me. When he saw Abū Sufyān seated on the rear of the animal, he said, “Abū Sufyān, the enemy of Allah! Praise be to Allah, Who has placed you in our power without any treaty or covenant.” He hurried toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while I urged the mule forward. I overtook him by the small distance with which a slow animal can outpace a man travelling on foot. I leapt from the mule and entered the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and ʿUmar entered as well. ʿUmar said, “O Messenger of Allah, this is Abū Sufyān. Allah has placed him in our power without any treaty or covenant, so allow me to strike off his head.” I said, “O Messenger of Allah, I have granted him protection.” I then sat beside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, held him close and said, “No, by Allah, no man besides me shall speak privately with him tonight.” When ʿUmar continued pressing the matter, I said, “Take it easy, ʿUmar! By Allah, had he been a man from Banū ʿAdī ibn Kaʿb, you would not have said this. You say it only because you know that he is a man from Banū ʿAbd Manāf.” ʿUmar replied, “Take it easy, al-ʿAbbās! By Allah, the day you embraced Islam, your acceptance of Islam was more beloved to me than the acceptance of Islam by al-Khaṭṭāb would have been had he embraced it. That was only because I knew that your Islam was more beloved to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than the Islam of al-Khaṭṭāb would have been.”\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Take him to your camp, O ʿAbbās, and bring him to me in the morning.” I took him to my camp, and he spent the night with me. In the morning, I brought him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he saw him, he said, “Woe to you, Abū Sufyān! Is it not time for you to know that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah?” He replied, “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you! How noble, kind to your relatives and forbearing you are! By Allah, I have come to realize that if there had been another deity besides Allah, it would have benefited me in some way.” He said, “Woe to you, Abū Sufyān! Is it not time for you to know that I am the Messenger of Allah?” He replied, “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you! How forbearing, noble and kind to your relatives you are! By Allah, until this moment I still harbored some doubt about that.” Al-ʿAbbās said, “Woe to you, Abū Sufyān! Embrace Islam and testify that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah before your neck is struck.” He then pronounced the testimony of truth and embraced Islam. I said, “O Messenger of Allah, Abū Sufyān is a man who loves distinction, so grant him something.” He replied, “Yes. Whoever enters the house of Abū Sufyān shall be safe; whoever closes his door shall be safe; and whoever enters the Sacred Mosque shall be safe.” When Abū Sufyān turned to leave, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “O ʿAbbās, hold him at the narrow part of the valley near the projecting edge of the mountain so that the armies of Allah may pass before him and he may see them.” I took him and detained him at the place where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had instructed me. The tribes then passed beneath their respective banners. Whenever a tribe passed, Abū Sufyān asked, “O ʿAbbās, who are these?” I would reply, “Sulaym.” He would say, “What have I to do with Sulaym?” Another tribe would pass, and he would ask, “O ʿAbbās, who are these?” I would reply, “Muzaynah.” He would say, “What have I to do with Muzaynah?” The tribes continued passing. No tribe passed without him asking, “Who are these?” I would reply, “Banū so-and-so,” and he would say, “What have I to do with Banū so-and-so?” Finally, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed in his green-clad battalion, which contained the Emigrants and the Anṣār. Because of their armor, nothing could be seen of them except their eyes. Abū Sufyān exclaimed, “Glory be to Allah, O ʿAbbās! Who are these?” I replied, “This is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among the Emigrants and the Anṣār.” He said, “No one has the ability or strength to confront these men. By Allah, O Abū al-Faḍl, the dominion of your nephew has become tremendous this morning!” I replied, “O Abū Sufyān, it is prophethood.” He said, “Then how excellent it is!” I said, “Hurry to your people!” When he reached them, he cried at the top of his voice, “O people of Quraysh! Muḥammad has come against you with a force you cannot withstand. Whoever enters the house of Abū Sufyān shall be safe.” His wife, Hind bint ʿUtbah, rose against him and seized his moustache, saying, “Kill this fat, wretched man! What a disgraceful scout for his people!” He said, “Woe to you! Do not let this woman deceive you concerning your own lives. A force has come against you that you cannot withstand. Whoever enters the house of Abū Sufyān shall be safe.” They replied, “Woe to you! What benefit can your house provide?” He replied, “Whoever closes his door shall be safe, and whoever enters the Sacred Mosque shall be safe.” The people then dispersed to their homes and to the Sacred Mosque."],[806,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported: During the year of the conquest, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed us that we would depart after two nights of Ramaḍān had passed. We set out while fasting, and when we reached al-Kadīd, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered us to break our fast. The following morning, some people were fasting while others were not. When we reached the closest stopping place facing the enemy, he ordered us to break our fast, so all of us did so."],[807,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ marched during the year of the conquest and news of this reached Quraysh, Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb, Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām and Budayl ibn Warqāʾ went out seeking information about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They continued until they reached Marr al-Ẓahrān, where they saw fires resembling the fires of ʿArafah. Abū Sufyān asked, “What are these? They look like the fires of ʿArafah!” Budayl ibn Warqāʾ replied, “They are the fires of Banū ʿAmr.” Abū Sufyān said, “Banū ʿAmr are too few for this.” Some of the guards of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw them, caught them and brought them before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Abū Sufyān then embraced Islam. When the Prophet ﷺ resumed the march, he said to al-ʿAbbās, “Hold Abū Sufyān at the projecting edge of the mountain so that he may observe the Muslims.” Al-ʿAbbās detained him there, and the tribes accompanying the Prophet ﷺ began passing before Abū Sufyān, battalion after battalion. A battalion passed, and he asked, “O ʿAbbās, who are these?” He replied, “These are Ghifār.” Abū Sufyān said, “What have I to do with Ghifār?” Juhaynah then passed, and he said the same. Saʿd ibn Hudhaym passed, and he said the same; then Sulaym passed, and he again said the same. Finally, a battalion unlike any he had previously seen approached. He asked, “Who are these?” Al-ʿAbbās replied, “These are the Anṣār, commanded by Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah, who is carrying the banner.” Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah said, “O Abū Sufyān, today is the day of fierce battle; today the sanctity of the Kaʿbah will be violated.” Abū Sufyān said, “O ʿAbbās, what a day for defending one’s people!” Another battalion then arrived. It was the smallest of the battalions and included the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions. The Prophet’s ﷺ banner was carried by al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed Abū Sufyān, he said, “Do you not know what Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah said?” He asked, “What did he say?” Abū Sufyān informed him. The Prophet ﷺ said, “Saʿd was mistaken. Rather, this is a day on which Allah will honor the Kaʿbah and a day on which the Kaʿbah will be clothed.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered that his banner be planted at al-Ḥajūn. ʿUrwah said: Nāfiʿ ibn Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim informed me that he heard al-ʿAbbās ask al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, “O Abū ʿAbdullāh, is this where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered you to plant the banner?” He said: On that day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered Khālid ibn al-Walīd to enter through the upper part of Makkah by way of Kadāʾ, while the Prophet ﷺ entered by way of Kudā. Two horsemen from Khālid’s force were killed that day: Ḥubaysh ibn al-Ashʿar and Kurz ibn Jābir al-Fihrī. Authentic, except for the statement, “On that day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered Khālid ibn al-Walīd to enter through the upper part of Makkah by way of Kadāʾ, while the Prophet ﷺ entered by way of Kudā,” which is anomalous. Narrated by al-Bukhārī (4280)."],[808,"Abū Hurayrah reported: Delegations came to Muʿāwiyah during Ramaḍān, and we used to prepare food for one another. Abū Hurayrah frequently invited us to his lodging. I thought: Why should I not prepare food and invite them to my lodging? So I ordered that a meal be prepared. I met Abū Hurayrah in the evening and said, “The invitation is at my lodging tonight.” He replied, “You have preceded me.” I said, “Yes.” I invited them, and Abū Hurayrah said, “O assembly of the Anṣār, shall I not relate to you a report concerning yourselves?” He then mentioned the conquest of Makkah, saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ advanced until he reached Makkah. He placed al-Zubayr over one flank, Khālid over the other flank and Abū ʿUbaydah over the troops without armor. They proceeded through the valley, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was within a battalion. He looked and saw me, then called, “Abū Hurayrah!” I replied, “At your service, O Messenger of Allah.” He said, “Let none come to me except the Anṣār.” A narrator other than Shaybān added that he said, “Call the Anṣār to me.” They gathered around him. Quraysh had gathered its rabble and followers and said, “We will send these men forward. If they achieve anything, we shall join them; but if they are defeated, we shall give whatever is demanded of us.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked, “Do you see the rabble and followers of Quraysh?” He gestured with his hands, placing one over the other to indicate striking them down, and said, “Then meet me at al-Ṣafā.”\nWe advanced, and anyone among us who wished to kill one of them did so, while none of them offered any effective resistance. Abū Sufyān came and said, “O Messenger of Allah, the main body of Quraysh has been exposed to destruction. There will be no Quraysh after today!” He said, “Whoever enters the house of Abū Sufyān shall be safe.” Some of the Anṣār said to one another, “The man has been overcome by longing for his hometown and compassion for his tribe.” Abū Hurayrah said: Revelation then came. When revelation descended upon him, its arrival was not hidden from us. No one would raise his eyes toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until the revelation had ended. When the revelation ended, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “O assembly of the Anṣār!” They replied, “At your service, O Messenger of Allah.” He asked, “Did you say: ‘The man has been overcome by longing for his hometown’?” They replied, “That did occur.” He said, “No. I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger. I emigrated to Allah and to you. My life is with you, and my death shall be with you.” They approached him weeping and said, “By Allah, we said what we said only because of our deep attachment to Allah and His Messenger.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Allah and His Messenger accept your truthfulness and excuse you.” The people then went to the house of Abū Sufyān, while others closed the doors of their homes. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ proceeded to the Black Stone, touched it and then circumambulated the House. He passed an idol beside the House that they used to worship. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was holding a bow by its curved end. When he reached the idol, he began piercing it in the eye while saying, “Truth has come, and falsehood has vanished.” After completing his circumambulation, he went to al-Ṣafā and ascended it until he could see the House. He raised his hands, praised Allah and supplicated as Allah willed him to supplicate. In another wording, he said, “What, then, would my name and identity be? No! I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger.”"],[809,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Rabāḥ reported: We came as a delegation to Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān, and Abū Hurayrah was among us. Each man would prepare food for his companions on an assigned day. When my turn arrived, I said, “O Abū Hurayrah, today is my turn.” They came to the lodging before our food was ready, so I said, “O Abū Hurayrah, why do you not relate something to us from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until our food is ready?” He said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of the conquest. He placed Khālid ibn al-Walīd over the right flank, al-Zubayr over the left flank and Abū ʿUbaydah over the infantry in the valley. He said, “O Abū Hurayrah, summon the Anṣār to me.” I called them, and they came hurrying. He asked, “O assembly of the Anṣār, do you see the rabble of Quraysh?” They replied, “Yes.” He said, “When you meet them tomorrow, cut them down completely.” He gestured with his hand, placing his right hand over his left, and said, “Your meeting place is al-Ṣafā.” No one from the enemy exposed himself to them that day without their striking him down. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ascended al-Ṣafā, and the Anṣār came and gathered around it. Abū Sufyān came and said, “O Messenger of Allah, the main body of Quraysh has been destroyed. There will be no Quraysh after today!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Whoever enters the house of Abū Sufyān shall be safe; whoever lays down his weapons shall be safe; and whoever closes his door shall be safe.” The Anṣār said, “The man has been overcome by compassion for his tribe and longing for his hometown.” Revelation then descended upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said, “You said: ‘The man has been overcome by compassion for his tribe and longing for his hometown.’ What, then, would my name and identity be?” He repeated this three times. “I am Muḥammad, the servant of Allah and His Messenger. I emigrated to Allah and to you. My life is with you, and my death shall be with you.” They said, “By Allah, we said it only because of our deep attachment to Allah and His Messenger.” He said, “Allah and His Messenger accept your truthfulness and excuse you.”"],[810,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: During the year of the conquest, al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib brought Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he embraced Islam at Marr al-Ẓahrān. Al-ʿAbbās said, “O Messenger of Allah, Abū Sufyān is a man who loves distinction, so perhaps you could grant him something.” He said, “Yes. Whoever enters the house of Abū Sufyān shall be safe, and whoever closes his door shall be safe.”"],[811,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib and ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Umayyah also met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Nīq al-ʿUqāb, between Makkah and Madinah, and sought permission to enter his presence. Umm Salamah spoke to him on their behalf, saying, “O Messenger of Allah, one is your cousin, and the other is the son of your paternal aunt and your brother-in-law.” He replied, “I have no need of either of them. My cousin attacked my honor, while the son of my paternal aunt is the one who said to me in Makkah what he said.” When this response reached them—and Abū Sufyān had a young son with him—he said, “By Allah, he must permit me to enter, or I shall take this son of mine by the hand and wander through the earth until we die of thirst and hunger.” When this reached the Prophet ﷺ, he felt compassion for them and permitted them to enter. They came before him and embraced Islam. Abū Sufyān then recited verses concerning his conversion and apologized to the Prophet ﷺ for his previous conduct: By your life, on the day I carried a banner That the cavalry of al-Lāt might defeat Muḥammad’s cavalry, I was like a bewildered night traveller enveloped in darkness; But now is the time when I am guided and follow guidance. A guide other than myself guided me and directed me to Allah— The very one whom I had driven away at every turn. I strove to turn away and distance myself from Muḥammad, Though I was called one of his kin even without claiming descent from him. They are what they are: whoever did not speak according to their desires, Even if possessing sound judgment, was blamed and denounced. I sought to please them, though I did not truly belong with them, Until guidance reached me wherever I sat. Tell Thaqīf that I have no desire to fight them; Tell Thaqīf to direct their threats toward someone other than me. I was not among the army that struck ʿĀmir, Nor did it occur through any deed of my tongue or hand. Those were tribes that came from distant lands, Scattered groups arriving from Sahām and Surdud. Ibn Isḥāq said: They claimed that when he recited before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, “The very one whom I had driven away at every turn directed me to Allah,” the Messenger of Allah ﷺ struck him gently on the chest and said, “You did indeed drive me away at every turn.”"],[812,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Satirize Quraysh in poetry, for it is more painful to them than being showered with arrows.” He summoned Ibn Rawāḥah and said, “Satirize them.” He did so, but his poetry did not fully satisfy the intended purpose. He then summoned Kaʿb ibn Mālik and afterward summoned Ḥassān ibn Thābit. When Ḥassān entered, he said, “The time has come for you to summon this lion that strikes with its tail.” He extended his tongue and moved it about, saying, “By Him Who sent you with the truth, I shall tear them apart with my tongue as leather is cut.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Do not be hasty. Abū Bakr is the most knowledgeable of Quraysh concerning their genealogies, and I share lineage with them. Wait until he distinguishes my ancestry for you.” Ḥassān went to Abū Bakr and then returned, saying, “O Messenger of Allah, he has distinguished your lineage for me. By Him Who sent you with the truth, I shall extract you from among them as a hair is drawn from dough.” ʿĀ’ishah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say to Ḥassān, “The Holy Spirit will continue supporting you for as long as you defend Allah and His Messenger.” She also said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, “Ḥassān satirized them, bringing relief to the believers and satisfying his own heart.” Ḥassān recited: You satirized Muḥammad, so I answered in his defense; And with Allah lies the reward for that. You satirized Muḥammad, righteous and upright, The Messenger of Allah, whose nature is faithfulness. My father, his father and my honor Are all a shield protecting Muḥammad’s honor from you. May I lose my daughter if you do not see our cavalry Raising clouds of dust from both sides of Kadāʾ. Their horses race against the reins as they ascend, Bearing upon their shoulders thirsting spears. Our steeds continue charging, drenched in exertion, While women strike their faces with their veils. If you stand aside from us, we shall perform ʿumrah; Victory will come, and the veil will be removed.\nOtherwise, prepare yourselves for the blows of a day On which Allah will grant honor to whomever He wills. Allah has said: “I have sent a servant Who proclaims the truth, in which there is no obscurity.” Allah has said: “I have prepared an army— They are the Anṣār, whose readiness is for battle.” Every day we face from Maʿadd Insults, fighting or poetic attack. How could one among you who satirizes the Messenger of Allah Be equal to one who praises and supports him?\nJibrīl, the messenger of Allah, is among us, And the Holy Spirit has no equal."],[813,"Abū Hurayrah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Our stopping place—if Allah wills, when Allah grants victory—will be al-Khayf, where they pledged themselves together upon disbelief.”"],[814,"Usāmah ibn Zayd reported: During the time of the conquest, he asked, “O Messenger of Allah, where will you stay tomorrow?” The Prophet ﷺ replied, “Has ʿAqīl left us any residence?” He then said, “A believer does not inherit from a disbeliever, nor does a disbeliever inherit from a believer.” Al-Zuhrī was asked, “Who inherited from Abū Ṭālib?” He replied, “ʿAqīl and Ṭālib inherited from him.”"],[815,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: During the year of the conquest, the Prophet ﷺ entered Makkah by way of Kadāʾ, which lies in the upper part of Makkah."],[816,"Anas reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered Makkah wearing a helmet. When he removed it, a man came and said, “Ibn Khaṭal is clinging to the coverings of the Kaʿbah.” He said, “Kill him.”"],[817,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Anṣārī reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered Makkah—meaning on the day of its conquest—wearing a black turban and not being in the state of consecration."],[818,"ʿAmr ibn Ḥurayth reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people while wearing a black turban."],[819,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: When the Prophet ﷺ arrived in Makkah, several young boys from Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib came to welcome him. He seated one in front of him and another behind him."],[820,"It is reported from Sayyidunā Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ entered Makkah and his banner was white. This report is not preserved (ghayr maḥfūẓ).\nImām al-Tirmidhī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: I asked Imām al-Bukhārī, may Allah have mercy on him, about this ḥadīth; he knew it through the route of Yaḥyā ibn Ādam. It is reported from Sayyidunā Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ entered Makkah wearing a black turban. Imām al-Bukhārī, may Allah have mercy on him, said that this is the original report. The truth is what the Imām said. Many reported it from the narrator Sharīk with the mention of the turban, such as ʿAlī ibn Ḥakīm al-Awdī, whose report is in Sahih Muslim (1358). The ḥadīth of al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn is in Sunan al-Nasāʾī (5345)."],[821,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Mughaffal al-Muzanī reported: On the day of the conquest, I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ riding his she-camel and reciting Sūrat al-Fatḥ—or a portion of Sūrat al-Fatḥ—prolonging and repeating the tones of his recitation. The narrator said: Muʿāwiyah then recited in imitation of Ibn Mughaffal’s recitation and said, “Were it not that the people would gather around you, I would repeat the tones just as Ibn Mughaffal did when imitating the Prophet ﷺ.” I asked Muʿāwiyah, “How did he repeat and prolong his voice?” He replied, “Ā, ā, ā,” three times."],[822,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reported: When Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah said to Abū Sufyān, “Today is the day of fierce battle; today the sanctity of the Kaʿbah will be violated,” the Prophet ﷺ said, “Saʿd was mistaken. Rather, this is a day on which Allah will honor the Kaʿbah and a day on which the Kaʿbah will be clothed.”"],[823,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Makkah, Qays was positioned at the front of his army. Saʿd—meaning Qays’s father—asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to remove him from that position out of fear that he might undertake some rash action. The Prophet ﷺ therefore removed him from it."],[824,"Ṣafiyyah bint Shaybah reported: After the Prophet ﷺ had become securely established in Makkah during the year of the conquest, he circumambulated the Kaʿbah while riding a camel. He touched the Corner with a hooked staff in his hand, while I watched him."],[825,"Ṣafiyyah bint Shaybah reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ settled in Makkah and the people became secure, he went to the Sacred House and circumambulated it seven times while riding. He touched the Corner with a hooked staff in his hand. After completing his circumambulation, he summoned ʿUthmān ibn Ṭalḥah and took the key of the Kaʿbah from him. The Kaʿbah was opened, and the Prophet ﷺ entered it. He found inside it a figure of a dove made from sticks. He broke it with his hand and threw it away. He then stood at the door of the Kaʿbah while the people had gathered around him in the Sacred Mosque."],[826,"ʿAbdullāh reported: The Prophet ﷺ entered Makkah while three hundred and sixty idols stood around the Kaʿbah. He began striking them with a stick in his hand and saying: “Truth has come, and falsehood has vanished. Indeed, falsehood is ever bound to vanish.” [al-Isrāʾ 17:81]"],[827,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Makkah, he refused to enter the Sacred House while idols remained inside it. He ordered that they be removed. Images of Ibrāhīm and Ismāʿīl were brought out, with divining arrows depicted in their hands. The Prophet ﷺ said, “May Allah condemn them! They certainly knew that these two never sought decisions through divining arrows.” He then entered the House, proclaimed Allah’s greatness in its various corners and left without praying inside it."],[828,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās reported: The Prophet ﷺ entered the House and found within it an image of Ibrāhīm and an image of Maryam. He said, “They have certainly heard that the angels do not enter a house containing images! Here is Ibrāhīm depicted—why is he shown seeking decisions through divining arrows?”"],[829,"Jābir reported: We entered Makkah with the Prophet ﷺ while three hundred and sixty idols worshipped besides Allah stood inside and around the Sacred House. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered that they be overturned upon their faces and then recited: “Truth has come, and falsehood has vanished. Indeed, falsehood is ever bound to vanish.” [al-Isrāʾ 17:81] The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then entered the House and performed two units of prayer inside it. He saw images of Ibrāhīm, Ismāʿīl and Isḥāq inside it. They had depicted the divining arrows in Ibrāhīm’s hand as though he used them to seek decisions. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “May Allah condemn them! Ibrāhīm never sought decisions through divining arrows.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then called for saffron and smeared it over those images."],[830,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: On the day of Ḥunayn, when Hawāzin confronted the Muslims, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had ten thousand men and the freed people of Makkah with him. They turned and fled. He then related the complete report, which will appear later in full."],[831,"Jarīr reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “The Emigrants and the Anṣār are allies of one another, while the freed people of Quraysh and the emancipated people of Thaqīf are allies of one another until the Day of Resurrection.”"],[832,"Muṭīʿ ibn al-Aswad reported: On the day of the conquest of Makkah, I heard the Prophet ﷺ say, “No Qurashī shall be executed as a captive after this day until the Day of Resurrection.”\nAnother narration adds: None of the men named al-ʿĀṣī from Quraysh had embraced Islam except Muṭīʿ. His name had been al-ʿĀṣī, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ renamed him Muṭīʿ."],[833,"Al-Ḥārith ibn Mālik ibn al-Barṣāʾ reported: On the day of the conquest of Makkah, I heard the Prophet ﷺ say, “This city shall not be invaded after this day until the Day of Resurrection.”"],[834,"The first sermon"],[835,"Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “No new tribal alliance is to be formed in Islam, while every virtuous alliance established during the pre-Islamic period is only strengthened by Islam.”"],[836,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ reported: During the year of the conquest, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people from the steps of the Kaʿbah. After praising Allah, he said among other things: “O people, every virtuous alliance established during the pre-Islamic period is only strengthened by Islam. No new tribal alliance is to be formed in Islam, and there is no migration after the conquest.” “The Muslims are united as one against all others. The lives of all Muslims have equal sanctity. A believer is not to be killed in retaliation for a disbeliever, and the blood money of a disbeliever is half the blood money of a Muslim.” “Indeed, there is no shighār marriage in Islam, nor the pre-Islamic practices of janab and jalab. Their obligatory charities are to be collected within their own territories.” “The protection granted by the least of the Muslims is binding upon them all, and the furthest detachment of the Muslims must return a share to the army as a whole.” He then descended."],[837,"ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb reported from his father, from his grandfather: When Makkah was conquered for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he said, “Lay down your weapons, except that Khuzāʿah may act against Banū Bakr.” He permitted them to act until the ʿAṣr prayer had been performed and then said, “Lay down your weapons.” The following day, a man from Khuzāʿah encountered a man from Banū Bakr at Muzdalifah and killed him. When this reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he stood to deliver a sermon. I saw him leaning his back against the Kaʿbah. He said: “The people most transgressive before Allah are those who kill within the Sacred Precinct, those who kill someone other than the actual killer in retaliation and those who kill because of the blood feuds of the pre-Islamic period.” A man stood and said, “So-and-so is my son.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “There is no claim of illegitimate lineage in Islam. The practices of the pre-Islamic period have ended. The child belongs to the marital bed, while the fornicator receives the stone.” They asked, “What is al-athlab?” He replied, “The stone.” He said, “For each finger, the blood money is ten camels; and for each wound that exposes the bone, it is five camels.” He also said, “There is no voluntary prayer after the dawn prayer until the sun rises, and no voluntary prayer after the ʿAṣr prayer until the sun sets.” He said, “A woman may not be married together with her paternal aunt or her maternal aunt, and a woman may not give a gift from marital property without her husband’s permission.” In another narration: When Allah granted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ victory over Makkah, he said, “Lay down your weapons, except that Khuzāʿah may act against Banū Bakr.” He permitted them to act until they had performed the ʿAṣr prayer and then said, “Lay down your weapons.” The following day, a man from Khuzāʿah encountered a man from Banū Bakr at Muzdalifah and killed him. When this reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he stood and delivered a sermon, saying: “The people most transgressive before Allah are those who commit aggression within the Sacred Precinct, those who kill someone other than the actual killer and those who kill because of the blood feuds of the pre-Islamic period.”\nA man said, “O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so is my son. I committed fornication with his mother during the pre-Islamic period.” He replied, “There is no claim of illegitimate lineage in Islam. The practices of the pre-Islamic period have ended. The child belongs to the marital bed, while the fornicator receives al-athlab.” It was asked, “O Messenger of Allah, what is al-athlab?” He replied, “The stone.” “For each finger, the blood money is ten camels; and for each wound that exposes the bone, it is five camels. There is no voluntary prayer after the dawn prayer until the sun has risen, and no voluntary prayer after the ʿAṣr prayer until the sun sets.” “A woman may not be married together with her paternal aunt or maternal aunt, and a woman may not give a gift from marital property without her husband’s permission.” “Fulfil the virtuous alliances of the pre-Islamic period, for Islam only strengthens them; but do not establish new tribal alliances in Islam.”"],[838,"A man from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ reported: The Prophet ﷺ delivered a sermon on the day of the conquest of Makkah and said: “There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah alone. He granted victory to His servant and defeated the confederate armies by Himself.” In another narration, Hushaym reported the wording: “Praise belongs to Allah, Who fulfilled His promise and granted victory to His servant.” “Indeed, every object of pride, privilege, blood claim and other claim from the pre-Islamic period is abolished beneath these two feet of mine, except the custodianship of the Sacred House and the provision of water to the pilgrims.” “Indeed, the victim of quasi-intentional killing”—Hushaym once specified, “with a whip, stick or stone”—“requires an aggravated blood payment of one hundred camels, forty of which must be pregnant.” In another narration, he said, “Forty camels ranging in age from thaniyyah to mature bāzil, all of them pregnant.”"],[839,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people on the day of the conquest of Makkah and said: “O people, Allah has removed from you the arrogance of the pre-Islamic period and its pride in forefathers.” “People are of only two kinds: a righteous, God-fearing person who is honored by Allah, and a wicked, miserable person who is insignificant before Allah.” “All people are the children of Ādam, and Allah created Ādam from dust.” Allah said: “O humanity, We created you from a male and a female and made you into peoples and tribes so that you may know one another. Indeed, the most honored of you before Allah is the most God-fearing. Allah is truly All-Knowing, All-Aware.” [al-Ḥujurāt 49:13]"],[840,"Abū Shurayḥ al-ʿAdawī reported: He said to ʿAmr ibn Saʿīd while the latter was dispatching military forces toward Makkah, “Permit me, O governor, to relate to you words spoken by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the morning following the day of the conquest.” “My ears heard it, my heart retained it and my eyes saw him when he spoke it.” He praised and glorified Allah and then said: “Allah made Makkah sacred; the people did not make it sacred.” “It is not lawful for anyone who believes in Allah and the Last Day to shed blood within it or cut down its trees.” “If anyone attempts to justify fighting within it by citing the fighting of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then tell him: Allah granted permission to His Messenger, but He has not granted permission to you.” “I was permitted to fight within it for only a brief period of one day. Its sanctity has now returned today just as it was sacred yesterday.” “Let those who are present convey this to those who are absent.” Abū Shurayḥ was asked, “What did ʿAmr say to you?” He replied: ʿAmr said, “I know this better than you, Abū Shurayḥ. The Sacred Precinct does not shelter a rebel, a fugitive guilty of bloodshed or a fugitive guilty of theft.”"],[841,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: On the day of the conquest of Makkah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “There is no migration after the conquest, but striving and sincere intention remain. When you are called to mobilize, then mobilize.” He also said, “Allah made this city sacred on the day He created the heavens and the earth. It therefore remains sacred by Allah’s decree until the Day of Resurrection.” “Fighting within it was not permitted to anyone before me, and it was permitted to me for only a brief period of one day. It remains sacred by Allah’s decree until the Day of Resurrection.” “Its thorny vegetation is not to be cut, its game is not to be frightened, its lost property is not to be picked up except by one who will publicly announce it, and its fresh vegetation is not to be cut.” Al-ʿAbbās said, “O Messenger of Allah, except idhkhir, for they use it for their blacksmiths and their homes.” He replied, “Except idhkhir.”"],[842,"Abū Hurayrah reported: When Allah granted His Messenger ﷺ victory over Makkah, he stood before the people, praised and glorified Allah and then said: “Allah restrained the elephant from Makkah and gave His Messenger and the believers authority over it.” “It was not made lawful for anyone before me. It was made lawful for me for only a brief period of one day, and it shall not be made lawful for anyone after me.” “Its game is not to be frightened, its thorny vegetation is not to be cut and its lost property is not lawful to take except for one who will announce it.” “The family of a person who has been killed has the choice between two options: accepting blood money or demanding legal retaliation.” Al-ʿAbbās said, “Except idhkhir, for we use it in our graves and homes.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied, “Except idhkhir.” A man from Yemen named Abū Shāh stood and said, “Have this written for me, O Messenger of Allah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Write it for Abū Shāh.”"],[843,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: During the year of the conquest, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in Makkah, he heard him say, “Allah and His Messenger have forbidden the sale of wine, carrion, swine and idols.” It was asked, “O Messenger of Allah, what about the fat of dead animals? It is used to coat ships, condition hides and provide oil for people’s lamps.” He replied, “No, it is forbidden.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said, “May Allah condemn the Jews. When Allah forbade animal fat to them, they rendered it down, sold it and consumed its price.”"],[844,"ʿAmr ibn Salamah reported: We lived beside a water source situated along a route travelled by the people. Travellers would pass us, and we would ask them, “What is happening with the people? What is happening? Who is this man?” They would reply, “He claims that Allah has sent him and revealed such-and-such to him.” I would memorize those words, and it was as though they were being firmly implanted within my heart. The Arabs were delaying their acceptance of Islam until the conquest. They would say, “Leave him to deal with his people. If he triumphs over them, then he is a true prophet.” When the conquest took place, every tribe hastened to embrace Islam, and my father was the first of my people to do so. When my father returned, he said, “By Allah, I have come to you from the true Prophet ﷺ. He instructed: ‘Perform such-and-such prayer at such-and-such time and such-and-such prayer at such-and-such time. When the time for prayer arrives, let one of you call the prayer, and let the one who knows the most Qur’an lead you.’” They looked among themselves and found no one who knew more Qur’an than I did because of what I had learned from the passing travellers. They therefore placed me before them as their prayer leader, although I was only six or seven years old. I was wearing a cloak that would draw up and expose me whenever I prostrated. A woman from the tribe said, “Will you not cover your reciter’s backside from us?” They purchased cloth and made a shirt for me. I had never been as delighted by anything as I was by that shirt."],[845,"Mujāshiʿ ibn Masʿūd al-Sulamī reported: After the conquest, I brought my brother Abū Maʿbad to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of Allah, accept his pledge to emigrate.” He replied, “The migration has passed with those who performed it.” I asked, “Then upon what will you accept his pledge?” He replied, “Upon Islam, striving and goodness.”"],[846,"Abū al-Aswad reported: He saw the Prophet ﷺ accepting the people’s pledges on the day of the conquest. He sat near Qarn Masfalah and accepted their pledges upon Islam and the testimony. He said: I asked, “What was the testimony?” He replied: Muḥammad ibn al-Aswad ibn Khalaf informed me that the Prophet ﷺ accepted their pledge upon belief in Allah and the testimony that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger."],[847,"ʿĀ’ishah reported concerning the women’s pledge: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never touched a woman with his hand. Rather, he would obtain her verbal pledge, and after she gave it, he would say, “Go, for I have accepted your pledge.”"],[848,"Umaymah bint Ruqayqah reported: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a group of women who pledged allegiance to him upon Islam. They said, “O Messenger of Allah, we pledge to you that we shall not associate anything with Allah, steal, commit fornication, kill our children, fabricate falsehood concerning children between our hands and feet or disobey you in what is right.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “To the extent of your ability and capacity.” She said: We replied, “Allah and His Messenger are more merciful to us than we are to ourselves. Come, O Messenger of Allah, and let us pledge to you by hand.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “I do not shake hands with women. My words to one hundred women are the same as my words to a single woman.”"],[849,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: Fāṭimah bint ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah came to pledge allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ, and he required from her the pledge stated in the verse: “O Prophet, when the believing women come to you pledging that they will not associate anything with Allah, nor steal, nor commit fornication…” [al-Mumtaḥanah 60:12] She placed her hand upon her head out of modesty, and the Prophet ﷺ admired the modesty he saw in her. ʿĀ’ishah said, “Accept it, woman. By Allah, we pledged only upon these conditions.” She replied, “Then yes,” and the Prophet ﷺ accepted her pledge according to the verse."],[850,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not shake hands with women when accepting their pledges."],[851,"Asmāʾ bint Yazīd reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “I do not shake hands with women when accepting the pledge.”"],[852,"Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stopped at Dhū Ṭuwā, Abū Quḥāfah said to one of his youngest daughters, “My daughter, take me up Mount Abū Qubays so that I may look from it.” His eyesight had already failed. She took him to an overlooking point, and he asked, “My daughter, what do you see?” She replied, “I see a large, dark mass gathered together.” He said, “That is the cavalry.” She said, “I see a man moving back and forth among that mass.” He said, “My daughter, that is the organizer”—meaning the man who commands and directs the cavalry. She then said, “By Allah, the dark mass has now spread out.” He said, “Then, by Allah, the cavalry has begun advancing. Hurry me back to my house.” She descended with him, but the cavalry encountered them before they reached his house. The girl was wearing a silver necklace. A man encountered her and tore it from her neck. She said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered Makkah and then entered the Sacred Mosque, Abū Bakr brought his father to him. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him, he said, “Why did you not leave the elderly man in his home so that I could go to him there?” Abū Bakr replied, “O Messenger of Allah, it is more fitting that he walk to you than that you walk to him.” The Prophet ﷺ seated him before him, stroked his chest and said, “Embrace Islam.” He then embraced Islam. When Abū Bakr brought him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, his hair was as white as the thaghāmah plant. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Change the color of this hair.” Abū Bakr then stood, took his sister by the hand and said, “By Allah and Islam, I appeal to whoever took my sister’s necklace to return it.” No one responded, so he said, “My dear sister, seek your reward from Allah for your necklace.”"],[853,"Anas ibn Mālik was asked about the Prophet’s ﷺ use of hair dye and replied: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had only a few gray hairs. Abū Bakr and ʿUmar after him, however, dyed their hair with henna and katam. He continued: On the day of the conquest of Makkah, Abū Bakr brought his father, Abū Quḥāfah, carrying and supporting him until he placed him before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Abū Bakr, “Had you left the elderly man in his home, we would have gone to him,” as an honor to Abū Bakr. Abū Quḥāfah embraced Islam. His beard and hair were as white as the thaghāmah plant. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Change the color of both, but avoid black.”"],[854,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: Hind bint ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah came and said, “O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, there was once no household on earth whose people I more desired to see humiliated than the people of your household. But today there is no household on earth whose people I more desire to see honored than the people of your household.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied, “And it will increase further, by Him in Whose hand is my soul.” She then said, “O Messenger of Allah, Abū Sufyān is a miserly man. Would I be committing a sin if I fed our dependants from his wealth?” He replied, “No, provided that you take only what is reasonable.”"],[855,"Wahb ibn Munabbih reported: I asked Jābir, “Did they take any spoils on the day of the conquest?” He replied, “No.”"],[856,"Umm Hāniʾ reported: During the year of the conquest, I went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and found him bathing while his daughter Fāṭimah screened him. I greeted him, and he asked, “Who is this?” I replied, “I am Umm Hāniʾ bint Abī Ṭālib.” He said, “Welcome, Umm Hāniʾ.” After finishing his bath, he stood and performed eight units of prayer while wrapped in a single garment. I said, “O Messenger of Allah, my maternal brother ʿAlī claims that he will kill a man to whom I have granted protection—so-and-so ibn Hubayrah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “We grant protection to whomever you have protected, O Umm Hāniʾ.” Umm Hāniʾ said: This occurred during the forenoon."],[857,"Umm Hāniʾ reported: The Prophet ﷺ entered her house on the day of the conquest of Makkah and performed eight units of prayer. I never saw him perform a prayer more lightly than that one, although he completed the bowing and prostration properly."],[858,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: The Prophet ﷺ entered Makkah on the day of the conquest wearing a helmet. When he removed it, a man came and said, “Ibn Khaṭal is clinging to the coverings of the Kaʿbah.” He said, “Kill him.” Mālik said: As far as we understand—and Allah knows best—the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was not in the state of consecration that day."],[859,"Abū Barzah al-Aslamī reported: I killed ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Khaṭal while he was clinging to the covering of the Kaʿbah."],[860,"Saʿd reported: On the day of the conquest of Makkah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted safety to the people except for four men and two women, whom he named, including Ibn Abī Sarḥ. He related the report and said: As for Ibn Abī Sarḥ, he concealed himself with ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called the people to pledge allegiance, ʿUthmān brought Ibn Abī Sarḥ and stood him before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying, “O Prophet of Allah, accept ʿAbdullāh’s pledge.” The Prophet ﷺ raised his head and looked at him three times, refusing each time. After the third request, he accepted his pledge. He then turned to his Companions and said, “Was there not a discerning man among you who, upon seeing me withhold my hand from accepting his pledge, would rise and kill him?” They replied, “O Messenger of Allah, we did not know what was in your mind. Why did you not signal to us with your eyes?” He said, “It is not appropriate for a prophet to employ deceptive signals of the eyes.” Abū Dāwūd said: ʿAbdullāh was ʿUthmān’s foster brother through nursing. Al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah was ʿUthmān’s maternal half-brother, and ʿUthmān administered the prescribed punishment upon him when he drank wine."],[861,"Al-Wāqidī said in al-Maghāzī that the Prophet ﷺ ordered six men and four women to be killed on that day."],[862,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar reported: On the day of the conquest, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ approached from the upper part of Makkah riding his mount with Usāmah ibn Zayd seated behind him. Bilāl and ʿUthmān ibn Ṭalḥah, one of the custodians of the Kaʿbah, accompanied him. He made his mount kneel in the Sacred Mosque and ordered ʿUthmān to bring the key of the House. It was then opened. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered with Usāmah, Bilāl and ʿUthmān. He remained inside for a considerable part of the day and then came out, whereupon the people raced to enter. ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar was the first to enter. He found Bilāl standing behind the door and asked him, “Where did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray?” Bilāl pointed to the place where the Prophet ﷺ had prayed. ʿAbdullāh said: I forgot to ask him how many units he had prayed."],[863,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived, he refused to enter the Sacred House while idols remained inside it. He ordered that they be removed. They brought out images of Ibrāhīm and Ismāʿīl with divining arrows depicted in their hands. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “May Allah condemn them! By Allah, they certainly knew that these two never sought decisions through divining arrows.” He entered the House, proclaimed Allah’s greatness throughout its various parts and did not pray inside it."],[864,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported: Quraysh became deeply concerned about the woman who committed theft during the Prophet’s ﷺ lifetime in the campaign of the conquest. They asked, “Who will speak to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on her behalf?” They said, “Who would dare to do so except Usāmah ibn Zayd, the beloved of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” She was brought before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Usāmah ibn Zayd spoke to him on her behalf. The face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ changed color, and he said, “Are you interceding concerning one of the prescribed limits of Allah?” Usāmah said, “Seek forgiveness for me, O Messenger of Allah.” That evening, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and delivered a sermon. He praised Allah as He deserves and then said: “To proceed: Those before you were destroyed because when a person of high status stole, they left him unpunished, but when a weak person stole, they carried out the prescribed punishment upon him.” “By Him in Whose hand is my soul, if Fāṭimah, the daughter of Muḥammad, were to steal, I would cut off her hand.” He then ordered that the punishment be carried out upon the woman who had stolen, and her hand was cut off. Yūnus reported that Ibn Shihāb said that ʿUrwah related from ʿĀ’ishah: Her repentance afterward was excellent, and she married. She would later come to me, and I would convey her needs to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[865,"ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reported: On that day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered Khālid ibn al-Walīd to enter through the upper part of Makkah by way of Kadāʾ, while the Prophet ﷺ entered by way of Kudā. Two horsemen from Khālid ibn al-Walīd’s force were killed that day: Ḥubaysh ibn al-Ashqar and Kurz ibn Jābir al-Fihrī."],[866,"There is no authentic report about the number of the polytheists who were killed. It is said that about twenty people were killed. The number twenty-four is also mentioned. There is also the view of seventy, and there are other statements and opinions besides these.\nThe correct view is what Sayyidunā Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb, may Allah be pleased with him, said: “O Messenger of Allah! The Quraysh’s strength has been wiped out; after today the standing of the Quraysh has been destroyed.” This ḥadīth indicates that a great number of the polytheists were killed."],[867,"Jābir was asked: “Did you take any spoils on the day of the conquest?” He replied, “No.”"],[868,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās reported: The Prophet ﷺ remained in Makkah for nineteen days, performing the four-unit prayers as two units."],[869,"Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb reported: On the day of the conquest, the Prophet ﷺ performed all the prayers with a single ablution and wiped over his leather socks. ʿUmar said to him, “Today you have done something that you did not ordinarily do.” He replied, “I did it deliberately, O ʿUmar.”"],[870,"Abū Hurayrah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us on an expedition and said, “If you find so-and-so and so-and-so, burn them with fire.” Then, when we were preparing to depart, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “I previously ordered you to burn so-and-so and so-and-so, but no one may punish with fire except Allah. Therefore, if you find them, kill them without burning them.”"],[871,"Ḥamzah ibn ʿAmr al-Aslamī, a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him with a group of men against a man from ʿUdhrah and said, “If you capture so-and-so, burn him with fire.” They set out, but when they had disappeared from his sight, he called them back—or sent someone after them and recalled them. He then said, “If you capture him, kill him, but do not burn him with fire, for no one may punish with fire except the Lord of the Fire.”"],[872,"Abū al-Ṭufayl reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Makkah, he sent Khālid ibn al-Walīd to Nakhlah, where the idol al-ʿUzzā stood. Khālid ibn al-Walīd reached it. It stood among hillocks covered with acacia trees, so he cut down the acacia trees and demolished the shrine built over them. He then returned to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him, but the Prophet ﷺ said, “Go back, for you have not yet accomplished anything.” Khālid returned. When the custodians of al-ʿUzzā saw him, they fled deep into the mountain, crying, “O ʿUzzā, drive him mad! O ʿUzzā, blind him! Otherwise, die in humiliation!” Khālid reached the site and found a naked woman with dishevelled hair, throwing dust upon her head. He struck her forcefully with his sword until he killed her, then returned to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him. The Prophet ﷺ said, “That was al-ʿUzzā.”"],[873,"In Ramadan of 8 AH, the expedition of Sayyidunā ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ was sent against Suwāʿ.\nWhen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Makkah, he sent Sayyidunā ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, to Suwāʿ, the idol of Hudhayl, to demolish it. ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him, related: I arrived there and met the keeper of the idol. He said, “What do you want?” I said, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has ordered me to demolish this idol.” He said, “You will not be able to.” I asked, “Why?” He replied, “It is protected.” I said, “You are still in falsehood! Woe to you, does it hear or does it see?” I went near it and broke it, and ordered my companions to demolish the treasury chamber, but they found nothing in that chamber. I said to the keeper, “What did you see?” He said, “I accept Islam for the sake of Allah.”"],[874,"In Ramadan of 8 AH, the expedition of Saʿd ibn Zayd al-Ashhalī was sent against Manāt.\nWhen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Makkah, he sent Saʿd ibn Zayd al-Ashhalī, may Allah be pleased with him, toward Manāt, which was at al-Mushallal the idol of Ghassān and of the Aws and Khazraj. On the day of the conquest of Makkah the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Saʿd ibn Zayd al-Ashhalī to demolish it. Saʿd set out with twenty horsemen and arrived there when a keeper was also present. The keeper said, “What do you want?” They said, “The demolition of Manāt.” He said, “You, and this task?” Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, advanced toward the idol, and just then a black, naked woman with disheveled hair came out toward them, cursing and beating her chest. The keeper said, “O Manāt, show your wrath!” Saʿd ibn Zayd al-Ashhalī, may Allah be pleased with him, struck her until she was killed. He directed his companions to the idol, but they found nothing in the treasury. Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, and his companions came into the service of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. This event took place in the month of Ramadan."],[875,"When Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allah be pleased with him, returned from breaking al-ʿUzzā, and the Prophet ﷺ was still staying in Makkah, he sent him in Shawwāl of 8 AH with three hundred and fifty men toward Banū Jadhīmah."],[876,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: The Prophet ﷺ sent Khālid ibn al-Walīd to Banū Jadhīmah. He invited them to Islam, but they did not know how to express, “We have embraced Islam.” Instead, they repeatedly said, “We have left our former religion! We have left our former religion!” Khālid began killing some of them and taking others captive. He handed one captive to each of us. Then, on a certain day, Khālid ordered every man among us to kill his captive. I said, “By Allah, I will not kill my captive, nor will any man among my companions kill his captive.” When we returned to the Prophet ﷺ, we informed him of what had happened. The Prophet ﷺ raised his hands and said twice, “O Allah, I declare my innocence before You concerning what Khālid has done.”"],[877,"Ibn Abī Ḥadrad al-Aslamī reported: On that day, I was among the cavalry of Khālid ibn al-Walīd. A young man from Banū Jadhīmah, approximately my own age, had his hands bound to his neck with a rope. A group of women stood not far from him. He called to me, “Young man!” I asked, “What do you want?” He said, “Will you take hold of this rope and lead me to those women so that I may fulfil a need with them, after which you may bring me back and do with me whatever you wish?” I replied, “By Allah, what you have asked is easy.” I took hold of his rope and led him until he stood before the women. He then said, “Farewell, Ḥubaysh, now that life is about to end”: Do you remember when I pursued you and found you At Ḥilyah, or encountered you at al-Khawāniq? Did a lover not deserve to be granted his desire After enduring night journeys and burning midday heat? No blame belongs to me, for when our families were together I said: Reward my love before one of death’s striking calamities. Return my affection before separation carries us far away And the commander departs with the beloved who must be parted from me. I have never betrayed the secret of a trust, Nor has any beauty after you delighted my eyes. Only what befell the tribe distracted me from love— Unless true mutual affection can overcome even that. Ibn Isḥāq said: Yaʿqūb ibn ʿUtbah ibn al-Mughīrah ibn al-Akhnas narrated to me from al-Zuhrī, from Ibn Abī Ḥadrad al-Aslamī, who reported that she replied, “And farewell to you. May you be greeted seven and ten times, then once and eight times in succession.”\nHe said: I then led him back, and his neck was struck."],[878,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: The Prophet ﷺ sent out an expedition. They captured spoils and took a man among them. The man said, “I am not one of these people. I fell in love with a woman and followed her here. Allow me to look at her once, and afterward do with me whatever you wish.” There stood a tall, dark-complexioned woman. He said to her, “Farewell, Ḥubaysh, before life comes to an end”: Had I followed you and caught up with you At Ḥilyah, or reached you at al-Khawāniq, Would a lover not deserve to be granted his desire After enduring night journeys and burning midday heat? She replied, “Yes, may I be ransomed for you.” He said: They brought him forward and struck his neck. Then the woman came and stood over him, gasped once or twice, and died. When they returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, they informed him of what had happened. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Was there not a single merciful man among you?”"],[879,"In Shawwāl of 8 AH came the expedition of al-Ṭufayl ibn ʿAmr al-Dawsī against Dhū al-Kaffayn, the idol of ʿAmr ibn Ḥumamah al-Dawsī. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to go to al-Ṭāʾif, he sent al-Ṭufayl ibn ʿAmr al-Dawsī toward Dhū al-Kaffayn, the idol of ʿAmr ibn Ḥumamah al-Dawsī, to demolish it. The Prophet ﷺ ordered him to seek help from his people and join him at al-Ṭāʾif. He hurried to his people. He demolished Dhū al-Kaffayn. He began setting fire to its face, burning it, and saying:\n“O Dhū al-Kaffayn, we are not among your servants. Our birth is before your birth. I have set fire to your heart.”\nFour hundred men of his people set out immediately with him. They reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ four days after he had come to al-Ṭāʾif. He ﷺ had also brought a dabbābah (siege engine) and a mangonel with him. He said: “O group of Azd, who will carry your banner?” al-Ṭufayl said, “The one who used to carry it in the Jāhiliyyah, al-Nuʿmān ibn Bāziyah al-Lahabī.” He said: “You have spoken rightly.”"],[880,"Allah the Exalted said: Allah has helped you on many battlefields, and on the day of Ḥunayn, when you rejoiced in your great numbers, but they availed you nothing, and the earth, for all its vastness, became narrow for you, and then you turned back, retreating. Then Allah sent down His tranquility upon His Messenger and upon the believers, and sent down forces you did not see, and punished the disbelievers; and that is the recompense of the disbelievers. Then after that Allah will grant repentance to whom He wills, and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful. [Sūrat al-Tawbah, verses 25-27]\nIbn Isḥāq relates that the Prophet ﷺ set out toward Hawāzin after the conquest of Makkah, on 5 Shawwāl, 8 AH. Al-Wāqidī relates in al-Maghāzī: Then the Prophet ﷺ departed on the sixth of Shawwāl, a Saturday. He appointed ʿAttāb ibn Usayd, may Allah be pleased with him, over Makkah to lead the people in prayer, and Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, to teach the people the Sunnah and fiqh. He ﷺ reached Ḥunayn on the tenth.\nḤunayn is a valley near Dhū al-Majāz, close to al-Ṭāʾif. It lies a few miles from Makkah toward ʿArafāt, and today it is called al-Sharāʾiʿ. The upper part of the valley is called al-Ṣadr and the lower part al-Sharāʾiʿ."],[881,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had completed the Conquest of Makkah, Mālik ibn ʿAwf al-Naṣrī assembled Banū Naṣr, Banū Jusham, Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr, a few groups from Banū Hilāl, and some people from Banū ʿAmr ibn ʿĀmir and ʿAwf ibn ʿĀmir. All of Thaqīf also joined him—the Aḥlāf and Banū Mālik. He then marched with them against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, taking along their wealth, women, and children. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard about them, he sent ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Ḥadrad al-Aslamī and said: “Go and enter among the people so that you may gather intelligence about them for us.” He entered among them and remained there for a day or two. He then returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him about them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb: “Do you not hear what Ibn Abī Ḥadrad is saying?” ʿUmar replied, “He has lied.” Ibn Abī Ḥadrad said, “By Allah, if you accuse me of lying, O ʿUmar, you have previously rejected the truth.” ʿUmar said, “Do you not hear, O Messenger of Allah, what Ibn Abī Ḥadrad is saying?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “You yourself were once astray, O ʿUmar, and Allah Almighty guided you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then sent to Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah and asked to borrow one hundred suits of armor that he possessed, together with their necessary equipment. Ṣafwān asked, “Are you taking them by force, O Muḥammad?” He replied: “No, it is a guaranteed loan, which we shall return to you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then set out."],[882,"Anas reported: On the Day of Ḥunayn, Hawāzin, Ghaṭafān, and others advanced with their livestock and children. The Prophet ﷺ had ten thousand men with him that day, as well as the freed people of Makkah. They retreated from him until he was left alone. That day he made two distinct calls without mixing one with the other. He turned to his right and called: “O company of the Anṣār!” They replied, “Here we are at your service, O Messenger of Allah! Rejoice, for we are with you.” Then he turned to his left and called: “O company of the Anṣār!” They replied, “Here we are at your service, O Messenger of Allah! Rejoice, for we are with you.” He was riding a white mule. He dismounted and declared: “I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger.” The polytheists were then defeated, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ acquired abundant spoils. He distributed them among the Emigrants and the freed people of Makkah but gave nothing to the Anṣār. The Anṣār said, “When hardship comes, it is we who are called, but the spoils are given to others.” This reached him, so he gathered them inside a tent and said: “O company of the Anṣār, what is this statement that has reached me concerning you?” They remained silent. He then said: “O company of the Anṣār, are you not pleased that the people should depart with worldly possessions while you depart with Muḥammad, taking him back to your homes?” They replied, “Indeed, O Messenger of Allah, we are pleased.” He then said: “If the people were to take one valley and the Anṣār another mountain pass, I would take the pass of the Anṣār.” Hishām said: I asked, “O Abū Ḥamzah, were you present for that?” He replied, “Where else would I have been?”"],[883,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: We conquered Makkah and then marched to Ḥunayn. The polytheists came in the finest battle formation I had ever seen. The cavalry formed a line, the fighting men formed another line, the women were positioned behind them, followed by the sheep and then the camels. We were a large force, having reached six thousand men, and Khālid ibn al-Walīd commanded one flank of our cavalry. Our cavalry began turning back behind us, and it was not long before our horsemen were driven away. The Bedouins and others whom we knew fled as well. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then called: “O Emigrants! O Emigrants!” Then he called: “O Anṣār! O Anṣār!” Anas said: This is a report from an eyewitness. We replied, “Here we are at your service, O Messenger of Allah!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ advanced. By Allah, we had scarcely reached the enemy before Allah defeated them. We took possession of that wealth and then proceeded to al-Ṭā’if, where we besieged them for forty nights. We then returned and camped near Makkah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began giving some men one hundred camels each."],[884,"The thought occurred to some of the Muslims: “Today we will not be defeated because of fewness of numbers.” Concerning this, Allah the Exalted revealed this blessed verse.\n﴿وَّ يَوْمَ حُنَيْنٍ١ۙ اِذْ اَعْجَبَتْكُمْ كَثْرَتُكُمْ﴾ [التوبة: ٢٥]\nAnd on the day of Ḥunayn (too), when your great numbers made you complacent.\nSeveral aḥādīth are reported on this. One ḥadīth is that Sayyidunā Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, said: At the Battle of Ḥunayn, a young Anṣārī among us said, “Today we will not be defeated because of fewness of numbers.” When we met the enemy, our men scattered. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was riding a mule. Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith was holding its bridle. His uncle Sayyidunā al-ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, was holding the mule’s saddle. We were fleeing through a narrow pass. So much dust had risen that we could not even see our own palms. Suddenly a man was seen running toward us. He asked him, “Who are you?” A voice said, “I am Abū Bakr. May my father and mother be sacrificed for you.” He had received more than ten wounds. Then another man was sensed coming. He asked, “Who are you?” He said, “I am ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. May my parents be sacrificed for you.” He too had received more than ten blows. Then suddenly another man came. He had received more than twenty wounds. He asked, “Who are you?” He said, “I am ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān. May my parents be sacrificed for you.” Then a man was seen coming. He had received more than ten wounds. He asked, “Who are you?” He said, “I am ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib. May my parents be sacrificed for you.”\nAfter this the other people began to come. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Let a loud-voiced man call out to the people.” A man called out in a loud voice. Everyone heard his voice. All began running back. Now the Muslims attacked together under his leadership. The polytheists left the field and fled. Among the polytheists, Durayd ibn al-Ṣimmah climbed a nearby mountain with six hundred men. Some people said, “We see an army coming.” Durayd said, “Leave them to me.” He asked, “What is the mark of these who are coming?” When the description was given, he said, “There is no need to fear them. These are the tribe of Quḍāʿah. They are going after the people.” Then some other people were seen coming. Durayd said, “There is no need to be afraid; I will deal with them. What is their mark?” When the mark was described, he said, “No trouble, these are Banū Sulaym.” Then a horseman was seen coming. When he was described, Durayd expressed regret and said, “Let him come here, I will gather him in.” When he asked for the mark, he was told that he was wearing a black turban. Durayd at once said, “This is al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām. By Allah! He alone will fight you and force you to flee from this place.” These people began to flee from there. Sayyidunā al-Zubayr pursued them. He killed most of them. He also cut off the head of Durayd ibn al-Ṣimmah and presented it to him. (Al-Bazzār, Kashf al-Astār, 1827)\nIn some reports a man said at the Battle of Ḥunayn, “Today we will not be defeated because of fewness of numbers.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ disliked this statement. Concerning this, Allah the Exalted revealed this verse:\n﴿وَّ يَوْمَ حُنَيْنٍ١ۙ اِذْ اَعْجَبَتْكُمْ كَثْرَتُكُمْ﴾ [التوبة: ٢٥]\nAnd on the day of Ḥunayn (too), when your great numbers made you complacent. (Dalāʾil al-Nubuwwah by al-Bayhaqī: 5/123)"],[885,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ reported: We fought Hawāzin alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. While we were eating our morning meal with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, a man arrived riding a red camel. He made it kneel, took a rope from the camel’s saddle strap and tethered it, then came forward and ate with the people while looking around. There were weak men among us, our riding animals were frail, and some of us were on foot. Suddenly, the man hurried away, reached his camel, untied it, made it kneel, mounted it, and urged it forward. The camel sped away, and a man riding a grayish she-camel pursued him. Salamah said: I set out running. I first reached the rear of the she-camel, then moved ahead until I reached the rear of the man’s camel. I continued forward until I seized the camel’s halter and made it kneel. When its knee touched the ground, I drew my sword and struck the man’s head, causing it to fall. I then returned leading the camel, with its saddle and the man’s weapons still upon it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the people met me, and he asked: “Who killed the man?” They replied, “Ibn al-Akwaʿ.” He said: “All of his personal spoils belong to him.”"],[886,"Sahl ibn al-Ḥanẓaliyyah reported that they traveled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of Ḥunayn and continued marching for a long time until evening. When it was time for prayer in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, a horseman arrived and said, “O Messenger of Allah, I rode ahead of you until I climbed such-and-such a mountain. I found all of Hawāzin gathered at Ḥunayn, together with their women, camels, and sheep.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled and said: “Those will be the spoils of the Muslims tomorrow, if Allah wills.” He then asked: “Who will guard us tonight?” Anas ibn Abī Marthad al-Ghanawī replied, “I will, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “Mount.” He mounted his horse and came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who instructed him: “Proceed toward this mountain pass until you reach its highest point, and do not let us be taken by surprise from your direction tonight.” When morning came, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to his place of prayer and performed two rakʿahs. He then asked: “Have you detected any sign of your horseman?” They replied, “No, O Messenger of Allah, we have not detected him.” The call announcing the commencement of prayer was made, and while praying, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ kept glancing toward the mountain pass. After completing the prayer and giving salām, he said: “Rejoice! Your horseman has come.” We began looking through the trees in the mountain pass, and there he was, approaching until he stopped before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and greeted him. He said, “I continued until I reached the highest point of this mountain pass, where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had ordered me to remain. When morning came, I surveyed both mountain passes but saw no one.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him: “Did you dismount at any time during the night?” He replied, “No, except to pray or relieve myself.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him: “You have secured the reward; there would be no blame upon you even if you performed no comparable deed after this.”"],[887,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ marched toward Ḥunayn. He then related the report, which includes: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah and asked him to lend one hundred suits of armor that he possessed, together with their necessary equipment. Ṣafwān asked, “Are you taking them by force, O Muḥammad?” He replied: “No, it is a guaranteed loan, which we shall return to you.”"],[888,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib reported that a man from Qays asked him, “Did you flee from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of Ḥunayn?” Al-Barāʾ replied, “But the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not flee. Hawāzin were skilled archers that day. When we charged them, they retreated, so we fell upon the spoils. They then confronted us with arrows. I certainly saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon his white mule, while Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith held its bridle, and he was saying: I am the Prophet—this is no lie; I am the son of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.” In another narration, he said: “As for me, I testify that the Prophet ﷺ did not turn away. Rather, the hasty men rushed forward, and Hawāzin showered them with arrows.”"],[889,"Abū Isḥāq reported that a man asked al-Barāʾ, “O Abū ʿUmārah, did you flee on the Day of Ḥunayn?” He replied, “No, by Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not turn away. However, some young and lightly equipped men among his Companions went forward without armor and with little or no weaponry. They encountered a people who were skilled archers, whose arrows scarcely ever missed—the combined forces of Hawāzin and Banū Naṣr. They showered them with arrows and hardly missed their targets. “They then returned toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was upon his white mule, with Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib leading it. He dismounted, sought Allah’s help and victory, and said: ʿI am the Prophet—this is no lie; I am the son of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.’ “He then arranged them in ranks.”"],[890,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: When we approached the valley of Ḥunayn, we descended into a deep, steeply sloping valley of Tihāmah, through which we could only descend sharply. It was in the darkness of early morning. The enemy had concealed themselves in its mountain paths, bends, and narrow passages, having assembled, prepared, and made themselves ready. By Allah, while we were descending, nothing startled us except that their battalions suddenly charged us as though they were a single man. The people were defeated and retreated, continuing onward without anyone turning to look at another. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ moved toward the right and called: “Come to me, O people! Come to me! I am the Messenger of Allah; I am Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh.” But nothing could stop the retreat; the camels crowded and collided with one another as the people fled. Only a small group of the Emigrants, the Anṣār, and members of his household remained with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Among the Emigrants who remained steadfast with him were Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. From his household were ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib; al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib; his son al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās; Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith; Rabīʿah ibn al-Ḥārith; Ayman ibn ʿUbayd, the son of Umm Ayman; and Usāmah ibn Zayd. A man from Hawāzin was riding a red camel at the front of the people. In his hand was a black banner fixed to the head of a long spear, while Hawāzin followed behind him. Whenever he caught up with someone, he stabbed him with his spear; and whenever the people moved beyond his reach, he raised his spear as a signal to those behind him, and they followed him. Ibn Isḥāq said: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah narrated to me from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jābir, from his father Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, who said: While that standard-bearer from Hawāzin was upon his camel doing what he was doing, ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib and a man from the Anṣār rushed toward him, intending to kill him.\nʿAlī approached him from behind and struck the camel’s hocks, causing it to collapse upon its hindquarters. The Anṣārī sprang upon the man and struck him with a blow that severed his foot halfway up the shin, causing him to fall from his saddle. The people then engaged one another in battle. By Allah, when those who had fled returned from their retreat, they found the captives already bound before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[891,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: “I certainly saw us on the Day of Ḥunayn, when both forces were turning away in retreat, while fewer than one hundred men remained with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[892,"Al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib reported: I was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of Ḥunayn. Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib and I remained close to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and did not leave him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was riding a white mule that Farwah ibn Nufāthah al-Judhāmī had given him as a gift. When the Muslims and the disbelievers met, the Muslims turned and retreated. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began urging his mule toward the disbelievers. Al-ʿAbbās said: I was holding the bridle of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ mule, restraining it so that it would not rush forward, while Abū Sufyān was holding the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ stirrup. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O ʿAbbās, call the Companions of the samurah tree.” Al-ʿAbbās had a powerful voice, so he said: I called at the top of my voice, “Where are the Companions of the samurah tree?” By Allah, when they heard my voice, they turned back as readily as cows return to their calves, saying, “Here we are! Here we are!” They then fought the disbelievers. The call was directed to the Anṣār: “O company of the Anṣār! O company of the Anṣār!” Then the call was restricted to Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj: “O Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj! O Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, mounted upon his mule and stretching himself upward, looked toward their fighting and said: “Now the furnace of battle has grown fierce.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then picked up some pebbles and threw them into the faces of the disbelievers. He said: “They are defeated, by the Lord of Muḥammad!” I went to look, and the battle still appeared to be continuing as before. Yet, by Allah, as soon as he threw those pebbles at them, I continued to see their strength weaken and their cause turn toward defeat."],[893,"Kathīr ibn al-ʿAbbās reported: Al-ʿAbbās and Abū Sufyān were with him—meaning the Prophet ﷺ. He addressed them and said: “Now the furnace of battle has grown fierce.” He also said: “Call out: O Companions of Sūrat al-Baqarah!”"],[894,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ reported: We fought at Ḥunayn with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When we confronted the enemy, I advanced and climbed a mountain pass. A man from the enemy confronted me, so I shot an arrow at him. He disappeared from my sight, and I did not know what had happened to him. I looked toward the enemy and saw that they had emerged from another mountain pass. They met the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, whereupon the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ turned back. I also returned in retreat. I was wearing two cloaks, one wrapped around my waist and the other over my shoulders. My lower garment came loose, so I gathered the two cloaks together. As I was retreating, I passed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who was riding his grayish-white mule. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Ibn al-Akwaʿ has certainly witnessed something terrifying.” When the enemy closed in upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he dismounted from the mule, took a handful of dust from the ground, threw it toward their faces, and said: “May the faces be disfigured!” There was not a single person among them whose eyes Allah did not fill with dust from that handful. They turned and fled, Allah Almighty defeated them, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed their spoils among the Muslims."],[895,"Ibn Shihāb reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ undertook the campaign of the Conquest—the Conquest of Makkah. He then set out with the Muslims who accompanied him, and they fought at Ḥunayn. Allah granted victory to His religion and to the Muslims. On that day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah one hundred camels, then another hundred, and then another hundred. Ibn Shihāb said: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me that Ṣafwān said: “By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave me what he gave me while he was the person I hated most. He continued giving to me until he became the person I loved most.”"],[896,"Anas reported: On the Day of Ḥunayn, the people retreated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ except for al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib and Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered al-ʿAbbās to call: “O Companions of Sūrat al-Baqarah! O company of the Anṣār!” He then intensified the call specifically to Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj. When they heard the call, they came forward. By Allah, I could only compare them to camels yearning for their young. When they met the enemy, the battle became fierce, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Now the furnace of battle has grown fierce.” He ﷺ took a handful of white pebbles, threw them, and said: “They are defeated, by the Lord of the Kaʿbah!” On that day, ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib fought more fiercely than anyone else before him."],[897,"Anas reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on the Day of Ḥunayn: “Cut them down completely,” and he gestured with his hand toward the throat."],[898,"Abū Qatādah ibn Ribʿī reported: We set out with the Prophet ﷺ in the year of Ḥunayn. When we met the enemy, the Muslims suffered a temporary setback. I saw a polytheist overpowering a Muslim, so I struck him from behind with my sword upon the junction of his neck and shoulder, cutting through his armor. He turned toward me and embraced me so tightly that I sensed the approach of death. Death then overtook him, and he released me. I caught up with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb and asked, “What has happened to the people?” He replied, “It is the decree of Allah Almighty.” The Muslims then returned, and the Prophet ﷺ sat down and declared: “Whoever kills an enemy and possesses evidence of it shall receive his personal spoils.” I asked, “Who will testify for me?” Then I sat down. The Prophet ﷺ repeated the same declaration, so I stood up. He asked: “What is the matter, O Abū Qatādah?” I told him what had happened. A man said, “He has spoken the truth. The slain man’s spoils are with me, so compensate him on my behalf.” Abū Bakr said, “No, by Allah! He will not deprive a lion from among the lions of Allah, who fights for Allah and His Messenger ﷺ, and give you his spoils!” The Prophet ﷺ said: “He has spoken the truth, so give them to him.” The man gave them to me, and I used them to purchase an orchard among Banū Salimah. That was the first property I accumulated in Islam."],[899,"Abū Muḥammad, the freedman of Abū Qatādah, reported that Abū Qatādah said: On the Day of Ḥunayn, I saw a Muslim fighting a polytheist while another polytheist was stealthily approaching the Muslim from behind to kill him. I hurried toward the man who was trying to take him by surprise. He raised his hand to strike me, but I struck his hand and severed it. He then seized me and embraced me so tightly that I became afraid. He eventually weakened and released his hold, so I pushed him away and killed him. The Muslims retreated, and I retreated with them. I then found ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb among the people and asked him, “What has happened to the people?” He replied, “It is the decree of Allah.” The people then returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared: “Whoever presents evidence that he killed an enemy shall receive his personal spoils.” I stood up to seek someone who could testify concerning the man I had killed, but I found no one to testify for me, so I sat down. I then reconsidered and mentioned the matter to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. A man sitting with him said, “The weapons of the slain man whom he mentioned are with me. Compensate him on my behalf.” Abū Bakr said, “No! He will not give them to a petty man from Quraysh while leaving a lion from among the lions of Allah who fights for Allah and His Messenger ﷺ.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then stood and returned the spoils to me. I purchased an orchard with them, and it was the first property I accumulated in Islam."],[900,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that Hawāzin came on the Day of Ḥunayn with their children, women, camels, and livestock. They arranged them in rows to make their numbers appear greater before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When the forces met, the Muslims turned and retreated, as Allah Almighty stated. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called: “O servants of Allah, I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger! O company of the Anṣār, I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger!” Allah then defeated the polytheists. ʿAffān said: “We did not strike with a sword or thrust with a spear.” The Prophet ﷺ declared that day: “Whoever kills a disbeliever shall receive his personal spoils.” Abū Ṭalḥah killed twenty men that day and took their spoils. Abū Qatādah said, “O Messenger of Allah, I struck a man at the junction of his neck and shoulder while he was wearing armor, but I was driven away from him. Find out who took it.” A man stood and said, “I took it. Compensate him for it and give it to me.”\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ was never asked for something without either giving it or remaining silent. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained silent. ʿUmar said, “No, by Allah! Allah would not grant it as spoils to one of His lions and then have it given to you!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed and said: “ʿUmar has spoken the truth.” Umm Sulaym had a dagger with her. Abū Ṭalḥah asked, “What is this you have with you?” She replied, “I took it so that if any polytheist comes near me, I can rip open his stomach with it.” Abū Ṭalḥah said, “O Messenger of Allah, do you not hear what Umm Sulaym is saying?”\nShe said, “O Messenger of Allah, kill those freed people who remained behind us; they fled and abandoned you.” He replied: “Allah has sufficed us and granted us an excellent outcome, O Umm Sulaym.”"],[901,"Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid reported: I saw the mark of a blow upon the hand of Ibn Abī Awfā. He said, “I sustained it while accompanying the Prophet ﷺ on the Day of Ḥunayn.” I asked, “Were you present at Ḥunayn?” He replied, “I was present even before that.”"],[902,"Anas reported that on the Day of Ḥunayn, Umm Sulaym carried a dagger. Abū Ṭalḥah saw her and said, “O Messenger of Allah, here is Umm Sulaym carrying a dagger.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked her: “What is this dagger?” She replied, “I took it so that if any polytheist comes near me, I can rip open his stomach with it.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began to laugh. She said, “O Messenger of Allah, kill those freed people who remained behind us; they fled and abandoned you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “O Umm Sulaym, Allah has sufficed us and granted us an excellent outcome.”"],[903,"It is reported from Sayyidunā Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: We took part with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the Battle of Ḥunayn, and he ﷺ said about a man who was called a Muslim, “This one is among the people of Hell.” When we entered the fighting, this man fought very fiercely and was wounded. It was then said to him ﷺ, “O Messenger of Allah! The man about whom you said just now, ‘He is among the people of Hell,’ fought very hard today, and he has died.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “He is headed to the Fire.” Some Muslims began to fall into doubt about this statement of his (wondering how such a devoted fighter could be one of the people of Hell). The people were in this state when it was said: He has not died, but he has been badly wounded. When night fell, he could not bear his wounds with patience and killed himself. When the Prophet ﷺ was informed of this, he said: “Allah is the Greatest. I bear witness that I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger.” Then he ordered Bilāl, may Allah be pleased with him, who announced among the people: “No one will enter Paradise except a soul that is upon Islam, and indeed Allah supports this religion even through wicked people.”"],[904,"Abū Mūsā reported: When the Prophet ﷺ had finished the Battle of Ḥunayn, he placed Abū ʿĀmir in command of an army and sent it to Awṭās. He encountered Durayd ibn al-Ṣimmah, who was killed, and Allah defeated his companions. Abū Mūsā said: The Prophet ﷺ sent me with Abū ʿĀmir. Abū ʿĀmir was struck in the knee by an arrow shot by a man from Jusham, and the arrow lodged in his knee. I reached him and asked, “O uncle, who shot you?” Abū ʿĀmir pointed and said, “That is the man who killed me—the one who shot me.” I headed toward him and caught up with him. When he saw me, he turned and fled. I pursued him, saying, “Are you not ashamed? Will you not stand your ground?” He stopped, and we exchanged two blows with our swords, and I killed him. I then returned to Abū ʿĀmir and said, “Allah has killed your assailant.” He said, “Remove this arrow.” I pulled it out, and fluid gushed from the wound. He said, “O son of my brother, convey my greetings of peace to the Prophet ﷺ and ask him to seek forgiveness for me.” Abū ʿĀmir appointed me to command the people after him. He remained alive for only a short while and then died. I returned and entered upon the Prophet ﷺ in his house. He was lying upon a bed woven with cords, with a mattress upon it, and the bed’s cords had left marks upon his back and sides. I informed him of what had happened to us and to Abū ʿĀmir, and told him that Abū ʿĀmir had asked him to seek forgiveness for him. He called for water, performed ablution, raised his hands, and prayed: “O Allah, forgive ʿUbayd Abū ʿĀmir.” I could see the whiteness of his armpits. He then prayed: “O Allah, on the Day of Resurrection, raise him above many of Your human creation.” I said, “Seek forgiveness for me as well.” He prayed: “O Allah, forgive ʿAbdullāh ibn Qays his sin, and admit him to an honorable entrance on the Day of Resurrection.” Abū Burdah said: One of the two supplications was for Abū ʿĀmir, and the other was for Abū Mūsā."],[905,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported that on the Day of Ḥunayn, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent an army to Awṭās. They encountered an enemy, fought them, overcame them, and took female captives. Some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ felt uneasy about having relations with them because they had husbands among the polytheists. Allah Almighty therefore revealed concerning this: “And also forbidden to you are married women, except those whom your right hands possess.” [Al-Nisāʾ 4:24] That is, they became lawful for them after their prescribed waiting period had ended."],[906,"Ḥanash al-Ṣanʿānī reported: We accompanied Ruwayfiʿ ibn Thābit al-Anṣārī in a campaign against a town in the western lands called Jarbah. He stood to address us and said: “O people, I will tell you only what I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say. He stood among us on the Day of Ḥunayn and said: “It is not lawful for a man who believes in Allah and the Last Day to water the crop of another with his own water”—meaning having intercourse with pregnant female captives. “Nor may he have intercourse with a previously married captive woman until he has ascertained that her womb is free of pregnancy”—meaning after acquiring her. “Nor may he sell any spoils before they have been divided; nor ride an animal belonging to the Muslims’ war booty until he exhausts it and then return it; nor wear a garment belonging to the Muslims’ war booty until he wears it out and then return it.”"],[907,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ besieged the people of al-Ṭā’if but did not achieve anything against them. He said: “We shall return, if Allah wills.” His Companions said, “Shall we return without conquering it?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told them: “Go out to fight tomorrow morning.” They went out to fight and suffered wounds. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then told them: “We shall return tomorrow.” They were pleased with this, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed."],[908,"Umm Salamah reported that an effeminate man was in her home while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was present. He said to Umm Salamah’s brother, “O ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Umayyah, if Allah grants you victory over al-Ṭā’if tomorrow, I shall direct you to the daughter of Ghaylān. She approaches with four folds and turns away with eight.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard him and said: “Such people must not enter upon you.” Al-Bukhārī added: At that time, he ﷺ was besieging al-Ṭā’if."],[909,"Abū Najīḥ al-Sulamī reported: We besieged the fortress of al-Ṭā’if with the Prophet of Allah ﷺ. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Whoever causes an arrow to reach the enemy shall receive one rank in Paradise.” He said: “That day, I caused sixteen arrows to reach their targets.”"],[910,"Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī reported: I heard Saʿd—who was the first person to shoot an arrow in the cause of Allah—and Abū Bakrah, who had climbed down from the fortress of al-Ṭā’if with a group of men and come to the Prophet ﷺ, say: We heard the Prophet ﷺ declare: “Whoever knowingly claims descent from someone other than his father, Paradise is forbidden to him.”"],[911,"A man from Thaqīf reported: We asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for three concessions, but he did not grant them to us. We said, “Our land is cold,” and asked him to grant us a concession concerning purification, but he did not grant it. We asked him to permit us to use gourd vessels for preparing intoxicating drinks, but he did not permit them to us even temporarily. We also asked him to return Abū Bakrah to us, but he refused and said: “He is one whom Allah has set free and whom His Messenger has set free.” Abū Bakrah had come out to the Prophet ﷺ and embraced Islam while he was besieging al-Ṭā’if."],[912,"Jābir reported: The people said, “O Messenger of Allah, the arrows of Thaqīf have pierced us, so supplicate to Allah against them.” He instead prayed: “O Allah, guide Thaqīf.”"],[913,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd ibn ʿĀṣim reported: When Allah granted His Messenger ﷺ the spoils on the Day of Ḥunayn, he distributed them among the people whose hearts were being reconciled to Islam, but gave nothing to the Anṣār. They appeared to feel some distress because they had not received what others had received. He addressed them, saying: “O company of the Anṣār, did I not find you astray and Allah guided you through me? Were you not divided and Allah united you through me? Were you not poor and Allah enriched you through me?” Whenever he said something, they replied, “Allah and His Messenger have bestowed the greater favor.” He asked: “What prevents you from answering the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” Whenever he said something, they replied, “Allah and His Messenger have bestowed the greater favor.” He said: “Had you wished, you could have said: ‘You came to us under such-and-such circumstances.’ Are you not pleased that the people should depart with sheep and camels while you depart with the Prophet ﷺ to your homes? Were it not for the migration, I would have been a man from the Anṣār. If the people took one valley and mountain pass while the Anṣār took another valley and pass, I would take the valley and pass of the Anṣār. The Anṣār are the inner garment, while the people are the outer garment. After me, you will encounter others being favored over you, so remain patient until you meet me at the Pool.”"],[914,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: When Allah granted His Messenger ﷺ the wealth of Hawāzin, the Prophet ﷺ began giving certain men one hundred camels each. Some of the Anṣār said, “May Allah forgive the Messenger of Allah ﷺ! He gives to Quraysh and leaves us, even though our swords are still dripping with their blood!” Anas said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was informed of their statement. He sent for the Anṣār and gathered them inside a leather tent, admitting no one else with them. When they had assembled, the Prophet ﷺ stood and asked: “What is this statement that has reached me concerning you?” The learned men of the Anṣār replied, “As for our leaders, O Messenger of Allah, they said nothing. But some of our younger men said, ‘May Allah forgive the Messenger of Allah ﷺ! He gives to Quraysh and leaves us, even though our swords are still dripping with their blood!’” The Prophet ﷺ said: “I give to men who only recently left disbelief, seeking to reconcile their hearts. Are you not pleased that the people should depart with wealth while you depart with the Prophet ﷺ to your homes? By Allah, what you take back with you is better than what they take back with them.” They replied, “O Messenger of Allah, we are pleased.” The Prophet ﷺ told them: “You will encounter severe favoritism after me. Remain patient until you meet Allah and His Messenger ﷺ, for I shall be waiting at the Pool.” Anas said: “But they did not remain patient.”"],[915,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: On the Day of Ḥunayn, Hawāzin, Ghaṭafān, and others advanced with their livestock and children. The Prophet ﷺ had ten thousand men with him, as well as some of the freed people of Makkah. They retreated from him until he was left alone. That day he made two distinct calls without mixing one with the other. He turned to his right and called: “O company of the Anṣār!” They replied, “Here we are at your service, O Messenger of Allah! Rejoice, for we are with you.” He then turned to his left and called: “O company of the Anṣār!” They replied, “Here we are at your service, O Messenger of Allah! Rejoice, for we are with you.” He was riding a white mule. He dismounted and declared: “I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger.” The polytheists were defeated, and abundant spoils were acquired that day. He distributed them among the Emigrants and the freed people of Makkah but gave nothing to the Anṣār. The Anṣār said, “When hardship comes, it is we who are summoned, but the spoils are given to others.” This reached him, so he gathered them inside a tent and asked: “O company of the Anṣār, what is this statement that has reached me concerning you?” They remained silent. He said: “O company of the Anṣār, are you not pleased that the people should depart with worldly possessions while you depart with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, taking him back to your homes?” They replied, “Indeed, O Messenger of Allah.” The Prophet ﷺ then said: “If the people took one valley and the Anṣār another mountain pass, I would take the pass of the Anṣār.” Hishām said: I asked, “O Abū Ḥamzah, were you present for that?” He replied, “Where else would I have been?”"],[916,"Anas reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O company of the Anṣār, did I not come to you while you were astray, and Allah Almighty guided you through me? Did I not come to you while you were divided, and Allah united you through me? Did I not come to you while you were enemies, and Allah reconciled your hearts through me?” They replied, “Indeed, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “Why do you not say: ‘You came to us afraid and we gave you security; expelled and we gave you shelter; abandoned and we supported you’?” They replied, “Rather, all favor in this belongs to Allah, Blessed and Most High, and to His Messenger.”"],[917,"Anas reported: Around the time of the Conquest of Makkah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed spoils among Quraysh, and the Anṣār became upset. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Are you not pleased that the people should depart with worldly possessions while you depart with the Messenger of Allah?” They replied, “Indeed.” He said: “If the people took one valley or mountain pass, I would take the valley or pass of the Anṣār.”"],[918,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd reported: On the Day of Ḥunayn, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ favored certain people in the distribution. He gave al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis one hundred camels and gave ʿUyaynah the same. He also gave to other Arab nobles and favored them in the distribution that day. A man said, “By Allah, this is a distribution in which justice was not observed and by which Allah’s pleasure was not intended!” I said, “By Allah, I shall inform the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” I went to him and told him what the man had said. His face changed until it became as red as ṣirf, and he said: “Who, then, will act justly if Allah and His Messenger do not act justly?” He then said: “May Allah have mercy upon Moses. He was harmed by more than this, yet he remained patient.” I said to myself, “Certainly, I shall never convey another such statement to him after this.”"],[919,"ʿAmr ibn Taghlib reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was brought some wealth—or captives—and distributed it. He gave to some men and left others without anything. He was informed that those whom he had left out were displeased. He praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: “To proceed: By Allah, I give to one man and leave another, although the one whom I leave is dearer to me than the one to whom I give. I give to certain people because of the anxiety and fear that I see within their hearts, while I entrust other people to the contentment and goodness that Allah has placed within their hearts. ʿAmr ibn Taghlib is among them.” By Allah, I would not exchange this statement of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for the finest red camels."],[920,"Ibn Shihāb said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went on an expedition for the conquest of Makkah, then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ marched toward Ḥunayn with the Muslims who were with him, and Allah granted victory to His religion and to the Muslims. And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah that day a hundred camels, then a hundred, then a hundred.\nIbn Shihāb said: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab told me that Ṣafwān said: By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave me what he gave me, although he was at that time the most hateful of people to me. He kept giving to me until he became the dearest of people to me."],[921,"Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb, Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah, ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn, and al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis one hundred camels each. He gave ʿAbbās ibn Mirdās less than that. ʿAbbās ibn Mirdās said: Do you place my share and the share of al-ʿUbayd Between ʿUyaynah and al-Aqraʿ? Neither Badr nor Ḥābis ever surpassed Mirdās when the people assembled. Nor was I inferior to either of them; Whomever you lower today will not be raised. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then completed his share to one hundred camels."],[922,"A Bedouin man reported: I crowded against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of Ḥunayn. I was wearing a thick sandal and stepped upon the foot of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He lightly struck me with a whip in his hand and said: “In the name of Allah! You have hurt me.” I spent the night reproaching myself and saying, “I hurt the Messenger of Allah ﷺ!” I passed such a night as only Allah knows. When morning came, a man called, “Where is so-and-so?” I said to myself, “By Allah, this must be because of what I did yesterday.” I went forward feeling afraid. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “Yesterday, you stepped upon my foot with your sandal and hurt me, so I struck you lightly with the whip. Here are eighty sheep; take them in compensation for it.”"],[923,"Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah reported that when the delegation of Hawāzin came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as Muslims, he stood to address them. They asked him to return their wealth and captives to them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Those whom you see are with me, and the speech dearest to me is that which is most truthful. Choose one of the two groups: either the captives or the wealth. I had already delayed the distribution while waiting for you.” After returning from al-Ṭā’if, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had waited for them for more than ten nights. When it became clear to them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would return only one of the two groups, they said, “We choose our captives.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood among the Muslims, praised Allah as He deserves to be praised, and then said: “To proceed: Your brothers have come to us in repentance, and I have decided to return their captives to them. Whoever among you is pleased to surrender his share freely may do so. Whoever wishes to retain his entitlement until we compensate him from the first spoils Allah grants us may do so.” The people replied, “We freely surrender them, O Messenger of Allah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “We cannot distinguish those among you who have granted permission from those who have not. Return, therefore, until your representatives convey your decision to us.” The people returned, and their representatives spoke to them. They then came back to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him that they had willingly granted permission. The narrator said: This is what reached me concerning the captives of Hawāzin."],[924,"ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb reported from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of Ḥunayn. Delegations from Hawāzin came to him and said, “O Muḥammad, we are a people of shared ancestry and kinship. Show us favor as Allah has shown favor to you, for a calamity has befallen us whose severity is not hidden from you.” He said: “Choose between your women and children on the one hand, and your wealth on the other.” They replied, “You have given us a choice between our families and our wealth; we choose our children.” He said: “Whatever belongs to me and Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib is yours. After I perform the noon prayer, say: ‘We seek the intercession of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with the believers, and the intercession of the believers with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, concerning our women and children.’” They did so. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “Whatever belongs to me and Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib is yours.” The Emigrants said, “Whatever belongs to us belongs to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” The Anṣār said the same. ʿUyaynah ibn Badr said, “As for what belongs to me and Banū Fazārah, no.” Al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis said, “As for me and Banū Tamīm, no.” ʿAbbās ibn Mirdās said, “As for me and Banū Sulaym, no.” But the two tribes replied to their leaders, “You have spoken falsely; rather, our shares belong to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O people, return their women and children to them. Whoever holds on to any portion of the spoils shall receive six camels of the prescribed zakāt-age classes from the first spoils Allah grants us.” He then mounted his riding animal. The people crowded around him, saying, “Distribute our spoils among us!” They pressed him against a samurah tree, and his cloak was pulled away. He said: “O people, return my cloak to me. By Allah, if you possessed livestock equal in number to the trees of Tihāmah, I would distribute it among you. You would then find me neither miserly, cowardly, nor dishonest.” He approached his camel, took a single hair from its hump, placed it between his index and middle fingers, raised it, and said: “O people, I have no share in these spoils—not even the equivalent of this hair—except the one-fifth, and even the one-fifth is returned for your benefit. Therefore, return even the thread and the needle, for stealing from the spoils will bring its perpetrator shame, fire, and disgrace on the Day of Resurrection.” A man stood holding a small ball of hair and said, “I took this to repair the saddle pad of my camel, whose back had become sore.” He said: “Whatever portion of it belongs to me and Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib is yours.” The man replied, “O Messenger of Allah, now that the matter has reached the seriousness I see, I have no need of it.” He threw it away."],[925,"Abū Mūsā reported: I was with the Prophet ﷺ while he was encamped at al-Jiʿrānah, between Makkah and Madinah, and Bilāl was with him. A Bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked, “Will you not fulfill what you promised me?” He replied: “Receive glad tidings.” The Bedouin said, “You have told me ‘Receive glad tidings’ too many times!” The Prophet ﷺ turned toward Abū Mūsā and Bilāl, appearing displeased, and said: “He has rejected the glad tidings, so you two accept them.” They replied, “We accept them.” He then called for a vessel containing water, washed his hands and face in it, and rinsed his mouth into it. He said: “Drink from it, pour some over your faces and chests, and receive glad tidings.” They took the vessel and did so. Umm Salamah called from behind the curtain, “Leave some for your mother.” So they left a portion of it for her."],[926,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mālik ibn Juʿshum al-Mudlijī reported from his father that Surāqah ibn Mālik told him: When the Prophet ﷺ set out as an emigrant toward Madinah, Quraysh offered one hundred camels to anyone who brought him back. While I was sitting in my tribe’s gathering place, a man came and said, “By Allah, I have just seen three riders pass by me, and by Allah, I believe one of them is Muḥammad, peace be upon him.” I signaled to him with my eyes to remain silent and said, “They are merely members of such-and-such a clan searching for a lost animal.” He replied, “Perhaps so,” and fell silent. I remained there for a short while, then stood and entered my home. I ordered that my horse be led to the bottom of the valley. I took my weapons out from behind my dwelling, took the arrows I used for divination, and put on my armor. I drew my divining arrows, and the result I disliked appeared: that I should not harm him. I had been hoping to bring him back and obtain the hundred camels. I mounted and followed his trail. While my horse was galloping with me, it stumbled and I fell from it. I again drew my divining arrows, and once more the result I disliked appeared: that I should not harm him. Nevertheless, I insisted upon pursuing him. I remounted. When the group came into view and I could see them, my horse stumbled beneath me. Its forelegs sank into the ground, and I fell from it. The horse pulled its legs free, and a cloud resembling smoke or dust rose behind them. I then knew that he had been protected from me and that he would prevail. I called out to them, “Wait for me! By Allah, I shall not cause you alarm, nor will anything you dislike come from me.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Ask him what he wants.” I said, “Write a document of protection for me.” He wrote it and threw it to me. I then remained silent and did not mention anything that had occurred. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Makkah and completed the campaign of Ḥunayn, I went out to him carrying the document he had written for me. As I headed toward him, I entered among one of the Anṣār’s battalions. They began prodding me with their spears and saying, “Keep away! Keep away!” until I drew near the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He was riding a she-camel, and I could see his leg in the stirrup, appearing like the heart of a palm tree. I raised my hand holding the document. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Today is a day of faithfulness and righteousness. Bring him near.” I then embraced Islam. Afterward, I returned home and later brought my obligatory charity to the Prophet ﷺ."],[927,"Anas reported: The Prophet ﷺ performed four ʿumrahs, all of them during Dhū al-Qaʿdah except the one performed together with his ḥajj: The ʿumrah from al-Ḥudaybiyah—or during the period of al-Ḥudaybiyah—in Dhū al-Qaʿdah; the ʿumrah in the following year during Dhū al-Qaʿdah; the ʿumrah from al-Jiʿrānah, where he distributed the spoils of Ḥunayn, during Dhū al-Qaʿdah; and the ʿumrah performed with his ḥajj."],[928,"In Muḥarram of 9 AH came the expedition of ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn al-Fazārī against Banū Tamīm, who were between al-Suqyā and the land of Banū Tamīm. The Prophet ﷺ sent ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn al-Fazārī, may Allah be pleased with him, with fifty Arab horsemen, among whom there was no Muhājir and no Anṣārī, toward Banū Tamīm. They travelled by night and stayed hidden by day, then attacked them in a wilderness.\nThe polytheists were grazing their livestock when they saw the Muslims and fled. Eleven men of them were captured. They found eleven women and thirty children in the settlement and dragged them to Madinah as well. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered that they be held in the house of Ramlah bint al-Ḥārith.\nSeveral chiefs of the tribe came, among them ʿUṭārid ibn Saʿd the chamberlain, al-Zibriqān ibn Badr, Qays ibn ʿĀṣim, al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis, Qays ibn al-Ḥārith, Nuʿaym ibn Saʿd, ʿAmr ibn al-Ahtam and Rabāḥ ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Mujāshiʿ.\nWhen the captives saw them, the women and children began to weep before them. They hurried to the door of the Prophet ﷺ and called out, “O Muḥammad, come out to us!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out, although Bilāl had already given the iqāmah for the prayer. They clung to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and spoke with him, and he stayed with them. Then he went, led the Ẓuhr prayer and sat in the courtyard of the mosque. They put ʿUṭārid ibn Ḥājib forward, and he spoke and delivered a speech. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered Thābit ibn Shammās, and he replied. It was about them that this verse was revealed: ﴿اِنَّ الَّذِيْنَ يُنَادُوْنَكَ مِنْ وَّرَآءِ الْحُجُرٰتِ اَكْثَرُهُمْ لَا يَعْقِلُوْنَ۰۰۴﴾ Those who call you from behind the chambers, most of them do not use reason. (The Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned their captives and prisoners to them.)\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ, may Allah be pleased with him, to Banū al-Muṣṭaliq, who were among Khuzāʿah. They had accepted Islam and built mosques. When they heard of al-Walīd’s coming, twenty of their men set out to bring sheep and camels to him in joy at his arrival. When he saw them, he returned to Madinah and told the Prophet ﷺ that they had fought him with weapons and had resisted the collection of charity. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to send men against them to fight them.\nThis news reached that people, and two riders who had met al-Walīd came to him ﷺ and informed the Prophet ﷺ of the true account of the event. Concerning this, this verse was revealed: ﴿يٰۤاَيُّهَا الَّذِيْنَ اٰمَنُوْۤا اِنْ جَآءَكُمْ فَاسِقٌۢ بِنَبَاٍ فَتَبَيَّنُوْۤا اَنْ تُصِيْبُوْا قَوْمًۢا بِجَهَالَةٍ﴾ O you who believe! If a wrongdoer (fāsiq) brings you news, verify it carefully, lest you harm a people out of ignorance.\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited the Qur’an to them. He sent ʿAbbād ibn Bishr with them to take the charity from their wealth, to inform them of the laws of Islam and to teach them the Qur’an. ʿAbbād did not go beyond what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had ordered, nor did he neglect any right. He stayed with them ten days, then came back to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ happily."],[929,"In Ṣafar of 9 AH, the expedition of Quṭbah ibn ʿĀmir ibn Ḥadīdah was sent toward the Khathʿam in the district of Bīshah, near Tubālah.\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Quṭbah ibn ʿĀmir ibn Ḥadīdah, may Allah be pleased with him, with twenty men toward the tribe of Khathʿam in the district of Tubālah. He ordered them to attack them suddenly. They set out riding ten camels, which they used in turns. They seized a man and questioned him, but he pretended to be mute before them. Then he began calling out to the tribe, so they struck off his neck. Then they waited until the tribe had gone to sleep and attacked them all at once. The fighting was so fierce that there were many wounded on both sides. Quṭbah ibn ʿĀmir killed whomever he struck.\nThey drove camels and sheep back to Madinah. A flood came and stood between the polytheists and the Muslims, and those people could find no way to reach Quṭbah. After the fifth was set aside, four camels fell to each man’s share, and one camel was counted as equal to ten sheep."],[930,"It is reported from Sayyidunā Ruʿayyah al-Suḥaymī, may Allah have mercy on him: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote a letter on red leather and sent it to him. He showed disrespect to the Prophet’s letter and put it under his bucket. The Prophet sent an army to teach him a lesson. The fighters went and took possession of everything he had. He alone fled, without clothes, and went to his daughter, who was married among Banū Hilāl. She had become Muslim, and the other members of the household had also accepted Islam. The place where all the people sat was in her house. Ruʿayyah climbed over the wall from behind the house and reached her. Seeing him, the daughter quickly threw a cloth over him and asked about the situation. He said, “A great calamity has struck your father. Everything I had has been seized: wealth, children, everything.” The daughter said, “You were invited to Islam; why did you not accept?” He asked, “Where is your husband?” She said, “In the camel pen.” He went to him. He asked in surprise what the matter was. He said, “Something terrible has happened. I have been robbed of everything. My wealth and children have been seized. I want to reach Muḥammad ﷺ before the children and wealth are distributed.” He said, “Very well, take my mount.” He said, “I do not need a mount; I have one.” He quickly took only some water with him.\nHis cloth was so small that if he covered his face his private parts were exposed, and if he covered his private parts his face showed. He tried not to be seen by anyone and so reach Madinah. He reached Madinah, tied his mount there and came to him wrapped in a cloak. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting in the mosque after the Fajr prayer. He said, “O Messenger of Allah! Stretch out your hand; I want to pledge allegiance to you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stretched out his blessed hand. When he went to pledge allegiance, he drew his hand back. He did this three times. The third time he asked, “Who are you?” He said, “I am Ruʿayyah al-Suḥaymī.” He took his hand and raised it and said: “Community of Muslims! This is the same Ruʿayyah al-Suḥaymī to whom I sent a letter. He put my letter under his bucket.” Now he began to plead, “O Messenger of Allah! Give me back my children and wealth.” He said: “Your wealth has been distributed. Take whichever of your children you find.” He went out and looked around. He saw his son standing by a mount in one place. He came back and said, “O Messenger of Allah! That is my son.” He said to Sayyidunā Bilāl, may Allah be pleased with him: “Bilāl, go with him. Ask the child, ‘Is this your father?’ If he acknowledges it, send him with him.” Sayyidunā Bilāl, may Allah be pleased with him, went to the boy and asked, “Is this your father?” He said, “Yes.” Sayyidunā Bilāl, may Allah be pleased with him, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said in amazement, “O Messenger of Allah! I have never seen such a lack of affection and feeling between father and child.” He said: “That is the nature of the Bedouins.”"],[931,"In Rabīʿ al-Awwal of 9 AH came the expedition of al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Sufyān al-Kilābī against Banū Kilāb. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent an army toward al-Qurṭāʾ, led by al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Sufyān ibn ʿAwf of Banū Abī Bakr al-Kilābī, may Allah be pleased with him, and with them was also Uṣayd ibn Salamah ibn Qurṭ. At al-Zujj Lādā they met those people and invited them to Islam, but they refused, so they fought them and defeated them.\nUṣayd met his father Salamah, who was on horseback at a pool in al-Zujj. He invited his father to Islam, but he abused him and his religion. Uṣayd struck both legs of his father’s horse with a sword. The horse fell, and Salamah stood in the water leaning on his spear. Uṣayd kept standing holding Salamah until someone else came to him and killed him; his son did not kill him."],[932,"In Rabīʿ al-Ākhir of 9 AH came the expedition of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, against al-Fuls, the idol of the tribe of Ṭayyiʾ.\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, with one hundred and fifty Anṣār, on a hundred camels and fifty horses, toward al-Fuls to demolish it. With them the rāyah (the large banner) was black and the liwāʾ (the small banner) was white.\nAt the break of dawn they fell suddenly upon the settlement of the family of Ḥātim, demolished and ruined al-Fuls, and filled their hands with captives, camels and sheep. Among the captives was the sister of ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim. In the treasury of al-Fuls three swords were found, one named Rasūb, the second al-Mikhdham and the third al-Yamānī, and three coats of mail were also found. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had appointed Abū Qatādah in charge of the captives and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAtīk over the livestock and goods. When they camped at Rakak, they divided the spoils. For the share of the Prophet ﷺ, Rasūb and al-Mikhdham were set aside; later the third sword also came to him, and the fifth was also set aside. The family of Ḥātim was spared; they did not distribute them but brought them to Madinah."],[933,"In Rabīʿ al-Ākhir of 9 AH came the expedition of ʿUkkāshah ibn Miḥṣan al-Asadī, may Allah be pleased with him, toward the land of ʿUdhrah and Balī."],[934,"And this expedition is called the Expedition of al-ʿUsrah (the expedition of hardship), according to the saying of Allah the Exalted:\nAllah has turned in mercy to the Prophet, and to the Muhājirīn and the Anṣār who followed him in the hour of hardship, after the hearts of a party of them had almost swerved; then He turned to them in mercy. Indeed He is Kind and Merciful to them. Al-Tawbah: verse 117\nAnd this was because there was a shortage of food, drink and mounts, the harshness of the weather, the great number of enemies, and the distance of the journey. It rarely happened that the Prophet ﷺ set out for an expedition and did not give out news of another one, except in this expedition, because in this expedition the Prophet ﷺ informed them beforehand so that they could prepare themselves. And it took place in the ninth year of the Hijrah, about a year after returning from the siege of al-Ṭāʾif.\nAnd the reason for setting out was that Heraclius, the ruler of Rome, gathered a large army of Romans and Arab tribes to fight the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the believers. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ learned of their intention, and his policy was that whenever he learned that some people were preparing for war, he would take the initiative in attacking them, that is, a defensive attack. So he decided to set out against them quickly, before they reached Madinah. The two armies assembled at Tabūk, and the army of the Muslims numbered more than about thirty thousand."],[935,"Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān reported that when ʿUthmān was besieged, he looked out over those surrounding him and said: “I adjure you—and I adjure only the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ—do you not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘Whoever digs the well of Rūmah shall have Paradise,’ and I dug it? Do you not know that he said: ‘Whoever equips the Army of Hardship shall have Paradise,’ and I equipped it?” They confirmed that what he said was true."],[936,"Kaʿb ibn Mālik reported: Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to undertake a campaign, he would usually conceal it by giving the impression that he intended another destination. The Campaign of Tabūk was the exception. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ undertook it during intense heat, facing a long journey through desert terrain and an expedition against a numerous enemy. He therefore made the matter clear to the Muslims so they could prepare properly to face their enemy, and he informed them of the destination he intended."],[937,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samurah reported: When the Prophet ﷺ was equipping the Army of Hardship, ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān came to him carrying one thousand dinars in his garment. He poured them into the Prophet’s ﷺ lap. The Prophet ﷺ began turning the coins over with his hand and repeatedly saying: “Nothing Ibn ʿAffān does after this day will harm him.”"],[938,"Abū Mūsā reported: My companions sent me to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to ask him for riding animals, as they wished to accompany him in the Army of Hardship—the Campaign of Tabūk. I said, “O Prophet of Allah, my companions have sent me to ask you to provide them with mounts.” He replied: “By Allah, I shall not provide you with any mounts.” I had unknowingly approached him while he was upset. I returned saddened by the Prophet’s ﷺ refusal and afraid that the Prophet ﷺ might have become displeased with me. I returned to my companions and informed them of what the Prophet ﷺ had said. Only a short while passed before I heard Bilāl calling, “O ʿAbdullāh ibn Qays!” I responded, and he said, “Answer the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; he is calling you.” When I came to him, he said: “Take these two paired camels and these two paired camels”—six camels that he had just purchased from Saʿd—“and take them to your companions. Tell them: ‘Allah—or the Messenger of Allah ﷺ—has provided you with these mounts, so ride them.’” Abū Mūsā said: I took the camels to my companions and said, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has provided you with these mounts. But by Allah, I shall not leave you until some of you accompany me to those who heard what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said when I first asked him on your behalf and he refused, and then saw him give them to me afterward. I do not want you to think that I have attributed to him something he did not say.” They replied, “By Allah, we regard you as truthful, but we shall do as you wish.”\nAbū Mūsā went with some of them to those who had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ initially refuse to provide the mounts and then give them afterward. They related to them exactly what Abū Mūsā had reported."],[939,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out for Tabūk, he appointed ʿAlī as his deputy. ʿAlī asked, “Are you leaving me behind with the women and children?” He replied: “Are you not pleased that your position in relation to me should be like the position of Aaron in relation to Moses, except that there is no prophet after me?”"],[940,"Kaʿb ibn Mālik reported that the Prophet ﷺ set out for the Campaign of Tabūk on a Thursday, and he liked to set out on Thursdays."],[941,"Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called the people to join the Campaign of Tabūk. I went to my family, but when I returned, the first group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had already departed. I began calling throughout Madinah, “Is there anyone who will provide a man with a mount in exchange for his share of the spoils?” An elderly man from the Anṣār called to me and asked, “Will his share belong to us if we take turns carrying him and provide his food?” I replied, “Yes.” He said, “Then travel with Allah’s blessing.” I set out with the finest of companions. When Allah granted us spoils, I received some young she-camels and drove them to him. He came out and sat upon one of his camels’ packs. He said, “Drive them away from me.” Then he said, “Drive them toward me.” He said, “I see that your young she-camels are fine animals.” I replied, “They are the spoils that belong to you according to our agreement.” He said, “Take your young she-camels, son of my brother. It was something other than your share that we sought.” In another narration, Wāthilah said: I set out to emigrate to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When the people assembled—some coming forward and others standing—the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would approach every seated person he saw and ask: “Do you have any need?” He began with the first row, then the second, then the third, until he came near me. He asked: “Do you have any need?” I replied, “Yes, O Messenger of Allah.” He asked: “What do you need?” I replied, “Islam.”\nHe said: “It is better for you.” He then asked: “Will you also migrate?” I replied, “Yes.” He asked: “The migration of the desert or permanent migration?” I asked, “Which is better?” He replied: “Permanent migration means that you remain with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The migration of the desert means that you return to your desert home while remaining obligated to hear and obey during hardship and ease, in what you dislike and what pleases you, and even when others are favored over you.” I extended my hand and pledged allegiance to him. He added a qualification for me that I had not added for myself: “To the extent of your ability.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then called the people to the Campaign of Tabūk. I went to my family and found my father sitting with his back toward the sun. I greeted him with the greeting of Islam. He asked, “Have you abandoned your religion?” I replied, “I have embraced Islam.” He said, “Perhaps Allah will place good in it for us and for you.” I was satisfied with this response from him. While I was with him, my sister came and greeted me. I said, “My sister, provide me with provisions as a woman provides her brother who is departing for battle.” She brought me some dough in a bucket, with the bucket placed inside a provision bag. I returned after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had already departed and began calling, “Is there anyone who will provide a man with a mount in exchange for his share of the spoils?” An elderly man from the Anṣār called to me and asked, “Will his share belong to us if we take turns carrying him and provide his food?” I replied, “Yes.” He said, “Then travel with Allah’s blessing.” I set out with the finest of companions. Beyond what we had agreed, he provided me with additional mounts and gave me food specifically for myself besides the food I shared with him. When Allah granted us spoils, I received some young she-camels and drove them to him while he was in his tent. I called him, and he came out and sat upon one of his camels’ packs. He said, “Drive them away from me,” so I drove them away. He then said, “Drive them toward me,” so I drove them toward him. He said, “I see that your young she-camels are fine animals.” I replied, “They are the spoils that belong to you according to our agreement.” He said, “Take your young she-camels, son of my brother. It was something other than your share that we sought.”"],[942,"Yaʿlā ibn Umayyah reported: I accompanied the Prophet ﷺ during the Campaign of the Army of Hardship. Yaʿlā used to say, “That campaign is the deed of mine in which I place the greatest confidence.” ʿAṭāʾ said: Ṣafwān reported that Yaʿlā said: I had a hired worker who fought with another man, and one of them bit the other’s hand. ʿAṭāʾ said: Ṣafwān told me which of the two bit the other, but I forgot. The man who had been bitten pulled his hand out of the biter’s mouth, causing one of the biter’s front teeth to be pulled out. They went to the Prophet ﷺ, who declared that no compensation was due for the tooth. ʿAṭāʾ said: I believe he also reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Was he supposed to leave his hand inside your mouth so that you could gnaw upon it as a male camel gnaws?”"],[943,"Abū Hurayrah—or Abū Saʿīd, as al-Aʿmash was uncertain—reported: During the Campaign of Tabūk, the people suffered severe hunger. They said, “O Messenger of Allah, if you permit us, we could slaughter our camels used for carrying water, eat their meat, and use their fat.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Do so.” ʿUmar then came and said, “O Messenger of Allah, if they do that, the number of riding animals will become too small. Instead, ask them to bring whatever remains of their provisions, then supplicate to Allah to place blessing in them. Perhaps Allah will provide through that.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “Yes.” He called for a leather mat and spread it out, then asked the people to bring the remnants of their provisions. One man brought a handful of millet, another brought a handful of dates, and another brought a piece of bread, until a small amount had accumulated upon the mat. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for blessing in it and then said: “Fill your containers.” They filled their containers until no container in the entire army remained without being filled. They ate until they were satisfied, and some food still remained. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “I testify that there is no deity except Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah. No servant will meet Allah with these two testimonies, without doubting them, and then be barred from Paradise.”"],[944,"Abū Hurayrah reported: We were traveling with the Prophet ﷺ when the people’s provisions ran out, to the extent that they considered slaughtering some of the camels carrying their supplies. ʿUmar said, “O Messenger of Allah, perhaps you could gather what remains of the people’s provisions and supplicate to Allah over them.” He did so. Those possessing wheat brought their wheat, and those possessing dates brought their dates. Mujāhid added: “Those possessing date pits brought their date pits.” The narrator asked, “What did they do with date pits?” He replied, “They would suck them and then drink water.” The Prophet ﷺ supplicated over the food until the people filled their provision bags. He then said: “I testify that there is no deity except Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah. No servant will meet Allah with these two testimonies, without doubting them, except that he will enter Paradise.”"],[945,"Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd al-Anṣārī reported: We accompanied the Prophet ﷺ on the Campaign of Tabūk, and the riding animals became severely exhausted. The people complained to the Prophet ﷺ about their animals’ exhaustion. He waited until they reached a narrow passage and then traveled through it, saying: “Pass through in the name of Allah.” The people passed before him with their riding animals, and he began lightly blowing upon their backs while supplicating: “O Allah, make them carry in Your cause. You provide conveyance upon the strong and the weak, and upon the moist and the dry, on land and at sea.” Before we reached Madinah, the animals had become so energetic that they pulled against us through their reins. Faḍālah said: “I understood the Prophet’s ﷺ supplication concerning the strong and the weak, but what did ‘the moist and the dry’ mean?” Later, when we reached Syria, we took part in the naval campaign of Cyprus. When I saw the ships upon the sea and what they carried, I understood the Prophet’s ﷺ supplication."],[946,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb was asked, “Tell us about the circumstances of the Army of Hardship.” He replied: We set out for Tabūk during intense heat. We stopped at a place where we became so thirsty that we thought our necks would break. A man would go out searching for water and remain away so long that we thought his neck had broken from thirst. A man would even slaughter his camel, squeeze the liquid from its stomach contents and drink it, then place the remainder over his abdomen. Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq said, “O Messenger of Allah, Allah has accustomed you to receiving good when you supplicate, so pray for us.” He asked: “Would you like me to do that?” Abū Bakr replied, “Yes.” The Prophet ﷺ raised his hands and had not yet lowered them when a cloud shaded us and poured down rain. The people filled all the containers they possessed. We then went to investigate and found that the rain had not extended beyond the army’s encampment."],[947,"Abū al-Ṭufayl reported: A disagreement of the kind that sometimes occurs between people arose between one of the men associated with the mountain pass and Ḥudhayfah. The man asked him, “I adjure you by Allah, how many were the men of the mountain pass?” The people said to Ḥudhayfah, “Answer him, since he has asked you.” He replied, “We used to be informed that they were fourteen. If you were among them, then they numbered fifteen. “I testify by Allah that twelve of them were at war with Allah and His Messenger in this world and on the Day when the witnesses arise. “Three were excused because they said, ‘We did not hear the caller of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we did not know what the others intended.’ “The Prophet ﷺ was traveling through rocky terrain and said: ‘The water is scarce, so no one must reach it before me.’ “He found that some men had preceded him to it, so he cursed them that day.”"],[948,"Abū al-Ṭufayl reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was returning from the Campaign of Tabūk, he ordered a caller to announce: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ will take the mountain pass, so no one else is to take it.” While Ḥudhayfah was leading the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ mount and ʿAmmār was driving it from behind, a group of masked men approached upon their riding animals. They closed in upon ʿAmmār while he was driving the Prophet’s ﷺ mount. ʿAmmār began striking the faces of their animals. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Ḥudhayfah: “Continue, continue,” until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ descended from the pass. When he reached the bottom, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dismounted and ʿAmmār returned. He asked: “O ʿAmmār, did you recognize the men?” He replied, “I recognized most of their riding animals, but the men had covered their faces.” He asked: “Do you know what they intended?” ʿAmmār replied, “Allah and His Messenger know best.” He said: “They intended to startle the Messenger of Allah’s mount and throw him from it.” ʿAmmār asked one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “I adjure you by Allah, how many do you know the men of the mountain pass to have been?” He replied, “Fourteen.” ʿAmmār said, “If you were among them, then they numbered fifteen.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ excused three of them because they said, “By Allah, we did not hear the caller of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we did not know what the others intended.” ʿAmmār said: “I testify that the remaining twelve are at war with Allah and His Messenger in this world and on the Day when the witnesses arise.” Abū al-Ṭufayl also mentioned that during that campaign, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was informed that the available water was scarce. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered a caller to announce: “No one is to reach the water before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached it, he found that a group of men had already arrived before him, so he cursed them that day."],[949,"Ḥudhayfah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out during the Campaign of Tabūk and was informed that the water was scarce. He ordered a caller to announce among the people: “No one is to reach the water before me.” When he reached the water, he found that some men had preceded him, so he cursed them."],[950,"Maḥmūd ibn Labīd reported from some men of Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal. The narrator said: I asked Maḥmūd, “Did the people recognize the hypocrites among them?” He replied, “Yes, by Allah. A man would recognize hypocrisy in his brother, father, paternal uncle, or other member of his clan, yet they continued to maintain normal relations with one another despite that.” Maḥmūd then said: Some men from my tribe informed me about a hypocrite whose hypocrisy was well known. He traveled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wherever he went. After what occurred to the people at al-Ḥijr, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated, and Allah sent a cloud that rained until the people had all quenched their thirst. We approached the hypocrite and said, “Woe to you! Can there be any doubt after this?” He replied, “It was merely a passing cloud.” Ibn Isḥāq said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued traveling until, at one point along the road, his she-camel went missing. His Companions went out to search for it. A Companion named ʿUmārah ibn Ḥazm was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He had participated in the pledge at al-ʿAqabah and the Battle of Badr and was the paternal uncle of Banū ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm. Traveling with him was Zayd ibn al-Luṣayt of Banū Qaynuqāʿ, who was a hypocrite. Ibn Isḥāq said: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah narrated to me from Maḥmūd ibn Labīd, from some men of Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal, who said: While Zayd ibn al-Luṣayt was staying at ʿUmārah’s encampment and ʿUmārah was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Zayd said, “Does Muḥammad not claim to be a prophet and inform you about news from heaven, while he does not even know where his she-camel is?”\nWhile ʿUmārah was with him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “A man has said: ‘This Muḥammad informs you that he is a prophet and claims to tell you about matters from heaven, yet he does not know where his she-camel is.’ By Allah, I know only what Allah teaches me. Allah has now shown me where it is. It is in this valley, in such-and-such a mountain path, where a tree has caught its reins. Go and bring it to me.” They went and brought it back. ʿUmārah ibn Ḥazm returned to his encampment and said, “By Allah, something astonishing has just happened. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed us of a statement made by a man, which Allah had disclosed to him. The man said such-and-such”—mentioning the words of Zayd ibn al-Luṣayt.\nA man who had been at ʿUmārah’s encampment but had not been present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “By Allah, Zayd made that very statement before you arrived.” ʿUmārah turned upon Zayd, striking him on the neck and saying, “Come here, servants of Allah! A calamity has been staying in my encampment without my knowledge! Get out of my camp, enemy of Allah, and do not accompany me!”"],[951,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: When the Prophet ﷺ passed through al-Ḥijr, he said: “Do not enter the dwellings of those who wronged themselves, lest what afflicted them should afflict you, unless you enter weeping.” He then covered his head and hastened onward until he had passed beyond the valley."],[952,"Sabrah ibn Maʿbad al-Juhanī reported that when the Prophet ﷺ stopped at al-Ḥijr, he told his Companions: “Whoever has kneaded anything using water from this—their well—must throw it away.” Those who had prepared dough threw away their dough, and those who had prepared ḥays threw away their ḥays."],[953,"Muḥammad ibn Abī Kabshah al-Anmārī reported from his father—whose name was ʿAmr ibn Saʿd, though it is also said to have been ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd—who said: During the Campaign of Tabūk, the people hastened to enter the dwellings of the inhabitants of al-Ḥijr. When this reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he called among the people: “Gather for prayer.” I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was holding his camel, and he was saying: “Why are you entering upon a people with whom Allah was angry?” One of the men called to him, “We are amazed by them, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “Shall I not tell you of something more astonishing than that? A man from among yourselves informs you of what occurred before you and what will occur after you. Remain upright and follow the proper course, for your punishment would be of no consequence to Allah; and there will come a people who will be utterly unable to protect themselves.”"],[954,"Abū Ḥumayd reported: We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Campaign of Tabūk. When we reached Wādī al-Qurā, we passed an orchard belonging to a woman. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Estimate its produce.” We estimated it, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ estimated it at ten wasqs. He told the woman: “Keep an accurate account of it until we return to you, if Allah wills.” We continued until we reached Tabūk. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “A violent wind will blow upon you tonight. None of you should stand during it, and whoever has a camel must securely fasten its tether.” A violent wind blew. One man stood up, and the wind carried him away until it threw him at the two mountains of Ṭayyiʾ. The messenger of Ibn al-ʿUlamāʾ, the ruler of Aylah, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a letter and presented him with a white mule. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote a reply to him and presented him with a mantle. We then continued on our return until we reached Wādī al-Qurā. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked the woman about her orchard: “How much produce did it yield?” She replied, “Ten wasqs.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I intend to travel quickly. Whoever wishes may hasten with me, and whoever wishes may remain behind.” We continued until we came within sight of Madinah. He said: “This is Ṭābah, and this is Uḥud—a mountain that loves us and that we love.” He then said: “The best of the Anṣār’s neighborhoods is the neighborhood of Banū al-Najjār, followed by that of Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal, then that of Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj, and then that of Banū Sāʿidah. There is goodness in every neighborhood of the Anṣār.” Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah caught up with us, and Abū Usayd said to him, “Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ranked the neighborhoods of the Anṣār and placed us last?” Saʿd caught up with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of Allah, you ranked the neighborhoods of the Anṣār and placed us last.” He replied: “Is it not sufficient for you to be among the best?”"],[955,"Shihāb al-ʿAnbarī reported: A companion and I came to Ibn ʿAbbās and encountered Abū Hurayrah at Ibn ʿAbbās’s door. He asked, “Who are you?” We told him. He said, “Go to some people whose knowledge consists of dates and water; every valley flows according to its capacity.” We replied, “May your goodness increase. Seek permission for us to enter upon Ibn ʿAbbās.” He then sought permission for us. We heard Ibn ʿAbbās narrating from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered a sermon on the Day of Tabūk and said: “Among the people, none is like a man who holds the reins of his horse, striving in the cause of Allah and avoiding the evils of the people; or like a man living in the desert among his sheep, who honors his guest and fulfills the rights due from him.” I asked, “Did he truly say that?” He replied, “He said it.” I asked again, “Did he truly say that?” He replied, “He said it.” I asked a third time, “Did he truly say that?” He replied, “He said it.” I then proclaimed Allah’s greatness, praised Allah, and gave thanks."],[956,"The saying of Allah the Exalted: And upon the three who were left behind, until, when the earth became narrow for them despite its vastness, and their own souls became narrow for them, and they realized that there is no refuge from Allah except in Him, then He turned to them in mercy so that they might repent. Indeed Allah is the Accepter of repentance, the Most Merciful.\nAnd those three were Kaʿb ibn Mālik, Hilāl ibn Umayyah and Murārah ibn al-Rabīʿ, may Allah be pleased with them, and all of them were Companions from the Anṣār."],[957,"Kaʿb ibn Mālik reported: I never remained behind when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went on a military campaign except during the Campaign of Tabūk. I had also remained behind during the Campaign of Badr, but no one who remained behind on that occasion was reproached. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had set out only to intercept the caravan of Quraysh, until Allah brought the Muslims and their enemy together without a prior appointment. I was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the night of al-ʿAqabah, when we pledged ourselves firmly to Islam. I would not exchange my attendance at al-ʿAqabah for attendance at Badr, even though Badr is more widely remembered among the people. As for my circumstances, I had never been stronger or more prosperous than I was when I remained behind during that campaign. By Allah, I had never possessed two riding animals at the same time before that campaign, but during it I possessed two. Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended a campaign, he would conceal its true destination by giving the impression that he intended somewhere else. This continued until that campaign. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ undertook it during intense heat, facing a long journey through desert terrain and a numerous enemy. He therefore made the matter clear to the Muslims so they could prepare properly for the campaign, and he informed them of the destination he intended. The Muslims accompanying the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were numerous, and no comprehensive register contained all their names—meaning a military record. Kaʿb said: Any man who wished to absent himself assumed that his absence would go unnoticed unless revelation from Allah came down concerning him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ undertook that campaign at a time when the fruits had ripened and the shade had become pleasant. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the Muslims accompanying him began preparing. I would go out in the morning intending to prepare with them, but I would return without accomplishing anything, saying to myself, “I am capable of preparing whenever I wish.” I continued delaying until the people became fully serious about their departure. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the Muslims accompanying him set out, while I had still made none of my preparations. I said to myself, “I shall prepare a day or two after his departure and then catch up with them.” After they had departed, I went out in the morning to prepare, but returned without accomplishing anything. The following morning I went out again, yet once more returned without accomplishing anything. I continued in this condition until they had traveled swiftly ahead and the campaign had passed me by. I considered setting out to catch up with them—and I wish I had done so—but that was not decreed for me. Whenever I went out among the people after the departure of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and walked among those who remained, it saddened me that I saw no one except a man suspected of hypocrisy or someone among the weak whom Allah had excused. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not mention me until he reached Tabūk. While sitting among the people there, he asked: “What has happened to Kaʿb?” A man from Banū Salimah replied, “O Messenger of Allah, his fine garments and his admiration of his appearance detained him.” Muʿādh ibn Jabal said, “What an evil thing you have said! By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, we have known nothing but good about him.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained silent. Kaʿb ibn Mālik continued: When I learned that the Prophet ﷺ had begun his return journey, anxiety came upon me. I began thinking of false excuses and asking myself, “How will I escape his displeasure tomorrow?” I sought the assistance of every wise member of my family in devising an excuse. But when it was announced that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was about to arrive, every false idea vanished from me. I realized that I could never escape the consequences by using any lie, so I resolved to tell him the truth. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in the morning. Whenever he returned from a journey, he would begin by going to the mosque, where he would perform two rakʿahs and then sit to receive the people. When he had done so, those who had remained behind came to him. They began presenting excuses and swearing oaths to him. They numbered a little over eighty men. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ accepted their outward declarations, renewed their pledges of allegiance, sought forgiveness for them, and entrusted their hidden intentions to Allah. I came to him, and when I greeted him, he smiled the smile of one who was angry. He then said: “Come forward.” I walked toward him and sat before him. He asked: “What caused you to remain behind? Had you not purchased your riding animal?” I replied, “Indeed, I had. By Allah, if I were sitting before anyone else in this world, I am confident that I could escape his displeasure by presenting an excuse, for I have certainly been granted skill in argument.” “But, by Allah, I know that if I tell you a lie today that makes you pleased with me, Allah may soon make you displeased with me. If I tell you the truth, however, and you become displeased with me because of it, I can still hope for Allah’s pardon. “No, by Allah, I had no excuse. By Allah, I had never been stronger or more prosperous than I was when I remained behind you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “As for this man, he has spoken the truth. Rise and wait until Allah passes judgment concerning you.” I rose, and some men from Banū Salimah sprang up and followed me. They said, “By Allah, we never knew you to have committed any sin before this. Yet you were incapable of presenting an excuse to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ like those who remained behind! The Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ prayer for forgiveness would have been enough to erase your sin.” By Allah, they continued reproaching me until I nearly returned to the Prophet ﷺ and declared my own earlier statement false. Then I asked them, “Has anyone else experienced the same thing as I have?” They replied, “Yes. Two men said the same thing you said, and they were given the same response you received.” I asked, “Who are they?” They replied, “Murārah ibn al-Rabīʿ al-ʿAmrī and Hilāl ibn Umayyah al-Wāqifī.” They named two righteous men who had participated in Badr and whose example could strengthen me. When they mentioned them, I continued on my way. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the Muslims from speaking to the three of us, distinguishing us from all the others who had remained behind. The people avoided us and changed their behavior toward us, until the very earth seemed unfamiliar to me and no longer appeared to be the land I knew. We remained in that condition for fifty nights. My two companions submitted quietly, remained in their homes, and wept. As for me, I was the youngest and strongest of the three. I would go out, attend the prayers with the Muslims, and walk through the markets, but no one would speak to me. I would approach the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and greet him while he sat among the people after prayer. I would ask myself, “Did he move his lips to return my greeting, or did he not?” I would then pray close to him and steal glances at him. Whenever I turned my attention toward my prayer, he would look at me; but whenever I turned toward him, he would turn away from me. When the people’s estrangement became too difficult for me, I walked to the orchard of Abū Qatādah, my cousin and the person dearest to me, and climbed over its wall. I greeted him, but by Allah, he did not return my greeting. I said, “Abū Qatādah, I adjure you by Allah: Do you know that I love Allah and His Messenger?” He remained silent. I repeated the question and adjured him again, but he remained silent. I repeated it a third time and adjured him. He replied, “Allah and His Messenger know best.” My eyes overflowed with tears. I turned away and climbed back over the wall. While I was walking through the marketplace of Madinah, I saw a Nabatean farmer from Syria who had come to sell food in Madinah. He was asking, “Who will direct me to Kaʿb ibn Mālik?” The people began pointing toward me. When he reached me, he handed me a letter from the Ghassānid king. It read: “To proceed: We have been informed that your companion has treated you harshly. Allah has not placed you in a land of humiliation and neglect. Join us, and we shall console and support you.” When I read it, I said, “This too is part of the trial.” I took the letter to the oven and burned it. When forty of the fifty nights had passed, a messenger from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to me and said, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commands you to separate yourself from your wife.” I asked, “Should I divorce her, or what should I do?” He replied, “No. Separate yourself from her and do not approach her.” The Prophet ﷺ sent the same command to my two companions. I told my wife, “Go to your family and remain with them until Allah judges this matter.” Kaʿb continued: The wife of Hilāl ibn Umayyah came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of Allah, Hilāl ibn Umayyah is an elderly, helpless man who has no servant. Would you object if I continued serving him?” He replied: “No, but he must not approach you.” She said, “By Allah, he has no desire for anything. By Allah, he has continued weeping from the time this matter began until this very day.” Some members of my family said to me, “Why do you not seek permission from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning your wife, just as he permitted the wife of Hilāl ibn Umayyah to serve him?” I replied, “By Allah, I shall not seek the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ permission concerning her. How do I know what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say if I sought his permission, when I am a young man?” I remained in that condition for another ten nights, completing fifty nights from the time the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had forbidden the people from speaking to us. After I performed the dawn prayer on the morning of the fiftieth night, I was upon the roof of one of our houses. As I sat in the condition Allah described—my soul had become constrained for me and the earth, despite all its vastness, had become constrained—I heard someone who had climbed Mount Salʿ calling at the top of his voice: “Kaʿb ibn Mālik, rejoice!” I immediately fell in prostration and knew that relief had arrived. After performing the dawn prayer, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced that Allah had accepted our repentance. The people set out to bring us the glad tidings, and other bearers of good news went toward my two companions. One man galloped toward me on horseback, while another man from Aslam ran and climbed the mountain. The voice reached me faster than the horse. When the man whose voice I had heard came to congratulate me, I removed my two garments and gave them to him as a reward for his glad tidings. By Allah, I possessed no other garments that day. I borrowed two garments, put them on, and set out toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. People met me group after group, congratulating me upon the acceptance of my repentance and saying, “May Allah’s acceptance of your repentance bring you happiness.” Kaʿb said: I entered the mosque and found the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seated with the people around him. Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillāh rose and hurried toward me, shook my hand, and congratulated me. By Allah, no other man from the Emigrants stood to greet me, and I shall never forget this kindness of Ṭalḥah. Kaʿb continued: When I greeted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, his face was radiant with joy, and he said: “Rejoice in the best day you have experienced since your mother gave birth to you.” I asked, “Is this from you, O Messenger of Allah, or from Allah?” He replied: “No, it is from Allah.” Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was pleased, his face would shine as though it were a piece of the moon, and we recognized this quality in him. When I sat before him, I said, “O Messenger of Allah, as part of my repentance, I wish to relinquish all my wealth as charity for Allah and His Messenger.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Retain some of your wealth, for that is better for you.” I replied, “Then I shall retain my share at Khaybar.” I then said, “O Messenger of Allah, Allah saved me only because I spoke the truth. Part of my repentance is that I shall speak nothing but the truth for as long as I live.” By Allah, I do not know of any Muslim whom Allah has tested and blessed in truthfulness, from the time I made that pledge to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, more favorably than He has tested and blessed me. From the day I made that pledge to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until this very day, I have never deliberately told a lie. I hope Allah will preserve me in truthfulness throughout the remainder of my life. Allah revealed to His Messenger ﷺ: “Allah has certainly accepted the repentance of the Prophet and the Emigrants…” [Al-Tawbah 9:117] through His words: “And be with the truthful.” [Al-Tawbah 9:119] By Allah, after guiding me to Islam, Allah has never bestowed upon me a blessing greater in my estimation than my truthfulness to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I did not lie to him and consequently perish as those who lied perished. When revelation came down, Allah spoke about those who lied in the harshest terms He used concerning anyone. He, Blessed and Most High, said: “They will swear by Allah to you when you return to them…” through His words: “Indeed, Allah is not pleased with the defiantly disobedient people.” [Al-Tawbah 9:95–96] Kaʿb said: The matter of the three of us was deferred, unlike the matter of those whose excuses the Messenger of Allah ﷺ accepted when they swore their oaths to him, renewed their pledges, and received his prayer for forgiveness. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ postponed judgment concerning us until Allah decided our case. It was concerning this that Allah said: “And also the three whose case had been deferred.” [Al-Tawbah 9:118] The words Allah used concerning our being “deferred” do not refer merely to our remaining behind from the campaign. Rather, they refer to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ postponing our case and separating it from the cases of those who swore oaths and offered excuses that he accepted."],[958,"Jābir reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained at Tabūk for twenty days, shortening the prayers."],[959,"Abū Ḥumayd al-Sāʿidī reported: We returned with the Prophet ﷺ from the Campaign of Tabūk. When we came within sight of Madinah, he said: “This is Ṭābah, and this is Uḥud—a mountain that loves us and that we love.”"],[960,"Anas reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the Campaign of Tabūk and drew near Madinah, he said: “There are people in Madinah who accompanied you in reward whenever you traveled any distance or crossed any valley.” They asked, “O Messenger of Allah, even though they remained in Madinah?” He replied: “Even though they remained in Madinah; a valid excuse prevented them from joining you.”"],[961,"Al-Sā’ib ibn Yazīd reported: I remember going out with the children to Thaniyyat al-Wadāʿ to welcome the Prophet ﷺ when he returned from the Campaign of Tabūk."],[962,"When the Prophet ﷺ was returning from Tabūk to Madinah, revelation from Allah came down upon the Prophet ﷺ at the place called Dhū Awān (al-Tawbah 107): And there are those who have built a mosque for harm and disbelief, and to cause division among the believers, and as a station for one who had fought Allah and His Messenger before. And they will surely swear, “We intended nothing but good,” and Allah bears witness that they are liars. ... So the Prophet of Allah ﷺ sent men, before going to Madinah, to demolish that mosque. Its details are in the Book of Tafsīr."],[963,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allah be pleased with him, in Rajab of 9 AH as an expedition with four hundred and twenty horsemen toward Ukaydir ibn ʿAbd al-Malik at Dūmat al-Jandal, which was fifteen nights’ journey from Madinah. Ukaydir was of the tribe of Kindah, had become their king, and was a Christian. Khālid, may Allah be pleased with him, reached him at a time when, on a moonlit night, he had come out of the fortress with his brother to hunt a wild ox. The army of Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allah be pleased with him, attacked him, and Ukaydir was captured. His brother Ḥassān resisted and fought until he was killed. Those who were with the two fled and entered the fortress. Khālid, may Allah be pleased with him, granted Ukaydir protection from being killed, on the condition that he come to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and hand Dūmat al-Jandal over to him. He agreed and made peace with Khālid for two thousand camels, eight hundred (heads of) animals, four hundred coats of mail and four hundred spears. They set aside a special share for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and distributed the rest among their companions, each man receiving five shares. Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allah be pleased with him, set out with Ukaydir and his brother Muṣāʾ, who was in the fortress, taking all the goods on which peace had been made. He brought Ukaydir to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He gave him a gift. He made peace with him on the jizyah (tax), and he and his brother both remained safe and both were set free. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote him a decree mentioning their security and the terms of peace. That day he made the mark of his thumb on the decree.\nThis was the time when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out from Tabūk toward Madinah in the direction of a caravan, as Ibn Isḥāq and al-Bayhaqī mentioned. The details of this gift are in the following ḥadīth:"],[964,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib reported: A silk garment was presented to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. His Companions began touching it and expressing amazement at its softness. He said: “Are you amazed by its softness? The handkerchiefs of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh in Paradise are better and softer than this.”"],[965,"Anas reported: A robe of fine silk was presented to the Prophet ﷺ, although he prohibited men from wearing silk. The people were amazed by it. He said: “By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, the handkerchiefs of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh in Paradise are finer than this.” Another narration states that Ukaydir of Dūmat al-Jandal presented a garment to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[966,"Qays ibn al-Nuʿmān reported: A neighbor of mine completed his recitation of the Qur’an before ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. He related: The cavalry of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out, and Ukaydir of Dūmat al-Jandal heard about it. He came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of Allah, I have learned that your cavalry has departed, and I fear for my land and wealth. Write me a document ensuring that nothing belonging to me will be harmed, for I acknowledge the obligation I owe.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote a document for him. Ukaydir then brought out a cloak woven with gold, of the kind that the Persian emperor used to bestow upon them. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Take your cloak back, for no one wears something like this in the world except that he will be deprived of it in the Hereafter.” The man took it back. When he reached his residence, he felt distressed that his gift had been rejected. He returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of Allah, we are a family who find it difficult to bear the rejection of our gift, so please accept my gift.” He told him: “Go and give it to ʿUmar.” ʿUmar had heard what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said, so he asked, “Has something new been revealed concerning me? You said about this cloak what I heard, and then you sent it to me?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed until he placed his hand over his mouth. He then said: “I did not send it to you so that you could wear it. I sent it so that you could sell it and benefit from its price.”"],[967,"He ﷺ sent a party in which Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb and al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah al-Thaqafī, may Allah be pleased with them both, took part. The commander of this party was Sayyidunā Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allah be pleased with him. When these people arrived there and the work of breaking the idol began, all the men, women and children of the tribe, even the veiled women, came out from behind their veils, and these people went far away from there. They did not want to see al-Lāt demolished, and they believed that the idol would protect itself. So al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, may Allah be pleased with him, took a large axe and said to his companions, “Shall I not show you a spectacle?” His companions said, “Why not?” He struck the idol one blow with that axe, gave a cry and fell on his face, pretending as if he had fainted. Meanwhile the valley of al-Ṭāʾif resounded with the joy and clamor of these people, saying that al-Lāt had struck down al-Mughīrah. They said, “Now tell us, Mughīrah! If you have courage, strike again! Did you not know that it destroys its enemy? Whoever has courage, let him step forward and break it for us to see! By Allah! Nothing can be done to it.”\nThey were saying things of this kind when al-Mughīrah, may Allah be pleased with him, rose laughing and said, “By Allah! O Banū Thaqīf! I was joking with you. This wretched thing is only clay and stone.” Then he struck the door and shattered it. Then he climbed up its walls and began pulling it down until he leveled it with the ground.\nIts keeper of the key said, “Its foundation will surely become angry and will make them sink into the earth.” When al-Mughīrah, may Allah be pleased with him, heard this, Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allah be pleased with him, said, “Leave me; I will dig up its foundation too.” He even dug out its soil, then burned it to ashes, and they took away its coverings that were placed over it and its jewelry and went off.\nBanū Thaqīf were greatly amazed and bewildered. An old woman of theirs said, “The shameless ones left it helpless at night, and they have broken it to pieces and gone.” The Muslims returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with the spoils. He distributed everything at once, and praised and glorified Allah the Exalted for having helped His Prophet and given His religion dominance.\nMūsā ibn ʿUqbah related this event at considerable length. In it is this addition: When the Muslims returned after carrying out the mission, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Sayyidunā Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb and Sayyidunā al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, may Allah be pleased with them both, with the people to demolish the idol temple. The two went with the people and reached al-Ṭāʾif. Sayyidunā al-Mughīrah wanted to send Sayyidunā Abū Sufyān ahead of himself among the people, but he refused and said, “You go to the people first.” Sayyidunā Abū Sufyān, may Allah be pleased with him, took his belongings and stayed at a place called Dhū Hadm. Sayyidunā al-Mughīrah went forward and began demolishing the idol temple. His people, Banū Muʿattib, tried to stop him. They feared that calamity would strike them as it had struck Sayyidunā ʿUrwah. The women of Banū Thaqīf began wailing and lamenting with uncovered heads…"],[968,"Abū Hurayrah reported that during the pilgrimage over which the Prophet ﷺ appointed Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq before the Farewell Pilgrimage, Abū Bakr sent him on the Day of Sacrifice with a group to announce among the people: “After this year, no polytheist may perform pilgrimage, and no naked person may circumambulate the House.”"],[969,"In the month of Rabīʿ al-Ākhir or Jumādā al-Ūlā of 10 AH, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allah be pleased with him, to Najrān, to Banū al-Ḥārith, and ordered him to invite them to Islam three times before fighting. If they accepted, good; otherwise he was to fight. So Khālid did so, and all these people became Muslim. Khālid began to teach them the religion and to teach them the noble Qur’an, and this is just what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had ordered Khālid, and Khālid ibn al-Walīd sent a letter to the Prophet ﷺ to this effect.\nبِسْمِ اللّٰهِ الرَّحْمٰنِ الرَّحِيْمِ (In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful)\nTo Sayyidunā Muḥammad, the Prophet, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, from Khālid ibn al-Walīd. Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. I praise Allah, other than whom there is no god. To proceed: O Messenger of Allah, may peace be upon you, you sent me to Banū al-Ḥārith ibn Kaʿb and ordered me to invite them to Islam for three days; then if they accepted Islam, I was to stay among them and teach them the rulings of Islam and the Qur’an and teach them the Sunnah of the Messenger; and if they did not accept Islam, I was to fight them. So I came to them and, as ordered, invited them to Islam for three days and sent horsemen to them, saying, “O Banū al-Ḥārith, accept Islam and you will be safe.” So these people accepted Islam and refrained from fighting. Now I am staying among them and am calling them to the commands, prohibitions and rulings of the religion. I will act in accordance with whatever order is issued from you in the future. And peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings.\nThe Prophet ﷺ sent this reply to Sayyidunā Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allah be pleased with him:\nIn the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful\nFrom Muḥammad, the Prophet, the Messenger of Allah, to Khālid ibn al-Walīd. Peace be upon you. I praise Allah the Exalted, other than whom there is no god. To proceed: Your letter reached us with the messenger, and we learned that Banū al-Ḥārith ibn Kaʿb accepted Islam and, before fighting, bore witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah, and this is the guidance of Allah that He has bestowed upon them. So give them the good news of the reward of Allah and warn them of the punishment of Allah, and bring with you to us some of their own men. And peace be upon you, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings.\nSo Sayyidunā Khālid, may Allah be pleased with him, on seeing this decree, brought with him these men of Banū al-Ḥārith and came into the presence of the Prophet: Qays ibn Ḥuṣayn Dhū al-Ghuṣṣah, Yazīd ibn ʿAbd al-Madān, Yazīd ibn al-Muḥajjal, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qurād al-Ziyādī, Shaddād ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Qanānī and ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḍibābī. When these people came into the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the Messenger of Allah saw them and said: “Who are these people? It seems they are Indians.” It was said, “O Messenger of Allah, these are Banū al-Ḥārith ibn Kaʿb.” These people greeted the Prophet with salām and said, “We bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah and that there is no god but Allah.” He said: “I too bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that I am His Messenger.”\nThen the Prophet ﷺ said: “You are the same people who, whenever you fight an enemy of yours, put him to flight.” They fell silent. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the same again. They still kept silent, until the Messenger said for the fourth time: “You are the same people who, whenever you fight anyone, put him to flight?” At that moment Yazīd ibn ʿAbd al-Madān said, “O Messenger of Allah! Yes, we are the same people who, whenever we fight anyone, put him to flight,” and he too said this four times. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “If Khālid had not written to me that you have accepted Islam, I would have laid your heads beneath your feet.” Yazīd ibn ʿAbd al-Madān said, “We are not grateful to you or to Khālid.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Then to whom are you grateful?” He said, “We are grateful to Allah, who guided us along with you, O Messenger of Allah.” The Messenger of Allah said: “You speak the truth.” Then he said: “Tell me, by what means did you prevail over your opponents in the days of Jāhiliyyah?” They said, “O Messenger of Allah, we did not prevail over anyone.” He said: “No, you did prevail.” Then they said, “O Messenger of Allah! We would unite and fight the enemy, and we never took the lead in wronging anyone.”\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “You have spoken the truth.” Then the Prophet ﷺ appointed Qays ibn Ḥuṣayn as commander of Banū al-Ḥārith and sent these people off at the end of Shawwāl or the beginning of Dhū al-Qaʿdah, and four months after these people reached their people, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away."],[970,"The expedition of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, toward Yemen. It is said that this expedition took place twice. One was in Ramadan of 10 AH. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Sayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, to Yemen, made a banner (liwāʾ) for him, tied a turban on his head with his own hands and said: “Go, and do not turn to look around. When you descend into their open ground, do not fight them until they fight you.” Sayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, set out with three hundred horsemen. This was the first army to enter those settlements, and that settlement was in the land of Madhḥij. He spread out his companions, and they brought back plunder and spoils, children and women, camels and sheep and so on. Sayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, had appointed Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb al-Aslamī over the spoils, and whatever the people obtained they gathered with him.\nSayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, met a group and invited them to Islam, but they refused and shot arrows and threw stones. He drew up his companions in ranks and gave his banner to Masʿūd ibn Sinān al-Aslamī, and attacked them. Twenty of the polytheists were killed, and then they fled. Sayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, refrained from pursuing them. He invited them to Islam, and they hurried and accepted it. A group of their chiefs pledged allegiance on Islam and said, “We are the chiefs of our people who are behind us; these are our charity payments, so take Allah’s right from them.” Sayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, gathered all the spoils, then divided them into five parts and wrote on one of the parts, “This is for Allah.” When lots were cast, the first to come out was the fifth. Sayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, distributed the remaining spoils among his companions, then returned and came to the Prophet ﷺ at Makkah, where he ﷺ had come for Ḥajj in 10 AH."],[971,"ʿAlī reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me to Yemen. I said, “O Messenger of Allah, you are sending me to people who are older than I am so that I may judge between them.” He replied: “Go, for Allah Most High will make your tongue firm and guide your heart.”"],[972,"Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb reported: The Prophet ﷺ sent ʿAlī to Khālid to take possession of the one-fifth share of the spoils. I disliked ʿAlī, and he had performed a ritual bath. I said to Khālid, “Do you not see what this man has done?” When we returned to the Prophet ﷺ, I mentioned the matter to him. He asked: “O Buraydah, do you dislike ʿAlī?” I replied, “Yes.” He said: “Do not dislike him, for he is entitled to more than that from the one-fifth.”"],[973,"Buraydah reported: I disliked ʿAlī with an intensity with which I had never disliked anyone else. I loved a man from Quraysh only because he also disliked ʿAlī. That man was appointed to command a cavalry force, and I accompanied him solely because of his dislike for ʿAlī. We acquired captives, so he wrote to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking him to send someone to divide the spoils and set aside the one-fifth. The Prophet ﷺ sent ʿAlī to us. Among the captives was a young woman who was one of the finest captives. ʿAlī set aside the one-fifth and distributed the remainder. He later came out with water dripping from his head. We asked, “Abū al-Ḥasan, what is this?” He replied, “Did you not see the young woman who was among the captives? I divided the spoils and set aside the one-fifth. She became part of the one-fifth, then part of the share belonging to the Prophet’s ﷺ household, and thereafter part of ʿAlī’s family share, so I had relations with her.” The commander wrote to the Prophet of Allah ﷺ concerning the matter. I said, “Send me with the letter,” so he sent me to confirm its contents. I began reading the letter and saying after every point, “He has spoken the truth.” The Prophet ﷺ took hold of my hand and the letter and asked: “Do you dislike ʿAlī?” I replied, “Yes.” He said: “Then do not dislike him. If you already love him, increase your love for him. By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, the share belonging to ʿAlī’s family from the one-fifth is greater than a single young woman.” Buraydah said: After this statement of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, no one among the people was dearer to me than ʿAlī."],[974,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported: While ʿAlī was in Yemen, he sent the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a piece of unrefined gold still mixed with soil. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ divided it among four men: al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis al-Ḥanẓalī, ʿUyaynah ibn Badr al-Fazārī, ʿAlqamah ibn ʿUlāthah al-ʿĀmirī of Banū Kilāb, and Zayd al-Khayr al-Ṭāʾī of Banū Nabhān. Quraysh became angry and said, “Do you give to the chiefs of Najd while leaving us?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “I have done so only to reconcile their hearts.” A man with a thick beard, prominent cheekbones, sunken eyes, a protruding forehead, and a shaved head came forward and said, “Fear Allah, O Muḥammad!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “Who would obey Allah if I disobeyed Him? He entrusts me with the people of the earth, yet you do not trust me?” The man then turned away. Another man from the gathering—whom the narrators believed to be Khālid ibn al-Walīd—asked permission to kill him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “From the progeny or kind of this man will emerge people who recite the Qur’an, but it will not pass beyond their throats. They will kill the people of Islam and leave the idolaters alone. They will pass through Islam just as an arrow passes through its target. If I live to encounter them, I shall destroy them as the people of ʿĀd were destroyed.”"],[975,"Al-Barāʾ reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent us to Yemen with Khālid ibn al-Walīd. He later sent ʿAlī to replace him and said: “Tell Khālid’s companions: Whoever wishes to remain and continue the campaign with you may do so, and whoever wishes to return may return.” I was among those who remained with ʿAlī, and I acquired several ounces of silver as spoils."],[976,"Saʿīd ibn Abī Burdah reported from his father that the Prophet ﷺ sent his grandfather Abū Mūsā and Muʿādh to Yemen and instructed them: “Make matters easy and do not make them difficult. Give glad tidings and do not drive people away. Cooperate with one another.” Abū Mūsā said, “O Prophet of Allah, in our land there is a drink made from barley called mizr and a drink made from honey called bitʿ.” He replied: “Every intoxicant is forbidden.” They departed, and Muʿādh asked Abū Mūsā, “How do you recite the Qur’an?” He replied, “Standing, sitting, and while riding my mount. I recite it in portions at different times.” Muʿādh said, “As for me, I sleep and I stand in prayer. I seek reward for my sleep just as I seek reward for standing in prayer.” Each set up a tent, and they would visit one another. Muʿādh visited Abū Mūsā and found a man bound there. He asked, “What is this?” Abū Mūsā replied, “He is a Jewish man who embraced Islam and then renounced it.” Muʿādh said, “I shall certainly strike off his head.”"],[977,"Abū Burdah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Abū Mūsā and Muʿādh ibn Jabal to Yemen, appointing each over a separate province, for Yemen was divided into two provinces. He instructed them: “Make matters easy and do not make them difficult. Give glad tidings and do not drive people away.” Each of them then proceeded to his assigned territory. Whenever either of them traveled through his own territory and came near his companion, he would renew his contact with him and greet him. Muʿādh was traveling through his territory near his companion Abū Mūsā. He rode his mule until he reached him. Abū Mūsā was sitting with the people gathered around him, and a man was present whose hands had been bound to his neck. Muʿādh asked him, “ʿAbdullāh ibn Qays, what is the matter with this man?” He replied, “This is a man who disbelieved after embracing Islam.” Muʿādh said, “I shall not dismount until he is executed.” Abū Mūsā replied, “He was brought here for that very purpose, so dismount.” Muʿādh said, “I shall not dismount until he is executed.” Abū Mūsā ordered that the man be executed, and Muʿādh then dismounted. He asked, “ʿAbdullāh, how do you recite the Qur’an?” Abū Mūsā replied, “I recite it in portions at different times.” He asked, “How do you recite it, Muʿādh?” Muʿādh replied, “I sleep during the first part of the night and then rise after receiving my allotted share of sleep. I recite whatever Allah has decreed for me, and I seek reward for my sleep just as I seek reward for standing in prayer.”"],[978,"Abū Mūsā reported that the Prophet ﷺ sent him and Muʿādh to Yemen and instructed them: “Make matters easy and do not make them difficult. Give glad tidings and do not drive people away. Cooperate with one another and do not disagree.”"],[979,"Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī reported: The Prophet ﷺ sent Muʿādh ibn Jabal and me to Yemen. I said, “O Messenger of Allah, a drink called mizr is made from barley in our land, and another drink called bitʿ is made from honey.” He replied: “Every intoxicant is forbidden.”"],[980,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Muʿādh ibn Jabal to Yemen, he told him: “You will come to a people who possess Scripture. When you reach them, invite them to testify that there is no deity except Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah. “If they obey you in that, inform them that Allah has obligated them to perform five prayers during every day and night. “If they obey you in that, inform them that Allah has obligated them to give charity that is taken from their wealthy and returned to their poor. “If they obey you in that, beware of taking the most valuable portions of their wealth. Fear the supplication of the oppressed, for there is no barrier between it and Allah.”"],[981,"Muʿādh reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to Yemen, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out with him to advise him. Muʿādh was mounted, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ walked beside and beneath his riding animal. When he had finished advising him, he said: “Muʿādh, perhaps you will not meet me again after this year. Perhaps you will pass by this mosque of mine and my grave.” Muʿādh wept intensely at the thought of separation from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ then turned his face toward Madinah and said: “The people closest to me are those who fear Allah, whoever they may be and wherever they may be.” In another narration, the Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not weep, Muʿādh, for this kind of weeping is from Satan.”"],[982,"Jarīr ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Bajalī reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “Will you not relieve me of Dhū al-Khalaṣah?” I replied, “Certainly.” I set out with one hundred and fifty horsemen from Aḥmas, who were skilled cavalrymen. I, however, could not remain firmly seated upon a horse. I mentioned this to the Prophet ﷺ. He struck my chest with his hand until I could see the mark of his hand upon my chest, and he prayed: “O Allah, make him firm and make him one who guides others and is himself rightly guided.” After that, I never fell from a horse. Dhū al-Khalaṣah was a temple in Yemen belonging to Khathʿam and Bajīlah. It contained idols that were worshipped and was called “the Kaʿbah.” Jarīr reached it, burned it with fire, and destroyed it. When Jarīr reached Yemen, there was a man there who sought decisions through divining arrows. The man was told, “The messenger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is here. If he captures you, he will strike off your head.”\nWhile the man was using the arrows, Jarīr came upon him and said, “You must break them and testify that there is no deity except Allah, or I shall strike off your head.” The man broke them and pronounced the testimony of faith. Jarīr then sent a man from Aḥmas, known as Abū Arṭāh, to bring the Prophet ﷺ the glad tidings. When he reached the Prophet ﷺ, he said, “O Messenger of Allah, by the One who sent you with the truth, I did not come until I had left it looking like a mangy camel.” The Prophet ﷺ invoked blessings five times upon the cavalry and men of Aḥmas."],[983,"Jarīr reported: During the pre-Islamic period, there was a temple called Dhū al-Khalaṣah. It was also known as the Yemeni Kaʿbah and the Syrian Kaʿbah. The Prophet ﷺ said to me: “Will you not relieve me of Dhū al-Khalaṣah?” I set out with one hundred and fifty riders. We destroyed it and killed those whom we found stationed there. I returned to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him, and he supplicated for us and for Aḥmas."],[984,"Ibn Isḥāq said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Makkah and had finished with Tabūk, and Thaqīf accepted Islam and pledged allegiance, the delegations of the Arabs began to come to him from every side. He ﷺ said: “Rather, the Arabs were waiting for Islam, waiting for the tribe of Quraysh and for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to give the command. For the Quraysh were the leaders and guides of the people, and the people of the Sacred House.” Sīrat Ibn Hishām (559-560). The story of the conquest is in al-Bukhārī (4302), in the ḥadīth of ʿAmr ibn Salamah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Arabs were waiting for the conquest to bring them to Islam. He said: They would say, “Leave him and his people; if he overcomes them, then he is a true prophet.” So when Makkah was conquered, the people began to accept Islam, and my father also accepted Islam.\nIbn Hishām said: Abū ʿUbaydah told me that this was in the ninth year of the Hijrah and that it is called the Year of Delegations.\nHowever, it cannot be denied that some delegations came even before this year, such as the delegation of Ḍimām ibn Thaʿlabah in the fifth year of the Hijrah, and the delegation of Muzaynah in the fifth year of the Hijrah, and others besides these.\nImām al-Bukhārī presented several of these delegations in his Ṣaḥīḥ with authentic reports. And Muḥammad ibn Saʿd was far more extensive in mentioning these delegations, whose total number was more than sixty; most of them he narrated from his teacher al-Wāqidī, who, despite his knowledge of the Sīrah and Maghāzī of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, is accused (of unreliability).\nHere I will mention only several delegations with authentic evidence, and some of them I will mention only as events, but this does not mean that they are not established or that they are fabricated. Rather I say with full certainty that the coming of these delegations to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is an undeniable historical fact, but the historians of the Prophetic Sīrah such as Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, al-Wāqidī and Ibn Saʿd did not take care to mention connected chains of transmission for them, as the ḥadīth scholars used to mention connected chains in their works on rulings and dealings, so that the critic could separate the sound from the unsound and recognize it, unlike the books of Sīrah and history. And Allah is the One whose help is sought."],[985,"Ṭāriq ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Muḥāribī said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the marketplace of Dhū al-Majāz wearing a red suit of clothing and saying: “O people, say, ‘There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah,’ and you will succeed.” A man followed him, throwing stones at him until he had made his heels and the tendons above them bleed. The man was saying: “O people, do not obey him, for he is a liar.” I asked, “Who is this?” It was said, “This is the young man of Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.” I asked, “Then who is this man following him and throwing stones at him?” They replied, “This is ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, Abū Lahab.” He continued: When Islam became dominant, we set out until we camped near Madīnah, accompanied by one of our women. While we were sitting, a man wearing two white garments came to us. He greeted us and asked: “From where have the people come?” We replied, “From al-Rabadhah.” We had a camel with us, and he asked: “Will you sell this camel?” We replied, “Yes.” He asked, “For how much?” We said, “For such-and-such a number of ṣāʿs of dates.” He took it without asking us to reduce the price and said: “I have taken it.” He then disappeared among the gardens of Madīnah. We began reproaching one another, saying: “You gave your camel to a man whom you do not know!” The woman said: “Do not reproach one another. I saw the face of a man who would never treat you contemptuously. I have never seen anything more closely resembling the moon on the night when it is full than his face.” In the evening, a man came to us, greeted us, and said: “I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to you. He says, ‘You may eat until you are satisfied and measure out for yourselves until you receive your payment in full.’” He said: We ate until we were satisfied and measured out the dates until we had received our payment in full. The following day, we entered Madīnah and found the Messenger of Allah ﷺ standing upon the pulpit delivering a sermon. He was saying: “The hand of the giver is the upper hand. Begin with those whom you support: your mother and your father, your sister and your brother, and then those closest to you, followed by those next closest.” A man stood up and said: “O Messenger of Allah, Banū Thaʿlabah ibn Yarbūʿ killed so-and-so during the pre-Islamic period, so take vengeance for us upon them.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his hands until I could see the whiteness of his armpits and said: “Indeed, no mother shall bear the guilt of her child! Indeed, no mother shall bear the guilt of her child!”"],[986,"Al-Wāqidī said: The first delegation to come to the Prophet ﷺ was that of four hundred men of Muzaynah, of the tribe of Muḍar. They came in the month of Rajab in the fifth year of the Hijrah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared their homes themselves to be a dār al-hijrah (abode of emigration), and said: “Wherever you are, there you are Muhājirūn. Go back to your properties.” So they returned to their towns."],[987,"Al-Wāqidī said: Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Sabrah narrated to me that Sharīk ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Namir reported from Kurayb that Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed him: Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr sent Ḍimām ibn Thaʿlabah in Rajab of the fifth year of the Hijrah, and he was a man with a great deal of hair. He came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and stood before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked him harshly who had sent him and with what he had sent him, and asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the laws of Islam, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ answered all of these. He became Muslim and returned to his people, who had deposed his equal, and told them what he had commanded them and what he had forbidden them. By that same day there was no man or woman among them except a Muslim, and they built mosques and gave the call to prayer for the prayer."],[988,"Anas ibn Mālik said: We were forbidden from asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ unnecessary questions, so we were pleased whenever an intelligent man from the desert came and questioned him while we listened. A Bedouin man came and said: “O Muḥammad, your messenger came to us and claimed that you say Allah has sent you.” He replied, “He spoke the truth.” The man asked, “Who created the sky?” He replied, “Allah.” He asked, “Who created the earth?” He replied, “Allah.” He asked, “Who erected these mountains and placed within them whatever He placed?” He replied, “Allah.” The man said, “By the One who created the sky, created the earth, and erected these mountains, did Allah send you?” He replied, “Yes.” The man said: “Your messenger also claimed that we are required to perform five prayers during each day and night.” He replied, “He spoke the truth.” The man asked, “By the One who sent you, did Allah command you to do this?” He replied, “Yes.” The man said, “Your messenger claimed that zakāh is due from our wealth.” He replied, “He spoke the truth.” The man asked, “By the One who sent you, did Allah command you to do this?” He replied, “Yes.” The man said, “Your messenger claimed that we must fast the month of Ramadan each year.” He replied, “He spoke the truth.” The man asked, “By the One who sent you, did Allah command you to do this?” He replied, “Yes.” The man said: “Your messenger claimed that whoever among us is able to find a way to the House must perform its pilgrimage.” He replied, “He spoke the truth.” The man then turned away, saying: “By the One who sent you with the truth, I shall neither add anything to these nor omit anything from them.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “If he is truthful, he will certainly enter Paradise.”"],[989,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Tomorrow, a people will come to you whose hearts are more tender toward Islam than yours.” The Ashʿarīs then arrived, among them Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī. As they drew near Madīnah, they began chanting: “Tomorrow we shall meet the beloved ones: Muḥammad and his companions.” When they arrived, they shook hands with one another, and they were the first to introduce handshaking."],[990,"Abū Hurayrah said: While travelling to join the Prophet ﷺ, I recited: “What a night, for all its length and hardship— yet it delivered me from the abode of disbelief.” A servant of mine ran away during the journey. When I reached the Prophet ﷺ, I pledged allegiance to him. While I was with him, the servant suddenly appeared. The Prophet ﷺ said to me: “O Abū Hurayrah, here is your servant.” I replied: “He is free for the sake of Allah.” I then set him free."],[991,"Jābir reported that al-Ṭufayl ibn ʿAmr al-Dawsī came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, would you like the protection of a strong fortress and a place of defence?” The narrator said: It was a fortress belonging to Daws during the pre-Islamic period. The Prophet ﷺ declined because of what Allah had reserved for the Anṣār. When the Prophet ﷺ emigrated to Madīnah, al-Ṭufayl ibn ʿAmr emigrated to join him, accompanied by a man from his tribe. They found the climate of Madīnah unhealthy. The man became ill and lost patience. He took some broad-headed arrows and cut the joints of his fingers with them. His hands bled profusely until he died. Al-Ṭufayl ibn ʿAmr later saw him in a dream. He appeared in a good condition, but al-Ṭufayl saw him covering his hands. He asked him: “What has your Lord done with you?” He replied: “He forgave me because of my emigration to His Prophet ﷺ.” Al-Ṭufayl asked: “Why do I see you covering your hands?” He replied: “I was told, ‘We will not restore what you yourself have ruined.’” Al-Ṭufayl related the dream to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, forgive even his hands.”"],[992,"Abū Hurayrah said: Al-Ṭufayl and his companions came and said: “O Messenger of Allah, Daws has persisted in disbelief and refused to accept Islam, so supplicate to Allah against them.” It was said, “Daws is ruined!” But he ﷺ said: “O Allah, guide Daws and bring them to me.”"],[993,"Abū Jamrah said: I used to sit with Ibn ʿAbbās, and he would seat me upon his couch. He said to me: “Stay with me so that I may assign you a share from my wealth.” I remained with him for two months. He then said: When the delegation of ʿAbd al-Qays came to the Prophet ﷺ, he asked: “Who are these people—or who is this delegation?” They replied, “Rabīʿah.” He said: “Welcome to the people—or the delegation—who shall suffer neither disgrace nor regret.” They said: “O Messenger of Allah, we are unable to come to you except during the sacred month, for between us and you lies this tribe of the disbelievers of Muḍar. Therefore, command us with a decisive matter that we may convey to those whom we have left behind and by which we may enter Paradise.” They also asked him about drinks. He commanded them to observe four things and forbade them four things. He commanded them to believe in Allah alone and asked: “Do you know what belief in Allah alone means?” They replied: “Allah and His Messenger know best.” He said: “To testify that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah; to establish prayer, give zakāh, fast Ramadan, and give one-fifth of the spoils of war.” He forbade them four types of vessels: al-ḥantam, al-dubbāʾ, al-naqīr, and al-muzaffat—perhaps he said al-muqayyar—and told them: “Memorize these instructions and convey them to those whom you have left behind.”"],[994,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī said: Some people from ʿAbd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “O Prophet of Allah, we are a tribe of Rabīʿah, and between us and you are the disbelievers of Muḍar. We are unable to reach you except during the sacred months. Therefore, command us with something that we may command those whom we have left behind and by which we may enter Paradise if we adhere to it.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I command you to observe four things and forbid you four things. Worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, establish prayer, give zakāh, fast Ramadan, and give one-fifth of the spoils of war. I forbid you four vessels: al-dubbāʾ, al-ḥantam, al-muzaffat, and al-naqīr.” They asked: “O Prophet of Allah, what do you know about al-naqīr?” He replied: “Indeed, it is a tree trunk that you hollow out. You place small dates—or Saʿīd said: dates—inside it and then pour water over them. When its fermentation subsides, you drink it until one of you strikes his cousin with the sword.” There was among the delegation a man who had suffered such an injury. He said: “I used to conceal it out of embarrassment before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” I asked: “Then from what should we drink, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “From leather waterskins whose mouths are tied with cords.” They said: “O Messenger of Allah, our land has many rats, and leather waterskins do not survive there.” The Prophet of Allah ﷺ replied: “Even if the rats eat them; even if the rats eat them; even if the rats eat them.” The Prophet of Allah ﷺ also said to al-Ashajj of ʿAbd al-Qays: “You possess two qualities that Allah loves: forbearance and deliberateness.”"],[995,"Thumāmah ibn Ḥazn al-Qushayrī said: I met ʿĀ’ishah and asked her about nabīdh. She informed me that the delegation of ʿAbd al-Qays came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him about nabīdh. He forbade them from preparing it in gourds, hollowed-out tree trunks, pitch-coated vessels, and earthenware jars."],[996,"Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib said: I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar speaking beside this pulpit—and he pointed toward the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ—saying: “The delegation of ʿAbd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked him about drinks. He forbade them gourds, hollowed-out tree trunks, and earthenware jars.” I asked him: “O Abū Muḥammad, what about pitch-coated vessels?” We thought he had forgotten to mention them. He replied: “I did not hear it from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar that day, although he disliked them.”"],[997,"Ṭalq ibn ʿAlī said: We were sitting with the Prophet ﷺ when the delegation of ʿAbd al-Qays arrived. He asked: “Why have your complexions turned yellow, your stomachs become enlarged, and your veins become prominent?” They replied: “Our leader came to you and asked you about a drink that suited us, but you forbade him from it, and we live in a fever-ridden land.” He said: “Then drink whatever wholesome drink suits you.”"],[998,"Al-Wāqidī said: Mūsā ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to me from a man of Banū Thaʿlabah, who narrated from his father, that he said: When he ﷺ came to Madinah in 8 AH, four men of Banū Thaʿlabah came into his presence and said, “O Messenger of Allah ﷺ! We are the envoys of the people left behind among our people. We and our tribe have accepted Islam.” He ﷺ ordered that they be hosted. They stayed in Madinah for several days, then came into his presence to obtain permission to depart. When they were about to leave, he ﷺ said to Bilāl, may Allah be pleased with him: “Give these also a reward in the same way as you give a delegation.” He brought some pieces of silver and gave each man of the delegation five ūqiyyah of silver. He said: We had no dirhams (currency), and we returned to our homeland."],[999,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: A delegation from Banū Asad came to the Prophet ﷺ. They spoke clearly and eloquently, saying: “O Messenger of Allah, all of Muḍar fought against you, but we did not fight you. We are neither the least numerous among them nor the weakest in strength, and we maintained your ties of kinship.” When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard their words, he asked Abū Bakr and ʿUmar: “Do they speak in this manner?” They replied: “O Messenger of Allah, their understanding is indeed limited, and Satan is certainly speaking upon their tongues.” Another narration adds that the following verse was revealed: “They regard their acceptance of Islam as a favour to you. Say, ‘Do not regard your Islam as a favour to me. Rather, Allah has conferred favour upon you by guiding you to faith, if you are truthful.’” [Al-Ḥujurāt: 17]"],[1000,"Ibn Hishām related from ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Udhaynah al-ʿAbdī, who related from Abū Isḥāq al-Sabīʿī, that the delegation of Hamdān came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Among them were Mālik ibn Namaṭ, Abū Thawr, Mālik ibn Ayfaʿ, Ḍimām ibn Mālik al-Salmānī and ʿUmayrah ibn Mālik al-Khārifī. All of them met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as he was returning from Tabūk. They wore embroidered Yemeni cloaks and Adenese turbans, and rode Mahrī and Arḥabī she-camels, and came walking with great courtesy and spirit. And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a letter written for them, in which it says:\nIn the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful\nThis is a letter from Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, to the townspeople of the tribe of Khārif, and to those of the highlands and the sandy desert land, with whom is their representative Dhū al-Mishʿār (Mālik ibn Namaṭ), and to those of their people who have accepted Islam. Theirs are their highlands and lowlands as long as they keep up the prayer and pay the zakāh. They will eat of its produce and feed its grass to their animals. For them is the covenant of Allah and His Messenger ﷺ, and the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār are witnesses to it."],[1001,"Al-Barāʾ said: The Prophet ﷺ sent Khālid ibn al-Walīd to the people of Yemen to invite them to Islam, but they did not respond to him. The Prophet ﷺ then sent ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib and instructed him to send Khālid and those accompanying him back, except that anyone who wished to remain and continue with ʿAlī could do so. Al-Barāʾ said: I was among those who remained with him. When we drew near the people, they came out to meet us. ʿAlī led us in prayer and arranged us in a single row. He then stepped forward before us and read the letter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to them. All of Hamdān embraced Islam. ʿAlī wrote to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informing him of their acceptance of Islam. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ read the letter, he fell down in prostration. He then raised his head and said: “Peace be upon Hamdān! Peace be upon Hamdān!”"],[1002,"The people of Najrān sent a delegation of sixty riders to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Madinah. In this delegation were twenty-four of the great nobles and honorable men of Najrān, including the Usquf (bishop), the Sayyid and the ʿĀqib. Three persons in this delegation were the chief authorities. The ʿĀqib was the commander of the people and one of those of opinion and counsel, without whose opinion and counsel no matter was decided; his name was ʿAbd al-Masīḥ. The second person was called the Sayyid; he was in charge of their affairs, their travel and their gatherings, and his name was Ayham. And the third was Abū Ḥārithah ibn ʿAlqamah of Banū Bakr ibn Wāʾil, their pope, scholar, imām and the custodian of their schools."],[1003,"Ḥudhayfah said: “Al-ʿĀqib and al-Sayyid, the two leaders of Najrān, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intending to invoke mutual cursing with him. “One of them said to the other, ‘Do not do it. By Allah, if he truly is a Prophet and we invoke mutual cursing against him, neither we nor our descendants after us will prosper.’ “They said, ‘We will give you what you have asked of us, but send with us a trustworthy man, and do not send with us anyone except one who is truly trustworthy.’ “He ﷺ said: ‘I will certainly send with you a man who is trustworthy in the fullest sense of the word.’ “The Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ eagerly looked forward to being chosen. “He said: ‘Stand, O Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ.’ “When he stood, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘This is the trustworthy man of this nation.’”"],[1004,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ concluded a treaty with the people of Najrān for two thousand garments: half to be paid in Ṣafar and half in Rajab, which they would deliver to the Muslims. “They were also to lend thirty coats of armor, thirty horses, thirty camels, and thirty items of every category of weaponry for use in military expeditions. The Muslims would be responsible for guaranteeing those items until they were returned to them if treachery or hostile action occurred in Yemen. “The agreement stipulated that no church of theirs would be demolished, no priest of theirs would be expelled, and they would not be forced away from their religion, so long as they did not introduce some violation or consume usury.” Ismāʿīl said: “They did consume usury.” Abū Dāwūd said: “If they violated any part of what had been stipulated upon them, then they had committed a breach.”"],[1005,"ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn said: “Banū Tamīm came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: ‘Receive glad tidings, O Banū Tamīm.’ “They replied, ‘Since you have given us glad tidings, then give us something.’ “The expression of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ changed. “Then some people from Yemen came, and the Prophet ﷺ said: ‘Accept the glad tidings, since Banū Tamīm did not accept them.’ “They said, ‘We accept them, O Messenger of Allah.’”"],[1006,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The people of Yemen have come to you. They have the most tender hearts and the softest hearts. Faith is Yemeni, and wisdom is Yemeni. Pride and arrogance are among the owners of camels, while tranquility and dignity are among the people who tend sheep.”"],[1007,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr reported: “A group from Banū Tamīm came to the Prophet ﷺ. “Abū Bakr said, ‘Appoint al-Qaʿqāʿ ibn Maʿbad ibn Zurārah as their leader.’ “ʿUmar said, ‘Rather, appoint al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis.’ “Abū Bakr said, ‘You only intended to contradict me.’ “ʿUmar replied, ‘I did not intend to contradict you.’ “They continued disputing until their voices became raised. “Then concerning that, the verse was revealed: ‘O you who believe, do not put yourselves forward…’ [al-Ḥujurāt: 1], continuing to the end of the passage.”"],[1008,"Ibn Abī Mulaykah said: “The two excellent men, Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, nearly came to ruin when the delegation of Banū Tamīm came to the Prophet ﷺ. “One of them suggested al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis al-Ḥanẓalī, the brother of Banū Mujāshiʿ, while the other suggested someone else. “Abū Bakr said to ʿUmar, ‘You only intended to contradict me.’ “ʿUmar replied, ‘I did not intend to contradict you.’ “Their voices became raised in the presence of the Prophet ﷺ, so the following was revealed: ‘O you who believe, do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet…’ until His saying, ‘a tremendous reward.’ [al-Ḥujurāt: 2–3] Ibn Abī Mulaykah said that Ibn al-Zubayr said: “After that, whenever ʿUmar spoke to the Prophet ﷺ, he would speak almost like someone whispering a secret. The Prophet ﷺ could scarcely hear him until he asked him to repeat himself.” He did not mention this concerning his father, meaning Abū Bakr."],[1009,"Al-Ashʿath ibn Qays said: “I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as part of the delegation of Kindah, and they regarded me as the best among them. “I said, ‘O Messenger of Allah, are you not from us?’ “He replied: ‘We are the descendants of al-Naḍr ibn Kinānah. We do not falsely attribute ourselves through our mother, nor do we deny our father.’”"],[1010,"In 9 AH, when the Prophet ﷺ returned from Tabūk, a delegation of Banū Fazārah came into his presence. It consisted of more than ten persons. In this delegation were Khārijah ibn Ḥiṣn and al-Ḥārith ibn Qays ibn Ḥiṣn, who were uncle and nephew. Al-Ḥārith was the youngest member of the delegation. The mounts on which they had come were very thin. As soon as they met him ﷺ, they mentioned their Islam and faith. He asked them with love and kindness about their tribe and region, and one of the members of the delegation said, “O Messenger of Allah, a very severe famine has overtaken us, the rains have stopped, the fields and orchards have dried up, the livestock have perished, and our whole tribe and our families are on the brink of ruin. So pray for us.” The Prophet ﷺ had seen their own condition and had also looked at their mounts, which were weak and exhausted from the famine. So he went up the minbar at that very moment and made this supplication: “O Allah, give water to Your servants and Your livestock, spread Your mercy, bring life to Your dead land. O Allah, give us water with rain of mercy, rain that is all-encompassing and widespread, pleasant and refreshing, that comes quickly and not late, that benefits as a rain of mercy and causes no harm. O Allah, give us that rain of mercy which is not a cause of punishment, which does not demolish buildings, nor drown, nor spread destruction. O Allah, give us plentiful water by rain, and grant us help and victory over the enemies.” Allah the Exalted sent rain at once, and for six days the people could not see the sky. When the rain began to be more than needed, the Prophet ﷺ went up the minbar again and prayed to Allah: “O Allah! Take the rain around us, hold it back from us, move it to the hills and mountains, the stones and rocks, the lowlands and highlands of the valleys, and the forests.” The Prophet ﷺ had just finished this supplication when the clouds over Madinah and the surrounding area split and vanished like an old cloth being torn, that is, the sky became completely clear."],[1011,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: “Musaylimah the Liar came during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying, ‘If Muḥammad gives me authority after him, I will follow him.’ “He came with a large number of his people. “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to him accompanied by Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ held a piece of palm stalk in his hand. “He stood before Musaylimah among his companions and said: ‘Even if you asked me for this piece, I would not give it to you. You will not escape what Allah has decreed concerning you. If you turn away, Allah will surely destroy you. I indeed see you as the one concerning whom I was shown what I was shown. And this is Thābit; he will answer you on my behalf.’ “Then he turned away from him.” Ibn ʿAbbās said: “I asked about the statement of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: ‘I indeed see you as the one concerning whom I was shown what I was shown.’ “Abū Hurayrah informed me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘While I was asleep, I saw two bracelets of gold in my hands, and their matter troubled me. It was revealed to me in the dream that I should blow upon them. I blew upon them and they flew away. I interpreted them as two liars who would emerge after me.’\n“One was al-ʿAnsī and the other was Musaylimah.”"],[1012,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “While I was sleeping, the treasures of the earth were brought to me, and two bracelets of gold were placed in my hands. They became burdensome to me, so Allah revealed to me that I should blow upon them. I blew upon them and they disappeared. I interpreted them as the two liars between whom I am situated: the man of Ṣanʿāʾ and the man of al-Yamāmah.”"],[1013,"Ibn ʿUbaydah ibn Nashīṭ—whose name in another place is given as ʿAbdullāh—reported that ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUtbah said: “We were informed that Musaylimah the Liar came to Madīnah and stayed in the house of the daughter of al-Ḥārith. He was married to the daughter of al-Ḥārith ibn Kurayz, who was the mother of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿĀmir. “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to him accompanied by Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās, who was known as the orator of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a small stick in his hand. “He stood before Musaylimah and spoke to him. “Musaylimah said, ‘If you wish, leave the authority between us and yourself, then assign it to us after you.’ “The Prophet ﷺ replied: ‘Even if you asked me for this stick, I would not give it to you. I indeed see you as the one concerning whom I was shown what I was shown. And this is Thābit ibn Qays; he will answer you on my behalf.’ “Then the Prophet ﷺ departed.”"],[1014,"Nuʿaym ibn Masʿūd al-Ashjaʿī said: “I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say to the two envoys when they read out the letter of Musaylimah the Liar: ‘And what do the two of you say?’ “They replied, ‘We say what he says.’ “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘By Allah, were it not that messengers are not to be killed, I would strike both of your necks.’”"],[1015,"Wahb ibn Munabbih said: “I asked Jābir about the matter of Thaqīf when they gave the pledge of allegiance. “He said: ‘They stipulated to the Prophet ﷺ that no charity would be required of them and that they would not be required to fight in jihād.’ “He later heard the Prophet ﷺ say: ‘They will give charity and fight in jihād once they have truly embraced Islām.’”"],[1016,"Al-Sharīd said: “There was a man afflicted with leprosy among the delegation of Thaqīf. “The Prophet ﷺ sent word to him: ‘We have accepted your pledge, so return.’”"],[1017,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥassān, a man from Banū Kaʿb of Balʿanbar, reported that his two grandmothers, Ṣafiyyah bint ʿUlayyah and Duḥaybah bint ʿUlaybah, narrated to him the account of Qaylah bint Makhramah. They had both been raised in her care, and Qaylah was the maternal grandmother of their father. Qaylah had been married to Ḥabīb ibn Azhar, a man from Banū Jināb, and she bore him daughters. He died at the beginning of Islām. The girls’ paternal uncle, Athwāb ibn Azhar, then took them away from her. So she set out seeking companionship to reach the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the early period of Islām. One of the young girls, Ḥudaybāʾ, began to cry, and Qaylah took her along. The girl was wearing a woolen wrap. While they were making the camel move quickly, a rabbit suddenly sprang out. Ḥudaybāʾ said: “By Allah, your standing will always remain above the standing of Athwāb in this affair!” Then a fox crossed their path, and she called it by a name that ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥassān later forgot, and she said about it the same as she had said about the rabbit. While they were again urging the camel forward, it suddenly knelt down and began trembling. Ḥudaybāʾ said: “By the trust, Athwāb’s seizure has overtaken you!” Qaylah said: “I was forced to rely upon her, so I said, ‘Woe to you, what should I do?’” She replied: “Turn your garments inside out, roll your back toward your belly, and overturn the saddle-cloths of your camel.” The girl removed her woolen wrap and turned it inside out, then rolled herself over. When Qaylah did what she had been told, the camel shook itself, stood up, passed wind, and urinated. The girl said: “Put your things back as they were.” Qaylah did so, and they continued quickly. Suddenly Athwāb was running behind them with a drawn sword. They sought refuge in a large enclosure. Qaylah said that she could still picture the place: the camel was brought to the middle section of the tent like a docile camel, and she rushed inside. Athwāb caught up with her and struck with the sword, its edge cutting part of the hair from her head. He said: “Give me my brother’s daughter, O foul woman!” She handed the girl to him, and he placed her upon his shoulder and took her away. The girl knew him better than anyone in the household. Qaylah then went to a sister of hers who was married among Banū Shaybān, still seeking someone who could accompany her to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. One night while she was staying with her sister and they thought she was asleep, her sister’s husband returned from an evening gathering and said: “By your father, I have found for Qaylah a trustworthy companion.” Her sister asked: “Who is he?” He replied: “Ḥurayth ibn Ḥassān al-Shaybānī. He will leave in the morning as the delegate of Bakr ibn Wā’il to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” Qaylah heard what they said, went early to her camel, prepared it, and searched for him. She found him not far away and asked to accompany him. He replied: “Yes, with honor.” Their riding animals were already kneeling and prepared, so she set out with him. She found him to be a trustworthy companion until they reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He was leading the people in the dawn prayer. The prayer had begun just as daybreak split, while the stars were still densely visible in the sky, and because of the darkness the men could scarcely recognize one another. Qaylah joined the row with the men, for she was a woman only recently removed from the customs of Jāhiliyyah. The man beside her asked: “Are you a woman or a man?” She replied: “No, rather I am a woman.” He said: “You have nearly caused me temptation. Pray with the women behind you.” There was a row of women near the chambers that she had not noticed when she first entered, so she joined them. When the sun had risen, she moved closer. Whenever she saw a man of impressive appearance, her gaze would rise toward him, thinking perhaps that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would stand out above the people. Eventually, after the sun had risen higher, a man came and said: “Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “And upon you be peace, and the mercy of Allah, and His blessings.” Qaylah described the Prophet ﷺ as wearing two worn garments that had once been dyed with saffron but whose color had faded. He had with him a stripped palm branch with only two leaves remaining at its top, and he was sitting with his knees drawn up. When she saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sitting with such humility, she began trembling with fear. The man sitting beside him said: “O Messenger of Allah, the poor woman is trembling.” Without turning to look at her, while she was behind him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O poor woman, be calm.” When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said this, Allah removed the terror that had entered her heart. Her companion then stepped forward first and pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon Islām, both for himself and on behalf of his people. Then he said: “O Messenger of Allah, write an agreement between us and Banū Tamīm regarding al-Dahnāʾ, so that none of them may cross it toward us except as a traveler or someone temporarily residing.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “O young man, write for him concerning al-Dahnāʾ.” When Qaylah saw that he had ordered the land to be written over to him, she became alarmed, because it was her homeland and home territory. She said: “O Messenger of Allah, he has not asked you for a fair portion of land. This al-Dahnāʾ may seem to you merely a place where a camel is tethered and sheep are pastured, but beyond it are the women and children of Tamīm!” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Stop, O young man. The poor woman has spoken the truth. The Muslim is the brother of the Muslim. Water and vegetation are sufficient for them both, and they cooperate against those who cause temptation and misguidance.” When Ḥurayth saw that the written grant had been prevented, he struck one hand against the other and said: “You and I have become like the saying: ‘The sheep carries its own death upon its hooves.’” Qaylah said: “By Allah, you were indeed an excellent guide in the darkness, generous with your mount, and honorable toward your female companion until you brought me to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. But do not blame me for seeking my own right when you sought yours.” He said: “What right do you have in al-Dahnāʾ, may you be bereft?” She replied: “The place where my camel is tethered—you seek it for your wife’s camel?” He said: “Very well. I call the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to witness that I will be your brother for as long as I live, since you have praised me before him in this way.” She replied: “Since you have begun it, I will not waste that bond.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said words concerning her son and her grief. Qaylah wept and said: “By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I had raised him to be a resolute man. He fought with you on the day of al-Rabadhah, then went to bring me provisions from Khaybar. Its fever afflicted him, and he left the women in my care.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “By the One in Whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, were you not a poor woman, we would have dragged you today upon your face—or I would have dragged you upon your face.” ʿAbdullāh was uncertain about the exact wording. He then said: “Is one of you incapable of living honorably with his companion in this world, then when someone with a greater claim over him comes between them, saying: ‘Indeed we belong to Allah, and to Him we return’?” Then he said: “My Lord, console me regarding what You have taken away, and aid me regarding what You have left. By the One in Whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, one of you may weep until his companion is moved to tears by his weeping. So, servants of Allah, do not torment your brothers.” He then wrote for Qaylah and the women who were the daughters of Qaylah upon a piece of red leather: “They are not to be wronged in any right, nor compelled into marriage. Every believing Muslim is their supporter. Do good and do not do evil.”"],[1018,"It is narrated from ʿĀmir ibn Shahr that Hamdān were encamped in the mountain of Ḥaql, and for this reason they were safe, until the Persians came and kept attacking them continually. Then Hamdān too made ready to fight them. The matter dragged on, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ proclaimed his prophethood. Hamdān said to me: “ʿĀmir ibn Shahr! You have long had dealings with kings. Will you go to the man who has proclaimed his prophethood? You are also our chief. We will do whatever you approve, and if you disapprove of something, we too will disapprove of it.” I said: “Very well, I am ready for it.” I set out and reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I was sitting with him when a group came and said: “O Messenger of Allah! Give us some counsel.” He said: “I counsel you to have fear of Allah (taqwā). You will act upon the words of Quraysh, but you will not pay attention to their deeds.” By Allah, I found this saying of his sufficient, and did not think it proper to ask anything further. From there, instead of going to my people, I thought it proper to go to the Negus (al-Najāshī), because he was also my friend. I went to him. I was sitting near him when one of his young sons passed by. The Negus asked him to read what was written on his tablet, and he read it out to him. At this I laughed. The Negus asked the reason for my laughter. I said: “By Allah! In the book that was revealed to ʿĪsā ibn Maryam it is written that when the rulers of the people are children, then a curse will fall upon the people of the earth. I said this because of this boy’s reading.” I returned from there and told my people that I had heard this saying from the Prophet ﷺ and this one from the Negus. Hearing this, my people became Muslims and came down from the mountain.\nIt is related that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Mālik ibn Murārah al-Rahāwī to Yemen. There ʿAkk Dhū Khaywān accepted Islam. It was said to ʿAkk: “Go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and obtain a letter of protection for the safety of your people and your property.” He had a separate settlement, where he had a great deal of goods and silver. He went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah! Mālik ibn Murārah al-Rahāwī came to us and invited us to accept Islam. We have become Muslims. I have land and silver in the settlement. Write something for me about this.” The Prophet ﷺ had a document written for him: “In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to ʿAkk Dhī Khaywān. If they are truthful regarding their land, property and silver, then they are granted protection. They are under the protection of Allah and His Prophet.” This document was written by Sayyidunā Khālid ibn Saʿīd."],[1019,"Laqīṭ ibn Ṣabirah said: I was the delegate of Banū al-Muntafiq—or was among the delegation of Banū al-Muntafiq—to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When we arrived, we did not find the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at his home, but we found ʿĀ’ishah, the Mother of the Believers. She ordered that khazīrah be prepared for us, and it was prepared. We were also brought a qināʿ—Qutaybah did not say “the qināʿ”—which is a platter containing dates. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then arrived and asked: “Have you been given anything to eat, or has anything been ordered for you?” We replied: “Yes, O Messenger of Allah.” While we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the shepherd brought the sheep into their resting place, accompanied by a young lamb that was bleating. He ﷺ asked: “What did it give birth to, so-and-so?” He replied: “A ewe lamb.” He ﷺ said: “Then slaughter a sheep for us in its place.” He then said: “Do not suppose that we slaughtered it specifically because of you. We own one hundred sheep and do not want their number to increase. Whenever the shepherd has a ewe lamb born, we slaughter a sheep in its place.” I said: “O Messenger of Allah, I have a wife whose tongue contains something”—meaning foul or abusive speech. He said: “Then divorce her.” I said: “O Messenger of Allah, she has been my companion for a long time, and I have a child by her.” He said: “Then instruct her”—meaning admonish her—“for if there is goodness in her, she will comply. Do not beat your wife as you would beat your slave woman.” I then said: “O Messenger of Allah, tell me about ablution.” He said: “Perform ablution thoroughly, pass your fingers between your fingers and toes, and inhale water deeply into the nose unless you are fasting.”"],[1020,"Muṭarrif ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr reported that his father said: I went as part of the delegation of Banū ʿĀmir to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. We said: “You are our master.” He replied: “The Master is Allah, Blessed and Exalted.” We said: “And you are the most excellent of us in virtue and the greatest of us in eminence.” He said: “Say what you ordinarily say, or part of it, and do not allow Satan to draw you along.”"],[1021,"The delegation of Banū Murrah came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the year 9 AH, on the return from the expedition of Tabūk. They were thirteen men, whose chief was al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAwf. With the permission of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, these men were each given ten ūqiyyahs of silver, and al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAwf was given more: twelve ūqiyyahs. They said: “O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, by Allah, we are people struck by drought; pray to Allah for us.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, give these people rain to drink.” So when they returned to their homeland, they found that rain had fallen on the very day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed for them."],[1022,"Then al-Wāqidī related that a man called ʿAmr ibn Mālik ibn Qays ibn Bujayd ibn Ruʾās ibn Kilāb ibn Rabīʿah ibn ʿĀmir ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and accepted Islam. Then he went to his people and invited them to Islam, but they said: “We will not accept Islam until we bring down a calamity upon Banū ʿUqayl ibn Kaʿb just as they brought one upon us.” Then he mentioned the killing among them, and that ʿAmr ibn Mālik had killed a man of Banū ʿUqayl. He said: “I tied my hand to my neck and set out intending to go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he ﷺ learned of this incident. He ﷺ said: ‘If this man (ʿAmr ibn Mālik) comes to me, I will certainly strike him above the neck.’ When I came, I greeted him with salām, and he ﷺ turned away from me. Then I came from his right side, and he ﷺ turned away from me. Then I came from the left side, and he ﷺ turned away from me. Then I came in front of him ﷺ and said: ‘O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, truly, when the Lord, Mighty and Majestic, is appeased, He is pleased. May Allah be pleased with you ﷺ; so be pleased with me ﷺ.’ He ﷺ said: ‘I am pleased with you.’”"],[1023,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained in Madinah for nine years without performing ḥajj. Then, in the tenth year, an announcement was made among the people that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would perform ḥajj. A great multitude came to Madinah, all seeking to follow the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and perform the rites as he performed them. The narrator then related the hadith in full."],[1024,"Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb reported that some Jewish people said, “Had this verse been revealed to us, we would have taken the day of its revelation as a festival.” ʿUmar asked, “Which verse?” They replied: “Today I have perfected your religion for you and completed My favor upon you.” [Al-Māʾidah 5:3] ʿUmar said, “I know precisely where it was revealed. It was revealed while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was standing at ʿArafah.”"],[1025,"Zayd ibn Arqam reported: The Prophet ﷺ participated in nineteen military campaigns. After migrating, he performed only one ḥajj and performed no other pilgrimage afterward: the Farewell Pilgrimage. Abū Isḥāq added: “He also performed another pilgrimage while in Makkah.”"],[1026,"Abū Hurayrah reported that after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ḥajj with his wives, he said: “This pilgrimage is sufficient; afterward, remain upon the backs of the mats”—meaning: remain in your homes."],[1027,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported in the account of the Farewell Pilgrimage: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued until he reached ʿArafah, where he found that a tent had been erected for him at Namirah, and he stopped there. When the sun passed its zenith, he ordered that al-Qaṣwāʾ be saddled. He rode to the bottom of the valley and addressed the people: “Your blood and wealth are sacred to one another, just as this day of yours is sacred, in this month of yours, in this city of yours. “Behold! Everything belonging to the pre-Islamic period is abolished beneath my feet. The blood claims of the pre-Islamic period are abolished. “The first claim of blood from among our claims that I abolish is that of the son of Rabīʿah ibn al-Ḥārith. He was being nursed among Banū Saʿd when Hudhayl killed him. “The usury of the pre-Islamic period is abolished. The first of our usury that I abolish is the usury of al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib. All of it is abolished. “Fear Allah concerning women. You have taken them under Allah’s protection and made marital relations with them lawful by the word of Allah. “Your right over them is that they must not allow anyone you dislike upon your bedding. If they do so, discipline them in a manner that causes no injury. “Their right over you is that you provide their food and clothing according to what is reasonable. “I have left among you that which, if you hold firmly to it, will prevent you from ever going astray: the Book of Allah. “You will be questioned concerning me, so what will you say?” They replied, “We testify that you conveyed the message, fulfilled your trust, and gave sincere counsel.” He raised his index finger toward the sky and then pointed it toward the people, saying three times: “O Allah, bear witness! O Allah, bear witness! O Allah, bear witness!”"],[1028,"Ibn Abī Bakrah reported from his father that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Time has returned to its original order as it was on the day Allah created the heavens and the earth. The year consists of twelve months, four of which are sacred. Three are consecutive: Dhū al-Qaʿdah, Dhū al-Ḥijjah, and Muḥarram; and the fourth is Rajab of Muḍar, which falls between Jumādā and Shaʿbān. “Which month is this?” We replied, “Allah and His Messenger know best.” He remained silent until we thought he might give it a different name. He asked: “Is it not Dhū al-Ḥijjah?” We replied, “Indeed.” He asked: “Which city is this?” We replied, “Allah and His Messenger know best.” He remained silent until we thought he might give it a different name. He asked: “Is it not the Sacred City?” We replied, “Indeed.” He asked: “Which day is this?” We replied, “Allah and His Messenger know best.” He remained silent until we thought he might give it a different name. He asked: “Is it not the Day of Sacrifice?” We replied, “Indeed.” Then he said: “Your blood and wealth”—Muḥammad said: I believe he also said, “and your honor”—“are sacred to one another, just as this day of yours is sacred, in this city of yours, in this month of yours. “You will meet your Lord, and He will question you concerning your deeds. “Beware! Do not return after me to misguidance, striking one another’s necks. “Let those who are present convey this to those who are absent. Perhaps someone to whom it is conveyed will understand it better than someone who heard it directly.” Whenever Muḥammad, the sub-narrator, related this hadith, he would say, “Muḥammad ﷺ spoke the truth.” The Prophet ﷺ then asked twice: “Have I conveyed the message?”"],[1029,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: The Prophet ﷺ arrived in Makkah, circumambulated the Kaʿbah, and performed saʿy between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah. After that circumambulation, he did not approach the Kaʿbah again until he returned from ʿArafah."],[1030,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: We traveled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from Madinah to Makkah, and he continued performing each four-rakʿah prayer as two rakʿahs until he returned. The narrator asked, “How long did he remain in Makkah?” Anas replied, “Ten days.”"],[1031,"Yazīd ibn Ḥayyān reported: Ḥuṣayn ibn Sabrah, ʿUmar ibn Muslim, and I went to Zayd ibn Arqam. When we sat with him, Ḥuṣayn said, “Zayd, you have encountered abundant goodness. You saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, heard his words, participated in campaigns with him, and prayed behind him. You have indeed encountered abundant goodness. Zayd, tell us what you heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” He replied, “Son of my brother, by Allah, I have grown old, much time has passed, and I have forgotten some of what I once retained from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Accept whatever I relate to you, and do not burden me regarding what I do not relate.” He then said: One day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood to address us at a watering place called Khumm, between Makkah and Madinah. He praised and extolled Allah, admonished us, and reminded us. He then said: “To proceed: O people, I am only a human being. The messenger of my Lord—the Angel of Death—may soon come to me, and I shall answer his summons. “I am leaving among you two weighty trusts. The first is the Book of Allah, containing guidance and light. Hold firmly to the Book of Allah and adhere to it.” He strongly encouraged and urged adherence to the Book of Allah. Then he said: “And the members of my household. I remind you of Allah concerning the members of my household. I remind you of Allah concerning the members of my household. I remind you of Allah concerning the members of my household.” Ḥuṣayn asked him, “Who are the members of his household, Zayd? Are his wives not members of his household?” He replied, “His wives are members of his household. However, the members of his household intended here are those for whom obligatory charity remained forbidden after him.” He asked, “Who are they?” Zayd replied, “The family of ʿAlī, the family of ʿAqīl, the family of Jaʿfar, and the family of al-ʿAbbās.” He asked, “Was obligatory charity forbidden to all of them?” He replied, “Yes.”"],[1032,"The Prophet ﷺ returned from the Farewell Pilgrimage in the same year, at the end of Dhū al-Ḥijjah. In the eleventh year, in the month of Ṣafar, he gathered the people for an attack of the Romans upon al-Balqāʾ and Palestine. Among them were also the elders of the Companions from the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār, but he appointed over them as commander Usāmah ibn Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with them both, who was eighteen years old.\nIbn Isḥāq says that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Usāmah ibn Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, may Allah be pleased with them both, towards Syria and commanded him to trample the land of Palestine, al-Balqāʾ and the towns of al-Dārūm. So the people stood up with him, and the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār joined Usāmah. Ibn Hishām said: This was the last army sent by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[1033,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed Usāmah as commander over a group of people, but they criticized his appointment. He said: “If you criticize his command, you previously criticized his father’s command. By Allah, his father was certainly worthy of command and was among the people dearest to me. This son of his is also among the people dearest to me after him.”"],[1034,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: I saw the Prophet ﷺ casting the pebbles from atop his riding animal on the Day of Sacrifice, while saying: “Learn your pilgrimage rites from me, for I do not know; perhaps I will not perform ḥajj after this pilgrimage of mine.”"],[1035,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported in his description of the Prophet’s ﷺ pilgrimage that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I have left among you that which, if you hold firmly to it, will prevent you from going astray: the Book of Allah. “You will be questioned concerning me, so what will you say?” They replied, “We testify that you conveyed the message, fulfilled your trust, and gave sincere counsel.” He raised his index finger toward the sky and then pointed it toward the people, saying three times: “O Allah, bear witness! O Allah, bear witness! O Allah, bear witness!”"],[1036,"Saʿīd ibn Jubayr reported that Ibn ʿAbbās said: “Thursday—and what a Thursday it was!” He then wept until his tears moistened the pebbles. I asked, “Ibn ʿAbbās, what happened on Thursday?” He replied: The Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ illness became severe, and he said: “Bring me writing materials so that I may write for you a document after which you will not go astray.” The people disagreed, although disagreement is not appropriate in the presence of a prophet. They said, “What is the matter with him? Is he speaking incoherently? Ask him to clarify.” He said: “Leave me, for the state in which I am is better. I instruct you regarding three matters: Expel the polytheists from the Arabian Peninsula, and honor visiting delegations as I used to honor them.” Ibn ʿAbbās remained silent concerning the third instruction, or he said, “He mentioned it, but I forgot it.”"],[1037,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was near death, several men were present in the house, including ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Come, let me write for you a document after which you will not go astray.” ʿUmar said, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has been overcome by pain. You possess the Qur’an; the Book of Allah is sufficient for us.” The people in the house disagreed and disputed. Some said, “Bring the writing materials so that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ may write for you a document after which you will not go astray,” while others supported what ʿUmar had said. When their argument and disagreement became excessive in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he said: “Leave.” ʿUbaydullāh said: Ibn ʿAbbās used to say, “The complete calamity was that their disagreement and clamor prevented the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from writing that document for them.”"],[1038,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: As the Prophet ﷺ approached death, he called for a sheet upon which he could write a document after which the people would not go astray. Jābir said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb opposed the request, and it was consequently left aside."],[1039,"This verse was revealed in 10 AH at the Farewell Pilgrimage on the Day of ʿArafah. The Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ knew that his time had come, yet the Prophet ﷺ lived for a long period after them, for he passed away about three months later."],[1040,"In this sūrah there is an indication that the time of the passing of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had come, so Allah the Almighty commanded him to conclude with tasbīḥ (glorification) and istighfār (seeking forgiveness)."],[1041,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: ʿUmar used to admit me into the company of the elders who had participated in Badr. Some of them asked, “Why do you admit this young man among us when we have sons of his age?” ʿUmar replied, “He is one whose knowledge and status you already know.” One day, ʿUmar summoned them and also summoned me. I believed he called me that day only to demonstrate my knowledge to them. He asked, “What do you say concerning Allah’s words: ‘When Allah’s help and the victory come, and you see the people entering Allah’s religion in multitudes…’ until the end of the sūrah?” Some replied, “We were commanded to praise Allah and seek His forgiveness when He grants us help and victory.” Others said, “We do not know,” while some said nothing. ʿUmar asked me, “Ibn ʿAbbās, do you say the same?” I replied, “No.” He asked, “What do you say?” I replied, “It refers to the appointed time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, which Allah made known to him: ‘When Allah’s help and the victory come’—meaning the Conquest of Makkah—that is the sign that your appointed time is near. ‘Then glorify your Lord with praise and seek His forgiveness. Indeed, He is ever accepting of repentance.’” ʿUmar said, “I understand nothing from it beyond what you have said.”"],[1042,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb used to bring Ibn ʿAbbās close to him. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf said, “We have sons of the same age as he.” ʿUmar replied, “He possesses the knowledge and status you know.” ʿUmar then asked Ibn ʿAbbās about the verse: “When Allah’s help and the victory come.” Ibn ʿAbbās replied, “It referred to the appointed time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, which Allah made known to him.” ʿUmar said, “I understand nothing from it beyond what you understand.”"],[1043,"Muʿādh ibn Jabal reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to Yemen, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out with him to advise him. Muʿādh was mounted, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ walked beside his riding animal. When he had finished advising him, he said: “Muʿādh, perhaps you will not meet me again after this year. Perhaps you will pass by this mosque of mine or my grave.” Muʿādh wept intensely at the thought of separation from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ then turned his face toward Madinah and said: “The people closest to me are those who fear Allah, whoever they may be and wherever they may be.”"],[1044,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: During the illness in which the Prophet ﷺ died, he called Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her, and whispered something to her, causing her to weep. He then called her again and whispered something else to her, causing her to laugh. We later asked her about this. She said, “The Prophet ﷺ privately informed me that he would die during the illness from which he was suffering, so I wept. He then privately informed me that I would be the first member of his family to follow him in death, so I laughed.”"],[1045,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: When the following verse was revealed: “When Allah’s help and the victory come,” the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called Fāṭimah and said: “My impending death has been announced to me.” She wept, so he said: “Do not weep, for you will be the first member of my family to join me.” She then laughed. Some of the Prophet’s ﷺ wives saw her and asked, “Fāṭimah, we saw you weep and then laugh.” She replied, “He informed me that his impending death had been announced to him, so I wept. He then told me: ‘Do not weep, for you will be the first member of my family to join me.’ So I laughed.”"],[1046,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: I used to hear that no prophet dies until he is given a choice between this world and the Hereafter. During the illness in which the Prophet ﷺ died, I heard him say in a hoarse voice: “With those upon whom Allah has bestowed His favor—the prophets, the steadfast affirmers of truth, the martyrs, and the righteous. What excellent companions they are!” [Al-Nisāʾ 4:69] I understood that he had been given the choice at that moment."],[1047,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: While the Prophet ﷺ was healthy, he used to say: “No prophet is taken in death until he is shown his place in Paradise and then given a choice.” When death approached him, his head was resting upon my thigh, and he lost consciousness. He then regained consciousness, fixed his gaze upon the ceiling of the house, and said: “O Allah, the Highest Companion.” I said to myself, “Then he will not choose to remain with us.” I recognized that this was the fulfillment of the hadith he had related to us while healthy. She said: His final words were: “O Allah, the Highest Companion.”"],[1048,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat upon the pulpit and said: “Allah gave one of His servants a choice between receiving whatever splendor of this world he desired and receiving what is with Allah. The servant chose what is with Allah.” Abū Bakr wept and said, “May our fathers and mothers be sacrificed for you.” We were surprised by him, and the people said, “Look at this elderly man! The Messenger of Allah ﷺ is speaking about a servant whom Allah gave a choice between the splendor of this world and what is with Him, yet he says, ‘May our fathers and mothers be sacrificed for you!’” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ himself was the servant who had been given the choice, and Abū Bakr understood him better than any of us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ also said: “Among all people, Abū Bakr has shown me the greatest generosity through his companionship and wealth. If I were to take an intimate friend from my nation, I would take Abū Bakr, but the brotherhood and affection of Islam are sufficient. Every small doorway opening into the mosque must be closed except the doorway of Abū Bakr.”"],[1049,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported: During the illness in which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, he came out to us while we were in the mosque. His head was wrapped with a cloth. He proceeded toward the pulpit, ascended it, and we followed him. He said: “By the One in whose hand is my soul, I can see the Pool from this very place where I stand.” He then said: “One of Allah’s servants was offered this world and its adornments, but he chose the Hereafter.” No one understood his meaning except Abū Bakr. His eyes overflowed and he wept, saying, “Rather, may our fathers, mothers, lives, and wealth be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah.” The Prophet ﷺ then descended from the pulpit and never stood upon it again until the present time."],[1050,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: I never saw anyone suffer more severe pain than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[1051,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that during the illness in which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, he repeatedly asked: “Where will I be tomorrow? Where will I be tomorrow?” He was awaiting ʿĀ’ishah’s allotted day. His wives gave him permission to remain wherever he wished, so he stayed in ʿĀ’ishah’s house until he died there. ʿĀ’ishah said: He died on the day that was allotted to me, inside my house. Allah took his soul while his head rested between my upper chest and neck, and his saliva mixed with mine. She continued: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr entered carrying a tooth-stick with which he was cleaning his teeth. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at it. I said, “ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, give me that tooth-stick.” He gave it to me. I cut off its end, softened it by chewing it, and then gave it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He cleaned his teeth with it while leaning against my chest."],[1052,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ became gravely ill and his pain intensified, he sought permission from his wives to be nursed in my house, and they permitted him. He came out supported between two men, with his feet dragging along the ground. One was al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, and the other was another man. ʿUbaydullāh said: I related what ʿĀ’ishah had said to ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās. He asked me, “Do you know who the other man was whom ʿĀ’ishah did not name?” I replied, “No.” Ibn ʿAbbās said, “It was ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib.” ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, also reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered her house and his pain intensified, he said: “Pour water over me from seven water skins whose ties have not been opened, so that I may go out and give final instructions to the people.” We seated him in a washing vessel belonging to Ḥafṣah, the Prophet’s ﷺ wife, and began pouring water over him from those skins until he gestured with his hand that we had poured enough. She said: He then went out to the people, led them in prayer, and addressed them."],[1053,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: During the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ illness, we poured medicine into the side of his mouth. He gestured to us not to do so. We thought this was merely a sick person’s dislike of medicine. When he regained consciousness, he said: “Everyone present must have the medicine administered to them in the same manner, except al-ʿAbbās, because he was not present with you.”"],[1054,"Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays reported: The Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ illness first began while he was in Maymūnah’s house. His illness became so severe that he lost consciousness. His wives consulted one another about administering medicine through the side of his mouth, and they did so. When he regained consciousness, he said: “This is the practice of women who came from that land,” pointing toward Abyssinia. Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays had been among those who lived there. They said, “O Messenger of Allah, we feared that you were suffering from pleurisy.” He replied: “That is a disease with which Allah would not afflict me. No one in the house must remain without having the medicine administered in the same way, except the uncle of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ”—meaning al-ʿAbbās. Maymūnah had the medicine administered to her that day even though she was fasting, in fulfillment of the firm command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[1055,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās reported that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib came out from the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the illness in which he died. The people asked, “Abū al-Ḥasan, how is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ this morning?” He replied, “By Allah’s praise, he is recovering.” Al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib took ʿAlī by the hand and said, “By Allah, within three days you will become subject to another’s authority. By Allah, I believe that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ will die from this illness. I recognize the appearance of the descendants of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib when death approaches.” “Come with me to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ so that we may ask him to whom this authority will belong. “If it belongs to us, we shall know that. If it belongs to someone else, we shall know that as well, and he can instruct his successor to treat us well.” ʿAlī replied, “By Allah, if we ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for it and he refuses us, the people will never give it to us afterward. “By Allah, I shall not ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for it.”"],[1056,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd reported: I came to the Prophet ﷺ during his illness while he was suffering from an extremely severe fever. I said, “You are suffering from an extremely severe fever.” I asked, “Is that because you receive twice the reward?” He replied: “Yes. No Muslim is afflicted by harm without Allah causing his sins to fall away from him just as leaves fall from a tree.”"],[1057,"One of the Prophet’s ﷺ wives reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became ill, and his suffering or pain grew extremely severe. I said, “O Messenger of Allah, you are showing intense distress from the pain. Had one of us women behaved like this, you would have been surprised by her!” He replied: “Do you not know that the believer’s suffering is intensified so that it may serve as expiation for his sins?”"],[1058,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ became ill, he would recite the protective sūrahs, lightly blow over himself, and wipe his body with his hand. During the illness in which he died, I began reciting over him the protective sūrahs that he used to recite and lightly blow, while wiping his body with the Prophet’s ﷺ own hand."],[1059,"Umm al-Faḍl bint al-Ḥārith reported: I heard the Prophet ﷺ recite Sūrat al-Mursalāt during the sunset prayer. He did not lead us in another prayer after that before Allah took his soul."],[1060,"ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUtbah reported: I entered upon ʿĀ’ishah and asked, “Will you not tell me about the illness of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” She replied: Yes. The Prophet ﷺ became gravely ill and asked: “Have the people prayed?” We replied, “No. They are waiting for you, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “Place water for me in the washing vessel.” We did so, and he bathed. He then attempted to rise but lost consciousness. When he regained consciousness, he asked: “Have the people prayed?” We replied, “No. They are waiting for you, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “Place water for me in the washing vessel.” We did so, and he bathed. He then attempted to rise but lost consciousness. When he regained consciousness, he asked: “Have the people prayed?” We replied, “No. They are waiting for you, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “Place water for me in the washing vessel.” We did so, and he bathed. He then attempted to rise but lost consciousness. When he regained consciousness, he asked: “Have the people prayed?” We replied, “No. They are waiting for you, O Messenger of Allah.” She said: The people remained gathered in the mosque, waiting for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to lead the final night prayer. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent word to Abū Bakr instructing him to lead the people in prayer. The messenger came to Abū Bakr and said, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commands you to lead the people in prayer.” Abū Bakr, who was tenderhearted, said, “ʿUmar, lead the people in prayer.” ʿUmar replied, “You have a greater right to do so.” ʿĀ’ishah said: Abū Bakr therefore led them in prayer during those days. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ later felt some relief and came out supported between two men, one of whom was al-ʿAbbās, for the noon prayer while Abū Bakr was leading the people. When Abū Bakr saw him, he began to step backward, but the Prophet ﷺ gestured to him not to retreat. He said to the two men: “Seat me beside him.” They seated him beside Abū Bakr. Abū Bakr remained standing and followed the Prophet’s ﷺ prayer, while the people followed Abū Bakr’s movements, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed seated. ʿUbaydullāh said: I entered upon ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās and asked, “Shall I relate to you what ʿĀ’ishah told me about the illness of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” He replied, “Proceed.” I related her report to him, and he objected to none of it except that he asked, “Did she name the man who was with al-ʿAbbās?”\nI replied, “No.” He said, “It was ʿAlī.”"],[1061,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Tell Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer.” ʿĀ’ishah said, “O Messenger of Allah, when Abū Bakr stands in your place, the people will be unable to hear him because of his weeping. Instruct ʿUmar to lead the people.” He repeated: “Tell Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer.” ʿĀ’ishah said: I told Ḥafṣah, “When Abū Bakr stands in his place, the people will be unable to hear him because of his weeping. Ask the Prophet ﷺ to instruct ʿUmar to lead them.” Ḥafṣah did so. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “You women are indeed like the women associated with Joseph! Tell Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer.” Ḥafṣah said to ʿĀ’ishah, “I have never received any good from following your suggestion.”"],[1062,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: I repeatedly appealed to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning that matter. The only reason I persisted was that I could not imagine the people ever loving a man who stood in his place after him. I believed that anyone who stood in his place would be regarded by the people as an ill omen, so I wanted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to divert that responsibility away from Abū Bakr."],[1063,"Abū Mūsā reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became ill, and his illness intensified. He said: “Tell Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer.” ʿĀ’ishah said, “O Messenger of Allah, Abū Bakr is a tenderhearted man. If he stands in your place, he will be unable to lead the people in prayer.” He replied: “Tell Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer. You women are indeed like the women associated with Joseph.” Abū Bakr therefore led the people in prayer during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[1064,"Anas ibn Mālik al-Anṣārī—who followed, served, and accompanied the Prophet ﷺ—reported that Abū Bakr had been leading the people in prayer during the illness from which the Prophet ﷺ died. On Monday, while the people were standing in rows for prayer, the Prophet ﷺ lifted the curtain of his chamber and stood looking at us. His face appeared like a page of the Qur’an. He then smiled broadly. We nearly became distracted from our prayer because of our joy at seeing the Prophet ﷺ. Abū Bakr stepped backward upon his heels to join the row, thinking that the Prophet ﷺ was coming out to lead the prayer. The Prophet ﷺ gestured to us to complete our prayer and lowered the curtain. He died later that same day."],[1065,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: “Thursday—and what a Thursday it was!” He then wept until his tears stained the pebbles. He said: The Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ pain became severe on Thursday, and he said: “Bring me writing materials so that I may write for you a document after which you will never go astray.” The people disagreed, although disagreement is not appropriate in the presence of a prophet. They said, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ is speaking incoherently.” He said: “Leave me, for the state in which I am is better than that to which you are calling me.” At the time of his death, he gave three instructions: “Expel the polytheists from the Arabian Peninsula, and honor visiting delegations as I used to honor them.” The narrator forgot the third instruction. Yaʿqūb ibn Muḥammad said: I asked al-Mughīrah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān about the Arabian Peninsula. He replied, “Makkah, Madinah, al-Yamāmah, and Yemen.”\nYaʿqūb said: Al-ʿArj is the beginning of Tihāmah."],[1066,"Abū ʿUbaydah reported: Among the final words spoken by the Prophet ﷺ were: “Expel the Jews of the Ḥijāz and the people of Najrān from the Arabian Peninsula. Know that the worst of people are those who took the graves of their prophets as places of worship.”"],[1067,"The Mother of the Believers ʿĀ’ishah reported that during the illness from which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not recover, he said: “May Allah curse the Jews and Christians; they took the graves of their prophets as places of worship.” She said: Were it not for this concern, his grave would have been made visible and exposed. However, it was feared that it would be taken as a place of worship."],[1068,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: Abū Bakr and al-ʿAbbās passed by a gathering of the Anṣār who were weeping. One of them asked, “What is making you weep?” They replied, “We remembered the gatherings in which the Prophet ﷺ used to sit among us.” He entered upon the Prophet ﷺ and informed him of this. The Prophet ﷺ came out with a strip of cloth wrapped around his head. He ascended the pulpit and never ascended it again after that day. He praised and extolled Allah, then said: “I instruct you to care for the Anṣār, for they are my close community and trusted confidants. They have fulfilled the obligations upon them, while the rights owed to them remain. Accept the good deeds of those among them who do well, and pardon those among them who do wrong.”"],[1069,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: During the illness in which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, he lifted the curtain while his head was wrapped and said three times: “O Allah, have I conveyed the message?” He then said: “Nothing remains of the glad tidings of prophethood except a righteous dream that a righteous servant sees or that is seen concerning him.”"],[1070,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib reported: Among the final words of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were: “Prayer, prayer! Fear Allah concerning those whom your right hands possess.”"],[1071,"Al-Aswad ibn Yazīd reported: It was mentioned in ʿĀ’ishah’s presence that ʿAlī had been specially designated as the Prophet’s ﷺ executor and successor. She asked, “When did he make such a bequest to him? I was supporting the Prophet ﷺ against my chest—or she said, in my lap—and he called for a basin. He then collapsed in my lap, and I did not initially realize that he had died. When, then, could he have made such a bequest to ʿAlī?”"],[1072,"Ṭalḥah ibn Muṣarrif reported: I asked ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā, “Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ leave a bequest?” He replied, “No.” I asked, “Why, then, was making a bequest prescribed for the Muslims, or why were they commanded to make bequests?” He replied, “He bequeathed adherence to the Book of Allah Almighty.”"],[1073,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: The Prophet ﷺ died while resting between my chest and chin. After witnessing the Prophet’s ﷺ severe experience of death, I will never dislike the severity of death for anyone."],[1074,"ʿĀ’ishah used to say: Among Allah’s blessings upon me is that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died inside my house, on my allotted day, between my upper chest and neck, and that Allah joined my saliva with his at the time of his death. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān entered carrying a tooth-stick while I was supporting the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I saw him looking at it and understood that he wanted the tooth-stick. I asked, “Shall I take it for you?” He nodded yes. I took it, but it was too hard for him. I asked, “Shall I soften it for you?” He nodded yes. I softened it for him. A small leather vessel—or a wooden container, as ʿUmar, the narrator, was uncertain—containing water was before him. He began placing his hands into the water, wiping his face with them, and saying: “There is no deity except Allah. Death truly has agonies.” He then raised his hand and continued saying: “With the Highest Companion.” This continued until his soul was taken and his hand fell."],[1075,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that shortly before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, while he was leaning against her chest, she listened closely and heard him saying: “O Allah, forgive me, have mercy upon me, and join me with the Highest Companion.”"],[1076,"ʿAlī reported: The final words of the Prophet ﷺ were: “Prayer, prayer! Fear Allah concerning those whom your right hands possess.”"],[1077,"Qāla Allāhu taʿālā: ﴿اِنَّكَ مَيِّتٌ وَّ اِنَّهُمْ مَّيِّتُوْنَٞ۰۰۳۰﴾ [Al-Zumar: 30]\nThe word of Allah the Exalted: Indeed, you yourself will die, and they too will die."],[1078,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was ill for 22 nights of Ṣafar. His pain began at the house of his female slave called Rayḥānah, who was one of the Jewish captives. The day he fell ill was a Saturday, and his death was on the tenth day, a Monday. Two nights of the month of Rabīʿ al-Awwal had passed, completing ten years since his ﷺ arrival in Madinah."],[1079,"Jarīr reported: I was in Yemen when I met two Yemeni men, Dhū Kalāʿ and Dhū ʿAmr. I began telling them about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Dhū ʿAmr said, “If what you are relating concerning your companion is true, then his appointed time must have passed three days ago.” They accompanied me. While we were traveling, a caravan appeared before us coming from the direction of Madinah, so we questioned them. They replied, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has died, Abū Bakr has been appointed as his successor, and the people remain righteous.” They said, “Inform your leader that we came, and perhaps we will return, if Allah wills.” They then returned to Yemen. I informed Abū Bakr about them, and he asked, “Why did you not bring them here?” Later, Dhū ʿAmr said to me, “Jarīr, you hold an honored place with me, so I shall tell you something: “You Arabs will continue to remain upon goodness so long as, whenever one ruler dies, you consult among yourselves and appoint another. “But when authority is seized by the sword, the rulers will become kings who become angry as kings become angry and pleased as kings become pleased.”"],[1080,"Abū al-Khayr reported that al-Ṣunābiḥī was asked, “When did you migrate?” He replied: We left Yemen as emigrants and reached al-Juḥfah, where a rider approached us. I asked him, “What is the news?” He replied, “We buried the Prophet ﷺ five days ago.” I asked, “Have you heard anything concerning the Night of Decree?” He replied, “Yes. Bilāl, the Prophet’s ﷺ caller to prayer, informed me that it is sought during the seven remaining nights of the final ten nights.”"],[1081,"Ibn ʿUmar reported: During the Prophet’s ﷺ lifetime, we were cautious about speaking freely and behaving casually with our wives, fearing that revelation might come down concerning us. After the Prophet ﷺ died, we spoke and behaved more freely with them."],[1082,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: On the day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered Madinah, everything within it became illuminated. On the day he died, everything within it became dark. We had scarcely withdrawn our hands from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while burying him before we felt that our hearts had changed and were no longer as they had been."],[1083,"Anas reported: When the Prophet ﷺ became gravely ill, the agonies of death began repeatedly overwhelming him. Fāṭimah said, “Oh, the suffering of my father!” He told her: “Your father will suffer no distress after today.” When he died, she said: “O my father, he answered the Lord who summoned him! O my father, the Garden of Firdaws is his dwelling! O my father, to Gabriel we announce his death!” After he was buried, Fāṭimah said, “Anas, how could your hearts allow you to cast earth over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?”"],[1084,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ experienced the agonies of death, Fāṭimah said, “Oh, the suffering of my father!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told her: “Your father will suffer no distress after today. That which spares no one has now come to your father; the final meeting will be on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1085,"ʿĀ’ishah used to say: Among Allah’s blessings upon me is that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died inside my house, on my allotted day, and between my upper chest and neck. The remainder of the hadith follows."],[1086,"ʿĀ’ishah and Ibn ʿAbbās reported that Abū Bakr kissed the Prophet ﷺ after his death."],[1087,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came riding a horse from his residence at al-Sunḥ. He dismounted and entered the mosque. He did not speak to the people before entering ʿĀ’ishah’s room. He went directly to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whose body was covered with a striped Yemeni cloth. He uncovered his face, bent over him, kissed him, and wept. He then said, “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you! By Allah, Allah will not cause you to experience death twice. You have now experienced the death that was decreed for you.”"],[1088,"Anas reported: On Monday, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lifted the curtain of his chamber and saw Abū Bakr leading the people in prayer. I looked at his face, which appeared like a page of the Qur’an, and he was smiling. We nearly became distracted from our prayer because of our joy at seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Abū Bakr intended to step backward, but the Prophet ﷺ gestured to him, “Remain where you are.” He then lowered the curtain and died later that same day. ʿUmar stood and said, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has not died. Rather, his Lord has summoned him just as He summoned Moses, who remained away from his people for forty nights. “By Allah, I hope that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ will remain alive until he cuts off the hands and tongues of hypocritical men who claim—or he said, who say—that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has died.”"],[1089,"Yazīd ibn Bābnūs reported: A companion and I went to ʿĀ’ishah and sought permission to enter. She placed a cushion for us and drew the curtain around herself. My companion asked, “Mother of the Believers, what do you say concerning al-ʿirāk?” She asked, “What is al-ʿirāk?” My companion struck me on the shoulder. She said, “Stop! You have hurt your brother.” She then asked, “What is al-ʿirāk? Do you mean menstruation? Use the expression Allah used: menstruation.” She continued: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would embrace me and attend to my hair while a garment separated me from him and I was menstruating. She then said: Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed my door, he would sometimes say a brief word through which Allah Almighty would bring benefit. One day, he passed without saying anything. He then passed again without saying anything. This occurred two or three times. I said, “Servant girl, place a cushion for me beside the door.” I wrapped my head. When he passed me, he asked: “ʿĀ’ishah, what is the matter with you?” I replied, “My head is aching.” He said: “Rather, it is my head that aches!” He left, but only a short time passed before he was brought back, carried in a cloak. He entered my room and sent word to his other wives, saying: “I have become ill and can no longer make the rounds among you. Give me permission to remain with ʿĀ’ishah.” I would help him perform ablution, though I had never done so for anyone before him. One day, while his head rested upon my shoulder, it inclined toward my head. I thought he wanted something from me. A small drop of cold saliva came from his mouth and fell into the hollow at the base of my throat, causing my skin to shiver. I thought he had lost consciousness, so I covered him with a garment. ʿUmar and al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah arrived and sought permission to enter. I admitted them and drew the curtain around myself. ʿUmar looked at him and said, “Oh, his unconsciousness! How severe is the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ loss of consciousness!” They then rose to leave. When they approached the door, al-Mughīrah said, “ʿUmar, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has died.” ʿUmar replied, “You have lied! You are a man overcome by tribulation. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ will not die until Allah Almighty destroys the hypocrites.” Abū Bakr then arrived, and I raised the curtain. He looked at the Prophet ﷺ and said, “Indeed, we belong to Allah, and to Him we shall return. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has died.” He approached him from the direction of his head, lowered his mouth, kissed his forehead, and said, “Oh, my Prophet!” He raised his head, then lowered his mouth again, kissed his forehead, and said, “Oh, Allah’s chosen one!” He raised his head once more, lowered his mouth and kissed him, saying, “Oh, my intimate friend! The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has died.” He went out to the mosque, where ʿUmar was addressing the people and saying, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ will not die until Allah Almighty destroys the hypocrites.” Abū Bakr began to speak. He praised and extolled Allah, then said: “Allah Almighty says: ‘Indeed, you will die, and they too will die.’” [Al-Zumar 39:30] He completed the verse. He then recited: “Muḥammad is only a messenger. Messengers have passed away before him. If he dies or is killed, will you turn back upon your heels?” [Āl ʿImrān 3:144] He completed the verse and then said: “Whoever worshipped Allah Almighty should know that Allah is living and never dies. Whoever worshipped Muḥammad should know that Muḥammad has died.” ʿUmar said, “Is that truly in the Book of Allah? I had not realized that it was in the Book of Allah!” ʿUmar then said, “O people, this is Abū Bakr, the venerable elder of the Muslims, so pledge allegiance to him.” The people then pledged allegiance to him."],[1090,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās reported: Abū Bakr came out while ʿUmar was speaking to the people and said, “Sit down, ʿUmar.” ʿUmar refused to sit, so the people turned toward Abū Bakr and left ʿUmar. Abū Bakr said: “To proceed: Whoever among you worshipped Muḥammad ﷺ should know that Muḥammad has died. Whoever among you worships Allah should know that Allah is living and never dies. “Allah says: ‘Muḥammad is only a messenger. Messengers have passed away before him…’ through the end of the verse, ‘and Allah will reward the grateful.’” [Āl ʿImrān 3:144] Ibn ʿAbbās said: By Allah, it was as though the people had not known that Allah had revealed this verse until Abū Bakr recited it. All the people learned it from him, and I heard every person reciting it. Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib informed me that ʿUmar said: “By Allah, as soon as I heard Abū Bakr recite it, my legs failed me and could no longer support me. I fell to the ground when I heard him recite it and realized that the Prophet ﷺ had indeed died.”"],[1091,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died at the age of sixty-three. Ibn Shihāb said: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib informed me of the same."],[1092,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ died at the age of sixty-three. Abū Bakr also died at the age of sixty-three, and ʿUmar died at the age of sixty-three."],[1093,"Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ died at the age of sixty-three, as did Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, and I am now sixty-three years old."],[1094,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained in Makkah for thirteen years and died at the age of sixty-three. Another wording states: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained in Makkah for thirteen years receiving revelation, and in Madinah for ten years. He died at the age of sixty-three."],[1095,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ died while Abū Bakr was at al-Sunḥ. Ismāʿīl explained that this was in the upper region of Madinah. ʿUmar stood and said, “By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has not died.” ʿĀ’ishah said that ʿUmar later explained, “By Allah, nothing else occurred to my mind. I believed Allah would surely raise him so that he might cut off the hands and feet of certain men.” Abū Bakr arrived, uncovered the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ face, kissed him, and said, “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you! You were pure in life and in death. By the One in whose hand is my soul, Allah will never make you experience death twice.” He then went out and said, “O man who is swearing, remain calm.” When Abū Bakr began to speak, ʿUmar sat down. Abū Bakr praised and extolled Allah and said: “Know that whoever worshipped Muḥammad should know that Muḥammad has died. Whoever worships Allah should know that Allah is living and never dies.” He recited: “Indeed, you will die, and they too will die.” [Al-Zumar 39:30] He also recited: “Muḥammad is only a messenger. Messengers have passed away before him. If he dies or is killed, will you turn back upon your heels? Whoever turns back upon his heels will never harm Allah in the least, and Allah will reward the grateful.” [Āl ʿImrān 3:144] The people then began sobbing.\nThe Anṣār assembled around Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah in the hall of Banū Sāʿidah and said, “There should be one ruler from us and one ruler from you.” Abū Bakr, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, and Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ went to them. ʿUmar intended to speak, but Abū Bakr silenced him. ʿUmar later said, “By Allah, I intended to speak only because I had prepared words that pleased me, and I feared that Abū Bakr might not express them as effectively.” Abū Bakr then spoke, and he was the most eloquent of the people. Among his words were: “We shall be the rulers, and you shall be the ministers.”\nAl-Ḥubāb ibn al-Mundhir said, “No, by Allah, we will not accept that. There must be one ruler from us and one ruler from you.” Abū Bakr replied, “No. We shall be the rulers, and you shall be the ministers. Quraysh occupy the central position among the Arabs in homeland and possess the clearest lineage. Pledge allegiance to either ʿUmar or Abū ʿUbaydah.” ʿUmar said, “Rather, we shall pledge allegiance to you. You are our leader, the best among us, and the one of us most beloved to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” ʿUmar took Abū Bakr’s hand and pledged allegiance to him, and the people also pledged allegiance. Someone said, “You have killed Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah.” ʿUmar replied, “May Allah deal with Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah.”"],[1096,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that he heard ʿUmar’s later address when he sat upon the pulpit on the day following the Prophet’s ﷺ death. ʿUmar pronounced the testimony of faith while Abū Bakr remained silent. He said: “I had hoped that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would live until he was the last of us to die. “But if Muḥammad has died, Allah Most High has left among you a light by which you may be guided—the light through which Allah guided Muḥammad ﷺ.” “Abū Bakr was the companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the second of the two in the cave. He therefore has the greatest right among the Muslims to manage your affairs. Rise and pledge allegiance to him.” A group of them had already pledged allegiance to him in the hall of Banū Sāʿidah. The general public pledge then took place at the pulpit. Al-Zuhrī reported from Anas ibn Mālik: I heard ʿUmar say to Abū Bakr that day, “Ascend the pulpit.” He continued urging him until Abū Bakr ascended the pulpit, whereupon the people generally pledged allegiance to him."],[1097,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: I used to teach the Qur’an to men from the Emigrants, including ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf. While I was staying at his residence in Minā during the final pilgrimage performed by ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān returned to me and said: “You should have seen the man who came to the Commander of the Believers today and said, ʿCommander of the Believers, what do you think of so-and-so? He says: “If ʿUmar dies, I shall pledge allegiance to so-and-so. By Allah, the pledge to Abū Bakr was merely an unexpected event, yet it was completed.”’” ʿUmar became angry and said, “If Allah wills, I shall stand before the people this evening and warn them against those who wish to seize control of their affairs.” ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: I told him, “Commander of the Believers, do not do that. The pilgrimage season gathers the common and disorderly people. They will be the majority surrounding you when you stand before the people. “I fear that you will make a statement that everyone will carry away from you, without understanding it or placing it in its proper context.” “Wait until you reach Madinah, for it is the home of migration and the Sunnah. There you can address the people of understanding and the respected leaders. You can deliver your statement with confidence, and the people of knowledge will understand it and place it in its proper context.” ʿUmar replied, “By Allah, if Allah wills, I shall certainly address this matter in the first gathering at which I speak in Madinah.” Ibn ʿAbbās continued: We reached Madinah near the end of Dhū al-Ḥijjah. When Friday came, I went early after the sun had passed its zenith and found Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl sitting beside a corner of the pulpit. I sat close to him until my knee touched his. It was not long before ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb came out. When I saw him approaching, I told Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl, “This afternoon, he will make a statement he has not made since becoming caliph.” Saʿīd rejected my suggestion and asked, “What could he possibly say that he has not said before?” ʿUmar sat upon the pulpit. When the callers to prayer had finished, he stood and praised Allah as He deserves to be praised. He then said: “To proceed: I am going to tell you something that it has been decreed for me to say. I do not know; perhaps I am saying it shortly before my death. “Whoever understands and retains it may relate it wherever his mount carries him. Whoever fears that he has not understood it is not permitted to attribute falsehood to me.”\n“Allah sent Muḥammad ﷺ with the truth and revealed the Book to him. “Among what Allah revealed was the verse concerning stoning. We recited it, understood it, and retained it. “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ carried out stoning, and we carried it out after him. “I fear that if a long time passes, someone will say, ‘By Allah, we do not find the verse of stoning in the Book of Allah,’ and people will go astray by abandoning an obligation Allah revealed.” “Stoning is a true ruling in Allah’s Book for married men and women who commit adultery when evidence is established, pregnancy occurs, or a confession is made. “Among what we used to recite from the Book of Allah was: ‘Do not deny your fathers, for denying your fathers is disbelief on your part’—or: ‘It is disbelief on your part to deny your fathers.’” “Behold! The Messenger of Allah ﷺ also said: ‘Do not praise me excessively as Jesus son of Mary was praised excessively. Rather, say: Allah’s servant and His Messenger.’” “It has reached me that one of you says, ‘By Allah, when ʿUmar dies, I shall pledge allegiance to so-and-so.’ “No one should deceive himself by saying, ‘The pledge to Abū Bakr was merely an unexpected event, yet it was completed.’ “It was indeed sudden, but Allah protected the Muslims from its potential harm. None among you possesses the stature toward which people’s necks turn as they did toward Abū Bakr.” “Whoever pledges allegiance to a man without consultation with the Muslims, neither he nor the person to whom he pledged should be followed, lest both expose themselves to being killed.” “What occurred when Allah took the soul of His Prophet ﷺ was that the Anṣār separated themselves from us and assembled in their entirety at the hall of Banū Sāʿidah. “ʿAlī, al-Zubayr, and those with them also remained apart from us. “The Emigrants assembled around Abū Bakr. “I said to Abū Bakr, ‘Abū Bakr, let us go to these brothers of ours from the Anṣār.’” “We set out toward them. “When we drew near, we met two righteous men from the Anṣār, who informed us of what the people had agreed upon. “They asked, ‘Where are you going, Emigrants?’ “We replied, ‘We are going to these brothers of ours from the Anṣār.’ “They said, ‘There is no need for you to approach them. Settle your own matter.’ “I said, ‘By Allah, we shall certainly go to them.’” “We continued until we reached them in the hall of Banū Sāʿidah. “A man wrapped in garments was lying among them. “I asked, ‘Who is this?’ “They replied, ‘This is Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah.’ “I asked, ‘What is wrong with him?’ “They replied, ‘He has a fever.’” “After we had been seated for a short while, their spokesman pronounced the testimony of faith and praised Allah as He deserves to be praised. He then said: “ʿTo proceed: We are the supporters of Allah and the army of Islam. You Emigrants are only a smaller group. “‘Yet a group from among your people has suddenly arrived, intending to separate us from our position and exclude us entirely from authority.’” “When he fell silent, I intended to speak. “I had prepared a speech that pleased me and wanted to deliver it before Abū Bakr spoke. I would sometimes try to moderate his gentleness with my firmness. “When I was about to speak, Abū Bakr said, ‘Remain calm.’ “I disliked making him angry.” “Abū Bakr then spoke. He was more forbearing and dignified than I. “By Allah, he did not omit a single statement from the speech I had prepared and admired without expressing spontaneously something equal or superior to it. He then said: “ʿWhatever goodness you have mentioned concerning yourselves, you are indeed worthy of it. “ʿBut the Arabs will recognize this authority only for this clan of Quraysh. They occupy the central position among the Arabs in lineage and homeland. “‘I approve either of these two men for you, so pledge allegiance to whichever of them you wish.’” “He took my hand and the hand of Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ, who was sitting between us. “Nothing he said displeased me except that proposal. “By Allah, I would rather be brought forward and have my neck struck—provided it did not bring me closer to sin—than become ruler over a people among whom was Abū Bakr. “Unless, perhaps, my soul suggests something to me at the time of death that I do not feel now.” “One of the Anṣār said, ‘I am its seasoned counselor and its honored, supported leader. Let there be one ruler from us and one ruler from you, people of Quraysh.’ “Clamor increased and voices rose until I feared that disagreement would occur. “I said, ‘Extend your hand, Abū Bakr.’ “He extended his hand, and I pledged allegiance to him. The Emigrants then pledged allegiance to him, followed by the Anṣār.” “We crowded around Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah. “One of them said, ‘You have killed Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah.’ “I replied, ‘May Allah deal with Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah.’” ʿUmar continued: “By Allah, under the circumstances confronting us, we found no course of action stronger than pledging allegiance to Abū Bakr. “We feared that if we left the people without securing a pledge, they would pledge allegiance to one of their own after our departure. “We would then either have to accept something with which we were not pleased or oppose them, resulting in disorder.” “Therefore, whoever pledges allegiance to a man without consultation with the Muslims must not be followed, nor should the man to whom he pledged be followed, lest both expose themselves to being killed.”"],[1098,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, the speakers of the Anṣār stood to address the people. Some of them said, “Emigrants, whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed a man from among you, he would place a man from among us beside him. We therefore believe this authority should be held by two men: one from you and one from us.” The speakers of the Anṣār continued repeating this proposal. Zayd ibn Thābit stood and said, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was from the Emigrants, so the leader should be from the Emigrants. We shall be his supporters just as we were the supporters of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” Abū Bakr stood and said, “May Allah reward you with goodness as a tribe, people of the Anṣār, and may He make your speaker steadfast.” He then said, “By Allah, had you done otherwise, we would not have reconciled with you.”"],[1099,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, the Anṣār said, “There should be one ruler from us and one ruler from you.” ʿUmar came to them and said, “People of the Anṣār, do you not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer? Which of you would willingly place himself ahead of Abū Bakr?” The Anṣār replied, “We seek refuge with Allah from placing ourselves ahead of Abū Bakr.”"],[1100,"Sālim ibn ʿUbayd reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became ill and lost consciousness. When he regained consciousness, he asked: “Has the time for prayer arrived?” They replied, “Yes.” He said: “Tell Bilāl to call the prayer, and tell Abū Bakr to lead the people.” He then lost consciousness again. When he regained consciousness, he asked: “Has the time for prayer arrived?” They replied, “Yes.” He said: “Tell Bilāl to call the prayer, and tell Abū Bakr to lead the people.” He then lost consciousness again. ʿĀ’ishah said, “My father is a deeply tenderhearted man. If only you would appoint someone else.” When the Prophet ﷺ regained consciousness, he asked: “Has the prayer commenced?” They replied, “No.” He said: “Tell Bilāl to announce its commencement, and tell Abū Bakr to lead the people.” ʿĀ’ishah said, “My father is a tenderhearted—or deeply sorrowful—man. If only you would appoint someone else.” He replied: “You women are indeed like the women associated with Joseph! Tell Bilāl to call the prayer, and tell Abū Bakr to lead the people.” Bilāl announced the prayer’s commencement, and Abū Bakr stepped forward. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ later regained consciousness and said: “Find people upon whom I can lean.” He came out leaning upon Barīrah and another person until he sat beside Abū Bakr. Abū Bakr intended to step backward, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prevented him. Abū Bakr continued leading the people in prayer. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, ʿUmar said, “If I hear anyone say that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has died, I shall strike him with my sword.” Sālim ibn ʿUbayd continued: They sent me, saying, “Go to the companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and summon him.” I went to Abū Bakr while he was in the mosque, and I was overwhelmed with shock. Abū Bakr asked me, “Has the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died?” I replied, “ʿUmar says, ‘If I hear anyone say that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has died, I shall strike him with my sword.’” Abū Bakr stood and took hold of my forearm. He and I arrived together, and he said, “Make room for me.”\nThey made room for him. He bent over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, touched him, placed his hands—or one hand—upon him, and recited: “Indeed, you will die, and they too will die.” They asked, “Companion of the Messenger of Allah, has the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died?” He replied, “Yes.” They then knew that the matter was as he had said. They were an unlettered people, and no prophet had previously lived among them. They asked, “Companion of the Messenger of Allah, should we perform the funeral prayer over him?” He replied, “Yes.” They asked, “How should we pray over him?” He replied, “Groups should enter one after another, pronounce the takbīr, pray, and supplicate. They should then leave, and another group should enter, until everyone has finished.” They asked, “Companion of the Messenger of Allah, should he be buried?” He replied, “Yes.” They asked, “Where should he be buried?” He replied, “In the place where Allah took his soul, for Allah takes a prophet’s soul only in a pure place.” They knew that the matter was as he had said. Abū Bakr then went out and instructed that the members of the Prophet’s ﷺ paternal family wash his body. The Emigrants assembled and consulted one another. They said, “The Anṣār have a rightful role in this matter.” They went to them, and one of the Anṣār said, “There should be one ruler from us and one ruler from the Emigrants.” ʿUmar stood and asked them, “Who possesses three distinctions like those of Abū Bakr? “‘The second of two when they were in the cave’—who were those two? “ʿWhen he said to his companion, “Do not grieve; indeed, Allah is with us”’—who were those two? “With whom was Allah Almighty?” He then took Abū Bakr’s hand and pledged allegiance to him. The people also pledged allegiance, and it was a good and honorable pledge."],[1101,"ʿĀ’ishah said: When they intended to wash the Prophet ﷺ, they said, “By Allah, we do not know whether we should remove the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ clothes as we do with our deceased, or wash him while his clothes remain on him.” When they differed over this, Allah caused sleep to overcome them until there was not a man among us except that his chin rested upon his chest. Then someone spoke to them from a corner of the house, though they did not know who he was, saying, “Wash the Prophet ﷺ while his clothes remain on him.” They then went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and washed him while his shirt remained on him, pouring water over the shirt and rubbing him with it rather than with their bare hands. ʿĀ’ishah used to say, “Had I known beforehand what I came to know afterward, none but his wives would have washed him.”"],[1102,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: When the people resolved to wash the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, no one was in the house except members of his family: his uncle al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās, Qutham ibn al-ʿAbbās, Usāmah ibn Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, and his freedman Ṣāliḥ. When they assembled to wash him, Aws ibn Khawli al-Anṣārī—one of Banū ʿAwf ibn al-Khazraj who had participated at Badr—called to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib from behind the door, saying, “O ʿAlī, I adjure you by Allah, and remind you of our share in the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” ʿAlī said to him, “Enter.” He entered and attended the washing of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, although he took no part in washing him. ʿAlī supported him against his chest while his shirt remained on him. Al-ʿAbbās, al-Faḍl, and Qutham helped ʿAlī turn him, while Usāmah ibn Zayd and his freedman Ṣāliḥ poured the water. ʿAlī washed him and saw none of the things ordinarily seen in a deceased person. He kept saying, “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you! How pure and pleasant you are, both alive and dead!”"],[1103,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was shrouded in three white garments from Saḥūl, containing neither a shirt nor a turban."],[1104,"ʿĀ’ishah said: I entered upon Abū Bakr, and he asked, “In how many garments did you shroud the Prophet ﷺ?” She replied, “In three white garments from Saḥūl, containing neither a shirt nor a turban.” He then asked her, “On what day did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ die?” She replied, “Monday.” He asked, “What day is today?” She replied, “Monday.” He said, “I hope that I will die sometime between now and tonight.” He looked at a garment he was wearing during his illness, which bore a stain of saffron, and said, “Wash this garment of mine, add two other garments to it, and shroud me in them.” I said, “But this is worn out.” He replied, “The living have a greater right to new clothes than the dead. It is only destined for the bodily discharge.” He did not die until Tuesday night had begun, and he was buried before morning."],[1105,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was shrouded in three white garments from Saḥūl."],[1106,"Abū ʿAsīb—or Abū ʿAsīm—reported, according to Bahz, that he witnessed the funeral prayer over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They asked, “How should we pray over him?” He replied, “Enter in separate groups, one group after another.” They would enter through one door, perform the funeral prayer over him, and then leave through the other door. When he ﷺ was placed in his grave niche, al-Mughīrah said, “Something near his feet has been left unfinished.” They said, “Then enter and put it right.” He entered, reached in with his hand, and touched the Prophet’s feet. He then said, “Pour the earth over me.” They poured earth until it reached halfway up his shins, after which he came out. He thereafter used to say, “I am the last of you to have had contact with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[1107,"Sālim ibn ʿUbayd, who was one of the People of the Ṣuffah, said: Abū Bakr entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ after he had died and then came out. He was asked, “Has the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died?” He replied, “Yes,” and they knew that the matter was as he said. He was then asked, “Should the funeral prayer be performed over him, and how should it be performed?” He replied, “They should come in groups, one group after another, and pray over him.” They knew that the matter was as he said. They asked, “Should he be buried, and where?” He replied, “In the place where Allah took his soul, for Allah takes a prophet’s soul only in a pure place.” They knew that the matter was as he said."],[1108,"Anas said: When the Prophet ﷺ died, there were two gravediggers in Madinah: one made niche graves, while the other made trench graves. They said, “We shall seek our Lord’s choice and send for both of them; whichever arrives first, we shall employ him.” They sent for them, and the man who made niche graves arrived first, so they made a niche grave for the Prophet ﷺ."],[1109,"ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ reported that Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ said during the illness in which he died: “Make a niche grave for me and set unbaked bricks over me in rows, just as was done for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[1110,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib said: I washed the Prophet ﷺ and looked for what is ordinarily seen in a deceased person, but I saw nothing. He ﷺ was pure and pleasant both alive and dead. Four persons, to the exclusion of the rest of the people, undertook his burial and concealment in the grave: ʿAlī, al-ʿAbbās, al-Faḍl, and Ṣāliḥ, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. A niche was made for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and unbaked bricks were set over him in rows."],[1111,"ʿĀ’ishah said: There were two gravediggers in Madinah: one made niche graves and the other made trench graves. They waited to see which of the two would come, and the one who made niche graves arrived. He therefore made a niche grave for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[1112,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: A red velvet cloth was placed in the Prophet’s ﷺ grave."],[1113,"ʿĀ’ishah, Mother of the Believers, said: We did not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had been buried until we heard the sound of the spades in the middle of the night preceding Wednesday."],[1114,"ʿĀ’ishah said: During his illness, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would repeatedly ask, “Where am I today? Where will I be tomorrow?” eagerly awaiting ʿĀ’ishah’s day. When my day came, Allah took his soul while he was resting between my upper chest and neck, and he was buried in my house."],[1115,"ʿUrwah reported that ʿĀ’ishah said to ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr: “Bury me alongside my fellow wives, and do not bury me in the house alongside the Prophet ﷺ, for I dislike being praised and declared especially virtuous on that account.” ʿUrwah also said: ʿUmar sent a request to ʿĀ’ishah, saying, “Permit me to be buried alongside my two companions.” She replied, “Yes, by Allah.” Whenever another man from among the Companions had previously sent her such a request, she would say, “No, by Allah! I will never prefer anyone over them.”"],[1116,"ʿĀ’ishah said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, they differed concerning where he should be buried. Abū Bakr said, “I heard something from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that I have never forgotten. He said: ‘Allah never takes the soul of a prophet except at the place where He wishes him to be buried.’”"],[1117,"ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith—the brother of Juwayriyyah bint al-Ḥārith and thus the Prophet’s ﷺ brother-in-law—said: At his death, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left neither a dirham nor a dinar, neither a male nor a female slave, nor anything else, except his white mule, his weapons, and some land that he had designated as charity."],[1118,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “My heirs shall not divide a single dinar among themselves. Whatever I leave after the maintenance of my wives and the expenses of my administrator is charity.”"],[1119,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ left neither a dinar nor a dirham, neither a sheep nor a camel, and he made no personal bequest."],[1120,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported that the Prophet ﷺ turned toward Mount Uḥud and said: “By Him in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, I would not be pleased if Uḥud were transformed into gold for the family of Muḥammad and I spent it in the cause of Allah, yet on the day I died I left behind even two dinars of it—except two dinars that I had set aside to repay a debt, if there were one.” He died leaving neither a dinar nor a dirham, neither a male nor a female slave. He left his armor pledged to a Jewish man in return for thirty sāʿs of barley."],[1121,"Abū Dharr reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I would not be pleased to possess the equivalent of Mount Uḥud in gold and then, on the day I died, have even a dinar or half a dinar of it remaining with me, unless I had reserved it for a creditor.”"],[1122,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said: The Prophet’s ﷺ wives sent ʿUthmān to Abū Bakr to ask him for their one-eighth share of what Allah had granted His Messenger ﷺ as spoils. I was the one who dissuaded them, saying, “Will you not fear Allah? Do you not know that the Prophet ﷺ used to say—referring to himself—‘We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity. The family of Muḥammad ﷺ may only receive their maintenance from this property’?” The Prophet’s ﷺ wives then desisted when I informed them of this. This charity later came under ʿAlī’s administration. ʿAlī denied al-ʿAbbās control of it and prevailed over him concerning it. It then came under the administration of al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, followed by al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, and then ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn and al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ḥasan, who administered it in turn. It later came under Zayd ibn al-Ḥasan’s administration. It was truly the charitable endowment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[1123,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that Fāṭimah and al-ʿAbbās came to Abū Bakr seeking their inheritance: the Prophet’s land at Fadak and his share of Khaybar. Abū Bakr said: “I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: ‘We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity. The family of Muḥammad may only receive their maintenance from this property.’ By Allah, maintaining ties with the relatives of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is dearer to me than maintaining ties with my own relatives.”"],[1124,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon me while I was ill and unconscious. He performed ablution, and some of the water he had used for ablution was poured over me, whereupon I regained consciousness."],[1125,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Prophet ﷺ used to enter the house of Umm Sulaym and sleep upon her bed while she was away. One day he came and slept on her bed. Someone went to her and said, “The Prophet ﷺ is sleeping in your house upon your bed.” She came and found that he had perspired and that his perspiration had collected upon a piece of leather on the bed. She opened her small container and began absorbing the perspiration and squeezing it into her bottles. The Prophet ﷺ awoke with a start and asked, “What are you doing, Umm Sulaym?” She replied, “O Messenger of Allah, we hope for its blessing for our children.” He said, “You have done well.”"],[1126,"Thumāmah reported from Anas that Umm Sulaym used to spread a leather mat for the Prophet ﷺ, upon which he would take his midday rest in her home. When the Prophet ﷺ slept, she would collect some of his perspiration and hair in a bottle and then mix it into sukk. When Anas ibn Mālik was near death, he instructed that some of that sukk be placed in the perfume used for his burial preparation, and it was placed in it."],[1127,"Al-Sā’ib ibn Yazīd said: My maternal aunt took me to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of Allah, my sister’s son is ill.” He stroked my head and prayed that I be blessed. He then performed ablution, and I drank from the water he had used. I stood behind him and looked at the Seal of Prophethood between his shoulder blades; it resembled the button of a bridal canopy."],[1128,"Abū Ayyūb reported that the Prophet ﷺ stayed in his home. He then related the report, saying that he used to prepare food for the Prophet ﷺ. When the dish was returned to him, he would ask where the Prophet’s fingers had touched it and would eat from those places."],[1129,"Abū Mūsā said: I was with the Prophet ﷺ while he was staying at al-Jiʿrānah between Makkah and Madinah, and Bilāl was with him. A Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “Muḥammad, will you not fulfill what you promised me?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him, “Receive glad tidings.” The Bedouin replied, “You have said ‘Receive glad tidings’ to me too many times.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then turned to Abū Mūsā and Bilāl with the appearance of someone displeased and said, “This man has rejected the glad tidings, so you two accept them.” They replied, “We accept them, O Messenger of Allah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then called for a vessel containing water. He washed his hands and face in it and rinsed his mouth into it. He then said, “Drink from it, pour it over your faces and chests, and receive glad tidings.” They took the vessel and did as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had instructed them. Umm Salamah called to them from behind the curtain, “Leave some of what is in your vessel for your mother.” They therefore left a portion of it for her."],[1130,"ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd al-Thaqafī said: By Allah, whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ expectorated, it would fall into the palm of one of them, who would then rub it over his face and skin. Whenever he commanded them, they hastened to carry out his command; and whenever he performed ablution, they would nearly fight one another over the water he had used."],[1131,"ʿAwn ibn Abī Juḥayfah reported that his father saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in a red leather tent. He said: I saw Bilāl bring out the water the Prophet ﷺ had used for ablution, and I saw the people rushing to obtain it. Whoever obtained some of it rubbed himself with it, and whoever obtained none took moisture from the hand of his companion."],[1132,"Abū Burdah said: I came to Madinah and met ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām. He said to me, “Come with me to my home, and I will give you a drink in a cup from which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ drank, and you may pray in a place of prayer in which the Prophet ﷺ prayed.” I went with him, and he gave me a drink of sawīq, fed me dates, and I prayed in his place of prayer."],[1133,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: After the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had thrown pebbles at the Jamrah, slaughtered his sacrificial animal, and was ready to shave his head, he presented his right side to the barber, who shaved it. He then summoned Abū Ṭalḥah al-Anṣārī and gave the hair to him. Next, he presented his left side to the barber and said, “Shave it.” After it was shaved, he gave the hair to Abū Ṭalḥah and said, “Distribute it among the people.”"],[1134,"ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Mawhab said: My family sent me to Umm Salamah with a cup of water in which some of the Prophet’s ﷺ hair had been placed. Whenever a person was afflicted by the evil eye or some other ailment, a vessel would be sent to her. I looked into a small container and saw some reddish hairs."],[1135,"Sahl ibn Saʿd reported: The Prophet ﷺ came and sat with his Companions in the shaded meeting place of Banū Sāʿidah. He then said, “Give us something to drink, Sahl.” So I brought out this cup and served them from it. Sahl later brought that same cup out for us, and we drank from it. Afterward, ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz asked Sahl to give it to him, and Sahl presented it to him as a gift."],[1136,"Sahl ibn Saʿd reported: A woman came to the Prophet ﷺ carrying a cloak. Sahl asked the people, “Do you know what a burdah is?” They replied, “It is a mantle.” Sahl explained, “It is a woven mantle with a border.” The woman said, “O Messenger of Allah, I have brought this for you to wear.” The Prophet ﷺ accepted it because he needed it and put it on. A man among the Companions saw him wearing it and said, “O Messenger of Allah, how beautiful this is! Give it to me to wear.” He replied, “Yes.” After the Prophet ﷺ had left, the man’s companions reproached him, saying, “You did not do well. You saw that the Prophet ﷺ accepted it because he needed it, yet you asked him for it, knowing that he never refuses anyone who asks him for something.” The man replied, “I hoped to obtain its blessing after the Prophet ﷺ had worn it, so that perhaps I might be shrouded in it.”"],[1137,"Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr said: This is the robe of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. It remained with ʿĀ’ishah until she died, and when she died, I took possession of it. The Prophet ﷺ used to wear it, and we wash it in water for the sick, seeking healing through it."],[1138,"Indeed, in these ḥadīths, which are very many and have been narrated in their proper places, there is a clear text from the Sharīʿah which is evidence for seeking blessing (tabarruk) from the Prophet ﷺ in three things:\n1. Seeking blessing from the blessed person of the Prophet ﷺ, such as his blessed body, hands and feet.\n2. Seeking blessing from things that became separated from his blessed body, which are pure and not impure, such as sweat, saliva, hair and the like.\n3. Seeking blessing from things that the Prophet ﷺ used, such as clothing, bedding, blankets, vessels and the like.\nAs for seeking blessing from the soil of the blessed grave, from the noble chamber (ḥujrah), and from the doors of the mosque, all of this is not prescribed, because it is not established from the Prophet ﷺ that he commanded doing so or approved of it. It is also not established from the Companions and the Followers that they sought blessing from it.\nAnd seeking blessing from the persons of righteous people and their relics is all forbidden, and it leads to shirk (associating partners) with Allah, like the people of the Jāhiliyyah among whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sent: they used to seek blessing from their idols, and ask them to pray for blessing in wealth, children and lives.\nThe Companions and the Followers were upon this pure creed taken from the Book and the Sunnah, until the era of the Rāfiḍah came, and they began to show seeking blessing from their imams and their graves. Some ignorant people among the Muslims adopted these polytheistic practices from them, built mosques over the graves of their saints, and became attendants (mujāwirs) around them. It is authentically established from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: “Do not make your houses graves, and do not make my grave a place of festival, and send blessings upon me, for your blessings reach me wherever you are.” This ḥadīth is narrated in its place. And for these reasons and others, when Sayyidunā ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb learned that people were coming to the tree called Shajarat al-Riḍwān and praying there, he warned the people and ordered that the tree be cut down, and it was cut down."],[1139,"The word of Allah the Exalted: O wives of the Prophet! You are not like other women. If you are God-fearing, then do not speak in a soft tone, lest one in whose heart is a disease should have an evil thought, but speak in a proper manner. And stay in your homes with dignity, and do not display your adornment as in the former days of ignorance, and keep up the prayer and give zakāh, and obey Allah and His Messenger. Allah only wishes, O members of the household of the Prophet, to remove (every kind of) impurity from you and to purify you thoroughly. And keep remembering what is recited in your homes of the verses of Allah and the ḥadīths of the Messenger. Indeed, Allah is Subtle, All-Aware."],[1140,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: The Prophet ﷺ would visit all his women within a single period of the night or day, and they numbered eleven. Qatādah said: I asked Anas, “Was he capable of doing that?” He replied, “We used to say that he had been given the strength of thirty men.”"],[1141,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ would visit all his wives in a single night, and at that time he had nine wives."],[1142,"§1. Khadījah bint Khuwaylid ibn Asad\nShe is Sayyidah Khadījah bint Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Quṣayy al-Qurashiyyah al-Asadiyyah, may Allah be pleased with her. She was first married to ʿAtīq ibn ʿĀʾidh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Makhzūm, then she came into the marriage of Abū Hālah Hind ibn Zurārah ibn Nabbāsh ibn ʿAdī al-Tamīmī. This is the statement of Qatādah, and Ibn Isḥāq agreed with it in the narration of Yūnus ibn Bukayr, and this is also the statement mentioned in the Kitāb al-Nasab of al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār, as al-Ḥāfiẓ has stated. But Ibn ʿAbd al-Barr said the opposite and considered that correct, although the first statement is transmitted from the experts of history.\nThen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married her when she was forty years old, as Ibn ʿAbd al-Barr mentioned. She was the first wife of the Messenger of Allah, and this marriage took place before the prophethood. She was a true supporter of Islam and the mother of all the children of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ except Ibrāhīm, who was from Māriyah, apart from the Copt (Māriyah), and she did not spend twenty-four years with the Prophet ﷺ, as Ibn ʿAbd al-Barr mentioned. The Prophet ﷺ did not marry another woman during her lifetime until she passed away. Her death took place in the tenth year of the prophethood, after leaving the valley (shiʿb) of Banū Hāshim, when she was sixty-four years and six months old. She was buried at al-Ḥajūn, and at that time the funeral prayer had not been commanded."],[1143,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The Prophet ﷺ did not marry another woman while Khadījah was alive, until she died."],[1144,"§2. ʿĀʾishah bint Abī Bakr al-Ṣiddīq\nThen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, after Khadījah, married ʿĀʾishah, the daughter of Sayyidunā Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, in Shawwāl of the tenth year."],[1145,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “You were shown to me in a dream for three nights. The angel brought you to me wrapped in a piece of silk and said, ‘This is your wife.’ I uncovered your face and saw that it was you. I then said, ‘If this is from Allah, He will bring it to pass.’”"],[1146,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported that the Prophet ﷺ contracted marriage with her when she was seven years old, and she was brought to live with him as his bride when she was nine. Her dolls remained with her, and he died when she was eighteen years old."],[1147,"§3. Sawdah bint Zamʿah ibn Qays al-Qurashiyyah al-ʿĀmiriyyah\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ married her after ʿĀʾishah, and she took care of raising the Prophet’s daughters, managing the affairs of the household, and stayed with them in Makkah. And after coming to Madinah, he married ʿĀʾishah."],[1148,"ʿĀ’ishah said: I never saw a woman in whose place I would have preferred to be more than Sawdah bint Zamʿah, despite the sharpness in her temperament. When she grew old, she gave her allotted day with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to ʿĀ’ishah. She said, “O Messenger of Allah, I have given my day with you to ʿĀ’ishah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would therefore allot two days to ʿĀ’ishah: her own day and Sawdah’s day."],[1149,"Sālim ibn ʿAbdullāh reported that he heard ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, relate that when Ḥafṣah bint ʿUmar became a widow after the death of Khunays ibn Ḥudhāfah al-Sahmī—one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who died in Madinah—ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb said: “I went to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān and offered Ḥafṣah to him in marriage. He replied, ‘I will consider the matter.’ Several nights passed, and then he met me and said, ‘I have decided not to marry at this time.’ “I then met Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq and said, ‘If you wish, I will marry Ḥafṣah bint ʿUmar to you.’ Abū Bakr remained silent and gave me no reply. I was more upset with him than I had been with ʿUthmān. “Several nights passed, and then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ proposed marriage to her, so I gave her to him in marriage. Later, Abū Bakr met me and said, ‘Perhaps you were upset with me when you offered Ḥafṣah to me and I gave you no reply?’ “I said, ‘Yes.’ “Abū Bakr said, ‘Nothing prevented me from responding to your offer except that I knew the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had mentioned her, and I would never disclose the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ secret. Had the Messenger of Allah ﷺ decided not to marry her, I would have accepted her.’”"],[1150,"She was given the title Umm al-Masākīn (Mother of the Poor), because she used to feed them and give charity to them. She was married to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh, who was martyred at Uḥud, so the Prophet ﷺ married her.\nIt is even said that she used to live with al-Ṭufayl ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, then after him his brother ʿUbaydah ibn al-Ḥārith took care of her, and on her mother’s side she was the sister of Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith, may Allah be pleased with her. The Prophet’s ﷺ consummation of the marriage with her took place after Ḥafṣah bint ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with her, and then she stayed with him only two or three months, and she passed away.\n§6. Umm Salamah Hind bint Abī Umayyah al-Qurashiyyah al-Makhzūmiyyah"],[1151,"Sayyidah Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, was in the marriage of Abū Salamah ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Asad, may Allah be pleased with him, who was the cousin and foster brother of the Prophet ﷺ. Abū Salamah passed away in the year 4 AH after returning from Uḥud, because of the wound he had received at Uḥud. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her. Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, died in the year 61 or 62 AH, which is considered more correct, while some have said otherwise."],[1152,"Umm Salamah reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “No Muslim is afflicted by a calamity and then says what Allah has commanded: ‘Indeed, we belong to Allah, and to Him we shall return. O Allah, reward me for my affliction and replace it for me with something better,’ except that Allah replaces it for him with something better.” She said: When Abū Salamah died, I thought, “Which Muslim could be better than Abū Salamah, the head of the first household to migrate to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” Nevertheless, I recited the supplication, and Allah replaced him for me with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\nShe continued: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah to propose marriage to me on his behalf. I said, “I have a daughter, and I am a woman who experiences jealousy.” He ﷺ replied, “As for her daughter, we shall pray to Allah to make her independent of her; and I shall pray to Allah to remove her jealousy.”"],[1153,"ʿĀ’ishah, Mother of the Believers, reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed the captives of Banū al-Muṣṭaliq, Juwayriyyah bint al-Ḥārith fell to the share of Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās—or one of his cousins—and she entered into a contract to purchase her freedom from him. She was a beautiful and attractive woman whom no one could see without being captivated by her. She came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seeking his assistance in paying her emancipation contract. ʿĀ’ishah said: By Allah, as soon as I saw her at the door of my chamber, I disliked her presence, because I knew that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would see in her what I had seen. Juwayriyyah entered and said, “O Messenger of Allah, I am Juwayriyyah, daughter of al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Ḍirār, the chief of his people. You are well aware of the affliction that has befallen me. I fell to the share of Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās—or one of his cousins—and entered into a contract to purchase my freedom. I have come to ask your assistance in paying it.” He ﷺ said, “Would you like something better than that?” She asked, “What is it, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied, “I will pay your emancipation contract and marry you.” She said, “Yes, O Messenger of Allah.” He ﷺ said, “I have done so.” The news then spread among the people that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had married Juwayriyyah bint al-Ḥārith. The people said, “They are now the in-laws of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ!” They consequently released the captives in their possession. ʿĀ’ishah said: One hundred households of Banū al-Muṣṭaliq were set free as a result of his marriage to her. I do not know of any woman who brought greater blessing to her people than she did."],[1154,"Allah the Exalted says: And when you said to the one upon whom Allah had bestowed favor and upon whom you had bestowed favor: “Keep your wife (Zaynab) with you and fear Allah,” and you were hiding in your heart something that Allah was going to reveal, and you feared the people, although Allah has more right that you should fear Him. Then when Zayd had fulfilled his need of her, We gave her in marriage to you, so that there may be no sin upon the believers regarding the wives of their adopted sons when they have fulfilled their need of them. And the command of Allah is bound to be carried out. Al-Aḥzāb: 37"],[1155,"Anas reported: Zayd ibn Ḥārithah came complaining about his marriage, and the Prophet ﷺ repeatedly said to him, “Fear Allah and keep your wife.” Anas said: Had the Messenger of Allah ﷺ been one to conceal any part of the revelation, he would have concealed this verse. Zaynab used to express her distinction over the other wives of the Prophet ﷺ by saying, “Your families arranged your marriages, but Allah Most High gave me in marriage from above the seven heavens.”"],[1156,"Umm Ḥabībah had been married to ʿUbaydullāh ibn Jaḥsh, who died in Abyssinia. The Negus then married her to the Prophet ﷺ and, on his behalf, gave her a dowry of four thousand dirhams. He sent her to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the company of Shuraḥbīl ibn Ḥasanah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not send her anything toward the dowry. The dowries of his other wives were four hundred dirhams. Abū Dāwūd said: Ḥasanah was his mother."],[1157,"Anas reported: I was riding behind Abū Ṭalḥah on the day of Khaybar, and my foot was touching the foot of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. We reached the people of Khaybar just as the sun rose. They had brought out their livestock and had gone out carrying their axes, baskets, and spades. When they saw the army, they exclaimed, “Muḥammad and the army!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Khaybar is ruined! When we descend into the territory of a people who have been warned, terrible is the morning that befalls them.” Allah Almighty defeated them. A beautiful female captive fell to the share of Diḥyah, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ acquired her from him in exchange for seven captives. He then entrusted her to Umm Sulaym so that she might prepare and adorn her for him. The narrator said: I think he also said that she was to observe her waiting period in Umm Sulaym’s house. This woman was Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ made dates, dried yogurt, and clarified butter the food for her wedding feast. Shallow pits were dug in the ground, leather mats were spread within them, and dried yogurt and clarified butter were brought. The people ate until they were satisfied. The people said, “We do not know whether he has married her or taken her as a bondwoman who may bear him a child. If he screens her, she is his wife; but if he does not screen her, she is a bondwoman.” When he was ready to depart, he screened her, and she sat upon the rear part of the camel. They then knew that he had married her. As they drew near Madinah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ urged his mount forward, and we did likewise. His she-camel al-ʿAḍbāʾ stumbled, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Ṣafiyyah both fell. He immediately stood and screened her from view. Some women were looking on and said, “May Allah remove the Jewish woman far away!” Anas was asked, “Abū Ḥamzah, did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ really fall?” He replied, “Yes, by Allah, he certainly fell.”"],[1158,"Yazīd ibn al-Aṣamm said: Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith informed me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married her while he was not in the state of iḥrām. She was both my maternal aunt and the maternal aunt of Ibn ʿAbbās."],[1159,"She was a captive of Banū Qurayẓah, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took her as his own share (ṣafiyyah). It was said that he freed her and married her, and it was also said that she was his female slave and he had relations with her with the rights of a slave woman, and this is the better-known statement, as Ibn al-Qayyim said in Zād al-Maʿād.\nAnd according to the well-known narration, she passed away before the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and was buried in al-Baqīʿ. It is also said that at the time of his ﷺ death she was in Makkah.\n§13. Māriyah al-Qibṭiyyah"],[1160,"She was the mother of Ibrāhīm. Al-Muqawqis, the ruler of the Copts, sent her as a gift to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the seventh year of the Hijrah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took her as his female slave and had relations with her with the rights of a slave woman; nevertheless, the veil of ḥijāb was also observed for her. She conceived from him ﷺ and gave birth to Ibrāhīm in Dhū al-Ḥijjah 8 AH. She passed away in Muḥarram 16 AH; Sayyidunā ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, led her funeral prayer and buried her in al-Baqīʿ."],[1161,"Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb reported: The ruler of the Copts presented the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with two Coptic bondwomen who were sisters, together with a mule. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to ride the mule. He took one of the two women as a concubine, and she gave birth to his son Ibrāhīm. He gave the other woman to Ḥassān ibn Thābit al-Anṣārī."],[1162,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: When the daughter of al-Jawn was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he approached her, she said, “I seek refuge in Allah from you.” He replied, “You have sought refuge in the Almighty. Return to your family.”"],[1163,"There is no disagreement that the number of his ﷺ daughters was four. Their names are:\n1. Zaynab 2. Ruqayyah 3. Umm Kulthūm 4. Fāṭimah al-Zahrāʾ\nIbn ʿAbd al-Barr said in al-Istīʿāb, in the biography of Fāṭimah: What the heart is satisfied with, and the reports are mutawātir (so widely transmitted), is that the first of the daughters of the Messenger of Allah in order is Zaynab.\nAs for the sons, according to the correct view and the majority opinion their number is three:\nQāsim, after whom his kunyah (Abū al-Qāsim) was derived,\nʿAbd Allāh, and Ṭayyib and Ṭāhir, these two being his titles (laqabs), and this is the correct statement, as Ibn al-Qayyim also said.\nThere is no disagreement that all of his ﷺ children were from Sayyidah Khadījah, may Allah be pleased with them both, except Sayyidunā Ibrāhīm, who was from Māriyah, and all of his children died before him except Sayyidah Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with them both, for she lived until six months after his ﷺ death. His ﷺ lineage continued through her, and its details are as follows.\n§1. Al-Qāsim"],[1164,"§1. Qāsim\nHe was the first child of his ﷺ, and his kunyah was named after him. He was born before the prophethood and died in childhood. And it is said: he had not yet reached the age of puberty.\nIt is narrated through Ibn Mājah and Abū Dāwūd: Hishām ibn Abī al-Walīd narrated to us, from his mother, from Fāṭimah bint al-Ḥusayn, from her father al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: When the son of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Qāsim, died, the Mother of the Believers Khadījah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: “O Messenger of Allah! Milk has gathered in the breast from which Qāsim used to nurse. If only Allah had kept him alive until the nursing period was completed.” So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “His nursing period is being completed in Paradise.”\n§2. ʿAbd Allāh"],[1165,"§2. ʿAbd Allāh\nHe was also called by the names Ṭāhir and Ṭayyib. It is said: he was given these names because his birth took place after the prophethood. And it is said: all the children of his ﷺ, other than Ibrāhīm, were born before Islam, and they died in childhood in Makkah. See al-Iṣābah (4324).\n§3. Ibrāhīm"],[1166,"§3. Ibrāhīm\nIbrāhīm’s mother was Māriyah al-Qibṭiyyah. He was born in the eighth year of the Hijrah and died in the tenth year of the Hijrah, at which time he was eighteen months old. He was buried in Jannat al-Baqīʿ."],[1167,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “A son was born to me tonight, and I named him after my father Ibrāhīm.” He then entrusted him to Umm Sayf, the wife of a blacksmith known as Abū Sayf. The Prophet ﷺ went to visit the child, and I followed him. We reached Abū Sayf while he was blowing his bellows, and the house was filled with smoke. I hurried ahead of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “Abū Sayf, stop; the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has arrived.” He stopped, and the Prophet ﷺ called for the child, embraced him, and said whatever Allah willed him to say. Anas continued: I saw Ibrāhīm struggling for breath in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Tears began flowing from the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ eyes, and he said: “The eyes shed tears, the heart grieves, and we say only what pleases our Lord. By Allah, Ibrāhīm, we are deeply saddened by your departure.”"],[1168,"Anas ibn Mālik said: I never saw anyone more compassionate toward children and dependents than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. His son Ibrāhīm was being nursed in the upper districts of Madinah. The Prophet ﷺ would go there, and we would accompany him. He would enter the smoke-filled house—for the child’s foster father was a blacksmith—take Ibrāhīm into his arms, kiss him, and then return."],[1169,"§4. Zaynab\nShe was the eldest daughter of his ﷺ, born some time before the prophethood, and she was the first of his ﷺ daughters to marry. Before Islam she was married to her maternal aunt’s son, Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ al-ʿAbshamī, who was the son of Sayyidah Hālah bint Khuwaylid. When Islam came, Sayyidah Zaynab, may Allah be pleased with her, accepted Islam, but her husband Abū al-ʿĀṣ refused to accept Islam. When the time of the Hijrah came, her sisters migrated to Madinah, but she remained in Makkah. Her husband Abū al-ʿĀṣ was taken prisoner in the Battle of Badr, so Sayyidah Zaynab, may Allah be pleased with her, sent some property as ransom for his release, among which was a necklace that belonged to Sayyidah Khadījah, may Allah be pleased with her, who had given it to Sayyidah Zaynab at the time of her marriage. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw it, he was overcome with intense tenderness, and he asked the noble Companions to approve his release, which they accepted. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took a promise from Abū al-ʿĀṣ that he would let her go free to join Sayyidah Zaynab. When he reached Makkah, he told Sayyidah Zaynab to go to her father in Madinah. When she set out for Madinah, at the place called Dhū Ṭuwā, Habbār ibn al-Aswad attacked her camel, so she fell, and because of this attack she miscarried and began to bleed. Even so, she continued her journey and joined her father in Madinah.\nAbū al-ʿĀṣ accepted Islam in Muḥarram 7 AH, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reunited Sayyidah Zaynab, may Allah be pleased with her, with him on the basis of the first marriage. Sayyidah Zaynab passed away at the beginning of 8 AH, and her father (the Messenger of Allah ﷺ) led her funeral prayer and buried her in al-Baqīʿ. Sayyidah Zaynab had two children by Abū al-ʿĀṣ: ʿAlī and Umāmah. ʿAlī died close to puberty, while Umāmah married Sayyidunā ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, after the death of her maternal aunt Fāṭimah, but she had no children. Therefore no line of descent continued from Sayyidah Zaynab, may Allah be pleased with her. See al-Iṣābah (11354).\n§5. Ruqayyah"],[1170,"§5. Ruqayyah\nRuqayyah was born before the prophethood and was married to ʿUtbah ibn Abī Lahab. But when the word of Allah the Exalted was revealed:\n(May the hands of Abū Lahab be ruined, and ruined is he. Al-Masad: 1)\nʿUtbah divorced her before the wedding was consummated. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married her in Makkah to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him. ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, migrated with her to Abyssinia, then returned to Makkah and migrated to Madinah. When he ﷺ was about to set out for Badr, she fell ill, and thus by the command of the Prophet ﷺ Sayyidunā ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, did not go to the Battle of Badr. While he ﷺ was still at Badr, she died and was buried in al-Baqīʿ. When the messenger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived at Badr with the good news of victory, the earth over her grave was just being leveled. From Sayyidah Ruqayyah, may Allah be pleased with her, ʿAbd Allāh was born to Sayyidunā ʿUthmān, and it was after him that Sayyidunā ʿUthmān’s kunyah was Abū ʿAbd Allāh. A rooster pecked ʿAbd Allāh in the eye, and because of this he died in childhood. After that Sayyidah Ruqayyah, may Allah be pleased with her, had no children, so no line of descent continued from Sayyidah Ruqayyah, may Allah be pleased with her.\n§6. Umm Kulthūm"],[1171,"§6. Umm Kulthūm\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ named her Umm Kulthūm; she had no other name besides this, and this was also her kunyah. She too was married to ʿUtaybah ibn Abī Lahab, just as her sister Sayyidah Ruqayyah was married to ʿUtbah ibn Abī Lahab. Then, when the verse was revealed,\n(May the hands of Abū Lahab be ruined, and ruined is he. Al-Masad: 1)\nʿUtaybah divorced her before the wedding was consummated, exactly as ʿUtbah had divorced Sayyidah Ruqayyah, may Allah be pleased with her.\nWhen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ migrated to Madinah, Sayyidah Umm Kulthūm, may Allah be pleased with her, migrated as well at that time. Then when Sayyidah Ruqayyah, may Allah be pleased with her, passed away in 2 AH, Sayyidunā ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, was married in 3 AH to her (Sayyidah Ruqayyah’s) sister Sayyidah Umm Kulthūm, may Allah be pleased with her. For this reason Sayyidunā ʿUthmān is called Dhū al-Nūrayn (Possessor of the Two Lights). Sayyidah Ruqayyah died in 9 AH; she had no children."],[1172,"Anas ibn Mālik said: We attended the burial of one of the daughters of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting beside the grave, and I saw tears flowing from his eyes. He asked, “Is there any man among you who did not have marital relations last night?” Abū Ṭalḥah replied, “I did not.” The Prophet ﷺ said, “Then descend into the grave.” Abū Ṭalḥah accordingly descended into her grave."],[1173,"§7. Fāṭimah al-Zahrāʾ\nShe was the youngest and the most beloved daughter of his ﷺ. She was born some time before the prophethood. After the Hijrah she came into the marriage of Sayyidunā ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, and she was married after the Battle of Badr. To her were born Sayyidunā al-Ḥasan, Sayyidunā al-Ḥusayn, Umm Kulthūm and Sayyidah Zaynab. She died in Madinah in 11 AH and was buried in al-Baqīʿ."],[1174,"Buraydah reported: Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, each proposed marriage to Fāṭimah, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “She is young.” Then ʿAlī proposed to her, and the Prophet ﷺ married her to him."],[1175,"ʿAlī said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ furnished Fāṭimah for her marriage with a velvet covering, a waterskin, and a pillow stuffed with fibers of idhkhir grass."],[1176,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, sent a request to Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq asking for her inheritance from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She then related a lengthy account in which it is stated: Fāṭimah lived for six months after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When she died, her husband ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib buried her at night without notifying Abū Bakr of her death, and ʿAlī performed the funeral prayer over her."],[1177,"The scholars described the shamāʾil (the noble characteristics and features) of the noble Prophet ﷺ in writing, each according to his ability, and they composed many books on this subject. The most famous is the Shamāʾil of al-Tirmidhī. The authentic narrations from this book I have recorded in al-Jāmiʿ al-Kāmil."],[1178,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was neither excessively tall nor short. He was neither extremely pale nor dark-complexioned. His hair was neither tightly curled nor completely straight. Allah Most High commissioned him as a prophet at the age of forty. He remained in Makkah for ten years and in Madinah for ten years, and Allah took his soul when he was sixty years old. There were not even twenty white hairs on his head and in his beard."],[1179,"Anas described the Prophet ﷺ, saying: He was of medium height among the people, neither tall nor short. His complexion was radiant—neither extremely pale nor dark. His hair was neither tightly curled nor completely straight, but moderately wavy. Revelation came to him when he was forty years old. He remained in Makkah receiving revelation for ten years and in Madinah for ten years. When he died, there were not even twenty white hairs on his head and in his beard. Rabīʿah said: I saw some of his hair, and it appeared red. I asked about it and was told that the perfume had made it red."],[1180,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had the most beautiful face and the finest physical form of all people. He was neither excessively tall nor short."],[1181,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib said: The Prophet ﷺ was neither tall nor short. His hands and feet were strong and substantial, his head was large, his joints were prominent, and the line of hair extending from his chest to his navel was long. When he walked, he leaned forward with strength, as though descending a slope. I never saw anyone like him, either before or after him ﷺ."],[1182,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a large head, large eyes, and long eyelashes. There was a reddish tinge in the whites of his eyes. His beard was thick, and his complexion was radiant. When he walked, he leaned forward as though ascending an incline. When he turned, he turned his entire body. His hands and feet were strong and substantial."],[1183,"Wahb Abū Juḥayfah al-Suwāʾī said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ and noticed some whiteness beneath his lower lip, in the ʿanfaqah."],[1184,"Abū Juḥayfah said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ, and al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī resembled him. Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid asked Abū Juḥayfah, “Describe him to me.” He replied, “He was fair-complexioned, and some whiteness had appeared in his hair. The Prophet ﷺ ordered that thirteen young she-camels be given to us, but he died before we received them.”"],[1185,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Busr, a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, was asked, “Did you see the Prophet ﷺ when he had become elderly?” He replied, “There were a few white hairs in the area beneath his lower lip.”"],[1186,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib said: The Prophet ﷺ was of medium height and broad between the shoulders. His hair reached the lobes of his ears. I saw him wearing a red outfit, and I never saw anything more beautiful than him. Another wording states: I never saw anyone with hair reaching the shoulders look more beautiful in a red outfit than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. His hair touched his shoulders, he was broad between them, and he was neither tall nor short."],[1187,"Anas ibn Mālik was asked, “What was the hair of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ like?” He replied, “His hair was moderately wavy, neither curly nor completely straight, and its length was between his ears and shoulders.”"],[1188,"Anas reported that the hair of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ touched his shoulders."],[1189,"Anas said: The hair of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached the middle of his ears."],[1190,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The hair of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was longer than a wafrah but shorter than a jummah."],[1191,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ initially let his hair hang down without parting it. The polytheists used to part their hair, while the People of the Scripture let theirs hang down. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ liked to agree with the People of the Scripture in matters concerning which he had received no specific command. Later, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ parted his hair."],[1192,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Prophet ﷺ had large, substantial hands, and I never saw anyone like him afterward. His hair was moderately wavy, neither curly nor completely straight."],[1193,"Anas said: The Prophet ﷺ had large, substantial hands and feet and a beautiful face. I never saw anyone like him, either before or after him. His palms were broad."],[1194,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib was asked, “Was the Prophet’s ﷺ face like a sword?” He replied, “No, rather it was like the moon.”"],[1195,"Jābir ibn Samurah said: Some white hairs had appeared at the front of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ head and in his beard. When he applied oil, they were not noticeable, but when his hair was disheveled, they became visible. His beard was thick. A man asked, “Was his face like a sword?” He replied, “No, rather it was like the sun and the moon, and it was somewhat rounded. I saw the Seal of Prophethood near his shoulder; it was like a pigeon’s egg and resembled the color of his body.”"],[1196,"Kaʿb ibn Mālik said, during the lengthy account of his remaining behind from the expedition of Tabūk: When I greeted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, his face was shining with happiness. Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was pleased, his face would become radiant as though it were a piece of the moon, and we could recognize his happiness from that."],[1197,"Abū al-Ṭufayl said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and no man who saw him remains upon the face of the earth besides me. He was asked, “What did he look like?” He replied, “He was fair-complexioned, handsome, and well-proportioned.”"],[1198,"Anas said: I never touched silk or brocade softer than the palm of the Prophet ﷺ, and I never smelled any fragrance more pleasant than the fragrance of the Prophet ﷺ. Another wording states: I never smelled ambergris, musk, or anything else more fragrant than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I never touched brocade or silk softer to the touch than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[1199,"Anas said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a radiant complexion, and his perspiration appeared like pearls. When he walked, he leaned forward with strength. I never touched brocade or silk softer than the palm of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I never smelled musk or ambergris more pleasant than the fragrance of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Another wording states: I never touched silk fabric or pure silk softer than the palm of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I never smelled musk or any perfume more fragrant than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[1200,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: The Prophet ﷺ used to enter the house of Umm Sulaym and sleep upon her bed while she was away. One day he came and slept upon her bed. Someone went to her and said, “The Prophet ﷺ is sleeping in your house upon your bed.” She came and found that he had perspired and that his perspiration had collected upon a piece of leather on the bed. She opened her small container and began absorbing the perspiration and squeezing it into her bottles. The Prophet ﷺ awoke with a start and asked, “What are you doing, Umm Sulaym?” She replied, “O Messenger of Allah, we hope for its blessing for our children.” He said, “You have done well.” Another narration states: Umm Sulaym used to spread a leather mat for the Prophet ﷺ, upon which he would take his midday rest in her home. When the Prophet ﷺ slept, she would collect some of his perspiration and hair in a bottle and mix it into sukk perfume while he slept. When Anas ibn Mālik was near death, he instructed that some of that sukk be placed in the perfume used to prepare his body for burial, and it was placed in it.\nAnother narration states: Anas said: The Prophet ﷺ entered our home and took his midday rest with us. He perspired, and my mother brought a bottle and began collecting the perspiration in it. The Prophet ﷺ awoke and asked, “Umm Sulaym, what are you doing?” She replied, “This is your perspiration. We mix it into our perfume, and it is among the finest of fragrances.”"],[1201,"Umm Sulaym reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to visit her and take his midday rest in her home. She would spread a leather mat for him, and he would sleep upon it. He perspired profusely, so she would collect his perspiration and place it in her perfume and bottles. The Prophet ﷺ asked, “Umm Sulaym, what is this?” She replied, “It is your perspiration. I mix it with my perfume.”"],[1202,"Jābir ibn Samurah said: I performed the noon prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He then left to go to his family, and I accompanied him. Some children met him, and he began stroking their cheeks one by one. He also stroked my cheek, and I felt a coolness and fragrance from his hand, as though he had just taken it from a perfumer’s box."],[1203,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Mālik ibn Buḥaynah reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, he kept his arms away from his sides until the whiteness of his underarms became visible. Another wording states: When he prostrated, he kept his arms away from his sides."],[1204,"Anas reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not ordinarily raise his hands so high during supplication, except when praying for rain. On that occasion, he would raise them until the whiteness of his underarms could be seen."],[1205,"Abū Juḥayfah reported: I came to the Prophet ﷺ in Makkah while he was at al-Abṭaḥ in a red leather tent. Bilāl came out carrying the water the Prophet ﷺ had used for ablution; some people obtained some of it, while others received drops sprinkled upon them. Then the Prophet ﷺ came out wearing a red garment, and it is as though I can still see the whiteness of his legs… the remainder of the hadith."],[1206,"Al-Barāʾ said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ reciting during the night prayer, “By the fig and the olive” [Al-Tīn: 1], and I never heard anyone with a more beautiful voice or recitation than his."],[1207,"Jābir ibn Samurah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a broad mouth, long openings to his eyes, and lean heels. The narrator said: I asked Simāk, “What does ‘broad-mouthed’ mean?” He replied, “His mouth was large.” I asked, “What does the description of his eyes mean?” He replied, “The openings of his eyes were long.” I asked, “What does ‘lean-heeled’ mean?” He replied, “There was little flesh upon his heels.”"],[1208,"Anas was asked, “Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dye his hair?” He replied, “Only a small amount of white hair was visible on him.”"],[1209,"Anas said: It is disliked for a man to pluck a white hair from his head or beard. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not dye his hair. The whiteness was confined to the area beneath his lower lip, his temples, and a few scattered hairs on his head."],[1210,"Anas was asked about the Prophet ﷺ dyeing his hair. He replied: His white hairs never became numerous enough to require dyeing. If I wished, I could count the white hairs in his beard."],[1211,"Anas was asked about the Prophet’s ﷺ white hairs and replied: Allah did not mar his beauty with extensive whiteness."],[1212,"Anas reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was not excessively tall… the remainder of the hadith. It also states: Allah took his soul when there were not even twenty white hairs on his head and in his beard."],[1213,"Jābir ibn Samurah was asked about the Prophet’s ﷺ white hairs. He replied: When he applied oil to his hair, no whiteness could be seen, but when he did not apply oil, some of it was visible."],[1214,"Umm Salamah brought out for us some of the Prophet’s ﷺ hair that had been dyed."],[1215,"Anas said: I saw some of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ hair dyed. Ḥammād said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl also informed us: I saw some of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ dyed hair in the possession of Anas ibn Mālik."],[1216,"Abū Rimthah said: I went with my father to see the Prophet ﷺ. His hair reached his earlobes and bore traces of henna, and he was wearing two green garments."],[1217,"Al-Sā’ib ibn Yazīd said: My maternal aunt took me to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of Allah, my sister’s son is ill.” He stroked my head and prayed that I be blessed. He then performed ablution, and I drank from the water he had used. I stood behind him and looked at the Seal of Prophethood between his shoulders; it resembled the button of a bridal canopy."],[1218,"Jābir ibn Samurah said: I saw the Seal upon the back of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and it resembled a pigeon’s egg."],[1219,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Sarjis said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ and ate bread and meat with him—or he said: tharīd. The narrator asked him, “Did the Prophet ﷺ seek forgiveness for you?” He replied, “Yes, and for you as well.” He then recited the verse: “Seek forgiveness for your sin and for the believing men and believing women.” [Muḥammad: 19] He continued: I then moved around behind him and looked at the Seal of Prophethood between his shoulders, near the upper part of his left shoulder. It formed a gathered cluster, with moles resembling small warts upon it."],[1220,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ possessed the finest character of all people."],[1221,"Anas reported: I served the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for ten years. By Allah, he never once said “Uff!” to me. Nor did he ever say concerning something I had done, “Why did you do that?” or concerning something I had not done, “Why did you not do that?”\nAnother narration states: I served the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for nine years, and to the best of my knowledge, he never once said to me, “Why did you do such and such?” Nor did he ever criticize anything I did."],[1222,"Anas reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Madinah, Abū Ṭalḥah took me by the hand and brought me to him. He said, “O Messenger of Allah, Anas is an intelligent boy, so let him serve you.” I therefore served him both while traveling and while at home. By Allah, he never said concerning something I had done, “Why did you do it in this manner?” Nor did he ever say concerning something I had not done, “Why did you not do it in this manner?”"],[1223,"Anas reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was among the finest of people in character. One day he sent me to attend to an errand. I said, “By Allah, I will not go,” although inwardly I intended to carry out what the Prophet of Allah ﷺ had commanded me. I went out and passed some children playing in the marketplace. Suddenly, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came up behind me and took hold of the back of my neck. I looked at him and saw that he was laughing. He said, “Little Anas, did you go where I instructed you?” I replied, “Yes, Messenger of Allah, I am going now.”"],[1224,"Saʿd ibn Hishām said: I asked, “Mother of the Believers, tell me about the character of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” She replied, “Do you not read the Qur’an?” I said, “Yes.” She said, “The character of the Prophet of Allah ﷺ was the Qur’an”—the remainder of the hadith."],[1225,"Yazīd ibn Bābnūs said: We asked ʿĀ’ishah, “Mother of the Believers, what was the character of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ like?” She replied, “The character of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the Qur’an.” She then recited from “Successful indeed are the believers” until she reached, “And those who carefully maintain their prayers.” [Al-Muʾminūn: 1–9] She then said, “Such was the character of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[1226,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ was asked, “Tell me how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is described in the Torah.” He replied: Yes, by Allah, he is described in the Torah with some of the same qualities by which he is described in the Qur’an: “O Prophet, We have sent you as a witness, a bearer of glad tidings, and a warner” [Al-Aḥzāb: 45], and as a refuge for the unlettered people. “You are My servant and Messenger. I have named you al-Mutawakkil—the one who places his trust in Allah. He is neither harsh nor hard-hearted, nor is he loud and clamorous in the marketplaces. He does not repay evil with evil; rather, he pardons and forgives. Allah will not take his soul until He straightens through him the crooked religion by causing people to say, ‘There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah,’ thereby opening blind eyes, deaf ears, and closed hearts.”"],[1227,"ʿĀ’ishah said: It is written in the Gospel concerning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “He is neither harsh nor hard-hearted, nor clamorous in the marketplaces. He does not repay an evil deed with one like it; rather, he pardons and overlooks.”"],[1228,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr said: The Prophet ﷺ was neither obscene in his speech nor deliberately indecent. He used to say, “Among the best of you are those who possess the finest character.”"],[1229,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Prophet ﷺ was neither abusive, obscene, nor given to cursing. When reproaching one of us, he would merely say, “What is the matter with him? May dust touch his forehead!”"],[1230,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was neither obscene nor deliberately indecent, nor was he loud and quarrelsome in the marketplaces. He did not repay an evil deed with another evil deed; rather, he pardoned and overlooked."],[1231,"Abū Hurayrah described the Prophet ﷺ, saying: His forearms were long—or broad—and his eyelashes were long. He was broad between the shoulders. When he turned toward someone or away from them, he turned his entire body. May my father and mother be sacrificed for him! He was neither obscene nor deliberately indecent, nor was he loud and quarrelsome in the marketplaces."],[1232,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Do not exaggerate in praising me as the Christians exaggerated in praising the son of Mary. I am only Allah’s servant, so say: ‘The servant of Allah and His Messenger.’”"],[1233,"Anas ibn Mālik said: A bondwoman from among the bondwomen of Madinah could take the Messenger of Allah ﷺ by the hand and lead him wherever she wished."],[1234,"Abū Hurayrah reported: Jibrīl was sitting with the Prophet ﷺ when he looked toward the sky and saw an angel descending. Jibrīl said, “This angel has never descended since the day he was created until this moment.” When the angel descended, he said, “Muḥammad, your Lord has sent me to ask you: Should He make you a prophet-king or a servant-messenger?” Jibrīl said, “Humble yourself before your Lord, Muḥammad.” The Prophet ﷺ replied, “Rather, a servant-messenger.”"],[1235,"ʿĀ’ishah narrated a similar report, with the additional wording: After that, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not eat while reclining. He would say, “I eat as a servant eats, and I sit as a servant sits.”"],[1236,"Abū Hurayrah reported: A Muslim man and a Jewish man exchanged insults. During an oath, the Muslim said, “By Him who chose Muḥammad above all worlds.” The Jewish man replied, “By Him who chose Moses above all worlds.” The Muslim raised his hand and struck the Jewish man across the face. The Jewish man went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him of what had occurred between him and the Muslim. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not declare me superior to Moses in this manner. The people will fall unconscious on the Day of Resurrection, and I will be the first to regain consciousness. I will then find Moses grasping the side of the Throne. I will not know whether he was among those who fell unconscious and regained consciousness before me, or whether he was among those whom Allah exempted.”"],[1237,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: The Prophet ﷺ was the most generous of all people, and he was at his most generous during Ramadan when Jibrīl met him. Jibrīl, peace be upon him would meet him every night of Ramadan and review the Qur’an with him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was more generous in dispensing goodness than a freely blowing wind."],[1238,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me, “When the wealth of Bahrain arrives, I shall give you this much, this much, and this much,” gesturing three times with his hands. The wealth of Bahrain did not arrive until after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had died. When it reached Abū Bakr, he ordered a herald to announce, “Whoever is owed a debt or promise by the Prophet ﷺ should come to me.” Jābir said: I went to Abū Bakr and informed him that the Prophet ﷺ had said, “When the wealth of Bahrain arrives, I shall give you this much, this much, and this much,” three times. Abū Bakr then gave me a portion. Jābir continued: I later met Abū Bakr and asked him, but he did not give me anything. I went to him again, but he did not give me anything. I then went to him a third time, but he still did not give me anything. I said, “I came to you and you did not give me; then I came again and you did not give me; and now I have come once more and you have not given me. Either give me, or you are withholding from me out of stinginess.” He replied, “Did you say that I am withholding from you out of stinginess? What disease could be worse than stinginess?” He repeated this three times. He then said, “Whenever I withheld from you, I intended all along to give you.” In another chain, ʿAmr narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī, who said: I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh say: I went to Abū Bakr, and he said, “Count it.” I counted the amount and found it to be five hundred. He said, “Take twice that amount in addition.”"],[1239,"Anas reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was never asked for anything for the sake of Islam except that he gave it. A man came to him, and he gave him a flock of sheep filling the space between two mountains. The man returned to his people and said: “My people, embrace Islam, for Muḥammad gives with the generosity of one who does not fear poverty.”\nIn another narration: A man asked the Prophet ﷺ for a flock of sheep filling the space between two mountains, and he gave it to him… the remainder of the hadith. Anas said therein: “A man might initially embrace Islam desiring nothing but worldly gain, yet he would not remain long before Islam became more beloved to him than the world and everything in it.”"],[1240,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: When the Kaʿbah was being rebuilt, the Prophet ﷺ and al-ʿAbbās carried stones. Al-ʿAbbās said to the Prophet ﷺ, “Place your waist-wrapper over your neck so that it will protect you from the stones.” The Prophet ﷺ immediately fell to the ground, and his eyes turned upward toward the sky. When he recovered, he said, “My waist-wrapper! My waist-wrapper!” He then fastened it securely around himself."],[1241,"Abū al-Ṭufayl reported: When the Sacred House was being rebuilt, the people carried stones, and the Prophet ﷺ carried stones with them. He took his garment and placed it over his shoulder, whereupon a voice called out to him, “Do not uncover your private parts.” He therefore put down the stone and put his garment back on."],[1242,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said: Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was given a choice between two matters, he chose the easier of the two, provided that it was not sinful. If it involved sin, he was the person farthest removed from it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never took revenge for a personal wrong unless one of Allah’s sacred limits had been violated."],[1243,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was more modest than a virgin secluded in her private chamber. Whenever he disliked something, we could recognize it from his face."],[1244,"Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never criticized any food. If he desired it, he ate it; and if he disliked it, he left it."],[1245,"Anas said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the most handsome of people, the most generous of people, and the most courageous of people. One night, the people of Madinah were alarmed by a sound. Some people set out in its direction, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met them as he was returning, having already preceded them to investigate it. He was riding Abū Ṭalḥah’s unsaddled horse with a sword hanging around his neck, and he was saying, “Do not be frightened! Do not be frightened!” He then said regarding the horse, “We found it as swift and smooth as the sea,” or, “It is indeed like the sea.”"],[1246,"Anas reported: There was an alarm in Madinah, so the Prophet ﷺ borrowed a horse belonging to Abū Ṭalḥah called al-Mandūb and rode it. When he returned, he said, “We found nothing to fear, and we found the horse to be as swift and smooth as the sea.”"],[1247,"Al-Barāʾ said: By Allah, whenever the battle became fierce, we would seek protection behind him. The courageous among us was the one who could remain alongside him—meaning the Prophet ﷺ."],[1248,"ʿAlī said: I saw us on the day of Badr seeking shelter behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He was the nearest of us to the enemy and was among the fiercest of people in battle that day."],[1249,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was never asked for anything and replied, “No.”"],[1250,"Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim reported: While he was accompanying the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the people on their return from Ḥunayn, some Bedouins crowded around the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking him for things. They pressed him against a thorny tree, which caught and pulled away his cloak. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stopped and said, “Give me back my cloak. If I possessed livestock equal in number to these thorny trees, I would distribute it all among you. You would then find me neither stingy, untruthful, nor cowardly.”"],[1251,"Anas ibn Mālik passed by some children and greeted them with salām. He then said: The Prophet ﷺ used to do this."],[1252,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “A son was born to me tonight, and I named him after my father Ibrāhīm.” He then entrusted him to Umm Sayf, the wife of a blacksmith known as Abū Sayf. The Prophet ﷺ went to visit the child, and I followed him. We reached Abū Sayf while he was blowing his bellows, and the house was filled with smoke. I hurried ahead of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “Abū Sayf, stop; the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has arrived.” He stopped, and the Prophet ﷺ called for the child, embraced him, and said whatever Allah willed him to say. Anas continued: I saw Ibrāhīm struggling for breath in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Tears began flowing from the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ eyes, and he said: “The eyes shed tears, the heart grieves, and we say only what pleases our Lord. By Allah, Ibrāhīm, we are deeply saddened by your departure.”"],[1253,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: Some Bedouins came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked, “Do you kiss your children?” They replied, “Yes.” The Bedouins said, “But by Allah, we do not kiss ours.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “What power do I have to help you if Allah has removed compassion from your hearts?” Al-Bukhārī’s narration reads: “What power do I have?”"],[1254,"Anas ibn Mālik said: I never saw anyone more compassionate toward children and dependents than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. His son Ibrāhīm was being nursed in the upper districts of Madinah. The Prophet ﷺ would go there, and we would accompany him. He would enter the smoke-filled house—for the child’s foster father was a blacksmith—take Ibrāhīm into his arms, kiss him, and then return. ʿAmr said: When Ibrāhīm died, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Ibrāhīm is my son. He died while still of nursing age, and he has two wet nurses who will complete his nursing in Paradise.”"],[1255,"Abū Hurayrah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ kissed al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī while al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis al-Tamīmī was sitting with him. Al-Aqraʿ said, “I have ten children, yet I have never kissed any of them.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at him and said, “Whoever does not show mercy will not be shown mercy.”"],[1256,"Jarīr ibn ʿAbdullāh reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Allah does not show mercy to one who does not show mercy to people.”"],[1257,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remembered Allah frequently, engaged very little in useless speech, lengthened the prayer, and kept the sermon brief. He did not consider it beneath him to walk with a widow or a poor person and fulfill their needs."],[1258,"Anas reported: The Prophet ﷺ had a camel driver named Anjashah who possessed a beautiful voice. The Prophet ﷺ said to him, “Gently, Anjashah! Do not break the glass vessels.” Qatādah said: He meant the physically delicate women."],[1259,"Anas reported: After the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed the dawn prayer, the servants of Madinah would come to him carrying vessels containing water. No vessel was brought to him without his dipping his hand into it. They would sometimes come to him on a bitterly cold morning, yet he would still place his hand in the water."],[1260,"Anas reported: A woman who had some mental impairment said, “Messenger of Allah, I have a need that I wish you to fulfill.” He replied, “Mother of so-and-so, choose whichever street you wish, and I will attend to your need.” He stood apart with her along one of the roads until she had finished explaining her need."],[1261,"Anas said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while the barber was shaving his head. His Companions surrounded him, not wanting a single hair to fall except into the hand of one of them."],[1262,"Anas reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was ready for the barber to shave his head, Abū Ṭalḥah took the hair from one side of his head in his hand. He then brought the hair to Umm Sulaym, who used to mix it into her perfume."],[1263,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never struck anything with his hand—neither a woman nor a servant—except when fighting in the cause of Allah. Whenever he was personally wronged, he never took revenge upon the offender, unless one of Allah’s sacred prohibitions was violated; then he acted for the sake of Allah Almighty."],[1264,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported that he joined the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on an expedition toward Najd. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned, Jābir returned with him. The midday heat overtook them in a valley filled with thorny trees. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dismounted, and the people dispersed among the trees to seek shade. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ rested beneath an acacia tree and hung his sword upon it. Jābir said: We slept for a while, and suddenly the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called us. We went to him and found a Bedouin sitting beside him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “This man drew my sword while I was asleep. I awoke to find him holding it unsheathed. He asked me, ‘Who will protect you from me?’ I replied, ‘Allah.’ Here he is now, sitting before you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not punish him."],[1265,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did something, thereby demonstrating its permissibility. News of this reached some of his Companions, but they seemed to dislike it and considered themselves too pious to do it. When this reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he stood and addressed the people, saying: “What is the matter with men who were informed that I did something permissible, yet they disliked it and considered themselves too pious to do it? By Allah, I possess greater knowledge of Allah than they do and fear Him more intensely.”"],[1266,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: The Prophet ﷺ had the finest character of all people. I had a younger brother called Abū ʿUmayr. Anas said: I believe he said that the boy had recently been weaned. Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and saw him, he would say: “O Abū ʿUmayr, what happened to the little nughayr?” The boy used to play with that bird."],[1267,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to provide him with a riding animal. The Prophet ﷺ said, “I shall mount you upon the offspring of a she-camel.” The man asked, “Messenger of Allah, what could I do with the offspring of a she-camel?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied, “Do camels give birth to anything other than the offspring of she-camels?”"],[1268,"Abū Hurayrah reported: The Companions said, “Messenger of Allah, you joke with us.” He replied, “I say nothing except the truth.”"],[1269,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I certainly joke, but I say nothing except the truth.”"],[1270,"Anas reported: A Bedouin man named Zāhir used to bring the Prophet ﷺ gifts from the desert. Whenever Zāhir was preparing to leave Madinah, the Prophet ﷺ would equip him with provisions. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Zāhir is our man of the desert, and we are his people of the city.” The Prophet ﷺ loved him, though Zāhir was not physically handsome. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to him one day while he was selling his merchandise and embraced him from behind, so Zāhir could not see who it was. Zāhir said, “Let me go! Who is this?” He turned and recognized the Prophet ﷺ. Once he recognized him, he did everything he could to keep his back pressed against the Prophet’s ﷺ chest. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began saying, “Who will buy this slave?” Zāhir said, “Messenger of Allah, by Allah, you would then find me to be unsellable merchandise.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied, “But you are not worthless in the sight of Allah,” or he said, “Rather, you are precious in the sight of Allah.”"],[1271,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Prophet ﷺ said to me, “O possessor of two ears!” One of the narrators explained: He said this jokingly."],[1272,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laugh until his back teeth became visible. The complete hadith contains the lengthy account of the last person to emerge from the Fire, and it will appear in its appropriate place."],[1273,"Jarīr ibn ʿAbdullāh said: From the time I embraced Islam, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ never refused me permission to enter upon him, and whenever he saw me, he smiled. Another wording states: Whenever he saw me, he smiled in my face."],[1274,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported: I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laugh so fully and heartily that I could see his uvula; rather, he would only smile… the remainder of the hadith."],[1275,"Abū Dharr said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laugh until his back teeth became visible. The complete hadith contains the lengthy account of the last person to emerge from the Fire, and it will appear in its appropriate place."],[1276,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ reported that the Prophet ﷺ mentioned both of his parents together for him on the day of Uḥud, saying, “Shoot! May my father and mother be sacrificed for you.” Saʿd said: One of the polytheists had inflicted many casualties upon the Muslims. The Prophet ﷺ said to me, “Shoot! May my father and mother be sacrificed for you.” I drew an arrow that had no arrowhead and struck the man in his side. He fell, and his private parts became exposed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed until I could see his back teeth."],[1277,"Jābir ibn Samurah was asked, “Did you use to sit with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” He replied, “Yes, frequently. He would remain in the place where he had performed the dawn prayer until the sun rose. Once it had risen, he would stand. The Companions would converse and recount matters from the pre-Islamic period. They would laugh, and he ﷺ would smile.”"],[1278,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith said: The laughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was generally no more than a smile."],[1279,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Jazʾ said: I never saw anyone smile more frequently than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[1280,"Abū Hurayrah said: I never saw anything more beautiful than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. It was as though the sun flowed across his face. Nor did I see anyone who walked more swiftly than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. It was as though the earth were folded up for him. We would exert ourselves to keep pace, while he walked without apparent effort."],[1281,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to set out on a journey, he would draw lots among his wives. Whichever wife’s lot was drawn, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would take her with him… the remainder of the hadith."],[1282,"ʿĀ’ishah reported: Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out on a journey, he would draw lots among his wives. On one occasion, the lot fell to ʿĀ’ishah and Ḥafṣah, so they both went out with him… the remainder of the hadith."],[1283,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that whenever something concerned the Prophet ﷺ, he would take hold of his beard in this manner. Ibn Mushir then demonstrated by grasping his own beard."],[1284,"Anas ibn Mālik said: I was walking with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was wearing a Najrānī cloak with a thick, coarse border. A Bedouin caught up with him and violently pulled him by the cloak. I looked at the side of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ neck and saw that the cloak’s border had left a mark upon it because of the force with which the man had pulled him. The Bedouin then said, “Muḥammad, order that I be given some of Allah’s wealth that is in your possession.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned toward him and laughed, then ordered that he be given a gift."],[1285,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ died after we had eaten our fill of the two black things: dates and water."],[1286,"Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr said: Do you not now possess whatever food and drink you desire? I saw your Prophet ﷺ when he could not even find enough inferior dates to fill his stomach."],[1287,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spend an entire day writhing from hunger, unable to find even enough inferior dates to fill his stomach."],[1288,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿĀbis reported from his father, who said: I asked ʿĀ’ishah, “Did the Prophet ﷺ prohibit eating the meat of sacrificial animals after three days?” She replied, “He did so only during a year in which the people suffered hunger. He wanted the wealthy to feed the poor. We would sometimes preserve a sheep’s leg and eat it after fifteen days.” She was asked, “What compelled you to preserve it for so long?” She laughed and said, “The family of Muḥammad ﷺ never ate their fill of wheat bread accompanied by another food for three consecutive days before he returned to Allah.”"],[1289,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would spend several consecutive nights hungry, while his family could find nothing for their evening meal. Most of the bread they ate was barley bread."],[1290,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: A tailor invited the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to a meal he had prepared. I accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to that meal. The tailor served him barley bread and a stew containing gourd. Anas said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ searching for the pieces of gourd around the dish, and I have continued to love gourd ever since that day. Another narration adds that Anas said: Whenever a meal was prepared for me after that, and it was possible to include gourd in it, gourd was added."],[1291,"Anas said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ being served a stew containing gourd and dried meat. I saw him searching for the pieces of gourd and eating them."],[1292,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Prophet ﷺ loved gourd. A meal was brought to him—or he was invited to one—and I began searching for the pieces of gourd and placing them before him because I knew that he loved it."],[1293,"Ḥakīm ibn Jābir reported from his father, who said: I entered the Prophet’s ﷺ house and saw some gourd in his possession. I asked, “What is this?” He replied, “This is pumpkin—it is gourd. We use it to increase the quantity of our food.”"],[1294,"Anas reported: Umm Sulaym sent me to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a basket of fresh dates. I did not find him at home, as he had recently gone to visit one of his freedmen who had invited him and prepared a meal for him. I reached him while he was eating, and he invited me to eat with him. The man had prepared tharīd with meat and gourd. I noticed that the Prophet ﷺ liked the gourd, so I began gathering the pieces and moving them nearer to him. After we finished eating, the Prophet ﷺ returned home. I placed the basket of dates before him, and he began eating from them and distributing them until none remained."],[1295,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ loved sweet foods and honey."],[1296,"Abū Hurayrah reported: We were with the Prophet ﷺ at a meal to which he had been invited. A foreleg was presented to him—and he liked the foreleg—so he took a bite from it… the remainder of the lengthy hadith concerning intercession."],[1297,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ most loved the foreleg among the meat-bearing bones, particularly the foreleg of a sheep. Poison had been placed in a foreleg, and he believed that the Jews were the ones who had poisoned it."],[1298,"Ḍubāʿah bint al-Zubayr ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib slaughtered a sheep in her home. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent someone to her with the request, “Give us some of your sheep to eat.” She told the messenger, “By Allah, nothing remains with us except the neck, and I feel embarrassed to send the neck to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” The messenger returned and informed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said, “Return to her and say: ‘Send it, for the neck leads the sheep, is the part nearest to goodness, and is farthest removed from harm.’”"],[1299,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ eating fresh dates with cucumber."],[1300,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to eat melon with fresh dates. Another narration adds that he would say, “We temper the heat of this with the coolness of that, and the coolness of that with the heat of this.”"],[1301,"Anas said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ eating fresh dates together with melon."],[1302,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever eats garlic or onions should remain away from us—or remain away from our mosque—and sit in his house.” A cooking pot containing various green vegetables was brought to him. He noticed its odor and asked about it, and he was informed which vegetables it contained. He said, “Bring it near,” directing that it be given to one of his Companions. When the Companion saw that the Prophet ﷺ disliked eating it, the Prophet ﷺ said to him, “Eat, for I hold intimate conversation with those with whom you do not converse.”"],[1303,"Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī said: Whenever food was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he would eat some of it and send the remainder to me. One day he sent me a dish from which he had eaten nothing because it contained garlic. I asked him, “Is it forbidden?” He replied, “No, but I dislike it because of its odor.” I said, “Then I dislike whatever you dislike.”"],[1304,"Jābir ibn Samurah reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed in the home of Abū Ayyūb. Whenever he ate a meal, he would send its remainder to Abū Ayyūb. One day he sent him a dish from which the Prophet ﷺ had not eaten. When Abū Ayyūb came to the Prophet ﷺ, he mentioned this to him. The Prophet ﷺ explained, “It contained garlic.” Abū Ayyūb asked, “Messenger of Allah, is it forbidden?” He replied, “No, but I dislike it because of its odor.”"],[1305,"Umm Ayyūb reported that the Prophet ﷺ stayed in their home. They specially prepared a meal for him containing some of these strong-smelling vegetables, but he disliked eating it. He said to his Companions, “Eat it, for I am not like any of you. I fear that I may offend my companion.”"],[1306,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported that Khālid ibn al-Walīd, who was known as the Sword of Allah, informed him that he entered the home of Maymūnah with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She was the maternal aunt of both Khālid and Ibn ʿAbbās. They found a roasted desert lizard in her home, which her sister Ḥufaydah bint al-Ḥārith had brought from Najd. The desert lizard was presented to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He rarely extended his hand toward food before being informed what it was and hearing its name. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached toward the desert lizard, whereupon one of the women present said, “Tell the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what you have presented to him. It is a desert lizard, Messenger of Allah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then withdrew his hand. Khālid ibn al-Walīd asked, “Is the desert lizard forbidden, Messenger of Allah?” He replied, “No, but it was not found in the land of my people, so I find myself disinclined to eat it.” Khālid said: I pulled it toward myself and ate it while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ watched me."],[1307,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported that the Prophet ﷺ was asked, “Which drink is most pleasant?” He replied, “That which is sweet and cold.”"],[1308,"Anas reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would drink in three breaths. Another narration adds that he would say, “It is more thirst-quenching, healthier, and more wholesome.”"],[1309,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: I gave the Messenger of Allah ﷺ some Zamzam water, and he drank it while standing."],[1310,"ʿAlī performed the noon prayer and then sat in the courtyard of Kūfah attending to the people’s needs until the time for the afternoon prayer arrived. Water was brought to him, and he drank some of it. He washed his face and hands, and the narrator also mentioned his head and feet. He then stood and drank the remaining water while standing. He said, “Some people dislike drinking while standing, but the Prophet ﷺ did exactly as I have done.”"],[1311,"Kabshah al-Anṣāriyyah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered my home and drank while standing from the mouth of a hanging waterskin. I then went to its mouth and cut it off to preserve it."],[1312,"Umm Sulaym reported that the Prophet ﷺ drank while standing from the mouth of a waterskin."],[1313,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ drink both while standing and while sitting."],[1314,"ʿĀ’ishah said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ drink both while standing and while sitting, pray both while wearing sandals and while barefoot, and turn after completing the prayer sometimes toward his right and sometimes toward his left."],[1315,"Kaʿb ibn Mālik reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to eat with three fingers and lick them after finishing."],[1316,"Anas reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ate, he would lick his three fingers. He said: “If a morsel belonging to one of you falls, let him remove any dirt from it and eat it, and not leave it for Satan.” He also instructed them to wipe the dish clean, saying: “You do not know in which portion of your food the blessing lies.”"],[1317,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “When one of you eats, let him lick his fingers, for he does not know upon which of them the blessing may be found.”"],[1318,"Anas ibn Mālik said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ sitting in a squatting posture while eating dates."],[1319,"Jābir ibn Samurah said: I entered the Prophet’s ﷺ home and saw him reclining upon a cushion. Some narrators add: He was reclining upon his left side."],[1320,"Anas said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ possessed a container or blend of perfume from which he would apply fragrance."],[1321,"ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wore a new garment, he would say: “O Allah, I ask You for its goodness and the goodness of the purpose for which it was made, and I seek refuge in You from its evil and the evil of the purpose for which it was made.”"],[1322,"ʿImrān ibn Abī Anas narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to apply antimony as kohl, applying it three times to the right eye and twice to the left eye."],[1323,"ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prohibited cauterization and disliked drinking extremely hot water. When applying kohl, he applied it an odd number of times, and when cleansing himself with stones, he used an odd number."],[1324,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd ibn ʿĀṣim saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lying on his back in the mosque with one leg resting upon the other."],[1325,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the courtyard of the Kaʿbah, sitting with his knees drawn up and held by his hands in this manner."],[1326,"Abū Hurayrah said: Whenever I saw al-Ḥasan, my eyes overflowed with tears. This was because the Prophet ﷺ once came out and found me in the mosque. He took me by the hand, and I accompanied him. He did not speak to me until we reached the marketplace of Banū Qaynuqāʿ. He walked around it and looked about, then departed while I accompanied him until we returned to the mosque. He sat with his knees drawn up and said, “Where is the little one? Call the little one to me.” Al-Ḥasan came running and fell into the Prophet’s ﷺ lap. Al-Ḥasan placed his hand in the Prophet’s beard, and the Prophet ﷺ opened his mouth and placed al-Ḥasan’s mouth against his own. He then said, “O Allah, I love him, so love him, and love whoever loves him.”"],[1327,"Qaylah bint Makhramah reported that she saw the Prophet ﷺ sitting in a squatting posture. She said: When I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sitting with such humility and submissiveness, I began trembling with awe."],[1328,"Abū Qatādah said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was traveling and stopped to rest during the night, he would lie upon his right side. If he stopped shortly before dawn, he would raise his forearm and place his head upon his palm."],[1329,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ retired to bed, he would say: “All praise belongs to Allah, who has fed us, given us drink, sufficed us, and granted us shelter. How many are those who have no one to suffice them and no one to shelter them!”"],[1330,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that every night when the Prophet ﷺ retired to bed, he would bring his palms together and blow lightly into them. He would recite into them: “Say: He is Allah, the One,” “Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak,” and “Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of humanity.” He would then wipe with his hands as much of his body as he could, beginning with his head, face, and the front of his body. He would do this three times."],[1331,"ʿĀ’ishah was asked, “What did the Prophet ﷺ do in his home?” She replied, “He occupied himself with the work of his family”—meaning that he served them—“and when the time for prayer arrived, he went out to pray.”"],[1332,"ʿUrwah reported: A man asked ʿĀ’ishah, “Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform any work in his home?” She replied, “Yes. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ mended his sandals, sewed his clothes, and worked in his home just as any of you works in his own home.”"],[1333,"ʿĀ’ishah was asked, “What work did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform in his home?” She replied, “He was only a human being like other human beings. He removed vermin from his clothes, milked his sheep, and served himself.”"],[1334,"Anas ibn Mālik was asked, “How was the Prophet’s ﷺ recitation?” He replied, “He lengthened the appropriate sounds.” He then recited: “In the name of Allah, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful,” lengthening the recitation of “In the name of Allah,” “the Most Compassionate,” and “the Most Merciful.”"],[1335,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him, “Recite the Qur’an to me.” Ibn Masʿūd asked, “Should I recite it to you when it was revealed to you?” He replied, “I desire to hear it from someone other than myself.” I recited Sūrat al-Nisāʾ until I reached: “How will it be when We bring a witness from every nation and bring you as a witness against these people?” [Al-Nisāʾ: 41] I raised my head—or a man beside me nudged me, so I raised my head—and saw tears flowing from the Prophet’s ﷺ eyes."],[1336,"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would converse with Abū Bakr at night concerning an affair of the Muslims, and I would be present with them."],[1337,"Al-Agharr al-Muzanī, who was a Companion, narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “A covering sometimes comes over my heart, and I seek Allah’s forgiveness one hundred times each day.” Another wording states: “O people, repent to Allah, for I repent to Him one hundred times each day.”"],[1338,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “If I were invited to a meal consisting only of a foreleg or a lower leg, I would accept the invitation; and if a foreleg or lower leg were presented to me as a gift, I would accept it.”"],[1339,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that when the Prophet ﷺ spoke, his words were so clear and measured that anyone wishing to count them could have done so."],[1340,"Anas reported that when the Prophet ﷺ said something, he repeated it three times so that it would be properly understood. When he came to a people and greeted them, he would offer the greeting three times."],[1341,"ʿĀ’ishah said to ʿUrwah: Are you not surprised by Abū Hurayrah? He came and sat beside my chamber, relating hadiths from the Prophet ﷺ loudly enough for me to hear. I was performing voluntary prayer, and he departed before I completed it. Had I reached him, I would have corrected him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not relate hadiths rapidly in the manner that you do. Another narration adds: Rather, he spoke distinctly, with clear pauses between his words, so that anyone sitting with him could memorize what he said."],[1342,"Anas said: The garment most beloved to the Prophet ﷺ was the ḥibarah."],[1343,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib said: I never saw anyone look more beautiful in a red outfit than the Prophet ﷺ."],[1344,"Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah said: I was traveling with the Prophet ﷺ when he said, “Mughīrah, take the water vessel.” I took it and accompanied him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ walked away until he was concealed from my sight and relieved himself. He then returned wearing a Syrian robe with narrow sleeves. He tried to bring his hands out through the sleeves, but they were too narrow, so he brought his hands out from underneath the robe. I poured water for him, and he performed the same ablution he would perform for prayer. He then wiped over his leather socks and prayed."],[1345,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The Prophet ﷺ went out one morning wearing a broad, patterned cloak made from black hair."],[1346,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that the Prophet ﷺ was ill, so he came out leaning upon Usāmah ibn Zayd. He was wearing a Qaṭarī garment draped over his shoulders, and he led the people in prayer."],[1347,"Qurrah ibn Iyās said: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a group from Muzaynah, and we pledged allegiance to him. The buttons of his shirt were open. After pledging allegiance to him, I placed my hand inside the opening of his shirt and touched the Seal of Prophethood. ʿUrwah said: After that, I never saw Muʿāwiyah or his son with the buttons of their shirts fastened, whether during winter or hot weather. They always left their buttons open."],[1348,"Umm Salamah said: The garment most beloved to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the shirt."],[1349,"Anas said: The sleeves of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ shirt reached his wrists."],[1350,"Asmāʾ bint Yazīd said: The sleeve of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ shirt reached his wrist."],[1351,"Anas reported that each of the Prophet’s ﷺ sandals had two straps. Another wording states: Anas brought out for us a pair of hairless leather sandals, each possessing two straps. Thābit al-Bunānī later narrated to me from Anas that they were the sandals of the Prophet ﷺ."],[1352,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: Each sandal of the Prophet ﷺ had two straps, with its thong doubled."],[1353,"Ibn ʿUmar was asked: “O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, I have seen you do four things that I have not seen any of your companions do.” He said: “What are they, Ibn Jurayj?” He mentioned them, including: “I have seen you wearing sabtī sandals.” Ibn ʿUmar replied: “As for the sabtī sandals, I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wearing sandals made from leather from which the hair had been removed, and he would perform ablution while wearing them. Therefore, I love to wear them”… the remainder of the hadith."],[1354,"Abū Hurayrah said: Each sandal of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had two straps."],[1355,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to write to the Byzantines, he was informed, “They do not read a letter unless it bears a seal.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ therefore had a silver signet ring made. It is as though I can still see its whiteness upon the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ hand. Its inscription was: Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah."],[1356,"Ibn ʿUmar said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a silver signet ring made. It remained in his possession, then afterward with Abū Bakr, then with ʿUmar, and then with ʿUthmān, until it fell into the well of Arīs. Its inscription read: Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah."],[1357,"Ibn ʿUmar reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a ring made from gold—or silver—and positioned its stone toward the palm of his hand. He had the words “Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah” engraved upon it. The people began making similar rings. When he saw that they had done so, he threw his ring away and said, “I shall never wear it again.” He then had a silver ring made, and the people likewise began wearing silver rings. Ibn ʿUmar said: After the Prophet ﷺ, Abū Bakr wore the ring, followed by ʿUmar and then ʿUthmān, until it fell from ʿUthmān into the well of Arīs."],[1358,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that he saw a silver ring upon the hand of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for one day. The people then had silver rings made and wore them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ discarded his ring, so the people discarded theirs as well."],[1359,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The signet ring of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was made of silver, and its stone was Abyssinian. Another narration states: Its stone was made from the same silver as the ring."],[1360,"Anas reported that when Abū Bakr became caliph, he sent him to Bahrain and wrote a document for him, which he sealed with the signet ring. The ring’s inscription consisted of three lines: Muḥammad — on one line Messenger — on one line Allah — on one line"],[1361,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wore a silver ring on his right hand. It contained an Abyssinian stone, which he positioned toward the palm of his hand."],[1362,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to wear his ring on his right hand."],[1363,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wearing his ring on his right hand."],[1364,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar said: The Prophet ﷺ used to wear his ring on his right hand."],[1365,"Anas said: The Prophet’s ﷺ ring was worn upon this finger, and he pointed to the little finger of his left hand."],[1366,"Anas reported that the Prophet ﷺ had a silver signet ring made and engraved upon it: Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah. He said to the people: “I have had a silver signet ring made and engraved upon it, ‘Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah.’ No one should engrave an inscription identical to its inscription.” Anas said: It is as though I can still see its gleam upon his little finger."],[1367,"It is narrated from Sayyidunā Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that when the noble Prophet ﷺ entered the toilet, he would take off his ring and put it aside. Abū Dāwūd says that this ḥadīth is munkar.\nImām al-Tirmidhī says: This ḥadīth is ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ gharīb. And in some copies it is: ḥasan gharīb."],[1368,"Abū Umāmah ibn Sahl said: The pommel of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ sword was made of silver."],[1369,"Al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām said: The Prophet ﷺ was wearing two coats of armor on the day of Uḥud. He attempted to climb onto a rock but was unable to do so, so Ṭalḥah crouched beneath him. The Prophet ﷺ stepped upon him until he stood firmly upon the rock. Al-Zubayr said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say, “Ṭalḥah has secured Paradise.”"],[1370,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported that the Prophet ﷺ entered Makkah wearing a black turban while not in the state of iḥrām."],[1371,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out wearing a cloak wrapped around his shoulders and a dark turban… the remainder of the hadith concerning the virtues of the Anṣār."],[1372,"Abū Juḥayfah reported: I came to the Prophet ﷺ in Makkah while he was at al-Abṭaḥ in a red leather tent. Bilāl came out carrying the water the Prophet ﷺ had used for ablution; some people obtained some of it, while others received drops sprinkled upon them. Then the Prophet ﷺ came out wearing a red garment, and it is as though I can still see the whiteness of his legs… the remainder of the hadith."],[1373,"Abū Burdah said: I entered upon ʿĀ’ishah, and she brought out for us a coarse waist-wrapper made in Yemen and a cloak of the type known as mulabbadah. She swore by Allah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died while wearing these two garments."],[1374,"ʿIkrimah reported that he saw Ibn ʿAbbās wearing his waist-wrapper with its front edge resting upon the tops of his feet while raising it higher at the back. I asked, “Why do you wear your waist-wrapper in this manner?” He replied, “I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wear his waist-wrapper in this manner.”"],[1375,"ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal said: I saw the Prophet’s ﷺ cup in the possession of Anas ibn Mālik. It had cracked, so Anas bound it with silver. It was a fine, broad cup made from naḍār wood. Anas said: I served the Messenger of Allah ﷺ drink from this cup on more occasions than I could count. Ibn Sīrīn said: The cup had an iron ring upon it. Anas wished to replace it with a ring of gold or silver, but Abū Ṭalḥah said to him, “Do not alter anything made by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” Anas therefore left it unchanged."],[1376,"Anas said: I served the Messenger of Allah ﷺ every kind of drink from this cup of mine: honey, nabīdh, water, and milk."],[1377,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The mattress upon which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ slept was made of leather and stuffed with palm fibers."],[1378,"ʿĀ’ishah said: The cushion upon which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reclined was made of leather and stuffed with palm fibers."],[1379,"Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī said: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was cleaning his teeth with a tooth-stick held in his hand. He was making the sound, “Aʿ, aʿ,” while the tooth-stick was in his mouth, as though he were retching."],[1380,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that whenever the Prophet ﷺ entered his home, he began by using the tooth-stick. Another narration states: Shurayḥ said: I asked ʿĀ’ishah, “What did the Prophet ﷺ begin with when he entered his home?” She replied, “The tooth-stick.”"],[1381,"Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān said: When the Prophet ﷺ rose during the night, he would vigorously clean his mouth with the tooth-stick."],[1382,"ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported that during the illness in which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, he repeatedly asked, “Where will I be tomorrow? Where will I be tomorrow?” eagerly awaiting ʿĀ’ishah’s allotted day. His wives permitted him to remain wherever he wished, so he stayed in ʿĀ’ishah’s house until he died there. ʿĀ’ishah said: He died in my house on the day that had been allotted to me. Allah took his soul while his head rested between my upper chest and neck, and his saliva became mixed with mine. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr entered carrying a tooth-stick with which he was cleaning his teeth. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at it, so I said, “ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, give me that tooth-stick.” He gave it to me. I bit and softened it, then gave it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He cleaned his teeth with it while leaning against my chest."],[1383,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ said: I led the gray mule carrying the Prophet of Allah ﷺ, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn until I brought them into the Prophet’s ﷺ chamber. One of the two boys was seated in front of him and the other behind him."],[1384,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I am the leader of the children of Adam on the Day of Resurrection, the first for whom the grave will be split open, the first to intercede, and the first whose intercession will be accepted.”"],[1385,"Ibn ʿAbbās reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I was granted victory by the east wind, while ʿĀd was destroyed by the west wind.”"],[1386,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was sent with concise, comprehensive speech; I was granted victory through fear; and while I was asleep, the keys to the treasures of the earth were brought to me and placed in my hand.” Abū Hurayrah said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has departed, while you are extracting those treasures.”"],[1387,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have been given what none of the prophets was given.” We asked, “What is it, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “I was granted victory through fear; I was given the keys of the earth; I was named Aḥmad; the earth was made a means of purification for me; and my nation was made the best of nations.”"],[1388,"ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb reported from his father, from his grandfather, that during the campaign of Tabūk, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood in prayer one night. Some of his Companions gathered behind him to guard him. When he had finished praying and turned toward them, he said: “Tonight I was granted five things that no one before me was granted. I was sent to all humanity, whereas those before me were sent only to their own people. I was granted victory over the enemy through fear: even if a month’s journey separated me from them, they would be filled with fear of me. The spoils of war were made lawful for me to consume, whereas those before me regarded consuming them as forbidden and would burn them. The earth was made for me a place of prayer and a means of purification; wherever the time for prayer reaches me, I perform dry ablution and pray. Those before me treated this matter differently and prayed only in their churches and synagogues. “As for the fifth—and what a thing it is!—I was told: ‘Ask, for every prophet has made his request.’ So I deferred my request until the Day of Resurrection. It is for you and for everyone who bears witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah.”"],[1389,"Abū Umāmah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “My Lord favored me over the prophets—or he said: over the nations—with four things: I was sent to all people; the whole earth was made a place of prayer and a means of purification for me and my nation, so wherever the time of prayer reaches a man from my nation, there he has his place of prayer and his means of purification; I was granted victory through fear cast into the hearts of my enemies from a month’s journey away; and the spoils of war were made lawful for us.”"],[1390,"Abū Dharr reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was granted five things that no one before me was granted: I was sent to the red and the black; the earth was made a means of purification and a place of prayer for me; the spoils of war were made lawful for me, though they had not been lawful for anyone before me; I was granted victory through fear, so that an enemy a month’s journey away from me is struck with fear; and I was told, ‘Ask and you will be granted.’ “I reserved my supplication as intercession for my nation. It will reach—if Allah wills—those among you who do not associate anything with Allah.”"],[1391,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have been given five things which were not given to any prophet before me: I have been aided by fear cast into the enemy from the distance of a month’s journey; the earth has been made for me a place of prayer and a means of purification, so wherever a man from my Ummah is when the time for prayer reaches him, let him pray; the spoils of war have been made lawful for me; a prophet used to be sent specifically to his own people, whereas I have been sent to all mankind; and I have been granted intercession.”"],[1392,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have been favored over the prophets in six ways: I have been given concise yet comprehensive speech; I have been aided by fear cast into the enemy; the spoils of war have been made lawful for me; the earth has been made for me a means of purification and a place of prayer; I have been sent to all creation; and the prophets were sealed with me.”"],[1393,"Ḥudhayfah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “We have been favored over the people in three ways: Our rows have been made like the rows of the angels; the entire earth has been made for us a place of prayer; and its soil has been made for us a means of purification when we cannot find water.”"],[1394,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, said: “On the Day of Uḥud I saw two men to the right and left of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, wearing white garments. I had never seen them before nor afterward.” He meant Jibrīl and Mīkāʾīl peace be upon them."],[1395,"Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “Telling a lie about me is unlike telling a lie about anyone else. Whoever deliberately lies about me, let him take his seat in the Fire.” I also heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “Whoever is lamented over is punished because of the lamentation made over him.”"],[1396,"ʿAlī reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not lie about me, for whoever lies about me shall enter the Fire.”"],[1397,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “I am only a human being, and I stipulated with my Lord, Mighty and Majestic, that any Muslim servant whom I rebuke or insult should have that made a purification and reward for him.”"],[1398,"Abū Hurayrah reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “O Allah, whichever believing servant I may have rebuked, make that a means of nearness to You for him on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1399,"Abū Hurayrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “O Allah, Muhammad is only a human being who becomes angry as other human beings become angry. I have taken from You a covenant that You will never break: whichever believer I may have harmed, rebuked, or struck, make it an expiation for him and a means by which You bring him near to You on the Day of Resurrection.” Another wording states: “Make it a blessing, purification, and means of nearness by which You bring him close to You on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1400,"ʿĀ’ishah said: Two men entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and spoke to him about something whose nature I do not know. They angered him, so he cursed and rebuked them. After they had left, I said, “O Messenger of Allah, no one has received as much good as these two have!” He asked, “Why is that?” I replied, “You cursed and rebuked them.” He said: “Do you not know the condition I made with my Lord? I said: ‘O Allah, I am only a human being, so whichever Muslim I curse or rebuke, make it a purification and reward for him.’”"],[1401,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: There was an orphan girl in the care of Umm Sulaym, the mother of Anas. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw the orphan girl and said: “Is it you? You have grown! May your age not increase!” The orphan girl returned to Umm Sulaym weeping. Umm Sulaym asked, “What is wrong, my daughter?” The girl replied, “The Prophet of Allah ﷺ supplicated against me that my age should not increase. Now my age will never increase”—or she said, “my lifetime.” Umm Sulaym hurried out, hastily wrapping her head-covering, until she met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He asked her: “What is the matter, Umm Sulaym?” She replied, “O Prophet of Allah, did you supplicate against my orphan girl?” He asked, “What do you mean, Umm Sulaym?” She said, “She claims that you supplicated that neither her age nor her lifetime should increase.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed and then said: “O Umm Sulaym, do you not know the condition I made with my Lord? I stipulated with my Lord, saying: ‘I am only a human being. I am pleased as human beings are pleased, and I become angry as human beings become angry. So if I supplicate against anyone from my nation with a supplication he does not deserve, make it a purification, a blessing, and a means by which You bring him near to You on the Day of Resurrection.’”"],[1402,"قال الله تعالى: ﴿وَ مَا كَانَ لَكُمْ اَنْ تُؤْذُوْا رَسُوْلَ اللّٰهِ وَ لَاۤ اَنْ تَنْكِحُوْۤا اَزْوَاجَهٗ مِنْۢ بَعْدِهٖۤ اَبَدًا١ؕ﴾ [الأحزاب: ٥٣ ]\nAnd it is not for you ever to harm the Messenger of Allah, nor to marry his wives after him, ever. (Sūrat al-Aḥzāb 53)"],[1403,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Do you think my attention is directed only toward my prayer direction here? By Allah, neither your humility nor your bowing is hidden from me. Indeed, I see you from behind my back.”"],[1404,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Perform the bowing and prostration properly. By Allah, I see you behind me”—or he may have said, “from behind my back”—“when you bow and prostrate.”"],[1405,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh said: When the Prophet ﷺ went out, his Companions would walk ahead of him and leave the space behind him for the angels."],[1406,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Whoever sees me in a dream will see me while awake—or it is as though he has seen me while awake—for Satan cannot assume my form.” This is Muslim’s wording. Al-Bukhārī adds that Ibn Sīrīn said: “This applies when he sees him in his actual form.”"],[1407,"Anas ibn Mālik reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Whoever sees me in a dream has truly seen me, for Satan cannot appear in my likeness. The dream of a believer is one of forty-six parts of prophethood.”"],[1408,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “Whoever sees me has truly seen the truth, for Satan cannot take on my form.”"],[1409,"Abū Qatādah reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “A good dream is from Allah, while a disturbing dream is from Satan. Whoever sees something he dislikes should lightly spit to his left three times and seek refuge from Satan, and it will not harm him. Satan cannot appear in my guise.”"],[1410,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever sees me in sleep has truly seen me, for Satan is not able to assume my form.” He also said: “When one of you has a disturbing dream, he should not tell anyone about Satan’s playing with him in his sleep.”"],[1411,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Whoever sees me in a dream has indeed seen me as though awake, for Satan cannot assume my form.”"],[1412,"ʿAwn ibn Abī Juḥayfah reported from his father that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Whoever sees me in a dream, it is as though he has seen me while awake. Satan cannot assume my form.” Ibn Ḥibbān’s wording states: “Satan cannot resemble me.”"],[1413,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “By Him in Whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, a day will certainly come upon one of you when he will no longer see me; then seeing me would be dearer to him than having his family and wealth together.” Abū Isḥāq said: “In my view, its meaning is: ‘Seeing me along with them would be dearer to him than his family and wealth.’ I regard the wording as containing an instance of transposition.”"],[1414,"قال الله تعالى: ﴿اِنَّاۤ اَعْطَيْنٰكَ الْكَوْثَرَؕ۰۰۱﴾ [سورة الكوثر: 1].\nThe saying of Allah the Exalted: Indeed, We have given you al-Kawthar."],[1415,"Abū ʿUbaydah narrated from ʿĀʾishah may Allah be pleased with her: I asked her about the statement of Allah Most High: “Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar.” [Sūrat al-Kawthar 108:1] She replied: “It is a river which your Prophet ﷺ was given. Along its two banks are hollowed pearls, and its vessels are as numerous as the stars.”"],[1416,"Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: When the Prophet ﷺ was taken up into heaven, he said: “I came upon a river whose two banks were domes of hollowed pearl. I asked: ‘What is this, O Jibrīl?’ He replied: ‘This is al-Kawthar.’”"],[1417,"Anas ibn Mālik may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “While I was walking in Paradise, I came upon a river whose two banks were domes of hollowed pearl. I asked: ‘What is this, O Jibrīl?’ He replied: ‘This is al-Kawthar which your Lord has given you.’ Its clay — or its fragrance — was intensely fragrant musk.” Hudbah was uncertain as to which wording was used."],[1418,"Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: On the night when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was taken on the Night Journey from the Sacred Mosque, he mentioned the lengthy ḥadīth of the Night Journey and Ascension. In it is: “In the lowest heaven there were two rivers flowing. He asked: ‘What are these two rivers, O Jibrīl?’ He replied: ‘These are the Nile and the Euphrates at their source.’ Then he took him onward through heaven, and there was another river upon which stood a palace of pearl and emerald. He struck his hand into it, and it was fragrant musk. He asked: ‘What is this, O Jibrīl?’ He replied: ‘This is al-Kawthar which your Lord has stored for you.’”"],[1419,"Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was among us one day, he briefly dozed off and then raised his head smiling. We asked: “What made you smile, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “A sūrah has just been revealed to me.” Then he recited: “Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar. So pray to your Lord and sacrifice. Indeed, your enemy is the one cut off.” [Sūrat al-Kawthar 108:1–3] Then he asked: “Do you know what al-Kawthar is?” We replied: “Allah and His Messenger know best.” He said: “It is a river which my Lord, Mighty and Majestic, has promised me. In it is abundant good. It is a Basin to which my Ummah will come on the Day of Resurrection. Its vessels are as numerous as the stars. A servant from among them will then be seized away, and I will say: ‘My Lord, he is from my Ummah.’ He will say: ‘You do not know what he introduced after you.’”"],[1420,"Anas may Allah be pleased with him recited the verse: “Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar.” [Sūrat al-Kawthar 108:1] He then said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was given al-Kawthar. It is a river flowing without having been dug out. Its two banks are domes of pearl. I struck my hand into its soil and found intensely fragrant musk, and its pebbles are pearls.”"],[1421,"Anas may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I entered Paradise and came upon a river whose two banks were tents of pearl. I struck my hand into that over which its water flowed, and it was intensely fragrant musk. I asked: ‘What is this, O Jibrīl?’ He replied: ‘This is al-Kawthar which Allah has given you.’”"],[1422,"Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked: “What is al-Kawthar?” He replied: “It is a river which Allah has given me — meaning in Paradise — whiter than milk and sweeter than honey. In it are birds whose necks are like the necks of camels.” Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said:\n“Those must indeed be delightful!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “Eating them is even more delightful.”"],[1423,"Ibn ʿUmar may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Al-Kawthar is a river in Paradise. Its two banks are of gold, and its channel flows over pearls and rubies. Its soil is more fragrant than musk, its water is sweeter than honey, and whiter than snow.”"],[1424,"Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, reported: When the verse was revealed: “Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar,” [Sūrat al-Kawthar 108:1] the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “It is a river in Paradise. Its two banks are of gold, and it flows over large stones of pearls and rubies. Its drink is sweeter than honey, whiter than milk, colder than snow, and more fragrant than musk.”"],[1425,"Anas ibn Mālik may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Some people from my Companions will certainly come to me at the Basin. When I recognize them, they will be seized away from me. I will say: ‘My Companions?’ Then it will be said: ‘You do not know what they introduced after you.’”"],[1426,"Anas may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The extent of my Basin is like the distance between Aylah and Ṣanʿāʾ in Yemen, and its pitchers are as numerous as the stars of the sky.”"],[1427,"Anas ibn Mālik may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “The distance between the two sides of my Basin is like the distance between Ṣanʿāʾ and Madinah.” In another narration:\n“Or like the distance between Madinah and ʿAmmān.” And in another narration:\n“The distance between the two sides of my Basin.”"],[1428,"Anas may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “In it will be seen pitchers of gold and silver as numerous as the stars of the sky.”"],[1429,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “By the One in whose Hand is my soul, I will certainly drive men away from my Basin just as a stray camel is driven away from a watering place.”"],[1430,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him used to relate that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “A group from my Companions will come to me on the Day of Resurrection, but they will be driven away from the Basin. I will say: ‘My Lord, my Companions?’ He will say: ‘You have no knowledge of what they introduced after you. They turned back upon their heels.’”"],[1431,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “While I was asleep, I saw a group. When I recognized them, a man came out between me and them and said: ‘Come.’ I asked: ‘Where?’ He replied: ‘To the Fire, by Allah.’ I asked: ‘What is the matter with them?’ He replied: ‘They turned back after you upon their heels.’ Then another group appeared. When I recognized them, a man came out between me and them and said: ‘Come.’ I asked: ‘Where?’ He replied: ‘To the Fire, by Allah.’ I asked: ‘What is the matter with them?’ He replied: ‘They turned back after you upon their heels.’ I do not think that any of them will escape except as few as stray, ownerless camels.”"],[1432,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to the graveyard and said: “Peace be upon you, abode of believing people. Indeed, if Allah wills, we will join you. I wish that we had seen our brothers.” They asked: “Are we not your brothers, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “You are my Companions. Our brothers are those who have not yet come.” They asked: “How will you recognize those from your Ummah who have not yet come, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “Suppose a man had horses with bright white markings on their foreheads and legs among completely black horses. Would he not recognize his own horses?” They replied: “Certainly, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “They will come with radiant marks on their faces and limbs from the effects of wuḍūʾ, and I will precede them to the Basin. Indeed, men will certainly be driven away from my Basin just as a stray camel is driven away. I will call to them: ‘Come here!’ But it will be said: ‘They changed after you.’ Then I will say: ‘Away with them! Away with them!’”"],[1433,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “My Basin is greater in extent than the distance from Aylah to ʿAdan. It is whiter than snow and sweeter than honey mixed with milk. Its vessels are more numerous than the stars, and I will keep people away from it just as a man keeps other people’s camels away from his watering place.” They asked: “O Messenger of Allah, will you recognize us on that day?” He replied: “Yes. You will have a distinguishing mark which no other nation possesses. You will come to me with radiant faces and limbs from the effects of wuḍūʾ.” In another narration: “My Ummah will come to me at the Basin, and I will drive other people away from it just as a man drives another person’s camels away from his own camels.”\nThey asked: “O Prophet of Allah, will you recognize us?” He replied: “Yes. You will have a distinguishing mark which no one else possesses. You will come to me with radiant faces and limbs from the effects of wuḍūʾ. A group from among you will certainly be turned away from me and will not reach me. I will say: ‘My Lord, these are from my Companions.’ Then an angel will come to me and say: ‘Do you know what they introduced after you?’”"],[1434,"Ibn ʿUmar may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Indeed, ahead of you is a Basin whose extent is like the distance between Jarbāʾ and Adhruḥ.”"],[1435,"Ibn ʿUmar may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, ahead of you is a Basin whose extent is like the distance between Jarbāʾ and Adhruḥ. In it are pitchers like the stars of the sky. Whoever comes to it and drinks from it will never thirst afterward.”"],[1436,"Ḥārithah ibn Wahb al-Khuzāʿī may Allah be pleased with him reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “His Basin is as extensive as the distance between Ṣanʿāʾ and Madinah.”\nAl-Mustawrid asked him: “Did you not hear him mention the vessels?” He replied: “No.” Al-Mustawrid said: “The vessels in it will be seen like the stars.”"],[1437,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I will precede you to the Basin. Men from among you will certainly be brought forward, then they will be seized away from me. I will say: ‘My Lord, my Companions?’ It will be said: ‘You do not know what they introduced after you.’”"],[1438,"ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over those who had been martyred at Uḥud eight years later, like one bidding farewell to the living and the dead. Then he ascended the pulpit and said: “I will precede you, and I am a witness over you. Your appointed meeting is at the Basin, and I can see it from where I stand now. I do not fear that you will associate partners with Allah after me, but I fear that you will compete with one another for the worldly life.” ʿUqbah said: “That was the last time I looked upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[1439,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “My Basin is the distance of a month’s journey. Its water is whiter than milk, its fragrance more pleasant than musk, and its cups are like the stars of the sky. Whoever drinks from it will never thirst again.”"],[1440,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “My Basin is the distance of a month’s journey. Its corners are equal. Its cups are as numerous as the stars of the sky. Its water is whiter than snow, sweeter than honey, and more fragrant than musk. Whoever drinks a single drink from it will never thirst afterward.”"],[1441,"Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I will be at the Basin, watching to see which of you comes to me. Some people will be taken away from me, and I will say: ‘My Lord, they are from me and from my Ummah!’ It will be said: ‘Do you know what they did after you? By Allah, they never ceased turning back upon their heels.’” Ibn Abī Mulaykah used to say: “O Allah, we seek refuge in You from turning back upon our heels or being tempted away from our religion.”\nRegarding the statement of Allah Most High: “If he dies or is killed, will you turn back upon your heels?” [Āl ʿImrān 3:144] that is: will you turn back and retreat?"],[1442,"Abū Ḥāzim reported: I heard Sahl ibn Saʿd may Allah be pleased with him say: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “I will precede you to the Basin. Whoever comes to it will drink from it, and whoever drinks from it will never thirst afterward. Certain people whom I recognize and who recognize me will come to me, but then a barrier will be placed between me and them.”\nAbū Ḥāzim said: Al-Nuʿmān ibn Abī ʿAyyāsh heard me narrating this and asked: “Is this how you heard Sahl narrate it?” I replied: “Yes.” He said: “And I testify that I heard Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī may Allah be pleased with him add that the Prophet ﷺ said: ‘They are from me.’ Then it will be said: ‘You do not know what they changed after you.’ So I will say: ‘Away with them! Away with those who changed after me!’”"],[1443,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I will precede you to the Basin. Whoever comes to it will drink from it, and whoever drinks from it will never thirst afterward. Certain people whom I recognize and who recognize me will come to me, but then a barrier will be placed between me and them.”\nI will say: “They are from me.” It will be said: “You do not know what they introduced after you.” Then I will say: “Away with them! Away with those who changed after me!”"],[1444,"Jundub ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Sufyān al-Bajalī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “I will precede you to the Basin.”"],[1445,"ʿĀʾishah may Allah be pleased with her said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say while he was among his Companions: “I will be at the Basin waiting for those among you who come to me. By Allah, men will certainly be taken away from me, and I will say: ‘My Lord, they are from me and from my Ummah!’ He will say: ‘You do not know what they did after you. They never ceased turning back upon their heels.’”"],[1446,"Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, may Allah be pleased with her said: I used to hear the people mentioning the Basin, but I had not heard anything about it directly from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then one day, while a servant-girl was combing my hair, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “O people!” I said to the servant-girl: “Move away from me.” She replied: “He only called the men; he did not call the women.” I said: “I am among the people.” Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I will precede you to the Basin. Beware that one of you should come and then be driven away from me as a stray camel is driven away. I will say: ‘Why is this?’ It will be said: ‘You do not know what they introduced after you.’ Then I will say: ‘Away with them!’”"],[1447,"Ḥudhayfah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I will precede you to the Basin. I will contend for certain people, but they will be taken away despite me. I will say: ‘My Lord, my Companions! My Companions!’ It will be said: ‘You do not know what they introduced after you.’”"],[1448,"Ḥudhayfah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “My Basin is greater in extent than the distance from Aylah to ʿAdan. By the One in whose Hand is my soul, I will drive men away from it just as a man drives unfamiliar camels away from his watering place.” They asked: “O Messenger of Allah, will you recognize us?” He replied: “Yes. You will come to me with radiant faces and limbs from the traces of wuḍūʾ—a distinction belonging to none other than you.”"],[1449,"Ḥudhayfah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The extent of my Basin is like the distance between Aylah and Muḍar. Its vessels are more numerous than—or he said as numerous as—the stars of the sky. Its water is sweeter than honey, whiter than milk, colder than snow, and more fragrant than musk. Whoever drinks from it will never thirst afterward.”"],[1450,"Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I asked: “O Messenger of Allah, what are the vessels of the Basin?” He replied: “By the One in whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad, its vessels are more numerous than the stars and constellations of the sky on a dark, clear night. They are vessels of Paradise. Whoever drinks from it will never thirst again. Two channels from Paradise pour into it. Whoever drinks from it will never thirst. Its width is equal to its length, extending like the distance from ʿAmmān to Aylah. Its water is whiter than milk and sweeter than honey.”"],[1451,"Thawbān may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: “I will be at the central part of my Basin, driving the people away so that the people of Yemen may drink. I will strike with my staff until the water pours out for them.” He was asked about its width and replied: “From where I stand to ʿAmmān.” He was asked about its drink and said: “Whiter than milk and sweeter than honey. Two channels gush into it and supply it from Paradise: one of them is of gold and the other of silver.”"],[1452,"Jābir ibn Samurah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, I will precede you to the Basin. The distance between its two ends is like the distance between Ṣanʿāʾ and Aylah, and its pitchers are like the stars.”"],[1453,"ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ said: I wrote to Jābir ibn Samurah through my servant Nāfiʿ: “Tell me something that you heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” He wrote back to me: “I heard him say: ‘I am the forerunner at the Basin.’”"],[1454,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allah be pleased with them, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “I will go ahead of you as your forerunner. If you do not find me, then I will be at the Basin, whose extent is from Aylah to Makkah. Men and women will come carrying vessels and waterskins, yet they will not taste anything from it.”"],[1455,"Al-Ṣunābiḥ al-Aḥmasī may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, I will precede you to the Basin, and I will take pride in your great numbers before the other nations. So do not fight one another after me.”"],[1456,"Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O my Lord, You created me as the leader of the children of Adam—and I say this without boasting—and I will be the first for whom the earth is split open on the Day of Resurrection—and I say this without boasting. Indeed, the Basin to which I will come is greater in extent than the distance between Ṣanʿāʾ and Aylah.”"],[1457,"Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I asked the Prophet ﷺ to intercede for me on the Day of Resurrection. He replied: “I will do so.” I asked: “O Messenger of Allah, where should I look for you?” He said: “First look for me at the Ṣirāṭ.” I asked: “What if I do not find you at the Ṣirāṭ?” He replied: “Then look for me at the Scale.” I asked: “What if I do not find you at the Scale?” He replied: “Then look for me at the Basin, for I will not be absent from these three places.”"],[1458,"Abū Umāmah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, Allah has promised me that He will admit seventy thousand from my Ummah into Paradise without reckoning.”\nIt was asked: “How vast is your Basin, O Prophet of Allah?” He replied: “Like the distance from ʿAdan to ʿAmmān—and wider, and wider,” gesturing with his hand. He said: “In it are two channels, one of gold and one of silver.”\nIt was asked: “What is the water of your Basin like, O Prophet of Allah?” He replied: “Water whiter than milk, sweeter in taste than honey, and more fragrant than musk. Whoever drinks from it will never thirst afterward, and his face will never become darkened.”"],[1459,"ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbd al-Sulamī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: A Bedouin stood before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked: “What is this Basin of yours that you speak about?” He replied: “It is like the distance between Ṣanʿāʾ and Buṣrā. Then Allah will extend to me within it a projecting channel whose two ends no human being among creation knows.”\nʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: “Allāhu Akbar!” The Prophet ﷺ then said: “As for the Basin, the poor among the Muhājirūn will crowd around it—those who fight in the path of Allah and die in the path of Allah. And I hope that Allah will bring me to that channel so that I may drink from it.”"],[1460,"Yaḥnas reported: When Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib may Allah be pleased with him came to Madinah, he married Khawlah bint Qays ibn Qahd al-Anṣāriyyah of Banū al-Najjār. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to visit Ḥamzah at her house, and she would narrate reports concerning the Prophet ﷺ. She said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to us one day, and I said: “O Messenger of Allah, it has reached me that you mention that on the Day of Resurrection you will have a Basin extending from such-and-such to such-and-such.” He replied: “Yes, and the people most beloved to me to be given their fill from it are your people.” She said: I then brought him a cooking pot containing bread—or khazīrah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed his hand into the pot to eat, and his fingers were burned by its heat. He said: “Ḥass!” Then he said: “The son of Adam, when cold afflicts him, says, ‘Ḥass!’ and when heat afflicts him, he says, ‘Ḥass!’”"],[1461,"Abū al-Dardāʾ may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I will precede you to the Basin, and I will certainly recognize when one of you is disputed with me.” In another narration:\n“I will certainly find myself disputed over one of you at the Basin, and I will say: ‘This is one of my Companions.’ Then it will be said: ‘You do not know what they introduced after you.’” Abū al-Dardāʾ said: “O Messenger of Allah, ask Allah not to make me one of them.” He replied: “You are not among them.”"],[1462,"ʿUmar may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I am holding you back by your waistbands from the Fire, while you overpower me and rush into it as moths and locusts rush forward. Soon I will release your waistbands and precede you to the Basin, and you will come to me both together and separately.”"],[1463,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say from this pulpit: “What is the matter with men who say that kinship with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ will not benefit his relatives? Rather, by Allah, my ties of kinship remain connected in this world and in the Hereafter. Indeed, O people, I will precede you to the Basin. When you come to me, one man will say: ‘O Messenger of Allah, I am so-and-so son of so-and-so.’ Another will say: ‘I am so-and-so son of so-and-so.’ I will say: ‘As for your lineage, I recognize it. But you introduced changes after me and turned backward upon your heels.’”"],[1464,"Abū Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Indeed, I have a Basin extending from Aylah to Ṣanʿāʾ, its width equal to its length. Into it pour two channels from Paradise, one of silver and the other of gold. Its water is sweeter than honey, colder than snow, and whiter than milk. Whoever drinks from it will not thirst until he enters Paradise. In it are pitchers as numerous as the stars of the sky.”"],[1465,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah reported: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād doubted the reality of the Basin, and there were some Ḥarūriyyah with him. He said: “What do you think of this Basin that is mentioned? I do not think it is anything!” Some of his companions said: “There are a number of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ with you. Send for them and ask them.” So he sent for a man from Muzaynah and asked him about the Basin, and the man narrated to him concerning it. Then he said: “Send for Abū Barzah al-Aslamī.” Abū Barzah came wearing two striped garments, one wrapped around his waist and the other draped over his shoulders. He was a stocky man of somewhat short stature. When ʿUbaydullāh saw him, he laughed and said: “This Muḥammadan of yours is certainly a short, stocky man!” The elderly Companion understood what he meant and said: “How astonishing! I never thought I would live to see a time when my people would regard companionship with Muḥammad ﷺ as a disgrace!” Those sitting with ʿUbaydullāh said: “The governor only summoned you to ask you about the Basin. Did you hear anything concerning it from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” He replied: “Yes. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mention it. Whoever denies it—may Allah not give him a drink from it.” He then shook out his cloak and departed angrily. ʿUbaydullāh then sent for Zayd ibn Arqam and asked him about the Basin. Zayd related to him an impressive report that pleased him. He asked: “Did you personally hear this from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” Zayd replied: “No, but my brother narrated it to me.” He said: “Then we have no need for your brother’s narration.” Abū Sabrah—one of ʿUbaydullāh’s companions—then said: “When your father went as a delegate to Muʿāwiyah, I accompanied him. I met ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and he narrated directly to me a ḥadīth that he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He dictated it to me and I wrote it down.” ʿUbaydullāh said: “I adjure you to ride this horse hard until it sweats and bring me that written document.” Abū Sabrah said: So I mounted the horse and drove it until it sweated, then brought him the document. In it was written: “This is what ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ narrated to me, that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘Indeed, Allah hates obscenity and deliberate obscene conduct. By the One in whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad, the Hour will not be established until obscenity and shameless conduct become widespread, bad treatment of neighbors and severing of family ties become widespread, the trustworthy person is betrayed, and the treacherous person is trusted. By the One in whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad, among the best Muslims is the one from whose tongue and hand the Muslims are safe, and the best migration is that of one who abandons what Allah has forbidden. By the One in whose Hand is my soul, the example of the believer is like a piece of gold: its owner blows upon it, yet it neither changes nor diminishes. By the One in whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad, the example of the believer is like the date-palm: it takes in what is good, produces what is good, and if it falls, it neither breaks nor spoils. Indeed, I have a Basin whose two sides are like the distance between Aylah and Makkah—or he said, between Ṣanʿāʾ and Madinah. Its pitchers are like the stars. Its water is whiter than milk and sweeter than honey. Whoever drinks from it will never thirst afterward.’” Abū Sabrah said: Then ʿUbaydullāh took the written document, and I became distressed over losing it.\nLater I met Yaḥyā ibn Yaʿmar and complained to him about that. He said: “By Allah, I have memorized it more firmly than I have memorized a sūrah of the Qurʾān.” Then he narrated it to me exactly as it had been written in the document."],[1466,"Abū Ḥamzah reported: Abū Barzah entered upon ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād, who said: “This Muḥammadan of yours is certainly a short, stocky man!” Abū Barzah replied: “I never thought I would live among people who regarded companionship with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as a disgrace.” They said: “The governor only summoned you to ask you about the Basin and its reality.” He said: “Is it true?” He replied: “Yes. Whoever denies it—may Allah not give him a drink from it.”"],[1467,"Abū Ṭālūt al-ʿAnazī reported: I heard Abū Barzah as he came out from the presence of ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād, breathing heavily. He said: “I never thought I would live long enough to remain among a people who reproach me for having been a Companion of Muḥammad ﷺ!” They said: “This Muḥammadan of yours is the short, stocky man!” He said: “I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning the Basin. Whoever denies it—may Allah not give him a drink from it.”"],[1468,"Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: Ziyād—or Ibn Ziyād—was mentioned in connection with the Basin, and he denied it. When this reached Anas, he said: “By Allah, tomorrow I will certainly displease him.” He then asked: “What is it that you deny concerning the Basin?” They asked: “Did you hear the Prophet ﷺ mention it?” He replied: “Yes. I lived to see elderly women in Madinah who would not perform a single prayer without asking Allah Most High to bring them to the Basin of Muḥammad ﷺ.”"],[1469,"Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād sent for me, so I came to him. He said: “What are these narrations that you relate and transmit from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ which we do not find in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic? You claim that he has a Basin in Paradise!” I replied: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ certainly told us about it and promised it to us.” He said: “You have lied! Rather, you are an old man who has become senile.” I replied: “My own two ears heard it and my heart retained it from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: ‘Whoever deliberately lies about me, let him take his seat in Hell.’ And I have not lied about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[1470,"Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and stopped at a place. He said: “You do not constitute even one part out of one hundred thousand parts of those who will come to me at the Basin.” It was asked: “How many of you were there that day?” He replied: “Seven hundred or eight hundred.”"],[1471,"Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us while we were nine—five and four—one of the two groups being Arabs and the other non-Arabs. He said: “Listen. Have you heard that after me there will be rulers? Whoever enters upon them, affirms their lies, and assists them in their oppression is not from me and I am not from him, and he will not come to me at the Basin. But whoever does not enter upon them, does not assist them in their oppression, and does not affirm their lies is from me and I am from him, and he will come to me at the Basin.”"],[1472,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allah be pleased with them, reported: The Prophet ﷺ said to Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah may Allah be pleased with him: “May Allah protect you from the rule of foolish leaders.” Kaʿb asked: “What is the rule of foolish leaders?” He replied: “Rulers who will come after me who do not follow my guidance and do not adhere to my Sunnah. Whoever affirms their lies and assists them in their oppression—such people are not from me and I am not from them, and they will not come to me at my Basin. But whoever does not affirm their lies and does not assist them in their oppression—such people are from me and I am from them, and they will come to me at my Basin.\nO Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, fasting is a shield, charity extinguishes sin, and prayer is an offering of nearness—or he said: a proof. O Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, flesh that has grown from unlawful earnings will not enter Paradise; the Fire has a greater claim to it. O Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, people go out in two ways: one purchases his own soul and sets it free, while another sells his soul and destroys it.”"],[1473,"Ḥudhayfah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “There will indeed be rulers who lie and act unjustly. Whoever affirms their lies and assists them in their oppression is not from me and I am not from him, and he will not come to me at the Basin. But whoever does not affirm their lies and does not assist them in their oppression is from me and I am from him, and he will come to me at the Basin.”"],[1474,"Sayyidunā Ḥudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrates from the noble Prophet ﷺ that he ﷺ said: “Soon there will be rulers who will lie and commit injustice. Whoever believes their lies and helps them in their injustice is not from us, and I am not from him, and he will not come to my Pool. And whoever does not believe their lies and does not help them in their injustice is from me and I am from him, and he will come to my Pool.”\n§Important points:\nAl-Qurṭubī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: It is obligatory upon every accountable person (mukallaf) to know and to affirm that Allah the Exalted has especially granted His Prophet Muhammad ﷺ al-Kawthar. This is the Pool which is mentioned clearly, with its name, attributes, drink and vessels, in many authentic and well-known ḥadīths. The collection of these ḥadīths produces certain knowledge and the certainty of tawātur, because this narration has been transmitted from the noble Prophet ﷺ by more than 30 Companions. Of these, more than 20 narrations are found in the Ṣaḥīḥayn (al-Bukhārī and Muslim), while the rest of the ḥadīths came with other authentic chains besides these, and they are well-known in terms of transmission. The Followers then transmitted this narration from these Companions, and these ḥadīths continued to be transmitted in every era and every region until they reached us. All of this establishes a proof from Allah against us, and it is binding upon us to believe in it and affirm it, as the Salaf and all the later scholars of Ahl al-Sunnah have agreed upon.\n§Those who deny the Pool of al-Kawthar:\nThe innovating groups tried to deny this Pool and to reinterpret it, although its apparent meaning is clear, and there is neither rational nor textual evidence for denying it. Their reinterpretation has been regarded as foolishness of intellect and contrary to the consensus of the Salaf.\nMeaning of al-Kawthar: Al-Kawthar is a river in Paradise which flows into the Pool of al-Kawthar, as is established in the authentic ḥadīths.\nThe meaning of uṣayḥābī and aṣḥābī in the ḥadīth: In the ḥadīth, by uṣayḥābī (diminutive) and aṣḥābī (plain form), two kinds of people are meant:\n1- Sinful apostates: These people apostatized not from Islam but from steadfastness. Their righteous deeds were turned into evil deeds, but these people will enter Paradise through the intercession of the Prophet ﷺ and will not remain in Hell forever.\n2- Those who apostatized into disbelief: These are the people who returned to true disbelief, such as the companions of Musaylimah the Liar, who apostatized after the death of the noble Prophet ﷺ. Sayyidunā Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, fought against them.\nThe misunderstanding of the Shīʿah: In these ḥadīths there is no evidence for the Shīʿah claim that all the Companions apostatized except a few. The truth is that none of the Companions apostatized; rather, apostasy occurred only among some of the Bedouins and the companions of Musaylimah."],[1475,"Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: A man from the Anṣār spoke privately with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “Will you not appoint me as you appointed so-and-so?” He replied: “Indeed, after me you will encounter others being preferred over you, so remain patient until you meet me at the Basin.”"],[1476,"Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: The Prophet ﷺ said to the Anṣār: “Indeed, after me you will encounter others being preferred over you, so remain patient until you meet me. Your appointed meeting place is the Basin.”"],[1477,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd ibn ʿĀṣim al-Māzinī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: The Prophet ﷺ said to the Anṣār: “Indeed, after me you will encounter others being preferred over you, so remain patient until you meet me at the Basin.”"],[1478,"Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “O assembly of the Anṣār, your appointed meeting place is my Basin. Its vessels are more numerous than the stars of the sky—or as numerous as the stars of the sky—and its width is like the distance between where I am and Ṣanʿāʾ, or like the distance between where I am and ʿAmmān.”"],[1479,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “What lies between my house and my pulpit is a garden from the gardens of Paradise, and my pulpit is upon my Basin.”"],[1480,"Sahl ibn Saʿd may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Every people has one who goes ahead of them, and I will be your forerunner at the Basin. Whoever reaches the Basin and drinks from it will not thirst, and whoever does not thirst will enter Paradise.”"],[1481,"Allah the Exalted said: We have sent you as a bearer of good news and a warner to all of mankind. [Sūrat Saba’: 28]\nAnd Allah the Exalted said: Say: O people! I am the Messenger of Allah to all of you. [Sūrat al-Aʿrāf: 158]\nAnd Allah the Exalted said: O people! Proof has come to you from your Lord, and We have sent down to you a clear light. [Sūrat al-Nisā’: 174]\nThere are many other verses like these."],[1482,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have been given five things which no prophet before me was given: I have been aided by fear cast into the enemy from the distance of a month’s journey; the earth has been made for me a place of prayer and a means of purification, so wherever a man from my Ummah is when the time for prayer comes, let him pray; the spoils of war have been made lawful for me; a prophet used to be sent specifically to his own people, whereas I have been sent to all mankind; and I have been granted intercession.”"],[1483,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “By the One in whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad, no one from this nation — whether Jew or Christian — hears about me and then dies without believing in that with which I was sent except that he will be among the people of the Fire.”"],[1484,"قال الله تعالى: ﴿وَ اِذْ صَرَفْنَاۤ اِلَيْكَ نَفَرًا مِّنَ الْجِنِّ يَسْتَمِعُوْنَ الْقُرْاٰنَ١ۚ فَلَمَّا حَضَرُوْهُ قَالُوْۤا اَنْصِتُوْا١ۚ فَلَمَّا قُضِيَ وَ لَّوْا اِلٰى قَوْمِهِمْ مُّنْذِرِيْنَ۰۰۲۹ قَالُوْا يٰقَوْمَنَاۤ اِنَّا سَمِعْنَا كِتٰبًا اُنْزِلَ مِنْۢ بَعْدِ مُوْسٰى مُصَدِّقًا لِّمَا بَيْنَ يَدَيْهِ يَهْدِيْۤ اِلَى الْحَقِّ وَ اِلٰى طَرِيْقٍ مُّسْتَقِيْمٍ۰۰۳۰ يٰقَوْمَنَاۤ اَجِيْبُوْا دَاعِيَ اللّٰهِ وَ اٰمِنُوْا بِهٖ يَغْفِرْ لَكُمْ مِّنْ ذُنُوْبِكُمْ وَ يُجِرْكُمْ مِّنْ عَذَابٍ اَلِيْمٍ۰۰۳۱﴾ [سورة الأحقاف: 29-31].\nوقال تعالى: ﴿قُلْ اُوْحِيَ اِلَيَّ اَنَّهُ اسْتَمَعَ نَفَرٌ مِّنَ الْجِنِّ فَقَالُوْۤا اِنَّا سَمِعْنَا قُرْاٰنًا عَجَبًاۙ۰۰۱﴾ [سورة الجن: 1].\nوقال تعالى: ﴿يٰمَعْشَرَ الْجِنِّ وَ الْاِنْسِ اَلَمْ يَاْتِكُمْ رُسُلٌ مِّنْكُمْ يَقُصُّوْنَ عَلَيْكُمْ اٰيٰتِيْ وَ يُنْذِرُوْنَكُمْ۠ لِقَآءَ يَوْمِكُمْ هٰذَا١ؕ قَالُوْا شَهِدْنَا عَلٰۤى اَنْفُسِنَا وَ غَرَّتْهُمُ الْحَيٰوةُ الدُّنْيَا وَ شَهِدُوْا عَلٰۤى اَنْفُسِهِمْ اَنَّهُمْ كَانُوْا كٰفِرِيْنَ۰۰۱۳۰﴾ [سورة الأنعام:130].\nAllah the Exalted said: And (remember) when We turned towards you a group of the jinn who were listening to the Qur’an. When they came to it, they said (to one another): “Be silent!” Then when (the recitation) was finished, they returned to their people as warners. (29) They said: “O our people! We have heard a Book which was sent down after Mūsā, confirming what came before it, guiding to the truth and to a straight path. (30) O our people! Respond to the caller of Allah and believe in him; He will forgive you some of your sins and protect you from a painful punishment.” [Sūrat al-Aḥqāf: 29-31]\nAnd Allah the Exalted said: Say: It has been revealed to me that a group of the jinn listened and said: “We have heard a wondrous Qur’an.” [Sūrat al-Jinn: 1]\nAnd Allah the Exalted said: O company of jinn and mankind! Did there not come to you messengers from among you, relating to you My verses and warning you of the meeting of this Day of yours? They will say: “We bear witness against ourselves.” And the life of this world deceived them, and they bore witness against themselves that they were disbelievers. [Sūrat al-Anʿām: 130]"],[1485,"Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not recite directly to the jinn on that occasion, nor did he see them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out with a group of his Companions heading toward the market of ʿUkāẓ, after the devils had been prevented from obtaining news from heaven. The narration states: “They set out throughout the eastern and western regions of the earth. The group that traveled toward Tihāmah passed by Nakhlah while heading toward the market of ʿUkāẓ, where the Prophet ﷺ was leading his Companions in the Fajr prayer. When they heard the Qurʾān, they listened attentively to it and said: ‘This is what has come between us and the news of heaven.’ They then returned to their people and said: ‘O our people, indeed we have heard a wondrous Qurʾān. It guides to right guidance, so we have believed in it, and we will never associate anyone with our Lord…’” The ḥadīth continues."],[1486,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that he used to carry a small vessel of water for the Prophet ﷺ for his ablution and personal needs. In the narration, he said: I asked: “What is the matter with bones and dung?” He replied: “They are from the food of the jinn. A delegation of the jinn of Naṣībīn came to me — and excellent jinn they were — and asked me for provisions. So I supplicated to Allah for them that whenever they passed by a bone or dung, they would find food upon it.”"],[1487,"ʿAlqamah reported: I asked Ibn Masʿūd may Allah be pleased with him: “Did any of you accompany the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the night of the jinn?” He replied: “No.” In the narration, the Prophet ﷺ said: “A caller from the jinn came to me, so I went with him and recited the Qurʾān to them.” Ibn Masʿūd said: He then took us and showed us their traces and the traces of their fires. The ḥadīth continues."],[1488,"Maysarah al-Fajr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: I asked: “O Messenger of Allah, when were you written as a Prophet?” He replied: “While Adam was between the soul and the body.”"],[1489,"Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: They asked: “O Messenger of Allah, when was Prophethood established for you?” He replied: “While Adam was between the soul and the body.”"],[1490,"Anas ibn Mālik may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “None of you believes until I am more beloved to him than his father, his child, and all people.”"],[1491,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “By the One in whose Hand is my soul, none of you will have faith until I am more beloved to him than his father and his child.”"],[1492,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Hishām, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: We were with the Prophet ﷺ while he was holding the hand of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb may Allah be pleased with him. ʿUmar said to him: “O Messenger of Allah, you are more beloved to me than everything except myself.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “No. By the One in whose Hand is my soul, not until I am more beloved to you than your own self.” ʿUmar then said: “Now, by Allah, you are more beloved to me than my own self.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Now, O ʿUmar.”"],[1493,"Al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib may Allah be pleased with him reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “He has tasted the sweetness of faith who is pleased with Allah as his Lord, Islam as his religion, and Muḥammad as his Messenger.”"],[1494,"Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: A Bedouin asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “When is the Hour?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him: “What have you prepared for it?” He replied: “Love for Allah and His Messenger.” He said: “You will be with those whom you love.”"],[1495,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Among those of my Ummah who will have the strongest love for me are people who will come after me. One of them would wish that he could see me even at the cost of his family and wealth.”"],[1496,"Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Juhanī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: While we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, two riders appeared. When he saw them, he said: “Two men from Kindah, from Madhḥij.” When they reached him, they turned out to be two men from Madhḥij. One of them came forward to pledge allegiance to him. The Prophet ﷺ took his hand, and the man asked: “O Messenger of Allah, what about one who saw you, believed in you, affirmed you, and followed you? What will he receive?” He replied: “Ṭūbā for him.” He then passed his hand over the man’s hand, and the man departed. The other man then came forward and took his hand to pledge allegiance. He asked: “O Messenger of Allah, what about one who believes in you, affirms you, and follows you but never sees you?” He replied: “Ṭūbā for him, then Ṭūbā for him, then Ṭūbā for him.” He then passed his hand over his hand, and the man departed."],[1497,"Abū Jumʿah Ḥabīb ibn Sibāʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: We ate a midday meal with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ may Allah be pleased with him was with us. He asked: “O Messenger of Allah, is there anyone better than us? We embraced Islam with you and strove alongside you.” He replied: “Yes. People who will come after you, who will believe in me although they never saw me.”"],[1498,"Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, whoever believes in You and testifies that I am Your Messenger, make meeting You beloved to him, make Your decree easy for him, and give him little of this world. And whoever does not believe in You and does not testify that I am Your Messenger, do not make meeting You beloved to him, do not make Your decree easy for him, and give him much of this world.”"],[1499,"قال الله تعالى: ﴿مَا كَانَ مُحَمَّدٌ اَبَاۤ اَحَدٍ مِّنْ رِّجَالِكُمْ وَ لٰكِنْ رَّسُوْلَ اللّٰهِ وَ خَاتَمَ النَّبِيّٖنَ١ؕ وَ كَانَ اللّٰهُ بِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ عَلِيْمًاؒ۰۰۴۰﴾ [سورة الأحزاب: 40].\nMuhammad ﷺ is not the father of any of your men, but he is the Messenger of Allah and the Seal (the last) of all the prophets. And Allah is ever All-Knowing of everything."],[1500,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “The Children of Israel were governed by the prophets. Whenever one prophet died, another prophet succeeded him. There will be no prophet after me, but there will be caliphs, and they will become numerous.” They asked: “What do you command us to do?” He replied: “Fulfill the pledge of allegiance to the first and then the next, and give them their rights, for Allah will question them concerning those whom He placed under their care.”"],[1501,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have been favored over the prophets in six ways.” He then mentioned the six qualities, among which was: “And the prophets were sealed with me.”"],[1502,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, I am the last of the prophets, and my mosque is the last of the mosques.”"],[1503,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “My example and the example of the prophets before me is like that of a man who built a house and made it excellent and beautiful, except for the place of one brick in one of its corners. The people began walking around it, admiring it and saying: ‘If only this brick were put in place!’ He said: ‘I am that brick, and I am the Seal of the Prophets.’”"],[1504,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “My example and the example of the prophets is like a man who built a house, completed it, and made it beautiful, except for the place of one brick. The people began entering it, marveling at it and saying: ‘If only the place of this brick were completed.’”"],[1505,"Abū Saʿīd may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “My example and the example of the prophets before me is like that of a man who built a structure and made it excellent and beautiful, except for the place of one brick in one of its corners. The people began walking around it, admiring it and saying: ‘If only this brick were put in place!’ He said: ‘I am that brick, and I am the Seal of the Prophets.’”"],[1506,"Ubayy ibn Kaʿb may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “My example among the prophets is like that of a man who built a house, made it excellent, completed it, and beautified it, but left the place of one brick unfinished. The people began walking around the building, marveling at it and saying: ‘If only the place of that brick were completed.’ Among the prophets, I occupy the place of that brick.”"],[1507,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ left ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib may Allah be pleased with him behind during the expedition of Tabūk. ʿAlī said: “O Messenger of Allah, are you leaving me behind with the women and children?” He replied: “Are you not pleased to be to me as Aaron was to Moses, except that there is no prophet after me?”"],[1508,"Jābir ibn Samurah narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Before the Hour there will be liars.”"],[1509,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “The Hour will not be established until lying impostors are raised up, nearly thirty of them, each claiming that he is the Messenger of Allah.”"],[1510,"Thawbān narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The Hour will not be established until tribes from my Ummah join the polytheists and worship idols. There will be thirty liars in my Ummah, each claiming that he is a prophet, while I am the Seal of the Prophets; there is no prophet after me.”"],[1511,"Ḥudhayfah narrated that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: “In my Ummah there will be twenty-seven liars and impostors, among them four women. And indeed, I am the Seal of the Prophets; there is no prophet after me.”"],[1512,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “Before the Hour there will be liars. Among them will be the man of al-Yamāmah, the ʿAnsī man of Ṣanʿāʾ, the man of Ḥimyar, and the Dajjāl, who will be the greatest of them in tribulation.” Jābir said: My companions also said that he said: “They will be nearly thirty liars.”"],[1513,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Nothing remains of prophethood except the glad tidings.” They asked: “What are the glad tidings?” He replied: “The righteous dream.”"],[1514,"Ibn ʿAbbās narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ drew back the curtain while the people were standing in rows behind Abū Bakr, and said: “O people, nothing remains of the glad tidings of prophethood except a righteous dream which a Muslim sees or which is seen concerning him. Indeed, I have been forbidden from reciting the Qur’an while bowing or prostrating. As for bowing, glorify the Lord, Mighty and Majestic, during it. As for prostration, strive earnestly in supplication, for it is especially deserving of being answered for you.”"],[1515,"Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished the morning prayer, he would ask: “Did any of you see a dream last night?” And he would say: “Nothing of Prophethood will remain after me except the righteous dream.”"],[1516,"Umm Kurz al-Kaʿbiyyah may Allah be pleased with her reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Prophethood has gone, and the glad tidings remain.”"],[1517,"Ḥudhayfah ibn Usayd may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Prophethood has gone. There is no Prophethood after me except the glad tidings.” He was asked: “What are the glad tidings?” He replied: “The righteous dream which a man sees or which is seen for him.”"],[1518,"ʿĀʾishah may Allah be pleased with her reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Nothing of Prophethood will remain after me except the glad tidings.” They asked: “O Messenger of Allah, what are the glad tidings?” He replied: “The righteous dream which a man sees or which is seen for him.”"],[1519,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allah be pleased with them, reported: We returned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from a journey until we came to a walled garden belonging to Banū al-Najjār. Inside it was a camel which would charge anyone who entered the garden. They mentioned this to the Prophet ﷺ. He came to the garden and called the camel. It approached with its lower lip lowered toward the ground until it knelt before him. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Bring its halter.” They haltered it, and he handed it to its owner. Then he turned toward the people and said: “There is nothing between heaven and earth except that it knows that I am the Messenger of Allah, except the disobedient among the jinn and mankind.”"],[1520,"Jābir ibn Samurah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, I know a stone in Makkah that used to greet me with salām before I was commissioned as a Prophet. I still know it now.”"],[1521,"Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them, reported: When the verse was revealed: “And warn your closest relatives,” [Sūrat al-Shuʿarāʾ 26:214] the Prophet ﷺ ascended al-Ṣafā and began calling: “O Banū Fihr! O Banū ʿAdī!” calling the various clans of Quraysh until they gathered. If a man was unable to come himself, he sent someone to find out what was happening. Abū Lahab and the people of Quraysh came. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Tell me: if I informed you that cavalry in the valley were about to attack you, would you believe me?” They replied: “Yes. We have never experienced anything from you except truthfulness.” He said: “Then indeed I am a warner to you before a severe punishment.” Abū Lahab said: “May you perish for the rest of this day! Is this why you gathered us?” Then there was revealed: “May the hands of Abū Lahab perish, and may he perish. His wealth and whatever he earned will not benefit him.” [Sūrat al-Masad 111:1–2]"],[1522,"قال الله تعالى: ﴿قُلْ اِنَّمَاۤ اَنَا بَشَرٌ مِّثْلُكُمْ يُوْحٰۤى اِلَيَّ اَنَّمَاۤ اِلٰهُكُمْ اِلٰهٌ وَّاحِدٌ١ۚ﴾ [سورة الكهف: 110]\nوقال تعالى: ﴿وَ مَا جَعَلْنَا لِبَشَرٍ مِّنْ قَبْلِكَ الْخُلْدَ١ؕ اَفَاۡىِٕنْ مِّتَّ فَهُمُ الْخٰلِدُوْنَ۰۰۳۴﴾ [سورة الأنبياء: 34].\nوقال تعالى: ﴿وَ قَالُوْا مَالِ هٰذَا الرَّسُوْلِ يَاْكُلُ الطَّعَامَ وَ يَمْشِيْ فِي الْاَسْوَاقِ١ؕ﴾ [سورة الفرقان: 7].\nAllah the Exalted said: Say: I am only a human being like you, (but) it is revealed to me that your God is only one God. [Sūrat al-Kahf: 110]\nAllah further said: We did not grant eternal life to any human being before you. So if you pass away, will they live forever? [Sūrat al-Anbiyā’: 34]\nLikewise He said: And they said: “What is the matter with this messenger, that he eats food and walks in the markets?” [Sūrat al-Furqān: 7]"],[1523,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I am only a human being like you. I forget just as you forget, so when I forget, remind me.”"],[1524,"Umm Salamah may Allah be pleased with her reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I am only a human being, and litigants come before me. Perhaps one of you is more eloquent in presenting his case than another, so I may think that he is truthful and judge in his favor on that basis. Whoever I award something that rightfully belongs to another Muslim, then it is only a piece of the Fire. So let him take it, or let him leave it.”"],[1525,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Allah, I am only a human being. Whichever Muslim I have reviled, cursed, or struck, make that for him a purification and mercy.”"],[1526,"ʿĀʾishah may Allah be pleased with her reported: Two men came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and spoke to him about something whose nature I do not know. They angered him, so he cursed and rebuked them. After they left, I said: “O Messenger of Allah, no one has attained such good as these two have attained!” He asked: “What do you mean?” I said: “You cursed and rebuked them!” He replied: “Do you not know the condition I made with my Lord? I said: ‘O Allah, I am only a human being. Whichever Muslim I curse or rebuke, make that for him a purification and a reward.’”"],[1527,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allah be pleased with them, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “I am only a human being, and I made a condition with my Lord, Mighty and Majestic, that whichever servant among the Muslims I revile or rebuke, it be made for him a purification and a reward.”"],[1528,"Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: There was an orphan girl under the care of Umm Sulaym — the mother of Anas. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw the orphan girl and said: “Is that you? You have grown! May your age not increase.” The orphan girl returned to Umm Sulaym crying. Umm Sulaym asked: “What is wrong, my daughter?” The girl replied: “The Prophet of Allah ﷺ supplicated against me that my age should never increase,” or she said, “my lifespan.” Umm Sulaym went out hurriedly, wrapping her head-covering as she went, until she met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked her: “What is wrong, O Umm Sulaym?” She replied: “O Prophet of Allah, did you supplicate against my orphan girl?” He asked: “What is that, O Umm Sulaym?” She said: “She claims that you supplicated that neither her age nor her lifespan should increase.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed and then said: “O Umm Sulaym, do you not know the condition I made with my Lord? I made a condition with my Lord and said: ‘I am only a human being. I become pleased as human beings become pleased, and I become angry as human beings become angry. So whichever person from my Ummah I supplicate against with a supplication which he does not deserve, make it for him a purification, a cleansing, and a means of nearness by which You bring him close to You on the Day of Resurrection.’” Abū Maʿn narrated the word: “yutayyimah” in the diminutive form in all three places in the ḥadīth."],[1529,"Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: The Prophet ﷺ prayed until his feet became swollen. It was said to him: “Do you exert yourself to this extent when Allah has forgiven you what preceded of your sin and what followed?” He replied: “Should I not then be a grateful servant?”"],[1530,"Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them, said: I heard ʿUmar may Allah be pleased with him say from the pulpit: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “Do not exaggerate in praising me as the Christians exaggerated in praising the son of Mary. I am only His servant, so say: ‘The servant of Allah and His Messenger.’”"],[1531,"Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: A man said: “O Muḥammad, O best of us and son of the best of us; O our master and son of our master.” He replied: “Say what you ordinarily say, but do not let Satan — or the devils — draw you beyond the proper limit. I am Muḥammad, the servant of Allah and His Messenger. I do not like for you to raise me above the rank in which Allah has placed me.”"],[1532,"Al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O people, do not raise me above my proper rank, for Allah made me a servant before He made me a Prophet.”"],[1533,"Muṭarrif ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr reported: My father, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I went with the delegation of Banū ʿĀmir to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we said: “You are our master.” He replied: “The Master is Allah, Blessed and Exalted.” We said: “And you are the greatest of us in virtue and the greatest of us in generosity.” He replied: “Say what you ordinarily say, or some of what you ordinarily say, but do not let Satan draw you beyond the proper limit.”"],[1534,"Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: The Prophet ﷺ noticed that Thābit ibn Qays was absent. A man said: “O Messenger of Allah, I can find out what has happened to him.” He went to him and found him sitting in his house with his head lowered. He asked: “What is wrong with you?” Thābit replied: “Something terrible! I used to raise my voice above the voice of the Prophet ﷺ, so my deeds have been nullified and I am among the people of the Fire!” The man returned to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him of what Thābit had said. Mūsā ibn Anas said:\nThe man returned to him the final time carrying tremendous glad tidings, saying: “Go to him and tell him: ‘You are not among the people of the Fire. Rather, you are among the people of Paradise.’”"],[1535,"Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: When the following verse was revealed: “O you who believe, do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet, nor speak loudly to him as you speak loudly to one another, lest your deeds become worthless while you do not realize it.” [Sūrat al-Ḥujurāt 49:2] Thābit ibn Qays, may Allah be pleased with him, said: “By Allah, I am the one who used to raise my voice in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I fear that I am among the people of the Fire.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Rather, he is among the people of Paradise.”\nAnas said: Afterward, we would see him walking among us as a man from the people of Paradise."],[1536,"قال الله تعالى: ﴿اَلَّذِيْنَ اٰتَيْنٰهُمُ الْكِتٰبَ مِنْ قَبْلِهٖ هُمْ بِهٖ يُؤْمِنُوْنَ۰۰۵۲ وَ اِذَا يُتْلٰى عَلَيْهِمْ قَالُوْۤا اٰمَنَّا بِهٖۤ اِنَّهُ الْحَقُّ مِنْ رَّبِّنَاۤ اِنَّا كُنَّا مِنْ قَبْلِهٖ مُسْلِمِيْنَ۰۰۵۳ اُولٰٓىِٕكَ يُؤْتَوْنَ اَجْرَهُمْ مَّرَّتَيْنِ بِمَا صَبَرُوْا وَ يَدْرَءُوْنَ بِالْحَسَنَةِ السَّيِّئَةَ وَ مِمَّا رَزَقْنٰهُمْ يُنْفِقُوْنَ۰۰۵۴﴾ [سورة القصص: 52-54]."],[1537,"Allah the Exalted said: Those to whom We gave the Book before it, they believe in it. And when it is recited to them, they say: “We believe in it; indeed, it is the truth from our Lord. Indeed, even before it we were Muslims.” These are the ones who will be given their reward twice, because they were patient, and they repel evil with good, and spend from what We have provided for them. [Sūrat al-Qaṣaṣ: 52-54]"],[1538,"Abū Sufyān, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The letter of the Prophet ﷺ to Heraclius contained: “In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful. From Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Heraclius, the ruler of the Romans. Peace be upon whoever follows guidance. To proceed: I invite you with the call of Islam. Accept Islam and you will be safe. Accept Islam and Allah will give you your reward twice. But if you turn away, then upon you will be the sin of the Arīsiyyīn. ‘Say: O People of the Scripture, come to a word that is equitable between us and you: that we worship none except Allah, associate nothing with Him, and that none of us take others as lords besides Allah. But if they turn away, then say: Bear witness that we are Muslims.’” [Āl ʿImrān 3:64]"],[1539,"Abū Mūsā may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Three people will be given their reward twice: A man who owns a slave woman, teaches her and perfects her education, disciplines her and perfects her upbringing, then frees her and marries her — he will have two rewards; a believer from the People of the Scripture who was a believer and then believed in the Prophet ﷺ — he will have two rewards; and a slave who fulfills the right of Allah and is sincere toward his master.” Al-Shaʿbī then said: “I have given this to you for nothing, while a man would once travel to Madinah for something less than this.”"],[1540,"Anas ibn Mālik may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I will come to the gate of Paradise on the Day of Resurrection and ask for it to be opened. The keeper will ask: ‘Who are you?’ I will reply: ‘Muḥammad.’ He will say: ‘I was commanded concerning you; I am not to open it for anyone before you.’”"],[1541,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was sent with concise yet comprehensive speech, and I was aided by fear cast into the enemy. While I was asleep, the keys to the treasuries of the earth were brought to me and placed in my hand.” Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has passed away, and now you are drawing forth those treasures.”"],[1542,"قال الله تعالى: ﴿وَ كَمْ مِّنْ مَّلَكٍ فِي السَّمٰوٰتِ لَا تُغْنِيْ شَفَاعَتُهُمْ شَيْـًٔا اِلَّا مِنْۢ بَعْدِ اَنْ يَّاْذَنَ اللّٰهُ لِمَنْ يَّشَآءُ وَ يَرْضٰى ۰۰۲۶﴾ [سورة النّجم: 26].\nوقال تعالى: ﴿قُلْ لِّلّٰهِ الشَّفَاعَةُ جَمِيْعًا١ؕ﴾ [سورة الزّمر: 44].\nوقال تعالى: ﴿مَنْ ذَا الَّذِيْ يَشْفَعُ عِنْدَهٗۤ اِلَّا بِاِذْنِهٖ١ؕ﴾ [سورة البقرة: 255].\nوقال تعالى:﴿وَ لَا يَشْفَعُوْنَ١ۙ اِلَّا لِمَنِ ارْتَضٰى﴾ [الأنبياء: 28]."],[1543,"Allah the Exalted says: And how many an angel there is in the heavens whose intercession is of no benefit at all, except after Allah gives permission for whom He wills and approves. [Sūrat al-Najm: 26]\nAllah the Exalted further says: (Say: All intercession belongs to Allah alone. [Sūrat al-Zumar: 44]\nAnd He said: Who is there that can intercede with Him except by His permission? [Sūrat al-Baqarah: 255]\nLikewise it was said: And they do not intercede except for one with whom He is pleased. [Sūrat al-Anbiyā’: 28]\nTwo conditions of intercession: It is known from these verses that two conditions are necessary for intercession:\n1- Allah’s permission: Allah gives the intercessor permission to intercede.\n2- Allah’s pleasure: Allah is pleased with the person for whom intercession is made.\nThis intercession is specific to the people of tawḥīd (monotheism), and the *polytheists* will be deprived of it, as He said: They will not have the power of intercession, except one who has taken a covenant with the Most Merciful. [Sūrat Maryam: 87]\nAllah the Exalted further said: So the intercession of the intercessors will not benefit them. [Sūrat al-Muddaththir: 48]\nAnd He said: And there will be no intercessors among their partners, rather they will deny their partners. [Sūrat al-Rūm: 13]\nThe three special intercessions of the Prophet ﷺ: Allah the Exalted has granted the noble Prophet ﷺ three special intercessions on the Day of Resurrection:\n1- The Greatest Intercession (al-Shafāʿah al-ʿUẓmā): This is the Praised Station (al-Maqām al-Maḥmūd), in which the Prophet ﷺ will intercede for all of humanity so that judgment may be made between them. All the prophets (Ādam, Nūḥ, Ibrāhīm, Mūsā, ʿĪsā, peace be upon them) will excuse themselves from interceding at that time, and the noble Prophet ﷺ will intercede at this station.\n2- Intercession for the people of Paradise: The Prophet ﷺ will intercede for the people of Paradise that they may enter Paradise after the reckoning.\n3- Intercession for Abū Ṭālib: The Prophet ﷺ interceded for the lightening of the punishment of his uncle Abū Ṭālib, by which his punishment was made lighter.\nOther intercessions: The Prophet ﷺ will also intercede in the following matters: for sinful people of tawḥīd who deserve the Fire, that they not enter the Fire; the removal from the Fire of those sinful people of tawḥīd who have already entered it; and intercession for raising the ranks of the people of Paradise. This last kind of intercession will also be made by other prophets, angels, the ṣiddīqūn (the truthful) and the martyrs.\nFurther study: For details on this topic, see: [al-ʿAqīdah al-Wāsiṭiyyah by Shaykh al-Islām Ibn Taymiyyah (pp. 156-159)] - commentary: Shaykh Dr. Ṣāliḥ al-Fawzān"],[1544,"Anas may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I will be the first of the people to intercede concerning Paradise, and I will have the greatest following among the prophets.” In another narration:\n“I will have the greatest following among the prophets on the Day of Resurrection, and I will be the first to knock at the gate of Paradise.”\nAnd in another narration: “I will be the first intercessor concerning Paradise. No prophet among the prophets was believed by as many people as believed me, and among the prophets was one whom only a single man from his nation believed.”"],[1545,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I am the leader of the children of Adam on the Day of Resurrection, the first for whom the grave will be split open, the first to intercede, and the first whose intercession will be accepted.”"],[1546,"Ubayy ibn Kaʿb may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “On the Day of Resurrection, I will be the leader of the prophets, their spokesman, and the possessor of their intercession—without boasting.”"],[1547,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Every prophet has a supplication which he makes, and I wish to reserve my supplication as intercession for my Ummah in the Hereafter.”"],[1548,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Every prophet has an answered supplication. Each prophet hastened to use his supplication, whereas I have reserved my supplication as intercession for my Ummah on the Day of Resurrection. It will reach, if Allah wills, whoever from my Ummah dies without associating anything with Allah.”"],[1549,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that he said to Kaʿb al-Aḥbār: The Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: “Every prophet has a supplication that he makes, and I intend, if Allah wills, to reserve my supplication as intercession for my Ummah on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1550,"Abū Hurayrah and Kaʿb met. Abū Hurayrah began narrating to Kaʿb from the Prophet ﷺ, while Kaʿb narrated to Abū Hurayrah from the earlier scriptures. Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Prophet ﷺ said: “Every prophet has an answered supplication, and I have reserved my supplication as intercession for my Ummah on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1551,"Anas may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Every prophet made a request—or he said: every prophet had a supplication which he made and which was answered—and I made my supplication an intercession for my Ummah on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1552,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Every prophet had a supplication which he made concerning his nation, and I have reserved my supplication as intercession for my Ummah on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1553,"Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I was in the mosque when a man entered and prayed. He recited in a manner that I objected to. Then another man entered and recited differently from the first. When we finished the prayer, we all went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I said: “This man recited in a manner that I objected to, and then another came and recited differently from him.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed both of them to recite, so they recited, and the Prophet ﷺ approved the recitation of both. Then there arose within me a degree of doubt such as had not occurred to me even during the Days of Ignorance. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw what had overcome me, he struck me upon the chest. I broke into a sweat, and it was as though I were looking toward Allah, Mighty and Majestic, out of intense fear. He said to me: “O Ubayy, I was instructed to recite the Qurʾān in one ḥarf, so I replied: ‘Make it easier for my Ummah.’ Then I was instructed a second time: ‘Recite it in two aḥruf.’ I replied: ‘Make it easier for my Ummah.’ Then I was instructed a third time: ‘Recite it in seven aḥruf. For every time you have been sent back with a response, you have a request which you may ask of Me.’ So I said: ‘O Allah, forgive my Ummah. O Allah, forgive my Ummah.’ And I postponed the third request until a Day when all of creation will turn to me seeking it, even Abraham, peace be upon him.”"],[1554,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿAqīl reported: We set out and came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and made our mounts kneel at his door. At first, there was no one more disliked by us than a person who entered upon him, yet by the time we left, there was no one more beloved to us than one who entered upon him. One of us asked: “O Messenger of Allah, why did you not ask your Lord for a kingdom like the kingdom of Solomon?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed and said: “Perhaps your companion possesses something with Allah better than the kingdom of Solomon. Allah never sent a prophet except that He gave him a supplication. Some of them used it for something of this world and were granted it, while others supplicated against their people when they disobeyed them and their people were destroyed through it. Allah has given me a supplication, and I have reserved it with my Lord as intercession for my Ummah on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1555,"Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: One day meat was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The foreleg was presented to him, and he liked it, so he took a bite from it and said: “I am the leader of mankind on the Day of Resurrection. Do you know why? Allah will gather the first and the last on the Day of Resurrection upon one level plain, so that the caller can be heard by all of them and the eye can encompass them. The sun will draw near, and the people will experience such grief and distress that they will be unable to bear or endure it. Some people will say to others: ‘Do you not see the state you are in? Do you not see what has befallen you? Will you not look for someone to intercede for you with your Lord?’ Some of them will say: ‘Go to Adam.’ They will come to Adam and say: ‘O Adam, you are the father of mankind. Allah created you with His Hand, breathed into you of His spirit, and commanded the angels to prostrate to you. Intercede for us with your Lord. Do you not see the state we are in? Do you not see what has befallen us?’ Adam will say: ‘Indeed, my Lord is angry today with an anger the like of which He has never been angry before, and the like of which He will never be angry afterward. He forbade me from the tree, but I disobeyed Him. Myself! Myself! Go to someone else. Go to Noah.’ They will come to Noah and say: ‘O Noah, you are the first messenger sent to the people of the earth, and Allah named you a grateful servant. Intercede for us with your Lord. Do you not see the state we are in? Do you not see what has befallen us?’ He will say: ‘Indeed, my Lord is angry today with an anger the like of which He has never been angry before, and the like of which He will never be angry afterward. I had a supplication which I used against my people. Myself! Myself! Go to Abraham.’ They will come to Abraham and say: ‘You are the Prophet of Allah and His close friend among the people of the earth. Intercede for us with your Lord. Do you not see the state we are in? Do you not see what has befallen us?’ Abraham will say: ‘Indeed, my Lord is angry today with an anger the like of which He has never been angry before, and the like of which He will never be angry afterward.’ He will mention the statements he made, then say: ‘Myself! Myself! Go to someone else. Go to Moses.’ They will come to Moses and say: ‘O Moses, you are the Messenger of Allah. Allah favored you above the people with His messages and by speaking directly to you. Intercede for us with your Lord. Do you not see the state we are in? Do you not see what has befallen us?’ Moses will say: ‘Indeed, my Lord is angry today with an anger the like of which He has never been angry before, and the like of which He will never be angry afterward. I killed a soul which I had not been commanded to kill. Myself! Myself! Go to Jesus.’ They will come to Jesus and say: ‘O Jesus, you are the Messenger of Allah. You spoke to the people in the cradle, you are His Word which He cast to Mary, and a spirit from Him. Intercede for us with your Lord. Do you not see the state we are in? Do you not see what has befallen us?’ Jesus will say: ‘Indeed, my Lord is angry today with an anger the like of which He has never been angry before, and the like of which He will never be angry afterward.’ He will not mention any sin for himself. ‘Myself! Myself! Go to someone else. Go to Muḥammad ﷺ.’ They will come to me and say: ‘O Muḥammad, you are the Messenger of Allah and the Seal of the Prophets. Allah has forgiven you what preceded of your sin and what followed. Intercede for us with your Lord. Do you not see the state we are in? Do you not see what has befallen us?’ So I will set out and come beneath the Throne, then fall in prostration before my Lord. Allah will then open for me and inspire me with praises of Him and beautiful commendation such as He had never opened for anyone before me. Then it will be said: ‘O Muḥammad, raise your head. Ask and you will be given. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will raise my head and say: ‘My Lord, my Ummah! My Ummah!’ It will be said: ‘O Muḥammad, admit into Paradise from your Ummah those who will have no reckoning through the right-hand gate of Paradise. They will also share with the rest of the people in the other gates.’ By the One in whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad, the distance between the two sides of a gate of Paradise is like the distance between Makkah and Hajar, or like the distance between Makkah and Buṣrā.”"],[1556,"Maʿbad ibn Hilāl al-ʿAnazī reported: We set out to visit Anas ibn Mālik may Allah be pleased with him and asked Thābit to intercede for us with him. We reached him while he was praying Ḍuḥā. Thābit sought permission for us, so we entered, and Anas seated Thābit with him upon his bed. Thābit said: “O Abū Ḥamzah, your brothers from the people of Baṣrah ask you to narrate to them the ḥadīth of intercession.” Anas said: Muḥammad ﷺ narrated to us: “On the Day of Resurrection, the people will surge against one another. They will come to Adam and say: ‘Intercede for your descendants.’ He will reply: ‘I am not suited for it. Go instead to Abraham, peace be upon him,, for he is the close friend of Allah.’ They will come to Abraham, who will say: ‘I am not suited for it. Go instead to Moses, peace be upon him,, for he is the one to whom Allah spoke.’ Moses will be approached and will say: ‘I am not suited for it. Go instead to Jesus, peace be upon him,, for he is a spirit from Allah and His Word.’ Jesus will be approached and will say: ‘I am not suited for it. Go instead to Muḥammad ﷺ.’ Then I will be approached, and I will say: ‘I am for it.’ I will set out and seek permission to enter upon my Lord, and permission will be granted to me. I will stand before Him and praise Him with praises which I am unable to produce now; rather, Allah will inspire them to me. Then I will fall in prostration before Him. It will be said to me: ‘O Muḥammad, raise your head. Speak and you will be heard. Ask and you will be given. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will say: ‘My Lord, my Ummah! My Ummah!’ It will be said: ‘Go. Whoever has in his heart the weight of a grain of wheat or barley of faith, bring him out from it.’ So I will go and do so. Then I will return to my Lord, praise Him with those praises, and fall in prostration before Him. It will be said to me: ‘O Muḥammad, raise your head. Speak and you will be heard. Ask and you will be given. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will say: ‘My Ummah! My Ummah!’ It will be said to me: ‘Go. Whoever has in his heart the weight of a mustard seed of faith, bring him out from it.’ So I will go and do so. Then I will return to my Lord and praise Him with those praises, then fall in prostration before Him. It will be said to me: ‘O Muḥammad, raise your head. Speak and you will be heard. Ask and you will be given. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will say: ‘My Lord, my Ummah! My Ummah!’ It will be said to me: ‘Go. Whoever has in his heart the slightest, the slightest, the slightest amount less than the weight of a mustard seed of faith, bring him out of the Fire.’ So I will go and do so.” Maʿbad said: This was the ḥadīth of Anas which he narrated to us. We left him, and when we reached the outskirts of the graveyard we said:\n“If only we went to al-Ḥasan.” We greeted him while he was hiding in the house of Abū Khalīfah. We entered and greeted him, then said: “O Abū Saʿīd, we have just come from your brother Abū Ḥamzah, and we have never heard anything like the ḥadīth he narrated to us concerning intercession.” He said: “Continue!” So we narrated the ḥadīth to him. He said: “Continue!” We replied: “He did not add anything more.” He said: “He narrated this to us twenty years ago, when he was still fully strong in body and memory, and he certainly omitted something. I do not know whether the shaykh forgot it or disliked narrating it to you lest you become complacent.” We said: “Then narrate it to us.” He laughed and recited: “Man was created of haste.” [Sūrat al-Anbiyāʾ 21:37] He said: “I only mentioned this because I intended to narrate it to you.” Then he continued: “Then I will return to my Lord a fourth time, praise Him with those praises, and fall in prostration before Him. It will be said to me: ‘O Muḥammad, raise your head. Speak and you will be heard. Ask and you will be given. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will say: ‘My Lord, give me permission regarding whoever said: There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah.’ He will say: ‘That is not for you,’ or He said, ‘That is not up to you. But by My Might, My Majesty, My Greatness, and My Supremacy, I will surely bring out whoever said: There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah.’”"],[1557,"Anas may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “The believers will gather on the Day of Resurrection and say: ‘If only we sought someone to intercede for us with our Lord.’ They will come to Adam and say: ‘You are the father of mankind. Allah created you with His Hand, caused His angels to prostrate to you, and taught you the names of all things. Intercede for us with your Lord so that He may relieve us from this place of ours.’ He will say: ‘I am not suited for that.’ He will mention his sin and become ashamed, then say: ‘Go to Noah, for he was the first messenger Allah sent to the people of the earth.’ They will come to him, and he will say: ‘I am not suited for that.’ He will mention that he asked his Lord concerning that of which he had no knowledge, and he will become ashamed. He will say: ‘Go to the close friend of the Most Merciful.’ They will come to Abraham, and he will say: ‘I am not suited for that. Go to Moses, a servant to whom Allah spoke and to whom He gave the Torah.’ They will come to him, and he will say: ‘I am not suited for that.’ He will mention that he killed a person without that person having killed another, and he will become ashamed before his Lord. He will say: ‘Go to Jesus, the servant and Messenger of Allah, His Word and a spirit from Him.’ Jesus will say: ‘I am not suited for that. Go to Muḥammad ﷺ, a servant whose former and latter sins Allah has forgiven.’ They will come to me. I will set out until I seek permission from my Lord, and permission will be granted to me. When I see my Lord, I will fall in prostration, and He will leave me in that state for as long as Allah wills. Then it will be said: ‘Raise your head. Ask and you will be given. Speak and you will be heard. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will raise my head and praise Him with a praise which He will teach me. Then I will intercede, and He will set a limit for me, and I will admit those people into Paradise. Then I will return to Him, and when I see my Lord, the same will occur. Then I will intercede again, and He will set a limit for me, and I will admit them into Paradise. Then I will return a fourth time and say: ‘None remains in the Fire except those whom the Qurʾān has confined therein and for whom everlasting punishment has become obligatory.’”\nAbū ʿAbdullāh said: The words: “Except those whom the Qurʾān has confined” refer to the statement of Allah Most High: “Abiding therein forever.”"],[1558,"Anas may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The Day of Resurrection will be prolonged for the people, so some of them will say to others: ‘Come, let us go to Adam, the father of mankind, so that he may intercede for us with our Lord, Mighty and Majestic, and He may judge between us.’ They will come to Adam and say: ‘O Adam, you are the one whom Allah created with His Hand and settled in His Paradise. Intercede for us with your Lord so that He may judge between us.’ He will say: ‘I am not suited for that. Rather, go to Noah, the foremost of the prophets.’ They will come to him and say: ‘O Noah, intercede for us with your Lord so that He may judge between us.’ He will reply: ‘I am not suited for that. Rather, go to Abraham, the close friend of Allah, Mighty and Majestic.’ They will come to him and say: ‘O Abraham, intercede for us with your Lord so that He may judge between us.’ He will say: ‘I am not suited for that. Rather, go to Moses, whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, chose with His messages and His speech.’ They will come to him and say: ‘O Moses, intercede for us with your Lord, Mighty and Majestic, so that He may judge between us.’ He will say: ‘I am not suited for that. Rather, go to Jesus, the Spirit of Allah and His Word.’ They will come to Jesus and say: ‘O Jesus, intercede for us with your Lord so that He may judge between us.’ He will say: ‘I am not suited for that. Rather, go to Muḥammad ﷺ, for he is the Seal of the Prophets, and he has been forgiven what preceded of his sin and what followed.’ Jesus will say: ‘Tell me: if some goods were inside a vessel that had been sealed, could anyone reach what was inside until the seal was broken?’ They will reply: ‘No.’ He will say: ‘Indeed, Muḥammad ﷺ is the Seal of the Prophets.’ The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “They will come to me and say: ‘O Muḥammad, intercede for us with your Lord so that He may judge between us.’ I will say: ‘Yes.’ I will come to the gate of Paradise, take hold of its ring, and ask for it to be opened. It will be asked: ‘Who are you?’ I will reply: ‘Muḥammad.’ It will be opened for me, and I will fall in prostration. I will praise my Lord, Mighty and Majestic, with praises with which no one before me praised Him and no one after me will praise Him. He will say: ‘Raise your head. Speak and you will be heard. Ask and you will be given. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will say: ‘My Lord, my Ummah! My Ummah!’ It will be said: ‘Bring out whoever has in his heart the weight of a grain of barley of faith.’ So I will bring them out. Then I will fall in prostration again and praise Him with praises with which no one before me praised Him and no one after me will praise Him. It will be said to me: ‘Raise your head. Ask and you will be given. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will say: ‘My Lord, my Ummah! My Ummah!’ It will be said: ‘Bring out whoever has in his heart the weight of a grain of wheat of faith.’ So I will bring them out. Then I will fall in prostration again and say likewise. It will be said: ‘Bring out whoever has in his heart the weight of an atom of faith.’ So I will bring them out.”"],[1559,"Anas ibn Mālik may Allah be pleased with him reported that the prophets, peace be upon them, were mentioned in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: “By the One in whose Hand is my soul, I will indeed be the leader of mankind on the Day of Resurrection—without boasting. In my hand will be the Banner of Praise, beneath which will be Adam peace be upon him and all those after him—without boasting. On that Day Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will call: ‘Adam!’ He will reply: ‘Here I am, my Lord, and at Your service.’ He will say: ‘Bring forth from your descendants the contingent of the Fire.’ Adam will ask: ‘What is the contingent of the Fire?’ He will say: ‘From every thousand, nine hundred and ninety-nine.’ There will come forth a number known only to Allah, Mighty and Majestic. They will come to Adam peace be upon him and say: ‘You are Adam. Allah honored you, created you with His Hand, breathed into you from His spirit, settled you in His Paradise, and commanded the angels to prostrate to you. Intercede for your descendants so that they will not be burned in the Fire today.’ He will say: ‘That is not for me today, but I will direct you to a servant whom Allah took as a close friend. I will go with you.’ They will come to Abraham, peace be upon him, and say: ‘O Abraham, you are a servant whom Allah took as a close friend. Intercede for the descendants of Adam so that they will not be burned in the Fire today.’ He will say: ‘That is not for me, but I will direct you to a servant whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, chose with His speech and His messages and upon whom He cast love from Himself: Moses. I will go with you.’ They will come to Moses and say: ‘O Moses, you are a servant whom Allah chose with His messages and His speech and upon whom He cast love from Himself. Intercede for the descendants of Adam so that they will not be burned in the Fire.’ He will say: ‘That is not for me today, but I will direct you to the Spirit of Allah and His Word: Jesus son of Mary.’ They will come to Jesus son of Mary peace be upon him and say: ‘O Jesus, you are the Spirit of Allah and His Word. Intercede for the descendants of Adam so that they will not be burned in the Fire today.’ He will say: ‘That is not for me today. Go to a servant whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, made a mercy to the worlds: Aḥmad. I will go with you.’ They will come to me and say: ‘O Aḥmad, Allah made you a mercy to the worlds. Intercede for the descendants of Adam so that we will not be burned in the Fire today.’ I will say: ‘Yes, I am the one for it.’ I will come and take hold of the ring of the gate of Paradise. It will be asked: ‘Who is this?’ I will reply: ‘I am Aḥmad.’ It will be opened for me. When I look toward al-Jabbār, Blessed and Exalted, I will fall in prostration. Then there will be opened for me forms of praise and glorification of the Lord, Mighty and Majestic, which creation cannot adequately perform. Then it will be said: ‘Ask and you will be given. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will say: ‘My Lord, let the descendants of Adam not be burned in the Fire today.’ He will say: ‘Go. Whoever you find with the weight of a dinar of faith in his heart, bring him out.’ Then they will return to me, saying: ‘The descendants of Adam—let them not be burned in the Fire today.’ I will again come and take hold of the ring of the gate of Paradise. It will be asked: ‘Who is this?’ I will reply: ‘Aḥmad.’ It will be opened for me. When I look toward al-Jabbār, Blessed and Exalted, I will fall in prostration, like my first prostration, and similar praise and glorification will be opened for me as was opened the first time. It will be said: ‘Raise your head. Ask and you will be given. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will say: ‘My Lord, let the descendants of Adam not be burned in the Fire today.’ He will say: ‘Bring out for him whoever has in his heart the weight of a qīrāṭ of faith.’ Then they will return to me, and I will do as I did before. When I look toward al-Jabbār, Mighty and Majestic, I will fall in prostration, prostrating as I did the first time, and similar praise and glorification will be opened for me. Then it will be said: ‘Ask and you will be given. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will say: ‘My Lord, let the descendants of Adam not be burned in the Fire today.’ He will say: ‘Go. Whoever you find with the weight of an atom of faith in his heart, bring him out.’ They will bring out a number known only to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, while most will remain. Then Adam will be given permission to intercede, and he will intercede for ten million. Then the angels and prophets will be permitted to intercede, and they will intercede, until even a believer will intercede for more people than Rabīʿah and Muḍar.”"],[1560,"Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Prophet of Allah ﷺ told me: “I will be standing, waiting for my Ummah to cross the Ṣirāṭ, when Jesus will come to me and say: ‘These prophets have come to you, O Muḥammad, asking you—or he said: gathering around you—and supplicating to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, to separate the assembled nations as He wills because of the anguish they are experiencing. The creation will be bridled in sweat. As for the believer, it will affect him like a cold, while the disbeliever will be overwhelmed by death.’ I will say: ‘O Jesus, wait until I return to you.’ Then the Prophet of Allah ﷺ will go until he stands beneath the Throne, where he will encounter that which no chosen angel and no sent prophet has encountered. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will reveal to Jibrīl: ‘Go to Muḥammad and tell him: Raise your head. Ask and you will be given. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will intercede for my Ummah so that one person out of every ninety-nine is brought out. I will continue returning to my Lord, Mighty and Majestic, and will not stand at any station except that I intercede, until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, grants me such that He says: ‘O Muḥammad, admit from your Ummah, from among Allah’s creation, whoever sincerely testified for even one day that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and died upon that.’”"],[1561,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have been given five things which no prophet before me was given.” Among them he mentioned: “And I have been given intercession.”"],[1562,"Abū Dharr may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was given five things which no prophet before me was given.” Among them he mentioned that it was said to him: “Ask, and you will be given.” He said: “So I reserved it as intercession for my Ummah. It will reach—if Allah wills—whoever among you meets Allah without associating anything with Him.”"],[1563,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them, reported: During the year of the expedition of Tabūk, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood during the night in prayer, while men from among his Companions gathered behind him to guard him. When he had finished praying and turned toward them, he said: “Tonight I have been given five things which no one before me was given.” He mentioned the lengthy ḥadīth, including: “And the fifth is what it is: it was said to me: ‘Ask, for every prophet has asked.’ So I postponed my request until the Day of Resurrection. It is for you and for whoever testifies that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah.”"],[1564,"Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: One morning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed the Fajr prayer and then remained seated. When the forenoon came, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed. He remained in his place until he prayed Ẓuhr, ʿAṣr, and Maghrib, without speaking throughout that time. Then he prayed the final ʿIshāʾ prayer and went to his family. The people said to Abū Bakr: “Will you not ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what happened to him? Today he did something he had never done before.” So he asked him, and the Prophet ﷺ said: “Yes. I was shown what will occur concerning the affairs of this world and the Hereafter. The first and the last were gathered on a single plain, and the people were terrified by that until they went to Adam peace be upon him, while sweat was nearly reaching their mouths. They said: ‘O Adam, you are the father of mankind, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, chose you. Intercede for us with your Lord.’ He will say: ‘I have encountered what you have encountered. Go to your father after your father—to Noah: “Indeed, Allah chose Adam and Noah and the family of Abraham and the family of ʿImrān over the worlds.”’ [Āl ʿImrān 3:33] They will go to Noah, peace be upon him, and say: ‘Intercede for us with your Lord. Allah chose you and answered your supplication, and He did not leave upon the earth a single dwelling disbeliever.’ He will say: ‘That is not for me. Go to Abraham, peace be upon him,, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, took him as a close friend.’ They will go to Abraham, and he will say: ‘That is not for me. Rather, go to Moses, peace be upon him,, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, spoke directly to him.’ Moses peace be upon him will say: ‘That is not for me. Rather, go to Jesus son of Mary, for he cured the blind from birth and the leper and brought the dead to life.’ Jesus will say: ‘That is not for me. Rather, go to the leader of the children of Adam. He will be the first for whom the earth will split open on the Day of Resurrection. Go to Muḥammad ﷺ, and he will intercede for you with your Lord, Mighty and Majestic.’ Then he will proceed, and Jibrīl, peace be upon him, will come to his Lord. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will say: ‘Admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise.’ Jibrīl will take him, and he will fall prostrate for the duration of a week. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will say: ‘Raise your head, O Muḥammad. Speak and you will be heard; intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ He will raise his head, but when he looks toward his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, he will fall prostrate for another week. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will say: ‘Raise your head. Speak and you will be heard; intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ When he is about to fall prostrate again, Jibrīl, peace be upon him, will take him by his upper arms, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will open for him words of supplication which He had never opened for any human being before. He will say: ‘My Lord, You created me as the leader of the children of Adam—and there is no boasting—and the first for whom the earth will split open on the Day of Resurrection—and there is no boasting. Indeed, more people will come to my Basin than the distance between Ṣanʿāʾ and Aylah.’ Then it will be said: ‘Call the truthful ones.’ They will intercede. Then it will be said: ‘Call the prophets.’ A prophet will come accompanied by a group; another prophet will come with five or six people; and another prophet will come with no one. Then it will be said: ‘Call the martyrs.’ They will intercede for whomever they wish. When the martyrs have done that, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will say: ‘I am the Most Merciful of those who show mercy. Admit into My Paradise everyone who did not associate anything with Me.’ So they will enter Paradise. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will say: ‘Look into the Fire. Do you find anyone who ever performed any good deed?’ They will find a man in the Fire and ask him: ‘Did you ever perform any good deed?’ He will reply: ‘No, except that I used to be lenient with people in buying and selling.’ Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will say: ‘Show leniency to My servant just as he used to show leniency to My servants.’ Then they will bring another man out of the Fire and ask him:\n‘Did you ever perform any good deed?’ He will reply: ‘No, except that I instructed my children: when I die, burn me with fire, then grind me until I become like kohl, then take me to the sea and scatter me in the wind. By Allah, the Lord of the worlds will never be able to overpower me.’ Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will say: ‘Why did you do that?’ He will reply: ‘Out of fear of You.’ Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will say: ‘Look at the kingdom of the greatest king. You shall have its like and ten times as much.’ He will say: ‘Why are You making fun of me when You are the King?’ The Prophet ﷺ said: ‘That is what I laughed about in the forenoon.’”"],[1565,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, may Allah be pleased with them, reported: The Prophet ﷺ recited the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, concerning Abraham, peace be upon him: “My Lord, indeed they have led many people astray. So whoever follows me is of me, and whoever disobeys me—then indeed You are Most Forgiving, Most Merciful.” [Sūrat Ibrāhīm 14:36] Then he raised his hands and said: “O Allah, my Ummah! My Ummah!” And he wept. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: “O Jibrīl, go to Muḥammad—and your Lord knows best—and ask him what makes him weep.” Jibrīl, peace be upon him, came to him and asked him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed him of what he had said—though Allah knows best. Then Allah said: “O Jibrīl, go to Muḥammad and say: ‘Indeed, We will please you concerning your Ummah and will not distress you.’”"],[1566,"Allah, Blessed and Exalted, has promised His Prophet ﷺ this station, as in His saying, Mighty and Majestic:\nTranslation: It may be that your Lord will raise you to a Praised Station. [Sūrat al-Isrā’: 79].\nThe people of knowledge said: “ʿAsā” (“it may be”) from Allah is definitely obligatory (a certainty); it is not based on doubt or hesitation."],[1567,"Ibn ʿUmar may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “A man will continue asking people until he comes on the Day of Resurrection with no piece of flesh remaining on his face.” He also said:\n“The sun will draw near on the Day of Resurrection until the sweat reaches halfway up the ear. While they are in that state, they will seek help from Adam, then Moses, then Muḥammad ﷺ. He will intercede so that judgment may be passed among creation.\nHe will proceed until he takes hold of the ring of the gate. On that Day Allah will raise him to the Praised Station, for which all the gathered people will praise him.”"],[1568,"Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, said: “On the Day of Resurrection, the people will become kneeling groups, every nation following its prophet and saying: ‘O so-and-so, intercede!’ Until the intercession reaches the Prophet ﷺ. That is the Day on which Allah will raise him to the Praised Station.”"],[1569,"Kaʿb ibn Mālik may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The people will be resurrected on the Day of Resurrection, and I and my Ummah will be upon a hill. My Lord, Blessed and Exalted, will clothe me in a green garment. Then permission will be granted to me, and I will say whatever Allah wills me to say. That is the Praised Station.”"],[1570,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The earth will be stretched out on the Day of Resurrection because of the greatness of al-Raḥmān. Then no human being from among the children of Adam will have more than the place occupied by his two feet. Then I will be the first of the people to be called, and I will fall in prostration. Then permission will be granted to me, and I will say: ‘My Lord, this one’—meaning Jibrīl, who will be on the right of al-Raḥmān, and by Allah, Jibrīl had never seen Him before that—‘informed me that You sent him to me.’ Jibrīl will remain silent and will not speak until Allah says: ‘He has spoken the truth.’ Then permission will be granted to me to intercede, and I will say: ‘My Lord, Your servants worshipped You throughout the farthest regions of the earth.’ That is the Praised Station.”"],[1571,"It is narrated from Sayyidunā ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām, may Allah be pleased with him, that: When the Day of Resurrection comes, your Prophet ﷺ will be brought and made to sit on His Kursī before Allah, Blessed and Exalted. (ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām) said to Abū Masʿūd (that is, al-Jurayrī): “When he is on the Kursī, he will be with Him.” (It was said): “This is the clearest ḥadīth before my eyes in this world.”\nRuling on the narration: Its chain is weak. It was narrated by Ibn Abī ʿĀṣim in al-Sunnah (786), al-Khallāl in al-Sunnah (237), al-Ājurrī in al-Sharīʿah (1097), and Ibn Jarīr in his Tafsīr. All of them transmitted it through the chain of Yaḥyā ibn Kathīr Abū Ghassān al-ʿAnbarī, who narrated from Salm ibn Jaʿfar, from Saʿīd al-Jurayrī, from Sayf al-Sadūsī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām. In it there are narrators who are unknown.\nStatement of Imām al-Dhahabī: Al-Dhahabī said in al-ʿUluww (203): This narration is mawqūf and its chain is not established.\nStatement of Imām al-Ḥākim: However, al-Ḥākim narrated it by another route (4/568-569) and said: It is in a long ḥadīth narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām, in which it says: “A Kursī will be set up for him on the right side of Allah.”\nThen he said: Its chain is ṣaḥīḥ, and al-Bukhārī and Muslim did not narrate it. It is not mawqūf but musnad, because ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām (who is an early Companion) mentioned the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in other places too. But the correct view is the opposite of this.\nLikewise, what is narrated from Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, is also not authentic: “I was with the Prophet ﷺ and I recited the Qur’an before him, until I reached this verse: ﴿عَسٰۤى اَنْ يَّبْعَثَكَ رَبُّكَ مَقَامًا مَّحْمُوْدًا۰۰۷۹﴾ (Sūrat al-Isrā’: 79)\nHe said: ‘I will be seated on the Throne.’”\nStatement of Imām al-Dhahabī: Al-Dhahabī mentioned it in al-ʿUluww (202) through the chain of Salamah al-Aḥmar, from Ashʿath ibn Ṭalīq, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, and said: This ḥadīth is munkar and one cannot be pleased with it. Salamah is matrūk al-ḥadīth (abandoned in ḥadīth), and Ashʿath did not meet Ibn Masʿūd.\nCriticism of the narrators: Salamah (Salamah ibn Ṣāliḥ al-Aḥmar al-Juʿfī al-Kūfī) has been declared weak by the critics (imams of criticism).\nThis narration is also not authentic from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, whether as marfūʿ or mawqūf:\nAl-Dhahabī stated in al-ʿUluww (329): Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, Jaʿfar, al-Salafī, ʿAlī ibn Bayān, Bushrā al-Fātinī, ʿUmar ibn Sabnak the judge, al-Ḥurr ibn Muḥammad ibn Ashkāb, ʿUmar ibn Mudrik al-Rāzī, Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm, Juwaybir, al-Ḍaḥḥāk, from Ibn ʿAbbās, narrated concerning: ﴿عَسٰۤى اَنْ يَّبْعَثَكَ رَبُّكَ مَقَامًا مَّحْمُوْدًا۰۰۷۹﴾ (Sūrat al-Isrā’: 79)\nThat: He will be seated on the Throne."],[1572,"Imām al-Dhahabī said: Its chain is defective (sāqiṭ), and this ʿUmar al-Rāzī is matrūk, and in it there is also Juwaybir—Imām al-Dhahabī said just this and passed no ruling on him, but he too is matrūk, as Imām al-Nasāʾī and al-Dāraquṭnī said that he is matrūk.\nThen Imām al-Dhahabī said: This statement is well-known from Mujāhid, and it has also been narrated as marfūʿ, but it is false (bāṭil).\nI say: It is narrated from Mujāhid through several routes that regarding ﴿عَسٰۤى اَنْ يَّبْعَثَكَ رَبُّكَ مَقَامًا مَّحْمُوْدًا۰۰۷۹﴾ he said: Allah will seat him with Himself on His Throne.\nAl-Ājurrī narrated it in al-Sharīʿah (4/1614-1617), al-Khallāl in al-Sunnah (1/212-213), and Ibn Jarīr in his Tafsīr,\nand all their chains are weak or broken (munqaṭiʿ), and none of them is a marfūʿ narration.\nImām al-Qurṭubī said in al-Tadhkirah (2/605) after mentioning the statement of Mujāhid: This is a disliked statement, and even if it were authentic, its interpretation would be that Allah will seat him with His prophets and angels.\nIbn ʿAbd al-Barr said in al-Tamhīd (7/175) after quoting the statement of Mujāhid: Regarding the saying of Allah ﴿وُجُوْهٌ يَّوْمَىِٕذٍ نَّاضِرَةٌۙ۰۰۲۲﴾ [Sūrat al-Qiyāmah: 22] he said: They will be awaiting reward, and this is not in the sense of looking. Although Mujāhid is distinguished in the exegesis of the Qur’an, he has two statements regarding the interpretation of two verses which are unacceptable and disliked according to the scholars.\nThe first statement is this one, and the second statement is about the saying of Allah ﴿عَسٰۤى اَنْ يَّبْعَثَكَ رَبُّكَ مَقَامًا مَّحْمُوْدًا۰۰۷۹﴾: that Allah will enlarge the Throne and seat him with Himself. And this statement contradicts the group of the Companions and those after them.\nThus the scholars have a consensus regarding the interpretation of this verse that the Praised Station (al-Maqām al-Maḥmūd) means the intercession.\nThe theoretical discussion on this issue is lengthy. End of quote.\nAl-Ḥāfiẓ al-Dhahabī said in al-ʿUluww (2/1081): As for the Prophet’s ﷺ sitting on the Throne, no text is established regarding it; rather, there is a very weak ḥadīth in this chapter.\nAnd the interpretation Mujāhid gave of this verse, as mentioned, was rejected by some of the people of kalām. Al-Marwazī stood up and sat down, and went to excess in supporting this statement, compiled a book, and transmitted the various routes of the statement of Mujāhid.\nHe said: Among those in that era who gave the fatwa that this athar should be accepted and not opposed were Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī, the author of al-Sunan, Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥarbī and others.\nEven the son of Imām Aḥmad said regarding the statement of Mujāhid: I regard as evil every person who denies this ḥadīth, and in my view he is an immoral person and under suspicion. I heard this narration from many people, and I did not see any muḥaddith deny it. And in our land only the Jahmiyyah deny it.\nThen he said: The faqīh Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Sulaymān al-Najjād, the muḥaddith, as narrated by Qāḍī Abū Yaʿlā al-Farrāʾ, said: If a man were to swear by three divorces that Allah will seat Muhammad ﷺ on the Throne, and ask me for a fatwa, I would say: You have spoken the truth and fulfilled your oath.\nImām al-Dhahabī commented on this, saying: See—may Allah protect you from desires—how the excess of this muḥaddith reached the point of obligating the acceptance of a munkar athar.\nAnd today the clear ḥadīths that indicate Allah’s highness are being denied, while some people of little knowledge are trying to reject the saying of Allah: ﴿اَلرَّحْمٰنُ عَلَى الْعَرْشِ اسْتَوٰى ۰۰۵﴾ [Sūrat Ṭā Hā: 5]."],[1573,"Shaykh al-Albānī, may Allah have mercy on him, says:\nAnd among the wonders before which the intellect is left astonished is that some of the earlier scholars gave a fatwa on this athar of Mujāhid, as Imām al-Dhahabī mentioned. Then he mentioned the aforementioned narration. See: al-Ḍaʿīfah (865).\nI say: If the matter is as Ibn ʿAbd al-Barr, al-Qurṭubī, al-Dhahabī and other early and contemporary people of knowledge have said, then the statement of Imām al-Ājurrī would not be acceptable: As for the ḥadīth of Mujāhid, concerning the virtue of the noble Prophet ﷺ and the interpretation of this verse that Allah will seat him on the Throne, this ḥadīth was received in the best manner by the shaykhs, the people of knowledge and transmission, and they took it with the finest acceptance, and did not deny it. Rather, they strongly denounced the one who rejects this ḥadīth and said: Whoever denies the ḥadīth of Mujāhid is an evil man. End of quote.\nFor matters of the unseen are not established by weak and mawqūf narrations. And it is established by authentic marfūʿ and mawqūf narrations that the Praised Station is the Greatest Intercession. So it is obligatory to adopt this, and this is what the group of the Companions and the Followers said, who had more knowledge than Mujāhid and others. Indeed, even from Mujāhid himself it is reported that the Praised Station is the intercession.\nIbn Jarīr narrated it in his Tafsīr (15/45) from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr, who said: Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us, who said: ʿĪsā narrated to us, and al-Ḥārith narrated to me, who said: al-Ḥasan narrated to us, who said: Warqāʾ narrated to us, all from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, and he from Mujāhid, who said regarding the saying of Allah the Exalted: ﴿عَسٰۤى اَنْ يَّبْعَثَكَ رَبُّكَ مَقَامًا مَّحْمُوْدًا۰۰۷۹﴾: The intercession of Muhammad ﷺ on the Day of Resurrection.\nAnd as for those who adopted the statement of Mujāhid, perhaps they said it to enrage the Jahmiyyah, because they refuse to accept that there is anything on the Throne. As Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī did, when he narrated it from Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā al-Rāzī, who said:\nMuḥammad ibn Fuḍayl narrated to us, from Layth, and he from Mujāhid, regarding the saying of Allah the Exalted: ﴿عَسٰۤى اَنْ يَّبْعَثَكَ رَبُّكَ مَقَامًا مَّحْمُوْدًا۰۰۷۹﴾, he said: Allah will seat him on His Throne.\nAbū Dāwūd said: Whoever denies it is under suspicion in our view. And he said: People have always narrated this, in order to enrage the Jahmiyyah, because the Jahmiyyah refuse to accept that there is anything on the Throne.\nImām al-Khallāl narrated it in al-Sunnah (244), and in its chain is Layth, who is Ibn Abī Sulaym, who is ṣadūq, but his memory deteriorated at the end, and his ḥadīth became indistinguishable, so it was abandoned. But if this narration were established, there would be no harm in accepting this report, as Imām Ibn Jarīr al-Ṭabarī said, although he stated clearly that the Praised Station is the intercession. He says: And this, although it is the correct interpretation of the verse: ﴿عَسٰۤى اَنْ يَّبْعَثَكَ رَبُّكَ مَقَامًا مَّحْمُوْدًا۰۰۷۹﴾, as we mentioned from the narrations transmitted from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, his Companions and the Followers, yet Mujāhid’s statement that Allah will seat Muhammad ﷺ on His Throne is a statement whose soundness cannot be denied, neither in terms of report nor in terms of reasoning. For its denial is not established from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, his Companions, or the Followers. End of quote."],[1574,"Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: It was asked: “O Messenger of Allah, who will be the happiest of people to receive your intercession on the Day of Resurrection?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “I thought, O Abū Hurayrah, that no one would ask me about this ḥadīth before you, because of what I have seen of your eagerness for ḥadīth. The happiest of people to receive my intercession on the Day of Resurrection will be the one who says: ‘There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah,’ sincerely from his heart or his soul.”"],[1575,"Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “On the Day of Resurrection, I will be granted intercession. I will say: ‘My Lord, admit into Paradise whoever has in his heart the weight of a mustard seed.’ So they will enter. Then I will say: ‘Admit into Paradise whoever has in his heart even the slightest amount.’”\nAnas said: “It is as though I can see the fingers of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[1576,"Anas ibn Mālik may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Some people will emerge from the Fire after its scorching has touched them. They will then enter Paradise, and the people of Paradise will call them al-Jahannamiyyūn.” In another narration: “Some people will certainly be touched by the scorching of the Fire because of sins they committed, as punishment. Then Allah will admit them into Paradise by the favor of His mercy, and they will be called al-Jahannamiyyūn.”"],[1577,"Abū Saʿīd may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “As for the people of the Fire who are its true inhabitants, they will neither die therein nor live. But there will be people whom the Fire afflicts because of their sins—or he said: because of their offenses—and it will cause them to die completely. When they have become charcoal, permission will be granted for intercession. They will then be brought forth in groups upon groups and scattered along the rivers of Paradise. Then it will be said: ‘O people of Paradise, pour water over them.’ Thereupon they will grow as a seed grows in the sediment carried by a flood.”\nA man among the people said: “It is as though the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had lived in the desert.”"],[1578,"Jābir may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Through intercession, people will emerge from the Fire as though they were al-thaʿārīr.” I asked: “What are al-thaʿārīr?” He replied: “Al-ḍaghābīs.” His teeth had already fallen out. I said to ʿAmr ibn Dīnār: “O Abū Muḥammad, did you hear Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh say: ‘I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: Through intercession, people will emerge from the Fire’?” He replied: “Yes.”"],[1579,"Yazīd al-Faqīr reported: I had become deeply attracted to one of the views of the Khawārij, so a sizeable group of us set out intending to perform Ḥajj and afterward rise against the people. We passed through Madinah and found Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh may Allah be pleased with them sitting beside a pillar, narrating to the people from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He mentioned al-Jahannamiyyūn. I said to him: “O Companion of the Messenger of Allah, what is this that you narrate? Allah says: ‘Our Lord, indeed whoever You admit into the Fire, You have certainly disgraced him, and the wrongdoers will have no helpers.’ [Āl ʿImrān 3:192] And: ‘As for those who defiantly disobeyed, their refuge will be the Fire. Whenever they wish to emerge from it, they will be returned to it, and it will be said to them: Taste the punishment of the Fire which you used to deny.’ [Sūrat al-Sajdah 32:20] So what is this that you are saying?” He asked: “Do you recite the Qurʾān?” I replied: “Yes.” He asked: “Have you heard of the Station of Muḥammad ﷺ”—meaning the station to which Allah will raise him? I replied: “Yes.” He said: “That is the Praised Station of Muḥammad ﷺ, through which Allah will bring out whomever He brings out.” He then described the placing of the Ṣirāṭ and the people passing over it. Yazīd said: “I fear that I did not preserve that portion precisely.” He continued: “However, he stated that people will emerge from the Fire after having been in it. They will emerge as though they were sesame stalks. They will enter a river from among the rivers of Paradise and wash in it, then emerge looking like sheets of paper.” We left and said:\n“Woe to you! Do you think this elderly Companion would lie about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” So we abandoned our former view. By Allah, none of us later went out in rebellion except one man—or as Abū Nuʿaym narrated."],[1580,"ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “People will emerge from the Fire through the intercession of Muḥammad ﷺ. They will then enter Paradise and will be called al-Jahannamiyyūn.”"],[1581,"Anas may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “My intercession is for the people who committed major sins from my Ummah.”"],[1582,"Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Indeed, my intercession on the Day of Resurrection is for those from my Ummah who committed major sins.”"],[1583,"Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I asked: “O Messenger of Allah, what about the intercession?” He replied: “The intercession is for those from my Ummah who committed major sins.”"],[1584,"ʿAwf ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stopped to rest one night, and every man among us used the foreleg of his mount as a pillow. Later in the night, I came to where the she-camel of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was, but there was no one in front of her. So I went looking for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and found Muʿādh ibn Jabal and ʿAbdullāh ibn Qays standing there. I asked: “Where is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” They replied: “We do not know, except that we heard a sound from the upper part of the valley, like the humming of saddle gear.” He said: “Remain here for a little while.” Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to us and said: “Tonight a visitor from my Lord came to me and gave me a choice between half of my Ummah entering Paradise and intercession, so I chose intercession.” We said: “We implore you by Allah and by our companionship with you to make us among those who receive your intercession.” He said: “You are indeed among those who will receive my intercession.” We then hurried back toward the people and found that they had become alarmed because they had lost sight of their Prophet. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Tonight a visitor from my Lord came to me and gave me a choice between half of my Ummah entering Paradise and intercession, so I chose intercession.” They said: “O Messenger of Allah, we implore you by Allah and by our companionship with you to make us among those who receive your intercession.” When they crowded around him, he said: “I call you to witness that my intercession is for whoever does not associate anything with Allah.”"],[1585,"ʿAwf ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey and stopped for the night. I got up looking for the Prophet ﷺ but could not find him. I found Muʿādh ibn Jabal and Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī. They asked: “What do you need?” I said: “Where is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?” They replied: “We do not know.” While we were in that state, we heard from the upper part of the valley a rumbling like the rumbling of a millstone. It was not long before the Prophet ﷺ came. We said: “O Messenger of Allah, we missed you tonight.” He replied: “A visitor from my Lord came to me and gave me a choice between my Ummah being half of the people of Paradise and intercession, so I chose intercession.” We said: “O Prophet of Allah, supplicate to Allah to make us among those who receive the intercession.” He said: “O Allah, make them among its people.” Then we went to the people and informed them. They said: “O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to make us among those who receive your intercession.” He said: “O Allah, make them among its people.” Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I call you to witness that my intercession is for everyone who dies without associating anything with Allah.”"],[1586,"Abū Mūsā, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one night. He stopped to rest and we rested as well. He said: “A visitor came after you from your Lord and gave me a choice between half of my Ummah entering Paradise and intercession, so I chose intercession.” We said: “O Messenger of Allah, make us among those for whom you intercede.” He replied: “You are among them.” We asked: “Should we not give the people glad tidings of this, O Messenger of Allah?” The men rushed toward him, and when many had gathered around the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he said: “It is for everyone who dies without associating anything with Allah.”"],[1587,"Ibn Dārah, the freedman of ʿUthmān, reported: We were in al-Baqīʿ with Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him when we heard him say: “I am the most knowledgeable of people concerning the intercession of Muḥammad ﷺ on the Day of Resurrection.” The people gathered closely around him and said: “Continue—may Allah have mercy upon you!” He said that the Prophet ﷺ says: “O Allah, forgive every Muslim servant who meets You believing in me and not associating anything with You.”"],[1588,"Abū Mūsā, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: The Prophet ﷺ used to be guarded by his Companions. I got up one night and did not find him in his sleeping place, so I became deeply worried and went looking for him. I then encountered Muʿādh, who had experienced the same concern as I had. We heard a sound like the humming of a millstone, so we remained where we were. The Prophet ﷺ came from the direction of the sound and said: “Do you know where I was and what I was doing? A visitor from my Lord, Mighty and Majestic, came to me and gave me a choice between half of my Ummah entering Paradise and intercession, so I chose intercession.” They said: “O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, to include us in your intercession.” He said: “You, and whoever dies without associating anything with Allah, are included in my intercession.”"],[1589,"Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “I will indeed be the first of the people for whom the earth is split open from my skull on the Day of Resurrection—without boasting. I will be given the Banner of Praise—without boasting. I will be the leader of mankind on the Day of Resurrection—without boasting. And I will be the first to enter Paradise on the Day of Resurrection—without boasting. I will come to the gate of Paradise and take hold of its ring. They will ask: ‘Who is this?’ I will reply: ‘I am Muḥammad.’ They will open it for me and I will enter. Then al-Jabbār will be before me, and I will prostrate before Him. He will say: ‘Raise your head, O Muḥammad. Speak and you will be heard. Say something and it will be accepted from you. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will raise my head and say: ‘My Ummah! My Ummah, O Lord!’ He will say: ‘Go to your Ummah. Whoever you find with the weight of a grain of barley of faith in his heart, admit him into Paradise.’ So I will go forward, and whoever I find with that in his heart, I will admit him into Paradise. Then al-Jabbār will be before me, and I will prostrate before Him. He will say: ‘Raise your head, O Muḥammad. Speak and you will be heard. Say something and it will be accepted from you. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will raise my head and say: ‘My Ummah! My Ummah, O my Lord!’ He will say: ‘Go to your Ummah. Whoever you find with half a grain of barley of faith in his heart, admit them into Paradise.’ So I will go, and whoever I find with that amount in his heart, I will admit them into Paradise. Then al-Jabbār will be before me, and I will prostrate before Him. He will say: ‘Raise your head, O Muḥammad. Speak and you will be heard. Say something and it will be accepted from you. Intercede and your intercession will be accepted.’ I will raise my head and say: ‘My Ummah! My Ummah!’ He will say: ‘Go to your Ummah. Whoever you find with the weight of a mustard seed of faith in his heart, admit him into Paradise.’ So I will go, and whoever I find with that amount in his heart, I will admit them into Paradise. Allah will then finish judging the people, and whoever remains from my Ummah will be admitted into the Fire with the people of the Fire. The people of the Fire will say: ‘What benefit did it bring you that you used to worship Allah, Mighty and Majestic, without associating anything with Him?’ Then al-Jabbār will say: ‘By My Might, I will surely free them from the Fire.’ So they will be brought out after having been burned. They will enter the River of Life and grow therein just as a seed grows in the debris carried by a flood. Between their eyes will be written: ‘These are the freed servants of Allah.’ Then they will be taken away and admitted into Paradise. The people of Paradise will say: ‘These are the Jahannamiyyūn.’ But al-Jabbār will say: ‘Rather, these are those whom al-Jabbār has freed.’”"],[1590,"Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I asked the Prophet ﷺ to intercede for me on the Day of Resurrection. He said: “I will do so.” I asked: “O Messenger of Allah, where should I look for you?” He said: “First look for me at the Ṣirāṭ.” I asked: “If I do not find you at the Ṣirāṭ?” He replied: “Then look for me at the Scale.” I asked: “If I do not find you at the Scale?” He replied: “Then look for me at the Basin, for I will not be absent from these three places.”"],[1591,"Umm Ḥabībah may Allah be pleased with her reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I was shown what my Ummah will encounter after me, including some of them shedding the blood of others. That had already been decreed by Allah, just as it had been decreed for the nations before them. So I asked Him to grant me intercession concerning them on the Day of Resurrection, and He did so.”"],[1592,"Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, said: We continued refraining from seeking forgiveness for people who committed major sins until we heard our Prophet ﷺ say: “Indeed, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, does not forgive associating partners with Him, but He forgives whatever is less than that for whomever He wills.” He said: “I have postponed my intercession for those from my Ummah who committed major sins until the Day of Resurrection.” Thereafter, we restrained ourselves from much of what had previously been in our minds concerning them."],[1593,"Anas may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “I will continue interceding before my Lord, and He will continue accepting my intercession, until I say: ‘My Lord, allow me to intercede for whoever said: There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah.’ He will say: ‘This is not for you, O Muḥammad. Rather, this belongs to Me. By My Might, My Forbearance, and My Mercy, I will not leave in the Fire anyone—or he said: any servant—who said: There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah.’”"],[1594,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever says when he hears the call to prayer: ‘O Allah, Lord of this complete call and the established prayer, grant Muḥammad al-Wasīlah and al-Faḍīlah, and raise him to the Praised Station which You have promised him,’ my intercession will become due for him on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1595,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Whoever among you is able to die in Madinah, let him die there, for I will intercede for whoever dies there.”"],[1596,"Ṣumaytah—a woman from Banū Layth who was under the care of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ—said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Whoever among you is able to die in Madinah, let him die there, for whoever dies there, I will intercede for him on the Day of Resurrection and bear witness for him.”"],[1597,"Subayʿah bint al-Ḥārith al-Aslamiyyah may Allah be pleased with her reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever among you is able to die in Madinah, let him die there, because no one dies there except that I will be an intercessor for him or a witness for him on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1598,"Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “No one patiently endures its hardships and severity except that I will be an intercessor for him or a witness for him on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1599,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “No one patiently endures its hardships and then dies except that I will be an intercessor for him or a witness for him on the Day of Resurrection, provided that he was a Muslim.”"],[1600,"Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Madinah is better for them, if only they knew. No one leaves it out of aversion to it except that Allah replaces him in it with someone better than him. And no one remains steadfast through its hardships and difficulties except that I will be an intercessor for him or a witness for him on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1601,"Abū Hurayrah may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “No one from my Ummah patiently endures the hardships and severity of Madinah except that I will be an intercessor for him on the Day of Resurrection or a witness for him.”"],[1602,"Abū Saʿīd may Allah be pleased with him reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ, when his uncle was mentioned in his presence, say: “Perhaps my intercession will benefit him on the Day of Resurrection, so he will be placed in a shallow part of the Fire reaching his ankles, because of which his brain will boil.”"],[1603,"Al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib may Allah be pleased with him said to the Prophet ﷺ: “What benefit have you brought to your uncle? He used to protect you and become angry on your behalf.” He replied: “He is in a shallow part of the Fire. Were it not for me, he would have been in the lowest depth of the Fire.”"],[1604,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have been given five things which were not given to any prophet before me: I have been aided by fear cast into the enemy from the distance of a month’s journey; the earth has been made for me a place of prayer and a means of purification, so wherever a man from my Ummah is when the time for prayer reaches him, let him pray; the spoils of war have been made lawful for me; a prophet used to be sent specifically to his own people, whereas I have been sent to all mankind; and I have been granted intercession.”"],[1605,"Abū Dharr may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I was given five things which no prophet before me was given: I was aided by fear, so that the enemy fears me from the distance of a month’s journey; the earth was made for me a place of prayer and a means of purification; the spoils of war were made lawful for me, though they had not been made lawful for anyone before me; I was sent to the red and the black; and it was said to me: ‘Ask, and you will be given.’ So I reserved it as intercession for my Ummah. It will reach — if Allah wills — whoever among you meets Allah without associating anything with Him.”"],[1606,"ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb narrated from his father, from his grandfather: During the year of the expedition of Tabūk, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood in prayer during the night. Men from among his Companions gathered behind him to guard him. When he finished praying and turned toward them, he said: “Tonight I have been given five things which no one before me was given: As for me, I have been sent universally to all people, whereas those before me were sent only to their own peoples; I have been granted victory over the enemy through fear, and even if there were a month’s journey between me and them, they would be filled with fear of me; the spoils of war have been made lawful for me to consume, whereas those before me regarded consuming them as a grave matter and would burn them; the earth has been made for me a place of prayer and a means of purification. Wherever prayer reaches me, I perform tayammum and pray, whereas those before me regarded this as a grave matter and would pray only in their churches and places of worship; and as for the fifth — it is what it is: it was said to me: ‘Ask, for every prophet has asked.’ So I postponed my request until the Day of Resurrection. It is for you and for whoever testifies that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah.”"],[1607,"Ibn ʿAbbās may Allah be pleased with them reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have been given five things which no prophet before me was given, and I do not say this boastfully: I was sent to all mankind, the red and the black; I was aided by fear cast into the enemy from the distance of a month’s journey; the spoils of war were made lawful for me, though they had not been lawful for anyone before me; the earth was made for me a place of prayer and a means of purification; and I was given intercession. I postponed it for my Ummah, and it is for whoever does not associate anything with Allah.”"],[1608,"Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Two categories from my Ummah will not be reached by my intercession: a tyrannical ruler, and every extremist who departs from the proper path.”"],[1609,"Maʿqil ibn Yasār may Allah be pleased with him reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Two men will not be reached by my intercession: a tyrannical and hostile ruler, and another who goes to extremes in religion and departs from it.”"],[1610,"Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Those who frequently curse others will not be witnesses or intercessors on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1611,"Rabīʿah ibn Kaʿb al-Aslamī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I used to spend the night with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and would bring him water for his ablution and whatever else he needed. He said to me: “Ask.” I said: “I ask you for your companionship in Paradise.” He asked: “Or anything other than that?” I replied: “That is what I want.” He said: “Then help me concerning yourself by making abundant prostrations.”"],[1612,"A servant of the Prophet ﷺ, either a man or a woman, reported: Among the things the Prophet ﷺ would say to the servant was: “Do you have any need?” Then one day the servant said: “O Messenger of Allah, I have a need.” He asked: “What is your need?” The servant replied: “My need is that you intercede for me on the Day of Resurrection.” He asked: “Who directed you to this?” The servant replied: “My Lord.” He said: “Then, if that is what you seek, help me through abundant prostration.”"],[1613,"Rabīʿah ibn Kaʿb, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “Ask me, and I will give you.” I said: “O Messenger of Allah, give me time to consider my matter.” He said: “Then consider your matter.” I reflected and said: “The affair of this world will come to an end, so I see nothing better than something I can take for myself for my Hereafter.” I then entered upon the Prophet ﷺ, and he asked: “What is your need?” I replied: “O Messenger of Allah, intercede for me with your Lord, Mighty and Majestic, so that He may free me from the Fire.” He asked: “Who instructed you to ask for this?” I replied: “No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, no one instructed me. But I considered my situation and saw that this world is passing away from its people, so I wished to obtain something for my Hereafter.” He said: “Then help me concerning yourself by making abundant prostrations.”"],[1614,"Imām al-Bayhaqī, may Allah have mercy on him, mentioned in al-Dalāʾil (1/10) from some of the people of knowledge that the proofs of the prophethood of the noble Prophet ﷺ reach nearly one thousand.\nHere I am mentioning only a few; the remaining proofs are mentioned in different parts of the book under different chapters. I am not mentioning them here, in order to avoid repetition and length."],[1615,"Khālid ibn Maʿdān reported from some Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that they said, “O Messenger of Allah, tell us about yourself.” He replied: “I am the answer to the supplication of my forefather Abraham and the glad tidings given by Jesus—peace be upon them both. While my mother was carrying me, she saw a light emerge from her by which the palaces of Buṣrā in the land of Syria were illuminated. “I was then placed in the care of a wet nurse among Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr. While I was with a foster brother of mine among some young sheep belonging to us, two men dressed in white came to me. They had with them a golden basin filled with snow. They laid me down, opened my abdomen, removed my heart and opened it. They took a black clot from it and cast it away. They then washed my heart and abdomen with that snow until they had thoroughly cleansed them, after which they restored my heart as it had been. “One of them then said to the other, ‘Weigh him against ten members of his nation.’ He weighed me against ten, and I outweighed them. He then said, ‘Weigh him against one hundred members of his nation.’ He weighed me against one hundred, and I outweighed them. He then said, ‘Weigh him against one thousand members of his nation.’ He weighed me against one thousand, and I outweighed them. “He then said, ‘Leave him, for if you weighed him against his entire nation, he would outweigh them.’”"],[1616,"ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbd al-Sulamī related that a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, “How did your story first begin, O Messenger of Allah?” He replied: “My nurse belonged to Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr. Her son and I went out among some young sheep belonging to us, but we took no provisions with us. I said, ‘My brother, go and bring us some provisions from our mother.’ My brother departed while I remained with the sheep. “Two white figures resembling eagles approached. One said to the other, ‘Is this he?’ He replied, ‘Yes.’ They rushed toward me, took hold of me, laid me flat upon my back, and opened my abdomen. They then removed my heart and opened it, extracting two black clots from it. “One of them said to the other—as Yazīd’s narration states—‘Bring me snow-water.’ They washed my abdomen with it. He then said, ‘Bring me hail-water,’ and they washed my heart with it. He then said, ‘Bring me serenity,’ and they sprinkled it into my heart. One of them then said to the other, ‘Stitch it.’ He stitched it and sealed it with the Seal of Prophethood.” Ḥaywah’s narration states: “Stitch it.” He stitched it and was told, “Seal it with the Seal of Prophethood.” The Prophet ﷺ continued: “One of them then said to the other, ‘Place him in one pan of the scale and place one thousand members of his nation in the other.’ I could see the thousand above me and feared that some of them might fall upon me. He said, ‘If his entire nation were weighed against him, he would outweigh them.’ “They then departed and left me. I became extremely frightened, so I went to my nurse and told her what had happened to me. She feared that something might have been inflicted upon me and said, ‘I seek Allah’s protection for you.’ “She prepared her camel, placed me upon the saddle—or, according to Yazīd, carried me upon it—and rode behind me until we reached my mother. She said, ‘I have fulfilled my trust and my obligation,’ and told her what had happened to me. “This did not alarm my mother. She said, ‘When I gave birth to him, I saw a light emerge from me by which the palaces of Syria were illuminated.’”"],[1617,"Al-ʿIrbāḍ ibn Sāriyah reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Indeed, I was recorded with Allah as the Seal of the Prophets while Adam—peace be upon him—was still lying in his clay. I shall tell you of the beginning of that: the supplication of my forefather Abraham, Jesus’s glad tidings concerning me, and the vision my mother saw. The mothers of the prophets likewise see such visions.”"],[1618,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “There was no prophet who was not given signs by which people would believe in him. What I was given was revelation that Allah revealed to me, so I hope to have the greatest number of followers on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1619,"Jābir ibn Samurah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I certainly know a stone in Makkah that used to greet me before I was commissioned as a prophet. I still know it now.”"],[1620,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said: The moon split into two parts during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ said: “Bear witness.”"],[1621,"Ibn Masʿūd said: The moon split while we were in Makkah. The disbelievers of Quraysh among the people of Makkah said, “This is magic with which the son of Abū Kabshah has bewitched us. Wait for the travelers who come to you. If they report that they saw what you saw, then it is true; otherwise, he has deceived you.” No traveler came to them from any direction without reporting that they had seen the same thing."],[1622,"Anas ibn Mālik related that the people of Makkah asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to show them a sign. He therefore showed them the moon split into two parts, until they could see Mount Ḥirāʾ between them."],[1623,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās reported that the moon split during the time of the Prophet ﷺ."],[1624,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said: The moon split during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Bear witness.”"],[1625,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported in the course of his lengthy hadith: We traveled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until we stopped in a wide valley. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to relieve himself, and I followed him carrying a vessel of water. He looked around but saw nothing with which he could conceal himself. There were two trees at the edge of the valley. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to one of them, took hold of one of its branches, and said: “Come under my command by Allah’s permission.” It came along with him like a haltered camel that complies with its handler. He then went to the other tree, took hold of one of its branches, and said: “Come under my command by Allah’s permission.” It came along with him likewise. When he reached the midpoint between them, he brought them together and said: “Join together over me by Allah’s permission.” So they joined together. Jābir said: I hurried away out of fear that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ might sense that I was nearby and move farther away—Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād said: “distance himself farther.” I sat speaking to myself, then happened to glance around and saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ approaching. The two trees had separated, and each had returned to stand upon its own trunk. I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stop briefly and move his head like this—Abū Ismāʿīl gestured by moving his head right and left—then he came toward me. When he reached me, he said: “O Jābir, did you see the place where I stood?” I replied: “Yes, O Messenger of Allah.” He said: “Go to the two trees, cut a branch from each of them, and bring them back. When you reach the place where I stood, put one branch on your right and the other on your left.” Jābir said: I got up, took a stone, broke and sharpened it until it became sharp, then went to the two trees and cut a branch from each. I dragged them back until I reached the place where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had stood, and I placed one branch on my right and the other on my left.\nThen I caught up with him and said: “I have done it, O Messenger of Allah. Why did you tell me to do that?” He said: “I passed by two graves whose occupants were being punished, and I wished, through my intercession, that their punishment might be lightened for as long as the two branches remain fresh”… the remainder of the hadith."],[1626,"Yaʿlā ibn Murrah said: I witnessed three things from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that no one before me had witnessed and no one after me would witness. I once traveled with him. While we were on part of the road, we passed a seated woman who had a young boy with her. She said, “O Messenger of Allah, this boy has been afflicted by a disorder, and we have suffered greatly because of it. He is seized by it I do not know how many times each day.” He said: “Hand him to me.” She lifted the boy to him, and he placed him between himself and the middle of the saddle. He then opened the boy’s mouth, blew lightly into it three times and said: “In the name of Allah. I am the servant of Allah. Be driven away, enemy of Allah!” He then returned the boy to her and said: “Meet us at this place on our return and tell us what happened to him.” We continued our journey and later returned. We found the woman at the same place with three sheep. He asked: “What happened to your boy?” She replied, “By the One Who sent you with the truth, we have not noticed anything affecting him up to this very moment. Take these sheep.” He said: “Dismount, take one of them, and return the others.” Yaʿlā continued: One day, I went with him toward the open land. When we had gone out into the open, he said: “Look around—may Allah rectify you—and see whether there is anything that can conceal me.” I replied, “I see nothing that could conceal you except a tree, but I do not think it can fully hide you.” He asked: “What is near it?” I replied, “Another tree like it, or close to its size.” He said: “Go to the two trees and tell them: ‘The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commands you both to come together by Allah’s permission.’” The two trees came together, and he went behind them to relieve himself. When he returned, he said: “Go to them and say: ‘The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commands each of you to return to its place.’” They both returned to their places. Yaʿlā continued: One day, while I was sitting with him, a camel came trotting toward him. It lowered the underside of its neck before him, and then its eyes began to shed tears. He said: “Go and see to whom this camel belongs, for it has a matter of concern.” I went out searching for its owner and discovered that it belonged to a man from the Anṣār. I summoned him to the Prophet ﷺ, who asked: “What is the matter with this camel of yours?” The man asked, “What is wrong with it?” He replied, “By Allah, I do not know what its problem is. We used it for work and for drawing water until it became unable to continue providing water. Last night we consulted one another and decided to slaughter it and divide its meat.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not do that. Give it to me as a gift or sell it to me.” The man replied, “Rather, it is yours, O Messenger of Allah.” The Prophet ﷺ marked it with the mark used for animals given in charity and then sent it away."],[1627,"Ibn ʿAbbās said: A Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked, “How can I know that you are a prophet?” He replied: “If I call this cluster of dates from this palm tree, and it testifies that I am the Messenger of Allah, will you believe?” The man replied, “Yes.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called it, and it began descending from the palm tree until it fell before the Prophet ﷺ. He then said: “Return.” It returned to its place, whereupon the Bedouin embraced Islam."],[1628,"Anas ibn Mālik said: Gabriel came to the Prophet ﷺ one day while he was sitting sorrowfully, stained with blood after some of the people of Makkah had struck him. Gabriel asked him, “What has happened to you?” He replied: “These people did such and such to me.” Gabriel—peace be upon him—asked, “Would you like me to show you a sign?” He replied: “Yes.” Gabriel looked toward a tree on the other side of the valley and said, “Call that tree.” The Prophet ﷺ called it, and it came walking until it stood before him. Gabriel said, “Command it to return.” He commanded it, and it returned to its place. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “That is sufficient for me.”"],[1629,"ʿImrān reported: We were traveling with the Prophet ﷺ and continued traveling by night until its final part. We then lay down and fell into a deep sleep—and no sleep is sweeter to a traveler than that. Nothing awakened us except the heat of the sun. The first to awaken was one man, then another, then another—Abū Rajāʾ used to name them, but ʿAwf forgot their names—and the fourth was ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. When the Prophet ﷺ slept, he would not be awakened until he awoke by himself, because we did not know what might be occurring to him in his sleep. When ʿUmar awoke and saw what had happened to the people—and he was a strong, resolute man—he proclaimed Allah’s greatness and raised his voice. He continued saying “Allāhu akbar” in a loud voice until the Prophet ﷺ awoke. When he awoke, they complained to him about what had happened. He said: “There is no harm—or: it will cause no harm. Resume your journey.” He traveled a short distance and then stopped. He called for water for ablution and performed ablution. The call to prayer was made, and he led the people in prayer. When he finished the prayer, he saw a man sitting apart who had not prayed with the people. He asked: “What prevented you, so-and-so, from praying with the people?” The man replied, “I was in a state of major ritual impurity, and there was no water.” He said: “Use the clean earth, for it is sufficient for you.” The Prophet ﷺ then resumed the journey. The people complained to him of thirst, so he stopped and summoned a man—Abū Rajāʾ used to name him, but ʿAwf forgot his name—and also summoned ʿAlī. He told them: “Go and search for water.” They departed and encountered a woman riding a camel between two large water-skins—or two leather water containers. They asked her, “Where is the water?” She replied, “The last time I was at the water was at this hour yesterday, and the men of our household are away.” They said, “Then come with us.” She asked, “Where?” They replied, “To the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” She asked, “The one who is called the Ṣābiʾ?” They replied, “He is the one you mean. Come with us.” They brought her to the Prophet ﷺ and told him what had happened. They helped her dismount from her camel, and the Prophet ﷺ called for a vessel. He poured some water into it from the mouths of the two water-skins—or containers—then tied their mouths and opened their lower spouts. A call was made among the people: “Give water to others and draw water for yourselves!” Everyone who wished was given water, and everyone who wished drew water. Finally, he gave the man who had been in a state of major ritual impurity a vessel of water and said: “Go and pour it over yourself.” The woman remained standing, watching what they were doing with her water. By Allah, when they stopped drawing from the water-skins, it appeared to us that they were even fuller than they had been at the beginning. The Prophet ﷺ then said: “Collect something for her.” They collected dates, flour, and roasted grain for her until they had gathered a quantity of food. They placed it in a cloth, lifted her onto her camel, and placed the bundle before her. He said to her: “You know that we have not diminished your water at all. Rather, it was Allah Who gave us water.” She returned to her people after having been delayed. They asked, “What detained you, so-and-so?” She replied, “Something astonishing! Two men met me and took me to the man called the Ṣābiʾ, and he did such and such. By Allah, either he is the greatest magician between these two”—she gestured with her middle and index fingers, raising them toward the sky, meaning between the heavens and earth—“or he truly is the Messenger of Allah.” Thereafter, the Muslims would raid the polytheistic tribes surrounding her people but would leave untouched the small community to which she belonged. One day she said to her people, “I think these people are deliberately sparing you. Would you consider accepting Islam?” They obeyed her and entered Islam."],[1630,"Jābir reported: The people became thirsty on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a small leather vessel before him and performed ablution from it. The people then approached him, so he asked: “What is the matter with you?” They replied, “O Messenger of Allah, we have no water with which to perform ablution or drink except what is in your vessel.” The Prophet ﷺ placed his hand in the vessel, and water began gushing from between his fingers like springs. We drank and performed ablution. I asked Jābir, “How many were you that day?” He replied, “Had we been one hundred thousand, it would have been sufficient for us. We were fifteen hundred.”"],[1631,"Al-Barāʾ said: You consider the conquest to be the conquest of Makkah—and the conquest of Makkah was indeed a conquest—but we regarded the true conquest as the Pledge of al-Riḍwān on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyah. We were fourteen hundred with the Prophet ﷺ. Al-Ḥudaybiyah had a well, and we drew all its water until we did not leave a single drop in it. News of this reached the Prophet ﷺ, so he came to the well and sat at its edge. He then called for a vessel of water, performed ablution, rinsed his mouth, supplicated, and poured the water into the well. We left it for a short while, after which it supplied all the water that we and our riding animals desired."],[1632,"Muʿādh ibn Jabal said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the campaign of Tabūk. He would combine the prayers, performing Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr together and Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ together. One day he delayed the prayer, then came out and performed Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr together. He went inside, then later emerged and performed Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ together. He then said: “Tomorrow, if Allah wills, you will reach the spring of Tabūk. You will not reach it until the morning has advanced. Whoever among you reaches it must not touch any of its water until I arrive.” When we reached it, two men had preceded us there. The spring was like a sandal strap, trickling only a small amount of water. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked them: “Did you touch any of its water?” They replied, “Yes.” The Prophet ﷺ rebuked them and said to them whatever Allah willed that he should say. The people then scooped small amounts of water from the spring with their hands until some had collected in a vessel. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ washed his hands and face in it and then poured it back into the spring. The spring began flowing with abundant, gushing water—Abū ʿAlī was uncertain which of the two expressions was used—until all the people drew water. He then said: “O Muʿādh, if you live a long life, you will soon see this entire place filled with gardens.”"],[1633,"Anas ibn Mālik said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when the time for the ʿAṣr prayer had arrived. The people searched for water with which to perform ablution but could not find any. A vessel containing a small amount of water was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He placed his hand in the vessel and then instructed the people to perform ablution from it. Anas said: I saw the water springing from beneath his fingers. The people continued performing ablution until the very last of them had done so."],[1634,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said: We used to regard miraculous signs as blessings, whereas you regard them as warnings that inspire fear. We were traveling with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when the water became scarce. He said: “Search for any remaining water.” They brought a vessel containing a small quantity of water. He placed his hand in the vessel and said: “Come to the blessed, purifying water. The blessing is from Allah.” I certainly saw water springing from between the fingers of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. We also used to hear the food glorifying Allah while it was being eaten."],[1635,"Jābir reported in the course of his lengthy hadith: We reached the encampment, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “O Jābir, call out for water for ablution.” So I called: “Is there any water for ablution? Is there any water for ablution? Is there any water for ablution?” I then said: “O Messenger of Allah, I have not found even a single drop among the people.” There was a man from the Anṣār who used to keep water cool for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in some leather water-skins placed upon a wooden frame. He said to me: “Go to so-and-so, the Anṣārī, and see whether there is anything in his water-skins.” I went to him and looked inside them, but found only a drop remaining in the spout of one of the water-skins; if I were to pour it out, the dry leather itself would absorb it. I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, I found only a drop in the spout of one of the water-skins. If I were to pour it out, the dry leather itself would absorb it.” He said: “Go and bring it to me.” So I brought it to him. He took it in his hand and began saying something that I could not make out while pressing it with his hands. Then he handed it to me and said: “O Jābir, call for a large basin.” I called out: “Bring the basin of the travelers!” It was brought, carried by people, and I placed it before him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed his hand in the basin like this, spreading his fingers apart, then put his hand at the bottom of the basin and said: “Take it, O Jābir. Pour upon my hand and say: ‘In the name of Allah.’” So I poured upon his hand and said: “In the name of Allah.” I saw water gush forth from between the fingers of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then the basin filled and overflowed until it was completely full. He said: “O Jābir, call everyone who has need of water.” The people came and drew water until they had all drunk their fill. I asked: “Is there anyone left who still needs water?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then lifted his hand from the basin while it was still full… the remainder of the hadith."],[1636,"Al-Barāʾ said: We numbered fourteen hundred on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyah. Al-Ḥudaybiyah had a well, and we drew from it until we did not leave a single drop. The Prophet ﷺ sat at the edge of the well, called for some water, rinsed his mouth, and spat the water into the well. We waited only a short while and then drew water until we had drunk our fill and our riding animals had also drunk their fill—or departed fully refreshed."],[1637,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ said: We arrived at al-Ḥudaybiyah with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, numbering fourteen hundred. Its well could not provide sufficient water even for fifty sheep. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat upon the raised stonework surrounding the well. He either supplicated or spat into it, whereupon the water surged forth. We gave water to our animals and drew water for ourselves."],[1638,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: Abū Ṭalḥah said to Umm Sulaym, “I have heard the voice of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sounding weak, and I recognize hunger in it. Do you have any food?” She replied, “Yes.” She brought out some small loaves of barley bread, took a head-covering of hers and wrapped part of it around the bread. She then concealed the bread under my arm, wrapped the remaining part of the cloth around me, and sent me to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I took it and found the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sitting in the mosque with other people. I stood before them, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Did Abū Ṭalḥah send you?” I replied, “Yes.” He asked: “Because of food?” I replied, “Yes.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said to those with him: “Get up.” He set out, and I went ahead of them until I reached Abū Ṭalḥah and informed him. Abū Ṭalḥah said, “Umm Sulaym, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has brought the people, but we do not have enough food to feed them.” She replied, “Allah and His Messenger know best.” Abū Ṭalḥah went out to meet the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came accompanied by Abū Ṭalḥah, and they entered the house. He said: “Bring whatever you have, Umm Sulaym.” She brought the bread. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered that it be crumbled, and Umm Sulaym squeezed clarified butter from a leather container over it to season it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said whatever Allah willed him to say and instructed: “Admit ten people.” Ten were admitted, and they ate until they were satisfied and then left. He again said, “Admit ten people.” They entered, ate until they were satisfied, and departed. He continued admitting them in groups of ten until all the people had eaten their fill. They numbered seventy or eighty men."],[1639,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ married and consummated his marriage. My mother, Umm Sulaym, prepared ḥays—a mixture of dates, clarified butter, and dried curds—and placed it in a stone vessel. She said, “Anas, take this to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and say: ‘My mother has sent this to you. She conveys her greetings of peace and says: This is only a small offering from us, O Messenger of Allah.’” I took it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “My mother conveys her greetings of peace and says: ‘This is only a small offering from us, O Messenger of Allah.’” He said: “Set it down.” He then said: “Go and invite so-and-so, so-and-so, and so-and-so, as well as anyone else you meet.” He named several men. I invited those whom he named and everyone else I encountered. The narrator asked Anas, “Approximately how many were there?” He replied, “Around three hundred.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “O Anas, bring the vessel.” The people entered until the shaded platform and the room were filled. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Let them form circles of ten, and let each person eat from what is directly before him.” They ate until they were satisfied. One group departed and another entered until everyone had eaten. He then said to me: “O Anas, remove it.” I removed the vessel, but I could not tell whether it had contained more food when I first set it down or when I lifted it afterward. Some groups remained sitting and conversing in the house of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was seated, while his wife had turned her face toward the wall. Their prolonged stay became burdensome for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he went out, greeted his other wives, and then returned.\nWhen they saw that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had returned, they realized that their presence had become burdensome to him. They hurried toward the door, and all of them departed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came, lowered the curtain and entered, while I remained seated in the room. A short while later, he came out to me after this verse had been revealed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out and recited it to the people: “O you who believe, do not enter the houses of the Prophet unless permission is given to you for a meal, without waiting for its preparation. But when you are invited, then enter; and when you have eaten, disperse without remaining for conversation. Indeed, that was troubling the Prophet.” [Al-Aḥzāb: 53] The remainder of the verse follows."],[1640,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr reported: The Companions of al-Ṣuffah were poor people. On one occasion, the Prophet ﷺ said: “Whoever has food sufficient for two should take a third person with him, and whoever has food sufficient for four should take a fifth or a sixth.” Or he said words to that effect. The Prophet ﷺ took ten people with him, while Abū Bakr brought three. Those at our home were myself, my father, and my mother—and I do not know whether he also mentioned my wife—as well as a servant shared between our household and that of Abū Bakr. Abū Bakr ate dinner with the Prophet ﷺ and remained until the ʿIshāʾ prayer had been performed. He then stayed until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had eaten dinner. He came home after as much of the night as Allah willed had passed. His wife asked him, “What detained you from your guests—or your guest?” He asked, “Have you not served them dinner?” She replied, “They refused to eat until you came. Food was presented to them, but they insisted on waiting for you.” I withdrew and hid. Abū Bakr exclaimed, “You foolish fellow!” He reproached and rebuked me, then told the guests, “Eat.” He also declared, “By Allah, I will never eat it.” By Allah, whenever we took a morsel from the food, more than it rose from beneath it. They ate until they were satisfied, yet the food became more abundant than it had been before. Abū Bakr looked at it and found that it was as plentiful as before—or even more so. He said to his wife, “Sister of Banū Firās!” She replied, “By the delight of my eyes, it is now three times more plentiful than it was before.” Abū Bakr ate from it and said that it had only been Satan—meaning that his oath not to eat had been prompted by Satan. He then ate a morsel from it and carried the food to the Prophet ﷺ, where it remained until morning. There was a treaty between us and another people, and its term came to an end. We divided ourselves into twelve groups, each under the leadership of a man, and Allah knows how many men accompanied each leader. The Prophet ﷺ sent them out, and all of them ate from that food—or he related words to that effect."],[1641,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr said: We were with the Prophet ﷺ, numbering one hundred and thirty. The Prophet ﷺ asked: “Does anyone among you have any food?” One man had approximately one ṣāʿ of food, so it was kneaded. A tall polytheist with disheveled hair then came driving some sheep. The Prophet ﷺ asked him: “Are they for sale or a gift?” —or he said, “A donation?” The man replied, “No, they are for sale.” The Prophet ﷺ bought a sheep from him, and it was prepared. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ ordered that its liver be roasted. By Allah, there was not one of the one hundred and thirty people for whom he did not cut a portion of its liver. If the person was present, he gave it to him; and if he was absent, he reserved it for him. He then placed the prepared food in two large dishes. All of us ate and were satisfied, yet food remained in both dishes, so I loaded them onto the camel—or he related words to that effect."],[1642,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh said: While the trench was being dug, I noticed that the Prophet ﷺ was suffering from severe hunger. I returned to my wife and asked, “Do you have any food? I have observed severe hunger in the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” She brought out a leather bag containing one ṣāʿ of barley. We also had a young domesticated animal, so I slaughtered it while she ground the barley. She finished grinding when I finished slaughtering, and I cut the meat and placed it in the cooking pot. I then went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. My wife said, “Do not embarrass me before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and those who are with him.” I approached him privately and said, “O Messenger of Allah, we have slaughtered a young animal of ours and ground one ṣāʿ of barley that we had. Please come with a small group of people.” The Prophet ﷺ called out loudly: “People of the trench! Jābir has prepared a meal, so come along!” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then instructed me: “Do not remove your pot from the fire, and do not bake your dough until I arrive.” I returned home, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came leading the people. When I reached my wife, she exclaimed, “May such-and-such happen to you!” blaming me for what I had done. I said, “I did exactly what you told me.” She brought out our dough. The Prophet ﷺ spat lightly into it and invoked blessing upon it. He then went to our cooking pot, spat lightly into it, and invoked blessing upon it. He said: “Call another woman to bake with you. Keep ladling from your pot, but do not remove it from the fire.” They numbered one thousand. I swear by Allah that they all ate until they left the food and withdrew fully satisfied. Our pot continued bubbling just as it had been, and our dough continued being baked just as it had been before."],[1643,"Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a military expedition and suffered severe hardship, until we considered slaughtering some of our riding animals. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ instructed us to gather our provision bags. We spread out a leather mat for him, and the people’s remaining provisions were collected upon it. I stretched upward to estimate how much there was and judged it to be approximately the size of a resting goat, although we numbered fourteen hundred. We all ate until we were satisfied and then filled our provision bags. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ asked: “Is there any water for ablution?” A man brought a small leather vessel containing only a little water and poured it into a cup. All fourteen hundred of us performed ablution from it, pouring from it freely. Eight people then arrived afterward and asked, “Is there any water for purification?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “The water for ablution has been exhausted.”"],[1644,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh said: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥarām died while owing a debt. I sought the assistance of the Prophet ﷺ in asking his creditors to reduce some of the debt. The Prophet ﷺ appealed to them, but they refused. The Prophet ﷺ then said to me: “Go and separate your dates according to their varieties: place the ʿajwah dates separately and the dates of ʿAdhq ibn Zayd separately. Then send for me.” I did so and sent for the Prophet ﷺ. He sat upon the upper part of the dates—or in their midst—and said: “Measure them out for the creditors.” I measured the dates for them until I had fully paid everything they were owed, yet my dates remained as though nothing had been taken from them."],[1645,"Abū Hurayrah said: I entered with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he found some milk in a cup. He said: “O Abū Hurayrah, go to the people of al-Ṣuffah and invite them to come to me.” I went to them and invited them. They came, sought permission to enter, and were granted permission. They then entered and drank."],[1646,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: Umm Mālik used to present clarified butter to the Prophet ﷺ in a leather container. Her sons would later come to her asking for something to eat with their bread when they had nothing else. She would go to the container in which she had sent the butter to the Prophet ﷺ and find butter in it. It continued providing the condiment needed by her household until she squeezed the container completely. She then came to the Prophet ﷺ, who asked: “Did you squeeze it?” She replied, “Yes.” He said: “Had you left it alone, it would have continued to provide for you.”"],[1647,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ asking for food. The Prophet ﷺ gave him half a wasq of barley. The man, his wife, and their guests continued eating from it until he measured it. He then came to the Prophet ﷺ, who said: “Had you not measured it, you would have continued eating from it, and it would have remained available for you.”"],[1648,"Abū Hurayrah said: We were traveling with the Prophet ﷺ when the people’s provisions were exhausted, until they considered slaughtering some of the animals carrying their loads. ʿUmar said, “O Messenger of Allah, why not gather whatever remains of the people’s provisions and supplicate to Allah to bless them?” The Prophet ﷺ did so. Those who had wheat brought their wheat, and those who had dates brought their dates. Mujāhid added, “Those who had date stones brought their date stones.” The narrator asked, “What did they do with the date stones?” He replied, “They would suck them and then drink water over them.” The Prophet ﷺ invoked Allah’s blessing upon the provisions until the people had filled their provision bags. He then said: “I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah. No servant will meet Allah affirming these two testimonies without doubting them except that he will enter Paradise.”\nAnother narration states: During the campaign of Tabūk, the people suffered from famine. They said, “O Messenger of Allah, if only you would permit us to slaughter our camels used for drawing water, so that we might eat their meat and use their fat.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Do so.” ʿUmar then came and said, “O Messenger of Allah, if you do that, there will be too few riding animals left. Instead, ask the people to bring the remnants of their provisions and then supplicate to Allah to bless them. Perhaps Allah will place blessing in them.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “Yes.” He called for a leather mat and spread it out. He then asked the people to bring the remnants of their provisions. One man brought a handful of millet, another brought a handful of dates, and another brought a piece of bread, until a small quantity had been collected upon the mat. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ invoked blessing upon it and then said: “Fill your containers from it.” They filled their containers until there was not a single container in the entire encampment that they had not filled. They ate until they were satisfied, and some food still remained. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah. No servant will meet Allah affirming these two testimonies without doubt and then be barred from Paradise.”"],[1649,"Abū Hurayrah reported: A sheep was cooked, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Give me a foreleg.” He was handed one. He again said: “Give me a foreleg.” He was handed another. He then said: “Give me a foreleg.” The man said, “O Messenger of Allah, a sheep has only two forelegs!” He replied: “Had you continued looking for them, you would have found them.”"],[1650,"Abū ʿUbayd reported: I prepared a pot of meat for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Hand me its foreleg.” I handed him one. He again said: “Hand me its foreleg.” I handed him another. He then said: “Hand me its foreleg.” I asked, “O Prophet of Allah, how many forelegs does a sheep have?” He replied: “By Him in Whose hand is my soul, had you remained silent, you would have continued handing me a foreleg each time I requested one.”"],[1651,"Samurah ibn Jundab said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, taking turns eating from a single large dish from morning until night. Ten people would rise after eating, and another ten would sit down to eat. We asked, “From where was the food continually replenished?” He replied, “Why are you astonished? It was replenished only from there,” and he pointed with his hand toward the sky."],[1652,"Abū ʿAmrah al-Anṣārī said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a military expedition when the people were afflicted by severe hunger. They sought the permission of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to slaughter some of their riding animals, saying, “Allah will enable us to reach our destination by means of their meat.” When ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb saw that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was about to permit them to slaughter some of their mounts, he said: “O Messenger of Allah, what will become of us if we encounter the enemy tomorrow while hungry and on foot? Instead, if you deem it appropriate, ask them to bring the remnants of their provisions. Gather them together and supplicate to Allah to bless them. Allah, Blessed and Most High, will enable us to reach our destination through your supplication”—or he said, “Allah will bless your supplication for us.” The Prophet ﷺ called for the remnants of their provisions. The people began bringing handfuls of food or a little more. The largest contribution anyone brought was one ṣāʿ of dates. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gathered it all together, stood, and supplicated for as long as Allah willed. He then called upon the army to bring their containers and instructed them to scoop food into them. Not a single container remained in the army without being filled, yet an equal amount of food still remained. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed until his molar teeth became visible and said: “I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah. No servant who believes in these two testimonies will meet Allah without the Fire being screened from him on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[1653,"Abū Hurayrah said: I brought some dates to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of Allah, ask Allah to bless them.” He gathered them together and invoked Allah’s blessing upon them for me. He then said: “Take them and place them in this provision bag of yours—or in this bag. Whenever you wish to take something from it, put your hand inside and remove what you need, but do not empty it out by scattering its contents.” From those dates I supplied many wasqs in the cause of Allah. We continued eating from them and feeding others. The bag never left my waist until the day ʿUthmān was killed, when it was cut away and lost."],[1654,"Dukayn ibn Saʿīd al-Muzanī said: Four hundred and forty riders came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking him for food. He said to ʿUmar: “Go and give them food.” ʿUmar replied, “O Messenger of Allah, only a few ṣāʿs of dates remain—hardly enough to sustain me.” He said: “Go and give them food.” ʿUmar replied, “I hear and obey.” ʿUmar took a key from his waistband and opened the door. There was a pile of dates resembling a young camel lying upon the ground. He told us, “Take what you need.” Each man took as much as he wished. I then looked back, as I was among the last of the people, and it appeared as though we had not diminished the pile by even a single date."],[1655,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said: We used to hear the food glorifying Allah while it was being eaten."],[1656,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh said: There was a palm trunk beside which the Prophet ﷺ used to stand. When the pulpit was erected for him, we heard the trunk making sounds like those of pregnant she-camels, until the Prophet ﷺ descended and placed his hand upon it."],[1657,"Ibn ʿUmar said: The Prophet ﷺ used to deliver his sermons beside a palm trunk. When a pulpit was made for him, he moved to it, whereupon the trunk cried with longing. He went to it and passed his hand over it."],[1658,"Abū Ḥāzim reported: Some people came to Sahl ibn Saʿd and asked, “From what was the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made?” He replied, “No one remaining among the people knows more about it than I do. It was made from the tamarisk wood of al-Ghābah. So-and-so, the freed slave of a certain woman, made it for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to lean against a palm trunk in the mosque, standing beside it when delivering a sermon. When the pulpit was made and he sat upon it, the trunk cried with longing. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to it and soothed it—although Abū Ḥāzim’s narration does not contain the words “and soothed it”—until it became quiet."],[1659,"Anas said: The people of Madinah suffered a drought during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. While he was delivering the Friday sermon, a man stood and said, “O Messenger of Allah, the horses and camels are perishing and the sheep are dying. Supplicate to Allah to send us rain.” The Prophet ﷺ extended his hands and supplicated. Anas said: The sky had been as clear as glass. A wind then arose and formed clouds. The clouds gathered together, and the sky opened its channels of rain. We left the mosque wading through water until we reached our homes.\nIt continued raining until the following Friday. The same man—or another man—stood and said, “O Messenger of Allah, the houses are collapsing. Supplicate to Allah to withhold the rain.” The Prophet ﷺ smiled and said: “O Allah, let it fall around us and not upon us.” I looked at the clouds as they separated around Madinah until they resembled a circular crown."],[1660,"Anas ibn Mālik said: The people were afflicted by drought during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was delivering the Friday sermon from the pulpit, a Bedouin stood and said, “O Messenger of Allah, our wealth and livestock are perishing and our families are hungry. Supplicate to Allah to send us rain.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his hands while there was not a single wisp of cloud in the sky. Clouds then arose resembling mountains. He had not yet descended from the pulpit when I saw rainwater flowing down his beard. Rain fell upon us that day, the following day, the day after that, and the next day, continuing until the following Friday. That Bedouin—or another man—then stood and said, “O Messenger of Allah, the buildings are collapsing and our wealth is being submerged. Supplicate to Allah for us.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his hands and said: “O Allah, let it fall around us and not upon us.” Whenever he pointed toward any part of the sky, the clouds dispersed from that direction, until Madinah resembled an open hollow surrounded by clouds. The valley of Qanāh continued flowing for a month. No one came from any direction without reporting abundant rainfall."],[1661,"ʿUrwah reported: The Prophet ﷺ gave him one dinar with which to purchase a sheep. He bought two sheep for that dinar and then sold one of them for a dinar. He returned to the Prophet ﷺ with both the original dinar and a sheep. The Prophet ﷺ supplicated that his business transactions be blessed. Thereafter, even if he had purchased dust, he would have made a profit from it."],[1662,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh said: I participated in a military expedition with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ caught up with me while I was riding a water-carrying camel of ours that had become exhausted and could scarcely move. He asked: “What is wrong with your camel?” I replied, “It has become exhausted.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell behind with it, urged it forward, and supplicated for it. Thereafter, it continued traveling at the front of the other camels, ahead of them. The complete hadith appears in the account of the sale of the camel."],[1663,"Anas said: The people were alarmed, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mounted a slow horse belonging to Abū Ṭalḥah and rode off alone at a gallop. The people then mounted their horses and galloped after him. He said: “You have nothing to fear. This horse is truly like a sea in its speed.” After that day, the horse was never outrun."],[1664,"Anas ibn Mālik reported: When ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām learned that the Prophet ﷺ had arrived in Madinah, he came to ask him several questions. He said: “I shall ask you three questions whose answers are known only to a prophet: What is the first sign of the Hour? What is the first food the people of Paradise will eat? And what causes a child to resemble either its father or its mother?” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “Gabriel informed me of these things just now.” Ibn Salām said, “He is the enemy of the Jews among the angels.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “As for the first sign of the Hour, it will be a fire that gathers the people from the east to the west. The first food eaten by the people of Paradise will be the extra lobe of a fish’s liver. As for the child, when the man’s fluid precedes the woman’s fluid, the child resembles him; and when the woman’s fluid precedes the man’s fluid, the child resembles her.” ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām said, “I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that you are the Messenger of Allah.” He then said, “O Messenger of Allah, the Jews are a people of falsehood and slander. Ask them about me before they learn that I have embraced Islam.” The Jews came, and the Prophet ﷺ asked them: “What kind of man is ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām among you?” They replied, “He is the best of us and the son of the best of us, the most virtuous of us and the son of the most virtuous of us.” The Prophet ﷺ asked: “What would you think if ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām were to embrace Islam?” They replied, “May Allah protect him from doing so.” He repeated the question, and they gave the same answer. ʿAbdullāh then came out before them and said, “I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.” They immediately said, “He is the worst of us and the son of the worst of us,” and began disparaging him. ʿAbdullāh said, “This is what I feared, O Messenger of Allah.”"],[1665,"Thawbān, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, said: I was standing beside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a Jewish rabbi came and said, “Peace be upon you, Muhammad.” I pushed him so forcefully that he nearly fell. He asked, “Why did you push me?” I replied, “Why did you not say, ‘O Messenger of Allah’?” The Jewish man said, “We call him only by the name his family gave him.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “My name is Muhammad—the name my family gave me.” The Jewish man said, “I have come to ask you some questions.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: “Will it benefit you if I answer you?” He replied, “I shall listen with my own ears.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began drawing upon the ground with a stick he held and said: “Ask.” The Jewish man asked, “Where will the people be on the day when the earth is changed into another earth, and the heavens as well?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: “They will be in darkness before the bridge.” He asked, “Who will be the first people permitted to cross?” He replied: “The poor among the Emigrants.” The Jewish man asked, “What will be presented to them when they enter Paradise?” He replied: “The extra lobe of a fish’s liver.” He asked, “What will their meal be after that?” He replied: “The bull of Paradise, which used to graze along its edges, will be slaughtered for them.” He asked, “What will they drink with it?” He replied: “They will drink from a spring therein called Salsabīl.” The Jewish man said, “You have spoken the truth.” He then said, “I have also come to ask you about something that no one on earth knows except a prophet, or perhaps one or two other men.” The Prophet ﷺ asked: “Will it benefit you if I tell you?” He replied, “I shall listen with my own ears. I have come to ask you about the child.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “The man’s reproductive fluid is white, and the woman’s reproductive fluid is yellow. When they meet and the man’s fluid prevails over the woman’s fluid, it results in a male by Allah’s permission. When the woman’s fluid prevails over the man’s fluid, it results in a female by Allah’s permission.” The Jewish man said, “You have indeed spoken the truth, and you are certainly a prophet.” He then turned and departed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “This man asked me about matters of which I had no knowledge until Allah gave me knowledge of them.”"],[1666,"Anas ibn Mālik said: A Christian man embraced Islam and learned Sūrat al-Baqarah and Sūrat Āl ʿImrān. He used to write for the Prophet ﷺ, but he later returned to Christianity and would say, “Muhammad knows only what I wrote for him.” Allah caused him to die, and his people buried him. By morning, the earth had cast him out. They said, “Muhammad and his Companions did this. Because he fled from them, they dug up our companion and threw him out.” They dug another grave for him and made it deeper. By morning, the earth had again cast him out. They again said, “Muhammad and his Companions did this. Because he fled from them, they dug up our companion and threw him out.” They then dug as deeply into the ground as they possibly could and buried him. By morning, the earth had once more cast him out. They realized that this was not the work of human beings, so they left his body exposed. Another wording states: There was a man from Banū al-Najjār among us who had learned Sūrat al-Baqarah and Sūrat Āl ʿImrān and used to write for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He fled and joined the People of the Book.\nThey held him in high esteem, saying, “This man used to write for Muhammad,” and they admired him. It was not long before Allah caused him to die among them. They dug a grave and concealed him in it, but by morning the earth had thrown him onto its surface. They dug another grave and buried him, but by morning the earth had again thrown him onto its surface. They dug and buried him a third time, but by morning the earth had once more thrown him onto its surface, so they left him lying exposed."],[1667,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib said: Abū Bakr came to my father at his home and purchased a camel saddle from him. He said to ʿĀzib, “Send your son with me to carry it.” I carried it with him, while my father accompanied us to receive its price. My father said, “Abū Bakr, tell me what happened when you traveled by night with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” Abū Bakr replied: Yes. We traveled throughout that night and continued through the following day until midday arrived and the road became deserted, with no one passing along it. A tall rock appeared before us with a shaded area untouched by the sun. We stopped beside it. With my own hands, I leveled a place where the Prophet ﷺ could sleep. I spread a hide over it and said, “Sleep, O Messenger of Allah, while I examine and clear the surroundings for you.” He slept, and I went out to inspect the surroundings. I then saw a shepherd approaching the rock with his sheep, seeking the same shade that we had sought. I asked him, “Whose servant are you, young man?” He replied, “A man from Madinah”—or Makkah. I asked, “Do your sheep have any milk?” He replied, “Yes.” I asked, “Will you milk one for us?” He replied, “Yes.” He took hold of a sheep, and I told him, “Clear its udder of dust, hair, and dirt.” At this point, I saw al-Barāʾ striking one hand against the other to demonstrate how the shepherd cleaned it. The shepherd milked a quantity of milk into a wooden bowl. I had with me a leather water vessel that I had carried for the Prophet ﷺ so that he could drink and perform ablution from it. I returned to the Prophet ﷺ. I disliked awakening him, but I arrived just as he awoke. I poured water over the milk until its lower portion became cool and said, “Drink, O Messenger of Allah.” He drank until I was satisfied that he had had enough. He then asked: “Has the time not come to depart?” I replied, “Yes.” We resumed our journey after the sun had passed its zenith. Surāqah ibn Mālik began pursuing us. I said, “They have reached us, O Messenger of Allah!” He replied: “Do not grieve; indeed, Allah is with us.” The Prophet ﷺ supplicated against Surāqah, and his horse sank into the firm ground up to its belly—or approximately that far; Zuhayr was uncertain. Surāqah said, “I believe that you have supplicated against me. Supplicate for me now, and I swear by Allah that I shall turn the pursuers away from you.” The Prophet ﷺ supplicated for him, and he was saved. Thereafter, whenever Surāqah encountered anyone searching for them, he would say, “I have dealt with everything in this direction for you.” He turned back everyone he met. Abū Bakr said: “He faithfully fulfilled his promise to us.”"],[1668,"Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a group of Anṣār to Abū Rāfiʿ the Jew and appointed ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAtīk as their leader. Abū Rāfiʿ used to harm the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and assist others against him. He lived in a fortress in the land of the Ḥijāz. When the men drew near after sunset, the people were returning with their grazing animals. ʿAbdullāh said to his companions, “Remain where you are. I shall go ahead and deal tactfully with the gatekeeper so that I may be able to enter.” He approached the gate and, when he came near it, covered himself with his garment as though relieving himself. By then, the people had entered the fortress. The gatekeeper called to him, “Servant of Allah, if you wish to enter, then enter, for I am about to close the gate.” ʿAbdullāh continued: I entered and concealed myself. When all the people had entered, the gatekeeper closed the gate and hung its keys upon a peg. I went to the keys, took them, and opened the gate. People used to gather with Abū Rāfiʿ in the evening, and he was staying in some upper rooms belonging to him. When his evening guests departed, I climbed up to him. Whenever I opened a door, I locked it behind me from the inside. I thought: If the people discover me, they will not be able to reach me before I kill him. I eventually reached him. He was in a dark room surrounded by his family, and I did not know where in the room he was. I called, “Abū Rāfiʿ!” He asked, “Who is that?” I moved toward the sound and struck him with my sword. I was startled and confused, so the blow accomplished nothing decisive. He cried out, and I left the room. I waited nearby for a short time and then reentered, saying, “What was that sound, Abū Rāfiʿ?” He replied, “May your mother be bereaved of you! A man in the house struck me with a sword a moment ago.” I struck him again with a blow that wounded him severely but did not kill him. I then placed the point of my sword against his abdomen and drove it through until it reached his back. I knew then that I had killed him. I began opening the doors one after another until I reached a stairway belonging to the house. Thinking I had reached the ground, I stepped forward and fell. It was a moonlit night, and my leg broke. I bound it with my turban. I then went and sat at the gate, saying to myself: I shall not leave tonight until I know whether I killed him. When the rooster crowed, a herald stood upon the wall and announced, “I proclaim the death of Abū Rāfiʿ, the merchant of the people of the Ḥijāz.” I went to my companions and said, “Escape! Allah has killed Abū Rāfiʿ.” When I reached the Prophet ﷺ, I told him what had happened. He said: “Extend your leg.” I extended it, and he passed his hand over it. Thereafter, it was as though I had never suffered any pain in it."],[1669,"Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd said: I saw the mark of a wound upon Salamah’s leg and asked, “Abū Muslim, what is this wound?” He replied: “This is a wound I received on the day of Khaybar. The people said, ‘Salamah has been wounded.’ I went to the Prophet ﷺ, and he blew lightly upon it three times. I have not felt any pain from it up to this very moment.”"],[1670,"Abū Hurayrah said: I said, “O Messenger of Allah, I hear many hadiths from you, but I forget them.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “Spread out your outer garment.” I spread it out. He made a scooping motion with his hand into it and then said: “Gather it and hold it against yourself.” I gathered it against myself, and after that I never forgot a hadith."],[1671,"Abū Zayd ʿAmr ibn Akhṭab al-Anṣārī said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: “Come near me.” He passed his hand over my head and beard and then supplicated: “O Allah, make him handsome and preserve his handsomeness.” He lived to be more than one hundred years old, yet there were only a few scattered white hairs upon his head and in his beard. His face remained smooth and radiant and did not become wrinkled or contracted before his death."],[1672,"Al-Juʿayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: I saw al-Sā’ib ibn Yazīd when he was ninety-four years old, still strong and upright. He said: “I know that the continued enjoyment of my hearing and eyesight is due only to the supplication of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. My maternal aunt took me to him and said, ‘O Messenger of Allah, my sister’s son is ill, so supplicate to Allah for him.’ He then supplicated for me.”"],[1673,"Abū al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿUmayr said: I was present with Qatādah ibn Milḥān when death approached him. A man passed by at the far end of the house, and I saw his reflection upon Qatādah’s face. Whenever I looked at him, it appeared as though oil had been applied to his face. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had passed his hand over his face."],[1674,"Sahl ibn Saʿd reported that on the day of Khaybar, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I shall certainly give this banner to a man through whose hands Allah will grant victory. He loves Allah and His Messenger, and Allah and His Messenger love him.” The people spent the night discussing among themselves which of them would be given the banner. When morning came, they all went early to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, each hoping that it would be given to him. He asked: “Where is ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib?” They replied, “O Messenger of Allah, he is suffering from pain in his eyes.” The Prophet ﷺ supplicated for him, and he was healed until it was as though he had never suffered any pain. The remainder of the hadith concerns the account of Khaybar."],[1675,"ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib said: “I have neither suffered inflammation of the eyes nor experienced a headache since the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed his hand over my face and spat lightly into my eye on the day of Khaybar, when he gave me the banner.”"],[1676,"Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ spat lightly upon the leg of ʿAmr ibn Muʿādh when his leg had been severed, and it was healed."],[1677,"ʿĀ’ishah said: During the illness in which the Prophet ﷺ passed away, he called his daughter Fāṭimah and whispered something to her, whereupon she wept. He then called her again and whispered something to her, whereupon she laughed. I later asked her about that, and she said: “The Prophet ﷺ privately informed me that he would pass away during the illness from which he was then suffering, so I wept. He then privately informed me that I would be the first member of his household to follow him, so I laughed.”"],[1678,"ʿĀ’ishah, Mother of the Believers, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The first of you to join me will be the one with the longest hand.” She said: They began measuring themselves to determine which of them had the longest hand. Zaynab proved to have the longest hand among us because she used to work with her hands and give generously in charity."],[1679,"ʿĀ’ishah reported that some of the Prophet’s wives asked him ﷺ: “Which of us will be the first to join you?” He replied: “The one of you with the longest hand.” They took a reed and began measuring their hands with it, and Sawdah had the longest hand among them. We later came to understand that the meaning of having a long hand was giving in charity. She was the first of us to join him, and she loved giving charity."],[1680,"Anas reported: The Prophet ﷺ announced the deaths of Jaʿfar and Zayd before news of their deaths arrived, while tears flowed from his eyes."],[1681,"Anas reported: Before news reached the people, the Prophet ﷺ announced to them the deaths of Zayd, Jaʿfar, and Ibn Rawāḥah. He said: “Zayd took the banner and was struck down. Then Jaʿfar took it and was struck down. Then Ibn Rawāḥah took it and was struck down.” His eyes were shedding tears as he continued: “Then one of the swords of Allah took the banner, and Allah granted them victory through him.”"],[1682,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The Hour will not be established until two groups fight one another and a great slaughter occurs between them, although their claim is one and the same.”"],[1683,"Abū Bakrah said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon the pulpit with al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī beside him. He would turn toward the people at one moment and toward al-Ḥasan at another, saying: “This son of mine is a leader, and perhaps Allah will bring reconciliation through him between two great groups of Muslims.”"],[1684,"ʿAmr ibn Maymūn reported that he heard ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd narrate from Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, who said: “Saʿd was a friend of Umayyah ibn Khalaf. Whenever Umayyah passed through Madīnah, he would stay with Saʿd, and whenever Saʿd passed through Makkah, he would stay with Umayyah. “After the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Madīnah, Saʿd set out to perform ʿumrah and stayed with Umayyah in Makkah. He said to Umayyah, ‘Find me a quiet time when I may perhaps circumambulate the House.’ “So Umayyah took him out around midday. Abū Jahl met them and said, ‘O Abū Ṣafwān, who is this with you?’ “He replied, ‘This is Saʿd.’ “Abū Jahl said to him, ‘I see you circumambulating Makkah in safety even though you have given refuge to those who have abandoned our religion, and you claim that you support and assist them. By Allah, were it not that you are with Abū Ṣafwān, you would not return safely to your family.’ “Saʿd raised his voice at him and said, ‘By Allah, if you prevent me from this, I will surely prevent you from something even more severe for you: your route through Madīnah.’ “Umayyah said to him, ‘Do not raise your voice, O Saʿd, against Abū al-Ḥakam, the chief of the people of the valley.’ “Saʿd said, ‘Leave us alone, O Umayyah. By Allah, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that they will kill you.’ “He asked, ‘In Makkah?’ “Saʿd replied, ‘I do not know.’ “Umayyah became extremely frightened by this. “When Umayyah returned to his family, he said, ‘O Umm Ṣafwān, did you not hear what Saʿd said to me?’ “She asked, ‘What did he say?’ “He replied, ʿHe claims that Muḥammad informed them that they will kill me. I asked him, “In Makkah?” and he said, “I do not know.”’ “Umayyah said, ‘By Allah, I will not leave Makkah.’ “Then, when the Day of Badr came, Abū Jahl called the people to arms, saying, ‘Go and protect your caravan!’ “Umayyah disliked going out. Abū Jahl came to him and said, ‘O Abū Ṣafwān, if the people see that you have stayed behind while you are the chief of the people of the valley, they will remain behind with you.’ “Abū Jahl continued urging him until Umayyah said, ‘Since you have overcome me, by Allah, I will buy the finest camel in Makkah.’ “Then Umayyah said, ‘O Umm Ṣafwān, prepare me for the journey.’ “She said, ‘O Abū Ṣafwān, have you forgotten what your Yathribī brother said to you?’ “He replied, ‘No. I only intend to accompany them for a short distance.’ “Whenever Umayyah stopped at a place after leaving Makkah, he would tether his camel, remaining prepared to flee. He continued doing so until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, caused him to be killed at Badr.”"],[1685,"Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the polytheists met in battle and fought one another. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned toward his camp and the others returned toward their camp, there was a man among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who would pursue every isolated enemy fighter and strike him with his sword. The people said, “No one among us has been as effective today as so-and-so.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, he is among the people of the Fire.” A man from the people said, “I will remain with him continuously.” So he went out with him. Whenever the man stopped, he stopped with him, and whenever he moved quickly, he moved quickly with him. The man was then severely wounded and became impatient for death. He placed the point of his sword on the ground and its tip against his chest, then threw his weight upon the sword and killed himself. The man who had followed him went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “I testify that you are the Messenger of Allah.” He ﷺ asked: “Why is that?” He said, “The man whom you mentioned earlier as being among the people of the Fire—the people found that difficult to comprehend, so I said, ‘I will keep watch over him for you.’ I followed him, and then he was severely wounded. He became impatient for death, placed the point of his sword on the ground and its tip between his breasts, then threw himself upon it and killed himself.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: “Indeed, a man may perform the deeds of the people of Paradise, as it appears to the people, while he is among the people of the Fire; and a man may perform the deeds of the people of the Fire, as it appears to the people, while he is among the people of Paradise.”"],[1686,"Abū Hurayrah said: “We were present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Khaybar. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning a man who was with him and claimed Islām: ‘This man is among the people of the Fire.’ “When the fighting began, the man fought with extreme intensity and suffered many wounds that incapacitated him. “One of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ came and said, ‘O Messenger of Allah, what about the man whom you said was among the people of the Fire? He fought in the cause of Allah with the fiercest fighting and has suffered many wounds.’ “The Prophet ﷺ said: ‘Indeed, he is among the people of the Fire.’ “Some of the Muslims almost began to doubt. While matters stood thus, the man felt the pain of his wounds, reached toward his quiver, pulled out an arrow, and killed himself with it. “Some Muslim men hurried to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, ‘O Messenger of Allah, Allah has confirmed the truth of what you said. So-and-so has killed himself.’ “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘O Bilāl, stand and announce: None shall enter Paradise except a believer, and indeed Allah may support this religion by means of a wicked man.’”"],[1687,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik reported that one of those who witnessed Khaybar with the Prophet ﷺ informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning a man who was with him: “Indeed, this man is among the people of the Fire.” When the fighting began, the man fought with extreme intensity until his wounds became numerous. Some men from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ came to him and said, “O Messenger of Allah, what about the man whom you said was among the people of the Fire? By Allah, he has fought in the cause of Allah with the fiercest fighting, and his wounds have become numerous.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, he is among the people of the Fire.” Some of the people nearly began to doubt. While they were in that state, the man felt the pain of his wounds, reached for his quiver, took an arrow from it, and killed himself with it. A Muslim man hurried to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “O Prophet of Allah, Allah has confirmed the truth of what you said. So-and-so has killed himself.”"],[1688,"It is narrated from Sayyidunā ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: When Allah, Blessed and Exalted, willed to guide Zayd ibn Saʿnah, Zayd ibn Saʿnah said: “There was no sign of prophethood that I had not seen in the face of Muhammad ﷺ when I looked at him, except two things that I had not tested in him: (1) whether his forbearance (ḥilm) always overcomes his ignorant behavior; (2) whether the intensity of ignorant behavior toward him only increases his forbearance. So I dealt with him with kindness and gentleness so that I could recognize his forbearance and his ignorance.” Zayd ibn Saʿnah said: “One day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out from the rooms, and with him was Sayyidunā ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him. A man came on his mount, like the Bedouins, and said: ‘O Messenger of Allah! The people of Buṣrā, a settlement of Banū so-and-so, have accepted Islam and entered into Islam. I had told them that if they accepted Islam they would receive abundant provision. But a time of famine and hardship has come upon them.’ That man said: ‘O Messenger of Allah! I fear that these people will leave Islam in the same way as they entered Islam out of greed. If you think it appropriate to help them by sending something to them, then do so.’ The noble Prophet ﷺ looked at a man sitting near him, who was probably ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: ‘We have nothing left.’” Zayd ibn Saʿnah says: “I came near and said: ‘O Muhammad! Will you buy from me a specified quantity of dates from such-and-such orchard for a fixed period?’ He ﷺ said: ‘No, O Jew! But I will sell you dates for a fixed period without mentioning such-and-such orchard.’ I said: ‘Fine.’ Then I opened my purse and paid 80 mithqāls of gold, and the agreement was settled. He ﷺ gave that gold to the man and said: ‘Give these people with justice and help them.’” Zayd ibn Saʿnah says: “When two or three days remained of the fixed period, I went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I seized his ﷺ shirt and cloak and said in a harsh tone: ‘O Muhammad! Pay my right! By Allah, you of Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib are known to me as people who delay payment.’ I saw Sayyidunā ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, his eyes rolling in anger like a revolving sphere. Then he glared at me and said: ‘O enemy of Allah! Are you speaking to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ like this and treating him like this? By the One who sent him with the truth, if I did not fear his reproach, I would cut off your head with my sword!’ The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was looking at Sayyidunā ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, with calm and gentleness and was smiling. Then he ﷺ said: ‘O ʿUmar! He and I were more deserving of something else from you: that you should order me to pay well and order him to ask well. ʿUmar! Take him, pay him his right, and give him twenty ṣāʿ of dates in addition.’” Zayd ibn Saʿnah says: “I asked Sayyidunā ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him: ‘Why are these extra dates?’ He replied: ‘This was the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, to make up for your treatment.’ I asked: ‘Do you know me, O ʿUmar?’ He said: ‘No, who are you?’ I said: ‘I am Zayd ibn Saʿnah.’ He said: ‘The scholar?’ I said: ‘Yes, that very scholar.’ Sayyidunā ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, asked: ‘Then why did you do this with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?’ I replied: ‘O ʿUmar! I had seen all the signs of the Prophet ﷺ in his face, except two things, which I had not yet tested: (1) whether his gentleness always overcomes his anger; (2) and whether, when ignorant behavior toward him intensifies, he becomes even more gentle in response to it. So I tested him, and I bear witness that I am pleased with Allah as Lord, with Islam as religion, and with Muhammad ﷺ as Prophet. I bear witness that half of my wealth, and I am among the wealthiest, is charity for the Ummah of Muhammad ﷺ.’ Sayyidunā ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ‘Not for all of them, for some, because you cannot give to all.’ I said: ‘Fine, for some.’” Then Zayd went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, accepted Islam and believed in him ﷺ. He took part with him ﷺ in many battles and attained martyrdom in the Battle of Tabūk."],[1689,"In this ḥadīth, Ḥamzah ibn Yūsuf is unknown (majhūl).\nIt was narrated by Ibn Ḥibbān, al-Ṭabarānī in al-Muʿjam al-Kabīr, al-Ḥākim, and al-Bayhaqī in Dalāʾil al-Nubuwwah. All of them narrated it through Muḥammad ibn al-Mutawakkil, who is Ibn Abī al-Sarī. He said: al-Walīd ibn Muslim informed us, from Muḥammad ibn Ḥamzah ibn Yūsuf ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām, from his father, from his grandfather.\nAnd Muḥammad ibn al-Mutawakkil has received a little criticism, which is not harmful. Moreover, he has also been supported by corroborating narrations (mutābaʿah, that is, in other chains). Thus Abū al-Shaykh al-Aṣbahānī narrated this ḥadīth in his book Akhlāq al-Nabī ﷺ (pp. 72-73) through al-Ḥawṭī—and al-Ḥawṭī is ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn Najdah, who is thiqah (trustworthy) as stated in al-Taqrīb. And Ibn Mājah (ḥadīth no. 2281) narrated it through Yaʿqūb ibn Ḥumayd ibn Kāsib, and he is weak as mentioned in al-Taqrīb. Both narrated this ḥadīth through al-Walīd ibn Muslim with the same chain.\nAl-Walīd ibn Muslim is a mudallis (one who conceals defects in chains), but he explicitly stated direct hearing (taḥdīth) in this ḥadīth. However, the axis of the ḥadīth is Ḥamzah ibn Yūsuf ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām, whom no one has declared thiqah. Ibn Ḥibbān mentioned him in his book al-Thiqāt (vol. 4, p. 170), which is his general practice of declaring unknown people trustworthy.\nOnly his son narrated from Ḥamzah, so he is majhūl according to the terminology of Ibn Ḥajar. However, al-Ḥāfiẓ Ibn Ḥajar said of him maqbūl, meaning that when he is corroborated his narration is accepted, otherwise his ḥadīth is layyin (weak). And here, since he has no corroboration, his ḥadīth will be called layyin or at the very least majhūl.\nAs for the statement of al-Ḥākim: This ḥadīth has a ṣaḥīḥ chain and al-Bukhārī and Muslim did not narrate it. It is among the gharar al-ḥadīth (rare, choice ḥadīths). And Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Sarī al-ʿAsqalānī is thiqah—this is based on laxity. He spoke about Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Sarī and declared him thiqah, although there is disagreement about him. Ibn Maʿīn declared him thiqah, while Abū Ḥātim called him layyin. Ibn ʿAdī said: Muḥammad made very many mistakes. Despite this, he is not alone in this ḥadīth, rather he was corroborated, as mentioned earlier.\nAl-Ḥākim did not speak about Ḥamzah ibn Yūsuf, although the axis of the chain is upon him, and he is unknown. And the ḥadīth of an unknown narrator is not ṣaḥīḥ.\nImām al-Dhahabī, in his rebuttal of this statement of al-Ḥākim, said: This ḥadīth is munkar and has things that warrant its abandonment, especially this phrase: “He was not one to retreat instead of confronting,” because there was no fighting (qitāl) in the Battle of Tabūk.\nLikewise, al-Ḥāfiẓ Ibn Ḥajar also spoke about Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Sarī and quoted the statements of the muḥaddithūn. See al-Iṣābah, the biography of Zayd ibn Saʿnah."],[1690,"Allah the Exalted knows best, but this ḥadīth is well-known among the people of knowledge in establishing the proofs of prophethood."],[1691,"Abū Hurayrah said: “When Khaybar was conquered, a poisoned sheep was presented to the Prophet ﷺ. “The Prophet ﷺ said: ‘Gather for me the Jews who are here.’ “They were gathered before him, and he said: ‘I am going to ask you about something. Will you tell me the truth about it?’ “They replied, ‘Yes.’ “The Prophet ﷺ asked them: ‘Who is your father?’ “They replied, ‘So-and-so.’ “He said: ‘You have lied. Rather, your father is so-and-so.’ “They said, ‘You have spoken the truth.’ “He asked: ‘Will you tell me the truth about something if I ask you?’ “They replied, ‘Yes, O Abū al-Qāsim. If we lie, you will recognize our lie just as you recognized it concerning our father.’ “He asked them: ‘Who are the people of the Fire?’ “They replied, ‘We will remain in it only for a short time, then you will replace us in it.’ “The Prophet ﷺ said: ‘Remain disgraced in it! By Allah, we will never replace you in it.’ “Then he asked: ‘Will you tell me the truth about something if I ask you?’ “They replied, ‘Yes, O Abū al-Qāsim.’ “He asked: ‘Did you put poison in this sheep?’ “They replied, ‘Yes.’ “He asked: ‘What caused you to do that?’ “They replied, ‘We wanted that, if you were a liar, we would be rid of you; and if you were a prophet, it would not harm you.’”"],[1692,"Sulaymān ibn Ṣurad said: “I heard the Prophet ﷺ say when the Confederates had been driven away from him: ‘Now we will attack them, and they will not attack us. We will march against them.’”"],[1693,"Abū Ḥumayd said: “We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the expedition of Tabūk. We came to Wādī al-Qurā and stopped by a garden belonging to a woman. “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘Estimate its produce.’ “We estimated it, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ estimated it at ten wasqs. He said to the woman: ‘Keep account of it until we return to you, if Allah wills.’ “We continued until we reached Tabūk. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘A severe wind will blow upon you tonight. None of you should stand during it, and whoever has a camel should firmly tie its hobble.’ “A severe wind then blew. A man stood up, and the wind carried him away until it threw him at the two mountains of Ṭayyiʾ. “The messenger of Ibn al-ʿUlamāʾ, the ruler of Aylah, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ carrying a letter and presented him with a white mule. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote back to him and gave him a mantle as a gift. “Then we returned until we reached Wādī al-Qurā. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked the woman about her garden: ‘How much did its produce amount to?’ “She replied, ‘Ten wasqs.’ “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: ‘I am going to travel quickly. Whoever wishes may hasten with me, and whoever wishes may remain.’ “We continued until we overlooked Madīnah. He said: ‘This is Ṭābah, and this is Uḥud. It is a mountain that loves us and that we love.’ “Then he said: ‘The best dwellings of the Anṣār are the dwellings of Banū al-Najjār, then the dwellings of Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal, then the dwellings of Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj, then the dwellings of Banū Sāʿidah; and there is good in all the dwellings of the Anṣār.’ “Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah caught up with us, and Abū Usayd said to him, ‘Have you not seen that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ranked the dwellings of the Anṣār and placed us last?’ “Saʿd caught up with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, ‘O Messenger of Allah, you ranked the dwellings of the Anṣār and placed us last.’ “He ﷺ said: ‘Is it not enough for you that you are among the best?’”"],[1694,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī said: “While we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as he was distributing some property, Dhū al-Khuwayṣirah, a man from Banū Tamīm, came to him and said, ‘O Messenger of Allah, be just!’ “He ﷺ said: ‘Woe to you! Who would be just if I were not just? You would indeed be ruined and lost if I were not just.’ “ʿUmar said, ‘O Messenger of Allah, give me permission to strike his neck.’ “He ﷺ said: ʿLeave him. He will have companions in comparison with whose prayer one of you will consider his own prayer insignificant, and in comparison with whose fasting one of you will consider his own fasting insignificant. They will recite the Qurʾān, but it will not go beyond their throats. They will pass out of the religion just as an arrow passes through the game animal. ʿThe arrowhead will be examined, but nothing will be found upon it. Then its shaft fitting will be examined, but nothing will be found upon it. Then its shaft itself will be examined, but nothing will be found upon it. Then its feathers will be examined, but nothing will be found upon them, for it will have passed through the dung and blood before either could cling to it. ‘Their sign will be a black man, one of whose upper arms will be like a woman’s breast, or like a piece of flesh that shakes loosely. They will emerge at a time when the people are divided.’” Abū Saʿīd said: “I testify that I heard this hadith from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I testify that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib fought them while I was with him. He ordered that the man described be sought out, and he was brought. I looked at him and found him exactly according to the description given by the Prophet ﷺ.”"],[1695,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “When Khosrow is destroyed, there will be no Khosrow after him; and when Caesar is destroyed, there will be no Caesar after him. By the One in Whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, their treasures will surely be spent in the cause of Allah.”"],[1696,"Jābir ibn Samurah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “When Khosrow is destroyed, there will be no Khosrow after him; and when Caesar is destroyed, there will be no Caesar after him. By the One in Whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, their treasures will surely be spent in the cause of Allah.”"],[1697,"ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim said: “While I was with the Prophet ﷺ, a man came to him and complained of poverty. Then another man came and complained about the danger of highway robbery. “He said to me, ‘O ʿAdī, have you seen al-Ḥīrah?’ “I replied, ‘I have not seen it, but I have been informed about it.’ “He said: ‘If you live long enough, you will surely see a woman traveling in her howdah from al-Ḥīrah until she circumambulates the Kaʿbah, fearing no one except Allah.’ “I said to myself, ‘Then where will the brigands of Ṭayyiʾ be, who have spread havoc throughout the land?’ “He continued: ‘And if you live long enough, the treasures of Khosrow will surely be conquered.’ “I asked, ‘Khosrow ibn Hurmuz?’ “He replied, ʿKhosrow ibn Hurmuz. And if you live long enough, you will surely see a man bring out a handful of gold or silver, seeking someone who will accept it from him, yet he will find no one willing to accept it. “‘And each one of you will surely meet Allah on the Day he meets Him, with no interpreter between him and Allah to translate for him. Allah will say, “Did I not send you a messenger who conveyed the message to you?” He will reply, “Yes.” He will say, “Did I not give you wealth and children and bestow favors upon you?” He will reply, “Yes.” Then he will look to his right and see nothing but Hell, and he will look to his left and see nothing but Hell.’” ʿAdī said: “I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: ‘Protect yourselves from the Fire even with half a date, and whoever cannot find even half a date, then with a good word.’ “I later saw a woman traveling in her howdah from al-Ḥīrah until she circumambulated the Kaʿbah, fearing none but Allah. And I was among those who conquered the treasures of Khosrow ibn Hurmuz. And if you live long enough, you will indeed see what the Prophet Abū al-Qāsim ﷺ said: ‘A man will bring out a handful.’”"],[1698,"Jābir ibn Samurah said: “I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘A group of Muslims—or believers—will surely conquer the treasure of the family of Khosrow that is in al-Abyaḍ.’”"],[1699,"Khabbāb ibn al-Aratt said: “We complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was reclining upon his cloak in the shade of the Kaʿbah. We said to him, ‘Will you not seek victory for us? Will you not supplicate to Allah for us?’ “He said: ʿAmong those before you, a man would have a pit dug for him in the ground and be placed inside it. Then a saw would be brought and placed upon his head, and he would be split into two halves, yet that would not turn him away from his religion. Iron combs would be drawn through his flesh down to his bones and sinews, yet that would not turn him away from his religion. ‘By Allah, this matter will surely be completed until a rider travels from Ṣanʿāʾ to Ḥaḍramawt, fearing none but Allah—or the wolf concerning his sheep—but you are being impatient.’”"],[1700,"Abū Nawfal said: “I saw ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr on the pass of Madīnah, and Quraysh and the people were passing by him, until ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar passed by. He stopped and said: ʿPeace be upon you, O Abū Khubayb. Peace be upon you, O Abū Khubayb. Peace be upon you, O Abū Khubayb. ʿBy Allah, I used to forbid you from this. By Allah, I used to forbid you from this. By Allah, I used to forbid you from this. ‘By Allah, as far as I knew you, you were one who fasted often, prayed much, and maintained ties of kinship. By Allah, a nation of which you are the worst member is certainly a good nation.’ “Then ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar departed. “When al-Ḥajjāj was informed of where ʿAbdullāh had stood and what he had said, he sent someone to the body, and Ibn al-Zubayr was taken down from the wooden beam and thrown into the graves of the Jews. “Then al-Ḥajjāj sent for his mother, Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr, but she refused to come to him. He sent the messenger back to her saying, ‘Either you come to me, or I will send someone who will drag you by your braids.’ “She refused and said, ‘By Allah, I will not come to you until you send someone to drag me by my braids.’ “He said, ‘Bring me my sandals.’ He took his sandals and hurried along until he entered upon her. “He said, ‘What do you think of what I have done to the enemy of Allah?’ “She replied, ʿI see that you have ruined his worldly life, while he has ruined your Hereafter. ʿI have been informed that you used to say to him, “O son of the woman of the two waist-belts!” By Allah, I am indeed the woman of the two waist-belts. As for one of them, I used it to secure the food of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr upon the riding animals. As for the other, it was the waist-belt that a woman cannot do without. ‘The Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed us that “Among Thaqīf there will be a liar and a destroyer.” As for the liar, we have already seen him. As for the destroyer, I do not think it is anyone but you.’ “So he stood up and left her without responding.”"],[1701,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās said: “Musaylimah the Liar came during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying, ‘If Muḥammad appoints me as his successor after him, I will follow him.’ “He came with a large number of his people. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went toward him accompanied by Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ held a piece of palm stalk in his hand. “He stood before Musaylimah among his companions and said: ‘Even if you asked me for this piece, I would not give it to you. You will never escape what Allah has decreed concerning you. If you turn away, Allah will surely destroy you. I indeed see you as the one concerning whom I was shown what I was shown. And this is Thābit; he will answer you on my behalf.’ “Then he turned away from him.” Ibn ʿAbbās said: “I asked about the statement of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: ‘I indeed see you as the one concerning whom I was shown what I was shown.’ “Abū Hurayrah informed me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘While I was sleeping, I saw two bracelets of gold on my hands, and their matter concerned me. It was revealed to me in the dream that I should blow upon them, so I blew upon them and they flew away. I interpreted them as two liars who would appear after me.’\n“One of them was al-ʿAnsī, and the other was Musaylimah.”"],[1702,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “While I was sleeping, the treasures of the earth were brought to me, and two bracelets of gold were placed in my hands. They became burdensome to me, so it was revealed to me that I should blow upon them. I blew upon them and they disappeared. I interpreted them as the two liars between whom I am situated: the man of Ṣanʿāʾ and the man of al-Yamāmah.”"],[1703,"Jābir reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from a journey. When he came near Madīnah, a violent wind arose that almost buried the rider. He reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “This wind has been sent because of the death of a hypocrite.” When he arrived in Madīnah, it was found that a prominent hypocrite had died."],[1704,"Abū Shahm said: “A slave girl passed by me in Madīnah, and I grabbed her by her side. “The next morning, the Messenger—meaning the Prophet ﷺ—was accepting the people’s pledges of allegiance. I came to him, but he did not accept my pledge. “He said: ‘The one who grabbed her!’ “I said, ‘By Allah, I will never do it again.’ “Then he accepted my pledge.”"],[1705,"Al-Faltān ibn ʿĀṣim said: “We were sitting with the Prophet ﷺ in the mosque when he fixed his gaze upon a man walking in the mosque. “He said, ‘O so-and-so, do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah?’ “He replied, ‘No.’ “He asked, ‘Do you read the Torah?’ “He replied, ‘Yes.’ “He asked, ‘And the Gospel?’ “He replied, ‘Yes.’ “He asked, ‘And the Qurʾān?’ “He replied, ‘By the One in Whose hand is my soul, if I wished, I could recite it.’ “Then he questioned him and said: ‘Do you find mention of me in the Torah and the Gospel?’ “He replied, ‘We find someone like you, a nation like your nation, and a place of emergence like yours. We had hoped that he would arise among us. But when you appeared, we feared that you might be him. Then we examined the description and found that you were not he.’ “He ﷺ asked, ‘Why is that?’ “The man replied, ‘With him will be seventy thousand from his nation upon whom there will be no reckoning and no punishment, whereas only a small number are with you.’ “He ﷺ said: ‘By the One in Whose hand is my soul, I am indeed that man, and it is indeed my nation. And they will be more than seventy thousand, and seventy thousand, and seventy thousand.’”"],[1706,"A man from the Anṣār said: “We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for a funeral. When we returned, we met the messenger of a Qurashī woman. “He said, ‘O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so invites you and those with you to a meal.’ “So he turned aside toward her, and we went with him. We sat in the places of boys in front of their fathers. “Then the food was brought. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ put his hand toward it, and the people put their hands toward it. “They noticed that he was chewing a morsel but could not swallow it, so they withdrew their hands. They initially forgot about us, then remembered us and took hold of our hands. A man would strike the morsel from our hands until it fell. “Then they kept hold of our hands and watched what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would do. He spat the morsel out and threw it away, saying: ‘I sense that this is the meat of a sheep that was taken without its owners’ permission.’ “The woman stood and said, ‘O Messenger of Allah, I had wanted to gather you and those with you for a meal. I sent someone to al-Baqīʿ but found no sheep for sale. ʿĀmir ibn Abī Waqqāṣ had purchased a sheep from al-Baqīʿ yesterday, so I sent someone to him saying, “Find me a sheep in al-Baqīʿ.” None was found, and I was told that you had purchased a sheep, so I sent to you asking that it be sent to me. The messenger did not find him, but found his family, and they handed it over to my messenger.’ “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘Feed it to the prisoners.’”"],[1707,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās said: “During the illness from which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, he came out wrapped in a sheet, with a dark-colored band tied around his head, until he sat upon the pulpit. “He praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: ‘To proceed: The people will increase in number while the Anṣār will decrease, until they become among the people like salt in food. Whoever among you is placed in authority over something by which he may harm some people and benefit others, let him accept the good deeds of the virtuous among the Anṣār and overlook the faults of those among them who do wrong.’ “This was the last gathering in which the Prophet ﷺ sat.”"],[1708,"Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “One hundred years will not pass while an eye that now blinks remains alive.” In another narration: “Indeed, Allah, Blessed and Most High, will send a wind at the end of one hundred years, and it will take the soul of every believer.”"],[1709,"Nāfiʿ ibn ʿUtbah said: “We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on an expedition when some people came to the Prophet ﷺ from the direction of the west, wearing woolen garments. They met him near a small hill while they were standing and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was seated. “I said to myself, ‘Go to them and stand between them and him, lest they assassinate him.’ “Then I thought, ‘Perhaps he is speaking privately with them.’ “So I went and stood between them and him. I memorized four statements from him, counting them on my hand. He said: ‘You will fight the Arabian Peninsula, and Allah will grant you victory over it. Then Persia, and Allah will grant you victory over it. Then you will fight the Romans, and Allah will grant you victory over them. Then you will fight the Dajjāl, and Allah will grant you victory over him.’ “Nāfiʿ then said, ‘O Jābir, we do not think that the Dajjāl will emerge until the Romans have been conquered.’”"],[1710,"ʿAwf ibn Mālik reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood among his Companions and said: “Is it poverty that you fear, or need, or is it this worldly life that concerns you? Indeed, Allah will open for you the lands of Persia and Rome, and the wealth of this world will be poured upon you abundantly, until nothing after me will cause you to deviate, if anything does, except it.”"],[1711,"Abū Dharr reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “You will conquer Egypt, a land in which the qīrāṭ is commonly mentioned. When you conquer it, treat its people well, for they have a covenant and ties of kinship—or he said: a covenant and ties through marriage. “And when you see two men disputing there over the space of a single brick, then leave it.” Abū Dharr said: “I later saw ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Shuraḥbīl ibn Ḥasanah and his brother Rabīʿah disputing over the space of a single brick, so I left Egypt.”"],[1712,"Abū Bakrah said: “Allah benefited me through a statement that I heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the events of the Camel, after I had nearly joined the people of the Camel and fought alongside them. “When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was informed that the people of Persia had appointed the daughter of Khosrow as their ruler, he said: ‘A people who entrust their affairs to a woman will never prosper.’”"],[1713,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “When Quraysh accused me of lying, I stood in al-Ḥijr, and Allah displayed Bayt al-Maqdis before me. I then began describing its signs to them while looking directly at it.”"],[1714,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I remember myself standing in al-Ḥijr while Quraysh questioned me about my Night Journey. They asked me about certain details of Bayt al-Maqdis that I had not firmly retained, and I became distressed with a distress unlike anything I had ever experienced. “Then Allah raised it before me so that I could look at it. They did not ask me about anything except that I informed them of it…” The hadith continues."],[1715,"Anas reported that when news reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Abū Sufyān was approaching, he consulted his Companions. “Abū Bakr spoke, but he turned away from him. Then ʿUmar spoke, but he turned away from him. “Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah stood and said, ‘Is it us you mean, O Messenger of Allah? By the One in Whose hand is my soul, if you commanded us to plunge into the sea, we would plunge into it. And if you commanded us to drive our camels all the way to Bark al-Ghimād, we would do so.’ “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then called the people to march. They set out until they encamped at Badr. “Some water-carriers of Quraysh came to them, among them a black slave belonging to Banū al-Ḥajjāj. They seized him. “The Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began asking him about Abū Sufyān and his companions. “He would say, ‘I have no knowledge of Abū Sufyān. But Abū Jahl, ʿUtbah, Shaybah, and Umayyah ibn Khalaf are here.’ “Whenever he said this, they would strike him. “He would then say, ‘Yes, I will tell you. Abū Sufyān is here.’ “When they stopped striking him and questioned him again, he would say, ‘I have no knowledge of Abū Sufyān, but Abū Jahl, ʿUtbah, Shaybah, and Umayyah ibn Khalaf are among the people.’ “Whenever he said this, they would strike him again. “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was standing in prayer. When he saw this, he finished and said: ‘By the One in Whose hand is my soul, you strike him when he tells you the truth, and you leave him alone when he lies to you!’ “Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘This is the place where so-and-so will be killed.’ “He would place his hand upon the ground and say: ‘Here, and here.’ “Not one of them moved away from the place where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had placed his hand.”"],[1716,"Thawbān reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Indeed, Allah drew the earth together for me, and I saw its eastern and western extremities. The dominion of my nation will surely reach all that was drawn together for me…” The hadith continues."],[1717,"Ḥārithah ibn Wahb said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “Give in charity, for soon a man will walk about carrying his charity, but the person to whom it is offered will say, ‘Had you brought it to us yesterday, I would have accepted it, but now I have no need of it.’ He will then find no one willing to accept it.”"],[1718,"Abū Mūsā reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “A time will certainly come upon the people when a man will go about carrying charity in gold but will find no one willing to take it from him. One man will be seen followed by forty women who seek refuge with him because of the scarcity of men and the abundance of women.”"],[1719,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: “The Hour will not be established until wealth becomes so abundant among you that it overflows, until the owner of wealth becomes concerned about who will accept his charity. He will offer it to someone, but the person to whom it is offered will say, ‘I have no need of it.’”"],[1720,"Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The Hour will not be established until a fire emerges from the land of the Ḥijāz that illuminates the necks of the camels at Buṣrā.”"],[1721,"Al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb al-Ḥaḍramī said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Busr showed me a mark upon the side of his head, so I placed my finger upon it. He said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed his finger upon it and then said, ‘You will certainly reach a century.’” Abū ʿAbdullāh said: He had long hair reaching his shoulders."],[1722,"Thawbān, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Two groups from my nation have been protected by Allah from the Fire: a group that will campaign in India, and a group that will be with ʿĪsā ibn Maryam.”"],[1723,"Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised us the campaign of India. If I live to see it, I will spend my life and wealth in it. If I am killed, I will be among the best of martyrs; and if I return, then I am Abu Hurayrah, the freed one."],[1724,"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh said: We returned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from a journey. When we reached one of the gardens belonging to Banū al-Najjār, there was a camel inside it that would charge anyone who entered the garden. They mentioned this to the Prophet ﷺ. He came to the garden and called the camel. It approached him with its muzzle lowered toward the ground until it knelt before him. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Bring me its halter.” He placed the halter upon it and handed it over to its owner. He then turned toward the people and said: “There is nothing between heaven and earth except that it knows I am the Messenger of Allah, apart from the disobedient among the jinn and humankind.”"],[1725,"قال الله تعالى: ﴿اِنَّ اللّٰهَ وَ مَلٰٓىِٕكَتَهٗ يُصَلُّوْنَ عَلَى النَّبِيِّ١ؕ يٰۤاَيُّهَا الَّذِيْنَ اٰمَنُوْا صَلُّوْا عَلَيْهِ وَ سَلِّمُوْا تَسْلِيْمًا۰۰۵۶﴾ [سورة الأحزاب: 56]"],[1726,"Allah the Exalted said: Indeed, Allah and His angels send blessings upon the Prophet. O you who believe! Send blessings upon him and greet him with abundant salām. [Sūrat al-Aḥzāb: 56]\nImām Sufyān al-Thawrī and other people of knowledge said: The ṣalāh from the Lord is mercy, and the ṣalāh from the angels is istighfār (seeking forgiveness). Imām al-Tirmidhī mentioned this after the ḥadīth of Abū Hurayrah (485), which is the ḥadīth that is coming, and al-Bukhārī mentioned, in the heading of the chapter before ḥadīth (4797), on the authority of Abū al-ʿĀliyah: The ṣalāh from Allah is His praising him before the angels, and the ṣalāh from the angels is supplication (duʿāʾ)."],[1727,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever sends one blessing upon me, Allah will send ten blessings upon him.”"],[1728,"Anas ibn Mālik narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever sends one blessing upon me, Allah will send ten blessings upon him, erase ten sins from him, and raise him by ten ranks.” Another narration states: “Whoever mentions me should send blessings upon me. Whoever sends one blessing upon me, Allah will send ten blessings upon him.”"],[1729,"Sahl ibn Saʿd narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out and encountered Abū Ṭalḥah, who stood and went forward to meet him. He said: “May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah! I can see happiness upon your face.” He replied: “Yes. Gabriel came to me just now and said: ‘O Muḥammad, whoever sends blessings upon you once, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, will record ten good deeds for him, erase ten evil deeds from him, and raise him by ten ranks.’” Ibn Ḥabīb, one of the narrators of the hadith, said: “I do not know it except that he also said: ‘And the angels send blessings upon him ten times.’”"],[1730,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whoever neglects to send blessings upon me has missed the path to Paradise.”"],[1731,"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Allah has angels who travel throughout the earth, conveying to me the greetings of peace from my nation.”"],[1732,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Do not make your houses into graves, and do not make my grave a place of recurring celebration. Send blessings upon me, for your blessings reach me wherever you may be.”"],[1733,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “No one sends greetings of peace upon me except that Allah restores my soul to me so that I may return his greeting of peace.”"],[1734,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā said: Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah met me and said: “Shall I give you a gift? The Prophet ﷺ came out to us, and we said, ‘O Messenger of Allah, we have learned how to greet you with peace, but how should we send blessings upon you?’” He replied: “Say: ‘O Allah, send blessings upon Muḥammad and upon the family of Muḥammad, as You sent blessings upon the family of Abraham. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy and Glorious. O Allah, bless Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad, as You blessed the family of Abraham. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy and Glorious.’”"],[1735,"Abū Ḥumayd al-Sāʿidī narrated: They asked: “O Messenger of Allah, how should we send blessings upon you?” He replied: “Say: ‘O Allah, send blessings upon Muḥammad, his wives, and his descendants, as You sent blessings upon the family of Abraham. Bless Muḥammad, his wives, and his descendants, as You blessed the family of Abraham. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy and Glorious.’”"],[1736,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī narrated: We said: “O Messenger of Allah, this is how we greet you with peace, but how should we send blessings upon you?” He replied: “Say: ‘O Allah, send blessings upon Muḥammad, Your servant and Messenger, as You sent blessings upon Abraham. Bless Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad, as You blessed Abraham and the family of Abraham.’”"],[1737,"Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to us while we were in the gathering of Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah. Bashīr ibn Saʿd said to him: “Allah has commanded us to send blessings upon you, O Messenger of Allah. How should we send blessings upon you?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained silent until we wished that Bashīr had not asked him. He then said: “Say: ‘O Allah, send blessings upon Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad, as You sent blessings upon Abraham. Bless Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad, as You blessed the family of Abraham throughout the worlds. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy and Glorious.’ As for the greeting of peace, it is as you have already been taught.”"],[1738,"Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillāh narrated: We asked: “O Messenger of Allah, how should blessings be sent upon you?” He replied: “Say: ‘O Allah, send blessings upon Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad, as You sent blessings upon Abraham and the family of Abraham. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy and Glorious. Bless Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad, as You blessed Abraham and the family of Abraham. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy and Glorious.’”"],[1739,"Zayd ibn Khārijah said: I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “How should blessings be sent upon you?” He replied: “Send blessings and strive earnestly in doing so, then say: ‘O Allah, bless Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad, as You blessed the family of Abraham. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy and Glorious.’”"],[1740,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: They asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “How should we send blessings upon you?” He replied: “Say: ‘O Allah, send blessings upon Muḥammad, and bless Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad, as You sent blessings and bestowed blessings upon Abraham and the family of Abraham throughout the worlds. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy and Glorious.’ As for the greeting of peace, it is as you have been taught.”"],[1741,"قال الله تعالى: ﴿اِنَّ اللّٰهَ وَ مَلٰٓىِٕكَتَهٗ يُصَلُّوْنَ عَلَى النَّبِيِّ١ؕ يٰۤاَيُّهَا الَّذِيْنَ اٰمَنُوْا صَلُّوْا عَلَيْهِ وَ سَلِّمُوْا تَسْلِيْمًا۰۰۵۶﴾ [الأحزاب: 56].\nAllah the Exalted said: Indeed, Allah and His angels send blessings upon the Prophet. O you who believe! Send blessings upon him and greet him with abundant salām. [Sūrat al-Aḥzāb: 56]"],[1742,"ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “No Muslim sends blessings upon me except that the angels continue sending blessings upon him for as long as he sends blessings upon me. Therefore, let the servant do little of that or much.”"],[1743,"Abū Hurayrah narrated: The Prophet ﷺ ascended the pulpit and said: “Āmīn. Āmīn. Āmīn.” He was asked: “O Messenger of Allah, you did not previously do this. What happened?” He replied: “Gabriel said to me: ‘May the nose of a servant be rubbed in the dust who lives to see both of his parents, or one of them, yet they do not become a cause for his entering Paradise.’ I said: ‘Āmīn.’ Then he said: ‘May the nose of a servant be rubbed in the dust who reaches Ramadan and is not forgiven.’ I said: ‘Āmīn.’ Then he said: ‘May the nose of a man be rubbed in the dust in whose presence you are mentioned but he does not send blessings upon you.’ I said: ‘Āmīn.’”"],[1744,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “May the nose of a man be rubbed in the dust in whose presence I am mentioned but he does not send blessings upon me. May the nose of a man be rubbed in the dust who reaches Ramadan, but it ends before he is forgiven. May the nose of a man be rubbed in the dust whose parents reach old age in his presence, yet they do not become a cause for his entering Paradise.”"],[1745,"Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The miser is the one in whose presence I am mentioned, yet he does not send blessings upon me.”"],[1746,"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ narrated that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say: “When you hear the caller to prayer, repeat what he says. Then send blessings upon me, for whoever sends one blessing upon me, Allah will send ten blessings upon him because of it. Then ask Allah to grant me al-Wasīlah, for it is a rank in Paradise that is appropriate for only one servant from among the servants of Allah, and I hope that I will be that servant. Whoever asks Allah to grant me al-Wasīlah will become entitled to my intercession.”"],[1747,"Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard a man supplicating during his prayer without first glorifying Allah or sending blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “This man has been hasty.” He then called him and said to him—or to someone else: “When one of you prays, let him begin by glorifying and praising his Lord. Then let him send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ. After that, let him supplicate for whatever he wishes.”"],[1748,"Abū Umāmah ibn Sahl ibn Ḥunayf said: “The Sunnah in the funeral prayer is that one pronounces the takbīr, then recites the Opening of the Qur’an, then sends blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, and then sincerely supplicates for the deceased. Recitation is performed only after the first takbīr. He then quietly gives the greeting of peace to his right.”"],[1749,"ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Qārī—who, during the caliphate of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, was responsible for the public treasury together with ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Arqam—narrated: ʿUmar went out one night during Ramadan, and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Qārī accompanied him. They walked around the mosque and found its people divided into scattered groups. Some men were praying individually, while others were leading small groups in prayer. ʿUmar said: “By Allah, I think that gathering these people behind a single reciter would be better.” ʿUmar then resolved to do so and instructed Ubayy ibn Kaʿb to lead them in prayer during Ramadan. Later, ʿUmar came out while the people were praying behind their reciter and said: “What an excellent innovation this is! Yet the prayer during the time in which you sleep is better than the prayer during the time in which you stand.” He was referring to the latter part of the night, although the people were performing the prayer during its earlier part. During the latter half of Ramadan, they would supplicate against the disbelievers: “O Allah, fight the disbelievers who obstruct people from Your path, deny Your messengers, and do not believe in Your promise. Divide their unity, cast terror into their hearts, and inflict upon them Your punishment and torment, O Allah of truth.” He would then send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, supplicate for the Muslims with whatever good he was able to ask, and seek forgiveness for the believers. When he had finished supplicating against the disbelievers, sending blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, seeking forgiveness for the believing men and women, and making his requests, he would say: “O Allah, You alone we worship. For You we pray and prostrate. Toward You we strive and hasten. We hope for Your mercy, our Lord, and fear Your severe punishment. Indeed, Your punishment will surely overtake those whom You oppose.” He would then pronounce the takbīr and descend into prostration."],[1750,"Abū Ḥumayd—or Abū Usayd—al-Anṣārī narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “When one of you enters the mosque, let him send greetings of peace upon the Prophet ﷺ and then say: ‘O Allah, open for me the gates of Your mercy.’ When he leaves, let him say: ‘O Allah, I ask You from Your bounty.’”"],[1751,"Abū Hurayrah narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “No people sit in a gathering in which they do not remember Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, except that it will be a source of regret for them on the Day of Resurrection, even if they enter Paradise because of other rewards.”"],[1752,"Jābir narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “No people gather and then disperse without remembering Allah and sending blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ except that they rise from something more foul-smelling than a carcass.”"],[1753,"Aws ibn Aws narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Among the best of your days is Friday. On it Adam was created, on it he died, on it the Trumpet will be blown, and on it the great blast will occur. Therefore, frequently send blessings upon me on that day, for your blessings are presented to me.” They asked: “O Messenger of Allah, how will our blessings be presented to you after your body has decayed?” He replied: “Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has forbidden the earth from consuming the bodies of the prophets.”"],[1754,"Ubayy ibn Kaʿb narrated: When two-thirds of the night had passed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would rise and say: “O people, remember Allah! Remember Allah! The first blast has come, followed by the second. Death has come with all that it contains. Death has come with all that it contains.” Ubayy said: I asked: “O Messenger of Allah, I frequently send blessings upon you. How much of my supplication should I devote to sending blessings upon you?” He replied: “Whatever you wish.” I asked: “One quarter?” He replied: “Whatever you wish, but if you increase it, that will be better for you.” I asked: “One half?” He replied: “Whatever you wish, but if you increase it, that will be better for you.” I asked: “Then two-thirds?” He replied: “Whatever you wish, but if you increase it, that will be better for you.” I said: “Shall I devote all of my supplication to sending blessings upon you?” He replied: “Then your worries will be taken care of, and your sins will be forgiven.”"],[1755,"The Companions used to send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ on other occasions as well, and all these forms are better than this:\n1- In sermons: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad (837) narrated: Manṣūr ibn Abī Muzāḥim narrated to us, Khālid al-Zayyāt narrated to us, from ʿAwn ibn Abī Juḥayfah, who said: My father was among the guards of ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, and he was beneath the minbar. My father narrated to me that ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, ascended the minbar, praised and glorified Allah the Exalted, sent blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ and said: “The best of this Ummah after its Prophet is Abū Bakr, and the second is ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both.” And he said: “Allah the Exalted places good wherever He wishes.” Its chain is ḥasan, because Khālid al-Zayyāt is acceptable (there is no objection to him).\nAnd the narration of sending blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ in the sermon is also reported from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, may Allah be pleased with them, and others.\nIbn al-Qayyim said in Jalāʾ al-Afhām (p. 526): Sending blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ in sermons was a well-known and famous matter among the Companions, may Allah be pleased with them."],[1756,"2- At the visit to the grave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: It is narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, would stand at (near) the grave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then send blessings upon the noble Prophet ﷺ and supplicate for Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both."],[1757,"It is narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār, who said: I saw ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, standing at (near) the grave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then he would send blessings upon the noble Prophet ﷺ and supplicate for Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both.\nAnd the scholars have objected to this narration of Yaḥyā, as Ibn ʿAbd al-Barr mentioned in his book."],[1758,"The athar from Sayyidunā Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, is narrated through other chains. Among them, Imām al-Bayhaqī narrated in Shuʿab al-Īmān (3854) that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bishr informed him, ʿUbayd Allāh informed him, Nāfiʿ informed him, from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that when he returned from a journey he would first go to the grave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, send ṣalāh and salām upon him, and supplicate for himself, and he would not touch the grave. After that he would greet Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq with salām, and after that he would say thus (السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكَ يَا أَبَةِ) Peace be upon you, O my respected father."],[1759,"3- On the hills of Ṣafā and Marwah: Wahb ibn al-Ajdaʿ says that I heard Sayyidunā ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, say: “When you stand on the hill of Ṣafā, say takbīr seven times, and between every two takbīrs praise and glorify Allah, send blessings upon the noble Prophet ﷺ, and supplicate for yourself, and do the same on the hill of Marwah too.”"],[1760,"Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated in his book *al-Ṣalāh ʿalā al-Nabī ﷺ (as mentioned in Jalāʾ al-Afhām p. 537): Hudbah ibn Khālid narrated to us, Hammām ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, from Nāfiʿ: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, would say takbīr three times on Ṣafā and would say: لا إله إلا الله وحده لا شريك له، له الملك وله الحمد وهو على كل شيء قدير, then he would send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, then supplicate, and would stand for a long time in supplication. Then he would do the same on Marwah as well. And its chain is also ṣaḥīḥ."],[1761,"§4- When going out to the marketplace or to a banquet and the like:\nʿĀmir ibn Shaqīq says that Abū Wāʾil said: Any servant of Allah who attends a gathering place or a dining table, then stands until he praises and glorifies Allah and sends blessings upon the noble Prophet ﷺ; and even if he goes to the marketplace, the most heedless of places, and then sits in that place, he should praise and glorify Allah and send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ. And its chain is ṣaḥīḥ."],[1762,"Many glad tidings regarding the sending of the noble Messenger ﷺ are found on the tongues of the noble prophets, peace be upon them. Such glad tidings are also found on the tongues of the followers of religions other than the heavenly religions, such as Hinduism, Buddhism, Jainism and Zoroastrianism, etc. Among these glad tidings is the supplication of Sayyidunā Ibrāhīm, peace be upon him, which he made after settling his wife Hājar, peace be upon her, and his infant son Sayyidunā Ismāʿīl, peace be upon him, in a barren valley near the Sacred House of Allah.\n﴿رَبَّنَاۤ اِنِّيْۤ اَسْكَنْتُ مِنْ ذُرِّيَّتِيْ بِوَادٍ غَيْرِ ذِيْ زَرْعٍ عِنْدَ بَيْتِكَ الْمُحَرَّمِ١ۙ رَبَّنَا لِيُقِيْمُوا الصَّلٰوةَ فَاجْعَلْ اَفْىِٕدَةً مِّنَ النَّاسِ تَهْوِيْۤ اِلَيْهِمْ وَ ارْزُقْهُمْ مِّنَ الثَّمَرٰتِ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَشْكُرُوْنَ۰۰۳۷﴾ (Ibrāhīm: 37)\nOur Lord! Indeed, I have settled some of my offspring in a valley without cultivation, near Your Sacred House (the Kaʿbah). Our Lord! So that they may establish the prayer; so make the hearts of some people incline towards them, and provide them with all kinds of fruits, so that they may be grateful.\nIn the Old Testament it is stated that Sayyidunā Ismāʿīl, peace be upon him, will be the father of a great nation. (Holy Bible, Genesis: 18/21) This is the blessing of this supplication of Sayyidunā Ibrāhīm, peace be upon him:\n﴿رَبَّنَا وَ ابْعَثْ فِيْهِمْ رَسُوْلًا مِّنْهُمْ يَتْلُوْا عَلَيْهِمْ اٰيٰتِكَ وَ يُعَلِّمُهُمُ الْكِتٰبَ وَ الْحِكْمَةَ وَ يُزَكِّيْهِمْ١ؕ اِنَّكَ اَنْتَ الْعَزِيْزُ الْحَكِيْمُؒ۰۰۱۲۹﴾ (al-Baqarah: 129)\nOur Lord! Send among them a messenger from among themselves who will recite Your verses to them and teach them the Book and wisdom and purify them. Indeed, You alone are the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.\nSome of the glad tidings mentioned on the tongues of the noble prophets, peace be upon them, in the Old Testament and the New Testament are mentioned here."],[1763,"§(1) Glad tidings in the words of Sayyidunā Mūsā, peace be upon him\n“This is the blessing with which Moses the man of God blessed the children of Israel before his death. He said: The Lord came from Sinai and dawned over them from Seir; he shone forth from Mount Paran, and he came with myriads of holy ones; at his right hand was a fiery law for them.” (The Holy Bible, Deuteronomy, 261/33)\nIn this there is a clear indication of the messengership of three prophets: Sayyidunā Mūsā, Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, and Sayyidunā Muḥammad the Noble, the Prophet ﷺ. This is supported by this statement of the Lord:\n﴿وَ التِّيْنِ وَ الزَّيْتُوْنِۙ۰۰۱ وَ طُوْرِ سِيْنِيْنَۙ۰۰۲ وَ هٰذَا الْبَلَدِ الْاَمِيْنِۙ۰۰۳﴾ (al-Tīn: 1-3)\nBy the fig and the olive, and by Mount Sinai, and by this secure city!\nThe land to which Sayyidunā Ibrāhīm, peace be upon him, migrated, and where Sayyidunā al-Masīḥ, peace be upon him, was born, is the land where fig and olive trees are cultivated. This is what is meant by the saying “the Lord came from Sinai and dawned over them from Seir.” On Mount Sinai, Allah the Exalted spoke to Sayyidunā Mūsā, peace be upon him. Mount Paran is in Makkah, and it is what is meant by the “secure city.” The proof that Paran is Makkah, the birthplace of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, is this: in the Torah, where the migration of Sayyidunā Ibrāhīm, peace be upon him, from the house of his wife Sayyidah Sarah with Sayyidah Hājar, peace be upon her, is mentioned, it says: “And God heard the voice of the boy, and the angel of God called to Hagar from heaven and said to her: What troubles you, Hagar? Do not be afraid, for God has heard the voice of the boy from where he is. Arise, lift up the boy and hold him with your hand. I will make him a great nation. Then God opened her eyes, and she saw a well of water, and she went and filled the skin with water and gave the boy a drink. And God was with the boy, and he grew up and lived in the wilderness and became an archer. And he lived in the wilderness of Paran, and his mother took a wife for him from the land of Egypt.” (The Holy Bible, Genesis: 17/21-21)\n§(2) The glad tidings of Isaiah, peace be upon him\nIsaiah, peace be upon him, is one of the greatest prophets of the Children of Israel. He settled in Jerusalem. His era was 800 or 700 years before the Common Era. He was cut in pieces with a saw, as is indicated in the Second Book of Kings (16/21). This was done on the order of King Manasseh son of Hezekiah, who became ruler of the province of Judah in 693 BCE. His rule began with acts of disbelief and with harshness and severity. The Book of Isaiah is number 23 in the Old Testament. It contains many glad tidings. We will mention some of them in the chapter on the Seal of the Prophets ﷺ.\n“Sing to the Lord a new song, and his praise from the end of the earth! You who go down to the sea, and all that is in it, you islands and their inhabitants! Let the wilderness and its towns lift up their voice, the villages that Kedar inhabits. Let the inhabitants of Sela sing; let them shout from the tops of the mountains.” (The Holy Bible, Isaiah: 10/42, 11)\nIn its purpose this glad tiding is even clearer than the first one, for it explicitly mentions the sending of a prophet in the Arabian Peninsula. The details are as follows:\n1) “Sing to the Lord a new song.” The new song here means a new law. It is also clear that ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, was not a prophet with an independent law; rather, he came to complete the law of Sayyidunā Mūsā, peace be upon him. Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, cannot be taken as the one meant by this glad tiding either, for several reasons which we will mention later. Inevitably, the one meant is Muḥammad the Noble ﷺ alone, because he alone is characterized by the attributes mentioned in it.\n2) “Let the wilderness and its towns lift up their voice, the villages that Kedar inhabits.” It is well known that Kedar was the name of the eldest son of Sayyidunā Ismāʿīl, peace be upon him, and that they lived in the Arabian Peninsula. Thus this passage is a clear text that the prophet with the new law would be sent among the Arabs, and that through his prophethood the people of the peninsula would receive extraordinary honor and standing. All of this indeed happened with the sending of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\n3) “Let the inhabitants of Sela sing; let them shout from the tops of the mountains. Let them give glory to the Lord and declare his praise in the islands.” These words of the glad tiding are also a clear proof of the Prophet’s mission. It is well known that Hajj is one of the five basic pillars of Islam. Mount ʿArafāt and the valley adjoining it have special importance in the supplications of Hajj, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Hajj is ʿArafah,” meaning standing at the plain of ʿArafāt, where the guests of the Most Merciful gathered from all parts of the world, and the pilgrims make supplication, glorification and remembrance. Those who first performed Hajj in the company of Muḥammad the Noble ﷺ were the inhabitants of Sela, who were the people of Madinah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ settled in Madinah, at Sela, and in the Farewell Pilgrimage he went for Hajj with his companions for the first time; at that time their number was close to one hundred thousand. They gathered from all around the Arabian Peninsula, calling out the talbiyah. He stayed with his companions on the mountain at ʿArafah. In this way the glad tiding came true, and the inhabitants of Sela sang from the tops of the mountains…\n§(3) Another glad tiding in the words of Isaiah, peace be upon him:\n“Behold my servant, whom I uphold, my chosen one, in whom my soul delights. I have put my Spirit upon him; he will bring forth justice to the nations. He will not cry out or raise his voice, nor will his voice be heard in the streets. A bruised reed he will not break, and a smoldering wick he will not quench. He will faithfully bring forth justice. He will not grow faint or be discouraged till he has established justice in the earth. The islands wait for his law… Thus says God: I, the Lord, have called you in righteousness; I will hold you by the hand and keep you; I will give you as a covenant for the people and a light for the nations, to open the eyes of the blind, to bring out the prisoners from the dungeon, and those who sit in darkness from the prison house.” (The Holy Bible: Isaiah 1/42-7)\nAbū ʿUbaydah (d. 582 AH), after quoting this passage, writes: This is entirely clear about Muḥammad the Noble ﷺ and his attributes. It is enough as a proof of his truthfulness. Then for what reasons are such explicit statements applied to anyone other than Muḥammad the Noble ﷺ? (Abū ʿUbaydah al-Khazrajī, Bayna al-Islām wa al-Masīḥiyyah, p. 225)\nThis passage contains several matters which clearly indicate that Muḥammad the Noble ﷺ is the one meant by this glad tiding, and they are as follows:\n(1) “He will bring forth justice to the nations.” It is not at all possible to take Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, as the one meant by this glad tiding, because Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, was not sent to any nation other than the Hebrews, as he himself stated clearly when he said: “I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.” (The Holy Bible, Gospel of Matthew: 24/15)\nThis statement of Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, is confirmed in the Noble Qur’an in this way:\n﴿وَ رَسُوْلًا اِلٰى بَنِيْۤ اِسْرَآءِيْلَ١ۙ۬﴾ (Āl ʿImrān: 49)\nHe was sent as a messenger to the Children of Israel.\nSayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, said to his disciples at the time of preaching: “Do not go among the Gentiles, and do not enter any town of the Samaritans, but go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.” (The Holy Bible, Gospel of Matthew: 5/10, 6)"],[1764,"Whereas Muḥammad the Noble ﷺ was sent for all of humanity. Imām al-Bukhārī, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have been given five things which were not given to any prophet before me: I have been made victorious by terror cast into the enemy from a distance of a month’s journey. The whole earth has been made a place of prayer and a means of purification for me, so whoever of my Ummah finds the time of prayer has come, let him pray right there. Spoils of war have been made lawful for me. Every prophet was sent to his own particular people, and I have been sent to all people. And I have been given the right of intercession.”\n(2) “He will not grow faint or be discouraged till he has established justice in the earth.” This passage also makes clear that the prophet of whom Sayyidunā Isaiah gave news will stand before the enemy with full strength and power, will overcome the enemy, and will make the truth prevail on the earth. The Christians have made every failed effort to apply this glad tiding to Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him. They claim that Sayyidunā ʿĪsā was victorious, and that he was crucified on the order of the ruler of the time. Could there be any greater failure and sheer insolence than this?\n(3) “The islands wait for his law.” These words also clearly indicate the universal call and messengership of the awaited prophet. It is also well known that the Jews settled in the Arabian Peninsula to await and welcome this awaited prophet. For this very reason they came here from all around.\n(4) “I will hold you by the hand and keep you, and I will give you as a covenant for the people and a light for the nations.” In this there is a clear indication that the awaited prophet would convey his prophethood and messengership with the protection and help of Allah the Exalted, and no one would be able to harm him. Likewise it indicates that the messengership of this prophet would be general, for the Children of Israel and other nations. These attributes apply only to him ﷺ, as we have explained before.\n§(4) Testimony in the words of Sayyidunā Daniel:\n“You, O king, saw a great statue. That great statue, whose brightness was immense, stood before you, and its appearance was terrifying. The head of that statue was of pure gold, its chest and its arms of silver, its belly and its thighs of bronze, its legs of iron, and its feet partly of iron and partly of clay. You watched until a stone was cut out without hands, and it struck the statue on its feet of iron and clay and broke them in pieces. Then the iron, the clay, the bronze, the silver and the gold were broken in pieces together, and became like the chaff of the summer threshing floors, and the wind carried them away so that no trace of them was found. And the stone that struck the statue became a great mountain and filled the whole earth.\nThis is the dream, and I will tell its interpretation before the king. You, O king, are a king of kings, to whom the God of heaven has given the kingdom, the power, the might and the glory; and wherever the children of men dwell, he has given the beasts of the field and the birds of the air into your hand and has made you ruler over them all. You are the head of gold. After you there will arise another kingdom inferior to you, and then another, a third kingdom of bronze, which shall rule over all the earth. And the fourth kingdom shall be strong as iron, and just as iron breaks to pieces and subdues all things, so it will break and crush. And as you saw that the feet and toes were partly of clay and partly of iron, the kingdom shall be divided; but, as you saw iron mixed with clay, there shall be in it some of the strength of iron. And as the toes of the feet were partly iron and partly clay, so the kingdom shall be partly strong and partly brittle. And as you saw the iron mixed with clay, they will mix with one another in marriage among the children of men, but they will not hold together, just as iron does not mix with clay. And in the days of those kings the God of heaven will set up a kingdom that shall never be destroyed, and its sovereignty shall not be left to another people; rather it shall break in pieces and bring an end to all these kingdoms, and it shall stand forever. Just as you saw that a stone was cut from the mountain without hands, and it broke in pieces the iron, the bronze, the clay, the silver and the gold, God Almighty has shown the king what is to be hereafter. The dream is certain, and its interpretation sure.” (The Holy Bible, Daniel: 31/2-45)\nThis passage of the Torah mentions the king’s dream and its interpretation. In the dream he saw a frightening scene. To interpret the dream he summoned the magicians and astrologers to his court, and threatened them with death if the interpretation proved wrong. He ordered these people to tell him his dream as well, without his relating it to them, because he himself had forgotten the dream. The purpose of the king in doing this was to find out the reality and truthfulness of these people and to learn the interpretation of the dream. No one was able to answer the king. The king issued an order for their execution, and they were to be put to death. This news reached Sayyidunā Daniel. He is one of the four great prophets of the Mosaic law. In order to save them from being killed, Allah the Exalted favored him by informing him through inspiration of the dream and its interpretation. He appeared in the royal court and told the king his dream and its interpretation, at which the king was very pleased. In this interpretation Sayyidunā Daniel had told that at the end the Prophet of Islam would be sent. The dream and interpretation told by Sayyidunā Daniel proved true. The periods that Sayyidunā Daniel described also proved true. For example:\n1: 701 BCE, the rule of Babylon. Sayyidunā Daniel ruled in the time of Nebuchadnezzar. This period of kingship was indicated by the head of gold.\n2: 612 BCE, the Chaldean kingdom in the era of the Medes, which was indicated by silver.\n3: 326 BCE, the Greek kingdom in the era of Alexander of Macedon, which was indicated by bronze.\n4: 53 BCE, the Roman empire in the era of Pompey, which was indicated by iron.\n5: 612 CE, the Byzantine empire in the West and the Sasanian Persian empire in the East.\n6: 647 CE, the state of Islam. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent letters of invitation to the kings of the world. Sacred jihad was undertaken to defend against the enemy. The Byzantine empire of the West was destroyed and the Persian empire of the East was ravaged.\n§(5) A testimony in the section of Deuteronomy is mentioned in this way:\n“The Lord your God will raise up for you, from among you, from your brothers, a prophet like me. To him you shall listen. This is according to all that you asked of the Lord your God, that I should not hear the voice of the Lord my God again, nor see this great fire any more, lest I die. And the Lord said to me: What they have said is right. I will raise up for them a prophet like you from among their brothers, and I will put my words in his mouth, and he shall speak to them all that I command him. And whoever will not listen to my words that he speaks in my name, I will require it of him. But the prophet who presumes to speak in my name a word that I have not commanded him to speak, or who speaks in the name of other gods, that prophet shall be put to death.” (The Holy Bible, Deuteronomy: 15/18-20)\nLikewise it is mentioned in the Book of Acts of the New Testament: “This is that Moses who said to the Children of Israel: God will raise up for you a prophet like me from among your brothers.” (The Holy Bible, Acts: 37/7)\nThis same glad tiding is found in those speeches of Mūsā, peace be upon him, which he delivered before all the Children of Israel. “These are the words that Moses spoke to all Israel beyond the Jordan… Moses spoke these words to them according to all that the Lord had commanded him for the Children of Israel… In the land of Moab Moses undertook to explain this law, saying: The Lord our God spoke to us at Horeb, saying…” (The Holy Bible, Deuteronomy, 1/1-6). In the fourth part of the speeches it says: “O Israel, listen to the statutes and the rules that I teach you, and do them…” (The Holy Bible, Deuteronomy: 1/4). In this testament Sayyidunā Mūsā, peace be upon him, taught the obligations and commands, and also gave the glad tiding that a prophet of great rank would be sent. The Christians have made a failed attempt to apply this glad tiding to Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him. Peter’s statement is: “…and that he may send the Christ who has been appointed for you, that is Jesus… For Moses said: The Lord will raise up for you from among your brothers a prophet like me. Listen to whatever he says to you; and it will be that whoever does not listen to that prophet will be utterly destroyed from among the people.” (The Holy Bible, Acts: 20/3-23). There would be no objection to accepting that the one meant is Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, provided that Sayyidunā ʿĪsā was like Mūsā; but the reality is the exact opposite of this, for several reasons, such as:\n(1) The saying of Sayyidunā Mūsā, peace be upon him, is: “raise up a prophet from among their brothers.” It is well known that the brothers of the Children of Israel were those whose parents were relatives of one another; they shared the same lineage with them through the forefathers. The word “brother” is used in the Old Testament in different senses. In Numbers it says: “And Moses sent messengers from Kadesh to the king of Edom, and said: Thus says your brother Israel: You know all the hardships that have befallen us.” (The Holy Bible, Numbers: 14/20)\nHere the “brother of Israel” means the nation of Israel, that is, the descendants of Sayyidunā Yaʿqūb ibn Isḥāq. Israel is the title of Sayyidunā Yaʿqūb, just as the king of Edom was from the descendants of ʿĪṣ (Esau) ibn Isḥāq.\n! For this, see the Dictionary of the Holy Bible, entry “Edom.”\nThe word “brother” in this sense appears explicitly in Deuteronomy. It says: “And command the people: You are about to pass through the territory of your brothers the sons of Esau, who live in Seir.” (The Holy Bible, Deuteronomy: 4/2). From these passages it becomes completely clear that the awaited prophet whom Sayyidunā Mūsā, peace be upon him, mentioned was not from the descendants of Israel, Yaʿqūb ibn Isḥāq. For if he had been among them, it would have been said “from among you,” and not “from their brothers.” Nor was he from the descendants of ʿĪṣ ibn Isḥāq, because the historians agree that no prophet came from his line. Therefore the saying “a prophet from among their brothers” applies only to Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ﷺ. His blessed lineage joins with that of Sayyidunā Ibrāhīm, peace be upon him, the supreme forefather of the Children of Israel.\n(2) “Like you” or “like me”: These words are a clear text that the glad tiding of the awaited prophet means none other than Muḥammad the Noble ﷺ. He is the only prophet who was sent some time after Sayyidunā Mūsā, peace be upon him. The attributes of “like you” and “like me” which Sayyidunā Mūsā, peace be upon him, described apply to no one but him, and they are as follows:\n(a) Sayyidunā Mūsā, peace be upon him, held the rank of an independent new law, just as Sayyidunā Muḥammad al-Muṣṭafā ﷺ was honored with an independent new law. His law abrogated the earlier laws, whereas Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, was not a prophet with an independent law, as is his own statement: “I have not come to abolish the books of the prophets but to fulfill them.” (The Holy Bible, Matthew: 17/5)\n(b) Sayyidunā Mūsā, peace be upon him, married before being sent as a prophet and had children. In the Book of Exodus it is mentioned: “So Moses took his wife and his sons and set them on a donkey and returned to Egypt.” (The Holy Bible, Exodus: 20/4). Likewise the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married before prophethood and had children. Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, never married at all, and was raised up to the heavens in that same state.\n(c) Sayyidunā Mūsā, peace be upon him, married more than once after prophethood. His first wife was Zipporah, the daughter of the priest of Midian. (The Holy Bible, Exodus: 21/2). Then he married a Cushite woman, about whom Sayyidunā Hārūn and his sister Sayyidah Maryam, peace be upon her, spoke, and the Lord defended him. (The Holy Bible, Numbers: 1/12-14). Likewise the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married more than once, for which the Jews and Christians and those of like nature pointed fingers. They have always made his plural marriages a target of criticism, whereas Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, had not married even once.\n(d) Sayyidunā Mūsā, peace be upon him, was a warrior prophet and also led the Children of Israel in wars.1 Likewise Sayyidunā Muḥammad the Noble ﷺ took part in several battles against the enemies of the religion, whereas Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, used to say: “Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar’s, and to God the things that are God’s.” (The Holy Bible, Matthew: 21/22)\nFor details of this see: The Holy Bible: (Exodus: 8/17-13), (Numbers: 1/1-3).\n(e) Sayyidunā Mūsā, peace be upon him, was born in the natural way, through a mother and a father. The blessed birth of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was also in the same way, whereas Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, was born miraculously from a mother alone, without a father."],[1765,"(1) The author of the Gospel of John has narrated, in the words of John, that the Baptist is the one. The passage is as follows: “This is the testimony of John, when the Jews sent priests and Levites from Jerusalem to ask him: Who are you? He confessed and did not deny, but confessed: I am not the Christ. They asked him: Then who are you? Are you Elijah? He said: I am not. Are you the Prophet? He answered: No. So they said to him: Then who are you? so that we may give an answer to those who sent us. What do you say about yourself? He said: I am, as the prophet Isaiah said, the voice of one crying in the wilderness: Make straight the way of the Lord. These had been sent from the Pharisees. They asked him: If you are neither the Christ, nor Elijah, nor the Prophet, then why do you baptize? John answered them: I baptize with water; among you stands one whom you do not know, that is, the one who comes after me, the strap of whose sandal I am not worthy to untie. These things took place in Bethany beyond the Jordan, where John was baptizing.” (The Holy Bible: John: 19/1-28) The Children of Israel were awaiting the coming of three prophets: Sayyidunā al-Masīḥ, Elijah,1 and the Special Prophet.\n1 The second awaited prophet was Sayyidunā Ilyās, peace be upon him.\nThe author of the Gospel of John has also mentioned other glad tidings, which are found in various places. For example, in one place he says:\n(2) “If you love me, you will keep my commandments; and I will ask the Father, and he will give you another Helper, to be with you forever.” (The Holy Bible, John: 15/4, 16)\n(3) “These things I have spoken to you while I am still with you. But the Helper, the Holy Spirit, whom the Father will send in my name, he will teach you all things and bring to your remembrance all that I have said to you.” (The Holy Bible, John: 25/14, 26)\n(4) “I will not say much more to you, for the ruler of the world is coming, and he has nothing in me.” (The Holy Bible, John: 30/14)\n(5) “But when the Helper comes, whom I will send to you from the Father, the Spirit of truth, who proceeds from the Father, he will bear witness about me.” (The Holy Bible, John: 26/15)\n(6) “Nevertheless I tell you the truth: it is to your advantage that I go away, for if I do not go away, the Helper will not come to you; but if I go, I will send him to you. And when he comes, he will convict the world concerning sin and righteousness and judgment.” (The Holy Bible, John: 7/16, 8)\n(7) “I still have many things to say to you, but you cannot bear them now. But when he, the Spirit of truth, comes, he will guide you into all truth, for he will not speak on his own, but whatever he hears he will speak, and he will declare to you the things that are to come.” (The Holy Bible, John: 12/16, 13)\nThe glad tidings of John are mentioned in the Gospel, and they are completely clear in what they indicate. There is no need for much reflection; any person of knowledge and insight, with a little thought, would understand this reality: that these glad tidings apply, word for word and exactly, to our beloved Messenger, Muḥammad the Noble ﷺ. This is what is mentioned in this statement of the Lord:\n﴿وَ اِذْ قَالَ عِيْسَى ابْنُ مَرْيَمَ يٰبَنِيْۤ اِسْرَآءِيْلَ اِنِّيْ رَسُوْلُ اللّٰهِ اِلَيْكُمْ مُّصَدِّقًا لِّمَا بَيْنَ يَدَيَّ مِنَ التَّوْرٰىةِ وَ مُبَشِّرًۢا بِرَسُوْلٍ يَّاْتِيْ مِنْۢ بَعْدِي اسْمُهٗۤ اَحْمَدُ١ؕ﴾ (al-Ṣaff: 6)\nAnd when ʿĪsā son of Maryam said: O Children of Israel! Indeed I am the messenger of Allah to you, confirming what came before me of the Torah, and giving glad tidings of a messenger who will come after me, whose name is Aḥmad.\nThe reasons why the above glad tiding applies to Sayyidunā Muḥammad the Noble ﷺ are as follows.\n(1) Sayyidunā al-Masīḥ, peace be upon him, described the awaited prophet as one who would be possessed of honor and nobility, and whose coming would be after him. It is also well known and established that the sending of Sayyidunā Muḥammad al-Muṣṭafā ﷺ came directly after Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him. This statement of Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, is narrated in the Noble Qur’an:\n﴿وَ مُبَشِّرًۢا بِرَسُوْلٍ يَّاْتِيْ مِنْۢ بَعْدِي اسْمُهٗۤ اَحْمَدُ١ؕ﴾ (al-Ṣaff: 6)\nand giving glad tidings of a messenger who will come after me, whose name is Aḥmad.\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ also encouraged the Children of Israel in such a way that if they believed in him, their good deeds would not be lost; rather they would be rewarded, and he gave them the glad tiding of a double reward: one for believing in Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, and one for believing in Sayyidunā Muḥammad the Noble ﷺ.\n(2) This saying of Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him: “When he comes he will convict the world… concerning sin, because they do not believe in me.” (The Holy Bible, John: 8/16, 9) It is a true reality that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ established the prophethood of Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, with proofs and evidences. He also declared whoever does not believe in him to be a disbeliever, and warned him of a painful punishment. The Exalted Lord says:\n﴿اِذْ قَالَ اللّٰهُ يٰعِيْسٰۤى اِنِّيْ مُتَوَفِّيْكَ وَ رَافِعُكَ اِلَيَّ وَ مُطَهِّرُكَ مِنَ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْا وَ جَاعِلُ الَّذِيْنَ اتَّبَعُوْكَ فَوْقَ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْۤا اِلٰى يَوْمِ الْقِيٰمَةِ١ۚ ثُمَّ اِلَيَّ مَرْجِعُكُمْ فَاَحْكُمُ بَيْنَكُمْ فِيْمَا كُنْتُمْ فِيْهِ تَخْتَلِفُوْنَ۰۰۵۵﴾\n(Āl ʿImrān: 55)\nWhen Allah said: O ʿĪsā! Indeed I will take you fully and raise you to Myself, and cleanse you of those who disbelieved, and I will place those who follow you above those who disbelieved until the Day of Resurrection. Then to Me is your return, and I will judge between you concerning that over which you used to differ.\nIn another place the Lord says:\n﴿فَاَمَّا الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْا فَاُعَذِّبُهُمْ عَذَابًا شَدِيْدًا فِي الدُّنْيَا وَ الْاٰخِرَةِ١ٞ﴾ (Āl ʿImrān: 56)\nThen as for those who disbelieved, I will punish them with a severe punishment in this world and the Hereafter.\nA warning has also been given to those who hold wrong beliefs about the raising of Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, from the world. The Lord says concerning this:\n﴿وَّ قَوْلِهِمْ اِنَّا قَتَلْنَا الْمَسِيْحَ عِيْسَى ابْنَ مَرْيَمَ رَسُوْلَ اللّٰهِ١ۚ وَ مَا قَتَلُوْهُ وَ مَا صَلَبُوْهُ وَ لٰكِنْ شُبِّهَ لَهُمْ١ؕ وَ اِنَّ الَّذِيْنَ اخْتَلَفُوْا فِيْهِ لَفِيْ شَكٍّ مِّنْهُ١ؕ مَا لَهُمْ بِهٖ مِنْ عِلْمٍ اِلَّا اتِّبَاعَ الظَّنِّ١ۚ وَ مَا قَتَلُوْهُ يَقِيْنًۢاۙ۰۰۱۵۷﴾ (al-Nisāʾ: 157)\nAnd for their saying: “We killed the Messiah, ʿĪsā son of Maryam, the messenger of Allah,” whereas they did not kill him, nor did they crucify him, but it was made to appear so to them. Indeed those who differed about him are surely in doubt about it. They have no knowledge of it except following conjecture, and they certainly did not kill him.\n(3) “I still have many things to say to you…” In this there is an indication that the law of Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, was not complete, because human intellect had not yet reached its stage of perfection. Sayyidunā Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ﷺ is the meaning of the saying of Sayyidunā al-Masīḥ, peace be upon him, “the Spirit of truth.” He is the one who completes his law and confirms him. He kept in place those universal principles and basic teachings that were common to all and that undergo no change with time and place, such as tawḥīd, the divine attributes, the creation of the universe, and matters relating to Paradise and Hell. He abrogated those matters that were specific to a particular people and time. In place of these abrogated rulings he brought new rulings that were perfectly suited to new conditions and events.\n(4) The saying of Sayyidunā al-Masīḥ, peace be upon him, “he will not speak on his own…” It is completely explicit in this that the Noble Prophet ﷺ would give news of the unseen through the revelation of Allah. He does not say anything of his own. The Lord says:\n﴿ذٰلِكَ مِنْ اَنْۢبَآءِ الْغَيْبِ نُوْحِيْهِ اِلَيْكَ١ؕ وَ مَا كُنْتَ لَدَيْهِمْ اِذْ يُلْقُوْنَ اَقْلَامَهُمْ اَيُّهُمْ يَكْفُلُ مَرْيَمَ١۪ وَ مَا كُنْتَ لَدَيْهِمْ اِذْ يَخْتَصِمُوْنَ۰۰۴۴﴾ (Āl ʿImrān: 44)\n(O Prophet!) These are tidings of the unseen which We reveal to you. And you were not with them when they cast their pens to decide which of them would be the guardian of Maryam, and you were not with them when they were disputing.\n(8) “Jesus said to them: Have you never read in the Scriptures: The stone that the builders rejected has become the cornerstone; this was the Lord’s doing, and it is marvelous in our eyes? Therefore I tell you that the kingdom of God will be taken away from you and given to a people who will produce its fruit. And whoever falls on this stone will be broken to pieces, but when it falls on anyone, it will crush him. When the chief priests and the Pharisees heard his parables, they understood that he was speaking about them.” (The Holy Bible, Matthew: 42/21-45)\nIn these sentences Sayyidunā al-Masīḥ, peace be upon him, mentions the evil deeds of the Children of Israel: their failure to uphold the law of Sayyidunā Mūsā, peace be upon him, their failure to adhere to the law of the prophets who came after him, and their rebellion against the law of Allah; all of these matters are mentioned. Along with this, Sayyidunā al-Masīḥ warned them that Allah the Exalted would take His kingdom away from them and give this honor to some other nation, because it would uphold the law, and would also receive its fruits, and that is the Ummah of Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ﷺ. Sayyidunā al-Masīḥ, peace be upon him, giving an example of the conduct and evil deeds of the Jews, said: “There was a man, a master of a house, who planted a vineyard, put a fence around it, dug a winepress in it and built a tower, and leased it to tenants and went to a far country. When the season of fruit drew near, he sent his servants to the tenants to get his fruit. And the tenants took his servants and beat one, killed another, and stoned another. Again he sent other servants, more than the first, and they did the same to them. Finally he sent his son to them, saying: They will respect my son. But when the tenants saw the son, they said to one another: This is the heir. Come, let us kill him and seize his inheritance. And they took him and cast him out of the vineyard and killed him.\nWhen the owner of the vineyard comes, what will he do to those tenants?\nThey said to him: He will put those wretches to a miserable death and let out the vineyard to other tenants who will give him the fruit in its season.” (The Holy Bible, Matthew: 33/21-41)\nAfter this living example for the Children of Israel, Sayyidunā al-Masīḥ, peace be upon him, spoke the saying that we have mentioned before."],[1766,"There is also a hadith with this meaning: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Your life compared with the previous nations is only like the time between ʿAṣr and the setting of the sun. The people of the Torah were given the Torah; they worked until midday and then grew unable, and they were given one qīrāṭ each. Then the people of the Gospel were given the Gospel; they worked until the ʿAṣr prayer and then grew unable to work, and they too were given one qīrāṭ each for it. Then we were given the Qur’an; we worked until sunset and we were given two qīrāṭs each.”\nIn the passage of Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, which we quoted earlier, there are several matters worthy of attention for the people of knowledge, such as:\n(a) “The stone that the builders rejected has become the cornerstone.” From the context of this passage it is learned that the stone the Jews rejected is the prophethood of Muḥammad ﷺ. The chain of prophethood and messengership is like the walls of a palace, in which one brick is laid upon another. In this way the chain reached Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, which the Children of Israel neglected, and because of their evil deeds they were unable to carry it. Then Sayyidunā Muḥammad al-Muṣṭafā ﷺ was sent, and through him every gap was filled, the palace of prophethood was completed, and the chain of prophethood came to an end. With his being sent all previous prophethoods were abrogated. He was honored with excellence and nobility over all the prophets and messengers. In this way the saying of Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, proved true: “The stone that the builders rejected has become the cornerstone.”"],[1767,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ explained this very thing in this statement of his: “My likeness and the likeness of the prophets before me is like that of a man who built a beautiful palace. He adorned it and decorated it well, leaving out only the place of a single brick on one side. People walk around that palace and look at it, and say in wonder: Why has the place of this one brick been left?” He said: “I am that brick; I am the Seal of the Prophets.”\nThe Christians have always acknowledged the fact that John used a Greek word (PARACLETUS) for the awaited prophet. Its meaning is: one who calls to a place, one who calls for one’s help, one who warns, one who cautions, one who gives hope. In the Hellenistic language its meaning is rendered as al-Musallī, which means: one who becomes a cause and means of comfort and reassurance. But the commentators on the Gospels do not agree on the translation of this word. Origen translated the word as (CONSOLATOR) and sometimes as (DEPRECTOR). But some other translators do not agree with this translation, because this translation is contrary to the rules of Greek. For this reason some have translated the word as (TEACHER). Tertullian and Augustine preferred the word (ADVOCATE), while some others considered (ASSISTANT), (COMFORTER), (CONSOLER) and the like to be better. Another Greek word, (PERICLYTOS), is also found, whose meaning in every respect is exactly that of the word Muḥammad. Its derivation is the closest to the word (PARACLETUS). (Mawdūdī, Yahūdiyyat awr Naṣrāniyyat, p. 459)\nFrom this it becomes clear that Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, spoke this word in the Syriac language, while the writers of the Gospels translated it into Greek, which is not the original word uttered by the tongue of Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him. The present Gospels give no guidance at all about the reality of this word. It was the Muslim historians who, by deep research and investigation, clarified the reality that the original word is Munḥamannā."],[1768,"Al-ʿAllāmah Ibn Isḥāq narrated this saying of Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him: “Whoever has been hostile to me has in truth been hostile to the Lord the Exalted. If I had not done before them deeds that no one before me did, they would have had no fault. But they have already begun to boast. They have supposed that they will gain the upper hand over me and over the Lord. But what is in the Law must be fulfilled. They hated me and were hostile to me without right. So if only Munḥamannā had come before them, whom Allah the Exalted will send to you! These are those who will come from the Lord the Exalted; they will be my witnesses, and you too are my witnesses, for you have been with me from of old. I have said this to you plainly, so that you may not complain of lack of knowledge.”\nAl-ʿAllāmah Ibn Isḥāq says that al-Munḥamannā in Syriac has the same meaning as the word Muḥammad, and in Roman (Greek) it is expressed as al-Barqalīṭis."],[1769,"It is not a matter of surprise that Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, used the Syriac word Munḥamannā, which in Arabic is the word Muḥammad and in Greek is Fāraqlīṭ. Testimony also comes from the house of the Church. The king of Abyssinia, al-Najāshī, acknowledging this fact, said:\n“Welcome to you, and to those from whom you have come. I bear witness that he is a true prophet. He is the very one who is also mentioned in our Gospel. He is the very true prophet of whom ʿĪsā son of Maryam, peace be upon him, gave glad tidings. Stay wherever you wish. By Allah! If it were not for the matters of my state, I would have come to him and had the honor of carrying his sandals.”\nBefore clarifying the meaning and purpose of these testimonies, it is necessary to explain the language of the people of Palestine in the time of Sayyidunā al-Masīḥ. The Syriac (SYRIAC) language was that of Aramaic. For the Hebrew language had ended in the time of the Seleucids (SELEUCIDE), about two hundred and fifty years before Sayyidunā al-Masīḥ, peace be upon him. The Greek language was limited, on a small scale, to matters relating to government functions and to a particular class. The people of Palestine spoke in a particular dialect of Syriac. From this it is possible that the present Gospels, which were written later, were written in the Greek language. Likewise the sayings and statements of Sayyidunā al-Masīḥ were not recorded in the original Syriac language. The writers of the Gospels compiled these Gospels relying on narrations famous among the people. Then they made their translations in Greek, the language of knowledge and culture. On this basis it is also not far-fetched that Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, mentioned the name of the awaited prophet as Aḥmad, just as is mentioned in the Qur’an. Then those who compiled the Gospels translated it into their own languages, and in this way a difference arose in its translation.\nHere a question arises: what was the source of al-Najāshī’s knowledge and awareness about the awaited prophet? The Gospel of John, or something else?\nThe answer can be of two kinds. The original copy of the Gospel of John may have existed up to the time of al-Najāshī, and al-Najāshī may have seen it and read in it the attributes of the awaited prophet. Or a copy of the Gospel of Barnabas, whose reading was forbidden to people for about a hundred years, may have reached al-Najāshī, in which his blessed name and his attributes and qualities are mentioned, as some parts of the Gospel of Barnabas will be mentioned shortly. Al-Najāshī may also have learned of the matters mentioned in this same Gospel. Information passed down orally from chest to chest without writing could also be a source of al-Najāshī’s knowledge. For a seeker of the truth, all these things are possible. One who keeps striving to reach the truth becomes a reference and source in this reality, and in the end he does find the truth. The reality is known only to Allah the Exalted."],[1770,"! According to the preponderant view, Barnabas was a disciple of Sayyidunā al-Masīḥ, peace be upon him, as is also explicitly stated in his Gospel. In the early fifth century CE, 401 CE, the grave of Barnabas was discovered in Cyprus. From it the Gospel was also found, which Mark had placed on Barnabas’s chest at the time of his burial. This news spread quickly in all directions. Scholars and preachers began to rely on this portion regarding the glad tiding of the awaited prophet.\nIn 492 CE the first Pope, Julius, took his seat on the papal throne. He declared reading the Gospel of Barnabas forbidden, because it contained a great deal that was contrary to the other Gospels, especially about the divinity of Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him. Then, until the eighteenth century, this Gospel disappeared from people’s sight. Then Cramer, a special adviser to the king of Prussia, found a copy in the Italian language. It was translated into English and then from that into Arabic. For further details see: Dirāsāt fī al-Yahūdiyyah wa al-Masīḥiyyah wa Adyān al-Hind, pp. 405, 415. It is not far-fetched that Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, mentioned the name of the awaited prophet as Aḥmad, as is in the Noble Qur’an. Those who translated the Gospels translated it differently into their own different languages."],[1771,"In chapter 43 it is mentioned as follows:\n1: And Jesus came down to the eight disciples who were waiting for him below.\n2: And the four told the eight the whole story of what they had seen. And in this way that day every doubt about Jesus was removed from their hearts, except from the heart of Judas Iscariot, who did not believe in anything. (His doubt did not go.)\n3: And Jesus sat at the foot of the mountain, and all those men ate of the fruits of the forest, because they had no bread.\n4: At that time Andrew said: Indeed you have told us many things about the Messiah, so kindly explain all things to us clearly.\n5: So Jesus answered: Everyone who works works only for some purpose in which he finds some rest.\n6: For this reason I say to you that Allah, since He is truly perfect, has no need of rest or sufficiency.\n7: For sufficiency is with Him Himself.\n8: And so when He intended to act, before everything else He created the spirit of His Messenger, the Messenger for whose sake He intended to create all things.\n9: So that creation might find joy and blessing from Allah.\n10: And that His Messenger might be pleased with all of His creatures, whom God has decreed to be His servants.\n11: And why, and did this happen just so, except that Allah willed it?\n12: I tell you truly that every prophet, when he comes, brings the sign of Allah’s mercy for one people only.\n13: And for this reason the words of those prophets did not go beyond the people to whom they were sent.\n14: But when the Messenger of Allah comes, Allah will give him something like the signet ring on his hand.\n15: So he will bring deliverance and mercy to all the nations of the earth that accept his teaching.\n16: And soon he will come upon the wrongdoers with force.\n17: And he will wipe out the worship of idols, so that Satan will be humiliated and disgraced.\n18: For Allah promised Abraham, peace be upon him, just so, and said: See, I will bless all the tribes of the earth through your descendants, and just as you, O Abraham, broke the idols to pieces, so will your descendants do.\n19: Jacob answered: O Master! Tell us to whom this covenant was made.\n20: For the Jews say that (this covenant) was with Isaac.\n21: And the Ishmaelites say that it was with Ishmael.\n22: Jesus answered: Whose son was David, and from whose line?\n23: Jacob said: From the offspring of Isaac, for Isaac was the father of Jacob, and Jacob the father of the Jews, from whose line David is.\n24: Then at that time Jesus said: And when the Messenger of Allah comes, from whose line will he be?\n25: The disciples answered: From the line of David.\n26: Then Jesus answered: Do not deceive yourselves.\n27: For David, in the Spirit, calls him by the name of Lord, saying: Allah, my Lord, said: Sit at my right hand, so that I may make your enemies a place for you to tread upon.\n28: Your Lord will send your spear, which will be victorious in the midst of your enemies.\n29: So if the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whom you call the Messiah, the son of David, is this one, then how would David call him Lord?\n30: Believe me to be truthful, for I tell you that indeed the covenant was made with Ishmael, not with Isaac.\n§And in chapter forty-four it is stated:\nIn chapter 44 his beautiful mention ﷺ is as follows:\n1: Then the disciples said: O Teacher! Is it said in the book of Moses that the covenant was made with Isaac?\n2: Jesus sighed deeply and answered: This is what is written.\n3: But Moses did not write it, nor did Jesus.\n4: Rather, our rabbis (religious scholars), who do not fear God, did.\n5: I tell you truly that if you reflect on the words of the angel Gabriel, you will come to know the wickedness of our scribes and jurists.\n6: For the angel said: O Abraham, peace be upon him, soon the whole world will know how Allah loves you.\n7: But how will the world come to know your love for Allah?\n8: Surely it is obligatory upon you to do something for the love of God.\n9: Abraham, peace be upon him, answered: This servant of God is ready to do whatever God wills.\n10: Then at that time Allah said to Abraham, peace be upon him: Take your firstborn son Ishmael and go up the mountain, so that you may offer him as a sacrifice.\n11: So how can Isaac be the firstborn, when Ishmael was seven years old at the time he was born?\n12: Then at that time the disciples said: Surely the deception of the jurists is plainly evident.\n13: So you yourself tell us the truth, for we know that you have been sent by God.\n14: Then Jesus answered: I tell you truly that Satan always intends to nullify the law of God.\n15: So for this reason he and his followers, the hypocrites and the evildoers, have today made everything unclean.\n16: The former through false teaching and the others through a dissolute way of life.\n17: Until almost nothing of the truth remains in existence.\n18: Woe to the hypocrites, for the praise of this world will soon be turned into disgrace for them and will become punishment in Hell.\n19: And so I tell you that surely the Messenger of Allah is a light that will gladden almost all the works of the Creator.\n20: For he is adorned with the spirit of understanding and counsel.\n21: With the spirit of wisdom and might.\n22: With the spirit of fear and love.\n23: With the spirit of insight and moderation.\n24: (He) is adorned with the spirit of love and mercy.\n25: With the spirit of justice and piety.\n26: With the spirit of kindness and patience; spirits such as these, among which this Messenger has received from Allah a threefold portion of what Allah has given to all His creatures.\n27: How blessed is the time in which this (Messenger) will come into the world.\n28: Believe me to be truthful, truly I saw him and offered honor and reverence before him (I have venerated him), just as every prophet has seen him.\n29: For Allah gives them (the prophets) his (the Messenger’s) spirit by way of prophecy.\n30: And when I saw him, I was filled with consolation and said: O Muḥammad, may Allah be with you, and make me worthy to untie the strap of your sandal.\n31: For if I obtain this (honor), I shall become a great prophet and a holy one of Allah.\n32: And when Jesus had said this, he gave thanks to Allah.\n§And it is stated in chapter fifty-four (after the account of what will happen near the Resurrection):\nIn chapter 54 the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the nearness of the Resurrection are mentioned in this manner:\n1: So when these signs have passed, darkness will cover the world for forty years, and in it there will be no one alive except Allah, who is One, the One to whom belong greatness and majesty forever.\n2: And when forty years have passed, then Allah will bring His Messenger to life, who at that time too will go out toward the sun, except that he will be shining like a thousand suns.\n3: So he will sit and not speak a word, because he will be as if bewildered.\n4: And Allah will also raise up four angels who are near to Allah, and they will search for the Messenger of Allah.\n5: Then when they find him, they will stand at the four corners of his place as his guards.\n6: After that He will give life to all the angels, who will come and form a circle around the Messenger of Allah like bees.\n7: And after that Allah will give life to all His prophets, who will all come behind Adam.\n8: So they will kiss the hand of the Messenger of Allah, placing themselves in the refuge of his protection and help.\n9: Then after that Allah will bring to life all His chosen (servants), who will cry out: O Muḥammad, remember us.\n10: So mercy will stir (in the heart of) the Messenger of Allah at their cries and pleas.\n§And it is stated in chapter fifty-five:\nIn chapter 55 the accepted Messenger ﷺ is written of in this way:\n15: And Allah will speak to His Messenger, saying: Welcome, O my trustworthy servant.\n16: So ask, and you will be given everything.\n17: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ will answer: O Lord! Remember that when You created me, You said then that You had intended to create the world, Paradise, the angels and men for the sake of love for me, so that they might declare Your worship together with me, who am Your servant.\n18: For this reason I beseech You, O Lord, merciful and just God, that You remember Your promise to Your servant.\n19: Then Allah, like a friend who jests with his friend, will answer thus and say: Do you have any witnesses to this, O my friend Muḥammad?\n20: So he will say with courtesy: Surely, O Lord!\n21: Then Allah will say: Go and bring them, O Gabriel.\n22: So Gabriel will come to the Messenger of Allah and say: O master, who are your witnesses?\n23: Then the Messenger of Allah will answer: They are Adam, Abraham, Ishmael, Moses, David and Jesus the son of Mary.\n24: So the angel will go and call the above-mentioned witnesses, who will present themselves there trembling with fear.\n25: Then when they have been brought forward, Allah will say to them: Do you remember what My Messenger has affirmed?\n26: So they will answer: O Lord, what is it? Then Allah will say: That I created all things out of love for him, so that all creation might praise Me together with him.\n27: At that time each of them will answer: O Lord! We have three witnesses more (reliable) than ourselves.\n28: So Allah will answer: And who are those three witnesses?\n29: Then Moses will say: The first (witness) is the book which You gave me.\n30: And David will say: The second (witness) is the book which You gave me.\n31: And this person who is speaking to you will say: O Lord! Indeed Satan led the whole world astray, so that it said that I was Your son and Your partner.\n32: But the book which You gave me has said that in truth I am only Your servant.\n33: And this book acknowledges what Your Messenger has affirmed.\n34: Then at that time the Messenger of Allah will speak and say: So says the book which You, O Lord, gave me.\n35: So when the Messenger of Allah has said this, Allah will speak to him, saying: Indeed what I have done at this time I have done only so that everyone may know the degree of My love for you.\n§And it is stated in chapter ninety-six:\nIn chapter 96 his beautiful qualities are described thus:\n1: And when the prayer had ended, the priest said in a loud voice: O Jesus, wait, for it is obligatory upon us to know who you are, for the reassurance of your people.\n2: Jesus answered: I am Jesus, the son of Mary, from the line of David, a man who has died; I fear Allah, and I ask that worship and honor be given to no one but God.\n3: The priest answered: It is written in the book of Moses that our Allah will soon send us the Messiah, who will come to tell us of Allah’s will and bring Allah’s mercy for the world.\n4: Therefore we hope that you will tell us whether you are that Messiah of Allah for whom we are waiting.\n5: Jesus answered: The truth is that Allah has promised just so. But I am not he, for he was created before me and will come after me.\n6: The priest answered: From your words and your signs we in any case believe that you are certainly a prophet and a holy one of Allah.\n7: Therefore I ask you in the name of all Judea and (the children of) Israel, to tell us for God’s sake in what manner the Messiah will come.\n8: Jesus answered: By the life of that Allah before whom my soul will stand, truly I am not that Messiah for whom all the tribes of the earth wait, as Allah promised our father Abraham, peace be upon him, saying: I will bless all the tribes of the earth through your descendants alone.\n9: But when Allah takes me up from the world, then Satan will a second time raise up the accursed trial once more, so that (they will say) I (Jesus) am Allah or the son of Allah.\n10: And because of this my word and my teaching will be defiled, until almost not even thirty believers remain.\n11: At that time Allah will have mercy on the world and will send that Messenger of His for whom all things were created.\n12: He is the one who will come from the south with power and will destroy the idols and the worshippers of idols.\n13: And he will take away from Satan the dominion that he has over human beings.\n14: And he will bring Allah’s mercy for the salvation of those who believe in him.\n15: And blessed is the one who believes in his word.\n§And it is stated in chapter ninety-seven:\nIn chapter 97 his praiseworthy qualities are described in this way:\n4: Then at that time Jesus said: Indeed your work does not console me, for a darkness is about to come in which what hope of light will you have?\n5: But my consolation is in the coming of that Messenger who will erase every false notion about me, and his religion will spread and become general throughout the whole world, for Allah promised our father Abraham, peace be upon him, just so.\n6: And what consoles me is that there is no limit to the religion of this Messenger, because Allah will keep him as a friend and protect him.\n7: The priest answered: After the coming of the Messenger of Allah will other messengers also come?\n8: Jesus answered: After him no true prophets sent by God will come.\n9: But a very great number of false prophets will come, and this is the very thing that grieves me. For Satan will incite them by the command of the just Allah, so they will hide behind the claim of my Gospel.\n10: Herod answered: How can it be that the coming of these disbelievers is by the command of the just God?\n11: Jesus answered: It is in justice itself that whoever does not believe in the truth for his salvation should believe in a falsehood for his own damnation.\n12: For this reason I tell you that indeed the world has always insulted the prophets and has loved liars, as is observed in the time of Hosea and Jeremiah, for a kind loves its own kind.\n13: Then at that time the priest said: What will the name of the Messiah be, and what is the sign that will announce his coming?\n14: Jesus answered: The name of the Messiah is wonderful, for when Allah created his person and placed him in heavenly light, He Himself named him.\n15: Allah said: O Muḥammad, be patient, for it is for you that I intend to create Paradise, the world, and a very great multitude of creatures which I will bestow on you, so that whoever blesses you will be blessed and whoever curses you will be cursed.\n16: And when I send you into the world I will make you My messenger for salvation, and your word will be true, until heaven and earth both grow weak, but your faith will never grow weak.\n17: Indeed his blessed name is Muḥammad ﷺ.\n8: At that time the common people raised a cry, saying: O Allah, send Your Messenger to us; O Muḥammad ﷺ, come quickly to save the world.\n§And it is stated in chapter one hundred thirty-six, in the words of the Messiah:\nIn chapter 136 his attributes are described in the words of Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, in this manner:\n10: And I say, I tell you that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ will go even there, to see the justice of Allah.\n11: Then at that time Hell will begin to tremble because of his coming.\n12: And the reason is that he (the Messenger of Allah ﷺ) has a human body. Every human having a body, upon whom the sentence of punishment has been enforced, will have the punishment lifted from him. So he will remain, without bearing punishment, for the duration of the Messenger of Allah’s ﷺ stay to look upon Hell. But the Messenger of Allah ﷺ will not stay there except for the space of a blink of an eye.\n13: And Allah will do this only so that all creation may know that it has gained some benefit from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\n14: And when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ goes there, the devils will cry out and try to hide beneath the burning coals of the fire, while each of them says to the other: Run! Run! For our enemy Muḥammad ﷺ has come.\n15: So when Satan hears this, he will strike his face with both hands and say with a cry: This one has been raised above me against my will, and this has been done merely unjustly.\n16: As for the condition specific to those believers who have the better ranks, together with those of two other ranks who had faith without good deeds, for the first group grieved over good works and the second were glad with evil, all of these will remain in Hell for seventy thousand years.\n17: And after those years the angel Gabriel will come to Hell and will hear them saying: O Muḥammad ﷺ, where is your promise to us that whoever is on your religion will not remain in Hell forever?\n18: Then at that time the angel Gabriel will return to Paradise, and after approaching the Messenger of Allah with courtesy, will tell him what he has heard.\n19: So at that time the Messenger of Allah will speak and say: O my Lord and Allah! Remember to me, Your servant, this promise of Yours, that those who accept my religion will not remain in Hell forever.\n20: Then Allah will answer: O my beloved, ask what you wish, for I will give you everything you ask.\n§And it is stated in chapter one hundred forty-two, in the words of the chief priests, the scribes and the Pharisees:\nIn chapter 142 it is mentioned in this way in the words of the physiognomists, the soothsayers and the chiefs of the scribes:\n16: But when this man becomes king, he will never be satisfied, unless they see the worship of God done just as Moses wrote it.\n17: And worse than this, he says that the Messiah will not come from the line of David, as his most intimate disciple told us, but he says that in truth he (the Messiah) will come from the line of Ishmael.\n18: And that the promise (of sacrifice) was made with Ishmael and not with Isaac.\n19: Then if we leave this man alive, what will be the result?\n20: It is certain that the men of the children of Ishmael will become men of standing with the Romans. Then they will give them our country as their property.\n21: And in this way the Israelites will become subject to slavery as they were in ancient times."],[1772,"It is stated in Chapter 52 as follows:\n10: And it will be incumbent upon me to present an account for it (to answer for it).\n11: By the life of God, the God in whose presence my soul stands: I too am a mortal man, like all human beings.\n12: Except that God has appointed me a prophet to the house of Israel for the healing of the sick and the reform of sinners. I am God’s servant.\n13: And bear witness, all of you, how I regard as evil those wicked ones who, after my departure from this world, will nullify the truth of my Gospel through the work of Satan.\n14: But I will return shortly before the end (of the world).\n15: And with me will be Enoch and Elijah.\n16: And all of us will testify against those wicked ones who will be cursed in the Hereafter.\n17: And after Jesus had spoken thus, he shed tears.\n18: Then his disciples also wept aloud, and they cried out, saying: O Lord God! Forgive, and have mercy on Your innocent servant.\n19: Then Jesus answered and said: Amen, Amen.\n§وجاء في الفصل الثالث والتسعين:\nChapter 93 mentions this matter in the following manner:\n1: At that time Jesus raised his hand as a sign for silence.\n2: And he said: There is no doubt that you, O Israelites, have fallen into great error, because you have called me God, whereas I am a man.\n3: And I fear that because of this God may send some plague upon the holy city (and) hand it over to foreigners (so that) they would desire to make it (their) slave.\n4: The Satan who has deceived you with this, a thousand curses have been laid upon him.\n5: And when Jesus said this, he struck his face with both hands.\n6: Right after that a great outcry and wailing arose, so that no one heard what Jesus said.\n7: Then he raised his hand a second time as a sign for silence.\n8: And when the weeping and wailing of the people ceased, he said again:\n9: I bear witness before heaven, and I call as witnesses all things that are on the earth, that I am indeed free of all (the things) that you have said.\n10: Because I am a man, born of a mortal human woman, and I am subject to God’s command. Like all other men I suffer the discomfort of eating and sleeping, and I endure the affliction of cold and heat.\n11: Therefore when God comes so that (creation) may be judged, (at that time) my work will be like a cutting sword that will cleave through every person who believes that I (Jesus) am greater than a human being.\n12: And as Jesus said this to them, he saw a troop of horsemen, and from that he knew that the governor, together with Herod and the chief of the priests, was coming.\n13: Then Jesus said: Perhaps these too have gone mad.\n14: So when the governor (ruler), together with Herod and the chief of the priests, arrived, they all dismounted from their mounts and came on foot.\n15: And they surrounded Jesus, so much so that the soldiers were unable to push back the common people, who wanted to hear Jesus speaking with the priest.\n16: Then Jesus approached the priest with reverence, but the priest intended to prostrate himself before Jesus.\n17: So Jesus said in a loud voice: Beware! O priest of the living God, what are you doing? Do not sin against God.\n18: The priest answered: Indeed the Jews are troubled because of your signs and your teaching, (saying) that you are God. So because of the people I was compelled to come here with the Roman governor and King Herod.\n19: So we hope from the depth of our hearts that you will be willing to put an end to this strife which has arisen because of you.\n20: For one party says that you are God, another (says) that you are the son of God, and yet another party (says) that you are a prophet.\n21: Jesus answered: And you, O chief of the priests, why did you yourself not put an end to the strife?\n22: Have you too become insane?\n23: Have the prophecies and the laws of God all been utterly destroyed in this wretched Judea, which Satan has led astray?\n§وجاء في الفصل الرابع والتسعين:\nChapter 94 mentions this matter in the following manner:\n1: And when Jesus had said this, he turned back and said (again): Indeed I bear witness before heaven, and I call as witness every dweller upon the earth, that I am free of all the things that people have said about me, that I (Jesus) am more than a human being.\n2: Because I am a man, born of a woman, and I am subject to God’s command; I live like all other men, subject to the common afflictions.\n3: By the life of God, the God in whose presence my soul will be ready (to be sacrificed): O priest, you have indeed committed a great error in saying what you have said.\n4: May God have mercy on this holy city, so that no great calamity may come upon it because of this sin.\n5: Then at that time the priest said: May God forgive us, and you too, pray for us.\n6: Then the governor and Herod said: O master, it is surely impossible for anyone to do what you do. So we do not understand what you are saying.\n7: Jesus answered: Indeed, whatever you say, God does good to man just as Satan does evil.\n8: For man is like a shop: whoever enters it of his own will is the one who works in it and does buying and selling.\n9: But, O governor, tell me, and you too, O king: you say this because you are ignorant of our law. For if you both read the covenant of our God, you will see that Moses, by means of his staff, changed the water of the river into blood, the dust into lice, the rain into a storm, and the light into darkness.\n10: He sent frogs and locusts upon the land of Egypt, so the land was covered; he struck down the firstborn, turned the river into a mountain, and drowned Pharaoh in it.\n11: Whereas I have done none of this.\n12: And everyone admits that Moses is now nothing but a dead man.\n13: And Joshua stopped the sun and divided the Jordan (river). Whereas these are two works which I have not yet done.\n14: And everyone acknowledges that Joshua is now nothing but a dead man.\n15: And Elijah apparently brought down fire from heaven and brought down rain, and these are two works that I have not done.\n16: And everyone admits that Elijah is indeed a man.\n17: Many others among the prophets, the pure ones and the friends of God, did, by the power of God, such works that the intellects of people who do not know our God, the Merciful, blessed forever, can never reach their reality."],[1773,"Chapter 112 mentions this matter as follows:\n13: So know, O Barnabas, that for this reason it is obligatory for me to guard myself, and soon one of my disciples will sell me for thirty pieces of coin.\n14: And on that account I am certain that the one who sells me will be killed in my (own) name.\n15: For God will raise me up from the earth and will change the appearance of the traitor, so that everyone will think that it is I.\n16: But when the holy Muhammad, the Messenger of God, comes, he will remove from me this stain of disgrace.\n17: And God will do this because I acknowledged the truth of the Messiah, the Messiah who gives me this good reward, namely that I will be known to be alive and that I am free of the stain of dying such a death.\nChapter 139 mentions this matter in the following manner:\n1: In any case, this writer, together with James and John, found Jesus.\n2: Then they said, weeping: O Teacher! Why did you flee from us? Indeed we searched for you while we were grieved; rather, all the disciples sought you weeping.\n3: Then Jesus answered: I fled because I came to know that an army of devils is preparing for me what you will see in a little while.\n4: So soon the chief priests and the elders of the people will rise up against me and demand from the Roman governor an order for my execution.\n5: For they fear that I will seize the kingdom of Israel.\n6: And besides that, one of my disciples will sell me and hand me over (to the enemy), just as Joseph was sold in Egypt.\n7: But the just God will soon bind him fast, as the prophet David says: whoever digs a pit for his brother will himself fall into it.\n8: But God will deliver me from their hands and will take me up from the world.\n9: Then the three disciples became afraid.\n10: But Jesus comforted them, saying: Do not be afraid, for not one of you will hand me over to the enemy. So they were somewhat comforted by this."],[1774,"The prophecies about the Prophet ﷺ in the religious books of Buddhism have caused a stir among Hindu and Muslim scholars. The learned people of Hinduism acknowledge the fact that some of their books are not revealed. As for the Muslim scholars, they are agreed that the religious books of Hinduism are not revealed by God. These books were written at different times by their own religious leaders and pandits themselves. I have mentioned some details about this in my book: Dirāsāt fī al-Yahūd wa al-Masīḥiyyah wa Adyān al-Hind.\nSome of the prophecies found in the books of Hinduism are mentioned here. Several religious leaders of Hinduism have written works in which they also mention these prophecies. Then why do they not believe in the Prophet whose testimony is present in their own books? The answer to this is still owed by them.\n§ذكر قصة وجود آدم وحواء\n§Mention of the story of Sayyidunā Adam and Hawwa, peace be upon them both\nA man was created whose name was Adamu, and a woman was created whose name was Hayuvati. (Bhavishya Purana: 18/4, 19) The word Adamu is mentioned only in the Bhavishya Purana. This word is not mentioned in the other books of Hinduism. The word Adam is Hebrew, and its meaning is “man”. In Assyrian culture the word Adamu is found, which means to act and to beget. Judaism, Christianity and Islam all three agree that the first human being whom God Almighty created was Sayyidunā Adam, peace be upon him. In the Book of Genesis in the Bible it says: The Lord formed man from the dust of the ground and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life, and man became a living being. (The Bible, Genesis: 7/2)\nThe creation of Adam is mentioned in more than one place in the Noble Quran. In one place He said:\n﴿وَ اِذْ قَالَ رَبُّكَ لِلْمَلٰٓىِٕكَةِ۠ اِنِّيْ خَالِقٌۢ بَشَرًا مِّنْ صَلْصَالٍ مِّنْ حَمَاٍ مَّسْنُوْنٍ۰۰۲۸ فَاِذَا سَوَّيْتُهٗ وَ نَفَخْتُ فِيْهِ مِنْ رُّوْحِيْ فَقَعُوْا لَهٗ سٰجِدِيْنَ۰۰۲۹ فَسَجَدَ الْمَلٰٓىِٕكَةُ كُلُّهُمْ اَجْمَعُوْنَۙ۰۰۳۰ اِلَّاۤ اِبْلِيْسَ١ؕ اَبٰۤى اَنْ يَّكُوْنَ مَعَ السّٰجِدِيْنَ۰۰۳۱﴾ (Al-Hijr: 28-31)\nWhen your Lord said to the angels: Indeed I am going to create a human being from dried clay, from black mud molded into shape. Then when I have fashioned him and breathed into him of My spirit, fall down before him in prostration. Then the angels all prostrated together, except Iblis; he refused to be with those who prostrated.\nThe word Adam occurs twenty-four times in the Noble Quran. The word Hayuvati is perhaps Hawwa (Eve) herself. It is a Hebrew word meaning life. She is the wife of Sayyidunā Adam, peace be upon him. She was named Hawwa for this reason, that she is the mother of all living human beings. And Allah knows best.\n§عصيان آدم ربه\n§Sayyidunā Adam, peace be upon him, disobeying his Lord\nOn the eastern side of the town of Bardan was its beautiful garden which God Almighty had created. Sayyidunā Adam, peace be upon him, went to see his wife who was in it. By God’s command Satan came in the form of a snake beneath the Tree of Sins and misled them. The wife of Adam ate the fruit of that tree, and both were sent down from Paradise to the world. (Bhavishya Purana: 31/4-33)\nThe disobedience of Sayyidunā Adam, peace be upon him, to the command of the Lord Most High is mentioned in the Noble Quran in this manner:\n﴿وَ قُلْنَا يٰۤاٰدَمُ اسْكُنْ اَنْتَ وَ زَوْجُكَ الْجَنَّةَ وَ كُلَا مِنْهَا رَغَدًا حَيْثُ شِئْتُمَا١۪ وَ لَا تَقْرَبَا هٰذِهِ الشَّجَرَةَ فَتَكُوْنَا مِنَ الظّٰلِمِيْنَ۰۰۳۵ فَاَزَلَّهُمَا الشَّيْطٰنُ عَنْهَا فَاَخْرَجَهُمَا مِمَّا كَانَا فِيْهِ١۪ وَ قُلْنَا اهْبِطُوْا بَعْضُكُمْ لِبَعْضٍ عَدُوٌّ١ۚ وَ لَكُمْ فِي الْاَرْضِ مُسْتَقَرٌّ وَّ مَتَاعٌ اِلٰى حِيْنٍ۰۰۳۶﴾ (Al-Baqarah: 35-36)\nWe said: O Adam! Live, you and your wife, in Paradise, and eat from it freely wherever you wish, and do not go near this tree, or you will both be among the wrongdoers. Then Satan made them slip from it, and so expelled them from that in which they were, and We said: Go down, some of you being enemies of others. For you there is on earth a dwelling place and provision for a time."],[1775,"Some of the causes and purposes of the sending of prophets in the books of Hinduism are mentioned here:\n1: When truth is erased and falsehood prevails, I will manifest Myself. (Bhagavad Gita: 7/4)\n2: Krishna, addressing Arjuna, said: To protect the servants of the Merciful, to uproot the evil-minded elements, and to make the true religion prevail, I Myself will descend again and again. (Bhagavad Gita: 18/4)\nKrishna is one of the prominent heroes of the Hindus. The Hindus regard him as God and consider him a manifestation of God. In their view God descended to the earth in the form of Krishna, which they call an avatar. This is a belief altered from prophethood and messengership. The Christians too were influenced by the personality of Krishna and the Hindu belief. They declared Sayyidunā the Messiah, peace be upon him, to be a manifestation of God Almighty; rather, according to them, God Almighty descended to the earth in bodily form. Before them, the Jews also went astray concerning the righteous people of the people of Sayyidunā Noah, peace be upon him, and gave them the rank of God. These various ideas and persons are mentioned in the Noble Quran in this way:\n﴿وَ قَالَتِ الْيَهُوْدُ عُزَيْرُ ا۟بْنُ اللّٰهِ وَ قَالَتِ النَّصٰرَى الْمَسِيْحُ ابْنُ اللّٰهِ١ؕ ذٰلِكَ قَوْلُهُمْ بِاَفْوَاهِهِمْ١ۚ يُضَاهِـُٔوْنَ قَوْلَ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْا مِنْ قَبْلُ١ؕ قٰتَلَهُمُ اللّٰهُ١ٞۚ اَنّٰى يُؤْفَكُوْنَ۰۰۳۰﴾(At-Tawbah: 30)\nThe Jews said: ʿUzayr is the son of God, and the Christians said: the Messiah is the son of God. That is a statement from their mouths; they imitate the statement of those who disbelieved before. May God destroy them! How they are turned away!\nIt is stated quite clearly in the fundamental books of Hinduism that God Almighty sends prophets and messengers, whom they describe as devtas (gods). The Noble Quran alludes to this in these words:\n﴿وَ لِكُلِّ اُمَّةٍ رَّسُوْلٌ١ۚ﴾ (Yunus: 47)\nTo every nation there came a messenger.\nIn another place He said:\n﴿وَ اِنْ مِّنْ اُمَّةٍ اِلَّا خَلَا فِيْهَا نَذِيْرٌ۰۰۲۴﴾ (Fatir: 24)\nThere has been a warner in every nation.\nThe meaning of the Hindu belief in avatars is: the descent of God in human form. Dayananda Saraswati has criticized this theory. He is one of the Hindu figures of the last century. In his book Satyarth Prakash he sharply criticized this theory and wrote that God Almighty is able to do all things; He is not in need of creating a human being, after nine months, from impure blood and water in a woman’s womb, in order to kill the impure and tyrannical Ravana and Kansa.\nSatyarth Prakash is a very dangerous book. In Chapter 14 the author discusses Islam. He launched severe attacks on the Quran and the Noble Prophet ﷺ. Many Hindus were influenced by it. This book was first printed in 1857. By 1972 the number of its editions was 35. It was translated into most local and world languages. Among the Muslims, Shaykh Thanāʾullāh Amritsarī, may Allah have mercy on him, wrote a refutation of it in Urdu under the name Ḥaqq Prakāsh; to my knowledge it has not been translated into Hindi. It was not distributed on a wide scale like Satyarth Prakash.\nI say: In this statement of his he has indeed testified to the truth. God Almighty has condemned those who do not esteem Him as He ought to be esteemed.\nThe Muslims began studying other religions at the very beginning of their history. This fact refutes the claim of the orientalists that the tendency among Muslims to study other religions arose after the third century, and that too only when it occurred to them that all the religions before Islam had been altered and distorted. God Almighty has given testimony about this in this way:\n﴿فَوَيْلٌ لِّلَّذِيْنَ يَكْتُبُوْنَ الْكِتٰبَ بِاَيْدِيْهِمْ١ۗ ثُمَّ يَقُوْلُوْنَ هٰذَا مِنْ عِنْدِ اللّٰهِ لِيَشْتَرُوْا بِهٖ ثَمَنًا قَلِيْلًا١ؕ فَوَيْلٌ لَّهُمْ مِّمَّا كَتَبَتْ اَيْدِيْهِمْ وَ وَيْلٌ لَّهُمْ مِّمَّا يَكْسِبُوْنَ۰۰۷۹﴾ (Al-Baqarah: 79)\nSo woe to those who write the book with their own hands, then say: This is from God, in order to gain a small price for it. So woe to them for what their hands have written, and woe to them for what they earn.\nIslam is the final religion. The Noble Quran confirms, through allusions, that the Old Testament and the New Testament were distorted. It is incumbent upon the scholars who study comparative religion to explain and elaborate on these allusions and brief references, and to clarify them with examples.\nThe books of Hinduism explicitly mention the sending of twenty-four prophets. All the prophets except the twenty-fourth have been sent. They are awaiting the sending of only one. This awaited prophet is mentioned in their books under various names:\n1: Kalki, which means: the one who purifies people from sins and impurity. Polytheism is the greatest sin and impurity.\n2: Narashansa, whose sense is: a praiseworthy human being.\n3: Jagat Guru, which means: the teacher of the whole world.\nThe followers of Hinduism have also fixed the time of the sending of the awaited prophet, which is the age of Kali Yuga.\nOn this basis the appearance of the awaited prophet will be in the last age, and that is the age of Kali Yuga, which began thousands of years ago. The history of mankind bears witness that after Sayyidunā ʿĪsā, peace be upon him, there has been no personality worthy of being a prophet and a savior other than Muhammad the Noble ﷺ. This fact is a call to reflection for Hindu scholars, that they should ponder and reflect on the awaited prophet who was sent fourteen and a half centuries ago.\nAmong the indications found in Hindu books about the sending of Muhammad ﷺ, some are quite clear. Some are brief and need clarification and explanation. It is also necessary to keep in mind that the date of composition and the reality of the books in which these prophecies are found are entirely unknown. The purpose of this discussion is only to establish the proof against them from their own books.\nWe Muslims do not need these Hindu books in order to prove or to believe in the prophethood and messengership of Muhammad the Noble ﷺ. His prophethood is established by every kind of proof and evidence, rational, sensory and transmitted.\nAfter this preface and introduction, the prophecies found in the Hindu books are mentioned. Then they will be explained in the light of Islamic evidence. If there is any disagreement anywhere, it means that distortion has occurred in their books regarding these prophecies, just as change upon change has continued to be made in other texts and places. God alone is the Protector and Helper."],[1776,"1: In the town of Shambhal, Kalki will be born in the house of a man named Vishnuyash, and he will be the guardian of the House of God (Bayt Allah). (Kalki Purana: 4/2)\nThe word Shambhal is composed of two words: Sham and Bhal. Sham means peace. Bhal means a town or city. The meaning of the whole word is: a town of peace, a city of peace and security.\nVishnuyash is also composed of two words. Vishnu means: God. Yash means: servant, slave, which is in the sense of ʿAbd Allah.\nKalki means: a personality free from errors and sins.\nIt is stated in the Noble Quran that Muhammad the Noble ﷺ was sent as a prophet, a purifier and a teacher. God Almighty, addressing His beloved ﷺ, said:\n﴿خُذْ مِنْ اَمْوَالِهِمْ صَدَقَةً تُطَهِّرُهُمْ وَ تُزَكِّيْهِمْ بِهَا وَصَلِّ عَلَيْهِمْ١ؕ اِنَّ صَلٰوتَكَ سَكَنٌ لَّهُمْ١ؕ وَ اللّٰهُ سَمِيْعٌ عَلِيْمٌ۰۰۱۰۳﴾(At-Tawbah: 103)\n(O Prophet!) Take charity from their wealth (so that) you may purify them, cleanse them by it, and pray for them; indeed your prayer is a source of tranquility for them; and God is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\nIn another place He said:\n﴿اِنَّمَا يُرِيْدُ اللّٰهُ لِيُذْهِبَ عَنْكُمُ الرِّجْسَ اَهْلَ الْبَيْتِ وَ يُطَهِّرَكُمْ تَطْهِيْرًاۚ۰۰۳۳﴾ (Al-Ahzab: 33)\nGod only wishes to remove impurity from you, O people of the household, and to purify you with a thorough purification.\nIn one place He said:\n﴿مَا يُرِيْدُ اللّٰهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُمْ مِّنْ حَرَجٍ وَّ لٰكِنْ يُّرِيْدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ وَ لِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهٗ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُوْنَ۰۰۶﴾ (Al-Ma’idah: 6)\nGod does not wish to place any hardship upon you, but He wishes to purify you and to complete His favor upon you, so that you may be grateful.\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ is a means of people’s purification and cleansing. Whoever believed in him, affirmed his message and prophethood, and acted upon his guidance will be pure and clean by the command and grace of God Almighty. One of his names is al-Māḥī, which means: the one through whom God Almighty will erase disbelief. Disbelief is the greatest impurity."],[1777,"It is from the hadith of Sayyidunā Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him. He says that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have various names: I am Muhammad, Aḥmad and al-Māḥī, through whom Allah Almighty will wipe out disbelief. I am al-Ḥāshir, before whom all people will be gathered. I am al-ʿĀqib.” The meaning of al-ʿĀqib is the one after whom there is no prophet. (This is synonymous with Khātam al-Nabiyyīn, the Seal of the Prophets.)"],[1778,"2: Maharshi Kalki will be born in the house of Vishnuyash, from his wife Sumati. (Kalki Purana: 11/2, and Kalki, the Awaited Prophet, and Muhammad, the Messenger of God ﷺ, p. 27) The meaning of the word Sumati is: one of peace and safety. In this sense there is a very deep connection between Sumati and the name of the Prophet’s mother, Āminah."],[1779,"3: Kalki will be born on the twelfth day of the moon in the month of Madhu. (Kalki Purana: 15/2; see also the previous source, pp. 29, 43) Madhu means: desirable and pleasing, that which is agreeable to souls, and it is the month of Rabīʿ. The scholars of biography and history agree that the blessed birth of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took place in the month of Rabīʿ al-Awwal."],[1780,"Imam Ibn Isḥāq says: His blessed birth was on the 12th of Rabīʿ al-Awwal. In Muṣannaf Ibn Abī Shaybah there is a narration from Sayyidunā Jābir and Sayyidunā Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both; they say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was born on Monday, the 18th of Rabīʿ al-Awwal. In this same month he was sent as a prophet. In this same month he was taken on the Miʿrāj (the Night Ascension). In this same month he made the Hijrah, and in this same month he passed away.\nḤāfiẓ Ibn Kathīr, may Allah have mercy on him, has recorded in al-Bidāyah wa al-Nihāyah that the correct date of his birth is the twelfth of Rabīʿ al-Awwal. On this reckoning the Gregorian date comes to 23 April 571 CE. Maḥmūd Pāshā al-Falakī has stated this as the definitive date."],[1781,"4: Maharshi Kalki will be characterized by eight attributes and qualities. (The Awaited Kalki and Muhammad, the Messenger of God ﷺ, p. 45)\nHere it is explained how these attributes apply to Muhammad the Noble ﷺ, because it is certain that no personality among the Hindus has been characterized by these attributes, especially in the age of Kali Yuga, in which the sending of this awaited prophet is being awaited."],[1782,"These eight attributes are as follows:\nFirst: (PRAGYA): its meaning is: one who gives news of the future. This points to the miracles which God Almighty bestowed upon His beloved ﷺ, and which are proof of the truthfulness of his prophethood. Many scholars have written independent books under the title Dalāʾil al-Nubuwwah (Proofs of Prophethood). The most famous book is that of Imam al-Bayhaqī. It contains hundreds of reports and narrations concerning the miracles.\nSecond: (CULINATA). Its meaning is: the prophet’s being the noblest and highest among his people."],[1783,"Imam Aḥmad narrated from al-Muṭṭalib ibn Abī Wadāʿah that Sayyidunā al-ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat on the pulpit and asked: “Who am I?” The people said: You are the Messenger of Allah. He said: “I am Muhammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib. Allah created the creation and placed me in the best part of the creation. Then He made that part into two groups and placed me in the better group. Then He made them into tribes and placed me in the best tribe. Then He made them into households and placed me in the best household. In person and in lineage I am the best of you all.”"],[1784,"It is narrated from Sayyidunā Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Allah chose Ishmael (peace be upon him) from the descendants of Abraham (peace be upon him). From the descendants of Ishmael He chose Banū Kinānah. From Banū Kinānah He chose Quraysh. From Quraysh He chose Banū Hāshim, and from Banū Hāshim He chose me.”"],[1785,"It is narrated from Sayyidunā Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I will be the master of the children of Adam on the Day of Resurrection. My grave will be the first to be split open. I will be the first to intercede, and the first whose intercession will be accepted.”\nSayyidunā Abū Sufyān, may Allah be pleased with him, before accepting Islam, gave testimony about his lineage in the court of Heraclius. He said that the Prophet ﷺ is of high ancestry and lineage among us. Upon this Heraclius said that prophets are like this, of distinguished ancestry and lineage among their people. The purpose of this is that the noble prophets, peace be upon them, in their own times are distinguished among their people in terms of ancestry, lineage, family and tribe.\nThird: (INDRIDAMAN). Its meaning is: one who has mastery over himself.\nIn this there is an indication that the Noble Prophet ﷺ had greater self-control than all other people. With every human being there is a satan companion who incites him to evil and makes the forbidden look attractive to him, and in this way the person commits the forbidden; but God Almighty protected His beloved ﷺ from the scheming and deception of Satan."],[1786,"It is narrated from Sayyidunā Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Noble Prophet ﷺ said: “Last night a rebellious jinn suddenly came to me,” or he ﷺ said a word like this, “in order to spoil my prayer, but Allah gave me power over him, and I thought of tying him to a pillar of the mosque so that in the morning all of you could see him as well. Then I remembered the supplication of my brother Solomon, peace be upon him: ﴿رَبِّ اغْفِرْ لِيْ وَهَبْ لِيْ مُلْكًا لَّا يَنْۢبَغِيْ لِاَحَدٍ مِّنْۢ بَعْدِيْ١ۚ﴾ meaning: O my Lord! Grant me a kingdom that will not be suitable for anyone after me.” Rawh said that the Noble Prophet ﷺ sent that jinn away in humiliation."],[1787,"The Mother of the Believers, Sayyidah ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, testified to this very attribute of his ﷺ in this manner. She says: The self-control that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had, none of you has such control over himself."],[1788,"And this is an allusion to the authentic blessed hadiths of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: “The strong and brave one is not the one who prevails in wrestling, but the strong and brave one is the one who controls himself when in a state of anger.”\nFourth: (SHRUT). Its meaning is: possessing divine knowledge. By this is meant divine revelation; the purpose is that his speech will be based on revelation from God Almighty. This very reality is stated in the Noble Quran in this way:\n﴿وَ مَا يَنْطِقُ عَنِ الْهَوٰى ؕ۰۰۳ اِنْ هُوَ اِلَّا وَحْيٌ يُّوْحٰى ۙ۰۰۴﴾ (An-Najm: 3, 4)\nNor does he speak from his own desire; it is nothing but revelation that is revealed.\nIn the same way the divine saying is:\n﴿وَ لَوْ تَقَوَّلَ عَلَيْنَا بَعْضَ الْاَقَاوِيْلۙ۰۰۴۴ لَاَخَذْنَا مِنْهُ بِالْيَمِيْنِۙ۰۰۴۵﴾ (Al-Haqqah: 44, 45)\nIf he had fabricated some sayings against Us, We would certainly have seized him by the right hand.\nIn another place He said:\n﴿وَ كَذٰلِكَ اَوْحَيْنَاۤ اِلَيْكَ قُرْاٰنًا عَرَبِيًّا لِّتُنْذِرَ اُمَّ الْقُرٰى وَ مَنْ حَوْلَهَا﴾ (Ash-Shura: 7)\nAnd thus We have revealed to you an Arabic Quran, so that you may warn the mother of the towns (the people of Makkah) and those around it.\nThere are a great many verses of this meaning in the Noble Quran.\nFifth: (PRAKRAM). Its meaning is: a personality of strong body whom no one can throw down. By this is meant a brave man who is perfect in his creation, without any defect. The purpose is not that he be heavily built."],[1789,"This is in the description of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, as in the hadith of Sayyidunā al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, may Allah be pleased with him, who says: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was of medium height. His chest was very broad and open, meaning he had broad shoulders."],[1790,"Concerning the description of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Sayyidunā ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, says: The Noble Prophet ﷺ was neither tall nor short; his hands and feet were full of flesh. His blessed head was somewhat large and his joints were thick. He had a long line of hair on his chest. When he walked, he leaned forward a little, as if descending a slope. I have seen no one like him, neither before him nor after him.\nThese are the qualities of a strong and brave man, who is powerful in birth and build. These traits are praiseworthy for men, not for women. He ﷺ was granted such awe and majesty that the enemy would become frightened on merely hearing of him."],[1791,"It is from the hadith of Sayyidunā Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have been given five things which were not given to anyone before me. I have been supported with awe (cast into the enemy’s hearts) from a distance of a month’s journey. The whole earth has been made for me a place of prayer and a means of purification, so whoever of my community finds that the time of prayer has come, let him pray right there. The spoils of war have been made lawful for me, which were not lawful for anyone before me. I have been given the right of intercession. The earlier prophets were each sent to their own particular people, whereas I have been sent to all mankind.”"],[1792,"In the hadith of Sayyidunā Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I have been sent with jawāmiʿ al-kalim (concise, comprehensive speech), and I have been supported with awe (cast into the enemy’s hearts).”"],[1793,"It is also narrated that the Noble Prophet ﷺ wrestled with Rukānah and threw him down.\nSixth: (ABHU BHASHITA). Its meaning is: sparing in speech.\nThe Noble Prophet ﷺ was distinguished with the quality of jawāmiʿ al-kalim. There are numerous hadiths about this."],[1794,"In it is the narration of Sayyidunā Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him; he says: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “I have been sent with jawāmiʿ al-kalim…”"],[1795,"The Mother of the Believers, Sayyidah ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, says about the manner of speech of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not speak rapidly as you do. He spoke clearly and slowly. The listener would remember what he said.\nSeventh: (DAN). Its meaning is: generous and bountiful, who is not stingy with anything he has. In generosity and bounty the Messenger of Allah ﷺ held such a high station that none was his equal in it."],[1796,"It is from the hadith of Sayyidunā Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him; he says: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never said “No” when asked for anything."],[1797,"And Sayyidunā ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, says: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the most generous of all people. And as for his generosity in Ramadan, what can be said! In Ramadan Jibrīl, peace be upon him, would come to him and review the Quran with him. When Jibrīl came, his generosity was greater than a swift wind."],[1798,"And Sayyidunā Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, says: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not store up anything for the next day."],[1799,"And likewise Sayyidunā Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, says: A man came to the Noble Prophet ﷺ and asked him for something. He gave him a large flock of sheep that lay between two mountains. The man ran quickly to his people and called out: O my people! Become Muslims, for Muhammad ﷺ gives so much. He does not even fear poverty and starvation.\nEighth: (KRITAGYATA). Its meaning is: one who acknowledges the good qualities of others.\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ would often praise the Ansar for their help and support. He used to wish: if only he too were one of them!"],[1800,"It is from the hadith of Sayyidunā Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. He says that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Whatever valley or mountain pass the Ansar take, I too will go with the Ansar in that valley. Were it not for the Hijrah, I too would have been one of the Ansar.”"],[1801,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said about Sayyidunā Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him: “No one’s wealth has benefited me as much as the wealth of Abū Bakr has benefited me.”\nIn return for all these attributes and qualities, one divine statement alone is sufficient:\n﴿وَ اِنَّكَ لَعَلٰى خُلُقٍ عَظِيْمٍ۰۰۴﴾ (Al-Qalam: 4)\nIndeed you are of a great moral character."],[1802,"5: Kalki will be born in the town of Shambhal, in the house of Vishnuyash. He will ride Shekhar Kami, that is, the swiftest horse. With a sword he will wipe out hundreds of tyrants who will be persons of standing like chieftains, that is, the chiefs of the tribes. A fragrance will spread from his pure body that will perfume people. (Bhagavata Purana: 18/2/12-21)\nThese attributes are quite clear regarding our Prophet ﷺ. The Hindu leaders’ riding a horse is not well known, but they hold the notion of the Seal of the Prophets as a horseback rider. That is why various pictures of their leaders are made in some of their sacred books. For example, in the picture of Rama he is shown shooting arrows. Behind Krishna a picture of a revolving circle is drawn. Kalki, the Seal of the Prophets, is shown riding a horse with four wings. Who is the personality mentioned in the Bhagavata Purana?"],[1803,"6: Kalki will be handsome and beautiful; rather, he will be more handsome than all the human beings on the face of the earth. In beauty and handsomeness no one will be his equal. He will be like the sun. (Bhagavata Purana, 20/2/12; Kalki, the Awaited Prophet, and Muhammad, the Messenger of God ﷺ, p. 43)"],[1804,"It is narrated from Sayyidunā Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a moonlit night; he was wearing a red ḥullah (cloak). Sometimes I looked at him and sometimes at the moon. He seemed to me more handsome and beautiful than the moon."],[1805,"Abū Isḥāq says that a man asked Sayyidunā al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, may Allah be pleased with him: Was the radiant face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ like a sword? He said: No, rather like the full moon."],[1806,"It is narrated from Sayyidunā Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, that there was a slight gap between the front teeth of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he spoke, it seemed as though light was bursting forth from between his teeth."],[1807,"7: This Kalki will have four special companions, with whose help he will defeat Satan. His companions will help him in every possible way. (Kalki Purana: 5/2; Kalki, the Awaited Prophet, and Muhammad, the Messenger of God ﷺ, p. 42)\nIn this there is an allusion to his four Caliphs, who during his blessed life were like ministers to him and his special supporters and helpers."],[1808,"8: Angels will descend to help Kalki in his battles and will help him. (Kalki Purana: 7/2)\nIn this prophecy there is an allusion to the event of Badr. It is mentioned in the Noble Quran in this way:\n﴿اِذْ تَسْتَغِيْثُوْنَ۠ رَبَّكُمْ فَاسْتَجَابَ لَكُمْ اَنِّيْ مُمِدُّكُمْ بِاَلْفٍ مِّنَ الْمَلٰٓىِٕكَةِ مُرْدِفِيْنَ۰۰۹ وَ مَا جَعَلَهُ اللّٰهُ اِلَّا بُشْرٰى وَ لِتَطْمَىِٕنَّ بِهٖ قُلُوْبُكُمْ١ۚ وَ مَا النَّصْرُ اِلَّا مِنْ عِنْدِ اللّٰهِ١ؕ اِنَّ اللّٰهَ عَزِيْزٌ حَكِيْمٌؒ۰۰۱۰ اِذْ يُغَشِّيْكُمُ النُّعَاسَ اَمَنَةً مِّنْهُ وَ يُنَزِّلُ عَلَيْكُمْ مِّنَ السَّمَآءِ مَآءً لِّيُطَهِّرَكُمْ۠ بِهٖ وَ يُذْهِبَ عَنْكُمْ رِجْزَ الشَّيْطٰنِ وَ لِيَرْبِطَ عَلٰى قُلُوْبِكُمْ وَ يُثَبِّتَ بِهِ الْاَقْدَامَؕ۰۰۱۱ اِذْ يُوْحِيْ رَبُّكَ اِلَى الْمَلٰٓىِٕكَةِ اَنِّيْ مَعَكُمْ فَثَبِّتُوا الَّذِيْنَ اٰمَنُوْا١ؕ سَاُلْقِيْ فِيْ قُلُوْبِ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوا الرُّعْبَ فَاضْرِبُوْا فَوْقَ الْاَعْنَاقِ وَ اضْرِبُوْا مِنْهُمْ كُلَّ بَنَانٍؕ۰۰۱۲ ذٰلِكَ بِاَنَّهُمْ شَآقُّوا اللّٰهَ وَ رَسُوْلَهٗ١ۚ وَ مَنْ يُّشَاقِقِ اللّٰهَ وَ رَسُوْلَهٗ فَاِنَّ اللّٰهَ شَدِيْدُ الْعِقَابِ۰۰۱۳﴾ (Al-Anfal: 9-13)\n(Remember) when you were crying out to your Lord for help, and He answered you (and said) that He would reinforce you with a thousand angels, following one after another. God made this (help) only as glad tidings, so that your hearts might be reassured by it; and help comes only from God. Indeed God is Mighty, All-Wise. (Remember) when He was causing drowsiness to overcome you from Himself as a security, and sending down upon you rain from the sky to purify you with it, remove from you the whisperings of Satan, strengthen your hearts, and make your feet firm by it. (O Prophet!) When your Lord was revealing to the angels: Indeed I am with you, so make firm those who believe; I will cast terror into the hearts of those who disbelieved, so strike them above the necks and strike every fingertip of theirs. That is because they opposed God and His Messenger; and whoever opposes God and His Messenger, then indeed God is severe in punishment."],[1809,"It is in Sahih al-Bukhari that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the Battle of the Trench, he took off his weapons and began to bathe. Jibrīl, peace be upon him, came to him and said: You have laid down your weapons, whereas by Allah, we have not yet laid down ours. Go out to deal with them. He said: “Where to?” Jibrīl, peace be upon him, pointed toward Banū Qurayẓah and said: This way. Then he went to them."],[1810,"9: After his birth he will go to the mountains to acquire education from Parashurama. Then he will go to the north and then return to his birthplace. (Kalki Purana: 1/3/1-5)\nPerhaps in this passage there is an allusion to three important events in the blessed life of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ:\n(1) Before his prophetic mission, his retreat into seclusion in the Cave of Ḥirāʾ for worship, the descent of Jibrīl al-Amīn, peace be upon him, with the first revelation, and his saying “Iqraʾ” (Read).\n(2) His emigration to Madinah, which lies to the north of Makkah.\n(3) His return to Makkah, his birthplace, at the Conquest of Makkah."],[1811,"10: People will be enchanted by the fragrance emanating from his pure body. The air will be perfumed by the fragrance that bursts forth from his clean body. Spirits and souls too will become fragrant. (Bhagavata Purana: 21/2/12)\nIt is well known among the characteristics of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that a very fine fragrance came forth from his blessed body. There are many narrations about this; a few are given below:"],[1812,"Sayyidunā Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, says: With my hand I never touched any thick silk, or fine silk, or any other soft thing, that was not softer and more tender than the blessed palm of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, nor did I ever smell a scent more fragrant and sweet than the scent of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[1813,"It is narrated from Sayyidunā Jābir ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him: One day I prayed the Ẓuhr prayer behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then he set out toward his house, and I went along with him. On the way he met two children. He showed affection to them and passed his blessed hand over their cheeks. He also placed his blessed hand on my cheek. The coolness and fragrance of his blessed hand was as though he had taken his hand out of a perfumer’s bottle."],[1814,"Sayyidunā Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, says: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to us, took a midday rest with us, and he sweated. My mother came with a bottle and began collecting his sweat in it. He woke up and said: “Umm Sulaym! What are you doing?” She said: I am collecting your blessed sweat in the bottle, which we mix into perfume; it becomes the finest perfume.\nIn one narration it is that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to come to the house of Sayyidah Umm Sulaym, may Allah be pleased with her, and take his midday rest there. She would spread a mat for him on which he would rest. He would sweat a great deal. She would collect his sweat in a bottle and mix it into perfume. One day the Noble Prophet ﷺ said: “Umm Sulaym! What are you doing?” She said: I scent my perfume with your sweat."],[1815,"Sayyidunā Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him, says that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had several distinguishing qualities. Whatever path he passed along, afterward any other passerby on that path would recognize that he had passed by from the fragrance of his sweat or the scent of his sweat.\nAs for the religious leaders and pandits of the Hindus, using perfume is forbidden for them in any case. Rather, to mortify the self they keep smearing earth on themselves. Several kinds of foul odor come from them.\n§11 - أحمدو صار كالشمس .\n11: Ahmad will be like the sun. (Sama Veda: 8/4/2)\nHe ﷺ was indeed like the sun. His religion spread to all sides and corners like the rays of the sun. His teachings perfumed the world, and the world was illuminated by his message. In the Veda’s prophecy the word Ahmadu is mentioned, which is a Sanskrit word. None of the Hindu scholars knows the real meaning of this word, because it is not a word of their language at all. In explaining this word they have groped about endlessly in the dark.\nMarkar, Wenil and Gupta, the authors of the Vedic dictionary, did not even mention this word in their books, as though both of them were entirely ignorant of it.\n§12- سوف يأتي محامد محمد المعلم مع رفقائه الكرام، ويكون الملك بهوج في الصحراء مشغولا في عبادة سيفا وتقديم الماء المقدس له، ويقول: يا ساكن الصحراء، وهازم الشيطان! سلام عليك، أنا عبدك أعيش تحت قدميك.\n12: Soon Mahamad will come, with his noble companions, as a teacher; his rule will be in the Bhoj desert. He will be engaged in the worship of Siva. Holy water will be offered to him. He will say: O dweller of the desert and defeater of Satan! Peace be upon you. I am your servant, I will live beneath your feet. (Bhavishya Purana: 5/3/3-8)\nThe word Mahamad (maḥāmid) does not exist in the Sanskrit language at all. This word is an altered form of Muhammad. The other attributes of Mahamad mentioned here are also not found in any Hindu leader. You ﷺ alone are the one to whom these attributes apply.\n§13 - وفي هذه الأدوار إذا جاء وقت ظهور الخير الجماعي للإنسان فإنه يعرفالحق، وبظهور محمد تنتهي الظلمات، ويطلع نور الفهم والحكمة».\n13: In this age, when the time of appearance of the well-wisher of humanity, the embodiment of goodness and benefit, arrives, then the truth will become clear. With the appearance of Muhammad the darkness will be dispelled. The light of wisdom and understanding will spread in every direction. (Bhagavata Purana: 76/2)\nThe period before the sending of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is known as the Age of Ignorance (Jāhiliyyah). The period after prophethood is the age of Islam, of knowledge and gnosis, and of collective goodness and benefit. With his sending all kinds of darkness were dispelled.\n§14 - أكني ديوي صاحب الشريعة الغراء جعلناك فوق سرة الأرض لتقديم القرابين .\n14: O Agni Devi, possessor of the bright law, we have made you master of the center of the earth, to receive offerings. (Rig Veda: 4/29/3)\nThe geographers say that Makkah is located exactly in the middle of the earth. In this there is an indication that the awaited prophet will gain mastery over Makkah. (See Sulaymān Manṣūrpūrī, Raḥmatun lil-ʿĀlamīn: 31/1)\nThe scholars of Hinduism have not identified the center of the earth. This shows that they found no such place in their own lands, and for that reason they remained silent about identifying it.\nBefore me is the commentary on the Rig Veda by the Hindu scholar Shri Sharma Acharya. He too did not explain these words of the Rig Veda. (Commentary on the Rig Veda: 492/1)\nThe Egyptian scholar Dr. Ḥusayn Kamāl al-Dīn himself has related his story about a strange discovery. He planned a research project. The aim was to gather information that would help worshippers in all parts of the world to determine the qibla, so that it would be possible for them to face the qibla, because on one of his journeys he strongly felt that Muslims face severe difficulties in determining the qibla in places where there is no mosque, no means of determining the qibla, or where they are in Western countries. For example, hundreds of students studying abroad face this difficulty. So Dr. Ḥusayn Kamāl al-Dīn planned to make a map that would help in determining the qibla in various directions and places.\nTo prepare this map he first made a preliminary introductory map. He drew the region of the five continents. The Egyptian doctor was astonished to see the discovery that Makkah al-Mukarramah is located exactly in the middle of the world. He took a compass, placed one side of it on the city of Makkah on the map, and rotated the other side over all the continents. He found that the stretches of dry land around Makkah on the globe lie in an extremely orderly arrangement. In this case too Makkah is exactly in the middle of the earth.\nFor further research and for peace of heart he made a map from before the discovery of America and Australia. In it too it was revealed that Makkah is the center of the dry land, even according to that ancient world as it was when the call of Islam began. (Makkah al-Mukarramah, the Center of the World, Majallat al-ʿIlm wa al-Īmān, issue 20, year 1977)\n§15 - إن الملائكة قدموا لكالكي فرسا اسمه آشو.\n15: The angels will present to Kalki a horse whose name will be Ashu. (Bhagavata Purana: 9/2/12)\nThe Hindu author writes in Ved Prakash: Muhammad ﷺ was also given a horse, whose name was al-Burāq. On it he was taken on a journey through the heavens. (Kalki, the Awaited Prophet, and Muhammad, the Messenger of God ﷺ, pp. 35, 36)\nIn this there is an allusion to the Isrāʾ and Miʿrāj. About this the divine statement is:\n﴿سُبْحٰنَ الَّذِيْۤ اَسْرٰى بِعَبْدِهٖ لَيْلًا مِّنَ الْمَسْجِدِ الْحَرَامِ اِلَى الْمَسْجِدِ الْاَقْصَا الَّذِيْ بٰرَكْنَا حَوْلَهٗ لِنُرِيَهٗ مِنْ اٰيٰتِنَا١ؕ اِنَّهٗ هُوَ السَّمِيْعُ الْبَصِيْرُ۰۰۱﴾ (Al-Isra: 1)\nGlory be to Him who took His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque, whose surroundings We have blessed, so that We might show him some of Our signs. Indeed He alone is the All-Hearing, the All-Seeing.\nThe horse mentioned in the prophecy means al-Burāq."],[1816,"Regarding the Miʿrāj there is a hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari; the Noble Prophet ﷺ says: “Then there was brought to me an animal smaller than a mule and larger than a donkey.”"],[1817,"Al-Jārūd says: O Abū Ḥamzah, is it al-Burāq? Sayyidunā Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, says: Yes, that is what is meant. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Its stride reached as far as its sight reached (that is, wherever its gaze fell, there it placed its foot).”"],[1818,"In the narration of Ibn Isḥāq there is a mursal narration from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī: Sayyidunā Jibrīl, peace be upon him, woke him, took him out through the door of the mosque and mounted him on al-Burāq. Al-Burāq was a white animal between a horse and a mule. It also had two wings, on its right and left, which assisted its feet. Its stride reached as far as its sight reached. Then they set out with him and stayed with him."],[1819,"Ibn Isḥāq narrated from Qatādah. He says that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “When I drew near to al-Burāq to mount it, it shied away. Sayyidunā Jibrīl placed his hand on it and said: O Burāq! Are you not ashamed of what you are doing? By Allah, no servant of Allah has ever mounted you before who has greater honor and dignity than Muhammad the Noble ﷺ.” Then al-Burāq, out of shame, stood still calmly, and it began to sweat. He mounted it."],[1820,"The word Narashansa is composed of two words: Nar, which means: human being, and Ashansa, which means (PRAISED), praiseworthy among people. The meaning of the two words together is: a commended man, one whom people praise and extol. In the introduction to the book of Salām Allāh Ṣiddīqī, Ramesh Prasad writes: The meaning of Narashansa in Arabic is Muhammad; he is the very one who is mentioned in the Atharva Veda.\nA few attributes of Narashansa from the Hindu books are mentioned here.\n§1 - إنه جميل، ويكون أجمل من في الأرض، ويخرج النور من وجهه.\nHe will be handsome and beautiful; in beauty and handsomeness he will surpass the human beings on the face of the earth. Light will burst forth from his face. (Rig Veda: 2/3/2)\n§2 - نحن نثني على نراشنس أي أكرم الأكرمين ومانح الراحة، أخرجنامن الذنوب والآثام مثل إخراجك طقوس الرحلة الدينية.\nWe praise Narashansa, who is the most honorable of all, a cause of comfort. He delivered us from sins and evil deeds, just as he delivered us from wrong religious rites and customs. (Rig Veda: 4/106/1)\nIn the twentieth chapter of the Atharva Veda the word Kantab is also present, which means: hidden. In the future the secret of this word will also be revealed and the truth will become clear to all. (Adya: 1/127/20, 2).\n§3 - نراشنس يرتفع ذكره، ويتغنى به الناس، ونحن نحفظ هذا الداعي إلى الأمن من ستين ألف وتسعين. مركبه الإبل. وله اثنتا عشرة زوجة. أنه منح مامح الكاهن مئة دينار، وعشرة عقود، وثلاثمثة فرس، وعشرة آلاف بقرة. أنه يتكلم كما يتكلم الطير على الأشجار المثمرة، ويسرع في الكلام، ويتحرك شفتاه مثل حادتي المقص.\nThe mention of Narashansa will be exalted, people will sing the praises of his glory. We will protect this caller to peace from sixty thousand and ninety enemies. His mount will be the camel. He will have twelve wives. Mamah the priest will give him a hundred dinars, ten necklaces, three hundred horses and ten thousand cows. He will speak as birds sing on fruit-bearing trees. He will speak somewhat quickly, and his lips will move like the two blades of a pair of scissors.\n§الشرح:\nExplanation: In the above passage there are quite clear indications about some of the attributes of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. A brief explanation of them is given here:\nHis mention will be exalted. In this there is perhaps an allusion to this divine statement:\n﴿وَ رَفَعْنَا لَكَ ذِكْرَكَؕ۰۰۴﴾ (Ash-Sharh: 4)\nAnd We have exalted for you your mention."],[1821,"Without doubt the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is praised (maḥmūd) in the heavens and praised on the earth as well. Everywhere there is talk of his praise and mention. This fact is quite clear from the event of the Miʿrāj. In every heaven the angels welcomed him. (Ibn Hishām, al-Sīrah al-Nabawiyyah: 1/407)\nIbn Isḥāq said: A narration of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached me through ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, that at every heaven, when Jibrīl took him and asked permission to enter, they would say: O Jibrīl, who is this (with you)? Jibrīl would say: Muhammad ﷺ! They would say: Has he been summoned? He would say: Yes! Then they would say: May Allah grant long life to this brother and friend. Until he reached the seventh heaven with him.\nSixty thousand and ninety enemies: in this there is an allusion to his Hijrah from Makkah to Madinah. It may be that at that time the number of people of Makkah and its surroundings was more than sixty thousand.\nWe will protect this caller: in this there is an allusion to his being protected from the enemies. The statement of the Lord Most High is:\n﴿وَ اللّٰهُ يَعْصِمُكَ مِنَ النَّاسِ١ؕ﴾ (Al-Ma’idah: 67)\nGod will protect you from the people (from their evil).\nHis mount will be the camel. Riding a camel is quite unknown in India. None of the Hindu heroes and religious leaders ever rode a camel.\nHe will have twelve wives. It is an accepted and known fact that in Hinduism polygamy is not permitted at all. There is no one among their leaders who had more than one wife, except what is related about Krishna. There was a large number of maidservants for his service, who were known by the title Gopi (GOPI). But his real wife was only one, who was Radha (RADHA). (al-Qāmūs al-Hindūsī, pp. 244-246)\nThat is why we see that the Hindus chant in this way to seek help: Hare Radha, Hare Krishna. This is their common practice in religious processions in the streets.\nAs for the pure wives of the Noble Prophet ﷺ, their number was the same as is stated here. This discussion has been mentioned earlier.\nThe word Mamah is composed of two words: Ma, which is originally Maha, meaning: great, and the second word is Mah, which means praise. The meaning of the two words is: the personality whose praise and glorification is talked of in all the worlds.\nIs there anyone other than our Prophet Muhammad the Noble ﷺ who possesses this attribute?\nAs for the pure dinars, this is an allusion to those sincere Companions of his who made the Hijrah to Abyssinia for the sake of the religion."],[1822,"And in their statement “ten necklaces” (ʿashrat ʿuqūd) the allusion is to those ten Companions of the Prophet ﷺ who were given the glad tidings of Paradise. In the Prophet’s ﷺ view these Companions held the same status as a necklace around the neck. And they are:"],[1823,"1. Sayyidunā Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, the first Caliph."],[1824,"2. Sayyidunā ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, the second Caliph."],[1825,"3. Sayyidunā ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, the third Caliph."],[1826,"4. Sayyidunā ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, the fourth Caliph."],[1827,"5. Sayyidunā Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ, may Allah be pleased with him, whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave the title “the Trustworthy One of this Ummah.”"],[1828,"6. Sayyidunā Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān al-Qurashī, may Allah be pleased with him, about whom the Prophet ﷺ testified to martyrdom while he was still alive. As is narrated in Sahih Muslim from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on Mount Ḥirāʾ, and with him were Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh and al-Zubayr. Then the rock began to move. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Be still! There is none upon you except a prophet, a ṣiddīq or a martyr.”"],[1829,"And he was martyred in the year 36 AH, in Jumādā al-Ākhirah; and it is said, in Rajab."],[1830,"7. Sayyidunā al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām ibn Khuwaylid, may Allah be pleased with him, the disciple (ḥawārī) of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He was the first to draw his sword in the way of Allah."],[1831,"8. Sayyidunā ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, one of the eight people who accepted Islam at the very beginning."],[1832,"9. Sayyidunā Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him."],[1833,"10. Sayyidunā Saʿīd ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him."],[1834,"These ten Companions of the Messenger ﷺ who were given glad tidings of Paradise have many virtues and merits. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave them the glad tidings of Paradise in this world on a single occasion. The statement of the Lord Most High is:\n﴿يٰۤاَيُّهَا النَّبِيُّ اِنَّاۤ اَرْسَلْنٰكَ شَاهِدًا وَّ مُبَشِّرًا وَّ نَذِيْرًاۙ۰۰۴۵ وَّ دَاعِيًا اِلَى اللّٰهِ بِاِذْنِهٖ وَ سِرَاجًا مُّنِيْرًا۰۰۴۶﴾ (Al-Ahzab: 45, 46)\nO Prophet! Indeed We have sent you as a witness, a bearer of glad tidings and a warner, and as one who calls to God by His permission, and as a shining lamp.\nThe preceding sayings and prophecies are from the Atharva Veda. The Hindus have always recited and related them in their religious programs, but history bears witness that these prophecies apply to no one other than our Prophet Muhammad the Noble ﷺ. For thousands of years the Hindus have been awaiting the arrival of this awaited prophet. They are proceeding on the path of knowing pretense of ignorance, although the prophet characterized by these attributes was sent more than fourteen and a half centuries ago. Their own scholars have also raised objections about this, as has already been mentioned.\nIn the Rig Veda there are further prophecies which, although not clear, also contain indications of the sending of the Noble Prophet ﷺ in the last age. There is also some room for interpretation in them. That is why wrong interpretations have been made of them, and in explaining and clarifying them the truth has been avoided. And Allah is the One whose help is sought."],[1835,"Among the prophecies of the Prophet found in Hindu books are also these:\nA man of good character came in the dream of the king of Sindh, Bhoj, and said: O king! Your religion (the Arya Dharma, in India) is better than all religions. But by the command of Ilah Akbar (the Great God) I will make dominant the religion of a man who will be circumcised (contrary to Hindu custom). He will not have a hanging, tied tuft of hair on his head (as Hindu women have). He will have a long beard. He will bring about a great revolution. He will make proclamation among the people. He will eat all lawful and pure things except the flesh of swine. His religion will abrogate all previous religions. We have named his followers Muslims. This religion will be revealed to him (by Ilah Akbar). (Bhavishya Purana: 23/3/3-27)"],[1836,"These glad tidings are also mentioned in Hindu books, on the tongue of Shri Ramchandra.\nRamchandra was asked: After how many stages does a person meet Allah the Almighty? He said: After fourteen stages. He was asked: Has anyone reached there? He said: No. He was asked: Not even you? He said: No. He was asked: Can anyone reach there? He said: Yes. He was asked: What is his name? He said: Mahāmid. He was asked: Where will he be born? He said: He will be born in rocky land, and from the oasis in which he is raised, his religion will spread throughout the whole world. What he says will be true. Whoever accepts his religion will deserve Paradise, and whoever disbelieves will deserve the Fire. He will eat meat and drink milk, he will fight his enemy in jihad with the sword, and he will gain victory over the king of the world. (ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz Jamāl al-Dīn, al-Bishārāt al-Muḥammadiyyah, pp. 118-119)\nVed Vyas wrote a book in the Sanskrit language about the Rama war, which took place in 600 BC. Tulsidas translated this book into Hindi verse during the Mughal era. Some of its verses also contain this mention:\nIn the year seven hundred Bikrami (which is six hundred CE) he will be born. He will appear with four companions. He will call people fully to his religion. People will enter his religion in groups upon groups, whose number will not be known. After his religion prevails, no one will find salvation without believing in Muhammad. He will have no equal and no like. The one who speaks this truth is Tulsidas.\n§4 - The glad tidings of the Chosen Prophet ﷺ on the tongue of Buddha\n§Glad tidings about the Prophet Muṣṭafā ﷺ on the tongue of Gautama Buddha\nThere are many reports that at the time of his death Gautama Buddha told his special disciple Ananda: Ananda! Do not be distressed. I am not the first Buddha to come to the earth, nor the last. After me a Buddha will come who will have a holy and radiant heart. He will be full of wisdom in action and will be blessed. He will be the unparalleled master of the universe and of mankind. The imperishable truths that I am revealing, he too will reveal. Like me, he will preach a complete and pure religious way of life. He will guide people to good and set them on the straight path. His teachings will last forever. His followers will be in the millions, while my followers will be a few hundred. Ananda! His name will be Maitreya. (Gospel of Buddha, by Carus, p. 217)\n(METTEYA) is a Pali word. Its Arabic meaning is mercy (raḥmah).\nOn one occasion Gautama Buddha said: The one who comes after me will be merciful and kind. He will lead the entire world, and he will be the best of all creatures on the face of the earth.\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ is a mercy for all the worlds. He is the Seal of the Prophets. His religion is for all of humanity until the Day of Resurrection.\n§5 - The glad tidings of the Chosen Prophet ﷺ on the tongue of Zoroaster\n§Glad tidings of the Seal of the Prophets ﷺ on the tongue of Zoroaster\nAccording to the preferred view, Zoroaster was born six hundred years before Christ. He is the founder of the Parsi religion. In his books, the Avesta and the Zend, there are indications of an awaited prophet who will be sent in the last age. Mercy is described as his attribute, and Mahmud is given as his title.\nThe several characteristics and glad tidings from other religions mentioned above are perfectly clear in their meaning and purpose. The Old Testament, the New Testament, the Torah and the Gospel, the Hindu books, the books of Buddhism and the books of the Zoroastrian religion all mention the glad tidings of the awaited prophet. The followers of these religions used to read these glad tidings day and night and were waiting for the coming of the awaited prophet, to the point that even now they do not know that their awaited prophet and the prophet of the world was born and sent fourteen and a half centuries ago. But prejudice against Islam and the Prophet of Islam ﷺ has made them blind and without understanding. Their ears too have become deaf to hearing the truth. Concerning those who follow this way, there is a divine warning:\n﴿وَ لَقَدْ ذَرَاْنَا لِجَهَنَّمَ كَثِيْرًا مِّنَ الْجِنِّ وَ الْاِنْسِ١ۖٞ لَهُمْ قُلُوْبٌ لَّا يَفْقَهُوْنَ بِهَا١ٞ وَ لَهُمْ اَعْيُنٌ لَّا يُبْصِرُوْنَ بِهَا١ٞ وَ لَهُمْ اٰذَانٌ لَّا يَسْمَعُوْنَ بِهَا١ؕ اُولٰٓىِٕكَ كَالْاَنْعَامِ بَلْ هُمْ اَضَلُّ١ؕ اُولٰٓىِٕكَ هُمُ الْغٰفِلُوْنَ۰۰۱۷۹﴾ (al-Aʿrāf: 179)\nIndeed, We have created for Hell many of the jinn and mankind. They have hearts (but) they do not understand (the truth) with them; they have eyes (but) they do not see (the truth) with them; they have ears (but) they do not hear (the truth) with them. They are like cattle; rather, they are even more astray. It is they who are the heedless.\nOnly that person will be safe who, by the mercy and generosity of Allah the Almighty, believed in him ﷺ, affirmed him in heart and deed, and entered the list of the believers.\nWe are deeply grateful to Allah, Lord of the worlds, for honoring us with the blessing of Islam. He granted us the success and guidance to believe in and affirm the master of the children of Adam ﷺ, the Prophet of mercy. May Allah send His mercy upon him, and bestow His grace and generosity upon his Companions and upon all who follow him with excellence and sincerity until the Day of Resurrection."],[1837,"The awaited Prophet ﷺ appeared in Makkah fourteen and a half centuries ago. After his appointment as a messenger and prophet, he faced many hardships and difficulties. The hardships began first from his own family, then came from the Quraysh, then from the people living around Makkah. Then they came from the polytheists of Arabia, then from the Jews, then from the Christians, and then the hypocrites joined as well. But Allah the Almighty helped and supported him against all of them. He departed from this world after Islam had prevailed over the Arabian Peninsula and its surroundings. Tawḥīd and the message of prophethood were raised high. Afterwards the noble Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, following in his footsteps, spread the religion to every corner of the earth. They conveyed the message of truth to Arabs and non-Arabs alike. In this way the word of Allah the Almighty was proven true:\n﴿هُوَ الَّذِيْۤ اَرْسَلَ رَسُوْلَهٗ بِالْهُدٰى وَ دِيْنِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهٗ عَلَى الدِّيْنِ كُلِّهٖ وَ لَوْ كَرِهَ الْمُشْرِكُوْنَؒ۰۰۹﴾ [al-Ṣaff: 9]\nHe is the One who sent His Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth, so that He may make it prevail over all religions, even if the polytheists dislike it.\nAll praise is for Allah the Almighty for the blessing of Islam, and for believing in the master of creation, the Prophet of mercy and peace ﷺ. May Allah’s mercies be upon him, upon his family, upon his noble Companions, and upon everyone who follows him with excellence until the Day of Resurrection.\nAuthor: may Allah pardon him\nMadīnat al-Muṣṭafā ﷺ\n٭…٭…٭"]]);
